glossaryData.push(...[{"name":"Guardian (Perpetual)","definition":"The “Perpetual Guardian Payments” obtained for the responsible group maintaining every Casey Machine site as a shrine. They are intended to provide Perpetual Care and continue indefinitely [CM:513].","citations":["CM:513"],"tags":["Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Guardians","definition":"Keepers and defenders in several contexts. George Duckhunter says his group is paid as “high priced guardians,” though he no longer clearly remembers the arrangement [CM:504]. X describes Devil worshippers guarding the Devil’s Crimean prison [MTM B6:204]. Zabotski affirms that angels guard the Ark and other things inaccessible to human guardians [MTM B9:280-281]. The armed Orthodoxers who threaten him are ironically called “guardians of pure science” [MTM B9:286]. A letter to Stein assigns the remaining defense against Principalities and Powers to jokers after the “proper defenders” have fallen asleep [A:266]. Finnegan kills two guards during his attack on the Pirate Hotel [D:231]; John Mogul also employs guards [MB:573].","citations":["CM:504","MTM B6:204","MTM B9:280-281","MTM B9:286","A:266","D:231","MB:573"],"tags":["Angels & Demons","The Devil","Finnegan","Money & Trade","Combat"]},{"name":"Guebwiller","definition":"Town in France, named among the places whose prehistoric paintings Finnegan discusses with Duffey. Finnegan maintains that apparent birds and butterflies are speech-and-song balloons in the original complex language; Duffey objects that the Neanderthal painters had neither speech nor writing [MTM B6:193].","citations":["MTM B6:193"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Guest Speaker","definition":"The advertised speaker at the New Catholic Press Guild meeting, called “The Most Important Person in the World” and later “The Majesty.” Duffey recognizes the Devil behind the grand claims and theatrical presentation [MTM B8:239-241].","citations":["MTM B8:239-241"],"tags":["Catholic Church","The Devil","Journalism","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Guilt","definition":"Finnegan considers guilt as one possible explanation of Seaworthy’s dream corpse, then concludes that the corpse was literally a corpse [D:74]. Seaworthy describes himself as guilty of “shattering drunkenness” [D:11]. The verse opening “Biloxi Brannagan” mourns the “guiltless slain” [D:196]. Vincent’s theory of a recently created world includes memories of “debts and guilts” for events that never happened [A:57]. Duffey rejects photographic blackmail because he has confessed his actual sins and been absolved [MTM B4:140]. “Oh Let the Devil Go” describes guilt as “an over-flow” in its vision of the Devil’s suffering [PG:521].","citations":["D:74","D:11","D:196","A:57","MTM B4:140","PG:521"],"tags":["Sin & Redemption","Duffey","Alcohol","Dreams","Creation","The Devil"]},{"name":"Guiness","definition":"The spelling Guiness appears in the supplied text. See <em>Guinness</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Alcohol"]},{"name":"Guinness","definition":"Guinness, the Irish beer associated with St. James’s Gate Brewery in Dublin, which Arthur Guinness leased in 1759. One of the four brews Patrick Stranahan includes in his compact rule of life, alongside an American, a Danish, and a Bohemian brew [A:110]. The text spells the name “Guiness.”","citations":["A:110"],"tags":["Alcohol"]},{"name":"Guitar","definition":"Salvation Sally carries a hymn-strumming guitar with a collecting cup attached [A:8]. Dotty doubts that Stein’s tuning can remove its twang and imagines Sally and Mary Virginia leaving only blond hairs and a piece of guitar string after a quarrel [A:147]. The instrument is later said to share Sally’s Australian accent [MTM B7:214]. The narrator contrasts the supposed gloom of guitar music with the banjo [MTM B2:52]. In Elena O’Higgins’s account, Casey replaces a sacred organ score with music for kazoo, peewee guitar, and musical saw [PG:525]. Electric guitarists have five Gates of their own in the enlarged Ekklesia [MB:562].","citations":["A:8","A:147","MTM B7:214","MTM B2:52","PG:525","MB:562"],"tags":["Music","Ekklesia","Liturgy"]},{"name":"Guiterrez, Sammy (Captain)","definition":"Tagalog captain called Sammy, met on Luzon [A:63], a friend of the Dirty Five who appears thirteen although about twenty-three. He calls the men “hairy old men” [A:66]. He admires Absalom Stein and teaches at the reopened school where Stein is also teaching. With Finnegan distracting the shoe-shine boys on a mouth organ, Sammy borrows their box without permission, earns four pesos from four shines, returns the box, and buys more whisky [A:67-68]. He joins the men’s walk to the field of white horses, an expedition numbered among the Argonauts’ heroic labors [A:68]. The episode continues on the next page: Sammy hums little songs and is the first of the six men to fall asleep in the grass [A:69].","citations":["A:63","A:66","A:67-68","A:68","A:69"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Finnegan","Soldiers","Alcohol","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Gulf","definition":"Used for several waters and their adjoining regions. Anastasia places mermaids in the Persian Gulf in her account of their former range [D:35]. Hillary Hilton boasts of selling refitted garbage scows to an Arabian Gulf potentate [A:255]. A projected account of Zabotski’s house places it on Lake Borgne where it opens into the Gulf of Mexico [MTM B9:288].<br><br>The Gulf also enters the characters’ ordinary surroundings: Gertrude is described as a gulf-state lady, and her home with Brannagan is full of gulf winds [D:200-201]. Gulf southerlies are among the winds in the account of ballooning from St. Louis [MTM B2:65]. The Stranahans plan annual expeditions after gulf marlin [A:103].","citations":["D:35","A:255","MTM B9:288","D:200-201","MTM B2:65","A:103"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Sailing","Fishing"]},{"name":"Gulf Coast Lady","definition":"Gertrude Brannagan is described as a “gulf-state lady,” full-built, dark, and pretty, whose presence makes Finnegan feel safe. Her age is given as forty-five, with fifteen years allowed in either direction [D:200].","citations":["D:200"],"tags":["Women Characters"]},{"name":"Gulf of Mexico","definition":"The editor’s note locates Zabotski’s enormous house on the coast of Lake Borgne where the lake opens into the Gulf of Mexico [MTM B9:288].","citations":["MTM B9:288"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Louisiana"]},{"name":"Gulf of Oman","definition":"Point of departure in the verse about the Dutchman who still sails from the Gulf of Oman carrying a cargo to Cutch [A:172].","citations":["A:172"],"tags":["Sailing","Maritime Trade","Seas & Oceans","Literature"]},{"name":"Gullab Coast","definition":"Coast named by Don Barnaby among the places where Madame Le Mourcheor’s Juju Joy Pills are not made. He says that they come from New Orleans [D:249]. The supplied page spells the name “Gullab”; its geographical identification remains uncertain.","citations":["D:249"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Gullah Coast","definition":"Earlier glossary spelling of Gullab Coast. See <em>Gullab Coast</em> [D:249].","citations":["D:249"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Gulls of Ship Island","definition":"Birds that join the sandpipers of Horn Island in making a glad noise as the <em>Argo</em> enters Biloxi to dock [MTM B13:408].","citations":["MTM B13:408"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","Islands"]},{"name":"Gum Arabic","definition":"Listed with formaldehyde in Casey’s purported recipe for Bakelite, item XVI in the notebook extracts [A:163].","citations":["A:163"],"tags":[]},{"name":"Gumbo-head","definition":"Casey’s self-description in his contrast between “feet of clay” and a “gumbo-head.” He immediately insists that he is almost the cleverest of the group and explains his apparent stupidity as the effect of having seen the odds against their endeavors [A:155-156].","citations":["A:155-156"],"tags":["Identity"]},{"name":"Gums (Lesser)","definition":"The unnamed lesser gum among Askandanakandrian’s seven sovereign substances for rehabilitating nervous merchandise. He also includes the gum among the four he would choose if forced to reduce his supply [A:257].","citations":["A:257"],"tags":["Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Gun","definition":"Firearms appear as weapons, contraband, and occasions for grim jokes. Manuel describes a force running guns and men in his country, which he says lies south of Mexico [D:56]. The Naxos attack includes rifles, BARs, machine guns, and the larger weapon aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em> [D:143] [D:146].<br><br>Sebastian “Shot Gun” Schaeffer’s nickname actually comes from a drink he invented. He is mistaken for a killer, and George “Basin Street” Becaud later frightens him by claiming that his bass case holds an enormous shotgun [D:222-223].<br><br>The young discoverer of Perverse Logic fires at Casey Gorshok, but the <em>Argo</em> men have already withdrawn from that time and place [MTM B13:428-429]. Air-guns and blow-guns occur among the proposed murder devices in John Mogul’s contest [MB:580]. The Stranahan family’s “Rod and Gun Club” is Maurine’s nickname for its hunters and fishermen [A:103].","citations":["D:56","D:143","D:146","D:222-223","MTM B13:428-429","MB:580","A:103"],"tags":["Weapons","Combat","Crime"]},{"name":"Gun cases","definition":"Cases in the Stranahan Club Room, which also contains a simulated fireplace serving as a sandbagged pistol range [A:103].","citations":["A:103"],"tags":["Weapons","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Gur'yev","definition":"Guryev, now Atyrau in Kazakhstan. Town described as standing at the east mouth of the Ural River in Tartary. After Duffey’s killing, his bones and smoking ashes appear in an unbusy street there. X and Kasmir Gorshok independently arrive at the same place and identify both as Duffey’s remains [MTM B13:442].","citations":["MTM B13:442"],"tags":["Duffey","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Gusher","definition":"A newspaper reporter calls the expected heavy rain a “gusher” while asking whether it will float Zabotski’s construction down Dumaine Street to Lake Borgne [MTM B9:289]. The Laughing Prince’s poisoned spring is also a gusher, defying hydraulic laws [MTM B13:441]. The proposed murder by swarming animals describes a victim’s viscera gushing out [MB:580-581].","citations":["MTM B9:289","MTM B13:441","MB:580-581"],"tags":["Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Gusty Joy","definition":"A quality attributed in the earlier glossary to the Boy King Melchisedech; the phrase and its cited occurrence remain unlocated.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Guts","definition":"Margaret Stone reports that the Sardinian Red Fisters threaten to wear her guts as scarves [MTM B7:225]. In <em>How Many Miles to Babylon?</em>, the entrails of Paul XI are used to strangle Hiram III and are then eaten in a further spectacle [MB:564-565].","citations":["MTM B7:225","MB:564-565"],"tags":["Death & Mortality","Popes","Combat"]},{"name":"Gutter","definition":"In Seaworthy’s dream, he tries dumping the corpse into a gutter but is forced to retrieve it [D:73]. Vincent’s story of two bums in a gutter mocks the refusal to try a good idea because it has been tried before [A:57]. Wooden crossings over mud gutters are among the details of other children’s lives appropriated by Melchisedech [MTM B1:16].","citations":["D:73","A:57","MTM B1:16"],"tags":["Dreams","Death & Mortality","History & Memory"]},{"name":"H Sound (Latin)","definition":"The sound that Casey’s notebook says corresponds in German or English to Latin K or hard C. Casey includes the correspondence in his summary of Grimm’s law [A:163].","citations":["A:163"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Habib","definition":"Form of address exchanged by Finnegan and an unnamed boy in Tangier. The boy is one of the runners who keep Finnegan informed and directs him to the bottom of the tell, where Don Lewis lies dead [D:104] [D:109].","citations":["D:104","D:109"],"tags":["Africa","Finnegan","Death & Mortality","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Habitation","definition":"The nursery track where Hans runs is the summer habitation of rabbits, coneys, ground squirrels, bull snakes, and many other small creatures [A:208]. X says that he has neither habitation nor name and expects to die without papers on his body [A:138].","citations":["A:208","A:138"],"tags":["Identity"]},{"name":"Habonim","definition":"Landing place of the <em>Argo</em> where the hilly “Plains of Megiddo” begin. The ship brings the figure identified as Peleus, Kasmir Gorshok, Prince Casimir, and Casey Szymansky, now manifested as the Antichrist, to rule from there for his appointed period [MTM B13:462].","citations":["MTM B13:462"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","Ships","The Devil"]},{"name":"Hackenschmidt of Weinsburg, Conrad Stone","definition":"Conrad Stone Hackenschmidt of Weinsburg in the Germanies leads the final Conclave of the Ekklesia in Babylonia Bagascia [MB:566]. With a Browning Automatic Rifle supplied by Monsignor X, he orders each prince to vote <em>Ego eligo effigiem</em> for the Straw-Man. He shoots Stockton Stone Crocker for voting for Joseph Cardinal Hedayat; six dissenting votes are ultimately recounted for the effigy [MB:567]. He orders X to burn the effigy and then the six bodies, announces the dissolution of their princely offices, and demands the destruction of all bells [MB:568]. He declares the thirteen absent princes dead, commissions Track and Total to kill them, and tries unsuccessfully to dispose of X [MB:568-569].","citations":["MB:566","MB:567","MB:568","MB:568-569"],"tags":["Germany","Ekklesia","Weapons","Death & Mortality","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Hacksaw","definition":"Tools used by three young men to cut both hands of the Great Delta Insurance Company tower clock down to stubs. One falls to his death while climbing down afterward [GD:483].","citations":["GD:483"],"tags":["Time & Eternity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Hadriaticus","definition":"Latin form associated with the Adriatic Sea. John Schultz, writing to Marie as a former Orpheus, recalls the <em>Argo</em> crossing the Hadriaticus westward from Illyria. Sometimes it had no far shore; sometimes the western shore was not Italy [MTM B6:186].","citations":["MTM B6:186"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans","Argonautica"]},{"name":"Haggada","definition":"The narrator cites the Haggada for the claim that the forbidden fruit was wormy, adding that St. Augustine seems to refer to the same detail. The worm in the fruit becomes the figure for the disquiet that breaks up the companions’ earthly paradise, beginning with Johnny Duckwalk’s decision to confess his theft and accept imprisonment [D:264].","citations":["D:264"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Sin & Redemption","Old Testament"]},{"name":"Hair","definition":"Duffey has sorrel hair, a color shared by his kit-fox totem; Mary Louise Byrne’s hair is still redder [MTM B1:6] [MTM B1:19] [MTM B2:40] [MTM B2:55]. Among the churchgoers whom Duffey watches is a chubby young woman with blonde hair beneath a black veil [MTM B3:88]. Dotty has chestnut hair [A:106]. Brannagan’s hair is sandy with a token touch of patriarchal gray [MTM B13:409]; Mary Virginia has gray hairs in the halo framing her face [MTM B11:318]. Patrick Stranahan has abundant body hair and exceptionally hairy eyebrows; it is his face that resembles blue emery paper [A:102]. The hairs on the giant spider’s toes are thicker than most trees [A:62].","citations":["MTM B1:6","MTM B1:19","MTM B2:40","MTM B2:55","MTM B3:88","A:106","MTM B13:409","MTM B11:318","A:102","A:62"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Haiti hoodoo joy pills","definition":"One of the varieties of joy pill listed by Don Barnaby, alongside High Harry, Deep Romany, Dead Frenchman, and Black Rooster. He distinguishes these from Madame Le Mourcheor’s Old Original Juju Joy Pills, whose efficacy he praises [D:248-249].","citations":["D:248-249"],"tags":["Caribbean","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Hal Clement","definition":"Author of <em>Left of Africa</em>, listed as the first book in Manuscript Press’s “Lost Manuscripts” series. In the publisher’s afterword to <em>Archipelago</em>, Rick Norwood says he obtained publication rights to an unpublished Hal Clement manuscript [A:285-286].","citations":["A:285-286"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Half-maps","definition":"Artificially aged portions of treasure maps made by Angela Cosquin’s father. Confidence men sell matching halves to distant victims, then collect further payments while bringing the owners together [D:261].","citations":["D:261"],"tags":["Crime","Money & Trade","Family"]},{"name":"Half-Master","definition":"Designation of X aboard the <em>Argo</em>. After Duffey’s death, Brannagan and Casey Gorshok continue their work as <em>Argo</em> Masters with “the Half-Master X”; Duffey subsequently resumes his duties in skeletal form [MTM B13:443].","citations":["MTM B13:443"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Duffey"]},{"name":"Hallahan, Larry","definition":"Larry and Olivia Hallahan belong to the “slippery people” of the Duffeys’ Chicago circle [MTM B3:100]. They remain at supper with Duffey after Demetrio Glauch leaves in disgust [MTM B4:127]. The Hallahans later belong to the salon group that wants to retain Duffey and exploit his magic [MTM B4:137].","citations":["MTM B3:100","MTM B4:127","MTM B4:137"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Hallahan, Olivia","definition":"One of the “slippery people” of the Duffeys’ Chicago circle, named with Larry Hallahan [MTM B3:100]. Olivia joins the women who hiss at Demetrio Glauch like she-adders when he leaves their supper; she remains to finish the meal with Duffey and the others [MTM B4:127].","citations":["MTM B3:100","MTM B4:127"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Hallock's Grocery Cart","definition":"Horse-drawn vehicle named in the Royal Pop lecturer’s account of the memories preserved by a flagstone in Decatur Street [MTM B11:328-329].","citations":["MTM B11:328-329"],"tags":["New Orleans","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Hallways","definition":"Long corridors in the house of Lily Koch’s kindred through which she leads the eight young people away from the formal dance to the attic billiard room [MTM B1:31-33].","citations":["MTM B1:31-33"],"tags":["Family"]},{"name":"Halo (Smokey)","definition":"The “unreality fringe” or smoky halo surrounding Duffey’s magnificent animations at the Rounders’ Club, including Hans, Marie, Henri, Dotty, Mary Virginia, and Absalom. The narration insists that these people are real despite the fringe [MTM B5:158].","citations":["MTM B5:158"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Ham (Inspired)","definition":"The theatrical exuberance of the mice Duffey summons to act in his puppet shows. The narrator calls their acting “inspired ham”; the living mice speak their roles and perform beside Letitia’s inanimate puppets [MTM B4:134].","citations":["MTM B4:134"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Theater"]},{"name":"Hammer","definition":"A young man uses a hammer to smash the glass of Melchisedech Duffy’s watch with exaggerated gentleness [GD:481]. Small drop-hammers also form part of Zabotski’s brass-smelting equipment [MTM B6:192].","citations":["GD:481","MTM B6:192"],"tags":["Duffey","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Hammerschmidt's Gym (St. Louis)","definition":"St. Louis gymnasium where Bagby brings the young Duffey to work out before boxing managers and promoters. They arrange his preliminary bout against Dandy Dan Dillard [MTM B2:50]. The episode belongs to the summer of 1915, when Duffey is approaching sixteen in this account of his youth [MTM B2:38] [MTM B2:49].","citations":["MTM B2:50","MTM B2:38","MTM B2:49"],"tags":["Duffey","Occupations","Combat"]},{"name":"Hammock","definition":"The four companions keep hammocks near their favorite bar at Basse-Terre, in a secluded spot where they spend weeks together [D:236]. Finnegan twice hears another man’s voice from Don Barnaby’s hammock while Barnaby continues sleeping. One asks him to repay fifty dollars owed to Anastasia; the other says that he has been dead in the sand for three years and begs to be released [D:238] [D:256].","citations":["D:236","D:238","D:256"],"tags":["Caribbean","Finnegan","The Dead","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Hammonton, New Jersey","definition":"Home of Phoebe Anne Ankerson, as recorded on the tag inside the suitcase stolen by Finnegan. The tag gives Hammonton as the return address after her visit to Minnie MacMasters [D:172].","citations":["D:172"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Hamper","definition":"Doll Delaney brings a hamper of food, drink, ice, and red roses to her graveyard meeting with Finnegan and X on the day of the Indianapolis race [D:276]. X asks for devil’s food cake, but Doll corrects him: it is gingerbread [D:277]. The picnic also includes wine, sandwiches, coffee, and cakes; they return the remnants and papers to the hamper [D:284].","citations":["D:276","D:277","D:284"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Alcohol","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Hand (Invisible Giant)","definition":"The hand summoned by eight-year-old Melchisedech to help ring the church bell when he is too light to move it alone. The giant is invisible, but its hand is visible to the other boys [MTM B1:14-15].","citations":["MTM B1:14-15"],"tags":["Giants & Titans","Duffey","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Hand (Right/Left)","definition":"The intended reference for this heading has not been established.","citations":[],"tags":[]},{"name":"Hand-washing money","definition":"Money Duffey receives when his “false kindred” rid themselves of responsibility for him. He still has some during boarding school, along with profits from his ventures with Rattigan [MTM B1:24]. At the end of school, a further hundred dollars is described as the final act of the hand-washing ceremony [MTM B2:38].","citations":["MTM B1:24","MTM B2:38"],"tags":["Duffey","Money & Trade","Family"]},{"name":"Handbook of Treasons","definition":"Work credited to Miles O’Connel in the collection of documents inserted into Book Five. Its quoted passage identifies the “spiritual army of evil in high stations” with the devils of the word-mills and says that some of those stations are within the Church [MTM B5:175-176].","citations":["MTM B5:175-176"],"tags":["Literature","Catholic Church","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Hands (Severed Giant)","definition":"Image in Duffey’s dream of a spreading chasm. His summoned giants’ hands arrive severed at the forearms, powerless and manacled; they then become a little boy’s mittens joined by their drawstring [MTM B2:68]. A painting of the Severed Giant Hands hangs above the doorway to the Elegant Riverboat Deck at the Rounders’ Club. In its presence, Duffey feels deprived of creative power by the independence and superiority of his own creations [MTM B5:156].","citations":["MTM B2:68","MTM B5:156"],"tags":["Giants & Titans","Duffey","Clubs & Societies","Dreams","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Handsomeness (Fevered)","definition":"Description of Don Lewis at the party’s formal dinner on Grand Canary. The narrator says he already bears the mark of death and will be the featured figure in the next act of the masque [D:100].","citations":["D:100"],"tags":["Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Hannigan (or Hanlon or O'Hanlon)","definition":"Possible surnames of Casey’s farm-boy schoolmate whom he now calls Hinnegan. Casey admits that knowing Finnegan has caused him to reconstruct the boy’s name and memory so that they rhyme [A:162].","citations":["A:162"],"tags":["History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Hans","definition":"See <em>Schultz, John G.</em>","citations":[],"tags":["Argonauts (Characters)"]},{"name":"Hanschen","definition":"Name used for Hans Schultz by Marie Monaghan. Salvation Sally asks what Marie calls him and adopts “Hanschen” in saying goodnight [A:9].","citations":["A:9"],"tags":["Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Harbor express","definition":"Hidden route to the wharfs used by Count Finnegan and his companion in the role of Herman Hercules to evade their pursuers. The crawl-way passes through cellars, connecting tunnels, storm sewers, electrical conduits, heat ducts, and thieves’ catacombs [MB:587]. The next page leaves the companion’s precise identity, including his possible role as Cardinal Erculo in his double’s disguise, uncertain [MB:588].","citations":["MB:587","MB:588"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Harbor House","definition":"Tavern where Hans Schultz and Joe Bushmaster compete in beer drinking under Dominion Rules [A:15] [A:19]. After Hans wins, the company buys a round for everyone in the house [A:23].","citations":["A:15","A:19","A:23"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Hard C sound (Latin)","definition":"Casey’s notebook says that a Latin K or hard C becomes H in German or English, as part of its summary of Grimm’s law [A:163].","citations":["A:163"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Hard Stuff","definition":"The earlier glossary associates this phrase with Teresa Piccone’s comments on Finnegan’s drinking, but the passage has not been located.","citations":[],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Harlow House","definition":"An old house on an abandoned farm in the New Jersey Barrens, where Gautier the goat-man often stays with his goats. The house may be haunted, but Gautier knows no ghost there except himself. His syrinx and bagpipe playing accounts for much of the ghost music in the surrounding wasteland. Finnegan knows the house through Gautier’s descriptions and never sees it himself [A:196-197].","citations":["A:196-197"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Music","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Harmonica (Pocket)","definition":"Instrument Finnegan plays during the graveyard meeting with X and Doll. His tunes comment on X’s narrative: “Arise, ye Saints, Arise,” Browne’s Prelude, “Oh what was your name in the States?,” “A policeman’s life is not a happy one,” and “Wake the town and tell the people” [D:284-285] [D:287].","citations":["D:284-285","D:287"],"tags":["Finnegan","Music","Identity"]},{"name":"Harpies","definition":"Margaret Stone calls Duffey’s visiting women “harpies” in her parody of the Gadarene Swine Song: “The harpies came from Illinois.” Her verses follow Mary Catherine Carruthers’s departure and the news that no more visitors from the North are expected for the moment [MTM B8:262].","citations":["MTM B8:262"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Folklore","Music","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Harrington, Charles O.A.","definition":"Named reviewer in the epigraph to chapter sixteen, “Liar’s Paradise.” His review in <em>Science for Today</em> attacks Royce Rollins’s <em>The Return of the Neanderthal</em>, subtitled <em>The Alien Within</em>, while outlining its theory of surviving Neanderthals and hidden bloodlines. He dismisses Rollins as a crank but warns that similarly outlandish notions have founded cults [D:240-242].","citations":["D:240-242"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"Harrison County, Iowa","definition":"One of the three Iowa counties offered as the birthplace of Melchisedech in the account of one of his childhoods, alongside Shelby and Pottawattamie. Seven towns or townships dispute the honor [MTM B1:7].","citations":["MTM B1:7"],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Hart, Charles","definition":"One of Duffey’s “pretended uncles.” When Mary Ellen Hart worries about the jars of blood Duffey keeps, Charles says he used to keep blood too, since it attracts weasels, and insists that there is no harm in the boy [MTM B1:18].","citations":["MTM B1:18"],"tags":["Duffey","Family"]},{"name":"Hart, Mary Ellen","definition":"One of Duffey’s “pretended kindred,” called Aunt Mary Ellen Hart. She says his first talisman came from a box of crackerjacks, that it has grown since then, and that he makes others like it. She also tells Charles that the boy keeps jars of blood [MTM B1:18].","citations":["MTM B1:18"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Harvest (The)","definition":"Salvation Sally announces that “The harvest is at hand” while preaching the Second Coming [A:8]. Mary Lightfoot applies the image to the end of the world, when the “blow-mouths” will be separated from the good and burned; Countess Margaret recalls the enemy who sowed them [MTM B4:130].","citations":["A:8","MTM B4:130"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","New Testament"]},{"name":"Harvesters' Dinner","definition":"Abundant meal served to Duffey’s party at the Colony Club in Chicago. Charlotte says that either her sister or one of Duffey’s ladies has ordered it. Roasting ears are central, followed by beef, potatoes, bread, cabbage, kale, sauerkraut, cheeses, sausages, and hot biscuits; the food keeps arriving [MTM B3:93-94]. Sebastian joins the table, and the meal belongs to the gathering of Duffey, the Koch sisters, and the Mullens/Cavendish party [MTM B3:93-95].","citations":["MTM B3:93-94","MTM B3:93-95"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Food & Dining","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Hash","definition":"A kilo of hash is given to every person in Babylonia Bagascia among the offerings and entertainments preceding the final Conclave. The narrator calls the custom “Hash and Circuses” [MB:566].","citations":["MB:566"],"tags":["Ekklesia","Carnival"]},{"name":"Hash-Bashers","definition":"One of the constituencies given special Stones in the enlarged Ekklesia, alongside Trippers, Dippers, Mushroomers, old-time Drunkards, and Fundamentalist Drunkards [MB:562].","citations":["MB:562"],"tags":["Ekklesia","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Hashed-Ectocarpus Collins","definition":"Cocktail ordered by Duffey from Honeybucket Kincaid during the <em>Argo</em>’s visit to a future Louisiana swamp settlement. Honeybucket gathers its ingredients from the water by manipulating electrical frequencies [MTM B13:416-418].","citations":["MTM B13:416-418"],"tags":["Louisiana","Duffey","Alcohol","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Haste","definition":"Joseph needs a partner who will not spoil a diamond deal through haste or nervousness. He warns Finnegan that realizing the value of a consignment may take years [D:94].","citations":["D:94"],"tags":["Crime","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Hat","definition":"Duffey associates a flat straw hat with banjo playing [MTM B2:52]. Beth Keegan puts on her extraordinary beauty as casually as a funny hat [MTM B2:57]. Casey wears a shako for his planned appearance as the Leader [MTM B4:117]. His later appearance as the initially unrecognized Kasmir Gorshok includes a black hat and ear locks [MTM B13:425-426].","citations":["MTM B2:52","MTM B2:57","MTM B4:117","MTM B13:425-426"],"tags":["Music","Theater","Identity"]},{"name":"Hat (Pearl-gray derby)","definition":"The small pearl-gray derby worn by the black crewman of the <em>Seabach</em>. It is too small for him. He wears it into battle on Naxos and dies laughing [D:135] [D:147].","citations":["D:135","D:147"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Islands","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Hat (Wool-Hat)","definition":"The customer who kills Betty’s father Hochstapler with the swindler’s own barkers’ mallet or gavel during a dispute over less than three hundred dollars. He claims he meant only to hit him and receives a sentence of a year and a day [A:217].","citations":["A:217"],"tags":["Carnival","Crime","Family"]},{"name":"Hatch follower","definition":"One of the Fundamentalers who questions Zabotski, asking whether his reference to the present year as 200,000 AD was a joke. Zabotski responds that the date is a riddle and challenges the certainty of ordinary chronology [MTM B9:281].","citations":["MTM B9:281"],"tags":["Time & Eternity","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Hatchet","definition":"Small hatchets are laid beside every diner’s plate on Zabotski’s ark for cutting tough rhinoceros and behemoth steaks [MTM B12:364]. Duffey attends the meal with Zabotski, Wife Waldo, and the children [MTM B12:363-364].","citations":["MTM B12:364","MTM B12:363-364"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","Ships"]},{"name":"Haussa boy, Joseph","definition":"Haussa diamond smuggler whom Finnegan befriends in Freetown. He is described as coming from Nigeria or the Gold Coast, with experience as a caravan boy and seaman. They drink palm wine called barn, then gin [D:93]. Joseph speaks English, Arabic, Ful, French, and Afrikaans and intends to learn proper Dutch for his trade. He recruits Finnegan as a trustworthy partner for foreign marketing [D:94]. He warns him about his evil shipmates, then supplies a heavy leather sack of diamonds; Finnegan markets from this and two later sacks and sends drafts to Freetown [D:95]. Duffey later says that their partnership continues and supplies Finnegan’s friends with diamonds [MTM B10:303].","citations":["D:93","D:94","D:95","MTM B10:303"],"tags":["Africa","Finnegan","Minor Characters","Money & Trade","Crime","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Havana","definition":"Destination of Duffey’s first journey in search of Finnegan’s reported death. From Havana he intends to investigate the nearby Marianao Coast. His companion is the detective Carmelo Mendoza, subsequently exposed as Mr. X using another disguise [MTM B12:367] [MTM B12:369-371].","citations":["MTM B12:367","MTM B12:369-371"],"tags":["Caribbean","Finnegan","Duffey","Death & Mortality","Identity"]},{"name":"Haven (Golden Childrens Home and Haven Orphanage)","definition":"Orphanage behind Zabotski’s property. The children enjoy his growing construction, but Widow Waldo brings the state inspector’s demand that he remove the part encroaching on its grounds [MTM B9:271] [MTM B9:274]. Its golden roof and the endowment from Harry Goldchild are described by the narrator [MTM B9:280]. The children bring their animate green-clay animals aboard the construction [MTM B9:284].","citations":["MTM B9:271","MTM B9:274","MTM B9:280","MTM B9:284"],"tags":["Ships","Women Characters","Money & Trade","Creation"]},{"name":"Haven (in the Shoals)","definition":"The refuge in the hewn cave of the Mooring Stone, among the Belling Shoals or Ringing Rocks. Finnegan, Gilberto Levine and O’Brien, and Herman Hercules leave the <em>Argo</em> there to join thirteen supposed doubles of murdered princes. Finnegan says that one of the thirteen is a surviving holy man rather than a shadow, so the line will remain unbroken before Armageddon [MTM B13:453].","citations":["MTM B13:453"],"tags":["Ships","Finnegan","Ekklesia","Apocalyptic","Identity"]},{"name":"Hawk (bird)","definition":"Anastasia says that hawks nest just out of sight in the sky and appear suddenly when they swoop down. She gives this teasing answer when Finnegan asks where the expected boat, the <em>Hawk</em>, will come from [D:131].","citations":["D:131"],"tags":["Women Characters","Ships"]},{"name":"Hawk (boat/ship)","definition":"Papa Devil’s small boat, called <em>Shouakh</em> and spelled <em>Seabach</em>. Finnegan says the name means hawk; Papa says that the boat is not fast but appears suddenly [D:122]. He hopes to use it in the Naxos operation but fears a counter-ambush by their clever antagonist [D:123]. Anastasia must signal the arriving boat from a narrow glen after moonset, while Finnegan remains uncertain where it will come from [D:127] [D:131]. It appears as the <em>Hawk</em> upon the Sea in his sonnet [D:134]. The vessel arrives from Anatolia, and the account of the battle says that the smaller boat is sinking after its attack [D:135] [D:146].","citations":["D:122","D:123","D:127","D:131","D:134","D:135","D:146"],"tags":["Ships","Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Combat"]},{"name":"Hay field","definition":"According to X’s family history, Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch Papadiabolous Chortovitch was born in a hay field near Krakow, perhaps nearer Skawine, in the year of Restored Salvation 1830 [D:278].","citations":["D:278"],"tags":["The Devil","Eastern Europe","Devils (Characters)"]},{"name":"Hayden, Mary Irene (Schultz)","definition":"Hans Schultz’s mother, Mary Irene Hayden, married to John William Schultz; Hans is their only child, born at St. Gallen, Wisconsin [A:207]. She has known Professor Kirol for years and views his influence with some doubt. She tells Hans that she first heard “phoney” from the Professor, applied to himself, and calls him a noble but compromised example of the old kind [A:211].","citations":["A:207","A:211"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Haydens (Family)","definition":"One of the family names in the older relative’s list of Duffey’s kin. The list belongs to the disputed account of his childhood between five and twelve; Duffey says the supposed relatives may instead belong to his twin [MTM B1:7].","citations":["MTM B1:7"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Hayloft","definition":"Place where Howland chants his jingle about five gophers while he and Finnegan lie up for the night during their journey toward Chicago [A:179].","citations":["A:179"],"tags":["Finnegan","Music"]},{"name":"Hazh-Khazh","definition":"Language in which Zabotski believes the instructions for his great construction are written. Duffey points out that Hazh-Khazh has never been deciphered; Zabotski says he must decipher it as he builds [MTM B9:272].","citations":["MTM B9:272"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Language & Words","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Head","definition":"Duffey’s television projection shows Casey as a horrifying head-only person carried by four black giants; the narration explicitly says the head is not shown as severed [MTM B12:381]. In <em>How Many Miles to Babylon?</em>, the severed head of Paul XI is displayed on a pike at the Spanish Stairs and continues speaking under torture [MB:562].","citations":["MTM B12:381","MB:562"],"tags":["Popes","Giants & Titans","Death & Mortality","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Head (Casey's Projected)","definition":"Image of Casey as a livid, grimacing, cursing head-only person, carried by four black giants over the television picture of <em>Goldfarb’s Alley</em>. Duffey animates it from a painting Adam Scanlon made of Casey without Casey’s knowledge. The eyes are sewn open, preventing the image from refusing to see [MTM B12:381].","citations":["MTM B12:381"],"tags":["Giants & Titans","Duffey","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Head (Papa Devil's)","definition":"Papa Devil’s visible head is compared to a great purple pumpkin. Finnegan cannot draw it convincingly because it is the living face of someone else; he reconstructs the wearer’s true face and paints that instead [D:62]. The painting anticipates the face he recognizes on the dead Papa Devil after the Naxos battle [D:86] [D:146]. X’s later account distinguishes this man as Papadiabolous II [D:285].","citations":["D:62","D:86","D:146","D:285"],"tags":["The Devil","Finnegan","Visual Arts","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Head (Putiphar's Wife's)","definition":"Portrait commission from Adrian Shapiro for his wife Mercedes, recast in the narration as Putiphar and his wife. Mercedes’s advances make Finnegan play Joseph: he leaves with his coat but abandons his paints and soon takes to the road again [A:193].","citations":["A:193"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Visual Arts","Old Testament"]},{"name":"Healeys (Family)","definition":"One of the family names in the older relative’s account of Duffey’s many kin during his disputed childhood. Duffey suggests that the relative is remembering his twin instead [MTM B1:7].","citations":["MTM B1:7"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Hearn, Lafcadio (1850--1904)","definition":"Writer invoked in Casey’s hostile review of <em>The Bark</em>: he dismisses its New Orleans sketches as work of a kind Lafcadio Hearn had done better in the previous century [A:131]. Hearn (1850-1904) is also known for his writing about Japan, including <em>Kwaidan: Stories and Studies of Strange Things</em> (1904).","citations":["A:131"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Heaven","definition":"Heaven is described by Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni as continuing, explosive growth rather than a static state; his tapestry analogy places Heaven, Hell, and Purgatory within the same universe, whose true face human beings cannot yet see [A:211] [A:213-214]. When Duffey loses his wealth, the narrator jokes that the camel and needle’s-eye problem has been solved: Duffey had wondered how he would get to Heaven without imagining that he might cease to be rich [MTM B4:109].","citations":["A:211","A:213-214","MTM B4:109"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Theology","Time & Eternity","Duffey"]},{"name":"Heavenly Twins (newspapers)","definition":"Bagby’s nickname for the <em>St. Louis Post Dispatch</em> and the <em>St. Louis Globe</em>, to which he sends flamboyant battle statements publicizing his boxing [MTM B2:50].","citations":["MTM B2:50"],"tags":["Combat","Journalism"]},{"name":"Heavyweight championship","definition":"Beer-drinking title at stake in the proposed Harbor House contest between Hans and Joe Bushmaster; Finnegan calls it the world heavyweight championship [A:17].","citations":["A:17"],"tags":["Alcohol","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Hebdomadal names","definition":"The set of names Gautier gives his seven youngest goat kids: Bownee, Luin, Mairt, Keadin, Ordin, Haoine, and Sahirn. The narrator calls these “hebdomadal names”; Luin is subsequently glossed as Monday [A:194-195].","citations":["A:194-195"],"tags":["Language & Words","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Hebert, Emily","definition":"Earlier heading supplied the surname Hebert, which the family passage does not establish. See <em>Emily (Henry Salvatore's aunt)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Louisiana","Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Hebert, Julien","definition":"One of Henry Francis Salvatore’s nearby Hebert uncles in the Morgan City family account [A:122-123].","citations":["A:122-123"],"tags":["Louisiana","Family","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Hebert, Mary Genevieve","definition":"Mother of Henry Francis Salvatore, whose father is Lawrence Salvatore. Their large family belongs to the Morgan City, Louisiana account of Henry’s upbringing [A:122-123].","citations":["A:122-123"],"tags":["Louisiana","Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Hebert, Nicole","definition":"One of Henry Francis Salvatore’s nearby Hebert uncles in the Morgan City family account [A:122-123].","citations":["A:122-123"],"tags":["Louisiana","Family","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Hebrew","definition":"One of the scripts in which the <em>Argo</em>’s name is painted when it brings the Peter-Bell to the mooring rock, alongside Latin, Greek, and Chaldee [MB:577]. Duffey proposes playing Hebrew melodies on his recorder-flute for the Ursuline play [MTM B9:266]. Book Six also opens with a passage about Melchisedech attributed in the novel to “Paul. Hebrews.” [MTM B6:185].","citations":["MB:577","MTM B9:266","MTM B6:185"],"tags":["Language & Words","Music","Ships","Duffey","Scripture","Theater"]},{"name":"Hebron","definition":"One of the possible locations of the burning bush in Finnegan’s poem: he will climb Sinai and see whether the bush is “at Hebron or Hest,” and whether it is burning [A:171].","citations":["A:171"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature","Old Testament"]},{"name":"Heckel, Charley horse","definition":"Regular at the Old Wooden Ship bar [D:215]. With Russ and Bob, Charley horse Heckel steers promising big spenders toward the bar; the narrator groups these three with Smokehouse and Bill Dugan as five men cut from the same side of the animal [D:220].","citations":["D:215","D:220"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Hedayat, Joseph Cardinal","definition":"Cardinal of Antioch and the primary of Count Finnegan, his double. In the <em>Resurrection of Count Finnegan</em> painting, their older forms are identical even though their contemporary appearances differ; Mr. X knows Joseph, and the widely traveled Joseph later visits the picture at Duffey’s Art Bijou [MB:552-553]. Hilary Hilton calls Hedayat the likeliest next Pope and reports a prediction that Finnegan will assume the crown under Hedayat’s identity, then be known as Peter the Second [MTM B12:378-379]. At the final Conclave, Hackenschmidt declares the prophecies about Hedayat void and commissions Track and Total to kill the thirteen absent princes; Hedayat heads X’s list of the targets [MB:568-571]. He is the last of the thirteen primaries reported killed, impaled upside down in the event called “Last Gate Swings” [MB:589]. This death appears in the <em>Deaths of the Cardinals</em> series attributed to Count Finnegan aboard the <em>Argo</em>; the narrative also raises the possibility of the accomplished amateur painter Hedayat’s participation [MTM B13:452-453].","citations":["MB:552-553","MTM B12:378-379","MB:568-571","MB:589","MTM B13:452-453"],"tags":["Popes","Finnegan","Ekklesia","Visual Arts","Death & Mortality","Resurrection","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Hedges (Thorn)","definition":"Thorn hedges in the Delectable Place. Duffey wants to cut through them; Patrick Plunket explains that they lie outside the direct route and have to be deliberately sought out by going the long way around. Duffey nevertheless favors the indirect route, while Plunket warns that its danger is real [MTM B12:391].","citations":["MTM B12:391"],"tags":["Duffey","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Heine, Heinrich (1797--1856)","definition":"German poet and writer whose <em>Buch der Lieder</em> (1827) brought lasting fame to his lyric poetry. Born in Düsseldorf in 1797, he moved to Paris in 1831 amid censorship of his work and died there in 1856. His writing joins Romantic lyricism to political criticism and satire. In <em>Archipelago</em>, Heine’s ballads strongly shape Hans’s “Chinese period,” mingling Rhineland sadness with Chinese imagery [A:223].","citations":["A:223"],"tags":["Germany","France","Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Hercules, Herman","definition":"Listed as the double of Martino Cardinal Erculo of Milan [MB:579]. In his conversation with Finnegan, he calls Erculo his primary, recalls his career as a wrestler, and describes himself as one of the long-timers [MB:584-585]. The narration later suggests that the man speaking as Hercules is Erculo in disguise, then leaves the masqueraders’ exact identities unresolved [MB:588].","citations":["MB:579","MB:584-585","MB:588"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Identity","Ekklesia","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Hidden Hand in the Various Military Intelligences","definition":"Short title of Private Gregory’s unpublished <em>Cycles of Subversion: The Hidden Hand in the Various Military Intelligences</em>, found among his old papers. Gregory says the work has angered the people it exposes and that they have hunted him down [A:78-79].","citations":["A:78-79"],"tags":["Soldiers","Literature"]},{"name":"Hidden Years (Duffey's)","definition":"The seven dark or lost years following Duffey’s disappearance from St. Louis. Later Duffey either cannot or will not explain them. A codicil of the circular log says he boarded the disguised <em>Argo</em> below Eads Bridge and traveled for seven nonconsecutive years, with land adventures not counted in that span [MTM B2:71]. Another account describes intervals removed from time, containing past, present, and future [MTM B10:298]. He collected the Seven Christs during these years [MTM B3:103], and gave Gabriel Szymansky the talisman intended for Casey before Casey’s conception or birth [MTM B4:120]. Bagby sent Duffey weekly letters even during this mysterious absence [MTM B4:145].","citations":["MTM B2:71","MTM B10:298","MTM B3:103","MTM B4:120","MTM B4:145"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Ships","Duffey","Time & Eternity","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Hidden Years (Hans')","definition":"Gap of four, five, or six years, depending on the recension, after Hans’s Bohemian spring and summer. He acquired unexplained resources that thereafter let him obtain needed money from a distance, but did not tell this part of his life to Finnegan. His Army enlistment followed in the spring of either 1941 or 1942 [A:240-241].","citations":["A:240-241"],"tags":["Money & Trade","History & Memory","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Higgenbotham, Horace","definition":"Marie Monaghan’s supposed Uncle Horace, invoked in Hans’s imagined exchange with her. When Hans disputes attribution of the Lotus Eaters’ perpetual afternoon to Horace, Marie specifies “my uncle Horace Higgenbotham” [A:16].","citations":["A:16"],"tags":["Family","Minor Characters","Literature"]},{"name":"High Master","definition":"Rank held by Count Finnegan as an accredited Astronaut of the <em>Argo</em>; it licenses his effigy’s presence among the ship’s absent Masters [MTM B13:449].","citations":["MTM B13:449"],"tags":["Argonautica","Argonauts (Characters)","Finnegan","Ships"]},{"name":"High Order of the Dirty Five","definition":"Name given to the Dirty Five fellowship of which Henry Salvatore is a member [A:124].","citations":["A:124"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"High Period (Trinali's)","definition":"Trinali Peterson’s name for her newly mature artistic phase. She says her style matured within the preceding eleven days and calls herself in her High Period [A:232]. She later dies from lilac dye, though the account leaves the manner of ingestion uncertain [A:239-240].","citations":["A:232","A:239-240"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"High Rider (of this Horse)","definition":"Title in the signs advertising the Leader’s appearance beside the equestrian statue in Henry Horner Park: the horse’s High Rider will become Leader of the World and perhaps an Angel out of Heaven. Casey arrives in elaborate regalia, but Letitia intercepts him before he can mount the white horse [MTM B4:116-117].","citations":["MTM B4:116-117"],"tags":["Prophecy & Visions","Angels & Demons"]},{"name":"High-school dance","definition":"Junior-year dance to which Maurine Stranahan wants Finnegan to take her. When he doubts he will be admitted, she proposes telling the sister that he is a junior from St. Philomena’s; she also wants permission to tell the other girls they are engaged [A:100-101].","citations":["A:100-101"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Hildebrand, Saint","definition":"Hildebrand, Pope Gregory VII (1073-1085), appears among the seamen aboard the <em>Navicula Petri</em> in Henry’s dream [A:44-45]. A leading reformer of the medieval Church, he opposed simony and enforced clerical celibacy. His resistance to lay investiture brought him into conflict with Henry IV, whom he excommunicated in 1076. Henry sought and received absolution at Canossa in 1077, but the conflict resumed. After Henry’s occupation of Rome and the violent intervention of Gregory’s Norman allies, Gregory withdrew to Salerno, where he died in 1085. His reported last words were “I have loved justice and hated iniquity, therefore I die in exile.” He was later canonized.","citations":["A:44-45"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Saints","Popes","Church History","Dreams"]},{"name":"Hildegarde","definition":"Liquor bottle named by Finnegan and his companions during their Basse-Terre beach storytelling. Their daily bottles receive women’s names: Hildegarde follows Agatha, Bathildes, Clotilda, Delphina, Etheldreda, Faustina, and Georgina [D:243]. She is half gone after Le Marin’s story and one-quarter full after Don Barnaby’s [D:248] [D:252]. When Finnegan’s story ends she is empty, and the four open Idabel for a final gulp before sleeping [D:256].","citations":["D:243","D:248","D:252","D:256"],"tags":["Caribbean","Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Hildegarde Katt","definition":"Wife of Tommy Katt, the tough policeman. At the Old Wooden Ship, she tells Finnegan that her husband is the cop with the little moustache named Tommy, prompting Finnegan and Le Marin to pretend that Tommy is the moustache’s name [D:229-230].","citations":["D:229-230"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Family","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Hill boy","definition":"The kind of island boy distinguished from Hans’s friend Sulem, who is specifically a shore boy rather than a hill boy [A:59].","citations":["A:59"],"tags":["Islands"]},{"name":"Hill-billies","definition":"The narrator’s term for inhabitants of New Jersey’s wild back country, described as more remote than people in Tennessee or Arkansas. Finnegan meets Gautier the goat-man in this landscape [A:193-194].","citations":["A:193-194"],"tags":["Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Hilton, Adrian","definition":"Banker and older brother of Sebastian Hilton, one of the moneyed members of Duffey’s Chicago circle [MTM B3:100] [MTM B4:123]. In the Depression debate, he argues that vested interests deliberately caused the crisis through the March 1933 bank moratorium. He alleges that objecting bankers were murdered and names his father as one; Rollo McSorley claims the father was killed by a jealous husband, and the two men fight until Duffey and others separate them [MTM B4:123-124].","citations":["MTM B3:100","MTM B4:123","MTM B4:123-124"],"tags":["Duffey","Money & Trade","Family","Combat"]},{"name":"Hilton, Hilary","definition":"Wealthy businessman, Sebastian Hilton’s nephew and Mary Catherine Carruthers’s employer; his wife is Mary Jean [MTM B12:377-378]. Having known Casey since childhood, he identifies him as the Antichrist and says he seeks others’ sacrifice rather than money. He believes Casey fears measuring himself against Finnegan or Bishop Salvatore, and reports investigators’ findings that Finnegan is alive and awakening [MTM B12:378]. Hilary arranges a party for Duffey and about a hundred friends to tempt the uninvited Casey to appear, and watches Duffey’s televised projection of Casey’s head [MTM B12:379-381]. He buys the <em>Resurrection of Count Finnegan</em> from Duffey for $20,000 and leaves it in the Walk-In Art Bijou, where he returns to contemplate it [MB:553].","citations":["MTM B12:377-378","MTM B12:378","MTM B12:379-381","MB:553"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","Finnegan","Duffey","Visual Arts","Money & Trade","Family","Resurrection"]},{"name":"Hilton, Sebastian","definition":"Duffey’s wealthy boarding-school friend, younger and slighter than he, pale and greenish in complexion but a fast, ruthless boxer, runner, and schemer. Duffey recognizes his intellectual superiority; Sebastian excels at the school subjects and understands the talismans more fully than Duffey himself [MTM B1:22-25]. He has traveled in France and Italy and claims an affair with a young countess; Duffey’s mental exploration confirms that a twelve-year-old countess exists [MTM B1:31]. Duffey gives him a talisman to pass to someone else, and Sebastian predicts it will work only partly [MTM B1:35]. He recognizes the three slant-faced pursuers and warns that his association with Duffey lets them triangulate on both targets [MTM B1:36]. He later drives ambulances in France and Italy; Duffey may have borrowed those experiences from his mind [MTM B2:72]. Sebastian attends the Colony Club dinner and belongs to the Chicago golden-age circle; Adrian is his older brother, and Hilary his nephew [MTM B3:94] [MTM B3:98-100] [MTM B12:378]. Duffey predicts his early death despite the family’s assurances of good health [MTM B3:102]. On his final New Orleans visit Sebastian anticipates their next meeting in the Kingdom. He marries Countess Margaret a month after returning to Chicago and is killed one month later [MTM B7:216].","citations":["MTM B1:22-25","MTM B1:31","MTM B1:35","MTM B1:36","MTM B2:72","MTM B3:94","MTM B3:98-100","MTM B12:378","MTM B3:102","MTM B7:216"],"tags":["Duffey","Magic & Sorcery","Family","Money & Trade","Combat","Death & Mortality","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Hind","definition":"English venturer among the treasure ships on Angela Cosquin’s father’s master map. Angela describes this map as the source of commissioned treasure maps and forged supporting documents for confidence men [D:261].","citations":["D:261"],"tags":["Ships","Maritime Trade","Crime"]},{"name":"Hindi","definition":"Language of Seaworthy’s dream in which he pushes an unaccountable corpse through an Indian city and cannot get rid of it. The people return it with the words “Ye tumhara hi kasur hai,” rendered in his account as “It’s your own fault” [D:72-73].","citations":["D:72-73"],"tags":["Dreams","Death & Mortality","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Hindi names (gems)","definition":"Names of gems that Amoy knows only in Arabic or Hindi, beyond Ignatius Ti’s ability to translate. In Ti’s expansive account of Amoy’s former life as a red-winged blackbird, the stolen gems are brought to his jewel-thief master [A:80-81].","citations":["A:80-81"],"tags":["Language & Words","Crime","Shapeshifting"]},{"name":"Hindrance (Aral)","definition":"The Malay boy’s gloss for <em>Aral</em>, one of the names of the canoe from which he and the Sergeant dive. The boy also calls it <em>Bumi</em>, “of the world,” and <em>Aragh</em>, “the direction”; the Sergeant consequently recognizes it as the Ship <em>Argo</em> [A:89].","citations":["A:89"],"tags":["Ships","Argonautica","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Hinnegan","definition":"Casey’s farm-boy school roommate, who teaches him the musical saw and joins him in compiling a book of formulas [A:162-163]. Their room is supervised by the perennial senior Buttercup Butler. Casey tells Finnegan that the boy’s real name may have been Hannigan, Hanlon, or O’Hanlon; since knowing Finnegan, he has reconstructed the roommate as Hinnegan to rhyme with him [A:162].","citations":["A:162-163","A:162"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Music","Identity"]},{"name":"Hiram (King of Tyre)","definition":"King of Tyre, conventionally placed in the tenth century BC. The biblical account associates him with Solomon’s building projects and maritime expeditions to Ophir. In Hans’s reverie about the far southern land, the men of Solomon and Hiram have spent three years on the trip to Ophir, yet Hans imagines his own destination farther away still [A:16].","citations":["A:16"],"tags":["Old Testament","Seas & Oceans","Money & Trade","Historical Figures"]},{"name":"Hiram Hunt","definition":"Attributed author of <em>The Death of the Species</em>, the Chapter Nineteen epigraph. It describes humanity as a tense union of two inherited elements, compares their balance to bow and string, and warns that a victory of either element would destroy the whole [D:283-284].","citations":["D:283-284"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Death & Mortality","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Hiram III (King of St. Kobarid)","definition":"Ruler of St. Kobarid in the Julian Alps, called the last king because he refuses the World Consensus General’s order to abolish his nation [MB:563-564]. At the Voltaire Festival in Babylonia Bagascia, fifty senior youths strangle him with the entrails of Paul the Eleventh. The killing takes two and a half or three hours, after which his body is surrendered to dismemberers and souvenir seekers [MB:564-565].","citations":["MB:563-564","MB:564-565"],"tags":["Fictional Places","Apocalyptic","Death & Mortality","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Hiram Walker","definition":"Liquor in Finnegan’s heavily stocked shoulder-bag when he leaves the Texas bars on foot during his search for the old Moonlight Cavalry [A:202].","citations":["A:202"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Hitler, Adolf (1889--1945)","definition":"Austrian-born Nazi leader and dictator of Germany from 1933 to 1945. His regime invaded Poland in 1939, beginning the Second World War in Europe, and carried out the Holocaust and other mass murders. Hitler died by suicide in Berlin in 1945. In Absalom Stein’s <em>Notes on the Finnegan Cycle</em>, Hitler is fantastically described as a raider from the future into Greater Neustria [MTM B6:185]. Tony Apostolo also jokes that even a living Hitler would be an unoriginal news story, claiming seven previous reporter encounters with seven Hitlers [PG:525].","citations":["MTM B6:185","PG:525"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Germany","World War II","History & Memory","Time & Eternity","Journalism"]},{"name":"Hoax","definition":"Duffey wonders whether the Royal Pop People’s demand for hospitality is a “howling hoax” [MTM B11:312]. Casey later claims that his childhood Leader-and-white-horse episode was only a hoax, but the narrator rejects that explanation: Letitia saw that he meant it seriously [MTM B4:118].","citations":["MTM B11:312","MTM B4:118"],"tags":["Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Hobo","definition":"Young man with whom Finnegan says he talked all night beside a fire in the north woods. At dawn the man appeared about forty years older; Finnegan fled, then heard his terrible wailing after he saw his aged reflection in a pool [D:14].","citations":["D:14"],"tags":["Finnegan","Time & Eternity","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Hochfelsen, Margaret (Countess)","definition":"See <em>Countess Margaret, The (Margaret Hochfelsen)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Women Characters","Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Hochheimer, Homer","definition":"Marie Monaghan’s supposed Uncle Homer in her conversation inside Hans’s mind. She says his color-blindness led him to mistake a purple cow for a black one and sell it cheaply to a butcher, who then made a million pounds from it [A:24].","citations":["A:24"],"tags":["Family","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Hochstapler","definition":"Nickname given by the Professor and others to Betty’s father, glossed in the novel as a high-class swindler. A victim called a wool-hat kills him with his own barker’s mallet or gavel over less than three hundred dollars, claiming he meant only to hit him; the killer receives a year and a day [A:217].","citations":["A:217"],"tags":["Crime","Family","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Hochstapler, Betty","definition":"Carnival acrobat and balancer, sixteen when Hans is fourteen, described as an unbreakable blonde doll. She first enjoys humiliating Hans but wants him as her sweetheart and prospective husband. Hans, embarrassed and feeling too young, encourages Angelo Angelino to marry her instead [A:216-217]. She admires her swindler father Hochstapler and is troubled by her inability to discover crookedness in Hans; even before marrying Angelo she has family connections with the Angelinos [A:217].","citations":["A:216-217","A:217"],"tags":["Women Characters","Carnival","Family"]},{"name":"Hock shop","definition":"Duffey’s pawnshop, part of the same establishment as his Walk-In Art Bijou, other businesses, and living quarters in New Orleans [MTM B11:311].","citations":["MTM B11:311"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Hodson's Superior Horse Whip","definition":"One of the whips named by the Royal Pop lecturer in his account of memories preserved by an old Decatur Street flagstone. The list evokes the sounds of horse traffic and its drivers [MTM B11:328-329].","citations":["MTM B11:328-329"],"tags":["New Orleans","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Hoe-down dancing","definition":"One of the everyday lively arts listed among those massively debased after the Devil’s release. The narrator places it beside cotillion dancing in the long inventory of threatened activities [MTM B6:207].","citations":["MTM B6:207"],"tags":["The Devil","Theater"]},{"name":"Hogan's Alley","definition":"Place invoked in the description of the shabby <em>Seabach</em>: the boat looks as though drunken Irish carpenters from Hogan’s alley cobbled it together, and the narrator adds that parts of it actually were [D:135].","citations":["D:135"],"tags":["Ships","Alcohol","Ireland"]},{"name":"Hogarth","definition":"William Hogarth (1697-1764), painter and engraver known for narrative series satirizing contemporary life, including <em>A Harlot’s Progress</em> and <em>A Rake’s Progress</em>. Patrick Stranahan names him with Watteau and Durer as one of the few acceptable sources of pictures in his restrictive rule of culture [A:109].","citations":["A:109"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Historical Figures"]},{"name":"Hokey-pokey pushcart","definition":"Cart from which an Italian man sells small cakes. Duffey gives the man his first talisman when he is no more than three years old; the memory resurfaces when Mr. X comes to New Orleans [MTM B1:18] [MTM B6:200].","citations":["MTM B1:18","MTM B6:200"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Hold him! (command)","definition":"Order shouted by the hammer-carrying young man to his two larger companions. They pinion Melchisedech Duffy while the first man carefully smashes his watch glass [GD:481].","citations":["GD:481"],"tags":["Duffey","Combat"]},{"name":"Holland Gin","definition":"Drink Joseph contrasts with the palm wine he and Finnegan share in Freetown. He says Finnegan would be drinking whisky or Holland gin with his shipmates in the hotel bar if he were not a fellow low-lifer [D:93].","citations":["D:93"],"tags":["Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Holland, Cyril","definition":"One of the people of a scientific bent in Duffey’s Chicago golden-age circle, listed beside Catherine Quick [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Duffey"]},{"name":"Hollow cliffs","definition":"Part of the densely packed Black Papua landscape, threaded with dozens of miles of sponge-like caves and described with extravagant numbers of lizards and birds [A:36-37].","citations":["A:36-37"],"tags":["Islands","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Holly","definition":"Scent to which the lingering odor of the burned Elephant Ear bush is compared. After Letitia’s death, Duffey speaks with the flaming bush and grudgingly accepts its explanation; the fire then subsides, leaving an odor “something like holly” [MTM B8:253].","citations":["MTM B8:253"],"tags":["Duffey","Death & Mortality","Miracles"]},{"name":"Holy Apostle the tippler","definition":"Apostle invoked by Show Boat in her letter to Dotty as <em>Sanctus Apostolus Potator Incognitus</em>. She reports Papa’s supposed old Italian legend of an apostle who finishes leftover wine, yet remains a successful evangelist; Papa cannot remember whether he was Thomas, Bartholomew, or Jude [A:262].","citations":["A:262"],"tags":["Prayer & Devotion","New Testament","Folklore","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Holy Argo","definition":"Sacred designation of the <em>Argo</em>. Count Finnegan predicts that the Holy <em>Argo</em> will carry Antichrist to the Plains of Megiddo while the unbroken line still has a reigning representative. Duffey, Brannagan, and Gorshok then seek means of preventing Antichrist from coming aboard [MTM B13:453-454]. Duffey also calls it the Holy Ship when recalling the high gestes preceding his shore leave and present lifetime [MTM B12:370] [MTM B12:387].","citations":["MTM B13:453-454","MTM B12:370","MTM B12:387"],"tags":["Argonautica","Apocalyptic","Prophecy & Visions","Ships","Duffey","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Holy Ghost","definition":"The Holy Ghost appears in Duffey’s bitter joke to Becky Stein that even the Holy Ghost has bad days, after she attacks the changed funeral Mass [MTM B12:354]. At the Mooring Rock Conclave, the narrator credits the Holy Ghost with revealing that Paul the Eleventh secretly appointed Finnegan a Cardinal [MB:598]. John Giwa also says the Holy Ghost would confirm the election made by even a lone surviving Cardinal [MB:596].","citations":["MTM B12:354","MB:598","MB:596"],"tags":["Theology","Finnegan","Duffey","Ekklesia","Popes"]},{"name":"Holy Grail (Sancgreal)","definition":"The Sancgreal, said to have been stolen from Glastonbury and recovered among the treasures of the <em>Argo</em> voyages [MTM B13:422].","citations":["MTM B13:422"],"tags":["Argonautica","Ships","Folklore"]},{"name":"Holy Missal","definition":"Book carried by Joseph of Maintz in the spherical chamber of his moon-launching device, alongside Scripture and other works, in Ignacio’s story of the Benedictine priest’s lunar voyage [A:271].","citations":["A:271"],"tags":["Liturgy","Literature","Folklore"]},{"name":"Holy Poverty","definition":"Spiritual poverty distinguished from squalor or deprivation. The narrator says it sacrifices good things, including money, rather than treating wealth itself as evil; both Holy Poverty and Holy Wealth are acknowledged [MTM B4:125]. Duffey tells Finnegan and Zabotski that the Devil can defame poverty and substitute degradation for it but cannot give true Holy Poverty. He links giving to willing receivers and to asking in good faith [MTM B6:194]. The Big Pot’s first six customers come from curiosity, and none is described as poor enough to belong to Holy Poverty [MTM B6:195].","citations":["MTM B4:125","MTM B6:194","MTM B6:195"],"tags":["Theology","Duffey","Money & Trade","The Devil"]},{"name":"Holy Slumgullion","definition":"Duffey’s name for the food offered from the Big Pot. The plan is to keep it boiling indefinitely for poor people; its first six diners take soup from curiosity rather than poverty. Duffey worries that the pot might stay full because no one asks for its Holy Slumgullion [MTM B6:194-195].","citations":["MTM B6:194-195"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","Theology"]},{"name":"Holy Water Font","definition":"Small chipped font on the bedroom wall in Finnegan’s intensely detailed memory of the twelve years he lived with Theresa. The account leaves the time and ontological status of that shared life uncertain [A:204].","citations":["A:204"],"tags":["Finnegan","History & Memory","Sacraments"]},{"name":"Holy Wealth","definition":"Wealth acknowledged by the narrator as capable of holiness, alongside Holy Poverty. In the passage defending the joy of money, Holy Poverty is meaningful because it sacrifices good things, and the narrator distinguishes both holy states from their unholy counterparts [MTM B4:125].","citations":["MTM B4:125"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Theology"]},{"name":"Home Town","definition":"Finnegan’s native New Orleans, whose mixed speech still cannot fully account for his peculiar dialect [A:6]. In his later disordered wanderings, he reaches the wheel-shaped city where he was born but cannot find his friends or control his movements through it [A:200].","citations":["A:6","A:200"],"tags":["New Orleans","Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Homer","definition":"Traditional name attached to the ancient Greek <em>Iliad</em> and <em>Odyssey</em>, conventionally dated to the late eighth or early seventh century BC. Homer’s identity and biography are uncertain; blindness belongs to the ancient tradition about him. The poems developed through oral performance, although their transition into written texts remains disputed. The <em>Iliad</em> centers on Achilles’ anger during the Trojan War, and the <em>Odyssey</em> on Odysseus’s return home. In the novel, Teresa claims that even Homer considered the Argonauts unaccountably ancient, insisting that her companions belong to fact older than Greek myth [MTM B7:233-234].","citations":["MTM B7:233-234"],"tags":["Argonautica","Greece","Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Homer Hoose","definition":"Dumaine Street neighbor of Zabotski. Zabotski’s building lies between Duffey’s and Hoose’s; Hoose in turn lies between Zabotski and Bryan Blackstone. Zabotski says Hoose has not looked out his back window for a long time and is unaware of the giant contraption under construction [MTM B9:268-271].","citations":["MTM B9:268-271"],"tags":["New Orleans","Minor Characters","Duffey"]},{"name":"Homicide","definition":"Charge for which Patrick Stranahan says he defended the seaman Doppio di Pinne in St. Louis [A:111]. Finnegan believes Don Barnaby has fled to his island refuge because of homicide [D:249]. In X’s account, Ifreann killed a man at fifteen and escaped the Krakow region after this punishable murder of his sixteenth year [D:280].","citations":["A:111","D:249","D:280"],"tags":["Crime","Finnegan","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Hondo","definition":"Texas town on Finnegan’s wandering route: after Uvalde on Monday, he passes through Sabinal, D’Hannis, and Hondo on Tuesday, then returns to D’Hannis seeking his old outfit, the Moonlight Cavalry [A:202].","citations":["A:202"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Honey","definition":"Seventy pounds brought back by Finnegan and Dotty after raiding four bee trees on a country frolic, then dumped into the Big Pot, appreciably changing the soup’s taste [A:134].","citations":["A:134"],"tags":["Finnegan","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Honey Tree","definition":"Imagined setting in Hans’s hypnosis act with Mabel the bear: he tells her she has robbed a honey tree and is covered with bees, prompting a genuine fear reaction [A:219]. Honey-tree raiding also appears among the everyday arts the narrator says were debased after the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207].","citations":["A:219","MTM B6:207"],"tags":["Carnival","Theater","The Devil"]},{"name":"Honeybucket","definition":"See <em>Kincaid, Harry A. (Honeybucket)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Honolulu","definition":"City associated with Niku Kazuko, double of Cardinal Runosake of Kobe. The list first calls him probably of Honolulu; the later account calls him of Honolulu without qualification [MB:579] [MB:592].","citations":["MB:579","MB:592"],"tags":["Islands"]},{"name":"Hoodoo Joy Pills (Haiti)","definition":"A variety of joy pill named by Don Barnaby, alongside High Harry, Deep Romany, Dead Frenchman, and Black Rooster pills. He distinguishes these from Madame Le Mourcheor's Old Original Juju Joy Pills. [D:248-249]","citations":["D:248-249"],"tags":["Caribbean","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Hooks","definition":"A boat hook is used against Duffey by one of the three slant-faced stevedores in his dream or trance. He later recognizes the remembered violence of the cruel hooked thing. [MTM B3:85] [MTM B3:98] Hooks and leather straps in the horse barn also supply the premise of Bagby's tall tale about suspending sleepers from the rafters. [MTM B2:44-45]","citations":["MTM B3:85","MTM B3:98","MTM B2:44-45"],"tags":["Duffey","Dreams","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Hooligan, Clement","definition":"Earlier heading attached Clement to the preceding person’s surname in a list. See <em>Temple, Clement</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Minor Characters","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Hooligan, Mary Carmel","definition":"One of the money-and-commerce people, or rich people, of the Chicago golden age. [MTM B3:100] She later attends the crowded farewell party for Duffey at Randal's. [MTM B12:383]","citations":["MTM B3:100","MTM B12:383"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Hoosier Hannah","definition":"Unresolved name previously associated with Casey and Ozark witches; a supporting passage has not been located.","citations":[],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Hope Ship","definition":"A name for the <em>Argo</em> in its role as a source of hope for unfortunates: its coming is one of their promises. [MTM B13:405]","citations":["MTM B13:405"],"tags":["Ships"]},{"name":"Horace (Quintus Horatius Flaccus) (65--8 BC)","definition":"Roman lyric poet, satirist, and critic, born in 65 BC and dead in 8 BC. His works include the Odes, Epodes, and Epistles; his adaptations of Greek poetic forms and treatments of moderation, life's brevity, and carpe diem belong to Augustan Latin literature. In <em>Argo</em>, Endymeon Ellenbogen boasts of translating Horace. [A:237] Marie's apparent quotation of the poet instead refers to her uncle Horace Higgenbotham. [A:16]","citations":["A:237","A:16"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Literary Figures","Literature","Italy"]},{"name":"Horace Pie","definition":"A gnarled, cheerful Scottish seaman whom Duffey meets at sea. He previously sailed with Duffey on a long voyage, met him again on several shores, and knew Finnegan. [MTM B12:370] He joins Duffey and X in Cuba to search for the sea-level tomb of the Sleeping Man; after several false leads they find Finnegan's empty tomb. [MTM B12:372-374]","citations":["MTM B12:370","MTM B12:372-374"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Duffey","Finnegan","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Horn (Post-boy's)","definition":"Horn sounded on Sebastian Hilton's hired carriage during the first compulsory dance outing. Charley Murray plays rousing fox-hunting tunes on the outward journey; Duffey blows it vigorously on the return ride. [MTM B1:32] [MTM B1:34]","citations":["MTM B1:32","MTM B1:34"],"tags":["Duffey","Music"]},{"name":"Horn bands","definition":"One of the popular lively arts whose genuine forms are being displaced by deformed counterparts after the Devil's release. [MTM B6:207]","citations":["MTM B6:207"],"tags":["The Devil","Music"]},{"name":"Hornblower","definition":"Unresolved entry: the claimed reference to Finnegan as a hornblower has not been located.","citations":[],"tags":[]},{"name":"Horned toad","definition":"Unresolved entry: the claimed comparison of Anastasia to a horned toad has not been located.","citations":[],"tags":[]},{"name":"Horned turtles","definition":"Unresolved entry: no supporting passage has been located for horned turtles inhabiting Purgatory's banks.","citations":[],"tags":[]},{"name":"Horner (Henry) Park","definition":"Chicago park with a large equestrian statue, announced in the Answer newsletter as the place where the Leader, Casey Szymansky, will appear. [MTM B4:116] Letitia intercepts Casey on his way to the appearance. [MTM B4:117]","citations":["MTM B4:116","MTM B4:117"],"tags":["Journalism"]},{"name":"Horse Carriages","definition":"Vehicles offered for hire on the Rounders' Club sign, alongside automobiles. [MTM B2:49]","citations":["MTM B2:49"],"tags":["Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Horse collars","definition":"Pieces of harness among the old junk in the horse barn that becomes Duffey's establishment. [MTM B2:41]","citations":["MTM B2:41"],"tags":["Duffey","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Horse Opera","definition":"A spectacular entertainment in which the Hoyden, the Countess, and their companions perform. They connect its display with the destruction of the remaining old human stronghold. [MTM B11:333] The Countess and Child Hero take part in a bullfight sequence, presented as a Cretan Bull Drama, in which human participants are mutilated and killed. [MTM B11:348-349]","citations":["MTM B11:333","MTM B11:348-349"],"tags":["Theater","Combat","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Horse shoes","definition":"Old horseshoes are among the horse barn's junk, and pitching horseshoes becomes an advertised entertainment at Duffey's establishment and the Rounders' Club. [MTM B2:41] [MTM B2:43] [MTM B2:49] Duffey drives steel stakes between paving slabs, and the men bring dirt and manure from the stable yard to make pitching pits; customers establish two additional courses on the first night. [MTM B2:46-47]","citations":["MTM B2:41","MTM B2:43","MTM B2:49","MTM B2:46-47"],"tags":["Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Horseflesh","definition":"Meat from a freshly dead old horse, supplied by X for the Big Pot and used for more than a week without telling its American customers. [A:134]","citations":["A:134"],"tags":["Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Horses","definition":"The Rounders' Club keeps fine horses, red-and-gold harness, and carriages in a divided-off part of its central room. [MTM B2:49] Duffey's premises had been a horse barn; the narrative attributes the decline of downtown St. Louis horses to streetcars and automobiles. [MTM B2:40] Pack horses had been rented by an earlier proprietor. [MTM B2:45] Cleveland Bays draw Sebastian Hilton's hired carriage. [MTM B1:32] At the announced appearance of the Leader in Henry Horner Park, a white former fire horse, dressed in gold lamé and cherished by its neighborhood, waits beside the equestrian statue. [MTM B4:116]","citations":["MTM B2:49","MTM B2:40","MTM B2:45","MTM B1:32","MTM B4:116"],"tags":["Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Hosiery","definition":"Unresolved entry: the claimed episode involving Finnegan as a silk-hosiery drummer has not been located.","citations":[],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Hot Stuff","definition":"The familiar, friendly name street boys call Margaret Stone. She notes the irony that most do not know her body temperature is 111 degrees, although a few medical students have heard of her case. [MTM B5:177-178]","citations":["MTM B5:177-178"],"tags":["Women Characters"]},{"name":"Hotel du Champ de Mars","definition":"The probable hotel in a Colmar, Alsace, childhood scene that Duffey has taken from Sebastian Hilton's memory. The scene gives its address as 2, Avenue de la Marna and places a meal of soupe aux grenouilles at the Rotisserie Schillinger shortly before the boy's thirteenth birthday. [MTM B1:35]","citations":["MTM B1:35"],"tags":["Duffey","France","History & Memory"]},{"name":"House and Home Happenings Magazine","definition":"Magazine presenting Duffey's account of the origin of the Zabotski House on Lake Borgne, its House of the Uncertain Interval. The introduction proposes a two-part feature, hoping to describe the house itself in the next issue. [MTM B9:267] An editor's note acknowledges how strange and rambling this history is for a mainly pop-architectural publication; there the title appears as <em>House and Home Happening Magazine</em>. [MTM B9:273]","citations":["MTM B9:267","MTM B9:273"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism"]},{"name":"House of Gold","definition":"A title of the Blessed Virgin Mary from the Litany, invoked together with Tower of Ivory as an image of real, holy, and joyous wealth. [MTM B4:125]","citations":["MTM B4:125"],"tags":["Prayer & Devotion","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"House of Israel","definition":"The group to whose children Duffey jokingly claims his puppet shows are restricted when a public-school teacher invites him. She answers that 63 percent of her pupils are of the House of Israel and that he, a flaming Irishman, has the distinction backwards. [MTM B4:135]","citations":["MTM B4:135"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words"]},{"name":"House of Ten Thousand Duty-Free Bargains","definition":"An advertised description of Duffey's Rounders' Club. He adds imported goods and art to its ten thousand bargains as the establishment prospers. [MTM B2:49]","citations":["MTM B2:49"],"tags":["Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Clubs & Societies","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Houseboat","definition":"Zabotski's large floating boat-castle on Lake Borgne, described as an ark filled with children, animals, homemade music, and conversation. [MTM B12:362-364]","citations":["MTM B12:362-364"],"tags":["Ships","Music","Louisiana"]},{"name":"Howard Stone Ostwald","definition":"See <em>Ostwald, Howard Stone</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia"]},{"name":"Howell","definition":"A talkative soldier in Ward Fourteen, considered moderately sane by the Sergeant. He claims to have catalogued all one thousand subjects of conversation. [A:75] [A:77] An unexplained twitch in his left hand leads the doctors to classify his trouble as mental; his humor worsens their judgment. Following the Captain's advice to deny the hand nothing, Howell pulls three hairs from the Captain's moustache. [A:82-83] In the ward's debate over ghost stories, he defines them as horror stories and discusses the terror of a corpse returning to life. [A:84-85]","citations":["A:75","A:77","A:82-83","A:84-85"],"tags":["Soldiers","Ghosts & Spirits","Literature"]},{"name":"Howland, Carter Miller","definition":"One of Howland Jerome Howland's seven brothers, sons of John Commodore Howland. Howland says their father chose names from old families he hated, perhaps to irritate them. [A:178]","citations":["A:178"],"tags":["Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Howland, Cooper Stone","definition":"One of Howland Jerome Howland's seven brothers, sons of John Commodore Howland. Howland says their father chose names from old families he hated, perhaps to irritate them. [A:178]","citations":["A:178"],"tags":["Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Howland, Francine","definition":"A pretty, dark girl whom Finnegan dates after meeting her in a Chicago bar. He introduces Howland to her, and she plans to obtain a kitchen job for him at her hotel and, if she can settle him, to marry him. [A:181-182] She becomes Howland's wife and remains close to Finnegan; the Howlands always keep a room for him, and she candidly criticizes their expensive way of life. [A:246-247]","citations":["A:181-182","A:246-247"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Howland, Gilbert Finnegan","definition":"The son of Howland Jerome and Francine Howland, described as carrying on the family's tradition of triple names. [A:246-247]","citations":["A:246-247"],"tags":["Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Howland, Harper Scott","definition":"Harper Scott Howland, one of Howland Jerome Howland's brothers. Their father gave the boys names from old families whom he disliked, apparently to irritate those families [A:178].","citations":["A:178"],"tags":["Family","Minor Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Howland, Howland Jerome","definition":"Son of John Commodore Howland and traveling companion of Finnegan on the journey north to Chicago. He catches rabbits for their meals; Finnegan helps him improve his reading and teaches him to print. [A:178-180] At eighteen he admires Finnegan intensely, meets Francine through him, and plays the comic part of Finnegan's valet during a visit to Casey. Francine and Howland become lasting friends and hosts to Finnegan. [A:181-182] Later Howland runs Chicago's luxurious Club Royal, is married to Francine, and has a son, Gilbert Finnegan Howland. [A:246-247]","citations":["A:178-180","A:181-182","A:246-247"],"tags":["Finnegan","Family","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Howland, John Commodore","definition":"Father of Howland Jerome Howland and his seven named brothers. According to Howland, his father chose their names from old families he hated; Howland guesses that the purpose was to irritate those families. [A:178]","citations":["A:178"],"tags":["Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Howland, Lansdown Filmore","definition":"One of Howland Jerome Howland's seven brothers, sons of John Commodore Howland. Howland says their father chose names from old families he hated, perhaps to irritate them. [A:178]","citations":["A:178"],"tags":["Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Howland, Mansville Burgess","definition":"One of Howland Jerome Howland's seven brothers, sons of John Commodore Howland. Howland says their father chose names from old families he hated, perhaps to irritate them. [A:178]","citations":["A:178"],"tags":["Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Howland, Orville Cannon","definition":"One of Howland Jerome Howland's seven brothers, sons of John Commodore Howland. Howland says their father chose names from old families he hated, perhaps to irritate them. [A:178]","citations":["A:178"],"tags":["Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Howland, Otis Hunter","definition":"One of Howland Jerome Howland's seven brothers, sons of John Commodore Howland. Howland says their father chose names from old families he hated, perhaps to irritate them. [A:178]","citations":["A:178"],"tags":["Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Howlands, The","definition":"Howland Jerome and Francine Howland and their family, with whom Finnegan often stays in Chicago. [A:182] [A:246-247] In <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> he includes the Howlands among those who are friendly to him in his normal life, while lamenting his own inability to make human contact. [D:90]","citations":["A:182","A:246-247","D:90"],"tags":["Finnegan","Family"]},{"name":"Howship's lacunar frequency","definition":"One of the technical measures invoked in the fictional Eighteenth Book of Strange Encounters as evidence that Duffey is more than four thousand years old. [MTM B8:250]","citations":["MTM B8:250"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"Hoyden, The (archetype)","definition":"A young Royal Pop Person whose individual identity the narrative distinguishes from a mere type. [MTM B11:322] In Bayougoula Park she challenges Mary Virginia's doubts about the new people's reality and insists that mountains and thunder test a place's authenticity. [MTM B11:331-334] She joins the dinner at Girardeau's and performs in the Horse Opera's Thunder Colt Game, helping feed a living human into a colt's egg. [MTM B11:345] [MTM B11:348-350] In the subsequent play frame, Cleo Mahoney plays the Hoyden in the Fifth Road episode. [MTM B12:352]","citations":["MTM B11:322","MTM B11:331-334","MTM B11:345","MTM B11:348-350","MTM B12:352"],"tags":["Women Characters","Theater","New Orleans","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Hsien","definition":"One of the rulers with whom Duffey claims to have sat in kings' conclaves, alongside Solomon, Saul, Ptolemy, Chandragupta, Nebuchadnezzar, Barbarossa, and others. [MTM B5:166]","citations":["MTM B5:166"],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Hubbard (Gurdon S.) Elementary School","definition":"A brand-new public school on Chicago's north side where a lively teacher persuades the Duffeys to perform. Its cleanliness frustrates Duffey's mouse act because there are no mice in the building to summon. [MTM B4:135]","citations":["MTM B4:135"],"tags":["Duffey","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Hubbard Street","definition":"Street whose tea rooms commonly had Casey Machines, according to Newton Prescott's account. [CM:503]","citations":["CM:503"],"tags":[]},{"name":"Hubbard's","definition":"A name on axe heads in Duffey's remembered catalogue of household iron words, many of them appropriated from other minds during his childhood. [MTM B1:17]","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Hugh de Turenne","definition":"A humanities scholar associated with Xavier in New Orleans who attends the Royal Pop historical sessions in Duffey's establishment. [MTM B11:322] As a speaker he declares that the Black Sea has been obliterated at Himself's wish, along with its memories, and that Athens, Jerusalem, and Rome never existed. He links the destruction of Colchis to an attack on the <em>Argo</em> and the Fleece. [MTM B11:336]","citations":["MTM B11:322","MTM B11:336"],"tags":["New Orleans","History & Memory","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Hugh Stranahan","definition":"One of Patrick Stranahan's four sons, brother of Philip, Timothy, and Vincent. [A:102] His bridge-playing friends visit the family's Cat Castle home, and he offers Finnegan work as a salesman. [A:104] [A:100]","citations":["A:102","A:104","A:100"],"tags":["Family"]},{"name":"Hugh Thatcher (the Yellow Kid)","definition":"Artist called the Yellow Kid, among the world-makers whose work Duffey sells and whose ideas contribute to his project of rebuilding the world. [MTM B6:198-199]","citations":["MTM B6:198-199"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts","Creation"]},{"name":"Hughes (Family)","definition":"One of the family names in an older relative's account of Duffey's many kindred during his childhood. The account places him with cousins between the ages of five and twelve, in Iowa and several cities, but Duffey disputes the claimed relations. [MTM B1:7]","citations":["MTM B1:7"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Hugo (Victor)","definition":"French writer Victor Hugo (1802-1885), named in the Postscript among celebrated people used to illustrate Havelock Ellis's belief in the equal potential of apparently ordinary people. [PS:476]","citations":["PS:476"],"tags":["France","Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Hugo Stone","definition":"The camera-carrying boy who frequented Duffey's Chicago bookstore from about eight or nine years old. Duffey identifies him with Absalom Stein, but Margaret Stone says Hugo was a joke identity shared by Stone cousins, especially the twins Myron and Efram; Casey denies knowing Stein in Chicago, although he fought Hugo twice and lost. [MTM B4:111-113] Duffey attributes compromising photographs of his conduct with Mary Frances Rattigan, Mary Catherine Carruthers, Mary Jean, and Ethyl Ellenberger to Hugo; the blackmailer calls Hugo a clever relative who saved material for future use. [MTM B4:139] Stein later says he traded Casey the old soul of Hugo Stone the Chicago red, and X calls Stein the infamous Chicago communist. [MTM B5:169] [MTM B6:202]","citations":["MTM B4:111-113","MTM B4:139","MTM B5:169","MTM B6:202"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Identity","Crime"]},{"name":"Hugos","definition":"One of the names attached to self-teaching language manuals on Duffey's bookstore shelves, among the resources available to Casey in his attempt to learn every language. [A:159]","citations":["A:159"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Hull","definition":"Duffey recounts finding the lost <em>Argo</em> again through a New Orleans hull dealer: the old oak hull floats lightly, though it does not resemble the <em>Argo</em> at first sight. He explains that it can recover its fittings by remembering them. The account is explicitly said to be symbolically accurate rather than accurate by ordinary standards. [MTM B13:412]","citations":["MTM B13:412"],"tags":["Ships","New Orleans","Duffey"]},{"name":"Hulme, Dotty","definition":"One of the Dottys associated with the Old Wooden Ship. She and Dotty Danvers have each been married to Buffalo Chips Dugan. She also previously married one of the two Sugarland, Texas, brothers to whom Dotty Danvers and Little Dotty Nesbitt were married. [D:220-221]","citations":["D:220-221"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Family"]},{"name":"Human","definition":"A contested category throughout the cycle. Finnegan doubts his own human identity and hears of another human race; the fictional review of Royce Rollins's book identifies that other race as Neanderthals, while reporting a later qualification that they might be another early species. [D:90-91] [D:240-242] Finnegan seeks reassurance in humanity among the Company of Fifty. [D:225] Professor Kirol describes humanity as contingent but endowed with hope of becoming fully real. [A:211-212] Hilary Hilton disputes Casey's advertised sacrifices for humanity, and the Royal Pop People treat old humans as weeds to be destroyed. [MTM B12:378] [MTM B11:331-334]","citations":["D:90-91","D:240-242","D:225","A:211-212","MTM B12:378","MTM B11:331-334"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Metaphysics","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Humility","definition":"A lesson that a piece of driftwood has learned from looking an oyster in the eye, in Trinali's fanciful account of the wood she selects to turn into a lamp. [A:232-233]","citations":["A:232-233"],"tags":["Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Humphrey Speckle","definition":"Artist among the people whose work Duffey sells and whose creative ideas help his project of rebuilding the world. [MTM B6:198-199]","citations":["MTM B6:198-199"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts","Creation"]},{"name":"Hungary","definition":"The source of a tavern-sign Christ in Duffey's collection, described as coming from the time of Béla Kun and Hungary's brief first communist period. The novel contrasts hostility to Christ imagery with the hearty image of Christ drinking beer from a mug decorated with spinning worlds. [MTM B3:103]","citations":["MTM B3:103"],"tags":["Eastern Europe","Visual Arts","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Hunters","definition":"Bounty hunters pursuing the thirteen shadow-men after Track and Total puts prices on their heads. [MB:589] They surround the approaches to Mooring-Stone but are frustrated and killed by the rapidly changing shoals while the fugitives reach the refuge. [MB:595]","citations":["MB:589","MB:595"],"tags":["Crime","Ekklesia"]},{"name":"Huracan","definition":"The hurricane bird of San Simeon: a giant storm bird said to carry away pieces of that country in its twisting flight, forming at least thirteen Antillean islands. [MB:557-558] See Hurricane birds (huracan).","citations":["MB:557-558"],"tags":["Folklore","Monsters & Creatures","Islands"]},{"name":"Hurricane birds (huracan)","definition":"Giant storm birds nesting in the limestone crags of San Simeon and spreading destruction over the Ocean of Mexico. Some accounts make them one bird that flies again and again; others make them half a dozen newly hatched birds each year. Their twisting flight carries away pieces of San Simeon and drops them into water or onto land; at least thirteen Antillean islands are said to have formed this way. [MB:557-558]","citations":["MB:557-558"],"tags":["Folklore","Monsters & Creatures","Islands"]},{"name":"Husband","definition":"Among the husbands named in Duffey's circle are Bascom Bagby, husband of his sister Mary Louise, and Erlenbaum, husband of Beth. [MTM B5:155-156] Gloria Mullens describes a recently dead husband involved in liquor shipments; Charlotte says she had a husband associated with Aga Gonof, without making Gonof himself that husband. [MTM B3:79] [MTM B3:84]","citations":["MTM B5:155-156","MTM B3:79","MTM B3:84"],"tags":["Family"]},{"name":"Hussar sabre","definition":"One of the weapons Casey wears in his Leader regalia, together with his father's Knights of Columbus Fourth Degree sword. [MTM B4:117]","citations":["MTM B4:117"],"tags":["Weapons"]},{"name":"Huxley the Bulldog","definition":"The slogan Huxley the Bulldog forever appears on the badge of a Vigilante Orthodoxer who threatens to shoot Zabotski while defending scientific orthodoxy. [MTM B9:286]","citations":["MTM B9:286"],"tags":["Combat"]},{"name":"Hydra","definition":"An image for the many-armed adversary. In a letter to Dotty, Show Boat identifies the Serpent of the Garden with an eight-armed hydra after reporting Vincent's warning about Communism and secular liberalism as limbs of one octopus. [A:263] Casey separately pictures Hans fighting his internal dragon and passing hydra-heads each morning. [A:155]","citations":["A:263","A:155"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Hydrogen","definition":"The lifting gas for Duffey's custom silk-bag balloon, renamed the <em>Argo Twelve and Half</em>. Venting it makes the balloon descend; the narrative explains the danger of flames when the gas is released around the gondola. [MTM B2:63-65] After the airborne dinner, Duffey extinguishes the candles, knocks Charley Murray's cigar overboard, and opens the gate valve to descend. [MTM B2:66]","citations":["MTM B2:63-65","MTM B2:66"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Hygrometer","definition":"An instrument whose falling reading Mary Virginia mentions, together with the barometer, when she senses another wind from the north. The exchange immediately precedes Charlotte Garfield's return from Chicago. [MTM B8:258]","citations":["MTM B8:258"],"tags":["Women Characters"]},{"name":"Hymn books","definition":"Among the books the goats are permitted to eat as they weed the looted library at Harlow House, along with devotional works and novels of bad vintage. [A:198]","citations":["A:198"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Hypnotism","definition":"The Leader's magnetic hold on the public is described as hypnotic. [MTM B4:115] Casey's later ability to superimpose his idealized face and eerie voice on television programs is attributed to combined electronic genius and psychic power, though that passage does not call the technique hypnotism. [MTM B12:380]","citations":["MTM B4:115","MTM B12:380"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Hypocrisy","definition":"Januarius O'Higgins accuses Casey of hypocrisy when Casey disclaims the Casey Machine, predicting that he will buy one through an intermediary as soon as it is available. The exchange appears in Josephine McSorely's <em>Underground Eagle</em> report. [CM:507]","citations":["CM:507"],"tags":["Journalism"]},{"name":"Iason","definition":"Jason, leader of the Argonauts in Greek myth, and the <em>Argo</em> identity assigned to Finnegan, John A. Solli. [A:39] [A:171] Teresa says Finnegan is Iason himself and gives Jason as the later Roman form. [MTM B7:234] Henry Salvatore is explicitly assigned Euphemus rather than Iason. [A:149] See Jason.","citations":["A:39","A:171","MTM B7:234","A:149"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Argonautica","Finnegan","Argonauts (Characters)","Identity"]},{"name":"Iatrikê (Technê)","definition":"The Art of Medicine by Galen (AD 129-c. 216), cited as <em>Techne Iatrike</em> beneath the epigraph to Chapter Eleven of <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>. The epigraph describes Tityroi or Satyrs as offspring of daemons and earthly women, says their line requires periodic renewal, and claims that one daemon begot children on thirteen women at Calchedon in a single night. [D:160-161] The attribution of this particular passage to the historical work remains unverified.","citations":["D:160-161"],"tags":["Literature","Monsters & Creatures","Angels & Demons"]},{"name":"Ibsen, Henrik (1828--1906)","definition":"Norwegian playwright (1828-1906) central to modern drama, known for social criticism, realistic dramatic form, and psychological conflict. His plays include A Doll's House (1879), Ghosts (1881), and Hedda Gabler (1890). In Casey's notebook, reading Ibsen directly proves more illuminating than Shaw's Quintessence of Ibsenism. [A:166] The Postscript names him in its account of Havelock Ellis's belief in ordinary people's equal creative potential. [PS:476]","citations":["A:166","PS:476"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Theater"]},{"name":"Icelandic","definition":"Language Endymeon Ellenbogen intends to learn before translating the <em>Elder Edda</em>; Hans answers that he already knows it [A:236-237].","citations":["A:236-237"],"tags":["Language & Words","Literature"]},{"name":"Ichor","definition":"In Greek mythology, the fluid that takes the place of blood in the gods. Anastasia is described as \"flesh and ichor\" and as \"transparent, translucent, transcendent,\" giving her an otherworldly aspect [D:13].","citations":["D:13"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Idabel","definition":"Bottle named by the Four during their sojourn in Basse-Terre. It follows Hildegarde: the four companions finish Idabel in a single gulp apiece and fall asleep. Earlier bottles include Agatha, Bathildes, Clotilda, Delphina, Etheldreda, Faustina, and Georgina [D:243] [D:256].","citations":["D:243","D:256"],"tags":["Caribbean","Alcohol","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Ideas","definition":"August Koch offers inherited European ideas as a dowry, and Duffey proposes reducing and locking them away [MTM B3:101]. Duffey goes to New Orleans to put Henry’s ideas into effect [A:117] and begins his schoolteaching with his own \"good ideas\" [MTM B1:11]. Casey’s ideas about his machine are treated in its account of joint invention and in Hilary Hilton’s hostile assessment of Casey [CM:507] [PG:531]. The proposed \"idea magazine\" is the <em>Crock</em> [MTM B4:138-140]. Pop History connects ideas about history with mass hypnotism and group amnesia [MTM B11:307]. Anastasia jokes that Finnegan can teach her Plato and develop her ideas [D:124]; Seaworthy says that a very evil man \"gets ideas\" [D:81].","citations":["MTM B3:101","A:117","MTM B1:11","CM:507","PG:531","MTM B4:138-140","MTM B11:307","D:124","D:81"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Literature","History & Memory","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Ifni","definition":"Former Spanish enclave on Morocco’s Atlantic coast, occupied by Spain in 1934 and returned to Morocco in 1969. The <em>Brunhilde</em> party visits Ifni after Arrecife on Lanzarote and before Agadir [D:101].","citations":["D:101"],"tags":["Ships","Spain","Africa"]},{"name":"Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch","definition":"The elder Papadiabolous in Mr. X’s account, whose grave bears the initials I.N.C.G.P. and the euphemistic surname Bogovitch. X says he was born near Krakow in 1830 to Katie Noonan, an Irish governess, and to the Devil, allegedly named as father in St. Elmo parish records; Gregory adopted him, accounting for Gregorovitch; X considers but rejects Gregory’s possible biological paternity [D:277-279]. X calls Ifreann Irish for \"Devil,\" although its ordinary meaning is \"hell.\" His story describes a large, brutal child with exceptional mentality but no intelligence, who killed cats, tried to kill children, and fled after killing a man at fifteen. X places his later travels in Austria, Russia, Greece, Smyrna, London, Ireland, Milano, Sicily, and America, and makes him a forty-year-old participant in the Paris Commune and disciple of Kropotkin [D:280-281]. At seventy he allegedly fathered two simultaneous sons by different mothers: Gregory McIfreann, Papadiabolous I, in Smyrna, and Gregory McColum, Papadiabolous II, in Cork, on opposite sides of the century’s boundary [D:285-286]. He died at a hundred in 1930. His son Gregory McIfreann was later buried above him, in the grave Finnegan helped Seaworthy deepen [D:288]. His full family name in X’s account is Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch Papadiabolous Chortovitch [D:278]. X also says he called himself a citizen of Krakow [D:280].\n\nIn <em>The Flame Is Green</em>, Ifreann Chortovitch is Dana Coscuin’s great antagonist and Catherine Dembinska’s feared acquaintance from childhood. Catherine repeats the 1830 birth and demonic paternity, reporting that he looked twelve at birth and murdered children; another witness says he looked twelve at two. Reports of his death are accompanied by a were-cat explanation of repeated lives (<em>The Flame Is Green</em>, PDF pp. 189, 210-211, 248). Huge, purple-complexioned, imposing and potentially handsome, he deals in bodies rather than love, threatens Catherine and says he can trace her. His heartiness and vitality exert a disturbing attraction. In one of his Paris houses, an orgy projects the men’s private hells; Charley Oceaan breaks the illusions, and the four companions drink Ifreann into stupor. Catherine and Charley remove documents afterward (<em>The Flame Is Green</em>, PDF pp. 187-190, 203-209). Following Catherine’s murder and the discovery of Ifreann’s signed message, Dana tracks his animal stench and staggering stride, then runs him through with a rapier and takes him for dead (<em>The Flame Is Green</em>, PDF pp. 254-257).\n\nIn <em>Half a Sky</em>, attacks on Dana’s companions follow their Amsterdam meeting, casting doubt on Ifreann’s death. He reappears to claim the Porte d’Enfer estate and Dana’s rock house, while witnesses debate his bodily and ghostly manifestations. Dana suspects that his four flunkies are zombie-like projections; O’Boyle is one of these agents. Ifreann later explains that he lay wounded for three days and delirious in a Krakow pest-house for six months, projecting that delirium onto Dana. He boasts of having fulfilled his earlier promises of violence and means to kill Dana while he is out of grace (<em>Half a Sky</em>, PDF pp. 22-23, 38-40, 53-58, 62-64, 84-86). He shoots Dana’s thumb during the cliff ambush with O’Boyle (<em>Half a Sky</em>, PDF pp. 90-93).\n\nHidden aboard the <em>Catherine</em>, he corrupts food and thoughts, induces sleep, starts fires and levitates objects; one man is killed. Lured by putrid pork and drugged rum, he becomes a monstrous glutton. Dana stabs him, the company throws him overboard, and he changes through marine forms before swimming away as a man. He returns weakened in Santiago, then recovers enough to assault Dana with visions of a destroyed world (<em>Half a Sky</em>, PDF pp. 111-112, 120-122, 127-132). Kemper’s broader theory of the Devil Unchained asks whether Ifreann is their own projection; Dana rejects this (<em>Half a Sky</em>, PDF pp. 134-136).\n\nIfreann commands the ironclad <em>Brami Piekielne</em>, glossed as Gates of Hell. Dana damages it off the Horn and escapes; Dana uses Kemper’s dead-man sight, while Ifreann has only his own night-eyes. The Lord of the Flies is Ifreann’s uncle (<em>Half a Sky</em>, PDF pp. 143-144, 146, 150-153, 160). Ifreann torments Dana with the claim that the child’s bones in his chest are Catherine’s son by Ifreann’s violation of her after death; Dana’s inspection finds no confirming demonic bone (<em>Half a Sky</em>, PDF pp. 191-193). In the final collision of their ships, the <em>Catherine</em> and <em>Gates of Hell</em> explode, and Dana and Jack Galopade pursue Ifreann into the sea. His final death is not narrated (<em>Half a Sky</em>, PDF pp. 197, 202-204).","citations":["D:277-279","D:280-281","D:285-286","D:288","D:278","D:280"],"tags":["The Devil","Ireland","Eastern Europe","Devils (Characters)","Family","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Ignace Wolff","definition":"Name to which <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> attributes an epigraph from <em>Gedanken von Leibern</em>. It says that bodies thwart non-contradiction, negate the principle of sufficient reason, and have no sufficient reason [D:67].","citations":["D:67"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Metaphysics","Paradox"]},{"name":"Ignacio","definition":"Lawyer who helps Finnegan in the Spanish-speaking town where Finnegan is unsure of his surroundings. He says the authorities know of Finnegan’s activities and remain friendly, recounts Joseph of Maintz’s moon-rocket story, and discusses the possibility of going to St. Kitts [A:269-272]. Elena calls him after Finnegan’s fight, and he has already contacted Finnegan’s friends [A:273]. Letters from Ignacio and Elena tell Dotty Yekouris that Finnegan is \"coming apart\" [MTM B8:237].","citations":["A:269-272","A:273","MTM B8:237"],"tags":["Finnegan","Minor Characters","Occupations"]},{"name":"Ignatius Cardinal Ti","definition":"Cardinal of Manila and one of the thirteen Princes of the Ekklesia in Flight targeted by John Mogul. The list of doubles pairs him with Bolo Manolo [MB:579]. He is among the eleven surviving princes who enter Mogul’s contest [MB:580]; his death is reported before the verse commanding that the next victim be flayed [MB:583]. His epitaph is \"My chalice indeed you shall drink\" [MB:589]. A later passage instead calls Lloyd Cardigan-Pembroke, the pig butcher of Tywyn, the double of the dead Cardinal Ti [MB:594].","citations":["MB:579","MB:580","MB:583","MB:589","MB:594"],"tags":["Ekklesia","Crime","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Illo tempore---","definition":"Part of Mr. X’s opening \"In illo tempore\" for the story of the Devil’s grave. The phrase functions as a Latin once-upon-a-time formula: X also begins in French and then resumes \"Once upon a time\" in English [D:284-285].","citations":["D:284-285"],"tags":["The Devil","Literature","Time & Eternity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Illumination (Interior)","definition":"Interior understanding that Duffey says is needed to interpret the <em>Argo</em>’s log, whose record extends nine thousand years backward and sixty-seven years forward [MTM B13:411].","citations":["MTM B13:411"],"tags":["Mysticism","Ships","Duffey"]},{"name":"Illyria","definition":"Ancient region of the Balkan Peninsula along the Adriatic. Gabrielovitch places Ragusa, now Dubrovnik, in Illyria and explains <em>argosy</em> through <em>Ragosy</em>, a fleet from Ragusa [A:127]. John Schultz’s letter associates the <em>Argo</em>’s westward voyages with Illyria and the Adriatic, allowing variant routes [MTM B6:186]. Finnegan’s travel poem wonders whether Illyrian fleets were lost in Illyrian fogs [A:172].","citations":["A:127","MTM B6:186","A:172"],"tags":["Eastern Europe","Ships","Sailing","Argonautica","Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Illyrian","definition":"Adjective for the fleets and fogs in Finnegan’s travel poem: he wonders whether all the Illyrian fleets were lost in Illyrian fogs [A:172].","citations":["A:172"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Imazu","definition":"One of the names Mr. X gives during a telephone call involving the \"Y\" code book. The passage does not identify its bearer or explain its significance [D:184].","citations":["D:184"],"tags":["Identity"]},{"name":"Immortality","definition":"Private Gregory calls the will immortal while explaining possession as hypnotism from beyond the grave [A:84]. Marie Courtois claims that the recurring earthly return of beings like herself, Demeter and her own Persephone, is the only immortality; Finnegan answers that she is wrong, although the proof is not there [D:91].","citations":["A:84","D:91"],"tags":["Immortality","Ghosts & Spirits","Finnegan","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Imperial Bar","definition":"Possible name of the Basse-Terre establishment the companions call Sloppy Joe’s; Finnegan is unsure whether its real name is the Imperial or the Royal [D:235]. Its proprietor is Joseph Chastaigne, and Angela Cosquin works there [D:266].","citations":["D:235","D:266"],"tags":["Caribbean","Bars & Taverns","Occupations"]},{"name":"Imperial Carriage","definition":"One of the vehicles named by the turbaned Pop Historian as he evokes the memories supposedly held by a Decatur Street flagstone [MTM B11:329].","citations":["MTM B11:329"],"tags":["New Orleans","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Imperial Quart","definition":"Beer measure described in <em>Archipelago</em> as thirty-nine ounces, with a \"jolt at the end.\" Finnegan, Vincent, Freddy, and Tom progress to it after steins [A:14]. In a separate contest, Joe Bushmaster downs his sixth imperial quart in one gulp, winning even Hans Schultz’s admiration [A:22].","citations":["A:14","A:22"],"tags":["Alcohol"]},{"name":"In Absentia, O Absentia!","definition":"Label for Giovanni, Finnegan’s place among the Seven Pillars of Righteousness on the <em>Bark</em> masthead drawing that Finnegan supplies [A:121-122].","citations":["A:121-122"],"tags":["Finnegan","Journalism","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"In hoc signo vincet","definition":"Latin motto on a pennant of the <em>Navicula Petri</em> in Henry Salvatore’s dream [A:44]. Its <em>vincet</em> means \"he will conquer\"; it recalls the traditional <em>In hoc signo vinces</em>, \"In this sign you will conquer,\" associated with Constantine’s vision before the battle of 312.","citations":["A:44"],"tags":["Miracles","Ecclesiastical","Ships","Dreams","Language & Words"]},{"name":"In Petto","definition":"Italian for \"in secret,\" literally \"in the breast.\" Paul XI names Finnegan a cardinal <em>in petto</em> after Finnegan paints his portrait, a week before the pope is murdered [MB:598]. The unreliable Mr. X also claims that a deceased pope made Finnegan a Papal Count <em>in petto</em> [MTM B12:367].","citations":["MB:598","MTM B12:367"],"tags":["Popes","Finnegan","Social Class","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Incarnation","definition":"The Creator’s visit to his own creation, discussed in an epigraph attributed to G. K. Chesterton’s <em>The Everlasting Man</em> [MTM B5:176]. A different idea, reincarnation, appears in Marie Courtois’s claim that she returns every year as both Demeter and her own Persephone [D:91].","citations":["MTM B5:176","D:91"],"tags":["Theology","Creation","Literature","Resurrection"]},{"name":"Incendiarism","definition":"Deliberate fire-setting. In Charles O. A. Harrington’s hostile review of Royce Rollins’s <em>The Return of the Neanderthal</em>, Rollins is accused of attributing revolutionary movements, incendiarism, and massacres to alien beings within humanity [D:240-242].","citations":["D:240-242"],"tags":["Literature","Crime"]},{"name":"Incense","definition":"The mesquite coals of the Padre Island fish fry give off a weedy smell with a touch of incense [D:34]. Incense bushes belong to the Boy King Melchisedech’s remembered domain [MTM B1:17]. Incense is burned during the offerings at the opening of the Princes’ Conclave in Babylonia Bagascia [MB:565-566].","citations":["D:34","MTM B1:17","MB:565-566"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Islands","Duffey","Ekklesia"]},{"name":"Incubus","definition":"Evil spirit said to lie upon sleepers. Planning the Sinbad panels, Finnegan says that William Gerecke, his Old Man of the Sea, \"looks like an incubus\" [D:62].","citations":["D:62"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Finnegan","Dreams","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Indelecio, John","definition":"John \"Little Alcohol\" Indelecio, a regular at the Old Wooden Ship, is the climbing and high-window specialist in a burglary partnership with John \"Big Alcohol\" Jereboam. The pair undertake jobs only when deeply in debt to Lazarus Reilly [D:215] [D:218].","citations":["D:215","D:218"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Crime","Occupations"]},{"name":"India","definition":"Recurring setting of Seaworthy’s dreams, which he calls the land of his unconscious. He describes a gray, colorless dream of an unidentified Indian city where he cannot dispose of a male corpse and a cart wheel repeatedly comes off. He also claims that a Hindu sect regards India as Purgatory and its members as already dead [D:72-73]. Finnegan recalls and interprets this conversation while swimming underwater; he decides the corpse is literal and the Indian setting may stand for a Western one [D:71] [D:74]. The Boy King has milk from India buffalo [MTM B1:17-18], and the hybrid Kasmir-Stein is described as a remote jewel of India [MTM B5:183].","citations":["D:72-73","D:71","D:74","MTM B1:17-18","MTM B5:183"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Dreams","Duffey","Devils (Characters)","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Indian Scalper","definition":"A figure of the sensational American adventure literature preserved in the Harlow House library, including the listed <em>Buffalo Hunter and Indian Scalper</em>. These are among the old goat-man’s favorite books [A:197-198].","citations":["A:197-198"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Indian War","definition":"Unspecified conflict during which Private Gregory says his papers were lost. He says losses during this war and the Civil War each allowed him to subtract decades from his recorded age, concealing his failure to age during a long U.S. Army career [A:77].","citations":["A:77"],"tags":["Soldiers","Identity"]},{"name":"Indian-colored","definition":"Description of the cigar-store Christ from St. Kitts among Duffey’s collected Christ images. A Black island artist uses the cigar-store Indian convention, including a hand shaped to hold cigars, but gives the bare-headed figure Christ’s face [MTM B3:103].","citations":["MTM B3:103"],"tags":["New Testament","Visual Arts","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Indianapolis race","definition":"The Indianapolis 500 automobile race, first held in 1911. Finnegan brings a portable radio to follow the race during his graveyard meeting with Doll Delaney and Mr. X; they choose the day because other cemetery visitors will make them less conspicuous [D:276].","citations":["D:276"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Indians (Cigar Store)","definition":"Four life-size wooden figures carved by Finnegan during his approximately three-week stay with Casey in Chicago, combining authenticity, humor, and burlesque. He sells them for a hundred dollars each; Hillary Hilton later keeps one for his love of Finnegan and will not sell it [A:183]. In <em>Not to Mention Camels</em>, a slightly larger-than-life Finnegan cigar-store Indian is described as \"woodenly vital\" and holds cigars that prove real. The same novel reports a disputed claim that Gregory van Ghi was Finnegan’s disciple, while noting that van Ghi was older. It also gives Count Finnegan a role in shaping the Narrow Corner: Anastasia recounts its prophetic frightfulness, and he projects it back through his visualization.","citations":["A:183"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Money & Trade","Literature","Creation"]},{"name":"Indies","definition":"The Indies are a projected destination in Finnegan’s travel poem [A:173], and Ignacio describes St. Kitts as an unusually interesting island among them [A:272]. Teresa quotes a prophecy that Duffey’s <em>Argo</em> will carry Count Finnegan to an obscure place in the Indies to be elected and crowned [MTM B12:388]. A painting of the risen Count associates his altered flesh with the uncoffined dead of poor people in the West Indies [MB:551].","citations":["A:173","A:272","MTM B12:388","MB:551"],"tags":["Islands","Caribbean","Finnegan","Prophecy & Visions","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Indigestion","definition":"Unverified association with Peter Wirt. The cited passage calls him a man with \"the mind of a wolverine,\" but gives him no digestive complaint [D:163].","citations":["D:163"],"tags":[]},{"name":"Indulgence","definition":"Finnegan’s distant appearance is compared to Dionysus before he became \"puffy with indulgence\" [A:6]. Seaworthy says that all their sort indulge in amnesia, and Finnegan thinks this is an indulgence he should abandon [D:11-12]. Lily Koch jokes that she envies people whose lifestyles permit them to indulge in murder [MTM B3:97].","citations":["A:6","D:11-12","MTM B3:97"],"tags":["Finnegan","Greek Mythology","Crime"]},{"name":"Indult","definition":"Ecclesiastical permission for an exception to Church law. The phrase \"at the drop of an indult\" describes how readily Duffey, the <em>Bark</em>’s theology reviewer, could name the hundred leading theologians [MTM B6:208].","citations":["MTM B6:208"],"tags":["Duffey","Ecclesiastical","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Industrial-Agricultural country","definition":"The underlying landscape in Tartary over which a false desert is sometimes superimposed. Duffey notices incorrectly made rocks and plants, suspicious skeletal remains, and fierce heat that does not move the sand or vegetation [MTM B13:439].","citations":["MTM B13:439"],"tags":["Duffey","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Ineffable Name","definition":"God’s name understood as unutterable or not to be spoken. No specific occurrence or identifiable allusion has been located in the three novels.","citations":[],"tags":["Theology","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Inertia","definition":"An unlocated label originally attached to the Great Day’s transformations. The cited episode shows apparently absent coffee cups collapsing, but does not discuss inertia [GD:485].","citations":["GD:485"],"tags":["Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Infidels (New)","definition":"The New Infidels have ruled Tartary for several generations when the <em>Argo</em> is sent to investigate the Laughing Prince. Tartary is no longer even an official name [MTM B13:438].","citations":["MTM B13:438"],"tags":["Fictional Places","Ships"]},{"name":"Infiltrates","definition":"Plotters or diabolists said to penetrate the People, the State, and the Church, recorded on index cards in the brown box Mr. X steals from Casey [MTM B6:203]. Casey says Duffey has indexed several thousand in laymen’s groups and would have them \"up by the thumbs\" if he could [A:42-43]. X supplements the file but warns that his information, like all information on the Infiltrates, should be taken with a grain of salt [A:136-137]. Duffey later keeps the file in his walk-in safe [A:143]. In a separate episode Manuel plans to infiltrate the apparatus that turns men into devils [D:56].","citations":["MTM B6:203","A:42-43","A:136-137","A:143","D:56"],"tags":["The Devil","Duffey","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Information","definition":"Mr. X collects secret information, supplements the Infiltrates file, and warns that it requires skepticism [MTM B6:200] [A:137]. He withholds accusations for which he lacks independent corroboration and says his supposed Devil informant knows only disconnected glimpses of the future [A:139-140]. Duffey’s later account again warns that X cannot be depended upon [MB:549]. X also displays knowledge of Mogul’s assassination contracts [MB:570-571]. Marie Courtois obtains Finnegan’s details for the ship’s register; Anastasia tells him Marie needs more information [D:21] [D:32]. The Naxos raiders rely on inadequate maps and a girl’s childhood memories [D:136], while X later questions Finnegan unsuccessfully about intelligence he might have gathered aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em> [D:188]. Angela Cosquin’s maps suggest she has obtained the locations of Finnegan’s hidden treasure from his mind [D:263]. The narrator gives supposedly exact coordinates for Paradise [D:234]. X says a document buried with the elder Devil contains dangerously valuable information, motivating Seaworthy’s grave-opening and X’s own proposed search [D:288] [D:290].","citations":["MTM B6:200","A:137","A:139-140","MB:549","MB:570-571","D:21","D:32","D:136","D:188","D:263","D:234","D:288","D:290"],"tags":["Ships","Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","Women Characters","History & Memory","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Infra-red cookers (Directional)","definition":"A proposed assassination device among the submissions to John Mogul’s murder contest. The fictional proposal imagines a directional cooker heating a victim’s internal organs without leaving an external mark [MB:581].","citations":["MB:581"],"tags":["Crime"]},{"name":"Infra-red rays","definition":"Part of the extravagant description of Teresa Piccone as a blue-light phenomenon: looking too long at her supposedly gives the viewer welders’ eye-burns [MTM B5:163].","citations":["MTM B5:163"],"tags":["Women Characters"]},{"name":"Ingraham","definition":"One of the aliases Mr. X assigns to Gregory McColum Papadiabolous, the Irish-born Papadiabolous II, also called Noonan and described as a son of Ifreann [D:285].","citations":["D:285"],"tags":["The Devil","Ireland","Devils (Characters)","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Inhabited World (Oikoumene)","definition":"An unlocated term originally associated with the <em>Argo</em>’s voyages. The cited passage says that the ship knows all the world’s seas and islands, but does not name the <em>Oikoumene</em> or say that it sails beyond the inhabited world [MTM B13:405].","citations":["MTM B13:405"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing"]},{"name":"Ink","definition":"Duffey uses squid ink kept in a bowl for official writing and uses it to answer the Royal Pop People’s invitation [MTM B11:312-313]. Finnegan draws Manuel in ink on the back of a poster during the visit to Demetrio’s bar [D:58].","citations":["MTM B11:312-313","D:58"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Inkling","definition":"Casey’s impression that rich men might practice a secret higher religion, prompted by seeing them enter the monsignor’s study after Benediction. The meeting is actually for a fundraising drive, and Casey never finds the imagined higher religion [A:158-159].","citations":["A:158-159"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Theology"]},{"name":"Inn","definition":"Casey drinks in a College Inn instead of attending a lecture during his irregular college years, 1938-1942 [A:167]. After swimming from Naxos to Paros, Finnegan sits at an inn for three days before Mr. X arrives [D:151-152]. Costerman’s Whalers’ Inn is among the waterfront establishments Duffey remembers [MTM B13:407].","citations":["A:167","D:151-152","MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Finnegan","Duffey"]},{"name":"Interior Garden, The (story)","definition":"Don Barnaby’s account of the courtyard at Madame Le Mourcheor’s establishment after he takes one of her joy pills. The apparently small garden expands to contain whole worlds; he experiences a marriage and children whose names and appearances continually change while their identities persist [D:249-250].","citations":["D:249-250"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Literature","Metaphysics","Family"]},{"name":"Investigator, The","definition":"Publication started by Absalom Stein, Melchisedech Duffey, and Gabrielovitch. It is printed at the Pelican Press but is separate from <em>The Bark</em>; the three men’s outside incomes help make it independent. Duffey writes for both journals [A:143].","citations":["A:143"],"tags":["Journalism","New Orleans","Duffey"]},{"name":"Iolcus","definition":"Ancient Thessalian city associated with Jason. Rosemary Riorden addresses the amnesiac Sergeant as Aeson’s son and tells him that he will never return in the flesh to Iolcus [A:89]. Gabrielovitch later identifies Ragusa in Illyria as the original Iolcus, within the novel’s shifting account of the Argosy and its voyagers [A:127].","citations":["A:89","A:127"],"tags":["Argonautica","Greek Mythology","Greece","Identity"]},{"name":"Ionian Sea","definition":"Sea between southeastern Italy and western Greece; included among the many seas sailed by the <em>Argo</em> and remembered by Melchisedech Duffey [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Italy","Greece","Ships","Duffey"]},{"name":"Iowa","definition":"Midwestern American state associated with one of Duffey’s three competing childhoods. An older relative places him with cousins in Iowa towns and on farms from ages five to twelve. Harrison, Shelby, and Pottawattamie are alternative counties of origin, while Minden, Underwood, Beebee Town, Neola, Crescent, Avoca, and Union Township dispute his birthplace [MTM B1:7]. The Iowa memories include the Lake Manawa encounter in which he holds a threatening man underwater by mental force, the substituted fat altar boy at the bell rope, and the farm episode with the sorrel kit fox [MTM B1:8-9] [MTM B1:14-15] [MTM B1:19-20]. Iowa’s red squirrels, apple trees, and wheat and cornfields contrast with his other childhood landscapes and the more convincing memories of the Boy King [MTM B1:17]. Neola, Iowa, was also R. A. Lafferty’s birthplace [D:295].","citations":["MTM B1:7","MTM B1:8-9","MTM B1:14-15","MTM B1:19-20","MTM B1:17","D:295"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory","Identity","Magic & Sorcery","Family","Literary Figures"]},{"name":"Ireland","definition":"A source of family identity, names, and stories, often treated with irony. Patrick Stranahan belongs to the “Little-Irish Rich” of St. Louis [A:102]. It is Captain Guiterrez, speaking to Vincent, who calls Irish claims of Milesian antiquity “toy history” [A:66]. Finnegan bears an Irish nickname [D:122], while his family history combines the Solli and McCracken names and his sister locates their childhood in the Irish Channel [A:173-175]. When Doll disputes his knowledge of Ireland, he claims a day and a half in Bantry unloading cargo [D:192].<br><br>X’s account makes Katie Noonan the Irish mother of Ifreann and places the birth of Gregory McColum, Papadiabolous II, in Cork [D:279] [D:285]. On the Cuban journey, Duffey’s seaman’s papers identify him as “Mike Duffey,” a citizen of the Irish Free State [MTM B12:369]. In the <em>Argo</em>’s legendary history, St. Brandon sails her from Ireland to America [MTM B13:404].","citations":["A:102","A:66","D:122","A:173-175","D:192","D:279","D:285","MTM B12:369","MTM B13:404"],"tags":["Ireland","Family","Identity","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Irish","definition":"Irish identities, manners, and speech recur throughout the novels. Duffey acquires Lace Curtain Irish elegance from Charley Murray and recalls the memorable-face saying when he meets Bagby [MTM B1:26] [MTM B2:43]. A teacher calls Duffey a flaming Irishman, Stein calls him a filthy Irishman, and Patrick Plunket calls him a snake-bit and lame-brained Irishman [MTM B4:135] [GD:493] [MTM B12:391-392]. His Mike Duffey papers identify him as an Irish Free State citizen; in Cuba, he can pass for any Irishman [MTM B12:369] [MTM B12:372]. Irish women attend St. Malachy’s; Irish and Italian guests attend the Stranahan-Piccone wedding; Duffey’s company dines at Girardeau’s Irish Restaurant [MTM B3:88] [MTM B5:183] [MTM B11:315] [MTM B11:318]. Plunket is called an Irish saint, magician, and medical doctor [MTM B12:389]. Patrick Stranahan is lace-curtain and grand-piano Irish rich, and Marie Monaghan belongs to old Irish Monaghan, Larkin, and Murphey families [A:102] [A:7]. Vincent credits the Irish with the bottle; Finnegan’s mixed speech includes waterfront Irish, and Howell discusses Irish wakes’ mixture of horror and humor [A:5-6] [A:85]. A Naxos boy calls Finnegan an Italian with an Irish name. Papadiabolous II identifies <em>Seabach</em> as an Irish nickname, and X identifies that dead double with the Irish policeman called Noonan or Flaherty [D:145] [D:122] [D:154]. X says Ifreann’s mother was the Irish governess Katie Noonan and attributes Irish accents in parts of Poland, Brazil, Italy, and Spain to such governesses and nuns [D:278-279]. He glosses <em>Ifreann</em> as Devil, although the Irish word means hell. X’s own Cork identity is Gregory McColum [D:285]. Finnegan drunkenly claims a brief visit to Bantry, Mary Catherine brings out Irish whisky, and Patti places the Solli family in New Orleans’ Irish Channel rather than the Quarter [D:192] [A:249] [A:175].","citations":["MTM B1:26","MTM B2:43","MTM B4:135","GD:493","MTM B12:391-392","MTM B12:369","MTM B12:372","MTM B3:88","MTM B5:183","MTM B11:315","MTM B11:318","MTM B12:389","A:102","A:7","A:5-6","A:85","D:145","D:122","D:154","D:278-279","D:285","D:192","A:249","A:175"],"tags":["Ireland","Duffey","Finnegan","Identity","Language & Words","Family"]},{"name":"Irish Channel","definition":"New Orleans neighborhood in which Patti Solli says her family grew up, contradicting Finnegan’s account of being raised in the heart of the Quarter [A:175].","citations":["A:175"],"tags":["New Orleans","Louisiana","Finnegan","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Irish saint and magician (Patrick Plunket)","definition":"Patrick Plunket, called a great Irish saint, magician, and medical doctor, serves as Duffey’s mentor in the seventh contingency. This is a dying vision, still short of death and finality [MTM B12:389]. Plunket insists that the landscape Duffey mistakes for artistic animation is real, identifies the Invisible Mountain as an interior ascent in intensity, and explains that they are in the Presence and walking with Permanence Itself [MTM B12:390-391].","citations":["MTM B12:389","MTM B12:390-391"],"tags":["Duffey","Ireland","Saints","Mysticism","Theology","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Iron Curtain","definition":"Name for the political division between Soviet-dominated eastern Europe and the West after World War II. In the discussion of imitations of Casey’s <em>Crock</em>, a <em>Tucalul</em> is said to exist behind the iron curtain [A:168].","citations":["A:168"],"tags":["Eastern Europe","History & Memory","Literature"]},{"name":"Iron Ladder","definition":"Sky-hung ladder in Hans Schultz’s exhilarating climbing dream. It rises thousands of feet through clouds and past golden eagles toward the sun; the ascent brings a sense of perfection and achievement, and the dream associates Hans with Apollo [A:61-62].","citations":["A:61-62"],"tags":["Dreams","Greek Mythology","Identity"]},{"name":"Iron Meadows","definition":"Places inhabited by Aspects in an epigraph attributed to Bascom Bagby’s <em>Letters After I Am Dead</em>. It says that some aspects of the Argo Legend dwell in one such meadow, unknown to both God and Duffey [MTM B5:177].","citations":["MTM B5:177"],"tags":["Fictional Places","Argonautica","Metaphysics","Duffey"]},{"name":"Iscamyl Acetate","definition":"Iscamyl Acetate is the novel’s printed form, apparently referring to isoamyl acetate or banana oil. See <em>Isoamyl acetate</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Isle of Bliss","definition":"The paradise Finnegan’s unwritten verse says he found, almost on Naxos Isle. The poem joins this remembered bliss to the hope of walking there again and to the presence or absence of dead friends [D:117-118].","citations":["D:117-118"],"tags":["Finnegan","Greece","Islands","Literature","The Dead"]},{"name":"Isles of Greece, the","definition":"Opening refrain of the verse attributed to the <em>Unwritten poems of Finnegan</em>, the epigraph to the Anastasia Demetriades chapter of <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>. It celebrates Anastasia and a briefly attained peace, then locates an Isle of Bliss almost on Naxos and imagines returning there; its closing lines remember friends, including the dead [D:117-118].","citations":["D:117-118"],"tags":["Finnegan","Greece","Islands","Literature","Women Characters","The Dead"]},{"name":"Isoamyl acetate","definition":"Casey Szymansky’s high-school notebook identifies a substance printed as <em>Iscamyl Acetate</em> as Banana Oil [A:162-163]. It apparently refers to isoamyl acetate, a compound also known as banana oil.","citations":["A:162-163"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Israel, Shirley","definition":"Member of the Chicago circle around the McSorleys, Calumets, and Hallahans. Her near-north-side salon is rumored to outdo the Duffeys’ in wit and information, and the group wants to retain Duffey because of his magic [MTM B4:137]. A compromising photograph of Duffey with Shirley forms part of the blackmail portfolio; the narrator calls the scene an entrapment and a badger-game trick [MTM B4:139-140]. Shirley disparages Duffey’s bars as old-fashioned, hisses at Demetrio Glauch, and serves drinks fortified with an additive. Rollo’s later report of the Green Mule appears to refer to these drinks [MTM B4:126-127] [MTM B4:129] [MTM B4:132].","citations":["MTM B4:137","MTM B4:139-140","MTM B4:126-127","MTM B4:129","MTM B4:132"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Duffey","Crime","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Italians","definition":"Italian people and identities recur in Duffey’s and Finnegan’s stories. Duffey gives his first talisman to an Italian confectioner; Piccone of the Star and Garter offers work to Mary Louise and Beth; Italian women, including a girl whose beauty elevates Duffey’s soul, attend St. Malachy’s [MTM B1:18] [MTM B2:58] [MTM B3:88]. Sebastian Hilton has traveled in Italy and is fancifully said to order parts for his better world from there [MTM B1:22] [MTM B1:25]. Duffey claims ambulance service in Italy with Hemingway, though his war memories may be borrowed, and witnesses place him there with the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B2:72]. Teresa Piccone’s Italian features and her enormous Italian meals are described; the cooking episode jokes that Italian vegetables are imported even in Italy and compares O’Conner’s and Peterson’s Italian restaurants [MTM B5:171] [MTM B7:230]. Irish and Italian guests attend the Stranahan-Piccone wedding. Margaret Stone is described as Italian and Jewish, with possible Greek and Pre-Adamite elements [MTM B5:183] [MTM B11:310]. Finnegan identifies his ancestry as Italian, speaks Italian with Palermo idlers, thinks the finger-count game Italian, and is called an Italian with an Irish name [D:93] [D:114] [D:104] [D:145]. A Greek Capuchin speaks Italian and chides Solli for inadequate Greek, arguing from southern Italy’s Greek ancestry [D:118-119]. Manuel plans to inform the Pope in Rome, while X attributes Irish-accented English in parts of Italy to Irish nuns and governesses [D:150-151] [D:279].","citations":["MTM B1:18","MTM B2:58","MTM B3:88","MTM B1:22","MTM B1:25","MTM B2:72","MTM B5:171","MTM B7:230","MTM B5:183","MTM B11:310","D:93","D:114","D:104","D:145","D:118-119","D:150-151","D:279"],"tags":["Italy","Duffey","Finnegan","Identity","Language & Words","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Italy","definition":"Country associated with several competing memories and fictional journeys. Sebastian Hilton travels there and is fancifully said to obtain parts for his better world from Italy [MTM B1:22] [MTM B1:25]. Duffey claims Italian ambulance service with Hemingway, though these memories may be borrowed; witnesses also place him there with the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B2:72]. Teresa’s cooking episode jokes about imported Italian vegetables, and Babylonia Bagascia, the site of the Last Conclave, is placed in Italy [MTM B7:230] [MB:570]. The Ionian Sea lies between Italy and Greece and appears in the <em>Argo</em>’s itinerary [MTM B13:406]. Finnegan claims Italian ancestry and speaks Italian in Palermo; the Naxos Capuchin explains southern Italy through Greek ancestry [D:93] [D:114] [D:118-119]. X recounts Ifreann’s life in Milano and Sicily, offers an Irish-governess explanation for Irish-accented English in parts of Italy, and has himself belonged to the Party there under another name [D:279-280] [A:137-138]. Patti contradicts Finnegan’s account of Italian art schooling: she says his only visit was two days in Naples between ships the previous year [A:175].","citations":["MTM B1:22","MTM B1:25","MTM B2:72","MTM B7:230","MB:570","MTM B13:406","D:93","D:114","D:118-119","D:279-280","A:137-138","A:175"],"tags":["Italy","Duffey","Finnegan","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Jackass Flats","definition":"Name of one of the precipitous places remembered in Don Lewis’s account of being lost with Santee and Casper on Willy Jones Island, after the killing of the small man called Yokipoki. Despite knowing many named valleys, they cannot identify the valley they have entered [D:44-46].","citations":["D:44-46"],"tags":["Fictional Places","Pacific Theater","World War II","Crime"]},{"name":"Jacko (young)","definition":"Youthful persona in Shakes Pearson’s unfinished first-person limerick about making chewing tobacco from old sacks, boots, and boot-jacks. Shakes is Marie Monaghan’s uncle [A:25-26].","citations":["A:25-26"],"tags":["Literature","Minor Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Jacksonville Drover, The (whip)","definition":"Whip named alongside the Cowles Buggy Whip by the turbaned speaker at a Royal Pop History gathering. It belongs to his catalogue of precisely remembered objects from the former world [MTM B11:329].","citations":["MTM B11:329"],"tags":["History & Memory","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Jake (Leg) Lewis","definition":"Resident regular of the Old Wooden Ship and, with Tommy (Cow Town) Borger, a salt-water cowboy who works aboard cattle boats. He wears cowboy gear, including boots, spurs, chaps, and gauntlets, though these cowboys use no horses and live through long gaps between voyages [D:216] [D:222].","citations":["D:216","D:222"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Crew & Sailors","Occupations","Maritime Trade"]},{"name":"Janeway, Celeste Lynne","definition":"Earlier heading inverted Janeway as though it were her surname. See <em>Lynne, Janeway Celeste</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Louisiana","Duffey","Ships"]},{"name":"Jangle chain","definition":"Long hanging chain associated with a zoot suit, typically looping from the waistband to a trouser pocket. The Armenian merchant Askandan, also called Asking Dan, promises to find one for Casey after selling him a zoot suit, pointed shoes, and a monstrous hat on credit [A:258] [A:260-261].","citations":["A:258","A:260-261"],"tags":["Social Class","Minor Characters","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Januarius O'Higgins","definition":"Literary friend named with Elena O’Higgins among Duffey’s Chicago company of the golden years [MTM B3:98]. In Josephine McSorley’s report on the Casey Machine, Januarius calls Casey a hypocrite and predicts that Casey will secretly buy one of the machines himself [CM:506-507].","citations":["MTM B3:98","CM:506-507"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Duffey","Literature"]},{"name":"January","definition":"Month of Hans Schultz’s birth on January 2, 1915 [A:207] [MTM B3:76]. Finnegan stops drinking at New Year’s in Port of Spain and resumes on January 26 at Freetown, the date he describes as his fall from grace [D:85] [D:93].","citations":["A:207","MTM B3:76","D:85","D:93"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Japan","definition":"Country identified with Xavier Cardinal Runosake of Kobe, whom X names among the thirteen contracted assassination targets [MB:571].","citations":["MB:571"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Ecclesiastical","Crime"]},{"name":"Japan Sea","definition":"Sea between Japan and mainland Asia, also called the Sea of Japan or East Sea. It is included among the many seas sailed by the <em>Argo</em> and remembered by Duffey [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Ships","Duffey"]},{"name":"Japanese soldier","definition":"Ragged little man who seems to be a Japanese soldier in Don Lewis’s account of October 1, 1944, on Pulau Petir or Willy Jones Island. The pursuing Americans are Charles Santee, Robert Casper, and Timothy Lorrigan; Don Lewis says he was one of them while withholding a firm identification. The small man beckons, twice evades capture by apparent disappearance, is wounded by Santee, and is finally killed by Santee and Casper [D:44-45]. Lewis later calls him Yokipoki, probably not his real name [D:46].","citations":["D:44-45","D:46"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Soldiers","Pacific Theater","World War II","Crime"]},{"name":"Jargon","definition":"Term in Finnegan’s letter to Dotty contrasting the unkindness expressed in jazz with that expressed in jargon. The letter attacks the moral and emotional character of several musical forms [A:263].","citations":["A:263"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words","Music"]},{"name":"Jars","definition":"Gautier carries dried goat meat mixed with melted fat and honey in jars or skin bags [A:197]. Duffey stocks his new establishment with five-gallon jars of pigs’ feet, spiced Polish sausage, and apple butter [MTM B2:41]. As a child, Duffey also keeps jars of blood; Charles Hart says he used to do the same and remarks that blood attracts weasels [MTM B1:18].","citations":["A:197","MTM B2:41","MTM B1:18"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Jascoyne","definition":"Great fish encountered by St. Brandon in the <em>Argo</em>’s legendary history. The ship is said to have been called <em>Land of Behest</em> when he sailed her from Ireland to America [MTM B13:404].","citations":["MTM B13:404"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Ireland","Saints","Ships","Sailing"]},{"name":"Jason","definition":"Leader of the Argonauts in the quest for the Golden Fleece, identified with Finnegan, John A. Solli, under the spelling Iason [A:39]. Solli’s birth year is 1919 [MTM B3:76]. In the classical tradition Jason is Aeson’s son, deprived of his inheritance by Pelias; the one-sandal prophecy leads Pelias to send him after the Fleece. Hera supports his voyage. In Apollonius’s <em>Argonautica</em>, Jason becomes leader with Heracles’ endorsement. He is Hypsipyle’s lover on Lemnos, but Heracles must spur the company to resume its journey; Apollodorus records their children. The voyagers undergo island mysteries, defeat the Earthborn with Heracles, and mistakenly fight their former hosts at night, when Jason kills Cyzicus. Heracles is later left behind in Mysia. Jason joins the fighting against the Bebrycians; the sons of Boreas drive off Phineus’s Harpies, and Phineus advises the passage of the Clashing Rocks. Tiphys steers, Euphemus releases the dove, and Athena helps the ship through. In Colchis, Medea’s divinely induced love and magical aid enable Jason to yoke the fire-breathing bulls, plough the field, sow the dragon’s teeth, and defeat the earthborn warriors. She subdues the Fleece’s guardian, and they escape together. In Apollonius, Jason kills Apsyrtus in a plot devised with Medea, and Circe purifies the pair. Their marriage among the Phaeacians prevents Medea’s surrender. In Libya Jason receives the goddesses’ riddle and Peleus interprets it, leading to the company’s portage of the ship. Medea defeats Talos, and Jason interprets Euphemus’s dream of the clod that becomes Thera. Apollodorus continues the story after the return: Medea contrives Pelias’s death, Acastus expels the couple, and Jason’s later marriage at Corinth provokes Medea’s revenge. His story combines leadership and resourcefulness with dependence on companions, divine aid, and Medea.","citations":["A:39","MTM B3:76"],"tags":["Argonautica","Argonauts (Characters)","Greek Mythology","Finnegan","Identity","Ships"]},{"name":"Java","definition":"Place from which Anne broadcasts for the Japanese to Allied troops during the war [A:46]. Brannagan later tells Finnegan that he has lived in Java’s interior [D:203].","citations":["A:46","D:203"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","World War II","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Java Sea","definition":"One of the seas sailed by the <em>Argo</em>, whose name recalls Duffey’s own voyages [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Ships","Duffey"]},{"name":"Jaw bones","definition":"Remains invoked in Hans’s comic argument that forks predate fingers: he claims that old jaw bones, brain pans, and pelvises are found, whereas equally old fingers are not [A:5].","citations":["A:5"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Jazz","definition":"Music that Finnegan dislikes, particularly in New Orleans; he objects to printing a jazz magazine and later tells Dotty that jazz can express more unkindness than jargon [A:115] [A:117-118] [A:263]. The <em>Crock</em>’s record reviews favor Chicago-style jazz, while Pelican Press plans its jazz paper for Fridays [A:41] [A:118]. Bulo Belonki is associated with the <em>Jazz Sheet</em>, and Howland’s Club Royal has an orchestra playing old classic jazz [A:126] [A:246]. At Good Guy’s, Monumental Jazz prompts Duffey’s reflection that classical jazz is growing old and playing its own funeral [MTM B7:220].","citations":["A:115","A:117-118","A:263","A:41","A:118","A:126","A:246","MTM B7:220"],"tags":["Music","Finnegan","Duffey","New Orleans","Journalism","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Jazz Magazine","definition":"Friday publication printed by the Pelican Press to help finance <em>The Bark</em> [A:118] [MTM B7:212]. Several successive suitors of Dotty Yekouris are connected with the magazine, and she calls each of them Benny B. Flat [MTM B7:212-213].","citations":["A:118","MTM B7:212","MTM B7:212-213"],"tags":["Journalism","Music","New Orleans","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Jazz Sheet","definition":"Pelican Press publication associated with Bulo Belonki [A:126]. After Letitia becomes editor of <em>The Bark</em>, she gives the <em>Jazz Sheet</em> and the other publications printed for money less attention [MTM B8:238].","citations":["A:126","MTM B8:238"],"tags":["Journalism","Music","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Jazzers","definition":"Jokers around Duffey’s Big Pot who fish conspicuously human bones from it and paint a sign calling it Cannibal Duffey’s Irish Restaurant. The three are medical students, and there are strong grounds for suspecting that they put the bones in themselves; the episode is taken in good humor [MTM B6:197].","citations":["MTM B6:197"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Jean, Mary","definition":"Earlier heading treated Jean as a surname; the novel uses Mary Jean and leaves her premarital surname unspecified. See <em>Mary Jean</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Family","Sacraments"]},{"name":"Jeannie","definition":"One of the women living at the Old Wooden Ship called salt-water widows, most of whom are married to seamen [D:221].","citations":["D:221"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Jebbies","definition":"Informal name for Jesuits in Finnegan’s story of painting a devil. An unnamed anticlerical friend uses it when Father Le Faye, S.J., arrives; Le Faye then exorcises the picture and orders it burned [D:254-255].","citations":["D:254-255"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Religious Orders","Language & Words","Finnegan","The Devil"]},{"name":"Jebel Shammar sect","definition":"Supposed sect whose three knife-flashing assassins threaten Margaret Stone for preaching the Real Presence at night, claiming nighttime presence belongs to their jealous Djinn. Margaret doubts the sect exists and suspects the men, who attend Tulane, are merely joking [MTM B7:225].","citations":["MTM B7:225"],"tags":["Minor Characters","New Orleans","Theology","Crime"]},{"name":"Jebus","definition":"Biblical name for Jerusalem, identified with it in Judges 19:10. Cyrus Roundhead’s false history instead treats Jebus as one of several separate ruined cities near the site of an imaginary Jerusalem; he calls it a Jewish city [MTM B11:337].","citations":["MTM B11:337"],"tags":["Old Testament","Middle East","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Jereboam (John, Big Alcohol)","definition":"John (Big Alcohol) Jereboam, an Old Wooden Ship regular and burglary partner of John (Little Alcohol) Indelecio. While Little Alcohol climbs to high windows, Big Alcohol handles the drill, saw, chisel, and pry-bar. They work mainly when heavily indebted to Lazarus Reilly [D:215] [D:218].","citations":["D:215","D:218"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Crime","Occupations"]},{"name":"Jerome (Saint)","definition":"Church Father and Doctor of the Church, born about 347 and dead in 419 or 420, known for biblical translation and revision underlying the Latin Vulgate, scriptural commentaries, and ascetic writings. His scholarship and spiritual teaching became influential in Western Christianity. In Ignacio’s story of Joseph of Maintz’s lunar journey, Joseph takes Jerome’s writings in his projectile [A:271]. Finnegan, answering Le Marin, names Jerome and John Chrysostom as possible authorities for an insular Paradise, while observing that they did not locate it in the Caribbean [D:234].","citations":["A:271","D:234"],"tags":["Patristics","Saints","Church History","Scripture","Literature"]},{"name":"Jersey","definition":"New Jersey. Gautier the goat-man, after years of roaming the Atlantic states, remains there throughout the year [A:196]. Trinali asks Hans to take him to two Jersey locations for driftwood and cypress knees [A:229]. Phoebe Anne Ankerson’s suitcase tag gives her home as Hammonton, New Jersey [D:172].","citations":["A:196","A:229","D:172"],"tags":["Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Jersey Shore","definition":"New Jersey’s coastal region. After abandoning his Manhattan career as Gregory van Ghi, Finnegan takes a taxi to the Jersey Shore, buys socks and a small hand grip, and sets out on the road again [A:191-193].","citations":["A:191-193"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Jerusalem","definition":"City discussed in sharply different accounts. A Book Eight epigraph attributed to Josephus says that Melchizedech, its righteous priest-king and first temple builder, changed its name from Salem to Jerusalem [MTM B8:237]. Cyrus Roundhead’s false history denies that Jerusalem ever existed: he calls the name a failed Turkish real-estate promotion near supposedly separate ruins named Jebus, Solyma, Hiero-Solyma, Aelia Capitolina, Uris Lem, and El Quds [MTM B11:337].","citations":["MTM B8:237","MTM B11:337"],"tags":["Middle East","Old Testament","History & Memory","Theology"]},{"name":"Jesus","definition":"Salvation Sally announces the Second Coming to Hans and Marie [A:8]. Sister Mary Sabina invokes Jesus, Mary, and Joseph when the young Duffey begins reorganizing her class [MTM B1:12]. A contrasting use occurs in Letitia’s scrapbook: a speaker rejects the Historical Christ, claims to see an a-Historical Christ in outcasts and criminals, and denies him in the man next door [MTM B8:251-252]. See also Jesus Christ.","citations":["A:8","MTM B1:12","MTM B8:251-252"],"tags":["New Testament","Theology","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Jesus Barabbas","definition":"The Barabbas released in place of Jesus in the Gospel trial narrative; Jesus Barabbas is a manuscript variant of his name in Matthew 27:16-17. John Schultz’s letter to Marie contrasts this revolutionary with Christ, whom he calls a defender and maker of the law, and says the Jesus People follow Barabbas [MTM B6:186].","citations":["MTM B6:186"],"tags":["New Testament","Theology","Identity"]},{"name":"Jesus Christ","definition":"Christ is evoked in Duffey’s explanation of the pelican as a symbol of his blood, when the friends debate a name for their magazine [A:121]. Sister Mary Sabina invokes Jesus, Mary, and Joseph as the young Duffey takes charge of his class [MTM B1:12]. In a letter to Marie, John Schultz contrasts Jesus Christ with Jesus Barabbas as part of his argument about dark companions and doubles [MTM B6:186]. See also Jesus.","citations":["A:121","MTM B1:12","MTM B6:186"],"tags":["New Testament","Theology","Sacraments","Identity"]},{"name":"Jesus People","definition":"People whom John Schultz accuses, in a letter to Marie, of following the revolutionary Jesus Barabbas while presenting themselves as followers of Christ. The contrast introduces his discussion of dark followers and correspondences [MTM B6:186]. The name probably alludes to the Jesus People movement, which combined evangelical Christianity with elements of the American counterculture in the late 1960s and 1970s.","citations":["MTM B6:186"],"tags":["Theology","New Testament","Identity","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Jew","definition":"Jewish identity appears in the verse accompanying a letter signed S. and, more substantially, in the portrayal of Absalom Stein [A:119]. Casey’s hostile <em>Crock</em> editorial calls Stein, writing as Hugo Stone, a Communist Jew and an antisemite; Stein later describes himself as a neo-Jew [A:128-131]. Kasmir Gorshok’s mixed disguise includes a black hat and ear locks like an old Jew [MTM B13:425-426]. On the Great Day, Duffey answers Stein’s Irish insult with a Jewish one; the exchange briefly seems to preserve their former friendship before Duffey discovers Stein’s lost intelligence [GD:493].","citations":["A:119","A:128-131","MTM B13:425-426","GD:493"],"tags":["Identity","Language & Words","Duffey","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Jew bread","definition":"Bread supplied by Absalom Stein among the <em>Argo</em>’s sea stores, alongside sea biscuit from Hans and Marie Schultz and Purgatorial Loaf from Bagby. The passage explains that some of the ship’s indispensable stores are intangible [MTM B13:446].","citations":["MTM B13:446"],"tags":["Ships","Food & Dining","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Jewel thieves","definition":"Jewel thieves in Karachi who, in Ignatius Ti’s freely translated account of Amoy’s former lives, employ Amoy while he is a red-winged blackbird to steal gems from shops [A:80].","citations":["A:80"],"tags":["Occupations","Crime"]},{"name":"Jewelry","definition":"Mercedes wears gold jangles at her neck, ears, fingers, wrists, and ankles [D:224]. Finnegan buys small pieces of jewelry for Anastasia in Tangier [D:103-104]. Asking Dan classes furs and jewelry as pseudo-nervous merchandise, sold by peddlers who pretend it is stolen [A:257]. Seen through Duffey’s eyes, Absalom Stein gives the impression of wearing splendid jewelry although he wears none [MTM B5:160].","citations":["D:224","D:103-104","A:257","MTM B5:160"],"tags":["Africa","Finnegan","Women Characters","Money & Trade","Identity"]},{"name":"Jewels","definition":"In Ignatius Ti’s story of Amoy’s life as a bird, Amoy steals and swallows a blue diamond as large as an almond, star sapphires, and lesser opals [A:80]. In Finnegan’s dream of following the Oreads, trees look like jewels on a mountain that is translucent like ice [D:138].","citations":["A:80","D:138"],"tags":["Crime","Dreams","Nymphs & Mermaids"]},{"name":"Jewess (Nefarious)","definition":"Rollo McSorley’s disparaging description of the woman who served the Green Mule drink in his account of the party; the preceding scene identifies the drink-maker as Shirley Israel [MTM B4:129] [MTM B4:132].","citations":["MTM B4:129","MTM B4:132"],"tags":["Alcohol","Journalism","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Jewish","definition":"The novels apply Jewish identifications and associations in several distinct ways. Duffey’s Boston Irish voice includes a touch of stage Yiddish [A:97]. Casey’s hostile article calls Absalom Stein, also Hugo Stone, a Communist Jew [A:128-129]. Margaret Stone is described as Italian and Jewish [A:143-144]; her account of the Stone family lists twenty male first cousins and disputes Duffey’s identification of Hugo with Absalom [MTM B4:112].","citations":["A:97","A:128-129","A:143-144","MTM B4:112"],"tags":["Italy","Duffey","Family","Language & Words","Journalism","Identity"]},{"name":"Jewish mother (unnamed)","definition":"An alleged reference to Absalom Stein’s unnamed mother that has not been located in the cited passages.","citations":[],"tags":["Family"]},{"name":"Jewishness (Lurid)","definition":"The narration, following Duffey’s view of the enlarged and overwhelming Absalom Stein in St. Louis, describes his wealthy and lurid Jewishness as a theatrical type last comparably expressed on the Elizabethan stage [MTM B5:160].","citations":["MTM B5:160"],"tags":["Theater","Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Jewry","definition":"Jewish people collectively. In Mr. X’s account of the Devil’s release in Crimea, Jewish leaders are among several groups he claims were disproportionately represented; Catholic clergy and the liberal intelligentsia are also singled out [MTM B6:204-205]. The narration dates the release to the last Saturday of May 1946, while also questioning whether it had occurred in 1846 [MTM B5:184].","citations":["MTM B6:204-205","MTM B5:184"],"tags":["The Devil"]},{"name":"Jews of Africa","definition":"Joseph’s name for the Haussa people in his account of their mercantile background. He says his father was a merchant and identifies his own trade as diamond smuggling [D:94].","citations":["D:94"],"tags":["Africa","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Jill of Hearts","definition":"Card identifying the dead young girl on a sofa in the first chamber of Elroy Redheart’s four-chambered heart artwork. The card is daggered to the wall; the girl’s removed red chocolate heart lies among the candy beside her [MTM B7:221].","citations":["MTM B7:221"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Weapons","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Jim Hogan","definition":"One of the theatrical people in Duffey’s Chicago circle, listed with Rosemary Hogan [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Theater"]},{"name":"Jingle","definition":"Short verse or song. In Marie’s story, her uncle Shakes Pearson enters a chewing-tobacco jingle contest requiring a limerick [A:25]. Howland chants a five-stanza gopher jingle to a hoe-down tune while he and Finnegan lie in a hayloft on their journey toward Chicago [A:179].","citations":["A:25","A:179"],"tags":["Music","Literature"]},{"name":"Joan of Arc","definition":"French saint and national heroine (1412-1431), born to a peasant family. Her reported visions led her to support Charles VII and the French campaign against the English during the Hundred Years’ War. Condemned for heresy, she was burned at Rouen at nineteen; the condemnation was later annulled, and she was canonized in 1920. Hilary Hilton names her, alongside Caesar, Napoleon, Cleopatra, the Queen of Sheba, and Marie Antoinette, among the exalted predecessors claimed by would-be reincarnations when he attacks Casey’s claims of soul trading [PG:531].","citations":["PG:531"],"tags":["Saints","Mysticism","France","Women Characters","Historical Figures","Identity"]},{"name":"Job enrichment","definition":"The newspaper assistant’s term for a new arrangement in which everyone is equally an editor, titles are abandoned, and employees do whatever work they feel like doing or none at all. He explains it to the protesting Duffey after the paper and building have lost their names [GD:487].","citations":["GD:487"],"tags":["Journalism","Occupations"]},{"name":"Jockey (Racing mules)","definition":"One of the past occupations claimed by Herman Hercules, who describes himself as Cardinal Erculo’s exact double and says his jobs have kept him near crowds and spectacles [MB:585-586].","citations":["MB:585-586"],"tags":["Occupations","Immortality","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Joe","definition":"A name occurring in several unrelated contexts: the Polish phrase “Jak sie masz, Joe” taught to islanders by the Dirty Five [A:73]; cabdriver Joe Sorrell [D:21]; <em>Brunhilde</em> crewman Joe Cross [D:30]; boxer Joe Gans, whom Finnegan extravagantly claims to have fought for ninety rounds [D:191]; and Joseph Chastaigne, called Sloppy Joe, whose bar Finnegan and his friends nickname Sloppy Joe’s [D:235] [D:266].","citations":["A:73","D:21","D:30","D:191","D:235","D:266"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Eastern Europe","Bars & Taverns","Identity"]},{"name":"John (Apostle)","definition":"One of the twelve apostles, traditionally associated with the Fourth Gospel. A layer of the Pop People’s parchment is identified as a first-century copy, perhaps the original, of that Gospel; the hoyden asserts that its writer belonged to their group [MTM B11:327].","citations":["MTM B11:327"],"tags":["New Testament","Scripture","Literature"]},{"name":"John of the Cross, Saint","definition":"See <em>St. John of the Cross</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Saints","Mysticism","Spain","Finnegan","Literature","Theology"]},{"name":"John the Greek","definition":"Owner of <em>John the Greek’s Famous French Restaurant</em>. During a Pop-History meeting at Duffey’s establishment in New Orleans, he challenges Cyrus Roundhead’s claim that Athens never existed: John came from Athens and lived there until he was thirteen. Roundhead answers that the Macedonians invented Athens and its culture [MTM B11:336-337].","citations":["MTM B11:336-337"],"tags":["New Orleans","Greece","Food & Dining","Occupations","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Johnson's Jump-Seat Buggy, the","definition":"A horse-drawn vehicle in the turbaned Pop speaker’s catalogue of carriages remembered by a flagstone in Decatur Street. It appears with the Acme Top, Acme Open, Fulton Road Cart, and other vehicles during the meeting hosted by Duffey [MTM B11:329]. A jump seat is a folding or movable auxiliary seat.","citations":["MTM B11:329"],"tags":["History & Memory","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Joint account","definition":"Bank account shared by Mike Melchiades and his wife. He tells Duffey that he withdrew its entire balance, a little over three hundred dollars, immediately after she left, and refused her request to withdraw twenty dollars [MTM B4:142-143].","citations":["MTM B4:142-143"],"tags":["Family","Money & Trade","Duffey"]},{"name":"Joker","definition":"A practical joker. Finnegan markets his dog-housebreaking preparation to pranksters in Buffalo [A:189-190]. Ignatius Ti describes medicos posing as corpses to frighten ward boys; Ti’s friend is the ward boy who retaliates against such a prank [A:85-87]. Mr. X calls both himself and Stein born jokers [A:265-266]. Van Ghi tells Finnegan, after the painting of a devil, that he has drawn the Joker instead of the Queen [D:255]. Duffey calls Bagby a joker even in death when his weekly letters continue arriving [MTM B8:237].","citations":["A:189-190","A:85-87","A:265-266","D:255","MTM B8:237"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Death & Mortality","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Joker-camp","definition":"Finnegan’s term for the place where he says he was held for a while and then evicted, during his postwar confusion about his identity and home [A:93]. The term is not formally explained; his earlier hospitalization for amnesia suggests a psychiatric setting [A:73].","citations":["A:93","A:73"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Jokers","definition":"Pranksters, especially the medicos who impersonate corpses and grab new ward boys in Ignatius Ti’s hospital story [A:85-86]. Finnegan also calls his former place of confinement a joker-camp [A:93].","citations":["A:85-86","A:93"],"tags":["Finnegan","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Jonah","definition":"Biblical figure swallowed by a great fish for three days and nights. Doctor Saul Rafelson deliberately reverses this image in his notes on Duffey: the world spending three days in Jonah’s belly illustrates a topological inversion in which a person contains the world [MTM B4:146-147].","citations":["MTM B4:146-147"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Duffey","Paradox"]},{"name":"Jones (Willy) Island","definition":"Also called Pulau Petir. In Don Lewis’s account, three American soldiers disappear there on October 1, 1944; he claims to be one of them without saying which [D:44]. The episode includes the shooting of the apparent Japanese soldier called Yokipoki and his burial in mud by Lewis [D:45-46]. Lewis sets his further story of the Big House on the same island: its inhabitants live indefinitely by making broth from visitors’ organs [D:48]. A later reference to a furtive man buried in the island’s mud recalls the earlier burial [D:50].","citations":["D:44","D:45-46","D:48","D:50"],"tags":["Immortality","Islands","World War II","Soldiers","Literature","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Jones, Willy","definition":"Mariner who sailed the <em>Argo</em> under the name <em>Flying Serpent</em> in the Moluccas, according to the ship’s talking-oak history [MTM B13:404-405]. Willy Jones Island bears the same name, but the island passage does not identify its namesake [D:44].","citations":["MTM B13:404-405","D:44"],"tags":["Ships","Islands","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Joseph","definition":"Several distinct people share this name.<br><br>Joseph the Haussa is the traveler and diamond smuggler Finnegan meets in Freetown on January 26. He recruits Finnegan to market stones abroad [D:93-95].<br><br>The biblical Joseph is alluded to when Finnegan declines to interpret Seaworthy’s dream, saying, “I am not Joseph” [D:73]. Saint Joseph is invoked with Jesus and Mary by Sister Mary Sabina [MTM B1:12]. A Joseph Salvatore is also listed among Henry’s brothers [A:122].","citations":["D:93-95","D:73","MTM B1:12","A:122"],"tags":["Saints","Scripture","Africa","Finnegan","Crime","Money & Trade","Family","Dreams"]},{"name":"Joseph of Maintz","definition":"Holy Benedictine priest in Ignacio’s story, which he attributes to Archbishop Turpin of Rheims. In the eighth century Joseph makes a great copper-and-iron tube, lines it with glass, loads it with powder and a chamber, and shoots himself to the moon to escape the world’s temptations. He takes a Missal, Scriptures, Augustine, Jerome, Pliny’s Natural History, bread, wine, an altar stone, grape cuttings, and wheat. His monthly bonfires supposedly show that he lives there for ninety years [A:271].","citations":["A:271"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Folklore","Scripture","Patristics","Food & Dining","Literature"]},{"name":"Josephus","definition":"Jewish historian Flavius Josephus (c.37-c.100 CE). The Book Eight epigraph quotes his account of Melchizedek as the Righteous King, a Canaanite prince who first served as priest of God, built a temple, and renamed Salem Jerusalem [MTM B8:237]. The passage corresponds to The Jewish War, 6.10.1; the epigraph labels its source Jerusalem and Rome.","citations":["MTM B8:237"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Middle East","Literature","Old Testament"]},{"name":"Joss (Pinkish)","definition":"Finnegan compares Loy Larkin’s hair to a pinkish joss fitting her like a tipsy crown in his “Antipodal Ode” [A:32]. A joss is an image of a Chinese deity; the poem does not specify an incense stick or explain the comparison further.","citations":["A:32"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Journal","definition":"A Journal is one of the preliminary ideas, with an Institute and a Pot, proposed by those to whom Henry Salvatore delegates rebuilding the world, including Duffey [MTM B6:195]. Newton Prescott writes a private journal as therapy for his compulsion to forget, with instructions to burn it afterward [CM:503].","citations":["MTM B6:195","CM:503"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism","Literature","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Journey","definition":"Casey’s school notebook records the maxim “Happiness is a journey, not a destination” [A:162-163]. Duffey’s excursion into the Fifth Contingency is also described as a visit to the bright devildom, from which he fears he has brought destructive changes back to ordinary life [MTM B12:354-355].","citations":["A:162-163","MTM B12:354-355"],"tags":["Duffey","The Devil","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Joy","definition":"Finnegan’s poem to Loy Larkin recalls “urchin joy” [A:32]. At Vincent’s wedding, the narrative says Mass should be heard with joy and recalls Theresa’s smile at prayer and after communion [A:113]. In <em>More Than Melchisedech</em>, joy in money is called primordial and not wholly evil; the discussion connects sacred wealth, the gold-lined chalice, and the sacrifice of good things in holy poverty [MTM B4:124-125]. Duffey’s “joyful labor” at the end of Book Twelve is the making of new Animated Marvels from dung, blood, discarded objects, and lightning [MTM B12:394].\n\nDon Barnaby recounts the lives and pleasures he experienced after taking Madame Le Mourcheor’s Juju Joy Pill [D:248-251]. Doll wishes Finnegan and X “a joyous death” before turning to her own work of saving the world [D:292]. “Promontory Goats” describes the damned’s “joyous gloating” over one another’s torture, even while enduring torture themselves [PG:533].","citations":["A:32","A:113","MTM B4:124-125","MTM B12:394","D:248-251","D:292","PG:533"],"tags":["Liturgy","Money & Trade","Creation","Heaven & Hell","Literature"]},{"name":"Joy Pills","definition":"Pills discussed in Don Barnaby’s fantastic account of curing a hangover. He distinguishes Haiti hoodoo, High Harry from Jamaica, Deep Romany, Dead Frenchman, and Black Rooster varieties [D:248]. Madame Le Mourcheor’s Old Original Juju Joy Pills, made in New Orleans, supposedly give him years of life, marriage, family, and travel within an apparently boundless garden, although fewer than fifteen minutes pass outside it [D:249-251].","citations":["D:248","D:249-251"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Caribbean","New Orleans","Time & Eternity","Family"]},{"name":"Joy Pills (Madame Le Mourcheor's Old Original)","definition":"See Joy Pills. Don Barnaby names Madame Le Mourcheor’s Old Original Juju Joy Pills as the exceptional variety made in New Orleans [D:249].","citations":["D:249"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","New Orleans","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Joyce, David / Dinah","definition":"David and Dinah Joyce are listed among the college and university people in Duffey’s Chicago circle [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Judas","definition":"Judas Iscariot, the apostle who betrayed Jesus. His name appears in Vincent’s exclamation “Judas priest” [A:11] and in Bagby’s label Judas Priests for clergy he regards as unorthodox [MTM B2:62-63]. In the <em>Argo</em>’s fantastic history, Saint Brandon carries the traitor Judas aboard the <em>Land of Behest</em> [MTM B13:404]; Casey later says the ship ferries him across a narrow strait for one night each year [MTM B13:426]. Casey also links the Judas Prophecy, without explaining it, to the prediction that Antichrist will sail on the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:454].","citations":["A:11","MTM B2:62-63","MTM B13:404","MTM B13:426","MTM B13:454"],"tags":["New Testament","Apocalyptic","Prophecy & Visions","Ships"]},{"name":"Judas Priestism","definition":"One of the Enemy’s seven contemporary heads named by Bascom Bagby, together with Dialectic Materialism, Artistic Degradation, Secular Liberalism, Panaceic Pentecostalism, Murderous Molochism, and Atheistic Communism [MTM B5:169].","citations":["MTM B5:169"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle","Theology","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Judas Priests","definition":"Bagby’s term for clergy whom he believes have lost orthodoxy [MTM B2:62-63]. George Koran, a Picayune reporter, tells Duffey that eight such priests respond with snickers when he exclaims “Judas Priest!” at the New Catholic Press Guild’s meeting [MTM B8:239-240].","citations":["MTM B2:62-63","MTM B8:239-240"],"tags":["Theology","Catholic Church","Journalism"]},{"name":"Judas Prophecy","definition":"A prophecy that Casey tentatively connects with the prediction that Antichrist will sail on the <em>Argo</em> to Megiddo. The three <em>Argo</em> Masters discuss how to prevent the foretold passenger from boarding, but the Judas Prophecy itself is not explained [MTM B13:454].","citations":["MTM B13:454"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","Prophecy & Visions","Ships"]},{"name":"Judas Traitor","definition":"The traitor Judas, carried by Saint Brandon aboard the <em>Land of Behest</em>, a former name of the <em>Argo</em>, in the ship’s account of Brandon’s voyage from Ireland to America [MTM B13:404].","citations":["MTM B13:404"],"tags":["New Testament","Saints","Ships","Sailing"]},{"name":"Jude (Saint)","definition":"Saint Jude, one of the apostles. Dotty jokingly calls him the patron of printers and little magazines and the only apostle who was a printer; Finnegan answers that he is the advocate of impossible causes [A:115]. He is one of three possible identities, with Thomas and Bartholomew, for Papa Piccone’s tippler apostle in the Italian legend retold by Show Boat [A:262]. Finding his tomb is included among the <em>Argo</em>’s discoveries [MTM B13:422].","citations":["A:115","A:262","MTM B13:422"],"tags":["Saints","New Testament","Folklore","Ships","Italy","Journalism"]},{"name":"Judgement Day","definition":"Title of the <em>Crock</em>’s serious review section [A:41]. The narration also says that no one, including Duffey, will fully understand his seven hidden years until the afternoon of judgment day [MTM B2:73].","citations":["A:41","MTM B2:73"],"tags":["Journalism","Duffey","History & Memory","Theology"]},{"name":"Judges (Drinking Contest)","definition":"Tom Shire and Freddy Castle, officials in the Hans Schultz versus Joe Bushmaster beer-drinking contest. Bushmaster proposes them because both sides trust them [A:15]. Stein instructs them to distinguish actual spillage from condensation on the glasses [A:20].","citations":["A:15","A:20"],"tags":["Alcohol","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Jug","definition":"The tippling jug accompanies Li Po Tai in the poem of the Chinese sage and fisher maid [A:222]. Maurine says Papa Piccone will provide three barrels of wine and a bunch of jugs for the wedding reception [A:105]. The future is imagined as emerging from a jug of smoky glass or rock crystal whose curvature distorts the forms seen inside it [MTM B10:297].","citations":["A:222","A:105","MTM B10:297"],"tags":["Alcohol","Literature","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Juggling","definition":"One of the popular arts included in the narrative’s account of the deliberate degradation of everyday arts by deformed counterparts after the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207-208]. Duffey also juggles names and numbers while alternating between sleep and waking before his encounter with Charlotte Mullens in a strange wasteland [MTM B3:85].","citations":["MTM B6:207-208","MTM B3:85"],"tags":["Duffey","Dreams","The Devil","Identity"]},{"name":"Juice","definition":"Used for drink, vitality, and animating power. Vincent imagines Finnegan created with a hangover from jungle juice that was never drunk [A:57]. Torpedo Juice is one of the Anopheles Network’s comic products [A:46], and Hans is powerful when “the juice” is in him [A:68]. The young Duffey calls the lightning in his talisman sticks “fire and juice” [MTM B1:11]. The word returns in the mingled fluids released when the thunder colt destroys the Finnegan effigy [MTM B11:351].","citations":["A:57","A:46","A:68","MTM B1:11","MTM B11:351"],"tags":["Alcohol","Creation","Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Juju Joy Fabrique","definition":"Don Barnaby’s term for the manufacture of joy pills. He likens Madame Le Mourcheor’s reforms to spring entering its musty halls: she supposedly discards tainted additives and returns to special sawdust, honey, and black-cat urine [D:248-249].","citations":["D:248-249"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Juju Joy Pills (Madame Le Mourcheor's Old Original)","definition":"See Joy Pills. Don Barnaby names Madame Le Mourcheor’s Old Original Juju Joy Pills as the exceptional variety made in New Orleans [D:249].","citations":["D:249"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","New Orleans","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Julep Stein","definition":"Earlier heading reversed the drink’s name and changed its printed spelling. See <em>Stein Julip</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Alcohol"]},{"name":"Julien Hebert","definition":"One of Henry Salvatore’s maternal uncles, listed with the other Hebert uncles in the account of Henry’s family [A:122-123].","citations":["A:122-123"],"tags":["Family","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Juliette","definition":"A romantic comparison applied to Theresa Piccone, described as half Juliette and half urchin as Patrick Stranahan considers Vincent’s approaching marriage [A:104]. The likely allusion is to Juliet Capulet of Shakespeare’s Romeo and Juliet, although the novel does not identify the play here.","citations":["A:104"],"tags":["Literature","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Jumps (Three big)","definition":"Judley Peacock's explanation, quoted in Tony Apostolo's <em>Chicago Tribune</em> report, of how the mule reached the traffic island: one jump over the fence and two more to the island. Peacock concedes that he initially thought the huge man had lifted the mule, but rejects that explanation [MTM B4:132-133].","citations":["MTM B4:132-133"],"tags":["Giants & Titans","Journalism"]},{"name":"June","definition":"Month of Giovanni A. Solli's (Finnegan's) birth, June 1, 1919 [A:173] [MTM B3:76]. In his verse, sea birds return to Dutch Harbor in June [A:171]. Charlotte Garfield visits New Orleans in June wearing her extravagant fur stole [MTM B8:258].","citations":["A:173","MTM B3:76","A:171","MTM B8:258"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Jung Hotel","definition":"New Orleans hotel where Finnegan, Henry, Duffey, Dotty, and Mary Schaeffer check in on a Sunday evening after leaving Vincent Stranahan's wedding reception. Dotty jokes that they are all Jungian archetypes [A:114-115]. <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> dates their departure to the last Saturday of May 1946 [MTM B5:184].","citations":["A:114-115","MTM B5:184"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Jungle","definition":"The soldiers' tropical surroundings. Henry says that he is better in the jungle than in town, and he and Casey walk into it to swim [A:38] [A:42]. Later islands have less dense jungle and occasional meadows [A:50]. The audience excuses its enthusiasm for Tania by saying that it has been in the jungle too long [A:53]. The companions cross jungle and fields, find horses, and sleep in a pasture on its edge [A:68-69]. Finnegan and Marie Courtois appear on a jungle-crowded beach although they should be aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em> in mid-Atlantic [D:92]. The steep jungle Duffey climbs is a metaphorical landscape inside the Majesty's mind [MTM B8:241].","citations":["A:38","A:42","A:50","A:53","A:68-69","D:92","MTM B8:241"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","Islands","Finnegan","Duffey"]},{"name":"Jungle juice","definition":"Liquor in Vincent's thought experiment about a newly created world: Finnegan could be created with a built-in hangover although neither the previous night's jungle juice nor that night had existed [A:57]. The Sergeant and the Malayan boy drink it from a gourd during their visionary travels; the text explicitly says that they are no longer in the ward or even on that island [A:88].","citations":["A:57","A:88"],"tags":["Alcohol","Creation","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Junk pile","definition":"Pile of old equipment in Duffey's establishment. Bagby points out padded leather straps and spins a joke about the former proprietor using them to hang sleeping lodgers from hooks. Other drinkers offer rival practical explanations: straps for Greely Pack Saddles or California Bucking Rolls [MTM B2:44-45].","citations":["MTM B2:44-45"],"tags":["Duffey","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Justice Department agents","definition":"Officials whom Mr. X visits, along with congressmen, at midnight, speaking in low, confidential earnestness [A:137].","citations":["A:137"],"tags":["Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Justin","definition":"Person whom Mary Virginia credits with news that the Jazz Museum has enough new material to take a week to see and hear [MTM B11:310].","citations":["MTM B11:310"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Music"]},{"name":"Justinian","definition":"Byzantine emperor Justinian I (482-565), remembered for his codification of Roman law. In an unlabeled first-person fragment in “Promontory Goats,” Justinian's skull houses a snake oracle: the snake emerges through the right eye socket for yes and the left for no, giving judgments to chieftains [PG:545].","citations":["PG:545"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Prophecy & Visions","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Juvenilia","definition":"Designation attached to Casey's early verse pamphlet, <em>Ballads of Kasmir Szymanski</em>, privately printed by <em>The Crock</em>, Chicago. Finnegan reads it and praises Casey's earlier poetry [A:153].","citations":["A:153"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"K. B. Lager","definition":"Beer named with Toohey's and Tooth's in the opening pub sequence, where they are described as the same beers the boys had in Paradise [A:2].","citations":["A:2"],"tags":["Alcohol","Heaven & Hell"]},{"name":"K.C.s","definition":"Unexpanded initials for one group among the many guests and acquaintances who visit Patrick Stranahan's sociable household [A:104].","citations":["A:104"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","Minor Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Kabouter","definition":"In Absalom Stein's explanation, an interior goblin, crankiness, complex, fixation, or psychological stumbling-stool. Stein says that he and Casey traded one kabouter each, rather than whole souls; each consequently knows some of the other's private secrets. Stein regards the kabouters as immaterial persons, while acknowledging the alternative description as crankinesses [PG:537-538].","citations":["PG:537-538"],"tags":["Identity","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Kalevala","definition":"Finnish epic compiled by Elias Lönnrot, published in an initial form in 1835 and an expanded form in 1849. Brannagan promises Finnegan that the part of his proposed theory, the Procession of the Epics, concerning the <em>Kalevala</em> will make his hair stand on end [D:210].","citations":["D:210"],"tags":["Other Mythologies","Literature"]},{"name":"Kandarsky","definition":"Composer of the <em>Painted Thunder Suite</em>, part of the prelude music at the Decatur Street Opera House [MTM B11:348]. The name may echo the painter Vasily Kandinsky (1866-1944), though the passage makes no explicit identification.","citations":["MTM B11:348"],"tags":["New Orleans","Music","Visual Arts","Theater"]},{"name":"Kandi","definition":"Place named among the destinations of eternal summer in Finnegan's verse, alongside Roi, Rangoon, Kuri, and Cai [A:171].","citations":["A:171"],"tags":["Literature","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Kangaroo","definition":"Animal imitated by Mabel the bear in Hans's hypnotic stage act: at his command, she bounces about the stage like a kangaroo [A:219].","citations":["A:219"],"tags":["Theater"]},{"name":"Kansas City","definition":"Missouri city included in one disputed account of Duffey's childhood with kindred between the ages of five and twelve [MTM B1:7]. Rockhurst is one possible location of his composite boarding-school education [MTM B1:24]; the actual school was chartered in 1910 and opened classes in 1914. Critics of Duffey's chronology claim that he was still at school in Omaha or Kansas City when he supposedly ran his St. Louis business, but Mary Louise and Bagby defend those business years [MTM B2:71]. Kansas City is also among the cities he revisits after returning in 1923 [MTM B2:73].","citations":["MTM B1:7","MTM B1:24","MTM B2:71","MTM B2:73"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Kaoline affiliate","definition":"Secret material among Askandanakandrian's seven sovereign aids for rehabilitating nervous merchandise. He lists it with an acid, lye, formaldehyde, ammonia, amyl acetate, and an unnamed lesser gum; when reducing his choices to four, he calls the corresponding material clay [A:257].","citations":["A:257"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Occupations"]},{"name":"Kapteyn's Star","definition":"One of the stars used in Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni's astronomical instruction. He can give a star's right ascension for any date, and teaches Hans to do so for frequently requested stars, including Kapteyn's Star, Alpha Centauri, Procyon, CC624, Rigel, Vega, and Betelgeuse [A:211] [A:214].","citations":["A:211","A:214"],"tags":[]},{"name":"Kara Sea","definition":"Arctic sea off Russia, named among the many seas sailed by Melchisedech and the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Karachi","definition":"City where Amoy, in Ti's freely translated story of his earlier life as a red-winged blackbird, joins jewel thieves after reaching his majority and steals gems from shops [A:80].","citations":["A:80"],"tags":["Crime","Folklore"]},{"name":"Karkik Kariki","definition":"Short form of <em>To Karkik Kariki</em>, one of the clandestine student publications descended from Casey's Crocks. Its later continuation adds repeated allusions to an attributed ancient Greek sexual trait; the narrator expressly distinguishes such developments from Casey's original intentions [A:167-168].","citations":["A:167-168"],"tags":["Literature","Journalism","Greece"]},{"name":"Karl Marx Stadt","definition":"Name used for Chemnitz in East Germany from 1953 to 1990. The novel places an <em>Argo</em> episode there near the upper Mulde: Duffey, Brannagan, and Gorshok confront Ralph Rolfe over his dangerous new logic, tentatively called <em>I wake up forgetting</em>, and destroy its written formulation [MTM B13:427-428].","citations":["MTM B13:427-428"],"tags":["Germany","Ships","Duffey","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Kasimir, Prince","definition":"Prince Casimir of Poland, identified in Duffey's account as an earlier manifestation of Casey Szymansky and as Saint Casimir. Duffey describes his aid to the poor, a vow of chastity frustrating King Casimir IV's succession plans, and an apparent death at twenty-three that is really a withdrawal from the world [PG:536]. The historical saint lived from 1458 to 1484 and died at twenty-five, so Duffey's age and designation of the period as the High Middle Ages belong to the novel's account. In a prophecy read aloud by Teresa, Prince Casimir is the second of three listed cargoes that a ghostly Duffey will carry on the <em>Argo</em>; he will be landed near Armageddon to reign temporarily as Anti-Christ. Duffey says he has no knowledge of these predicted acts [MTM B12:388].","citations":["PG:536","MTM B12:388"],"tags":["Saints","Apocalyptic","Ships","Eastern Europe","Duffey","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Kasmir-Stein (Der)","definition":"Hybrid identity or essence produced by the contact between Casey Szymansky and Absalom Stein after their exchange of an old soul. The narrator calls it an ancient, remote jewel of India and says that it is neither Casey nor Stein, but an abiding spark struck from their contact [MTM B5:183].","citations":["MTM B5:183"],"tags":["Identity"]},{"name":"Kate's Neanderthal Bar","definition":"Waterfront bar in Biloxi that Duffey thinks is almost always for sale. Eva wants to buy it, rename it Eva's Neanderthal Bar, and attract others of her Neo-Neanderthal kind [MTM B13:407]. She asks for a $14,000 down payment. Duffey produces 280 fifty-dollar gold pieces, but Eva questions their plausible use; Brannagan's replacement banknotes all have the same serial number. Gertrude finally offers to fetch her checkbook and arrange matters herself [MTM B13:414-415].","citations":["MTM B13:407","MTM B13:414-415"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Duffey","Money & Trade","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Kathisate","definition":"Greek invitation to sit down, offered by the woman at a farmhouse when Finnegan and Anastasia stop for water on their way to Anastasia's grandmother. They have no time to stay [D:124].","citations":["D:124"],"tags":["Greece","Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Katie","definition":"Waitress at the Lotus Eaters who keeps Hans and Marie's glasses filled during their apparently timeless conversation. Marie tells Katie that she is engaged, and Katie replies that even a blind woman could see it [A:28-29].","citations":["A:28-29"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Women Characters","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Katt, Tommy","definition":"Tough policeman, husband of Hildegarde Katt, in the port town of the Old Wooden Ship [D:229-230]. He encounters Finnegan after the assault on Art Emery. After Finnegan's violent attack on the Pirate Hotel, Katt sees the apparent werecat become the bleeding Finnegan and drives him to the dock in time to escape by boat [D:230-232].","citations":["D:229-230","D:230-232"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Finnegan","Crime","Shapeshifting"]},{"name":"Kava sunan ka?","definition":"Question Joseph, described as a Haussa man, asks Finnegan in Freetown. Finnegan understands it as asking his name and replies that he is Finnegan [D:93]. Joseph then identifies his trade as diamond smuggling [D:94].","citations":["D:93","D:94"],"tags":["Africa","Finnegan","Crime","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Kazuko, Niku","definition":"Double of the dead Xavier Cardinal Runosake of Kobe. The initial list calls Niku Kazuko probably of Honolulu; the later passenger account simply calls him of Honolulu [MB:579] [MB:592].","citations":["MB:579","MB:592"],"tags":["Identity","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Kea","definition":"Greek island. In Brannagan's account, shops on Kea, Siros, and Tinos sell mermaids' hair from Naxos, while an ancient reluctance prevents its sale on Naxos itself [D:207].","citations":["D:207"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Islands","Greece"]},{"name":"Keadin","definition":"One of the goat-man's seven youngest kids, listed fourth among Bownee, Luin, Mairt, Keadin, Ordin, Haoine, and Sahirn. The goat-man gives these kids names for the days of the week [A:194]; Keadin appears to represent Irish <em>Céadaoin</em>, Wednesday.","citations":["A:194"],"tags":["Folklore","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Keegan, Beth","definition":"Elizabeth Keegan, later Beth Erlenbaum, Mary Louise Byrne's close friend and the teasing handmaid she introduces to Duffey. Beth kisses him at their first meeting and says she will keep him. Small, black-haired, and ivory-skinned, with tiny feet and waist and lavish curves, she strikes him as an impossible living ivory statue, cold and strong to the touch. Her costume, voice, and walk are theatrical performances, and her laughter exposes the joke without ending his infatuation or confusion [MTM B2:56-57]. She attends college with Mary Louise and joins Duffey's youthful St. Louis outings [MTM B2:58]. Her mock love-making draws on plays and comic magazines; the narrative likens her to Etruscan and Cretan statuary [MTM B2:60]. After Duffey's return, she is married, has two daughters, and writes and performs comic skits at the Star and Garter. She is godmother to Piccone's infant daughter. The narrator calls Beth ordinary, but says Duffey never realizes this [MTM B2:74]. Later she and her husband join Duffey and the Bagbys at Rounders'; she describes his Animated Marvels as brighter, more colorful versions of him multiplied ten or eleven times [MTM B5:154-155].","citations":["MTM B2:56-57","MTM B2:58","MTM B2:60","MTM B2:74","MTM B5:154-155"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Theater","Family","Identity","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Keel","definition":"Structural backbone along the bottom of a vessel. Duffey says the <em>Argo</em>'s keel was laid at Ragusa, now Dubrovnik, and that her hull was built of oak from the Dinaric Alps, including pieces of a talking oak. The surrounding account explicitly claims symbolic rather than ordinary historical accuracy [MTM B13:412].","citations":["MTM B13:412"],"tags":["Ships","Argonautica"]},{"name":"Kelly pool","definition":"Rotation-style pocket-billiards game. Duffey and seven companions play it and rotation in the soundproof attic billiard room after escaping a formal school dance [MTM B1:33]. Finnegan also invites an agitated Papa Devil to a game in Tangier, but Papa Devil is in no mood for it [D:103].","citations":["MTM B1:33","D:103"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Kelly's Steak House","definition":"St. Louis restaurant frequented during Duffey's youthful social life with Mary Louise Byrne, Beth Keegan, Charley Murray, Edward Ranwick, and Philip Manford. Its adjoining music bar is a later-night destination [MTM B2:58-59]. Kelly's is also a stop on the farewell round before Duffey's Seven Lost Years [MTM B2:69].","citations":["MTM B2:58-59","MTM B2:69"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Duffey","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Kellys (Family)","definition":"Family named among Duffey's alleged relatives in one account of his childhood. Duffey disputes the older relative's account and the authenticity of this extensive kindred [MTM B1:7].","citations":["MTM B1:7"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Kelp syrup","definition":"Accompaniment Duffey requests for a chlamydomonas, a green alga, served as the fruit course of his extraordinary meal with Leonard Archive, Oliver Greenflag, and Harry A. Kincaid. The visit occurs during an <em>Argo</em> expedition into a future Louisiana swamp [MTM B13:416-417].","citations":["MTM B13:416-417"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Louisiana","Duffey"]},{"name":"Kenealy, Cletus","definition":"One of Duffey's musical friends during the North Chicago golden age of 1925-1935. The group encompasses opera, ragtime, Chicago-hot music, and string bands, without assigning Kenealy an individual specialty [MTM B3:98-99]. He also attends Duffey's late gathering of old friends [MTM B12:383].","citations":["MTM B3:98-99","MTM B12:383"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Music","Duffey"]},{"name":"Kennedys (Family)","definition":"Family included in the older relative's list of Duffey's kindred in one version of his childhood, an account whose authenticity Duffey contests [MTM B1:7].","citations":["MTM B1:7"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Kephos Stone","definition":"Name linking Absalom Stein to Kephos in Cleo Mahoney’s play about Melchisedech. Duffey recognizes Stein as “Kephos of the blooming nose” and seeks his advice; the narrator connects Kephos with stone and Stein [MTM B9:265-266].","citations":["MTM B9:265-266"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words","Theater","Identity"]},{"name":"Kerosene lamp","definition":"Light used, along with candles, for Duffey and Rattigan's late-night school sales after the gas is shut off [MTM B1:26]. Charley Murray later proposes kerosene or compressed-gas lighting for Duffey's balloon, the <em>Argo Twelve and a Half</em>. Duffey wins the argument for electric lighting by citing explosions on balloons using other lights [MTM B2:64-65].","citations":["MTM B1:26","MTM B2:64-65"],"tags":["Duffey","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Kerygmies","definition":"Word in the verse fragment, 'Before (in catch-words and kerygmies) / Falsetto Chorus raised its plaint.' The glossary's proposed location, A:9, does not match the available text; the primary-source location remains unverified.","citations":[],"tags":["Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Kettle (contributions)","definition":"Small kettle for contributions to Duffey's perpetual soup crock. Dotty rings a bell to attract people, and late-night roisterers sometimes put in four bits or a dollar for a bowl [A:133-134].","citations":["A:133-134"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Money & Trade","Duffey"]},{"name":"Kettle drums","definition":"Equipment named with barbells in the mail-order physical training by which John William Schultz seeks to make his son John, or Hans, as strong as himself [A:208].","citations":["A:208"],"tags":["Family"]},{"name":"Key (grave key)","definition":"Powerful information in a document buried with Ifreann, father of the two Papadiabolous brothers, in the grave where Papadiabolous I is later also buried. According to Mr. X, the father was buried in 1930; Seaworthy subsequently killed Papadiabolous I while they were reopening that grave to seek the information [D:285] [D:288]. X calls the document a key and plans to retrieve it so that no single possessor has its dangerous advantage. He expects Seaworthy to intercept him that night [D:290].","citations":["D:285","D:288","D:290"],"tags":["Crime","The Devil","The Dead"]},{"name":"Key (latch-key)","definition":"Figurative key to Hell in the narrator's description of Marie Courtois aboard ship, before identifying her as Seaworthy's seagoing secretary. It marks the sinister side of a woman who remains an attractive companion [D:32].","citations":["D:32"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Women Characters","Ships"]},{"name":"Key (safety deposit box key)","definition":"Key to the safe-deposit box holding Aga Gonof's stash. Duffey obtains its mental image, reads its stock number and the code for its notches, kerfs, and dips with his inner eye, and writes down the information. Charlotte Mullens goes to have the key made while they still await the box's location and number [MTM B3:84].","citations":["MTM B3:84"],"tags":["Duffey","Crime","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Keyboard (played by time-table)","definition":"Casey's metaphor for the common direction of propagandistic public voices. He tells Finnegan that phrases first coined within his movement are echoed on schedule by apparently independent cultural and public figures, all played by the same keyboard [A:185-186].","citations":["A:185-186"],"tags":["Journalism","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Khamis Mushait","definition":"City in southwestern Saudi Arabia. Margaret Stone tells her purported Jebel Shammar enemies that New Orleans is a truce city like Khamis Mushait, though she suspects that the three alleged sect members are Tulane students joking with her [MTM B7:225].","citations":["MTM B7:225"],"tags":["New Orleans","Middle East","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Khanate of the Golden Horde","definition":"Mongol successor state of the thirteenth to early sixteenth centuries, ending in 1502. In Mr. X's anachronistic account of the Devil's imprisonment in 946 or 947, Tmutarakan is an advance host of the Golden Horde and shares the Crimean peninsula with the Kingdom of Kiev [MTM B6:204].","citations":["MTM B6:204"],"tags":["The Devil","History & Memory","Eastern Europe"]},{"name":"Kiboshes","definition":"Amenities or stalls in the suddenly present Bayougoula Park, listed alongside gazebos, kiosks, taverns, and tabernacles [MTM B11:331-332]. Margaret Stone buys crackers at one of the kiboshes to feed a dying dragon [MTM B11:334]. The word may play on 'kiosks,' but the structures' form is not specified.","citations":["MTM B11:331-332","MTM B11:334"],"tags":["New Orleans","Shapeshifting","Food & Dining","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Kid (little)","definition":"A term applied to different people. A colonel calls Sergeant Finnegan a kid when dismissing a captain's complaint that Finnegan has threatened to meet him in Hell [A:74]. Mr. X's letter calls the women 'kids' and says that they have the Church and are justified [A:265]. In the separate 'Great Day' episode, Margaret Stone pushes down an outside wall and kills a little kid on the sidewalk [GD:493].","citations":["A:74","A:265","GD:493"],"tags":["Finnegan","Soldiers","Catholic Church","Death & Mortality","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Kidd (Captain Kidd)","definition":"Pirate whose name gives supposed authenticity to treasure maps forged by Angela Cosquin's father [D:259-260]. Charlotte Mullens boasts that she has stolen and reburied more loot than Kidd or Blackbeard ever saw [MTM B3:93].","citations":["D:259-260","MTM B3:93"],"tags":["Crime","Money & Trade","Maritime Trade"]},{"name":"Kidneys","definition":"Organs used in the life-preserving broth in Don Lewis's Big House story; the daughter threatens to feast on Ali's kidneys [D:48-49]. One of the three slant-faced killers eats Duffey's kidneys after they strip the flesh from his bones. Although the poisoned-spring scene is unreal, the narrative explicitly calls his death real [MTM B13:441-442]. Among the murder-contest proposals in 'More Than Melchisedech,' fictional directional infra-red cookers would destroy a victim's kidneys or other organs without an outer mark [MB:581].","citations":["D:48-49","MTM B13:441-442","MB:581"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Crime","Duffey","Death & Mortality","Immortality"]},{"name":"Kiev (Kingdom of)","definition":"Kyivan Rus', the medieval state centered on Kyiv, conventionally dated from the late ninth century to the Mongol conquest in the thirteenth. Mr. X says that the Kingdom of Kiev shared the Crimean peninsula with Tmutarakan when the Devil was imprisoned there in 946 or 947 [MTM B6:204]. This territorial account belongs to his narrative of the imprisonment.","citations":["MTM B6:204"],"tags":["Eastern Europe","History & Memory","The Devil"]},{"name":"Killers (hired)","definition":"Men who pursue Don Lewis in Tangier under Seaworthy's threat. Don considers Art, Harry, Papa Devil, and even other shipmates as possible killers, but Marie Courtois kills him [D:106] [D:112-113]. Doppio di Pinne, called Dopey, belongs to the related plot to substitute a double for Finnegan. An early account describes him as killed in Finnegan's place [D:51], but his body is not found; later accounts leave his disappearance, possible killing, and possible absorption into Finnegan unresolved [D:64] [D:121-122]. These passages do not establish him as one of Don Lewis's pursuers.","citations":["D:106","D:112-113","D:51","D:64","D:121-122"],"tags":["Africa","Finnegan","Crime","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Kincaid, Harry A. (Honeybucket)","definition":"Old Wooden Ship patron, called Harold A. (Honeybucket) Kincaid in its roster and Harry A. Kincaid in his anecdote. He seeks to sell seventy-five billion dollars of stock for electrically harvesting plankton as livestock feed; the narrator reveals that he has never tried it and knows nothing of plankton's appearance. His one real camel remains behind the Ship after a failed amusement ride [D:216] [D:218-219].\n\nIn futuristic Louisiana, Harry joins Leonard Archive and Oliver Greenflag in entertaining Duffey and Brannagan. Their frequency technology gathers food, drink, chemicals, and creatures from the water; Honeybucket operates the dials and electrodes for gar and Blooper Fish, makes Foraminifera Julips, and summons three twenty-one-year-old women in a rowboat [MTM B13:417-419]. Archive credits laziness for their discoveries, while Greenflag calls Puff Fish a moral problem. Duffey destroys the Puff Fish frequency himself; Honeybucket thinks the equipment can be repaired, then cannot recall what the missing frequency attracted [MTM B13:420-421].","citations":["D:216","D:218-219","MTM B13:417-419","MTM B13:420-421"],"tags":["Fishing","Louisiana","Duffey","Food & Dining","Money & Trade","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Kinetic Intuition Indicators","definition":"Instruments screening people entering the Decatur Street Opera House during the Great Day events. They flag about twelve people in a thousand for a further test in which washing in human ashes produces flames if the subject is human [MTM B11:346-347]. The same passage also calls an instrument a 'Kinetic Intention Indicator.'","citations":["MTM B11:346-347"],"tags":["New Orleans","Theater","Apocalyptic","Identity"]},{"name":"King","definition":"Royal title recurring in several forms. Duffey is the magical Boy King, with a childhood lasting thousands of years, and the King of Salem [MTM B1:15] [MTM B1:17] [MTM B5:166]. His later recollections repeat both the ancient kingship and his disguised kingship in Iowa [MTM B13:464] [MTM B13:469]. Genesis supplies the priest-king of Salem, and Hebrews the titles King of Justice and King of Peace [MTM B2:38] [MTM B6:185].\n\nOther kings include the Balbo Mad Kings Gaetano I-VI of San Simeon, whose expected successor Gaetano VII becomes a churchman instead [MB:558-559]; King Casimir IV, father of the prince identified with Casey in Duffey's commentary [PG:536]; the former royal owner of Duffey's Spanish ash receptacle [MTM B1:6] [MTM B13:442]; King Richard of England, said to have recaptured the <em>Argo</em>, and the other English king for whom Robert of Namur sailed it [MTM B13:404]. Amraphel of Shinar, Arioch of Ellasar, and Thadal of the Nations follow Chodorlahomor of Elam [MTM B7:227-228]. Henri Salvatore's letter says that all are kings in the Kingdom and invokes the holy Leper King [MTM B5:179-180].","citations":["MTM B1:15","MTM B1:17","MTM B5:166","MTM B13:464","MTM B13:469","MTM B2:38","MTM B6:185","MB:558-559","PG:536","MTM B1:6","MTM B13:442","MTM B13:404","MTM B7:227-228","MTM B5:179-180"],"tags":["Old Testament","New Testament","Magic & Sorcery","Ships","Spain","Duffey","Social Class","Identity"]},{"name":"King of Justice","definition":"Biblical title of Melchisedech in the Hebrews epigraph. It gives the meaning of his name as King of Justice, before explaining his further title, King of Salem, as King of Peace [MTM B6:185].","citations":["MTM B6:185"],"tags":["Scripture","New Testament","Duffey"]},{"name":"King of Peace","definition":"Title explained in the Hebrews epigraph as the meaning of Melchisedech's kingship of Salem [MTM B6:185]. Salem is traditionally identified with Jerusalem.","citations":["MTM B6:185"],"tags":["Scripture","New Testament","Middle East","Duffey"]},{"name":"King of Salem","definition":"Title of the biblical Melchisedech in the Genesis epigraph [MTM B2:38]. Duffey claims it together with his identities as Magus and Boy King, joining kingship to his ministry of bread and wine [MTM B5:166]. He signs a letter to Absalom Stein as the fifty-ninth king of the dynasty and, when questioned, says that he does not know why he wrote it but is sure he had fifty-eight predecessors [PS:478].","citations":["MTM B2:38","MTM B5:166","PS:478"],"tags":["Scripture","Duffey","Identity","Time & Eternity","Social Class"]},{"name":"King Stephen of Hungary","definition":"Saint Stephen I of Hungary (c. 975-1038). Duffey names him and Conrad the Second of the Germanies as the newest rulers attending the Kings' Conclaves while he still took part [MTM B5:166].","citations":["MTM B5:166"],"tags":["Saints","Historical Figures","Eastern Europe","Duffey","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"King's Cross","definition":"District on Finnegan and Vincent's pub-visiting route, followed by Darlinghurst and the descent to Down-Town [A:2].","citations":["A:2"],"tags":["Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","Australia"]},{"name":"King's Ship","definition":"One name, alongside Gold Ship and Shimmering Ship, for what the narrator calls an almost universal boys' dream. Such dreams are presented as simultaneous happenings with a basis in reality [MTM B13:436].","citations":["MTM B13:436"],"tags":["Ships","Dreams","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Kingdom Come","definition":"An idiom for fatal destruction in the account of balloonists blown up while using non-electric lights; Duffey's <em>Argo Twelve and a Half</em> instead uses electric lights [MTM B2:64-65]. The phrase also names the divine kingdom demanded in the 'Sonnet to Obdurate God,' whose speaker insists that God hasten and clarify it [PG:533-534].","citations":["MTM B2:64-65","PG:533-534"],"tags":["Ships","Duffey","Literature","Theology"]},{"name":"Kingdom of Colchis","definition":"Ancient region on the eastern Black Sea, associated in Greek myth with Medea and the Golden Fleece. Henri Cardinal Salvatore says that the <em>Argo</em> sails under the Kingdom of Colchis's flag when discussing the ship's continuing voyages and the intermittent memory its people retain ashore [MTM B12:386].","citations":["MTM B12:386"],"tags":["Argonautica","Greek Mythology","Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Kingdom of God","definition":"The divine Kingdom anticipated beyond ordinary mortal life. Sebastian tells Duffey that their next meeting will be in the Kingdom, rather than in unsanctified flesh [MTM B7:216]. After Finnegan refuses a last drink with his intended executioner, saying that he will drink it new, Niccolo wonders whether drink is available in the Kingdom [A:279].","citations":["MTM B7:216","A:279"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Theology","Duffey","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Kingdoms","definition":"Duffey tells Casey that those trying to corrupt the <em>Crock</em> have offered him the world's riches and all its kingdoms, likening their offer to Satan's [A:39-40]. During a solemn three-way handshake with Henry and Finnegan, Hans quotes the line 'where three men stand together are kingdoms less by three' [A:69].","citations":["A:39-40","A:69"],"tags":["The Devil","Duffey","Finnegan","Literature","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Kings' Conclaves","definition":"Conclaves that Duffey claims to have attended as the Boy King and King of Salem. He names Solomon, Saul, Ptolemy, Chandragupta, Nebuchadnezzar, Hsien, and Barbarossa, then calls Stephen of Hungary and Conrad the Second of the Germanies the newest attendees he remembers [MTM B5:166]. The claim occurs while Charley Murray questions how much Duffey has been drinking.","citations":["MTM B5:166"],"tags":["Old Testament","Historical Figures","Duffey","Clubs & Societies","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Kipling, Rudyard (1865--1936)","definition":"British journalist, poet, and novelist (1865-1936), author of The Jungle Book (1894), Kim (1901), and 'If-' (1910), and winner of the 1907 Nobel Prize in Literature. August Koch invokes Kipling's devil asking 'Is it art?' while discussing Duffey's Seven Christs [MTM B3:103]. The allusion is to 'The Conundrum of the Workshops' (1890), with its recurring devil's challenge to human creation.","citations":["MTM B3:103"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Literary Figures","Literature","Journalism","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Kiriwinans","definition":"Island people who, in Archipelago's account, help build the Papuans' lodges. The narrator says that they understand their dependence on the Papuans' vast unconscious and would vanish if the Papuans ceased to sustain them there [A:37].","citations":["A:37"],"tags":["Islands","Metaphysics","Creation"]},{"name":"Kirol Cardinal Gabrailovitch","definition":"Cardinal of Zagreb, the second of the thirteen Princes in Hiding killed in John Mogul's campaign [MB:578]. His double is Mihail Majic of Trieste, who later reaches Mooring-Stone after a fifty-mile swim [MB:579] [MB:595]. Finnegan's series depicting the deaths of thirteen cardinals includes the 'Hanging of Cardinal Gabrailovitch' [MTM B13:452].","citations":["MB:578","MB:579","MB:595","MTM B13:452"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Finnegan","Visual Arts","Crime","Death & Mortality","Identity"]},{"name":"Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni, Professor","definition":"Professor and carnival operator, described as the smartest man in the world and the last man who knew everything. The narrative places his birth about 1860 in Bavaria but immediately allows a different place or time; in spring 1930 he is about seventy, give or take a decade [A:211]. He knew Hans Schultz’s parents before their marriage and separately paid for their passages to America. Irene calls him a phoney of an older, nobler school and reports his maxim that dignity belongs to middling minds [A:211-212].\n\nHans leaves home with him in a truck resembling a medicine-show wagon, and they travel to Wisconsin Rapids to join the carnival. He teaches rapid calculation and prodigious memory as tools he calls Cerebral Athleticism, insisting that they are not thought itself. He condemns chosen dullness and urges a Sabbath for the mind, calling humanity morning dew but also manna [A:212]. His teachings include the trees of knowledge, an expanding Heaven, the Tree of Language and a literal Babel account, and rapid astronomical calculations. He says a properly equipped learner requires at least a month to learn a language [A:213]. He describes material and spiritual reality as aspects of one universe, the visible cosmos as the reverse of Heaven’s tapestry, and re-entrant time as an approach to eternity [A:212-215]. Admitting that he is a charlatan, he says he has returned to the Church and that the Father’s House has room even for charlatans [A:215].\n\nHe rents and leases carnival equipment and performs as a mind-reader, memory expert, hypnotist, magician, and answer man. He retains strength but has lost much agility and endurance [A:215-216]. He claims to have known Elena Angelino’s great-grandparents and insists that her great-grandfather was a bear [A:218]. His teaching distinguishes learning that asks why from information that asks what. By the spring of 1935 or 1936, when Hans leaves, he is visibly slowing down [A:220]. His carnival circle includes Betty Hochstapler, Angelo Angelino, and the performing bear Mabel [A:216-219].","citations":["A:211","A:211-212","A:212","A:213","A:212-215","A:215","A:215-216","A:218","A:220","A:216-219"],"tags":["Carnival","Magic & Sorcery","Germany","Theology","Language & Words","Time & Eternity","Minor Characters","Occupations","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Kissimee","definition":"Kissimmee, the central Florida city, spelled 'Kissimee' in the novel. Finnegan, Doll Delancy, and X include it among their evening drives through the citrus and scrub, along with Orlando, Pine Castle, Apopka, and sometimes Daytona [D:183].","citations":["D:183"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Kit (how-to-build-it kit)","definition":"Zabotski's construction instructions, copied from an ancient rock-drawing whose language he believes is Hazh-Khazh. He deciphers the writing while building, initially unsure whether the result is a barn or castle [MTM B9:272]. The accompanying scenario specifies six younger people in three married couples; Widow Waldo insists the contraption is a ship, castle, anti-time machine, and shoe [MTM B9:275]. In the separate 'Great Day' account, kits supply an unspecified substitute for vehicle wheels [GD:489].","citations":["MTM B9:272","MTM B9:275","GD:489"],"tags":["Time & Eternity","Language & Words","Ships","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Kit (love-as-catch-word kit)","definition":"Phrase in the verse fragment 'Nor used the love-as-catch-word kit / A multitude of sins to cover.' The glossary's proposed location, A:9, does not match the available text; the primary-source location remains unverified.","citations":[],"tags":["Literature","Language & Words","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Kit-fox","definition":"A small North American fox and Duffey's totem animal. When Duffey is about eleven, a sorrel-haired, grinning fox resembling him warns him of the three slant-faced boys. It attacks one low while Duffey bowls him over, enabling escape from the ambush; Duffey is badly knifed and nearly bleeds to death. The narrator calls the fox providential [MTM B1:19-20].","citations":["MTM B1:19-20"],"tags":["Duffey","Combat","Death & Mortality","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Kithara (nine-stringed)","definition":"Nine-stringed instrument played by Shinar in the Brimstone Combo, in Trinali's poem about the old saloon near Iniquity Square [A:235].","citations":["A:235"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Music","Literature"]},{"name":"Kithara Shinar","definition":"Earlier heading joined the instrument to the name of its player, Shinar. See <em>Kithara (nine-stringed)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Music","Literature"]},{"name":"Kitty (card game)","definition":"Earlier heading confused the collection of tips after a musical performance with a card game. See <em>Kitty (tip collection)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Music","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Kitty (tip collection)","definition":"Collection for tips after Falo and Nauti's music at their café: they say they will pass the kitty after playing. The same scene also includes card play, but 'kitty' here refers to the collection [A:65].","citations":["A:65"],"tags":["Music","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Kiunker (Georgia)","definition":"Printed variant of the car’s name. See <em>Klunker (car)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Finnegan","Crime"]},{"name":"Klunker (car)","definition":"The older second car with a Georgia license plate, acquired so that X, Doll, and Finnegan can escape in two directions if necessary [D:184]. Finnegan leaves in it before dawn after X’s briefing, followed half an hour later by a leftfooted killer and his companion [D:193]. The book calls it both Klunker and Kiunker. After his pursuers repeatedly shoot at him, Finnegan drives it into a ditch and fires back, then continues in the damaged car. In the next town he parks it at the police station to delay pursuit before finding refuge with Brannagan and Gertrude [D:197-199].","citations":["D:184","D:193","D:197-199"],"tags":["Finnegan","Crime","Combat"]},{"name":"Knacker","definition":"A dealer in animal carcasses or worn-out livestock. John Mogul applies the word to his business of killing unwanted people, likening their disposal to rendering carcasses into useful materials [MB:573].","citations":["MB:573"],"tags":["Crime","Occupations","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Knees","definition":"The word links several images. Casey recites a line attributed to Belloc about hacking horses at the knees [A:160]. Trinali seeks cypress knees as craft materials, and Hans later sees her as made of driftwood with cypress knees [A:229] [A:234]. Endymeon Ellenbogen complains figuratively that wolves have eaten his legs up to the knees while describing his failure to make money from popular songs [A:237].","citations":["A:160","A:229","A:234","A:237"],"tags":["Literature","Visual Arts","Music","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Knife","definition":"Weapon appearing in several episodes. Casey's school notebook calls chiv a Romany word for knife [A:162] [A:164]. Ignatius Ti tells of a ward attendant using a flip-knife to frighten a medico pretending to be dead, then of a woman who unknowingly sleeps with the ghost of her knife-murdered husband [A:86-87]. A clipping apparently reporting X's death mentions an old knife blade in the shoulder; Dotty calls the report an antic [A:141]. Finnegan says that a hostile bistro crowd cut a Z in his cheek with switchblades [A:263-264].\n\nThe three slant-faced boys carry knives and nearly kill young Duffey; their later counterparts strip the flesh from his bones in the unreal desert scene, causing a death explicitly described as real [MTM B1:12] [MTM B1:19-20] [MTM B13:441-442]. George Koran reports a knife-brandishing guard at the New Catholic Press Guild meeting [MTM B8:239-240]. A knife hilt protrudes between the shoulders of the dead man identified as Cardinal Runosake at Mogul's door [MB:574].","citations":["A:162","A:164","A:86-87","A:141","A:263-264","MTM B1:12","MTM B1:19-20","MTM B13:441-442","MTM B8:239-240","MB:574"],"tags":["Weapons","Crime","Duffey","Finnegan","Ghosts & Spirits","Death & Mortality","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Knights of Columbus","definition":"Catholic fraternal organization founded in 1882. During his would-be appearance as the world-conquering Leader, Casey wears his father's Knights of Columbus Fourth Degree sword with his other regalia [MTM B4:117].","citations":["MTM B4:117"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Weapons","Clubs & Societies","Family"]},{"name":"Knistjanses (Swede)","definition":"Old Wooden Ship regular listed as Swede Knistjanses [D:215]. He belongs to the five men called Swedes although none is Swedish: collectively they are Norse, Danish, Icelandic, German, and from the Orkneys. The text does not assign those origins individually [D:217].","citations":["D:215","D:217"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Knobs (stone)","definition":"Stone features called merlons on a small Gothic-style hotel. Finnegan, likened to a living gargoyle, perches on one after climbing six stories up the ornamented exterior [D:162].","citations":["D:162"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Knockers (olive)","definition":"Playful name for children in Vincent and Show Boat's response to their wedding Mass's Gradual, which compares children around the table to young olive plants [A:113].","citations":["A:113"],"tags":["Liturgy","Family","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Knowledge (false)","definition":"The pseudodendron of false knowledge and the crooked way, the one forbidden tree in Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni's account to Hans. He contrasts it with the many trees of fruitful knowledge [A:211-212].","citations":["A:211-212"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Theology"]},{"name":"Knowledge (of achievement and arriving)","definition":"One form of fruitful knowledge in Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni's teaching to Hans about the many trees of knowledge: achievement and arriving [A:211-212].","citations":["A:211-212"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Theology"]},{"name":"Knowledge (of awakening and growing)","definition":"One form of fruitful knowledge in Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni's teaching to Hans about the many trees of knowledge: awakening and growing [A:211-212].","citations":["A:211-212"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Theology"]},{"name":"Knowledge (of expanding and seeing)","definition":"One form of fruitful knowledge in Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni's teaching to Hans about the many trees of knowledge: expanding and seeing [A:211-212].","citations":["A:211-212"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Theology"]},{"name":"Knowledge (of love and realization)","definition":"One form of fruitful knowledge in Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni's teaching to Hans about the many trees of knowledge: love and realization [A:211-212].","citations":["A:211-212"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Theology"]},{"name":"Knowledge (Tree of)","definition":"Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni tells Hans that all the trees were trees of knowledge: love and realization, achievement and arriving, awakening and growing, expanding and seeing and unfolding. Only the pseudodendron of false knowledge and the crooked way was forbidden. His image leads into continuing growth from caterpillars through butterflies to stars [A:211-213].","citations":["A:211-213"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Theology","Creation"]},{"name":"Kobe","definition":"Japanese port city associated with Xavier Cardinal Runosake, one of Mogul's thirteen contracted targets [MB:571]. Mogul learns that Runosake has just escaped a Buddhist compound in Kobe; shortly afterward a dying man identified as the cardinal appears at Mogul's Marseille door, and the targets remaining number twelve [MB:572-574].","citations":["MB:571","MB:572-574"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Ecclesiastical","Crime","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Koch's Galleries","definition":"Chicago art galleries run by Lily Koch and her sister Letitia, who keeps her half-interest after marrying Duffey [MTM B3:92]. The galleries stand two blocks from the Duffeys' establishment, down its prosperous street rather than the poorer street on its other side [MTM B3:100].","citations":["MTM B3:92","MTM B3:100"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Duffey","Women Characters","Family","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Koch, August","definition":"Father of Lily and Letitia Koch and husband of Elinore. A neat, imposing, muscular man of integrity and financial competence, he favors the European custom of dowry; Duffey limits the provision for his own marriage to a quarter of August's intended amount, drawing only interest for twenty-five years [MTM B3:101]. August separately offers a million-dollar dowry for a suitable husband for Lily and asks Duffey to help find one [MTM B3:102]. He gives Duffey an additional Christ artwork to stand with the Seven Christs and owns an Argo-named yacht on Lake Michigan [MTM B3:103-104]. Around the beginning of the fourth quarter of 1929 he stores more than twenty tons of gold, valued in the narrative at about twelve million dollars, at the Duffeys' place [MTM B4:110].","citations":["MTM B3:101","MTM B3:102","MTM B3:103-104","MTM B4:110"],"tags":["Ships","Duffey","Family","Money & Trade","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Koch, Elinore","definition":"Wife of August Koch and mother of Lily and Letitia, one of the family circle in Duffey's Chicago golden age [MTM B3:98]. Duffey falls in love with his mother-in-law at first sight when she welcomes him with outstretched arms and an enormous kiss, a gesture shared by all three Koch women. The narrator praises her style and calls such affection good luck [MTM B3:101]. Duffey continues to love all three Koch ladies [MTM B4:110].","citations":["MTM B3:98","MTM B3:101","MTM B4:110"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Koch, Letitia","definition":"Wife of Melchisedech Duffey, younger sister of Lily Koch, and daughter of August and Elinore. Duffey's rib-shaped talisman, entrusted to Lily in childhood, is associated with Letitia's formation and their eventual meeting [MTM B1:30] [MTM B3:90-91] [MTM B3:101]. Initially described as chubby, with smiling eyes and a dazzling soul, she has cascades of hair between blonde, scarlet, and walnut, a ruddy complexion, a high-comedy expression, and eyes between sea-green and Melchisedech blue. She is taller than Duffey and helps move their belongings into their Chicago home above the bookstore; their marriage opens the golden age beginning in 1925 [MTM B3:91-92] [MTM B3:98]. Her beautiful, haunting voice is used in radio advertisements, and she intercepts Casey's appearance as the world-conquering Leader [MTM B4:114] [MTM B4:117]. Duffey says she can read his mind. During the portfolio blackmail, she goes away on a purportedly necessary trip, returning without examining or expressing interest in the pictures [MTM B4:140] [MTM B4:144-145]. In New Orleans the narrator describes her as transfigured into proto-legendary beauty [MTM B7:211]. After Dotty's disappearance she edits <em>The Bark</em>, supports it from private funds, and attends the New Catholic Press Guild meeting with Duffey and Mary Virginia [MTM B8:238-239]. Her death is sudden; at Margaret Stone's insistence her eyes remain open, seeming to follow everyone at the old-fashioned wake in her home [MTM B8:253-254].","citations":["MTM B1:30","MTM B3:90-91","MTM B3:101","MTM B3:91-92","MTM B3:98","MTM B4:114","MTM B4:117","MTM B4:140","MTM B4:144-145","MTM B7:211","MTM B8:238-239","MTM B8:253-254"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Family","Magic & Sorcery","Journalism","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Koch, Lily","definition":"Lily Koch, Letitia's sister and August and Elinore's daughter, is Letitia's partner in Koch's Galleries [MTM B3:92] [MTM B3:98]. As a schoolgirl merchant at St. Mary Major's, she combines boarding and day attendance, with wealthy kindred across the road. She holds private Wednesday sales and public Saturday sales, obtains whisky through Duffey, and sells art, including Sebastian Hilton's hundred-dollar statuette [MTM B1:28-29]. Her merchant trademark is a pair of celluloid cuffs or gauntlets. She and Duffey communicate within her mind, and she kisses him after recognizing his talisman as magi-made. Already 'finished' herself and expecting no children, she undertakes to use it for Letitia [MTM B1:29-30]. Years later the rib-shaped talisman is still displayed in the gallery as effectively priceless; Lily brings Duffey and Letitia together [MTM B3:90-91]. August says she and Sebastian had been engaged 'in a sort of way' [MTM B3:102]. She belongs to the Chicago golden-age circle [MTM B3:98]. After Letitia's death she flies to New Orleans and stays several weeks [MTM B8:254] [MTM B8:256]. She proposes to Duffey, says her old million-dollar dowry has tripled, and explains that Letitia asked her to care for him. By an unexplained calculation she declares it his four-thousandth birthday and arranges the enormous cake and party [MTM B8:257].","citations":["MTM B3:92","MTM B3:98","MTM B1:28-29","MTM B1:29-30","MTM B3:90-91","MTM B3:102","MTM B8:254","MTM B8:256","MTM B8:257"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family","Duffey","Magic & Sorcery","Visual Arts","Money & Trade","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Kofeinik (Rose Ivanova's)","definition":"Rose Ivanova's restaurant in an unnamed, bustling trucking town encountered during Duffey's Tartary expedition. Its sign claims that truck drivers' presence proves it is the best. Duffey emerges from the Laughing Prince's desert scenes, eats a hearty meal, and drinks eleven glasses of water. Nobody in the town has heard of the Prince. As Duffey leaves for the newspaper office, the town vanishes and the desert returns [MTM B13:438-440].","citations":["MTM B13:438-440"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","Dreams"]},{"name":"Kopolatry","definition":"Unexplained mystical term in Marie Courtois's conversation with Finnegan on a jungle-crowded beach, although they should be aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em> in mid-Atlantic. With his head in her lap and her hair wound around him, she discusses kopolatry beside the gremium matris, identified with the Earth and her lap. Earth worship or fertility symbolism is a possible contextual reading; the word's precise meaning and derivation remain uncertain [D:92].","citations":["D:92"],"tags":["Mysticism","Finnegan","Dreams","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Koran, George","definition":"Reporter for the New Orleans Picayune whom Duffey meets at the New Catholic Press Guild address by 'The Majesty,' the released Devil [MTM B8:240]. He lists the speaker's enormous itinerary among financiers, publishers, politicians, labor leaders, historians, and a Student Repudiation Congress [MTM B8:243]. The next day's engagements include an early communion breakfast in Oklahoma City, Waco Baptists, Dallas, Cow Town, San Antonio, and Houston [MTM B8:244].","citations":["MTM B8:240","MTM B8:243","MTM B8:244"],"tags":["Journalism","The Devil","Duffey","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Korsakoff, Sergei (1854--1900)","definition":"Russian psychiatrist Sergei Korsakoff, also transliterated Sergey Korsakov (1854-1900), whose studies of alcoholic polyneuritis and memory disturbance gave his name to Korsakoff's syndrome. The syndrome involves severe memory impairment associated with thiamine deficiency, commonly in severe alcohol use disorder. In Archipelago, Finnegan tells a Travelers' Aid worker that his trouble is 'Korsakofs Psychosis' [A:244].","citations":["A:244"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Eastern Europe","History & Memory","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Korsakofs Psychosis","definition":"Finnegan's name for his memory trouble when speaking to the Travelers' Aid woman. He declines a doctor, saying doctors do not understand the condition and ascribe it to drinking; he compares himself with his father's strange behavior [A:244].","citations":["A:244"],"tags":["Finnegan","History & Memory","Family"]},{"name":"Kos","definition":"Greek island in the Dodecanese. In Anastasia's account of mermaids, its mermaid beaches were already documented by the beginning of Egypt's Eighteenth Dynasty, conventionally dated about 1550-1295 BCE. This documentation belongs to the novel's mermaid lore [D:34-35].","citations":["D:34-35"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Nymphs & Mermaids","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Krakow","definition":"Polish city associated with Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch's birth in 1830, near Krakow or Skawine, in X's account [D:278]. X places St. Elmo's parish near Krakow and says Ifreann claimed lifelong citizenship of the Republic of Krakow after fleeing the region at about fifteen [D:279-280]. The historical Free City existed from 1815 to 1846; X's statement that it existed only in that one year is inaccurate [D:280].","citations":["D:278","D:279-280","D:280"],"tags":["Eastern Europe","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Kraut","definition":"Epithet repeatedly applied to Hans. The narrator first calls him merry-looking and powerful [A:5]. Before their drinking contest Bushmaster boasts of having beaten krauts, and during it he calls Hans an American kraut [A:15] [A:22]. Another description makes this 'kraut-head' simultaneously Faust and Apollo [A:169].","citations":["A:5","A:15","A:22","A:169"],"tags":["Language & Words","Germany"]},{"name":"Kreb's Cycle Consonance","definition":"One of the supposed age-measuring criteria in the fictional Eighteenth Book of Strange Encounters, written by the editors of the Sixteenth and Seventeenth Books. Along with birefringence flow index and Howship's lacunar frequency, it is said to indicate that Duffey is over four thousand years old, despite a body appearing about fifty-five [MTM B8:250].","citations":["MTM B8:250"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Kristine","definition":"Woman in Johnny Duckwalk's barroom story, set uncertainly in Lisbon or Stockholm. She offers money to have her supposedly insured husband killed with an axe. Duckwalk and his shipmate Mike Merganser discover that each was being set up as the other's victim; Kristine admits she wanted their rolls of money [D:246-247].","citations":["D:246-247"],"tags":["Women Characters","Crime","Crew & Sailors","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Kropotkin, Peter","definition":"Russian anarchist thinker Peter Kropotkin (1842-1921), author of The Conquest of Bread (1892) and Mutual Aid (1902), who emphasized cooperative social life and opposed state compulsion. X says that Ifreann Gregorovitch became an ironic disciple after his involvement in the Paris Commune; he contrasts the supposedly crooked saint Kropotkin with the honest devil Ifreann [D:280]. This Ifreann is the father of Papadiabolous I, not Papadiabolous I himself [D:285].","citations":["D:280","D:285"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Eastern Europe","France","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Krym","definition":"X's Russian name for Crimea in his account of the Devil's prison. He dates the imprisonment to 946 or 947 and describes a prison dug beneath the Crimean Mountains [MTM B6:204]. Absalom Stein dates the release after a thousand years to 1946 [MTM B6:185].","citations":["MTM B6:204","MTM B6:185"],"tags":["The Devil","Eastern Europe","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Kumanek","definition":"One of the godfather or sponsor titles used while the narrator questions Duffey's relationship to his talismanic children, especially Casey: Pate, Kumanek, Nonos, Nasho, Athair Baiste, Sponsor, Padrino, Godfather. The passage asks whether his role exceeds ordinary sponsorship and resembles that of a creating sorcerer [MTM B4:119].","citations":["MTM B4:119"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","Creation","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Kuri","definition":"Place-name in Finnegan's verse, grouped with Roi, Rangoon, Kandi, and Cai as places where summer is eternal [A:171].","citations":["A:171"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Kutz (Soft-Talk Susie)","definition":"Soft-Talk Susie Kutz, barmaid at the Old Wooden Ship and an angel unaware. Her reports to the Lord make the patrons seem a little better than they are and help account for their unexpected prosperity; the narrative distinguishes regulars from the more casual visitors Finnegan and Le Marin [D:216]. Some later accounts of the patrons are said to derive from her blessed reports, whose relation to truth the narrator leaves playfully open [D:225].","citations":["D:216","D:225"],"tags":["Angels & Demons","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Kymatic patterns","definition":"Electrical patterns in the brain, contrasted with akymatic patterns, in Casey Szymansky's discussion of spirits reading brain activity. He distinguishes verbal, subliminal, electrical, and chemical indications from access to the immaterial mind, and says the spirits must use physical means [PG:539-540].","citations":["PG:539-540"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Metaphysics","Language & Words"]},{"name":"L'Ouverture, Toussaint","definition":"Haitian revolutionary leader Toussaint Louverture (c. 1743-1803). Carmody Overlark's fictional Studies in the Imagination includes him in a catalogue of supposed Neanderthal-like 'pumpkin heads,' alongside Plato, Homer, Aquinas, Rabelais, Luther, Christopher Smart, Rasputin, and Steinmetz. Overlark associates this imagined type with criminals, religious innovators, leaders, poets, and inventors [D:270-272].","citations":["D:270-272"],"tags":["Caribbean","Historical Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"La Barque","definition":"French words meaning 'the boat' in the caption to Henry's dream of a storm on a lake. As wind and waves grow more convincing, he recognizes the biblical wording and prefers the Vulgate's Latin to the French [A:45]. The allusion is Luke 8:23, where the sleeping Jesus' boat takes in water during a squall.","citations":["A:45"],"tags":["New Testament","Ships","Dreams","Language & Words"]},{"name":"La Paz","definition":"Place-name in the poem by Finnegan that Dotty discovers: 'I ought to go visit La Paz and Lierre / And make them acquainted with me' [A:173].","citations":["A:173"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Ladies' Bar","definition":"One of the Plaza's forty-eight bars; the narrator jokes that the Ladies' Bar has never known man [A:3].","citations":["A:3"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Lafitte, Jean","definition":"Pirate and privateer Jean Lafitte, probably born in the early 1780s, whose men and supplies helped Andrew Jackson's defense of New Orleans in 1815; the details of his death are uncertain. In Casey's imagined revolutionary uprising, torpedoes carrying sawed-off shotguns take the role of Jean Lafitte's pirates among the rising workers [A:160].","citations":["A:160"],"tags":["Historical Figures","New Orleans","Combat","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Lake Borgne","definition":"Lake in the New Orleans region where the House, Home and Happenings article locates Zabotski and Widow Waldo's dwelling on Pristine Cove [MTM B9:267]. Zabotski tells a reporter he plans to move his great structure there and describes the lake as opening into the Gulf [MTM B9:289]. During the flood the contraption reaches Lake Borgne and rides on a small kedge anchor [MTM B9:287]. The later Zabotski Flats comprise two hundred lakeside acres, two-thirds sweet meadow and one-third salt meadow, extraordinarily lush in contrast to neighboring land; Zabotski attributes the improvement to God's favor [MTM B12:362].","citations":["MTM B9:267","MTM B9:289","MTM B9:287","MTM B12:362"],"tags":["New Orleans","Louisiana","Ships","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Lamb","definition":"An emblem on Henry's dream ship <em>Navicula Petri</em>, among the Greek Cross, Fish, and Six-Pointed Star [A:44]. In Christian symbolism the lamb represents Christ, the Lamb of God.","citations":["A:44"],"tags":["New Testament","Ships","Dreams"]},{"name":"Land of Behest","definition":"One of the <em>Argo</em>'s former names. In its narrated history St. Brandon sails it from Ireland to America, encounters the great fish Jascoyne, and carries Judas, who is said not to be its most hellish passenger [MTM B13:404]. Brandon is the voyager St. Brendan of Clonfert, traditionally dated c. 484-577; the American voyage here belongs to the ship's legendary history.","citations":["MTM B13:404"],"tags":["Saints","Ships","Sailing","Ireland","Identity"]},{"name":"Land of the Cyclops","definition":"Texas in the narrative of Finnegan's bewildered wandering: a dread country of tall, one-eyed or squint-eyed men where he despairs of seeing home and friends again [A:201]. The comparison evokes Odysseus's encounter with the Cyclopes in Book 9 of the Odyssey.","citations":["A:201"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Land of the Lotus Eaters","definition":"The Lotus Eaters tavern, where Hans and Marie arrange a date and where he waits for her [A:16] [A:25]. Its clock is painted and its calendar permanently reads the thirty-first of spring. Katie keeps their glasses filled; inside it remains afternoon even when they finally emerge into a late night outside [A:29]. Its name recalls the Odyssey's Lotus-eaters, while 'always afternoon' closely echoes Tennyson's The Lotos-Eaters. Marie jokingly attributes the saying to her uncle Horace Higgenbotham [A:16].","citations":["A:16","A:25","A:29"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Time & Eternity","Literature"]},{"name":"Langenscheidt","definition":"German language-learning publisher founded by Gustav Langenscheidt in Berlin in 1856. Its manuals are among the books Casey uses at Duffey's, alongside Thimms, Hugos, Marlboroughs, Berlitz, Cortina, and the German-written Metoula manuals [A:159].","citations":["A:159"],"tags":["Germany","Duffey","Language & Words","Literature"]},{"name":"Laonome","definition":"Mary Virginia's ancient name in Teresa Showboat's account of the companions' Argonaut identities: 'Mary Virginia is Laonome' [MTM B7:234]. Mary Virginia recalls fragments of that life and says she hated the name then and still does; her own reply prints the variant 'Laonone' [MTM B7:235].","citations":["MTM B7:234","MTM B7:235"],"tags":["Argonautica","Women Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Larkin, Loy","definition":"Red-haired stewardess whom Finnegan and Vincent meet with Margaret in a city lounge; she claims to have a man in every major port [A:2]. The narrator calls her a pink cloud [A:6], and Finnegan addresses his “Antipodal Ode to Loy Larkin,” celebrating her in Sydney Town [A:32]. Her letter to John Solli, headed Cambeltown, New South Wales, Thursday February 11, 1943, invites the friends to meet the train at 7:45 a.m. for a picnic. It includes Hans, Marie, Vincent, and the as-yet-unmet Casey; she and Margaret profess love for the friends, while Loy complains that her November garden is all weeds and says her father killed the fatted calf for her return [A:27]. Much later Finnegan sketches her again as a pink cloud [A:278].","citations":["A:2","A:6","A:32","A:27","A:278"],"tags":["Women Characters","Australia","Finnegan","Family","Literature"]},{"name":"Larkins","definition":"Loy Larkin's family, named by Marie Monaghan with the Murpheys among the old Irish families in Australia. Marie gives her own Monaghan family a residence of 156 years, then adds that the Larkins and Murpheys are old families too [A:7].","citations":["A:7"],"tags":["Australia","Ireland","Family"]},{"name":"Larrigan","definition":"One surname in the worldwide Angelino clan, listed beside Obermeyer, Viskochil, Borg, and Trevino [A:218].","citations":["A:218"],"tags":["Family","Carnival"]},{"name":"Last Judgement","definition":"In Christian teaching, the final judgment of humanity at Christ's return, distinguished from the particular judgment at death. In 'The Casey Machine,' a soggy sinner asks his pastor whether everyone will know all other people's thoughts and deeds after the General or Particular Judgment. The pastor says this knowledge brings joy to the saved and suffering to the damned; the sinner perversely anticipates enjoying everyone else's shame [CM:504].","citations":["CM:504"],"tags":["Theology","Sin & Redemption","Heaven & Hell"]},{"name":"Last Night of Her Life, The (perfume)","definition":"Perfume Margaret Stone wears before the visit to the Decatur Street Opera House [MTM B11:346]. Its name anticipates her death there at the hands of the thunder colt [MTM B11:351].","citations":["MTM B11:346","MTM B11:351"],"tags":["Women Characters","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Last Rally, the","definition":"The gathering summoned by bells rung by a few opponents of the destruction of the world's central institution, the Ekklesia. The narrative immediately locates Finnegan's return to the <em>Argo</em> in the Third Year of the Bells [MTM B13:450].","citations":["MTM B13:450"],"tags":["Ekklesia","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Lateral line remainder of the post-auditory placodes","definition":"An alleged anatomical age-marker listed in the fictional Eighteenth Book of Strange Encounters among the tests said to show Duffey is over four thousand years old, even though his present body appears about fifty-five [MTM B8:250].","citations":["MTM B8:250"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Latin","definition":"Language of education, scriptural echoes, and inscriptions throughout the books. Latin is among the subjects at Duffey's boarding school, where Sebastian excels; Charlotte says 'Sum etiam erudita ipse,' glossed as 'I'm educated myself' [MTM B1:25] [MTM B3:80]. Finnegan begins with a Latin creation formula, and Vincent wishes he could render his two-men-in-a-bar beginning in Latin [A:2]. It is Marie who tells Sally that charity is a Latin word for love [A:9]. Henry's <em>Navicula Petri</em> dream carries the mottoes 'Ih hoc signo vincet,' 'Nisi esses sollicitus,' 'Ubicumque fuerit corpus illic congregabuntur et aquilas,' 'Tu es Petrus,' 'Portae inferi non praevalebunt,' and 'tibi dabo claves regni'; the second is crossed out and replaced with 'If you can't be careful, be good' [A:44]. The storm is narrated in Latin before a French caption appears [A:45]. Casey's notebook gives supposed Latin-to-Germanic sound correspondences, including K to H, F to B, and P to F [A:163]. Hans learns Latin from his father John William [A:208]. The noon-day devils address Finnegan with 'Dic ut lapides isti panes fiant' [A:205]. A Latin scroll identifies the risen Count in Finnegan's self-portrait, and the <em>Argo</em> bringing the Peter-Bell displays its name in Latin, Greek, Hebrew, and Chaldee letters [MB:551] [MB:577].","citations":["MTM B1:25","MTM B3:80","A:2","A:9","A:44","A:45","A:163","A:208","A:205","MB:551","MB:577"],"tags":["Language & Words","Literature","Scripture","Finnegan","Duffey"]},{"name":"Laughing Prince of Tartary","definition":"A formidable adversary within the group called Evil Prince, Purple Prince, Mocking Prince, and Laughing Prince; the Laughing Prince of Tartary may be the strongest and trickiest of them. Reportedly the Prince of the Third Way, he claims allegiance to neither God nor the Devil and says both have rejected him [MTM B13:437-438]. Reports connect him with the dialectic pit, concealed partisanship, total amorality, and an odd laugh. Nobody has seen his eyes behind his black-lensed glasses. His precise realm is uncertain despite the name Tartary. He boasts that his only cooling spring is poisoned [MTM B13:438]. The <em>Argo</em>'s investigators include X, Casey Gorshok, Biloxi Brannagan, and Duffey. Casey meets him only through séances, Brannagan searches the shores, and Duffey enters an industrial-agricultural country overlaid by a false desert [MTM B13:438-439]. After temporary escapes into ordinary surroundings, Duffey succumbs to the desert's thirst and drinks from the spring [MTM B13:440-441]. The Prince giggles, and Duffey calls him the Giggling Prince. His immense psychic mentality is contrasted with limited intelligence; his black lenses conceal tiny televisions through which, with his own hallucinations, he experiences the world [MTM B13:441-442]. The water paralyzes Duffey, but the three slant-faced killers actually kill him by cutting away his flesh; a separate trio of slack-faced men burns the remains. When the desert fades, X and Casey recover Duffey's bones and ashes in Gur'yev. The Prince's final fate is unstated [MTM B13:441-442].","citations":["MTM B13:437-438","MTM B13:438","MTM B13:438-439","MTM B13:440-441","MTM B13:441-442"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Duffey","Magic & Sorcery","Dreams","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Law, The","definition":"One of the three necessities for preserving and rebuilding the world, with worship of God and deeds of kindness and charity. Duffey greatly respects The Law and has a working respect for ordinary laws [MTM B7:228]. The narrative treats Law and worship as congruent under the name Faith, then seeks a synthesis of art as Law, worship, kindness, charity, and creation [MTM B7:229].","citations":["MTM B7:228","MTM B7:229"],"tags":["Theology","Duffey","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Lawrence Salvatore","definition":"Father of Henry Francis Salvatore and husband of Mary Genevieve Hebert, in Henry's Louisiana family [A:122]. Tired of disputes over Henry's schooling, Lawrence examines him, discovers that he is already educated despite considering him the dullest of his boys, and gives him freedom early in his thirteenth year [A:123]. Later he questions Henry's haste to enter the seminary and discusses his reasons and the Church's dangers with him [A:125].","citations":["A:122","A:123","A:125"],"tags":["Family","Catholic Church","Louisiana"]},{"name":"Laycheck, Colonel","definition":"Colonel who, with Majors Meyerhofer, Twicherby, and Terwilliger, reprimands Casey for questioning Stein during an authorized discussion period. None of these officers is a line officer. Laycheck forbids criticism of Stein, threatens a disloyalty charge and ten years, and admits writing Stein's speeches [A:47]. After Casey persuades Stein to prompt a raid on the Australian brigadier through the false Tania story, the scandal brings down Laycheck's star 'like lightning' [A:54].","citations":["A:47","A:54"],"tags":["Soldiers","World War II"]},{"name":"Lazarus","definition":"The poor man in the parable of the rich man and Lazarus (Luke 16:19-31). Duffey jokingly claims that Dives could have been saved if he had requested a mint julep or Tom Collins rather than water, calling this the test that he failed [A:117].","citations":["A:117"],"tags":["New Testament","Duffey","Heaven & Hell","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Lazarus Reilly","definition":"Proprietor of the Old Wooden Ship. Taking to heart the warning about entertaining angels unawares, he gives newcomers a room, bed, beer credit, and money rather than risk slighting an angel. He says he has much to make up for. The narrator identifies the barmaid Soft-Talk Susie Kutz as the actual angel among the company [D:216]. The hospitality allusion is Hebrews 13:2.","citations":["D:216"],"tags":["Angels & Demons","Bars & Taverns","Minor Characters","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Le Faye, Father","definition":"Jesuit and exorcist in Finnegan's account of painting a devil in Florence. He arrives unexpectedly after an overnight train journey, goes directly to the covered picture, recognizes the devil, performs a short exorcism, and orders the painting burned and its ashes swept out [D:254-255]. Van Ghi then identifies the figure as an old repeater in Bruegel, Giotto, El Greco, Notre Dame stonework, Etruscan vases, and Aztec statuettes, and advises Finnegan to paint without inspiration, saying he has drawn the Joker instead of the Queen [D:255].","citations":["D:254-255","D:255"],"tags":["Religious Orders","The Devil","Finnegan","Italy","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Le Marin, Charles","definition":"French seaman and companion of Finnegan [D:232]. He joins Finnegan, Johnny Duckwalk, and Don Barnaby in their earthly Paradise at Basse-Terre [D:235-237]. His <em>Sarcophagus</em> story alleges that Castleman's men gradually replaced the crew with doubles, including his own replacement at Fort-de-France; he shifts into speaking as one of the raiders, yet says Castleman doubted whether the switch on him had worked. He escapes with valuable contraband and later confronts Castleman [D:243-246]. He eventually leaves Paradise for the sea, saying stomach trouble will not improve until he has a ship beneath him [D:266].","citations":["D:232","D:235-237","D:243-246","D:266"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Caribbean","France","Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Leader, The","definition":"Casey Szymansky's first bid for universal power, when he is ten. His Answer proclaims the Leader in a sensational lead article [MTM B4:114]. Medals disappear from Duffey's shop, where he sells residue from Gabriel's former pawn shop [MTM B4:115-116]. An appearance at 7 p.m. beside Henry Horner Park's equestrian statue draws about a hundred thousand people and three bands; a magnificent retired white fire horse waits to be mounted [MTM B4:116-117]. Casey approaches in a scarlet tunic, shako, boots, spurs, belts and bandolier, wearing a hussar sabre and his father's Knights of Columbus sword. His decorations include the Croix de la Legion D'honneur, Croix de Guerre, Medal of Honor, Navy Medal of Honor, Iron Cross, Order of the Golden Fleece medallion, Crown of St. Stephen, Star of the Ninth Fusiliers, and Chicago lodge medals. He says mounting the horse will initiate Plan Beta, taking Chicago, America, and the world [MTM B4:117]. 'Aunt Letitia' intercepts him and makes him pack away the regalia; they go to admire the horse and bands as spectators. The Leader never appears and the Answer ceases. Casey later calls the venture a hoax, but Letitia knows he intended it seriously [MTM B4:117-118].","citations":["MTM B4:114","MTM B4:115-116","MTM B4:116-117","MTM B4:117","MTM B4:117-118"],"tags":["Journalism","Identity","Weapons","Family"]},{"name":"Leakley, Nelly","definition":"Resident regular at the Old Wooden Ship [D:215]. She is one of the women called salt water widows, whose husbands are seamen [D:221].","citations":["D:215","D:221"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Family"]},{"name":"Learning","definition":"A recurring practical and spiritual activity. Duffey's school circle is said to live in enterprise, diligence, happiness, learning, and purity [MTM B1:27]. Bagby teaches Duffey to delegate in business, and the two learn to select winds while ballooning [MTM B2:46] [MTM B2:64]. The document collection pairs a warning attributed to Dotty O'Toole's sonnet with Paul's counsel in Philippians 4:4-9 to practice what has been learned [MTM B5:175]. From beyond death Bagby questions whether contentment is something he is meant to learn, while affirming his growing happiness [MTM B8:249]. Duffey rapidly learns and recites his part in Cleo Mahoney's play [MTM B9:265]. In his commentary Stein says exchanging kabouters with Casey has made each privy to the other's secrets [PG:537-538].","citations":["MTM B1:27","MTM B2:46","MTM B2:64","MTM B5:175","MTM B8:249","MTM B9:265","PG:537-538"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Theater","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Leaven","definition":"Image opposed to the lump in Cleo Mahoney's <em>Seven Roads</em> epigraph: 'Be you either lump or leaven' [MTM B10:290]. In 'Great Day,' Duffey rebukes his transformed companions as the lump rather than the leaven, accuses them of failing him, and leaves [GD:495].","citations":["MTM B10:290","GD:495"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Theology"]},{"name":"Lecturing","definition":"One of Special Service Sergeant Absalom Stein's activities. The narrator describes his lectures as plausible and the listeners as uninformed; Casey's questions expose problems, after which the question period is abolished and Casey is reprimanded [A:46-47].","citations":["A:46-47"],"tags":["Soldiers","World War II"]},{"name":"Left Lectionary League, The","definition":"Organization honored with sacrifices of goats among the broad assortment of offerings at the Conclave in Babylonia Bagascia [MB:565-566].","citations":["MB:565-566"],"tags":["Ekklesia","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Left-handed","definition":"Finnegan's teasing description of Australian table manners. Tom Shire explains that Australians keep the knife in the right hand and fork in the left, while Americans transfer the fork after cutting [A:4]. In a separate notebook entry Casey describes left- and right-handed rocks according to formation and cleavage [A:164].","citations":["A:4","A:164"],"tags":["Australia","Food & Dining","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Leftfooted killer","definition":"Unidentified pursuer asking for Finnegan in Florida. Finnegan says that the man wants to kill him on an earlier assignment, unrelated to the latest trouble [D:182-183]. After another inquiry in Winter Garden, the killer and a companion follow Finnegan, who has departed in the Georgia Kiunker; they do not ride in his car [D:193]. Under repeated fire, Finnegan ditches the Klunker and disables the pursuing car, then wounds both men as they flee [D:197].","citations":["D:182-183","D:193","D:197"],"tags":["Finnegan","Minor Characters","Crime","Combat"]},{"name":"Legends of the Persian Gulf","definition":"Work credited to Moisha El-Gazma in the epigraph to 'The Wives of Sindbad.' Its account imagines Bassorah as a fire-scarred rock slab in muddy shallows, perhaps once a launching pad for space fleets, and invokes a missing Night about Sindbads trading between worlds [D:53].","citations":["D:53"],"tags":["Folklore","Ports & Harbors","Middle East","Literature"]},{"name":"Legion","definition":"The Pop Countess's answer both to Duffey's question about the number of her people and to Stein's question about the name of her society and principality [MTM B11:319]. It echoes Mark 5:9 and Luke 8:30, where the possessing spirit or possessed man answers Jesus with the name Legion because the demons are many.","citations":["MTM B11:319"],"tags":["New Testament","Angels & Demons","Clubs & Societies","Identity"]},{"name":"Leonardo da Vinci (1452--1519)","definition":"Italian Renaissance artist and investigator of anatomy, engineering, mechanics, and flight, whose paintings include The Last Supper and Mona Lisa. Jimmy Fitzjames says a newly discovered Leonardo would bring a hundred thousand dollars and claims Adam Scanlon is better [A:230]. The narrator calls Scanlon possibly as good as Leonardo and likens their habit of painting while talking to an audience; it is the narrator, not Scanlon, who calls Adam the better talker [A:233-234]. Casey's notebook XXXII attributes to Leonardo a question about rivers becoming deeper and clearer after a great pestilence, then mocks it as an example of a great man's silliness [A:165].","citations":["A:230","A:233-234","A:165"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Italy","Visual Arts","Literature"]},{"name":"Les Cent Nouvelles nouvelles","definition":"The fifteenth-century French collection Les Cent Nouvelles nouvelles, or One Hundred New Tales, here printed as 'Les Cents Novelles Nouvelles.' Hans buys it at a New York secondhand bookstall after giving a young scholar a quarter for Jaufre and Brunissende [A:224]. The scholar introduces himself as James Fitzjames on the following page [A:225].","citations":["A:224","A:225"],"tags":["France","Literature"]},{"name":"Les Cents Novelle Nouvelles","definition":"Earlier heading differed from both the standard title and the novel’s printed Les Cents Novelles Nouvelles. See <em>Les Cent Nouvelles nouvelles</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["France","Literature"]},{"name":"Les Contes Drolatiques","definition":"Ribald tales by Honoré de Balzac (1799-1850), first published in collections between 1832 and 1837. A favorite of Gautier the goat-man, who reads French books aloud to his goats on winter nights in the Harlow House library; he says that Balzac appeals to goats and to the goat in man [A:196-198].","citations":["A:196-198"],"tags":["France","Literature"]},{"name":"Lessing, Miriam","definition":"Mother of Casey Szymansky and wife of Gabriel Szymansky, the pawnbroker and antique dealer. Casey is their only child, born in Chicago on October 7, 1921 [A:153-154].","citations":["A:153-154"],"tags":["Family","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Letters After I am Dead","definition":"Work attributed to Bascom Bagby in an epigraph. It proposes Iron Meadows inhabited by Aspects and says some aspects of the Argo Legend dwell in a meadow unknown to both God and Duffey [MTM B5:177].","citations":["MTM B5:177"],"tags":["Literature","Argonautica","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Levant","definition":"The eastern Mediterranean region. Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni is said to know all the tongues of Europe and the Levant, as well as the world's important languages [A:213].","citations":["A:213"],"tags":["Middle East","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Leviathan","definition":"Biblical sea monster, notably in Job 41, Psalm 74:14 and Isaiah 27:1. Casey reports Duffey's political analogy: liberals formerly wanted Leviathan only a little larger than the other whales, but now want it to devour them [A:42]. In Trinali Peterson's image for the Elephants' Graveyard, the largest driftwood becomes the bones of Leviathan, older than elephants, mammoths and behemoths [A:232]. Leviathans graze in the salt-water portions of Zabotski Flats, and leviathan steak appears in the children's grace at Zabotski's floating dinner table alongside hippopotamus, rhinoceros, behemoth, alligator, buffalo, elephant and green turtle [MTM B12:362-363].","citations":["A:42","A:232","MTM B12:362-363"],"tags":["Old Testament","Monsters & Creatures","Food & Dining","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Leviathan, Edward Cardinal","definition":"Cardinal of Edinburgh, one of the thirteen primaries on John Mogul's list. His double is Douglas McAfee of London [MB:579].","citations":["MB:579"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Minor Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Levin","definition":"An archaic word for lightning. In the 'Ici Parle Isolde' section of Casey's youthful ballad, the levin 'dances fire' beneath grumbling clouds [A:152-153].","citations":["A:152-153"],"tags":["Language & Words","Literature"]},{"name":"Levine y O'Brien, Gilberto","definition":"Rio double of Carlos Cardinal Artemis of Santa Cruz [MB:579]. After the thirteen primaries have been killed, Gilberto, Finnegan and Herman Hercules travel aboard the <em>Argo</em>; all thirteen shadows are still alive at this stage [MB:589]. Their masked, skeletal pilot is Melchisedech [MB:590]. Gilberto fears that the identifying marks he made on his own skin may have been forged, and refuses Finnegan's offer to inspect them. This uncertainty is separate from Finnegan's secret masterpiece imprinted inside Artemis's skin: the narrative leaves open whether that painting is inside Gilberto's present skin or the flayed trophy possessed by John Mogul [MB:590-591].","citations":["MB:579","MB:589","MB:590","MB:590-591"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Finnegan","Duffey","Identity","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Levites","definition":"Members of the Israelite tribe of Levi, assigned to assist the priesthood in Numbers 3:6-10. In the Rounders' Club farewell scene, the narrator includes 'priests and Levites' among the city's people who come to honor Duffey [MTM B2:70].","citations":["MTM B2:70"],"tags":["Old Testament","Ecclesiastical","Duffey","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Lewis, Don (Timothy Lorrigan)","definition":"<em>Brunhilde</em> crewman, dark and exceptionally handsome, who shares a cabin with Finnegan and Joe Cross and belongs to the narrative's 'good people' [D:30-31]. His campfire story identifies him indirectly as PFC Timothy Lorrigan: with Sergeant Charles Santee and Corporal Robert Casper he disappeared from patrol on Pulau Petir, or Willy Jones Island, on October 1, 1944. After the other two kill the elusive man called Yokipoki, Lorrigan buries him; apparently resurrected movement proves to be rats at the body [D:44-47]. Despite calling himself a coward, Don secretly organizes resistance aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em>, brings Joe, Manuel, Anastasia and Finnegan into the affair, communicates toward the Devil's resurrection without understanding a critical part of it, and tips the balance so Finnegan lives and Dopey dies [D:105]. Hunted in Tangier, he finds Seaworthy at the consulate and cannot buy refuge from the man who hides fugitives [D:106-108]. Marie Courtois kills him by throttling him and breaking his neck. Finnegan finds him in a weed patch, laments how little he knew his friend, and buries him in clean sand beneath aromatic creepers [D:109-110] [D:112-113]. Later an unidentified dead man's voice comes from the sleeping Don Barnaby's hammock and asks Finnegan to repay Anastasia fifty dollars; the episode suggests a return of Don Lewis without naming the voice [D:238].","citations":["D:30-31","D:44-47","D:105","D:106-108","D:109-110","D:112-113","D:238"],"tags":["Ships","Crew & Sailors","World War II","Soldiers","Pacific Theater","Africa","Finnegan","Death & Mortality","Ghosts & Spirits"]},{"name":"Lewis, Jake (Jake-Leg)","definition":"Old Wooden Ship associate and salt-water cowboy. Jake-Leg Lewis and Tommy Cow-Town Borger ride cattle boats, not horses, but always wear boots, spurs, chaps and gauntlets; dwindling live-cattle shipments leave long intervals between voyages [D:215-216] [D:222]. His nickname may allude to 'Jake leg,' the gait impairment caused by adulterated Jamaica ginger consumed during Prohibition, though the novel does not explain his nickname or diagnose him.","citations":["D:215-216","D:222"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Li Tai Po","definition":"Chinese poet of the Tang period (701-762), also known as Li Po. In Hans Schultz's unpublished ballad he sings while playing the flute, turns youthful heartbreak into songs of kings and working people, drinks, and joins a fisher maid in her boat. After telling her he loved her in another form long ago, he tumbles into the water and drowns [A:221-223].","citations":["A:221-223"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Music","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Liberal Catholic","definition":"The description Casey plans to give the <em>Crock</em> as a vehicle for 'Their' handouts and quotations, according to Mary Catherine Carruthers. She regards his continued membership in the Church as making him more valuable to his new associates [A:98].","citations":["A:98"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Journalism"]},{"name":"Liberals","definition":"A recurrent target of the characters' political and religious polemic. Casey reports Duffey's accusation that collectivists have appropriated the name, reversed liberal preferences from minimal to maximal government, and allowed only one creed of thought; Casey himself remarks that Duffey's analogies are difficult [A:42]. Henry Salvatore describes a Secular-Liberal Religion working inside the Church, and Patrick Stranahan denounces secular-liberal influence as an internal parasite [A:125] [A:110]. Bascom Bagby names Secular Liberalism among seven heads of the enemy, with Dialectic Materialism, Artistic Degradation, Judas Priestism, Panaceic Pentecostalism, Murderous Molochism and Atheistic Communism [MTM B5:169]. X's account of the Devil's Crimean release describes the Liberal Consensus and the intelligentsia as disproportionately represented among the attendees [MTM B6:204-205].","citations":["A:42","A:125","A:110","MTM B5:169","MTM B6:204-205"],"tags":["Catholic Church","The Devil","Cosmic Struggle","Duffey"]},{"name":"Lien of Extreme Need","definition":"Finnegan's purported common-law justification for possessing a commandeered car 'for a while.' He offers the phrase when the girl driving it suspects that neither the money he gives her nor the car is honestly his [D:168].","citations":["D:168"],"tags":["Finnegan","Crime","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Life","definition":"Duffey's overlapping lives organize the narrative introduced as a magus's daily life. He remembers three contemporaneous childhoods and an older, continuing existence; the Boy King's life is presented as more substantial than its later shadows [MTM B1:5-6] [MTM B1:17]. His analyst interprets part of his childhood as a girl's life, while his mind-piracy lets him appropriate other people's experiences, including Sebastian's youthful countess affair [MTM B1:16] [MTM B1:31]. The narrator jokes about Sebastian's apparently short-lived appearance, treats education as an ultimate passion, and makes art expertise a lifelong Duffey vocation [MTM B1:22] [MTM B1:29] [MTM B1:34]. A watershed dream anticipates Duffey's departure; his seven hidden years are compared with supposed gaps in famous lives and yield seven Christ artworks and remembered loves he cannot revisit [MTM B2:68] [MTM B2:72] [MTM B3:103] [MTM B4:110]. Zabotski says years in the Pristine World can fill a day or two in the Molasses World [MTM B9:279]. Duffey recalls coming ashore in 1923 into a young life he can choose again; the long-lived <em>Argo</em> pilots owe extraordinary service, and the Sea of the Seven Lost Years joins nonconsecutive times and shores [MTM B12:370] [MTM B13:405-406]. His death, ashes and living flesh coexist; returning through an earlier shore restores the life ahead of him. Later he emerges aged twenty-three after seven variant lives and deaths, yet even the reality of his lives is questioned in his conversation with little Teresa [MTM B13:445] [MTM B13:465] [MTM B13:472]. Related treatments include Hilary Hilton's accusation that Casey flees life and reality, the Casey Machines' invasive experiences across death, and Finnegan's painting of his own aged resurrection [PG:531] [CM:507-509] [MB:551]. In 'Great Day,' remaining persons and verses must be disposed of before the world's conclusion; in 'How Many Miles to Babylon,' the Covenant is called the world's life-line [GD:501] [MB:594].","citations":["MTM B1:5-6","MTM B1:17","MTM B1:16","MTM B1:31","MTM B1:22","MTM B1:29","MTM B1:34","MTM B2:68","MTM B2:72","MTM B3:103","MTM B4:110","MTM B9:279","MTM B12:370","MTM B13:405-406","MTM B13:445","MTM B13:465","MTM B13:472","PG:531","CM:507-509","MB:551","GD:501","MB:594"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Time & Eternity","History & Memory","Identity","Death & Mortality","Resurrection","Metaphysics","Dreams"]},{"name":"Lightfoot, Mary","definition":"One of Duffey's Chicago friends, listed with the college and university people, and wife of Isaac Lightfoot [MTM B3:99] [MTM B4:130]. A peacemaker during the argument about moving the mule, she urges Duffey to let the 'blow-mouths' grow until the final harvest separates them from good people [MTM B4:130]. She appears with Duffey in a compromisingly presented photograph [MTM B4:139]. Both Lightfoots attend the great farewell gathering at which Duffey sees his old friends for the last time in normal flesh [MTM B12:383].","citations":["MTM B3:99","MTM B4:130","MTM B4:139","MTM B12:383"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Lilith","definition":"Lilith, the woman created alongside Adam in the Alphabet of Ben Sira's alternative-wife tradition. In Hans's reflections on Marie's eyes, 'Lillith who was before Eve' is called a witch and therefore green-eyed, giving green-eyed women primogeniture [A:24].","citations":["A:24"],"tags":["Folklore","Women Characters","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Lillith","definition":"Lillith is the spelling printed in the novel for Lilith. See <em>Lilith</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Folklore","Women Characters","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Limit","definition":"The first item in the good column of the Pythagorean table described in Casey's biography, opposed to the unlimited in the evil column [A:154]. Aristotle's Metaphysics 986a likewise places limit and unlimited in opposing columns aligned with good and bad.","citations":["A:154"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Theology"]},{"name":"Lincoln, Abraham (1809--1865)","definition":"Sixteenth president of the United States (1809-1865), who led the Union during the Civil War, issued the Emancipation Proclamation in 1863, and articulated the war's democratic purpose in the Gettysburg Address. The narrator includes Lincoln among historical figures supposedly possessing seven hidden or dark years, as a parallel for Duffey's missing years [MTM B2:72].","citations":["MTM B2:72"],"tags":["Historical Figures","History & Memory","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Lines in Her Duffey Book (by Mary Virginia Schaeffer)","definition":"Mary Virginia Schaeffer's speculative account of Duffey as a visitor from one of nine discarded worlds preceding the present tenth world. She expressly admits that the idea has no scriptural or revelatory authority. In her conjecture those worlds remain inhabited, and Duffey's genius, lack of parents and endless circular life derive from another world's conditions. She anticipates his collision with this world and his scattering into seven voyages [MTM B10:291-292].","citations":["MTM B10:291-292"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Literature","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Lines Written in Margin of Perrone's Praelectiones Theologicae","definition":"Margaret Stone's note asking whether Duffey, pulled back after reaching his own edge of time, has drawn his companions back within the same fabric of time. The preceding year feels to her as though it has been lived several times; Salvation Sally reports the same sensation [MTM B10:291].","citations":["MTM B10:291"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Literature","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Lingo","definition":"Finnegan's speech includes Little Italy dialects unintelligible in the next street [A:6]. Anastasia's sarcastic account of Marie Courtois says she 'tosses the lingo' [D:33]. In the account of Duffey's animated creations, sorcerers call their smoky halo the 'unreality fringe' [MTM B5:158].","citations":["A:6","D:33","MTM B5:158"],"tags":["Language & Words","Finnegan","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Linus","definition":"St. Peter's immediate successor as bishop of Rome. The Vatican lists his pontificate as 68-79, although early chronologies differ. Irenaeus says the apostles entrusted the episcopate to Linus and identifies him with the Linus of 2 Timothy 4:21. His traditional martyrdom is historically uncertain; he is named in the Roman Canon, and his feast is September 23. In Henry's dream, Linus, Clement and Cletus are among the crew of the <em>Navicula Petri</em>, whose sailors may be dolls or living people [A:44-45].","citations":["A:44-45"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Saints","Popes","New Testament","Liturgy","Church History","Ships","Dreams"]},{"name":"Lion of the South","definition":"Jimmy Hansen's announced title for Joe Bushmaster in the world championship beer-drinking contest against Hans Schultz, the Milwaukee Seidel [A:19].","citations":["A:19"],"tags":["Alcohol"]},{"name":"Lisbon","definition":"City that may still be looking at its bay in Finnegan's travel poem, which begins 'I'll drive me a boat'; Dotty finds the verses among Finnegan's papers [A:171-173]. Johnny Duckwalk cannot remember whether he met Kristine in Lisbon or Stockholm [D:246].","citations":["A:171-173","D:246"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Little Alcohol (John Indelecio)","definition":"John Indelecio, a burglar and regular at the Old Wooden Ship [D:215]. He does the climbing and high-window work, while his partner John (Big Alcohol) Jereboam handles drills, saws, chisels and pry-bars. They work only when deeply in debt to Lazarus Reilly [D:218].","citations":["D:215","D:218"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Crime","Occupations"]},{"name":"Little Daisey Pancake Turners","definition":"One thousand gross of pancake turners peddled by Hillary Hilton and Finnegan, along with four carloads of acid, before planned dealings in a bridge and distressed cigars [A:253].","citations":["A:253"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Little Dotty Nesbitt","definition":"Old Wooden Ship regular [D:215], one of four Dottys whose identities and marriages Finnegan finds confusing: Nesbitt, Danvers, Peisson and Hulme. Little Dotty and Dotty Danvers married two brothers in Sugarland, Texas; one of those brothers had also married Dotty Hulme [D:220]. Little Dotty's first husband and an earlier husband of Dotty Danvers had the same name, which does not by itself establish that they were the same man [D:221].","citations":["D:215","D:220","D:221"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Little Dutch Eckel","definition":"Engineer at a Texas City refinery who still lives at the Old Wooden Ship because its people let him stay when he was poor. Now the 'prince of the place,' he spends more there than anyone else [D:221].","citations":["D:221"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Money & Trade","Occupations","Social Class"]},{"name":"Little Fat-Head","definition":"One of the familiar valleys Don Lewis names while recalling how he, Santee, and Casper became lost after Yokipoki’s killing. He says that they knew these named valleys, but did not recognize the valley they were in [D:45-46].","citations":["D:45-46"],"tags":["Fictional Places","Pacific Theater","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Little Italy","definition":"A source of the mixed dialects in Finnegan's speech: his 'Little Italy lingos' are unintelligible in the next street [A:6].","citations":["A:6"],"tags":["Italy","Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Little Joker Canyon","definition":"One of the familiar valleys in Don Lewis’s account of the patrol on Willy Jones Island. The soldiers know its name, but cannot identify the valley in which they become lost after Yokipoki’s killing [D:45-46].","citations":["D:45-46"],"tags":["Fictional Places","Pacific Theater","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Little Looking Glass","definition":"The title Finnegan recalls, tentatively as Specchietto, from an Italian reader. A boy lends his face to the figure in his little mirror; the mirror boy also borrows his shadow and name and never gives them back. The original boy remains invisible in a dark corner. Finnegan identifies himself with him, reduced to a comic mask, a monicker and the shadow of a shadow [A:55-56].","citations":["A:55-56"],"tags":["Italy","Finnegan","Literature","Identity"]},{"name":"Little Miss Resurrection","definition":"Finnegan's name for Anastasia Demetriades. After she washes and shaves him and treats his injuries, he associates her name with resurrection and his restored respectability [D:16]. He later addresses her as 'Little Miss Resurrection' while reminding her to love her enemies [D:33].","citations":["D:16","D:33"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Resurrection"]},{"name":"Little Oyster Bar, The","definition":"Establishment called the Little Oyster where Finnegan wins eight hundred dollars at dice during an interval when the world seems newly interesting to him [D:20].","citations":["D:20"],"tags":["Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Little Road Crawler","definition":"Car Finnegan wants to buy. Doll proposes adding it to the Celestial Omnibus and Georgia Kiunker, then drawing lots for the three cars and scattering in different directions [D:185]. The later departure scene calls Doll's car the Road Runner; the passage does not establish that this is a separate fourth vehicle [D:193].","citations":["D:185","D:193"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Living Gothic","definition":"The architectural metaphor applied to Gertrude Brannagan, a style to which Finnegan is partial. About forty-five, with fifteen years' leeway either way, she is full-built, pretty, dark and 'frenchy,' with curly hair and a smile; the narrator likens her volutes and parapets to a fortress [D:200].","citations":["D:200"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Livy (Titus Livius) (64/59 BC--17 AD)","definition":"Roman historian, born in 64 or 59 BCE and traditionally dated to 17 CE at his death, though 12 CE is another proposed date. His Ab Urbe Condita traces Rome from its legendary foundation to 9 BCE; thirty-five of its 142 books survive complete. Its treatment of civic and personal virtue helped shape later readings of Roman history, including Renaissance literature and political thought. Patrick Stranahan considers Livy sufficient for Latin and, with Cobbett, history [A:109].","citations":["A:109"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Literary Figures","Italy","Literature","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Lloyd-Spencer, David Cardinal","definition":"Cardinal of Cardiff, one of the targets named in John Mogul's thirteen-man assassination contract [MB:571]. The primary-double list pairs him with 'Llyod CardiganPembroke of Tywyn' [MB:579], although the later narrative calls Lloyd Cardigan-Pembroke the double of Cardinal Ti, an inconsistency in the text [MB:594].","citations":["MB:571","MB:579","MB:594"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Minor Characters","Crime","Identity"]},{"name":"Locke, John (1632--1704)","definition":"English philosopher and physician (1632-1704), a major exponent of empiricism and liberal political thought. An Essay Concerning Human Understanding makes experience central to knowledge; Two Treatises of Government develops natural rights, consent and the limits of political authority. These works became central to Enlightenment philosophy and later democratic thought. In notebook XXXIV, the schoolboy Casey admits that a supposed howler in Locke ceased to look like one after he read further and understood it better [A:166].","citations":["A:166"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Literary Figures","Literature","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Logorrhea","definition":"Excessive use of words. Sergeant Kelly regards Howell in Ward Fourteen as moderately sane apart from his logorrhea; the narrator adds that Howell talks a lot [A:75-76]. Finnegan also gets logorrhea when he is drinking and celebrating St. Patrick's Day [D:189].","citations":["A:75-76","D:189"],"tags":["Language & Words","Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Lost Years (The Seven","definition":"Duffey's seven years aboard the <em>Argo</em>, beginning when he boards the disguised ship below the Eads Bridge in St. Louis during his fifth or sixth youth. The years are not consecutive: land adventures are excluded from the count and do not reduce his lifespan [MTM B2:71] [MTM B13:406]. They mix past, present and future in intervals held apart from ordinary time, sometimes revisitable without fracturing reality [MTM B10:298]. In their own context they are the Seven Golden Years; the Fourteenth Voyage belongs to them and is certain to occur, without excluding other contingencies [MTM B10:300]. Duffey obtains the Seven Christs in these years and remembers women he says he cannot revisit [MTM B3:103] [MTM B4:110]. During the hidden years, before Casey's conception, he gives Gabriel Szymansky Casey's talisman; other talismans are bestowed during or possibly before them [MTM B4:120-121]. Bagby somehow sends weekly letters throughout Duffey's absence, and later compares Duffey's fractured, adolescent state with his condition during the Lost Years [MTM B4:145] [MTM B10:302]. When the seven-arrowed sign blocks an ordinary future, Duffey can proceed only through their unusual context [MTM B10:290]. The Sea of the Seven Lost Years has scattered channels and shores and connects to the Sea of Amnesia by a hidden strait [MTM B13:406]. Later Duffey can re-enter from shores at different ages, become young aboard ship and leave through an earlier shore with his long life still ahead [MTM B13:445].","citations":["MTM B2:71","MTM B13:406","MTM B10:298","MTM B10:300","MTM B3:103","MTM B4:110","MTM B4:120-121","MTM B4:145","MTM B10:302","MTM B10:290","MTM B13:445"],"tags":["Ships","Seas & Oceans","Duffey","Magic & Sorcery","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Love","definition":"Affection, desire, family attachment and compassion recur in overlapping forms. Duffey and Lily Koch love each other, though Lily directs him toward her sister Letitia [MTM B1:30] [MTM B3:90]. Duffey declares love for Gretchen Sisler, almost nine, while he is almost sixteen; Evelyn London calls him her boy and her love [MTM B2:39-40]. He feels sudden, frightening love for Mary Louise Byrne, called his sister, consort and an exteriorized part of himself; their actual kinship remains uncertain. Beth Keegan declares love at their first meeting, Mary Louise says their whole circle loves one another, and Beth calls Charley Murray her second love [MTM B2:55-56] [MTM B2:60] [MTM B2:67]. Duffey also declares love for nine-year-old Charlotte Mullens [MTM B3:78]. Letitia tells Duffey that he already loves her as they arrange their marriage; August Koch's concern for Lily expresses a father's love [MTM B3:91-92] [MTM B3:102]. Duffey's affections extend to Letitia, Lily, Elinore Koch, women in St. Louis and the unrevisitable loves of his hidden years [MTM B4:110]. Patrick Stranahan and Duffey love Teresa Piccone [MTM B5:163]. The narrator describes Hans as loving everyone, with a particular affection for Trinali Peterson [A:224]; Finnegan's resolve to avenge Anastasia follows the loss of the person he loves best [D:157-158]. Paul’s exhortation to dwell on things loved and honored appears among the guiding documents [MTM B5:175]. In the Casey commentaries, Demetrio Glauch questions whether extreme compassion for the Devil has become sinful; Hilary Hilton reluctantly obeys the sign that Casey must be loved and describes Casey's love of farce. Finnegan affirms Casey's genuine compassion for vulnerable creatures and natural beauty while lamenting its misdirection toward a powerful monster [PG:518-519] [PG:531-532] [PG:543-544].","citations":["MTM B1:30","MTM B3:90","MTM B2:39-40","MTM B2:55-56","MTM B2:60","MTM B2:67","MTM B3:78","MTM B3:91-92","MTM B3:102","MTM B4:110","MTM B5:163","A:224","D:157-158","MTM B5:175","PG:518-519","PG:531-532","PG:543-544"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Women Characters","Family","Identity","New Testament","Theology"]},{"name":"Loyalty","definition":"The Fiefdoms maintain an uneven loyalty in the struggle against the world's enemy, while Absalom Stein is absolutely loyal to ordered, structured arts [MTM B8:246]. X compares the Devil's awaiting servants with the loyal subjects of enchanted kings who expect their return [MTM B6:205]. Gautier's seven youngest goat kids share loyalty to the ideal of climbing a fallen sycamore [A:194]. Colonel Laycheck threatens to charge Casey with disloyalty and impose ten years if he continues opposing Stein's instruction [A:47]. X also speculates that Ifreann Gregorovitch may have felt loyalty to the Krakow region, despite his rebellion against authority and opportunistic appeals to patriotism [D:280].","citations":["MTM B8:246","MTM B6:205","A:194","A:47","D:280"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","Soldiers","Cosmic Struggle","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Lsuno College","definition":"New Orleans institution where Danny Degas is a history professor [MTM B11:322]. The name points to LSUNO, Louisiana State University in New Orleans, the institution subsequently known as the University of New Orleans.","citations":["MTM B11:322"],"tags":["New Orleans","Occupations","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Luin","definition":"Luin, or Monday, one of Gautier's seven youngest goat kids. She alone cannot climb the fallen sycamore until Finnegan gives her a boost, and the others acknowledge his help [A:194-196].","citations":["A:194-196"],"tags":["Finnegan","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Luluway","definition":"Joseph's correction of Finnegan's 'lulus' as the plural for diamonds. Joseph says Finnegan will learn the word and must develop his own smuggling technique rather than copy another man's [D:95].","citations":["D:95"],"tags":["Language & Words","Finnegan","Crime"]},{"name":"Lupido","definition":"Cafe proprietor who favors candlelight. He gives Trinali Peterson stubs after burning the candles only partway down, and she steals longer candles from him. He also supplies her with old bread and leftover wine [A:228]. When her landlord cuts off the heat and she barricades herself in her room to resist eviction, she lowers a basket from the window and Lupido feeds her through it. Her 'old moustache' is Lupido himself, not a name for the bread [A:229].","citations":["A:228","A:229"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Food & Dining","Alcohol","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Luther, Martin","definition":"German theologian (1483-1546) and leading figure of the Protestant Reformation. In the fictional epigraph from Carmody Overlark's Studies in the imagination, Luther appears with Plato, Homer, Aquinas, Rabelais, Smart, Rasputin, Toussaint L'Ouverture and Steinmetz among people alleged to have a Neanderthal-type head. The passage associates its low brow and enlarged back-brain with unusual imagination, spiritual gifts and criminality [D:270-272].","citations":["D:270-272"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Church History","Germany","Literature"]},{"name":"Luzon","definition":"The largest Philippine island. After the Second World War, the Dirty Five wait there for a ship home with no duties, expecting to leave in about a month; Hans reproaches Finnegan for his red eyes [A:63]. They drink with Captain Sammy Guiterrez at the Buffalo Bar [A:66]. Hans knows of two white horses nearby and subsequently rides the stallion as part of the group's Argonautic play [A:68].","citations":["A:63","A:66","A:68"],"tags":["Islands","Pacific Theater","World War II","Soldiers","Bars & Taverns","Finnegan","Argonautica"]},{"name":"Lynne, Janeway Celeste","definition":"Janeway Celeste Lynne, one of three twenty-one-year-old cousins who arrive by rowboat in waterlogged rural Louisiana during the <em>Argo</em>’s visit, only two and a half minutes after Honeybucket Kincaid activates his frequency apparatus [MTM B13:416-419]. She says that an extraordinary tide seemed to catch their boat and propel it along. Duffey points out that they came against the ocean tide and attributes their movement to Honeybucket Kincaid’s frequency modulation. Introducing herself as Janeway, she names her cousins Candy Sue Pirogue and Etta Mae Mansion to the company, which includes Leonard Archive, Duffey and Biloxi Brannagan [MTM B13:416-419].","citations":["MTM B13:416-419"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family","Duffey","Seas & Oceans","Magic & Sorcery","Minor Characters","Louisiana","Ships"]},{"name":"Lynx","definition":"Animal in a quotation Casey attributes to Aristotle in notebook XXXIII: the lynx supposedly hides its urine because people use it for purposes including signets. Casey calls this one of Aristotle's better observations [A:165-166]. A closely corresponding ancient account occurs in Theophrastus's On Stones, section28, where lyngourion used for seals is connected with concealed animal urine; this is a historical belief about a supposed stone, not established lynx biology.","citations":["A:165-166"],"tags":["Literature","Language & Words","Folklore"]},{"name":"Lyonesse","definition":"Legendary lost land off Cornwall. During Hans Schultz’s New York period, the narrator describes the city as more than Baghdad, Paris, Heidelberg, and Lyonesse [A:224].","citations":["A:224"],"tags":["Folklore","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"M","definition":"See Melchisedech Duffey.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Machiavelli, Niccolò (1469--1527)","definition":"Italian political writer, author of <em>The Prince</em> (1532). Machiavelli is among the writers whose ideas Casey mixes into <em>The Answer</em>; the narration places his contributions among the newspaper’s worse borrowings [MTM B4:114] [MTM B4:118].","citations":["MTM B4:114","MTM B4:118"],"tags":["Italy","Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Madame Le Mourcheor","definition":"Maker of Old Original Juju Joy Pills in New Orleans. Don Barnaby praises her recipe of frankincense-tree sawdust, honey from the meadows around Megapolis in Arcady, and a black cat with glanders. He says she omits the fanciful additives used by other makers, including a red-eyed goat’s stomach stone and a swamp dragon’s blood [D:249].","citations":["D:249"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","New Orleans","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Mae (Old Wooden Ship)","definition":"One of the women living at the Old Wooden Ship who are called “salt water widows.” She is also named in its roster of resident regulars [D:216] [D:221].","citations":["D:216","D:221"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Mae, Etta","definition":"Earlier incomplete heading, which treated Mae as the surname. See <em>Mansion, Etta Mae</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Women Characters"]},{"name":"Magellan, Ferdinand (c. 1480--1521)","definition":"Portuguese navigator whose Spanish expedition of 1519–1522 made the first circumnavigation of the globe. Magellan died in the Philippines in 1521, before the voyage was completed. Dotty invokes him when she teases Finnegan for not knowing whether he has been around the world; Finnegan replies that Magellan was in command and he was not [A:145].","citations":["A:145"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Sailing","Spain","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Magic Lantern","definition":"Anastasia’s assembly of reflecting mirrors and a lantern, housed in an old boat’s binnacle case. Explicitly distinguished from a projector, it accompanies her and Finnegan’s nighttime vigil [D:131].","citations":["D:131"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Magkaano ito?","definition":"Finnegan’s Tagalog question about the price of liquor at Falo and Nauti’s unfinished cabaret. Nauti answers that it costs a peso a bottle because they are the first customers [A:64].","citations":["A:64"],"tags":["Language & Words","Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Magnesite","definition":"Material that Casey’s seventh high-school notebook entry says is used chiefly to make refractory bricks for furnace linings [A:162-163].","citations":["A:162-163"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Magnifica","definition":"Admiring Spanish exclamation directed at Marie Courtois by a man ashore on the Mexican side. When Anastasia tells him that Marie can carry a man on her shoulders, he answers “Interesante” [D:57].","citations":["D:57"],"tags":["Language & Words","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Magpie","definition":"Bird loved and married by Amoy during his supposed life as a red-winged blackbird and jewel thief. Ignatius Ti calls her a common magpie, but Amoy protests and Ti interprets his words as meaning an uncommon individual of the common species [A:80].","citations":["A:80"],"tags":["Shapeshifting","Crime","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Magus","definition":"Magician or sorcerer, the role of Melchisedech Duffey. The opening describes his many mantles of magic and foretells their loss; his strongest gift is manufacturing persons, or marking unfinished human clay with his fabricator’s mark [MTM B1:5]. He summons an invisible giant’s hand to ring a bell [MTM B1:15], and his school confessor accepts his vocation while warning him against pride [MTM B1:28]. Casey is named one of his creations [MTM B4:118]. Duffey defends his powers against Doctor Morris Poor [MTM B4:128], though Margaret Stone calls him a “moth-eaten sorcerer” [MTM B4:112]. He produces a living mouse after offering, without certainty that he can do so, to produce a dinosaur [MTM B5:170]. The Loosed Devil seeks his creative services; Duffey asserts that he actually creates and refuses compulsion [MTM B10:302-303]. Later the magus recognizes himself as Melchisedech, King of Salem, called Duffey in latter-day contexts [MTM B13:402].","citations":["MTM B1:5","MTM B1:15","MTM B1:28","MTM B4:118","MTM B4:128","MTM B4:112","MTM B5:170","MTM B10:302-303","MTM B13:402"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Giants & Titans","Creation","Sin & Redemption","The Devil"]},{"name":"Mahoney, Cleo","definition":"Ursuline Academy student and playwright of <em>Seven Roads</em>. She is among the girls who explain the painted sign “The Future Begins Right Here,” gives Duffey the script, and describes its problem: what happens when Melchisedech, who cannot have an ending, reaches the end of the world [MTM B9:264-265]. At the Ghost Coffee Shop she calls the roads seven scenarios or variant futures, selected through encounters with particular potholes [MTM B10:293]. She describes the play as a study of frustrations [MTM B10:297]. Later she supplies revised lines that strengthen its Fifth Road, in which she plays the Hoyden [MTM B12:352].","citations":["MTM B9:264-265","MTM B10:293","MTM B10:297","MTM B12:352"],"tags":["Women Characters","Theater","Prophecy & Visions","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Main Frequency Modulator","definition":"First frequency modulator, linked to all the others. Duffey dives head-first into it while Biloxi Brannagan prevents interference. Lightning and a stench follow; Duffey emerges with his clothing and body hair burned off. He says he has abolished the Puff Fish frequency in past, present, and future without breaking the apparatus [MTM B13:420].","citations":["MTM B13:420"],"tags":["Duffey","Time & Eternity","Creation"]},{"name":"Mainland","definition":"Main body of land, opposed to an island. In her dispute with Hans and Marie about religious authority, Salvation Sally invokes island dwellers; Hans supplies their insistence that their island is the mainland, and Sally suggests the analogy may apply to her Catholic listeners [A:8].","citations":["A:8"],"tags":["Islands","Theology","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Majesty, The","definition":"Title prescribed for the Devil at the New Catholic Press Guild meeting. The introducer calls the being an androgyne beyond grammar and directs that it be adored by snickering [MTM B8:239-240]. Its human appearance conceals a giant whose mind Duffey enters [MTM B8:240-241]. That mind declares its aim of undoing Creation through hatred, conspiracy, slander, disorder, and final nullity [MTM B8:242]. “Holding More Meaningful Liturgical Services” disguises its promised “Devouring of Entrails in the Holy Places.” Duffey finds that the public speech and the interior speech convey the same message beneath different vocabulary [MTM B8:243].","citations":["MTM B8:239-240","MTM B8:240-241","MTM B8:242","MTM B8:243"],"tags":["The Devil","Giants & Titans","Duffey","Catholic Church","Journalism","Language & Words","Creation"]},{"name":"Maker Man","definition":"Teresa Piccone Stranahan’s name for Duffey in a private letter. She says he lacks firsthand knowledge of the experiences she describes, instead raiding other people’s minds and pretending to have known their contents already: “That Maker Man, he just doesn’t have it” [MTM B6:188].","citations":["MTM B6:188"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Malaki ibon","definition":"“Big bird,” the self-description of the small parakeet Amoy in the narrative’s Tagalog-Parakeet language [A:79].","citations":["A:79"],"tags":["Language & Words","Identity"]},{"name":"Malay boy","definition":"Boy who enters the hospital ward, calls the amnesiac Sergeant by name, and tells him it is time to go home. The pair then find themselves in Sydney, where the boy leaves him at the Plaza Hotel to visit old friends [A:88]. Back in the islands, they dive from a canoe named <em>Aral</em>, <em>Bumi</em>, and <em>Aragh</em>, which the Sergeant recognizes as the <em>Argo</em> [A:89].","citations":["A:88","A:89"],"tags":["Islands","Australia","Finnegan","Ships","Identity"]},{"name":"Malay boy (2)","definition":"Uncertain entry. The cited Tangier episode describes a little Arab boy who organizes runners for Finnegan, but does not identify a Malay boy among them [D:104].","citations":["D:104"],"tags":["Africa","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Malaya","definition":"Region off which, in Finnegan’s account of island origins, a bronze bowl broke into long pieces that became islands [A:35].","citations":["A:35"],"tags":["Folklore","Islands","Finnegan","Creation"]},{"name":"Malayan dialect","definition":"What the outsiders called Gorgios mistake for the speech taught to islanders called Jojos. Finnegan explains that the Dirty Five taught them Polish while the Jojos themselves believed it was English [A:73].","citations":["A:73"],"tags":["Islands","Language & Words","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Maleficent","definition":"The harmful or evil side in Papa Devil’s formula for the struggle: the old game of monsters and men, policemen and thieves, the Beneficient and the Maleficent [D:120].","citations":["D:120"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Cosmic Struggle","The Devil"]},{"name":"Maliit ibon","definition":"“Little bird,” the name the parakeet Amoy angrily rejects with “hindi, hindi.” The narration glosses his reply as “no, no, no” [A:79].","citations":["A:79"],"tags":["Language & Words","Identity"]},{"name":"Malmo","definition":"Malmö, the Swedish city. The poem opening Finnegan’s chapter says that Malmo is crisp in the fall [A:171-172].","citations":["A:171-172"],"tags":["Literature","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Malory","definition":"One of the surnames used by Gregory McColum Papadiabolous, the Irish Papa Devil or Papadiabolous II, according to X [D:285].","citations":["D:285"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Ireland","Identity"]},{"name":"Mamma Monica","definition":"Narrator’s designation for Monica Stranahan as she asks Maurine to help with Vincent and Theresa’s wedding arrangements [A:104-105].","citations":["A:104-105"],"tags":["Family","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Mammon","definition":"Wealth personified as an object of worship. At Babylonia Bagascia, the Unilateral Workers’ League receives a money offering and enthrones its Mammon statue during the ceremonies outside Conclave Hall [MB:566].","citations":["MB:566"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Clubs & Societies","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Man from Minneapolis, The","definition":"One of Endymeon Ellenbogen’s three published novels, financed through subsidy presses. Endymeon claims that his novels have been solid successes with the people who count, while wondering whether he can afford further such successes [A:237].","citations":["A:237"],"tags":["Literature","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Man of Mystery","definition":"Code name for the Antichrist whom the three <em>Argo</em> Masters try to keep from boarding their ship [MTM B13:454] [MTM B13:456]. He is subsequently manifested as Kasmir Gorshok, Prince Casimir, Casey Szymansky, and Peleus, within a future whose shimmering marks its lack of absolute finality [MTM B13:454] [MTM B13:462].","citations":["MTM B13:454","MTM B13:456","MTM B13:462"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","Ships","Identity","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Man-killing panther","definition":"Panther in Finnegan’s catalogue of the terrors of the Chosky Bottoms, a legendary spook place said to be just beyond the next ridge [D:274].","citations":["D:274"],"tags":["Folklore","Finnegan","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Man-sized germ","definition":"Biloxi Brannagan’s image for the agent of the world’s recurring “brain damage.” He calls it a parasite, “the alien within,” and fears it will destroy both its host and itself [D:202-203].","citations":["D:202-203"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Mandarin picture (Adam Scanlon might sell)","definition":"Adam Scanlon painting recommended by Jimmy when Hans proposes furnishing his flat in the Chinese style. Jimmy jokes that Scanlon might accept a thousand dollars, then discusses a price nearer fifteen. Hans answers that for a thousand dollars he would paint a Mandarin himself [A:229-230].","citations":["A:229-230"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Manford, Philip","definition":"Young man from the school of music who accompanies Duffey on outings with Mary Louise and Beth Keegan in St. Louis. They sometimes use Manford’s Overland automobile for rides, dinner, and theatre visits [MTM B2:58].","citations":["MTM B2:58"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Music","Duffey","Theater"]},{"name":"Manila","definition":"City to which the group travels by truck after supper on New Year’s Eve. Just after midnight they begin boarding the ship for home [A:70].","citations":["A:70"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","Ships"]},{"name":"Manolo","definition":"Charlotte Mullens’s son, who accompanies her and Gloria Mullens to Duffey’s north-side Chicago hotel [MTM B3:81]. When Duffey suggests that her son and sister are projections or ventriloquist’s figures, Charlotte explains that she uses pliable flesh-and-blood people to make compliant figures [MTM B3:83]. In Duffey’s nocturnal vision, she commands Manolo and Gloria to stop the stevedore attacking him [MTM B3:85]. Manolo and Gloria are later found tortured and murdered in the hotel; Letitia says the killers sought information the pair did not possess [MTM B3:97].","citations":["MTM B3:81","MTM B3:83","MTM B3:85","MTM B3:97"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Family","Crime","Death & Mortality","Duffey"]},{"name":"Mansion, Etta Mae","definition":"Etta Mae Mansion, cousin of Janeway Celeste Lynne and Candy Sue Pirogue. The three twenty-one-year-old women arrive by rowboat after Honeybucket Kincaid activates frequencies intended to attract them [MTM B13:419].","citations":["MTM B13:419"],"tags":["Women Characters"]},{"name":"Mansions (in Father's House)","definition":"John 14:2 allusion in the Professor’s account of Heaven and his own return to the Church. He assures Hans that the Father’s House has mansions even for charlatans [A:214-215].","citations":["A:214-215"],"tags":["New Testament","Heaven & Hell","Scripture"]},{"name":"Mansville Burgess Howland","definition":"Brother named by Howland Jerome Howland when he tells Finnegan about his father, John Commodore Howland, and his seven brothers. Their father gave them names from old families he disliked, apparently to irritate those families [A:178].","citations":["A:178"],"tags":["Family","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Mantles of magic","definition":"Duffey’s initial abundance of magical gifts, most of which he is expected to lose. The opening enumerates his production of gold, invasion of minds, limited movement through time, command of invisible giants, and fabrication of persons [MTM B1:5].","citations":["MTM B1:5"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Giants & Titans","Duffey","Time & Eternity","Creation"]},{"name":"Manuel","definition":"Stowaway and self-declared spy aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em> [D:54]. He comes from farther south than Mexico and seeks the source of a power turning men into devils; he fears that Papa Devil will kill him [D:56]. After the Naxos fighting, he is alive and crying when Finnegan meets him. The two leave the dead behind and hide together on the mountain [D:146-148].","citations":["D:54","D:56","D:146-148"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Ships","Finnegan","Crime"]},{"name":"Map of the City and its environs","definition":"Map obtained by Dotty when she reaches the city where Finnegan is staying. Ignacio traces the routes of Finnegan’s daily walks, and Dotty buys a pistol before setting out to find him [A:281]. The setting is Havana, identified by the lawyer’s letter that brings her there; Ignacio has already told Finnegan that he is not in New Orleans [A:276-277].","citations":["A:281","A:276-277"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Maps (Angela Cosquin's Father)","definition":"Maps hand-drawn by Angela Cosquin’s cartographer father on old leather, vellum, and parchment. According to Angela, he first made decorative showpieces, then supplied confidence men with purported treasure maps and old affidavits. Treasure-chest marks such as T, K, and BP suggested Teach, Kidd, and Bartolomo Portuguese [D:259-260]. He also invented shipwreck maps, placing wrecks where he thought ships ought logically to founder among the Antilles’ passages. His master map showed fifty or more real or imaginary wrecks [D:260]. Named ships include <em>Mary Jane</em>, <em>Red Fox</em>, <em>Halcon</em>, <em>Loutre</em>, <em>Jabali</em>, <em>Moonraker</em>, <em>Estrella del Mar</em>, <em>Torre del Marifi</em>, <em>Virgen la mas Benigna</em>, <em>Casa del Oro</em>, <em>Golden Mastiff</em>, <em>Indiaman</em>, <em>Hind</em>, <em>Crane</em>, <em>Blaireau</em>, <em>Megčre</em>, and <em>Renard Volante</em>. Buyers could choose a wreck and even move its location before commissioning supporting papers [D:261].","citations":["D:259-260","D:260","D:261"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Ships","Shipwrecks","Crime","Family","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Maravillosa","definition":"Admiring Spanish exclamation spoken about Marie Courtois by a man ashore on the Mexican side, paired with “Magnifica” [D:57].","citations":["D:57"],"tags":["Language & Words","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Marbles","definition":"Sack in Phoebe Anne Ankerson’s suitcase, which Finnegan and Doll have stolen. Its label says the marbles once belonged to Aaron and are intended for Harold Michael; some are agates [D:172].","citations":["D:172"],"tags":["Crime","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"March","definition":"Month of the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s arrival at Naxos, dated March 14, Passion Sunday [D:115]. At Finnegan and Anastasia’s later vigil, the March night is expected to turn chilly, and the moon lacks a week of being full [D:132].","citations":["D:115","D:132"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"March 4th","definition":"Date in Trinali Peterson’s poem title “To Trinali at Dawn on March 4th.” She says she wrote it on the previous Monday, March 4, and that it symbolizes all March 4ths from the beginning [A:226].","citations":["A:226"],"tags":["Literature","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"March 6, 1933","definition":"Date on which banker Adrian Hilton says the Depression began. He attributes it to the bank moratorium of March 6-9, 1933, in his polemical account of banking and political power [MTM B4:123].","citations":["MTM B4:123"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Historical Figures"]},{"name":"Marcus Tullius Cicero (106--43 BC)","definition":"Uncertain identification. The cited <em>Archipelago</em> passage refers to Cicero as a destination for buying cigars [A:253]; the cited <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> passage names Cicero Brazil among Duffey’s academic acquaintances [MTM B3:99]. Neither passage identifies the Roman orator.","citations":["A:253","MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Literary Figures"]},{"name":"Mare Nectaris","definition":"Lunar sea that the <em>Argo</em> is said, in one of its old logs, to have sailed [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Margie","definition":"Familiar form of Margaret Murphey’s name in Loy Larkin’s letter to Finnegan. Loy reports Margie’s love, and Margaret adds “Me too” beneath Loy’s signature [A:27].","citations":["A:27"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Margie la Maga","definition":"Theresa Piccone’s name for herself as a magician. She mentions it to Finnegan while explaining that puppetry, concertina playing, and tricks have shaped her fingers [A:108].","citations":["A:108"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Finnegan","Theater","Identity"]},{"name":"Marianao Coast","definition":"Coast a few miles from Havana where X says Finnegan and Dotty were killed in a futuristic episode, while refusing to say their deaths were final [MTM B8:238]. Duffey later travels there to investigate Finnegan’s disappearance, guided in part by <em>The Resurrection of Count Finnegan</em>, a painting of his apparent grave and emergence from an ocean cave [MTM B12:367-368].","citations":["MTM B8:238","MTM B12:367-368"],"tags":["Caribbean","Finnegan","Death & Mortality","Resurrection"]},{"name":"Marie Celeste","definition":"Johnny Duckwalk’s mistaken name for the derelict ship when he accuses Le Marin of recycling its story. Le Marin, calling himself a purist, corrects it to <em>Mary Celeste</em> [D:246].","citations":["D:246"],"tags":["Ships","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Marietta (Aunt)","definition":"One of Henry Salvatore’s two aunts living in his family’s neighborhood, the other being Emily [A:122-123].","citations":["A:122-123"],"tags":["Louisiana","Family","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Marin, Le","definition":"Charles Le Marin, one of Finnegan’s companions with Don Barnaby and Johnny Duckwalk in a society described as a dark counterpart to the Dirty Five [A:188] [D:233]. He tells how Castleman’s men infiltrated the <em>Sarcophagus</em>, replacing crewmen with lookalikes at successive ports. His account leaves his own replacement uncertain; he survives the later killing of the loyal crew by hiding and eventually swimming ashore [D:244-246].","citations":["A:188","D:233","D:244-246"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Finnegan","Clubs & Societies","Ships","Identity"]},{"name":"Marine Room","definition":"Orlando bar where Finnegan and Doll Delancy trade jokes about his knowledge, drinking, and supposed lack of brains [D:190].","citations":["D:190"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Mark (of Cain)","definition":"Biblical mark associated with Cain in Genesis 4:15. In his transport upon shadowy history, Finnegan asks whether Cain belonged to his people and which people bear the mark, describing a lower facial projection like another nose [D:212-213].","citations":["D:212-213"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Mark (Salvatore)","definition":"One of Henry Francis Salvatore’s seven brothers, in the family of Lawrence Salvatore and Mary Genevieve Hebert of Morgan City, Louisiana [A:122].","citations":["A:122"],"tags":["Louisiana","Family","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Mark McClatchy","definition":"See <em>McClatchy, Mark</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Market Street","definition":"Uncertain location. The cited account places Duffey’s newly purchased St. Louis building on Walnut Street, not Market Street [MTM B2:40].","citations":["MTM B2:40"],"tags":["Duffey","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Marks (Cabalistic, on fingernails)","definition":"Witch-signs that Finnegan jokingly says appear on the moons of Theresa Piccone’s fingernails only on Fridays. She replies that she will wear gloves [A:108].","citations":["A:108"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Marks (Cabalistic, on lead weight)","definition":"Meaningless cabalistic-looking designs invented by Finnegan and marked on the lead weight he throws through a hotel window during his attack on Seaworthy’s gathering [D:163-164].","citations":["D:163-164"],"tags":["Finnegan","Weapons","Crime"]},{"name":"Marks (Finishing marks on Sulem's statues)","definition":"Finishing details Sulem proposes that his uncles add after Hans has roughed out wooden figures with lathes and power saws. The marks would help the machine-made objects seem primitive. Hans refuses the production scheme [A:59].","citations":["A:59"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Occupations","Family","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Marks (on wrists of Finnegan/Seaworthy)","definition":"Scar or tattoo inside Seaworthy’s left wrist. He claims to see the same mark beneath Finnegan’s skin, although Finnegan denies having it [D:10]. After the Naxos shooting, X notices an octopus mark on Finnegan’s wrist; Finnegan says it appeared at the shooting and now has that shape [D:156].","citations":["D:10","D:156"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Mars","definition":"Planet to which Hans compares the barren surface of islands passed on the way to Luzon. He jokingly cites people who have visited both places before explaining his account of the birds’ nests harvested there [A:62].","citations":["A:62"],"tags":["Islands","Pacific Theater"]},{"name":"Marshal of the Opera","definition":"Official who tries to persuade both the Finnegan Image and Margaret Stone to withdraw their demonstrations of humanity at the Decatur Street Opera House. He calls their flames false and their acts pranks; both refuse [MTM B11:348-349].","citations":["MTM B11:348-349"],"tags":["Theater","New Orleans","Identity","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Martinique","definition":"Origin of the chef at Howland Jerome Howland’s Club Royal in Chicago [A:246]. Angela Cosquin also names Martinique among what she calls the seven true passages through the Antilles when describing her father’s shipwreck maps [D:260].","citations":["A:246","D:260"],"tags":["Caribbean","Food & Dining","Clubs & Societies","Shipwrecks"]},{"name":"Martyr-Bell","definition":"Second of the summoning bells, brought by Count Finnegan on a raft to a mooring-rock in the shoals north of San Simeon, two days after the Sea-Bell began to sound [MB:575]. It comes from Ste. Genevieve, where the narrative says English raiders killed thirty-nine Christians in 1594. After the raiders’ ship <em>Lord Cramner</em> was wrecked, its salvaged bronze cannon were cast into a bell that rang by itself when enemies of the faith approached [MB:576].","citations":["MB:575","MB:576"],"tags":["Liturgy","Finnegan","Shipwrecks","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Marx, Karl (1818--1883)","definition":"Political writer and, with Friedrich Engels, author of <em>The Communist Manifesto</em> (1848). After reading the New Testament, Stein ranks it alongside Marx and Freud among important books, within his claim that all great books were written by Jews [A:69]. Karl Marx also appears in the place-name Karl Marx Stadt, where the <em>Argo</em> Masters interrupt Ralph Rolfe’s new system of logic [MTM B13:427].","citations":["A:69","MTM B13:427"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Germany"]},{"name":"Mary (Old Wooden Ship)","definition":"Resident of the Old Wooden Ship, listed among its regulars and the women called “salt water widows” [D:216] [D:221]. She works at the clubs and disputes Dutch Duquesne’s boasts about his job and tips, saying that he is a kitchen boy who cleans fish and shucks oysters [D:221].","citations":["D:216","D:221"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Occupations"]},{"name":"Mary Jean","definition":"Mary Jean, a girl Casey has known since childhood, who marries Hillary Hilton and becomes involved with Casey while he is at college [A:168-169]. Mary Catherine later reports that Mary Jean has married Casey, while considering the union invalid because Mary Jean’s first marriage was in the Church [A:248]. Hillary says he gave Mary Jean the house in their settlement and Casey moved in [A:251-252]. Later <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> passages again call Mary Jean Hilton’s wife and pair the two together [MTM B12:378] [MTM B12:383].","citations":["A:168-169","A:248","A:251-252","MTM B12:378","MTM B12:383"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Family","Sacraments"]},{"name":"Mas de azul?","definition":"“Bluer?” Ignacio’s Spanish repetition of Finnegan’s remark that the people have become bluer. Finnegan still believes himself in New Orleans, though Ignacio later tells him that he is elsewhere [A:270] [A:276].","citations":["A:270","A:276"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Mass","definition":"The Catholic liturgy recurring throughout the cycle. Hans and Marie stop for Mass at St. Marys after talking all night [A:9-10]. Duffey attends at St. Malachy’s in Chicago, where he first sees his future wife [MTM B3:87-88]. Monica reports that Papa Piccone plans accordion music at Vincent and Theresa’s wedding Mass, but Maurine says they are teasing her [A:104-105]. Ramires and Elena Musquiz find Finnegan on their way to Mass in D’Hannis [A:203]. In his later days in Havana, he rises early to attend Mass before his daily walks [A:276-277].","citations":["A:9-10","MTM B3:87-88","A:104-105","A:203","A:276-277"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Liturgy","Finnegan","Duffey","Family"]},{"name":"Mass of the Holy Precursor Melchisedech","definition":"Mass said over Duffey’s bones aboard the <em>Argo</em> by Biloxi Brannagan in his person of St. Brandon. The narration assigns it to April 31 under the old calendar [MTM B13:443].","citations":["MTM B13:443"],"tags":["Liturgy","Ships","Duffey","Saints"]},{"name":"Mast-Head (Bark/Barque depiction)","definition":"Finnegan’s drawing of the endangered little boat Henry has dreamed, used as the masthead of <em>The Bark</em> [A:121-122]. In his attack in <em>The Crock</em>, Casey says the barque is wrongly shown as square-rigged rather than composite-rigged and declares it “not Seaworthy” [A:128].","citations":["A:121-122","A:128"],"tags":["Ships","Journalism","Visual Arts","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Mast-Head (Towers school paper)","definition":"Image of Gregory Hall’s towers on <em>The Towers</em>, the St. Bonaventure school paper dominated by boys from that hall. The rival <em>Chambers</em> uses Audifax O’Hanlon’s drawings of chamber pots with similar silhouettes [A:167].","citations":["A:167"],"tags":["Journalism","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Master Map","definition":"Angela Cosquin’s father’s most elaborate map, showing fifty or more real or imaginary sunken ships. It hangs in his study and attracts confidence men who commission supposed treasure maps from it [D:260]. Named wrecks include <em>Mary Jane</em>, <em>Red Fox</em>, <em>Halcon</em>, <em>Loutre</em>, <em>Jabali</em>, <em>Moonraker</em>, <em>Estrella del Mar</em>, <em>Torre del Marifi</em>, <em>Virgen la mas Benigna</em>, <em>Casa del Oro</em>, <em>Golden Mastiff</em>, <em>Indiaman</em>, <em>Hind</em>, <em>Crane</em>, <em>Blaireau</em>, <em>Megčre</em>, and <em>Renard Volante</em>. Buyers may choose a different location for a wreck as well as supporting papers [D:261].","citations":["D:260","D:261"],"tags":["Ships","Shipwrecks","Money & Trade","Crime","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Master of the Ship","definition":"Office among the masters of the <em>Argo</em>. Duffey says the man he is to collect at Biloxi has waited thirty years and is himself a Master of the Ship [MTM B13:401]. The man is Biloxi Brannagan, whose wife confirms the long wait [MTM B13:409].","citations":["MTM B13:401","MTM B13:409"],"tags":["Ships","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Masters of the Argo","definition":"Members of the <em>Argo</em>’s interdependent company, including Duffey, Biloxi Brannagan, and Kasmir Gorshok. Each plays a particular role in the others’ lives [MTM B13:446] [MTM B13:454]. An amnestic mechanism normally prevents them remembering the ship while ashore [MTM B12:386]. Absent sailors leave working residues aboard; returning Masters absorb their effigies, which then become part of them [MTM B13:415] [MTM B13:449]. Their recoveries include the Lord’s Table, Sancgreal, Holy Shroud, Ring of the Nebelungs, Philosopher’s Stone, tomb of St. Jude, Northwest Passage, Roland’s Horn, Alaric’s Sword, Aaron’s Rod, Seamless Garment, Baptist’s Head, Magic Flute, Great Mogul Diamond, Iron Crown, and Lost Dutchman Mine. They deliberately lose the perilous Sword and Scabbard of Saint Sécaire three times [MTM B13:422]. They intervene against Ralph Rolfe’s perverse logic, though Casey lets one crucial scrap of writing escape, and they cure a young man of stuttering at Weinsburg [MTM B13:427] [MTM B13:430].","citations":["MTM B13:446","MTM B13:454","MTM B12:386","MTM B13:415","MTM B13:449","MTM B13:422","MTM B13:427","MTM B13:430"],"tags":["Ships","Crew & Sailors","Duffey","Creation","Time & Eternity","Identity"]},{"name":"Mastodon","definition":"Uncertain entry. The supplied citation does not contain the asserted “Paean to Archipelago” comparison with the mastodont [A:9].","citations":["A:9"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Mat (woven)","definition":"In Finnegan’s island-creation story, a woven mat falls into the ocean and is torn apart by great sea turtles. The pieces put down roots as green, fiber-colored islands; Guinea is the stitched center left by the turtles, its stitching forming the mountainous spine [A:35].","citations":["A:35"],"tags":["Folklore","Islands","Finnegan","Creation"]},{"name":"Mathematics","definition":"For the schoolboy Casey, almost the whole of thought. His notebook’s entry XXXVII dismisses Johnny Carruthers’s <em>Physics without Mathematics</em> as a head without brains and declares that people who dislike mathematics dislike thinking [A:166].","citations":["A:166"],"tags":["Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Matthew","definition":"Evangelist who appears in a parenthetical exchange with Luke. Matthew quotes the prohibition against putting new wine into old bottles and directs the reader to 9:17; Duffey tells the two evangelists to settle their dispute with boxing gloves [GD:500].","citations":["GD:500"],"tags":["New Testament","Scripture","Literary Figures"]},{"name":"Matthew (Salvatore)","definition":"One of Henry Francis Salvatore’s nearby uncles, listed among the Salvatore relatives [A:122].","citations":["A:122"],"tags":["Family","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Maugham, Somerset (1874--1965)","definition":"Writer of <em>Of Human Bondage</em> (1915) and <em>The Razor’s Edge</em> (1944). In the publisher’s closing note, Rick Norwood recalls a <em>Have Gun-Will Travel</em> episode in which Paladin says he is reading a privately circulated Maugham manuscript. This mention belongs to the publisher’s paratext, not Lafferty’s narrative [A:285].","citations":["A:285"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Maurine (Stranahan)","definition":"Member of the Stranahan household who discusses the wedding arrangements with Monica and expects to try on the bridesmaid’s dress [A:104-105]. At the reception she is among the women with whom Finnegan romps [A:113].","citations":["A:104-105","A:113"],"tags":["Family","Women Characters","Finnegan"]},{"name":"May (Month)","definition":"Month of Vincent and Theresa’s wedding, scheduled for the last Saturday of May 1946 [A:95]. The poem opening Finnegan’s chapter sends dragons back to Yushu in May [A:171]. During his later Chicago visit, Finnegan can narrow the current month only to the interval between May and November [A:246].","citations":["A:95","A:171","A:246"],"tags":["Finnegan","Time & Eternity","Literature"]},{"name":"Mayfield's Meadow","definition":"Meadow where the young Duffey hides for a couple of hours after escaping his aunt’s house to avoid three slant-faced boys who intend to kill him [MTM B1:12-13].","citations":["MTM B1:12-13"],"tags":["Duffey","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Mazagan","definition":"Stop on the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s itinerary between Safti and Casablanca [D:101].","citations":["D:101"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Ships","Africa"]},{"name":"McAbney, Chris","definition":"Scottish crewman aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em>, disliked by Anastasia and grouped by the narrator with Harry Scott and Art Emery among the wrong sort of people. He reports to Papa Devil on the day Finnegan first comes aboard [D:30-31]. At a later departure, he does not sail with them, and Finnegan hears he is dead [D:71].","citations":["D:30-31","D:71"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Ships","Finnegan","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"McAfee, Douglas","definition":"London double of Edward Cardinal Leviathan of Edinburgh, one of the shadow-men pursued by John Mogul’s organization [MB:578-579]. He comes from Bimini toward the Mooring-Stone in a luxury yacht with Bolo Manolo and Lloyd Cardigan-Pembroke [MB:594].","citations":["MB:578-579","MB:594"],"tags":["Ekklesia","Identity","Ships","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"McClatchy, Mark","definition":"Scientifically inclined member of Duffey’s Chicago circle [MTM B3:99]. Stein names him among the scientific friends he consults about whether spirits can read minds, a discussion that distinguishes the mind from the physical and verbal activity of the brain [PG:539].","citations":["MTM B3:99","PG:539"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"McDearmott, Wimbish A.","definition":"Name attached to the <em>Panegyrica</em> epigraph at the opening of chapter twelve of <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>. The passage places the sciences of God and man above the science of things and calls John of the Cross an experimental investigator through mysticism [D:176].","citations":["D:176"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Mysticism"]},{"name":"McDearmott, Wimbush A.","definition":"Earlier misspelling. See <em>McDearmott, Wimbish A.</em> [D:176].","citations":["D:176"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"McFadden, young","definition":"Server at McFadden’s North End Tavern. At Duffey’s request he brings a seventy-two-ounce pitcher of beer and fresh glasses; Duffey also orders onions for himself and Mike Melchiades [MTM B4:141-142].","citations":["MTM B4:141-142"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Duffey"]},{"name":"McGeehan, Audery","definition":"Musical friend in Duffey’s Chicago circle, listed with Newbold McGeehan among composers, performers, and stagers whose work ranges from opera to ragtime and string-band music [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Music","Women Characters"]},{"name":"McGeehan, Newbold","definition":"One of Duffey’s musical friends, named with Audery McGeehan in the list of composers, players and stagers. The preceding names are Enniscorthy and Mary Sweeny; Newbold is not a Sweeny [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Music"]},{"name":"McGonigal","definition":"Figure appearing at the left hand of the demiurge Teilhard on the altar at St. Michael’s, with McLuhan at the right. All three are transfigured with light while the assembled worshippers call for three tents [GD:497].","citations":["GD:497"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Liturgy","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"McGuigan, Father","definition":"Priest who conducts Vincent and Teresa’s wedding in the parish church shared by the Piccones and Stranahans [MTM B5:183]. He joins the men’s discussions of creation and magic [MTM B5:163], rejects their account of human person-making as unlawful metaphor [MTM B5:166], and tries to divert Bagby’s attack on causes the narration says he supports [MTM B5:169]. Monica reports his acceptance of Papa Piccone’s proposed accordion music for the wedding, but Maurine says the story is a joke [A:105]. He permits Theresa to have Masses said for the three children of her other life with Finnegan, though he does not understand her account [A:204].","citations":["MTM B5:183","MTM B5:163","MTM B5:166","MTM B5:169","A:105","A:204"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Liturgy","Finnegan","Duffey","Family","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"McGuire's","definition":"House whose Philadelphia-made chamber pot, with scrolled porcelain lettering, Duffey can visualize although he has never entered it. The recollection belongs to his appropriation of things noticed in other people’s minds [MTM B1:17].","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory","Language & Words"]},{"name":"McIfreann, Ian","definition":"Named author of <em>Psychologia Nugarum</em> in the epigraph to Chapter Six of <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>. The passage argues that dreams have a topography, with particular dreams recurring among voyagers in particular sea passages, and asks what will happen in the sectors of space [D:80-81].","citations":["D:80-81"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Dreams","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"McMasters, Timothy","definition":"Artist whose work Duffey sells early in his New Orleans venture and whose ideas contribute to the company’s proposed rebuilding of the world [MTM B6:199].","citations":["MTM B6:199"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"McSorley, Josephine","definition":"Literary member of Duffey’s Chicago circle, listed with Rollo McSorley [MTM B3:98]. The pair urge Duffey toward Fleshpot Row, and Josephine joins the hostile response when Demetrio Glauch rejects the company [MTM B4:125] [MTM B4:127]. In “The Casey Machine,” her report in the <em>Underground Eagle</em> declares the machine born from the group’s Particular Judgment, even while recording Casey’s denial that it has been invented [CM:506].","citations":["MTM B3:98","MTM B4:125","MTM B4:127","CM:506"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Journalism","Literature","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"McSorley, Rollo","definition":"Newspaperman in Duffey’s Chicago circle, listed with Josephine McSorley [MTM B3:98]. He opposes Tony Apostolo’s opinions [MTM B4:122], encourages Morris Poor’s challenge to Duffey, and bets a hundred dollars against Duffey moving a mule by magic [MTM B4:129]. During the demonstration, Sebastian Hilton strikes and fells both challengers, so they miss the feat. Rollo’s <em>Daily News</em> report, “I was kicked by a flying mule,” ruefully acknowledges the lost bet [MTM B4:131-132]. He later accompanies the group attempting to blackmail Duffey into admitting their ideas to <em>The Crock</em> [MTM B4:140-141].","citations":["MTM B3:98","MTM B4:122","MTM B4:129","MTM B4:131-132","MTM B4:140-141"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism","Magic & Sorcery","Crime"]},{"name":"Medea","definition":"Identity Teresa Piccone assigns to Dotty Yekouris in the <em>Argo</em> company. Teresa calls her Medea the vile sorceress but immediately says that the vile name and reputation are a mistake [MTM B7:234].","citations":["MTM B7:234"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Argonautica","Women Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Medicos","definition":"Medical men who, in Ignatius Ti’s account, drunkenly pretend to be corpses in a dark supply room so that they can rise and seize new ward boys [A:85-86].","citations":["A:85-86"],"tags":["Occupations","The Dead","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Medina County","definition":"Texas county named, alternatively with Frio County, as the setting of Finnegan’s 105 hours in the chaparral, from Tuesday sundown to Sunday morning. The narrator suggests that he remained northeast of the Frio branch [A:202]. The ancestral city of the buffalo is conjecturally placed across the line in Frio County, with the narrator remarking that no such city is recorded in Medina County [A:205].","citations":["A:202","A:205"],"tags":["Finnegan","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Mediterranean Sea","definition":"Sea traversed in the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s last three weeks before the catastrophe, along a route described through the North African littoral, the Mediterranean, Sicily, and Greece. Finnegan later paints scenes from this journey [D:114].","citations":["D:114"],"tags":["Ships","Seas & Oceans","Italy","Greece","Africa","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Megapolis (in Arcady)","definition":"Place in Arcady whose surrounding meadows supply the bees’ honey used in Madame Le Mourcheor’s Old Original Juju Joy Pills, according to Don Barnaby’s account [D:249].","citations":["D:249"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Folklore"]},{"name":"Megčre (Ship on Master Map)","definition":"One of the French treasure ships on the master map used by Angela Cosquin’s father to supply fabricated treasure maps and supporting documents to confidence men. Angela describes all the vessels as treasure-laden and sunk [D:261].","citations":["D:261"],"tags":["Ships","Shipwrecks","Crime","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Mehitabel","definition":"The alley cat in Don Marquis’s <em>archy and mehitabel</em>. Anastasia Demetriades insists to Finnegan that she is a lady, “even if of the Mehitabel variety,” while disparaging her cabinmate Marie Courtois [D:33].","citations":["D:33"],"tags":["Literature","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Meinkmueller's French Restaurant","definition":"St. Louis restaurant styled French where Duffey and Bagby celebrate his business success with zwiebelsuppe, rinderbraten, walnuts, brandy, and cigars [MTM B2:47]. Duffey later takes Mary Louise Byrne and Beth Keegan there with such companions as Charley Murray, Edward Ranwick, or Philip Manford [MTM B2:58]. Before his seven hidden years, the company gathers there and is joined by Monica Drexel and Monica Murray, who somehow know he is leaving [MTM B2:69].","citations":["MTM B2:47","MTM B2:58","MTM B2:69"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Melchiades, Mike","definition":"Poor Chicago double of Duffey, found in McFadden’s North End Tavern, about forty, with short sandy-orange hair, tired fire-blue eyes, and hands continually weaving and clapping [MTM B4:141]. His voice resembles Duffey’s. Both men describe themselves as half shaded over; Mike calls Duffey a devil and insists that he himself is the tornado of which Duffey is a spinoff. He says witnesses attribute a devil’s misconduct to him, and his wife believes them [MTM B4:142]. After she leaves, he withdraws their joint savings of just over three hundred dollars, refuses her twenty dollars despite her tears, and admits this action was wrong. He asks Duffey to send her home if he sees her. Duffey suspects that Mike’s tornado claim may indeed be true, at least sometimes [MTM B4:143].","citations":["MTM B4:141","MTM B4:142","MTM B4:143"],"tags":["Duffey","Identity","Family","Social Class","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Melchisedech","definition":"Biblical king of Salem and priest of God Most High who blesses Abraham; Hebrews 7 treats his continuing priesthood as a figure for Christ’s. Casey says Duffey took Melchisedech as his confirmation name at forty [A:42]. Teresa calls Duffey the original Melchisedech [MTM B7:234], and the magus later remembers himself as Melchisedech Himself, King of Salem, sometimes named Duffey in latter-day contexts [MTM B13:402]. In Cleo Mahoney’s <em>Seven Roads</em>, Duffey plays this priest, whom she describes as having neither father nor end [MTM B9:265]. Duffey also signs a letter to Stein as the fifty-ninth king of the dynasty and says he remembers having fifty-eight predecessors [PS:478].","citations":["A:42","MTM B7:234","MTM B13:402","MTM B9:265","PS:478"],"tags":["Old Testament","New Testament","Scripture","Duffey","Theater","Theology","Identity","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Melchisedech Duffey","definition":"See <strong>Duffey, Melchisedech</strong>.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Melchisedech Duffey Proprietor","definition":"Closing wording of the red-lettered sign Duffey paints on a horse-stall board for his new St. Louis establishment. It advertises ten thousand reduced-price items, food and lodging, drinks, grooming, baths, entertainment, and a gentlemen’s bar and club [MTM B2:41].","citations":["MTM B2:41"],"tags":["Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Money & Trade","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Melchisedech Paradoxes, The","definition":"Paradoxes of Duffey’s being that the Invisible God compares with Zeno’s during Duffey’s seventh contingency, a dying vision of walking in a garden. God immediately adds that every person is equally unique and surrounded by wonderful paradoxes [MTM B12:389].","citations":["MTM B12:389"],"tags":["Duffey","Metaphysics","Paradox","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Melchisedech Prime","definition":"Name for Duffey considered apart from the experiences he appropriates from others. The narrator distinguishes a slightly bovine element in the tangled, largely sister-derived St. Louis childhood from what belonged to Melchisedech Prime [MTM B1:16].","citations":["MTM B1:16"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Melchisedech-in-St.-Louis","definition":"Duffey in his early St. Louis years of flourishing enterprises, the owner of a “multitudinous, golden-touch world.” The narrator says this world ended after most of two years [MTM B2:66], as dreams announce his departure for seven hidden years [MTM B2:68-69].","citations":["MTM B2:66","MTM B2:68-69"],"tags":["Duffey","Money & Trade","Time & Eternity","Identity"]},{"name":"Meleager","definition":"Vincent Stranahan’s ancient counterpart [A:39]. Rosemary includes Meleager among the Sergeant’s legendary companions in the Sydney episode [A:89], and Teresa explicitly identifies Vincent with him [MTM B7:234]. In Apollonius’s <em>Argonautica</em> 1.190-201, Meleager is the young son of Oeneus from Calydon, accompanied by his uncles Laocoon and Iphiclus. The poet says that with another year’s growth only Heracles would have surpassed him. During the mistaken night battle with the Doliones, he kills Itomeneus and Artaceus (1.1046-1048). At Colchis he rises with the other volunteers willing to face Aeetes’ trial if Jason cannot undertake it, although his beard has not yet begun to grow (3.504-522). The poem describes other episodes collectively: the Argonauts seek initiation at the island of Electra (1.910-921), fight the Earthborn giants (1.989-1011), guard and celebrate Jason and Medea’s wedding (4.1121-1169), and carry their ship across Libya for twelve days and nights (4.1380-1392). These passages give Meleager no separate exploit. In Apollodorus’s <em>Library</em> 1.8.2-3, his mother Althaea saves the burning brand on which his life depends. He kills the Calydonian boar and gives its hide to Atalanta, who first wounded it. When Althaea’s brothers take the prize from her, he kills them; his mother burns the brand in grief, causing his death. Apollodorus also records a different account in which he dies fighting the Curetes.","citations":["A:39","A:89","MTM B7:234"],"tags":["Argonautica","Argonauts (Characters)","Greek Mythology","Family","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Melk (Crissie verse)","definition":"Short form of Melchisedech used in a Crissie verse quoted by Cleo Mahoney while explaining the seventh possible outcome of <em>Seven Roads</em> [MTM B10:296].","citations":["MTM B10:296"],"tags":["Duffey","Theater","Identity"]},{"name":"Melk (Nickname for Melchisedech Duffey)","definition":"Earlier heading generalized a short form attested in the quoted Crissie verse. See <em>Melk (Crissie verse)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey","Theater","Identity"]},{"name":"Melky (Nickname used by associates for Duffey)","definition":"Familiar form of Duffey’s name used by Charley Murray [MTM B1:21], Evelyn London [MTM B2:40], Mary Louise Byrne [MTM B2:56] [MTM B7:218], Beth Keegan [MTM B2:59], Lily Koch [MTM B3:90], and Absalom Stein [MTM B9:266]. Duffey’s aunt also uses it during his childhood [MTM B1:13].","citations":["MTM B1:21","MTM B2:40","MTM B2:56","MTM B7:218","MTM B2:59","MTM B3:90","MTM B9:266","MTM B1:13"],"tags":["Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Melville, Herman (1819--1891)","definition":"American novelist and poet, author of <em>Moby-Dick</em> (1851). Finnegan invokes him while accusing Hans of telling phony cannibal stories, calling Melville the last person who could tell one with a clean heart and then conceding that he too was somewhat phony [A:58]. Melville’s literary standing revived in the twentieth century.","citations":["A:58"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Members of the Famous Steeplechase Club (Patrons of Rounders' Club)","definition":"Status promised to patrons of Duffey’s Rounders’ Club by its sign: customers automatically become members of the Famous Steeplechase Club [MTM B2:49].","citations":["MTM B2:49"],"tags":["Duffey","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Memories (Duffey's/Bagby's 'exterior state' letters)","definition":"Bagby’s weekly letters to Duffey, written with intelligence and compassion on lined tablet paper. They sustained Duffey during the seven hidden years and the Chicago crisis, seeming part of his introspection while remaining external objects [MTM B4:145]. They appear on his table every Monday before ordinary delivery, bearing almost familiar but otherwhere stamps and postmarks. Bagby calls them letters of another context outside daily time; their unconscious-to-unconscious origin is suggested rather than established [MTM B7:218-219]. They continue for many years after his funeral, despite Duffey’s initial explanation that Bagby had arranged advance mailing [MTM B8:237].","citations":["MTM B4:145","MTM B7:218-219","MTM B8:237"],"tags":["Duffey","Time & Eternity","History & Memory","Death & Mortality","Paradox"]},{"name":"Memrhar","definition":"Stop on the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s northward coastal itinerary after Dakar and St. Louis and before Port Etienne [D:96].","citations":["D:96"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","Africa"]},{"name":"Mendacious Midget Doll","definition":"Charlotte’s doll, which she says displays her real age, now about two hundred years, more effectively than the Dorian Gray picture in Duffey’s art bijou. She mentions it when Margaret Stone taunts her about something that reveals her true age [MTM B7:217].","citations":["MTM B7:217"],"tags":["Women Characters","Magic & Sorcery","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Mendacious Midget Doll (Margaret Stone's nickname for Charlotte Garfield)","definition":"Earlier heading that mistook Charlotte’s doll for a nickname applied to Charlotte. See <em>Mendacious Midget Doll</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Women Characters","Magic & Sorcery","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Mendel","definition":"Gregor Mendel (1822-1884), the researcher whose plant experiments founded genetics. During his Transport, Finnegan asks whether Mendel and other authorities could be wrong, since an angry primordial appears again after a thousand generations [D:214].","citations":["D:214"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Finnegan","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Mendoza, Carmelo","definition":"Private detective and disguise of Mr. X, accompanying Duffey’s investigation of Finnegan’s possible death. He first asks what Finnegan was a count of; Duffey supplies the possibility “Count of nothing” [MTM B12:367]. Small, lively, pale-haired and pale-eyed, with Austrian-sounding rolled r’s, he looks happy and wistful. Teresa Stranahan recommends him as a superb companion versed in high trickery, though only a so-so detective. He obtains seaman’s papers naming Duffey as Mike Duffey of the Irish Free State and himself as Karl Metz of West Germany; they sail as working seamen for six days and nights [MTM B12:369]. He asks Duffey for the life history behind his surface life [MTM B12:370]. Horace Pie’s “Mr. Eggs” clue exposes him as X, the dealer of doubtful information [MTM B12:371]. He asks about Duffey’s gold-coining power and joins Duffey and Pie in offering coins to witnesses along the Marianao coast [MTM B12:372].","citations":["MTM B12:367","MTM B12:369","MTM B12:370","MTM B12:371","MTM B12:372"],"tags":["Duffey","Finnegan","Identity","Minor Characters","Crew & Sailors","Caribbean","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Merchant Marine papers","definition":"Documents Finnegan says are in order when discussing work with Duffey. They would let him ship out of New Orleans; he also considers returning to river barges or tending bar with Dotty, who has saved for a place of her own [A:99].","citations":["A:99"],"tags":["Finnegan","Ships","Crew & Sailors","Occupations"]},{"name":"Mercurius","definition":"Mercury, the Roman counterpart of Hermes. The narrator lists Mercurius, Don Vincent de Ollos, and Austin as identities of Vincent Stranahan [A:39]. Earlier the narrator compares Finnegan’s distant appearance to young Mercury’s lightness and grace, alongside Dionysus’s strength before indulgence [A:6].","citations":["A:39","A:6"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Meredith, George","definition":"English novelist and poet (1828-1909) whose verse supplies the epigraph to “Promontory Goats”: “You with shelly horns, rams! / And promontory goats!” [PG:517]. The lines come from “Phoebus with Admetus.”","citations":["PG:517"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Merhione, Sister Mary","definition":"Topless nun interviewed at her bar in a clipping in Letitia’s scrapbook. The report ironically calls her gentle and forgiving while quoting her denunciation of objectors as prurient. She says the work lets her feel the human heart and sells fifty-cent reprints of her article “Topless Before God” from <em>Paralplegic Church Today</em> [MTM B8:251-252].","citations":["MTM B8:251-252"],"tags":["Women Characters","Bars & Taverns","Journalism","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Merlin the wizard","definition":"Wizard invoked in the description of Seaworthy’s striking appearance at dinner: the narrator says Seaworthy could have been Merlin if wizardry paid as well as his own line of work [D:100].","citations":["D:100"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Folklore"]},{"name":"Mermaid's hair","definition":"Naxos’s least-known export in Brannagan’s account, sold from there in shops on Siros, Tinos, and Kea but not on Naxos itself [D:207]. Finnegan recalls handling the short-cropped black hair with lavender lights, which gave off blue sparks when its owner combed it [D:208].","citations":["D:207","D:208"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Maritime Trade"]},{"name":"Mermaids","definition":"Sea people described chiefly through Anastasia Demetriades’s account. She calls them close-cropped, boyish, island Greek in feature, and air-breathing even when fishtailed [D:34]. She says they live in island caves rather than underwater palaces, dismisses Norse blond mermaids as sea-cows, and traces their decline after the high Middle Ages [D:35]. She belongs to a Naxian mermaid family that has produced no fishtail for six generations; she estimates that most surviving mermaids now have two feet [D:36]. <em>Archipelago</em> separately says that Finnegan drank on the Grand Canary and learned about mermaids from one of them [A:188].","citations":["D:34","D:35","D:36","A:188"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Folklore","Seas & Oceans","Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Meropen","definition":"Word that Casey’s notebook entry XXII says the Romanies use for both life and death, without confusion because of its manner of use [A:164].","citations":["A:164"],"tags":["Death & Mortality","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Merry de Val, Cardinal","definition":"Cardinal named among the thirteen men whose executions or murders appear in the “Deaths of the Cardinals” paintings attributed to Finnegan on the bulkheads of the <em>Argo</em>’s Bread and Wine Room [MTM B13:452].","citations":["MTM B13:452"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Death & Mortality","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Merry Monk","definition":"Nickname for Henry or Henri Salvatore, one of Duffey’s talismanic creations [MTM B5:171]. He becomes “Henry the Merry Monk” when he leaves to study for the secular priesthood [MTM B6:195]. Finnegan uses the name in the Seven Pillars of Righteousness caption beneath the <em>Bark</em> drawing [A:121] and in a letter to Theresa [A:146]. Casey’s hostile editorial insists that Henry is neither merry nor a monk [A:128].","citations":["MTM B5:171","MTM B6:195","A:121","A:146","A:128"],"tags":["Identity","Journalism","Ecclesiastical","Duffey"]},{"name":"Mesa Negra","definition":"Provenance given for a hard-stone Staghorn Christ in the collection discussed by Duffey and August Koch: a man impaled on a giant stag’s antlers in a crucifixion posture, said to have been carved nine thousand years before Christ’s birth [MTM B3:104].","citations":["MTM B3:104"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Theology","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Message blurbs (Bird/butterfly shapes on cave paintings)","definition":"Finnegan’s name for apparent birds and butterflies in cave paintings and in his own canvas. He says they are speech-and-song balloons in an original complex language, with writing on both the inside and outside, rather than the Pleistocene lepidoptera that specialists imagine [MTM B6:193].","citations":["MTM B6:193"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Messene","definition":"Greek place invoked in the comparison of Anastasia’s head to Diana Artemis. The narrator describes the image seen by Pausanias in its sanctuary of Asclepius as painted marble with a natural expression; a separate cypress-wood image belongs to Delos [D:133].","citations":["D:133"],"tags":["Greece","Greek Mythology","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Messina","definition":"Sicilian place on the <em>Brunhilde</em>’s route after Palermo and before Zakinthos [D:114-115]. The Capuchin speaks of it as the home of Finnegan’s people and says Sicilian Naxos was sixty miles away [D:119]. In “More than One,” Nicholas Cardinal Gregorio is identified as of Messina [MB:571].","citations":["D:114-115","D:119","MB:571"],"tags":["Italy","Ships","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Messina's","definition":"One of Margaret Stone’s dawn-coffee haunts in New Orleans, along with Anthony Ghost’s. She says the Sardinian Red Fisters who threaten to kill her are always there and spare her to hear the next morning’s shaggy-duck joke [MTM B7:225].","citations":["MTM B7:225"],"tags":["New Orleans","Food & Dining","Crime","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Metanephrio","definition":"Term in “lobation of Metanephrio,” one of the purported age tests in the quoted <em>Eighteenth Book of Strange Encounters</em>. Its editors claim this and numerous other measurements establish Duffey as more than four thousand years old [MTM B8:250].","citations":["MTM B8:250"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Metanoia","definition":"Greek term for a change of mind or heart, repentance or regret. In “The Great Day,” Duffey remembers a prophecy that the metanoia would arrive grotesquely and not wholly holy, while refusing to accept the handless clock as the promised turning [GD:483].","citations":["GD:483"],"tags":["Sin & Redemption","Prophecy & Visions","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Metoula manuals","definition":"Small German-language manuals among the language-learning books on Duffey’s shelves, named in the account of Casey’s ambition to learn every language [A:159].","citations":["A:159"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Language & Words","Germany"]},{"name":"Metz, Karl","definition":"West German identity on the seaman’s papers used by Carmelo Mendoza, who proves to be Mr. X, while sailing with Duffey to Cuba to investigate Finnegan [MTM B12:369]. Horace Pie’s recognition of him as Mr. Eggs leads Duffey to recognize the disguise [MTM B12:371].","citations":["MTM B12:369","MTM B12:371"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Caribbean","Germany","Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Mexican-Prisoner-Story Dealers","definition":"One class of confidence men for whom Angela Cosquin’s father makes maps and half-maps [D:260-261].","citations":["D:260-261"],"tags":["Crime","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Mexico","definition":"Escape destination Vincent proposes to Finnegan while frightened of marrying Theresa [A:112]. Manuel denies that Mexico is his country, saying he comes from farther south [D:56]. Later, finding Spanish everywhere, the confused Finnegan wonders whether New Orleans has been ceded to Mexico [A:269]; the supposed New Orleans is actually Havana [A:276].","citations":["A:112","D:56","A:269","A:276"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Mexico City","definition":"One corner, with New Orleans, Havana, and Tegucigalpa, of an imperfect square used to give San Simeon’s rough position. The intersection of its diagonals lies just off San Simeon’s north shore [MB:558].","citations":["MB:558"],"tags":["Fictional Places","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Miami","definition":"University destination hoped for by the eleven-year-old Saucerite, who says he will attend the University of Miami next season if he obtains a chess scholarship [MB:592].","citations":["MB:592"],"tags":["Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Mice","definition":"Live performers in Duffey and Letitia’s puppet shows, summoned from walls and floor spaces, dressed by Letitia in tiny token costumes, and able to speak or relay voices in an explicitly magical performance [MTM B4:134-135]. At the new Hubbard school, seven exhausted mice arrive after Duffey prays; a zoology teacher identifies them as Central American. Paper oxygen masks revive them before the show [MTM B4:136]. Duffey also pours a living mouse from his hands [MTM B5:170]. In the <em>Argo</em> voyage, Ralph Rolfe says mice ate his writing paper [MTM B13:427-428]. In Duffey’s altered childhood encounter, Philip Stranahan describes young Teresa Piccone carrying pet mice in her pockets [MTM B13:471].","citations":["MTM B4:134-135","MTM B4:136","MTM B5:170","MTM B13:427-428","MTM B13:471"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Theater","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Michael","definition":"Name claimed for Duffey by an older relative or pretended relative; Duffey replies that she probably remembers his twin [MTM B1:7]. Casey separately says Michael is Duffey’s given name, with Melchisedech acquired at confirmation [A:42]. In “The Great Day,” Michael instead names the speaking statue of Saint Michael, whom Duffey addresses as Mike [GD:496].","citations":["MTM B1:7","A:42","GD:496"],"tags":["Duffey","Identity","Angels & Demons"]},{"name":"Michael, Harold","definition":"Intended recipient of a little sack of marbles in Phoebe Anne Ankerson’s stolen suitcase. The label says they belonged to Aaron, who has outgrown them, and include some agates [D:172].","citations":["D:172"],"tags":["Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Michael, Saint (talking statue)","definition":"Speaking stone statue in the former entry of Saint Michael’s during the Great Day. As people unstructure the church, he tells Duffey that its living water has gone and no Mass or even morning remains [GD:496]. With one eye gouged out, broken toes, and a dull stone sword, he wishes for a blade-sharpener so he can fight the destroyers. Duffey leaves him crying stone tears [GD:497].","citations":["GD:496","GD:497"],"tags":["Angels & Demons","Duffey","Weapons","Catholic Church","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Michaelangelo","definition":"Michaelangelo is the spelling used in the supplied text and earlier heading. See <em>Michelangelo</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Italy","Visual Arts","Literary Figures"]},{"name":"Michaels, Mary Anne","definition":"Pupil at Saint Mary Major’s who threatens to demand that her mother take her home rather than endure the formal dance arrangements [MTM B1:32-33]. She joins the four boys and four girls who follow Lily Koch to the attic billiard room, where they eat, play pool, and dance informally; she becomes especially friendly with Charley Murray [MTM B1:33].","citations":["MTM B1:32-33","MTM B1:33"],"tags":["Women Characters","Duffey"]},{"name":"Michelangelo","definition":"Michelangelo Buonarroti (1475-1564), Italian sculptor, painter, architect, and poet. Stein claims that the artist’s own nose was flattened in youth and that his resulting de-emphasis of noses shaped conventional depictions, unlike the noble noses of archangels in earlier imagery [A:127]. His works include <em>David</em>, the <em>Pietà</em>, and the Sistine Chapel ceiling; he also worked on Saint Peter’s Basilica and its dome.","citations":["A:127"],"tags":["Italy","Visual Arts","Literary Figures"]},{"name":"Middle Ages","definition":"Period repeatedly invoked in the company’s arguments, legends, and disputed histories. Patrick Stranahan dismisses Dante as a rimester, Petrarch as a sonnet writer, and famous Renaissance artists as craftsmen who should have stayed anonymous like their medieval betters [A:111]. Lawrence Salvatore tells Henry that he can bring back the sixteenth century but not the thirteenth [A:125]. Ignacio tells of Joseph of Maintz, a Benedictine priest in the eighth century, launching himself to the moon with a Missal, Scripture, Augustine, Jerome, and Pliny, in a story supposedly chronicled by Turpin of Rheims [A:271]. Henry’s unsent letter says Otto I imprisoned the Devil at Aachen in 946 and transferred him to the Euxine Chersonese later that year [MTM B5:180]. X gives the date as 946 or 947, describes delivery through Mohammedans to Devil worshippers, says the peninsula had already been called the Prison for over a thousand years, and compares the captive Devil with enchanted or imprisoned Barbarossa, Alaric, Brian Boru, the Cid, and Arthur [MTM B6:204-205]. Zabotski conjectures that a thousand-year Dark Age may be a delirious invention of a boy chronicler during the plague of 1348 [MTM B9:281-282]. Duffey’s account gives Casey two medieval forms: compassionate ninth-century sorcerer Kasmir Gorshok, who makes artificial people to shelter body lice, and Prince or Saint Casimir, defender of the poor, who defies Casimir IV through chastity and supposedly dies at twenty-three, actually withdrawing from the world [PG:535-536]. Henry is identified as the Emperor Henry of Neustria [MTM B5:183], while Duffey proposes his medieval recorder-flute as an instrument suitable to a patriarch [MTM B9:266].","citations":["A:111","A:125","A:271","MTM B5:180","MTM B6:204-205","MTM B9:281-282","PG:535-536","MTM B5:183","MTM B9:266"],"tags":["History & Memory","Time & Eternity","Folklore","The Devil","Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Literature"]},{"name":"Midge (Charlotte Garfield nickname)","definition":"Name Margaret Stone uses for Charlotte while telling her that her chosen game offers no warning or last-minute escape [MTM B7:217].","citations":["MTM B7:217"],"tags":["Women Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Mihailović, Draža (1893--1946)","definition":"Yugoslav Serb general and Chetnik leader during World War II, initially supported by the Allies before their preference shifted to Tito’s Partisans. He was tried and executed by the Yugoslav authorities in 1946. Casey’s hostile <em>Crock</em> editorial calls him a traitor and condemns Gabrielovitch for defending him in the Slavic-language press and attacking Tito [A:129].","citations":["A:129"],"tags":["Historical Figures","World War II","Soldiers","Journalism"]},{"name":"Mike (Duffey's alias)","definition":"See <em>Mike Duffey (Alias)</em>. This is the given name on Duffey’s Irish Free State seaman’s papers for the Cuban journey [MTM B12:369].","citations":["MTM B12:369"],"tags":["Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Mike Duffey (Alias)","definition":"Identity on the seaman’s papers Carmelo Mendoza obtains for Duffey’s voyage to Cuba. They identify him as an Irish Free State citizen, and he travels as a working seaman [MTM B12:369].","citations":["MTM B12:369"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Ireland","Caribbean","Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Miles O'Connel","definition":"Name credited with <em>Handbook of Treasons</em>, an excerpt denouncing the spiritual army of evil in high stations, especially the word-mills and stations within the Church [MTM B5:175-176].","citations":["MTM B5:175-176"],"tags":["Literature","Catholic Church","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Milesians","definition":"Legendary ancestors invoked in claims about Ireland’s antiquity. Sammy Guiterrez calls Vincent’s tracing the Irish back to the Milesians “toy history” [A:66]. Casey’s notebook says Hinnegan puts them in Ireland before the Flood; Casey answers that knowledge of this would require Noah to have brought two Irishmen on the ark, provoking Hinnegan’s anger [A:164-165].","citations":["A:66","A:164-165"],"tags":["Folklore","Ireland","Old Testament"]},{"name":"Milford Quirt, The","definition":"Whip named by the turbaned opening speaker at the Royal Pop Historians’ gathering among the objects supposedly remembered by an old Decatur Street flagstone [MTM B11:329].","citations":["MTM B11:329"],"tags":["History & Memory","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Military and Thalassocratic Adventure","definition":"The narrator’s phrase for the military and sea campaign omitted from the account as the Dirty Five’s battery moves beyond New Guinea to another Dutch island [A:50].","citations":["A:50"],"tags":["World War II","Pacific Theater","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Milky Way","definition":"Unverified reference. The cited page describes “milky ears of corn” during Finnegan and Howland’s journey, but does not mention the Milky Way [A:179].","citations":["A:179"],"tags":[]},{"name":"Million dollars","definition":"Threshold in Hilary Hilton’s prep-school pact: each of four boys must make a million dollars before thirty or kill himself. Hilton says he succeeded, one boy killed himself, and the other two evaded the pledge [A:254]. Askandan says he would not cross Finnegan’s enemy for a million dollars; Finnegan answers that the sum was slightly larger, with the narrator questioning his truthfulness [A:258]. August Koch offers a million-dollar dowry for Lily [MTM B3:102].","citations":["A:254","A:258","MTM B3:102"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Finnegan","Death & Mortality","Family"]},{"name":"Mind-power","definition":"Faculty Brannagan invokes after producing 140 hundred-dollar bills by rubbing his hands. Eva objects that they share one serial number; he says making distinct numbers by mind-power would take a week with rests, because the faculty has limits [MTM B13:415].","citations":["MTM B13:415"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Mind-reader","definition":"One of Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni’s performance roles, alongside memory and information expert, hypnotist, nightclub magician, and answer man. He has worked in circuses, music halls, and vaudeville theatres around the world [A:215-216].","citations":["A:215-216"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Carnival","Occupations"]},{"name":"Minden","definition":"Iowa town in Pottawattamie County, one of seven places disputing the honor of Duffey’s birthplace in the account of one of his possible childhoods [MTM B1:7].","citations":["MTM B1:7"],"tags":["Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Minnesota deer","definition":"Game in proposed annual hunting and fishing expeditions arranged by the Stranahan men with Hans and Henry, alongside gulf marlin and Dakota pheasant. The arrangements remain conditional on God and circumstances [A:103].","citations":["A:103"],"tags":["Family","Fishing"]},{"name":"Mint Julip (Drink)","definition":"Cocktail in Duffey’s comic gloss on the rich man and Lazarus: he says Dives could have been saved by asking for a Mint Julip or Tom Collins instead of water, calling this the failed test of the parable [A:117].","citations":["A:117"],"tags":["Alcohol","Duffey","Theology","New Testament"]},{"name":"Minute hand (of watch)","definition":"Watch part removed by the leader of three young men restraining Duffey. The man says the watch may still mark hours but minutes no longer matter in the Great Day; Duffey disputes the men’s originality and their identification of the event [GD:482].","citations":["GD:482"],"tags":["Duffey","Time & Eternity","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Miraculous Medal","definition":"Devotional medal already on the chain around Finnegan’s neck when he adds the Heliogabalus figure after baptizing it [A:61].","citations":["A:61"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Prayer & Devotion","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Miriam (Szymansky's wife)","definition":"Wife of Gabriel Szymansky, belonging to the Chicago circle around Duffey and Letitia [MTM B3:98]. A garbled encyclopedia entry quoted by Silas and Maud Whiterice identifies her as Miriam Lessing, mother of Kasmir or Casey Szymansky [PG:528].","citations":["MTM B3:98","PG:528"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Missal","definition":"Liturgical book among the furnished books in the six-boy chambers of Saint Martin’s Hall at Saint Bonaventure’s, alongside dictionary, Bible, Rule of Saint Benedict, and atlas [A:161-162]. Ignacio’s story also puts a Holy Missal among Joseph of Maintz’s books and supplies for his lunar journey [A:271].","citations":["A:161-162","A:271"],"tags":["Liturgy","Literature","Scripture"]},{"name":"Missouri","definition":"The Missouri River is one source of local river winds in the narrator’s explanation of Duffey’s ballooning from St. Louis: it enters the Mississippi from the west above the city [MTM B2:65]. St. Louis is explicitly placed in the state of Missouri elsewhere in the account [MTM B2:40].","citations":["MTM B2:65","MTM B2:40"],"tags":["Duffey","Sailing"]},{"name":"Mistakes? He never made any mistakes (Phrase)","definition":"Dotty’s defense of her father’s printing-business judgment, immediately after saying he went broke on thirteen magazines and repeatedly left the family without food. When Duffey asks whether Papa learned from mistakes, she insists he made none and played every deal correctly [A:119].","citations":["A:119"],"tags":["Women Characters","Money & Trade","Family","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Mixed company","definition":"Narratorial description of a varied gathering at Duffey’s where Morris Poor challenges his sanity and magical powers [MTM B4:128]. The phrase also describes the influential argumentative company at a Viennese café during the <em>Argo</em> voyage [MTM B13:433].","citations":["MTM B4:128","MTM B13:433"],"tags":["Duffey","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Mixed drinks (Served at Club Royal)","definition":"Drinks priced at a dollar and a quarter at Howland Jerome Howland’s plush Club Royal in Chicago, whose newly prosperous customers enjoy being overcharged for luxuries [A:246].","citations":["A:246"],"tags":["Alcohol","Money & Trade","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Mocking Bird (Dawn chorus participant)","definition":"Bird in the narrator’s account of the dawn chorus as Finnegan wakes in a dump: an unnamed bird calls before the mocking bird, which in turn stirs before the lark [A:177].","citations":["A:177"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Mocking Prince, the (Adversary)","definition":"One of the Argonauts’ most shattering adversaries, listed with the Evil Prince, the Purple Prince, and the Laughing Prince of Tartary [MTM B13:437].","citations":["MTM B13:437"],"tags":["Argonautica","Argonauts (Characters)","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Mogul, John","definition":"Head of Track and Total, usually called the Mogul although John Mogul is his actual name. X confronts him at his Marseille apartment over contracts to kill thirteen genuine Cardinals in flight or hiding, and Mogul describes his control over the separate contracts and his preference for dispassionate killers [MB:571-573] [MB:579]. He subsequently tracks their doubles as well [MB:578-579]. He extends six digits on one hand and five on the other to count eleven surviving targets [MB:578]. Later John Giwa, Cardinal Doki’s double, breaks in on him in Marseille, severs twelve digits from his hands, skewers his tongue, and carries the severed fingers and thumbs to the conclave in the underground room of Mooring Rock, where they writhe as if swearing vengeance [MB:596-597].","citations":["MB:571-573","MB:579","MB:578-579","MB:578","MB:596-597"],"tags":["Crime","France","Ecclesiastical","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Mohammed (c. 570--632 AD)","definition":"Arab prophet and founder of Islam, regarded by Muslims as the final prophet. The revelations received by him were collected as the Qur’an, the central source of Muslim faith and practice. In Lawrence Salvatore’s argument with Henry, Mohammed’s time is recalled as an occasion when people thought the world would be lost; Henry answers that half was lost [A:125].","citations":["A:125"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Theology","History & Memory","Middle East"]},{"name":"Molasses Time","definition":"See <em>Molasses World (Zabotski's term)</em>. This index heading refers to time in that world; Book Nine calls the world itself the “Everyday-or-Molasses World” [MTM B9:279].","citations":["MTM B9:279"],"tags":["Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Molasses World (Zabotski's term)","definition":"Zabotski’s name for the slow everyday world, also called the “Everyday-or-Molasses World.” He says that it occupies the same space as the Pristine World, but in a different way [MTM B9:277] [MTM B9:279]. Duffey compares the apparent distance of the past with a distorting medium in which people themselves are immersed; Zabotski identifies that medium as molasses [MTM B9:272] [MTM B9:273]. The contrast concerns the content of experience as well as its duration: Zabotski says that he can live overflowing years in the Pristine World during a day or two here [MTM B9:279]. Duffey subsequently reports such a journey himself, spending three or five years there and returning in two days [MTM B9:284]. See <em>Pristine World.</em>","citations":["MTM B9:277","MTM B9:279","MTM B9:272","MTM B9:273","MTM B9:284"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","History & Memory","Paradox"]},{"name":"Moles at the Grass Roots (Article)","definition":"Article in the <em>Crock</em> identified by its style as Duffey’s work; the narrator says most of that issue was written by Duffey [A:41].","citations":["A:41"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism","Literature"]},{"name":"Moloch (Deity)","definition":"Recipient of the child offerings cast into the Moloch Furnace during the Babylonia Bagascia festivities before the conclave. The victims include children, babies, infants, and living foetuses [MB:566]. The name recalls the biblical Molek or Molech, to whom offering children is forbidden in Leviticus 18:21 and 20:2-5.","citations":["MB:566"],"tags":["Old Testament","Cosmic Struggle","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Moloch Furnace (Place)","definition":"Furnace into which a dozen children, infants, and living foetuses are cast as offerings to Moloch and his Molochites during the festivities preceding the Babylonia Bagascia conclave. One foetus speaks from the furnace, identifying himself as Little Hugh of Lincoln, and the crowd laughs [MB:566].","citations":["MB:566"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle","Death & Mortality","Ekklesia"]},{"name":"Molochites","definition":"Moloch’s associates, named with him as recipients of the children and foetuses cast into the Moloch Furnace at Babylonia Bagascia [MB:566].","citations":["MB:566"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Molucca Sea (Sailed by Argo)","definition":"Sea sailed by the <em>Argo</em>, listed with the Ceram Sea and Banda Sea [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Moluccas (Islands)","definition":"Indonesian island group also known as the Spice Islands, where Willy Jones sailed the ship when she was named the <em>Flying Serpent</em> [MTM B13:405].","citations":["MTM B13:405"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Islands","Identity"]},{"name":"Mona (Location/Passage)","definition":"Passage Angela Cosquin names among the seven true passages through the Antilles [D:260]. Mona also appears among the beaches in the opening poem of Finnegan’s chapter, “I’ll drive me a boat” [A:171].","citations":["D:260","A:171"],"tags":["Caribbean","Sailing","Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Monaghan, Marie Schultz","definition":"Australian wife of Hans Schultz, named Marie Anna Monaghan at their secret marriage in Sydney’s Annunciation Church on February 15, 1943. Hans lacked army permission, so the guests were her relatives rather than their friends; they honeymooned on an uncle’s farm [A:31]. Chubby, roguish, tart-talking, red-haired, freckled, and green-eyed, she habitually brushes her hair back with her hand. Hans admires her classical proportions and calls her “at least eternal” in a poem [A:6] [A:24-25]. Her claim to 156 years in Sydney refers to the Monaghan family [A:7]. Theresa tells Vincent that a letter from Marie has disclosed a secret, apparently the marriage [A:49]. Hans and Marie settle in St. Louis, with Mary Catherine staying in their apartment [A:97]. Dotty’s affectionately exasperated letter blames Marie for funding Salvation Sally’s journey to America [A:147-148]. At the Rounders’ gathering Marie disputes Duffey’s claim to have made them, arguing that he collected their forgotten souls and gave them setting and sparkle; he disagrees [MTM B5:158-159]. The list of his twelve talismanic creations calls her Mary Monaghan Schultz [MTM B5:161]. She insists she has recently been on the <em>Argo</em>, explaining her refreshed waking [MTM B12:387]; Hans and Marie supply the ship with sea biscuit [MTM B13:446].","citations":["A:31","A:6","A:24-25","A:7","A:49","A:97","A:147-148","MTM B5:158-159","MTM B5:161","MTM B12:387","MTM B13:446"],"tags":["Australia","Women Characters","Family","Duffey","Creation","Ships","World War II"]},{"name":"Monday (Day)","definition":"Recurring publication and correspondence day: the first <em>Answer</em> arrives on Monday morning, with issues scheduled for Monday, Wednesday, and Friday [MTM B4:113]; Bagby’s unexplained letters appear on Duffey’s table every Monday and continue after his death [MTM B7:219] [MTM B8:237]; the Pelican Press publishes the <em>Seamen’s Paper</em> on Mondays [GD:490]. Duffey also returns from the Fifth Contingency to a Monday he has already lived [MTM B12:356].","citations":["MTM B4:113","MTM B7:219","MTM B8:237","GD:490","MTM B12:356"],"tags":["Journalism","Literature","Duffey","Time & Eternity","The Dead"]},{"name":"Monday (Luin) (Goat)","definition":"Luin, or Monday, is one of the goat-man’s seven youngest kids, named for the days of the week. Unable to make the leap into the old sycamore’s crotch, she receives a boost from Finnegan, winning him the friendship of all seven [A:194-195].","citations":["A:194-195"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Money (Medium)","definition":"Finnegan’s source of money puzzles Mary Catherine; X claims in his letter to Absalom that he knows it and is deeply involved, but postpones the story [A:248] [A:266]. Hilary Hilton reports a pact requiring four schoolboys to make a million dollars by thirty or kill themselves [A:254]. When Askandan says he would not cross Finnegan’s enemy for a million dollars, Finnegan claims a slightly larger sum, but the narrator questions his truthfulness [A:258]. His stolen suitcase is called the world’s most valuable, without a stated amount [A:188]. Duffey funds the perpetual soup crock with $15 collected from the gang, $45 from Zabotski, and $40 from panhandling [A:133]. At Basse-Terre the narrator calls money necessary for Paradise, especially for the undeserving, as the four friends recline in hammocks [D:236]. Duffey’s facility for making money is the first of his talents to diminish and the least missed [MTM B4:109]. Casey inherits money from his father’s estate [MTM B4:119]. The Depression weakens the joy of money, though the narrator says that joy is not entirely evil [MTM B4:124].","citations":["A:248","A:266","A:254","A:258","A:188","A:133","D:236","MTM B4:109","MTM B4:119","MTM B4:124"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Finnegan","Duffey","Family","Heaven & Hell"]},{"name":"Monica (Charley Murray's girlfriend)","definition":"Monica Drexel is sometimes Charley Murray’s girlfriend and joins the farewell company at Meinkmuellers with his sister Monica Murray, whom she resembles. Duffey initially gives a talisman to the wrong Monica; the other recognizes it as intended for her, but the passage does not name which receives it [MTM B2:69]. A girl named only Monica accompanies Charley to the balloon dinner [MTM B2:65]. The later list of talismanic creations identifies Vincent as the son of Monica Murray Stranahan, making Monica Murray the likelier intended recipient [MTM B5:161].","citations":["MTM B2:69","MTM B2:65","MTM B5:161"],"tags":["Women Characters","Duffey","Magic & Sorcery","Identity"]},{"name":"Monroney, Moira","definition":"One of the three red-haired girls the Sergeant meets at Sydney’s Plaza Hotel with Rosemary Riorden and Minnie McGinty. They drink and become friendly with his party [A:88].","citations":["A:88"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Women Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Monster Giulio","definition":"Giulio Solli, a Teras and father of John Solli, or Finnegan [MTM B3:107] [MTM B5:161]. He is the first customer at Duffey’s St. Louis establishment: dark, powerful, unusually put together, with very wide feet and rough rivermans-blue clothing. A riverboat, ocean-ship, and dock worker, he asks for food, a bath, and lodging, pays a silver dollar, and bathes in a horse trough [MTM B2:41-43]. Duffey gives him a talisman for an unborn son; Bagby then identifies him with the Gadarene Swine and sings of his pursuit and threatened drowning, while Duffey calls him good [MTM B2:47-48]. Later Giulio says he applied the talisman to his wife’s belly and placed it in his son’s hands at birth [MTM B3:107]. Among Duffey’s Chicago friends [MTM B3:99], he is modeled by Mona Greatheart and engraved in Doré-like style by Groben. Countess Margaret says a relative of his inhabited her childhood castle and imagines one in every Transylvanian castle cellar; she distinguishes Teras from Troll [MTM B3:106-107]. Duffey says Giulio has been dead at least ten years, but Bagby reports a recent meeting and says Teras councils include both living and dead [MTM B5:165].","citations":["MTM B3:107","MTM B5:161","MTM B2:41-43","MTM B2:47-48","MTM B3:99","MTM B3:106-107","MTM B5:165"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Finnegan","Duffey","Family","Magic & Sorcery","Music","Crew & Sailors","The Dead","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Monsters","definition":"Overlapping names, images, and rival accounts of unusual beings, rather than a single settled classification. <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> invites the reader to arrange its devils, monsters, corpses, and revenants, while Papa Devil calls the plot the old game of monsters and men [D:6] [D:120]. Finnegan calls himself a gargoyle, and the narrator later depicts him as a living gargoyle perched on a hotel; Anastasia identifies both herself and Finnegan as teras or drakon, with her Oread sisters sharing the condition [D:39] [D:139] [D:162]. His resemblance to the Cliff People includes great noses, chinlessness, a shambling gait, and sudden strength and speed [A:59]. Anastasia’s grandmother claims the other blood for herself and partly for her granddaughter, while Brannagan says he and Finnegan carry it in varying recensions, containing the conflict within themselves [D:128] [D:209-210]. In confession Finnegan fears the old animal and Devil’s kindred within him; the priest acknowledges another kindred but assumes salvation includes it [D:119-120].\n\nOther accounts are more hostile. An epigraph credited to Saint Brendon describes unredeemable, long-lived creatures, usually evil but sometimes indifferent or helpful, and always untrustworthy [D:130]. Another, credited to Galen, makes Tityroi or Satyroi the animal-like, human-looking offspring of Daemons and women, with destructive passions and differing fertility in successive generations [D:160-161]. The Asimov epigraph suggests a groomed Neanderthal could pass unnoticed [D:9]. A hostile review of Royce Rollins’s <em>The Return of the Neanderthal</em> reports his theory of surviving elites and hidden ancestry erupting into destructive primordials, with apparitions, doubles, and second sight as ordinary powers of that species [D:240-241]. The epigraph credited to Carmody Overlark links pumpkin heads, bulbous noses, a basal brain bulge, unusual imagination, and ghostliness; these are the book’s attributed speculations [D:270-272]. Finnegan’s Transport gives the older people their own grievance: displaced by newcomers, some hid in the conquerors’ blood and reemerge for vengeance [D:213-214]. He finally declares himself renegade against their destructive purpose, while Doll disputes his and X’s claim that ordinary people cannot save themselves [D:292]. X himself remains unsure that alien flesh exists, though he speaks of a race crossing the races [D:156].\n\nGiulio Solli, the Monster Forgotten, is Finnegan’s father; Stein’s account questions how much of the son’s experience is filial memory supplied through X [MTM B6:187]. Duffey’s first St. Louis customer, Giulio is dark, powerful, oddly assembled, wide-footed, and dressed in rivermans-blue, working on riverboats, ocean ships, and docks [MTM B2:41-43]. He receives a talisman for an unborn son and later describes giving it to his “dago son”; he explicitly confirms that he is a Teras [MTM B2:47] [MTM B3:107]. Bagby reports Giulio’s participation in councils attended by living and dead despite Duffey’s statement that he has long been dead [MTM B5:165]. Finnegan’s parentage is explicit at their later meeting [MTM B5:174].\n\nPapa Devil’s huge purple appearance and noiseless movement are repeatedly emphasized [D:30] [D:70]. Seaworthy calls him the very Devil [D:83], but X distinguishes the original alien conspirator from the Irish policeman Noonan or Flaherty who impersonated him; both have died by the Naxos aftermath [D:146] [D:154-155]. Private Gregory, the purple pumpkin-headed storyteller who looks ghostly in the ward, is identified by Finnegan as Papadiabolous, without specifying which member of the family [A:84-85] [A:278]. Marie Courtois is Finnegan’s Golden Ogress and a primordial figure of immense strength [D:32] [D:37]. Anastasia proposes painting Marie as an ogress; Marie assents, but Finn actually paints the women as Sindbad’s wives and proposes Papa Devil for the man-eating ogre [D:62].\n\nThe Loosed Devil meets Duffey to demand his creative service, while speaking of a client and leaving his precise rank uncertain [MTM B10:302-304]. Henry’s unsent letter dates the Devil’s imprisonment to 946; X’s account gives 946 or 947, a Crimean prison, and a recent postwar release, describing the prisoner as misshapen, slant-faced, and printing distorted letters [MTM B5:180] [MTM B6:204-206]. The preaching figure attacks order through defamation, hatred, crooked lines, excess words, and the intended negation of creation [MTM B8:242]. Countess Margaret claims that her horse-sized family wolves frighten the Devil [MTM B8:256]. In <em>Episodes of the Argo</em>, a song accuses him of choosing evil, wasting innocents, and poisoning springs, yet asks to suffer in his place; the accompanying sermon calls him the troll beneath the bridge, and another fragment says he knows the past fully but the future only in fragments [PG:521] [PG:523] [PG:526]. Margaret Stone claims her promise against final loss covers the devil she knows, but she and Duffey disagree whether he is the Great Devil [MTM B13:447]. Show Boat’s letter reports Casey’s financial debt to Seven Devils and his refusal to let them inhabit him again [A:262].\n\nDuffey’s invisible giants can perform physical work, including ringing a massive bell with a visible hand; his talismans create or activate persons, with Finnegan called his greatest masterpiece [MTM B1:5] [MTM B1:15] [MTM B1:18] [MTM B5:182]. Four dark giants of demiurgic appearance also hold Casey’s head in the terrifying televised animation Duffey makes from Scanlon’s painting [MTM B12:381]. Uncreations and broken effigies hiss at Duffey after losing their houses and bodies [MTM B11:314]; the Pop historians later attack pseudo-people, human remnants, morphic dragons, and papier-mâché fire lizards, while Mary Virginia questions the victims’ supposed unreality [MTM B11:338]. Morpheus argues that sleep and waking are illusions, says the cosmos is a single cell’s dwindling dream, and wrestles Duffey for the remaining identity [GD:499-500]. Related prodigies include the still-speaking and weeping head of Paul XI, the Cap-Stone, which is silenced when his brains are removed [MB:562-563], and the devil in Finnegan’s painting, exorcised before its destruction in his story of studying under Van Ghi [D:255].","citations":["D:6","D:120","D:39","D:139","D:162","A:59","D:128","D:209-210","D:119-120","D:130","D:160-161","D:9","D:240-241","D:270-272","D:213-214","D:292","D:156","MTM B6:187","MTM B2:41-43","MTM B2:47","MTM B3:107","MTM B5:165","MTM B5:174","D:30","D:70","D:83","D:146","D:154-155","A:84-85","A:278","D:32","D:37","D:62","MTM B10:302-304","MTM B5:180","MTM B6:204-206","MTM B8:242","MTM B8:256","PG:521","PG:523","PG:526","MTM B13:447","A:262","MTM B1:5","MTM B1:15","MTM B1:18","MTM B5:182","MTM B12:381","MTM B11:314","MTM B11:338","GD:499-500","MB:562-563","D:255"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Finnegan","Duffey","The Devil","Devils (Characters)","Giants & Titans","Magic & Sorcery","Creation","Identity","The Dead","Dreams","Visual Arts","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Moon (Celestial body)","definition":"A crescent moon is among the devices on the pennants of the <em>Navicula Petri</em> in Henry Salvatore's dream [A:44]. Ignacio tells of Joseph of Maintz shooting himself to the moon and living there for ninety years, lighting a bonfire at each full moon to show that he is alive [A:271]. During Finnegan and Anastasia's vigil on Naxos, they discuss birds flying behind the moon; the narrator adds that the Greeks invented it [D:132].","citations":["A:44","A:271","D:132"],"tags":["Prophecy & Visions","Dreams","Greece","Folklore"]},{"name":"Moon (game)","definition":"Game Casey Szymansky plays around the clock in college back rooms [A:167].","citations":["A:167"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Moonlight Cavalry (Finnegan's old outfit)","definition":"Finnegan’s former army outfit. When he seeks it in D’Hannis, locals say it has not been there for seven or eight years and none of its boys has returned; some now live in San Antonio. He had maneuvered in this neighborhood during his early army days [A:202].","citations":["A:202"],"tags":["Finnegan","Soldiers","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Moonraker (Ship on Master Map)","definition":"One of the ships represented on the Master Map associated with Angela Cosquin’s father and its treasure-fraud history [D:261].","citations":["D:261"],"tags":["Ships","Shipwrecks","Crime"]},{"name":"Mooring Rock, the (Location)","definition":"Mooring rock in the shoals north of San Simeon, initially described as six hundred by three hundred yards. Its rock-hewn fresh-water well, high natural palisade, and imported soil support hidden gardens and about fifty permanent residents, while several hundred visitors, including millionaires and fisher folk, share its secret. Two tramp steamers call there [MB:575-576]. Count Finnegan brings the Martyr-Bell, and the <em>Argo</em> later brings the Peter-Bell [MB:575] [MB:577-578]. A hewn room reached from the well-shaft becomes the site of the conclave [MB:577] [MB:596]. The later upheaval describes Mooring Rock as three square kilometers, a changed scale, and raises it a hundred meters above the surrounding land; Giwa blesses it as Mooring-Stone and renames Oceanic Babylon Holy Babylon [MB:597]. In the earlier narrative, Count Finnegan names its surroundings the Belling Shoals and Ringing Rocks; its cave receives thirteen apparent doubles of dead princes. He says one will actually be a dead holy man still alive, preserving the line until the last battle [MTM B13:453].","citations":["MB:575-576","MB:575","MB:577-578","MB:577","MB:596","MB:597","MTM B13:453"],"tags":["Fictional Places","Islands","Fishing","Ships","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Mooring Stone, the (Location)","definition":"Name used for Mooring Rock, including in Giwa’s blessing. See <em>Mooring Rock, the (Location)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Fictional Places","Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia","The Dead","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Moral Sanctions Committee for the Removal of Eye-Sores and Abominations, The","definition":"Committee that confronts Zabotski about his growing contraption. A woman claims that eight years earlier he promised to act against the eyesore “on the morrow”; he corrects her that he promised not to act. Members then remember both yesterday and eight years ago as the time of their previous complaint, which Zabotski explains through Pristine History and their slower historical context [MTM B9:276-277].","citations":["MTM B9:276-277"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","Time & Eternity","Paradox"]},{"name":"Morpheus","definition":"Sleep god who confronts Duffey during the Great Day. He calls sleep and waking illusions, connects his name with <em>morphē</em>, or shape, and maintains that shape exists only in sleep [GD:499]. He says the cosmos began as a single cell’s dream and is shrinking back into it. He and Duffey wrestle for the illusion of being the remaining cell, finally becoming a creature swallowing itself; Morpheus calls this another beginning [GD:500-501].","citations":["GD:499","GD:500-501"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Duffey","Dreams","Metaphysics","Creation"]},{"name":"Morphē","definition":"Greek <em>morphē</em>, form or shape. Morpheus claims that it and his own name are really the same word, arguing that shape is known only in sleep and that a waking world would be formless [GD:499].","citations":["GD:499"],"tags":["Language & Words","Dreams","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Morro Castle (Implied location)","definition":"Unverified identification of a Havana fortress with the vicinity of Finnegan’s burial cave. The supplied citations do not mention Morro Castle; the source locates the burial on the Marianao coast without naming this fortress [MTM B5:174].","citations":["MTM B5:174"],"tags":["Finnegan","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Morrow Hanna (Port)","definition":"Port at which one of the <em>Sarcophagus</em> crew supposedly jumps ship and is replaced by an indistinguishable dock rat in Le Marin’s account of the vessel’s infiltration. It is listed between Buxton and Port of Spain [D:244].","citations":["D:244"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Ships","Crew & Sailors","Identity"]},{"name":"Mosca, Count (Character)","definition":"Count Mosca of Stendhal’s <em>The Charterhouse of Parma</em>, called Count Mosco in <em>Archipelago</em>. The older goats enjoy the intrigues of him, Gina Pietranera, and Fabrizio while the goat-man reads aloud; the younger goats fall asleep [A:198].","citations":["A:198"],"tags":["Literature","Literary Figures"]},{"name":"Moscovy","definition":"Muscovy or Russia, named Third Rome in Henry Salvatore’s dream of the <em>Navicula Petri</em>. It appears among the ship’s shattered and fallen masts, with Albion and Gaul; other masts still totter [A:45].","citations":["A:45"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Ships","Eastern Europe","Dreams"]},{"name":"Moses","definition":"Biblical leader commissioned to bring the Israelites out of Egypt and given the tablets of the law on Mount Sinai (Exodus 3:10-12; 31:18). Margaret Stone invokes Luke 16:31, saying that those who disbelieve Moses and the Prophets will not believe one risen from the dead, as part of her urgent street preaching [A:144].","citations":["A:144"],"tags":["Old Testament","New Testament","Scripture","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Moth wings","definition":"Wings Duffey listens for after returning from his lost years. He asks Beth whether moth wings can be heard across three or four hundred miles [MTM B2:74-75]. In Omaha the summons acquires direction and draws him to Chicago, where the moth proves to be Letitia Koch [MTM B3:77] [MTM B3:90-91].","citations":["MTM B2:74-75","MTM B3:77","MTM B3:90-91"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Moths","definition":"Images surrounding Duffey’s call to and recognition of Letitia Koch. A moth call draws him from Omaha to Chicago [MTM B3:77]; Charlotte takes him to St. Malachy’s, where he meets the woman without recognizing her [MTM B3:87-89]. At the Koch shop he names the evoking, seeking, and joyful moths as <em>Tinea evocata</em>, <em>Tinea indagatio</em>, and <em>Tinea letitia</em>. Lily instead calls Letitia a butterfly transformed into Psyche herself [MTM B3:90-91].","citations":["MTM B3:77","MTM B3:87-89","MTM B3:90-91"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Dreams","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Mottled Tree (Synonym for Sycamore)","definition":"Sycamore owned by Bryan Blackstone, whom Zabotski compares to its mottled trunk and trashy fruit. Zabotski curses the tree to die by tomorrow, explaining that he can command a tree’s death but not a man’s [MTM B9:270]. When Duffey returns, it is gone: Bandicoot says it died and was cut down long ago, revealing the years that have slipped through Duffey’s apparent overnight interval [MTM B9:276].","citations":["MTM B9:270","MTM B9:276"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Time & Eternity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Mountain-Building for Fun and Profit (History topic)","definition":"Topic on the program of the Society for Creative History or Royal Pop Historians, among the titles Margaret Stone recites when announcing her intention to attend [MTM B11:307].","citations":["MTM B11:307"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Mountains of the Moon (Possible location)","definition":"Unverified reference; no supporting passage has been identified for this place-name.","citations":[],"tags":[]},{"name":"Mouse","definition":"Young living mouse Duffey pours from his hands onto the table after offering to produce a dinosaur and other creatures. Papa Piccone accepts the mouse proposal, and the company laughs; Patrick Stranahan asks whether Duffey calls that a dinosaur [MTM B5:170].","citations":["MTM B5:170"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Mrs. George Brubaker's house (Location)","definition":"The Brubaker house in Newark, outside which Finnegan and Doll leave George Brubaker’s stolen car. Doll has used Mrs. George Brubaker’s identity when questioned en route; Finnegan leaves a note warning George about unlocked cars and signs it Roving Magistrate Number Sixteen [D:169].","citations":["D:169"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Crime","Identity"]},{"name":"Mt. Ararat (Mountain/Location)","definition":"Mountain in eastern Turkey traditionally associated with Noah’s Ark, although Genesis 8:4 names the mountains of Ararat rather than a single peak. Askandan claims it as the secluded center of the other blood in his own Armenia [A:258]. In the Fundamentalers’ discussion Duffey argues that wooden remains in its snow are old castles rather than vessels; a questioner invokes angelic guards as evidence for the Ark, while Zabotski says angels guard whatever becomes remote from human sight [MTM B9:281].","citations":["A:258","MTM B9:281"],"tags":["Old Testament","Middle East","Angels & Demons","Ships","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Muck of Animation (Duffey's term for creative material)","definition":"Duffey’s name for the dung he molds into new Animated Marvels while half-buried in a storm-struck dungheap. He uses his own blood as integument, selects broken glass and metal as talismans, and welcomes lightning as the power for infusing the forms [MTM B12:394].","citations":["MTM B12:394"],"tags":["Duffey","Creation","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Muk Muk Sea (Location)","definition":"Sea in Turkestan from which Hugh de Turenne says most Black Sea Sturgeon actually come. He uses the claim to explain the surviving commercial label while insisting that the Black Sea itself never existed [MTM B11:336].","citations":["MTM B11:336"],"tags":["Fictional Places","Seas & Oceans","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Mullens, Charlotte (Alias/Persona)","definition":"Persona of Charlotte Garfield, the confidence woman who appears as a nine-year-old on Duffey’s train to Chicago, plays cards, and drinks whisky with him and her supposed mother Gloria [MTM B3:78-79]. She claims to be thirty-eight and calls Gloria her sister and Manolo her son, but later explains that she makes compliant figures from pliable people, often actors [MTM B3:80-83]. She and Duffey communicate psychically: he finds the key and location of Aga Gonof’s hidden funds, she enters his experience as a psychosomatic trance, and she helps him find Letitia, his moth [MTM B3:84-87]. Gloria and Manolo die in the trance and are later found tortured and murdered in the hotel; Letitia says Charlotte escaped with the millions [MTM B3:85-86] [MTM B3:97]. Charlotte later attends the confidence-men’s convention in New Orleans as Garfield, still looking nine and wearing cowgirl attire. Her Mendacious Midget Doll reveals an age near two hundred, and she admits lying about her age [MTM B7:216-217]. Later she uses Carrollton and advertises for a new family willing to take the name Darnley [MTM B8:259].","citations":["MTM B3:78-79","MTM B3:80-83","MTM B3:84-87","MTM B3:85-86","MTM B3:97","MTM B7:216-217","MTM B8:259"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Crime","Identity","Magic & Sorcery","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Mullens, Gloria (Traveler/Con artist)","definition":"Charlotte Mullens’s companion on the Chicago train, posing as her mother. She shares food, cards, and whisky with Duffey and tells a story of destitution after her husband’s death [MTM B3:78-79]. Charlotte calls her a sister, then explains that she shapes pliable people into family roles [MTM B3:80-83]. Gloria assists Charlotte’s compromising-and-blackmailing confidence game [MTM B3:87]. She and Manolo are killed in Duffey’s trance and later found tortured with crushed skulls in the Chicago hotel; Letitia says the killers sought information they did not possess and Charlotte had already escaped [MTM B3:85-86] [MTM B3:97].","citations":["MTM B3:78-79","MTM B3:80-83","MTM B3:87","MTM B3:85-86","MTM B3:97"],"tags":["Women Characters","Crime","Identity","Family","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Mullens, Manolo (Son of Charlotte Mullens/Garfield)","definition":"Companion of Charlotte and Gloria Mullens, introduced by Charlotte as her son and accompanying them and Duffey to a Chicago hotel [MTM B3:80-81]. Charlotte later says she forms such family figures from pliable flesh-and-blood people [MTM B3:83]. She orders Manolo and Gloria to divert the attacking stevedore in Duffey’s trance, where they die; the pair are later reported tortured and murdered in the hotel [MTM B3:85-86] [MTM B3:97].","citations":["MTM B3:80-81","MTM B3:83","MTM B3:85-86","MTM B3:97"],"tags":["Crime","Family","Dreams","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Munster, Peggy","definition":"Artist named among Duffey’s associates in building and inventing worlds and scenes [MTM B6:198]. Duffey recognizes landscapes associated with her work while entering the mind of “The Majesty” [MTM B8:241]. She is not identified here with Peggy Conner, who appears with Mike Conner among the golfers [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B6:198","MTM B8:241","MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Minor Characters","Duffey"]},{"name":"Murphy, Margaret","definition":"Australian stewardess, also called Margie, who meets Finnegan and Vincent with Loy Larkin in Sydney [A:2] [A:5-6]. She looks like a little girl dressed as an adult [A:5]. Loy’s February 11, 1943 letter plans a picnic and says Margie loves Finnegan too; Margaret adds her own signature, spelled Murphey [A:27].","citations":["A:2","A:5-6","A:5","A:27"],"tags":["Australia","Women Characters","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Murray, Charley","definition":"St. Louis friend of Duffey, about two years his senior, met at boarding school. A collector of magic sets and trick books, Charley asks Duffey to build props and accidentally discovers his real magic and giant helpers. He has already attended school with Sebastian Hilton [MTM B1:21-22]. He climbs the school walls in terror after their outings and performs tricks during the boys’ clandestine sales; Mary Anne Michaels becomes friendly with him at the informal attic gathering [MTM B1:25-26] [MTM B1:33]. Later a St. Louis University day student, he performs at Rounders’ and buys half the club, enabling Duffey’s travels [MTM B2:53]. He manages it at the 1946 gathering [MTM B5:162]. Duffey gives him a talisman to pass to his sister’s unborn child; Vincent is identified as Monica Murray Stranahan’s son and one of the talismanic creations [MTM B1:35] [MTM B4:120] [MTM B5:161]. By Duffey’s late St. Louis visit Charley has died and left his half-interest to nephew Vincent [MTM B12:385].","citations":["MTM B1:21-22","MTM B1:25-26","MTM B1:33","MTM B2:53","MTM B5:162","MTM B1:35","MTM B4:120","MTM B5:161","MTM B12:385"],"tags":["Duffey","Magic & Sorcery","Clubs & Societies","Family","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Murray, Monica Stranahan","definition":"Sister of Charley Murray, wife of Patrick Stranahan, and mother of Vincent [MTM B5:161-163]. She comes to Rounders’ with Charley, Patrick, and Papa Piccone; Duffey remembers the serenity she gave him and still feels it [MTM B5:162-163]. Earlier, two look-alike Monicas, Murray and Drexel, attend Duffey’s farewell; he gives a talisman to the wrong one before the other claims it, without identifying either woman in that exchange [MTM B2:69]. Other passages make Vincent a talismanic creation and Charley the intermediary for his sister’s unborn child [MTM B4:120] [MTM B5:161]. She outlives Patrick and Charley and is present at Duffey’s late St. Louis visit [MTM B12:385].","citations":["MTM B5:161-163","MTM B5:162-163","MTM B2:69","MTM B4:120","MTM B5:161","MTM B12:385"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family","Duffey","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Murry","definition":"Variant spelling of Monica Stranahan’s maiden name in Vincent’s birth account [A:94]. See <em>Murray, Monica Stranahan</em>.","citations":["A:94"],"tags":["Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Mutant Coyotes","definition":"Unverified creature reference. The cited Transport passage concerns primordials hidden in human blood and their recurrent war, but mentions no coyotes [D:214].","citations":["D:214"],"tags":[]},{"name":"N (code name for Gregory Noonan McColum)","definition":"X’s code designation for Gregory McColum, the Irish twin of Papadiabolous I and the man known as Noonan or Papadiabolous II. According to X, he entered police work at twenty-five and worked undercover for decades: he was N as his colleague was X [D:285-287]. He impersonated his dead twin and was killed by Seaworthy on Naxos, where Seaworthy failed to recognize the dead man as his recent companion [D:289].","citations":["D:285-287","D:289"],"tags":["Crime","Identity","Family","The Devil"]},{"name":"Name forgetting","definition":"Recurrent loss of names and identity in Finnegan’s story. The soldier detained while the Dirty Five are boarding for home cannot say which of their surnames is his: all seem part of him, and he hopes to identify himself by joining the others and seeing who is missing [A:71-72]. The narrative later explicitly recalls Finnegan’s weeks in the Green Islands without his name or personality [D:50]. He also forgets helping Seaworthy bury a still-moving corpse; his drinking to oblivion follows, rather than explains, the burial [D:75].","citations":["A:71-72","D:50","D:75"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","History & Memory","Paradox"]},{"name":"Nattkarlet","definition":"One of the first three Crocks, Casey Szymansky’s clandestine college magazines, alongside the <em>Night Charley</em> and the <em>Nocnik</em>. They begin during his college years, 1938-1942, with a precedent in the schoolboy <em>Chambers</em> and its chamber-pot mastheads [A:167]. Casey starts a <em>Crock</em> at each of his seven colleges but usually loses control within two or three weeks; the narrator distinguishes his humorous work from the more salacious successors [A:168].","citations":["A:167","A:168"],"tags":["Journalism","Literature"]},{"name":"Natural Method","definition":"Part of the mock advertising slogan for Uncle Joe Tompkinson’s Torpedo Juice, promising an explosive personality by the Natural Method. The imaginary sponsor is heard on Stein’s G.I. radio station, the Anopheles Network [A:46].","citations":["A:46"],"tags":["Alcohol","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Nauti","definition":"Girl who, with Falo, builds an American-style cabaret shack in the Luzon jungle after the war has ended. Vincent and Finnegan become its first customers [A:63-64]. Nauti calls Finnegan a tao-bunduk, an older mountain creature rather than a man; quotes a peso a bottle for the whisky; and offers to teach him Tagalog [A:64]. She plays harp and expects her uncle in Batangas to bring a piano, which she will play. The four play Robber Cassino with a damaged pack supplemented by paper substitutes, and Falo wins [A:65].","citations":["A:63-64","A:64","A:65"],"tags":["Women Characters","Pacific Theater","World War II","Monsters & Creatures","Finnegan","Music"]},{"name":"Navajo pottery","definition":"One of the many goods Hillary Hilton says he can sell in his distressed-merchandise business, listed alongside marked money [A:255].","citations":["A:255"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Occupations"]},{"name":"Navicula Petri (Peter Ship)","definition":"The “little ship of Peter,” the name of Henry Salvatore’s dream boat. It resembles a galleon but is something more. Its pennants include the source’s “Ih hoc signo vincet,” the Dirty Five’s “Ubicumque fuerit corpus illic congregabuntur et aquilas,” and the Petrine sequence “Tu es Petrus,” “Portae inferi non prevalebunt,” and “tibi dabo claves regni.” Finnegan’s left-handed “Nisi esses sollicitus-” is scratched out and replaced by “If you can’t be careful, be good.” Devices include a Lamb, Greek Cross, Fish, six-pointed star, Woman, Serpent, and Crescent Moon [A:44]. The crew comprises the Apostles; Stephen, Paul and the Baptist; Linus, Clement and Cletus; Barbara and Catherine; Gregory and Constantine; Jerome and Augustine; Francis and Anthony; Thomas and Patrick; Hildebrand and Adrian the Dutchman; the French and Spanish Theresas; Joan and Xavier. Half toy and half more-than-real ship, it is storm-tossed, with broken masts associated with Albion, Moscovy as Third Rome, and Gaul [A:45]. Henry recognizes the <em>Argo</em>, its quest sanctified through the millennia, and recognizes his vocation in saving it [A:46]. His later proposal to name the magazine <em>Bark</em> recalls this dream; Finnegan supports the name and clairvoyantly draws its damaged ship for the masthead [A:121].","citations":["A:44","A:45","A:46","A:121"],"tags":["Ships","Dreams","Catholic Church","Saints","Finnegan","Journalism","Argonautica","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Naxos","definition":"Island of the Cyclades in the Greek Aegean, the ancestral home in Anastasia’s account of her mermaid family [D:35-36]. The <em>Brunhilde</em> reaches it on March 14, Passion Sunday; the narrative calls it the home of Ariadne and white wine [D:115]. It describes a mountainous island twelve miles square, producing figs, olives, lemons, grapes, honey, white wine and emery, with the Demetriades farm and Anastasia Grandmother up the mountain road [D:125]. Finnegan is said elsewhere to have touched eternity there [A:188]. After the shooting at the landing, he finds Papadiabolous, Joe Cross and five unfamiliar men in olive shirts dead in the street, while another man remains dead aboard the sinking smaller boat. Anastasia has been shot and recovered from the water [D:146-147]. X later explains that Seaworthy killed Noonan, Papadiabolous II, there without recognizing the impersonator [D:289].","citations":["D:35-36","D:115","D:125","A:188","D:146-147","D:289"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Nymphs & Mermaids","Finnegan","The Dead","Death & Mortality","The Devil"]},{"name":"Neanderthal Conspiracy / Teras Species","definition":"Interlocking accounts of an older, partly concealed people, their survival within humanity, and a struggle over humanity’s future. These are delivered through competing characters, visions, narration and attributed writings, rather than one consistently settled taxonomy. Duffey says his creations contain unusually large amounts of Teras blood, naming Gargoyles, Neanderthals, Boogers, Vagaries and Variants [MTM B5:170]. Giulio Solli identifies himself as a Teras; Finnegan is his son [MTM B3:107] [MTM B5:174].\n\nThe <em>Brunhilde</em> company is described as the other human race, and Finnegan wonders whether he belongs to it. His accompanying pumpkin-head vision shows their crew extinguishing the lights inside other people [D:50]. Later the narration makes him a member of both human races [D:114]. He confesses a felt kinship with old animals and the Devil; the priest replies that salvation extends to that other kindred [D:119]. Anastasia Grandmother claims the other blood for herself and a little for her granddaughter [D:128]. In Finnegan’s mountain dreams, the Oreads call him a teras or drakon and deny him ascent; he resists, then detects their own kinship. Anastasia confirms that she, Finnegan and her Oread sisters are of this kind, while insisting that some must help people [D:138-139].\n\nA hostile review of Royce Rollins’s <em>The Return of the Neanderthal: The Alien Within</em> presents its author as a crank. His thesis claims that Neanderthals survived their apparent replacement fifty thousand years ago, either isolated or hidden in the human bloodstream; authentic primordials might reappear through prodigious siring, and their return would destroy their successors. The account associates them with ghosts, fetches and second sight [D:240-241]. An epigraph attributed to Carmody Overlark proposes a larger brain with a reduced forebrain and enlarged back-brain, connecting this arrangement with unconscious and ghostly powers. Its physiognomy includes bulbous noses, pumpkin heads and a basal bulge; modern examples are said to include criminals or exceptional seers, poets, humorists and inventors [D:270-272]. These are the novel’s attributed theories.\n\nIn a Transport, Finnegan experiences the grievances of the people before the people: dispossession, persecution and a contemplated revenge against the later arrivals, themselves called aliens. The older people have survived as the Aliens Within, leaving those of double blood to wonder whether they must protect humanity from their own kind [D:213-214]. Brannagan and Finnegan discuss multiple recensions of that blood and the conflict within themselves [D:209-210]. Finnegan recognizes his paradise companions Le Marin, the Duke, Barnaby and Duckwalk as people of double blood [D:237]. An epigraph attributed to Hiram Hunt proposes interbreeding, cannibal absorption and tension between the older stock and a mutation, warning that destroying either element destroys both [D:283-284]. X offers a different genealogical emphasis, connecting his family and the twin Papa Devils with a purported Devil-father and a secret counter-subversive history [D:278-281] [D:285-289].\n\nPapa calls the struggle the game of monsters and men, or Policia y Ladrones, a partially related-species conflict whose dialogue is written in blood [D:120-121]. He compares the conspirators’ concealment with the Devil’s success in persuading people that he does not exist [D:87]. Finnegan calls himself a gargoyle, later sits as a living hotel gargoyle, and observes Seaworthy’s gathering as a coven [D:39] [D:162-163]. Great noses recur: Brannagan suggests Finnegan’s conceals his chinlessness [D:202], and an unnamed stranger’s nose exceeds his [D:177]. Eventually Finnegan declares himself renegade against those who would roast and eat the human world and so destroy themselves. X still distrusts ordinary humans; Doll objects to both old gargoyles’ dismissal of them [D:290-292].","citations":["MTM B5:170","MTM B3:107","MTM B5:174","D:50","D:114","D:119","D:128","D:138-139","D:240-241","D:270-272","D:213-214","D:209-210","D:237","D:283-284","D:278-281","D:285-289","D:120-121","D:87","D:39","D:162-163","D:202","D:177","D:290-292"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Finnegan","Duffey","The Devil","Ghosts & Spirits","Identity","Family","Cosmic Struggle","Dreams","History & Memory","Literature"]},{"name":"Neo-Centrist","definition":"Label in Stein’s proposed title for the magazine that becomes the <em>Bark</em> [A:121]. Casey later attacks the journal in the <em>Crock</em> as a self-described neo-Centrist, anti-Communist Catholic publication without ecclesiastical sanction, while mockingly questioning whether the term means anything [A:128].","citations":["A:121","A:128"],"tags":["Journalism","Catholic Church","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Neo-Centrist Review of Evolvate Socio-Geopolitical Motifs in a Disintegrating WorlD","definition":"Absalom Stein’s proposed title for the journal eventually called the <em>Bark</em>, with the subtitle <em>Thesis and Antithesis</em>. Several colleagues object to its length. Henry proposes <em>Bark</em> from his dream of a little boat, and Finnegan’s supporting vote settles the choice [A:121].","citations":["A:121"],"tags":["Journalism","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Neo-Jew","definition":"Absalom Stein’s self-description. He imagines a group of neo-Jews but says he may be its only member; Duffey asks whether this would mean becoming Christian [A:131]. Stein insists that his grandfather left the Temple, whereas he himself has never personally entered or left it: the Church would have to come to him there, and his genes will not let him join it. The narrator says he remains the only one of exactly his kind [A:132].","citations":["A:131","A:132"],"tags":["Identity","Theology"]},{"name":"Neo-Neanderthals","definition":"People exemplified by Eva, whom the narration explicitly calls a Neo-Neanderthal. It attributes their verbal dexterity, easy command of languages and ability to read words from minds to their unusually shaped heads and the resulting emphasis in their brains. Eva hopes to buy Kate’s Neanderthal Bar in Biloxi as a gathering place for her kind [MTM B13:407].","citations":["MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Women Characters","Language & Words","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Nervous fish","definition":"Askandan’s expression for fish requiring rehabilitation as merchandise. His discussion concerns spoiled food, including turning fish, and he claims that his seven chemical sovereigns can rescue everything from nervous fish to nervous furniture [A:256-257].","citations":["A:256-257"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Nervous furniture","definition":"The inanimate end of Askandan’s range of nervous merchandise: he says his seven sovereigns will serve for rescuing nervous fish through nervous furniture, whether the goods are organic or inorganic [A:257].","citations":["A:257"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Occupations"]},{"name":"Nervous man","definition":"The narrator’s punning description of Askandan: although both he and Hillary Hilton speak of distressed and nervous merchandise, Askandan is more a nervous man and Hilton more a distressed one [A:256].","citations":["A:256"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Nervous merchandise","definition":"Askandanakandrian’s term for troubled stock, organic or inorganic, which he claims to rehabilitate with seven sovereigns: an acid, lye, formaldehyde, ammonia, a secret kaoline affiliate, amyl acetate and an unnamed lesser gum. He says they can rescue fish or furniture; forced to keep only four, he would choose acid, base, clay and gum. Soft fruits, wilted lettuce, turning meat and fish, and rancid oils need processing; furs and jewelry belong to a pseudo-nervous category and may be sold through peddlers pretending they are stolen. He also boasts of his ability to handle weevily grain [A:257]. Both he and Hilton use nervous and distressed for their merchandise [A:256].","citations":["A:257","A:256"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Occupations","Food & Dining","Crime"]},{"name":"Nesbitt, Little Dotty","definition":"Regular at the Old Wooden Ship [D:215]. She is one of four Dottys frequently confused because of their overlapping marital histories. Little Dotty and Dotty Danvers have been married to two brothers in Sugarland, Texas, one previously married to Dotty Hulme; Danvers also married a man with the same name as Little Dotty’s first husband [D:220-221].","citations":["D:215","D:220-221"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Nests for birds'-nest soup","definition":"Edible nests made from swiftlet saliva. Hans gives a fanciful account while the soldiers pass the Talaud Islands en route to Luzon: he claims these barren islands are the sole source, and that the birds build nests from regurgitated fish and limestone for Chinese soup [A:62-63].","citations":["A:62-63"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Islands","Pacific Theater"]},{"name":"Neustria","definition":"Name of the western Frankish kingdom in the Merovingian period, broadly the part of modern France west of the Meuse and north of the Loire. In Absalom Stein’s <em>Notes on the Finnegan Cycle</em>, Neustria or Latter Neustria instead names the thousand-year empire of Christendom from 946 to 1946. Stein describes Hitler as a man of or from the future raiding backwards into that thousand-year province, Greater Neustria [MTM B6:185].","citations":["MTM B6:185"],"tags":["History & Memory","France","World War II","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Never-Break Dog Whip, The","definition":"One of the whips named by the turbaned speaker at the Royal Pop Historians’ gathering, as he imagines the memories stored in an old Decatur Street flagstone [MTM B11:329].","citations":["MTM B11:329"],"tags":["Weapons","History & Memory","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Nevis","definition":"Caribbean island invoked in Angela Cosquin’s story of her father’s forged shipwreck-treasure maps. She includes the pass between Basseterre and Nevis among seven supposed false or shoal passages of the Antilles where wrecks accumulated, or logically ought to have accumulated [D:260].","citations":["D:260"],"tags":["Islands","Caribbean","Shipwrecks","Crime"]},{"name":"New Catholic Press Guild","definition":"New, already powerful organization described by the narrator as somewhat suspect. Duffey, Letitia and Mary Virginia attend its paradoxically secret yet heavily advertised meeting. Two-thirds of its members also belong to the older Catholic Press Association; the other third, the narrator says, have come out from under rocks. Its supposedly supremely important guest speaker is the figure Duffey recognizes as Old Clootie [MTM B8:239].","citations":["MTM B8:239"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Duffey","Journalism","Clubs & Societies","The Devil"]},{"name":"New Day Dawner","definition":"The only drink offered at the Stumble-Bum on the Great Day. After Duffey unsuccessfully orders a sazerac, salty dog and old fashioned, the barkeep insists that everyone drinks the New Day Dawner. Duffey leaves [GD:498].","citations":["GD:498"],"tags":["Alcohol","Bars & Taverns","Duffey"]},{"name":"New Heaven","definition":"Adam Scanlon’s phrase for the High Renaissance sky in his symbolic seascape, set over an Old Abyss: a sea he assigns to the Low Middle Ages after the Empire’s death. He also claims to have suggested the absence of the <em>Argo</em>, a meaning even Hans has not understood [A:234]. The phrase recalls the new heaven of Revelation 21:1, though Adam does not cite the verse.","citations":["A:234"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Seas & Oceans","New Testament","Argonautica"]},{"name":"New Jersey","definition":"State reached through the tunnel on Finnegan and Doll Delancy’s flight from New York; he offers to release and pay her there if she wants to stop driving for him [D:167]. In another wandering, he crosses to the Jersey Shore, then meets the goat-man in the state’s remote back country, described as bogs, Barrens, low mountains and beaches [A:193-194]. The goat-man’s stories include the possibly haunted Harlow House on an abandoned farm; Finnegan never sees it himself [A:197]. Trinali asks Hans to take her to two places in Jersey for driftwood and cypress knees [A:229].","citations":["D:167","A:193-194","A:197","A:229"],"tags":["Finnegan","Folklore","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"New Men, Valley of the","definition":"Name asserted by the older people in Finnegan’s Transport: they were a Valley, and its name was Valley of the New Men, despite their later classification as the old people. The grievance is that they have now been denied every valley [D:213].","citations":["D:213"],"tags":["Finnegan","Monsters & Creatures","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"New Orleans","definition":"Louisiana city central to the company’s lives and publishing work. Giovanni (John) A. Solli, Finnegan, is born there on June 1, 1919, to Giulio Solli and Mary McCracken [A:173]. His sister says the family came from Irish Channel, despite his stories of a Quarter childhood; his only art teachers, she says, were school sisters and drunken painters in Pirates’ Alley [A:175]. His extraordinary dialect combines New Orleans OI sounds, waterfront Irish, Little Italy speech, Cajun French and more peculiar ingredients [A:6]. His familiarity with oyster bars there contributes to his seafood expertise [D:40].\n\nDotty comes from New Orleans for the St. Louis wedding [A:95]. After it, Finnegan, Henry, Duffey, Dotty and Mary Schaeffer drive south, check into the Jung Hotel, and begin the print-shop project [A:114-116]. The Pelican Press and its magazine occupy the Quarter [A:142]. During a later disordered return, Finnegan experiences his birthplace as a city built like a wheel [A:200]. Still later, he believes he is in New Orleans although everything has become Spanish-speaking, he cannot find his old haunts, and a man tells him the boat to New Orleans has already left [A:269-270]. New Orleans friends send mail that reaches him at Palermo [D:114-115], and Don Barnaby places Madame Le Mourcheor’s Juju Joy Pill manufacture in the city [D:249].","citations":["A:173","A:175","A:6","D:40","A:95","A:114-116","A:142","A:200","A:269-270","D:114-115","D:249"],"tags":["New Orleans","Louisiana","Ports & Harbors","Finnegan","Duffey","Journalism","Language & Words","Women Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"New South Wales","definition":"Australian state associated with Marie Monaghan’s country childhood, compared with Hans’s Wisconsin childhood as they talk through the night [A:9-10]. Loy Larkin’s letter to John Solli, also signed by Margaret Murphey, is headed Cambeltown, New South Wales, February 11, 1943 [A:27].","citations":["A:9-10","A:27"],"tags":["Australia","World War II","Women Characters"]},{"name":"New Splendor","definition":"Coffee-powder brand poured into the urn at the newly fashionable breakfast place. Duffey has never heard of New Splendor and is uncertain whether he likes it, though he finds many of its new tastes pleasant; it echoes the establishment’s commands to be splendid [MTM B11:316].","citations":["MTM B11:316"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Duffey"]},{"name":"New Testament","definition":"Christian scriptural collection read straight through by Absalom Stein. He calls it important enough to rank with Marx and Freud, but rough-hewn and more primitive than the Old Testament despite the sophisticated world into which Christ was born. He remains interested but full of reservations [A:69-70].","citations":["A:69-70"],"tags":["New Testament","Scripture","Literature"]},{"name":"New World","definition":"The American side of the contrast in Anastasia’s account of mermaids. Her taxonomy allows no New World varieties: even giant Mississippi catfish, despite human-looking eyes, fall short of mermaids. She also denies true mermaids in the Atlantic, distinguishing them from northern sea-cows [D:35].","citations":["D:35"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Seas & Oceans","Folklore"]},{"name":"New Year's","definition":"Holiday spent by the <em>Brunhilde</em> at Port of Spain, where the ship stays eight or nine days over Christmas and New Year’s. Finnegan stops drinking on New Year’s and lasts until January 26 at Freetown, Sierra Leone, almost completing his first dry January [D:85].","citations":["D:85"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","Finnegan","Alcohol","Caribbean","Africa"]},{"name":"New Year's Eve","definition":"The night the soldiers truck into Manila after supper and begin boarding the homeward ship just after midnight [A:70]. The ensuing identity crisis leaves one of the Dirty Five behind in hospital [A:71-73].","citations":["A:70","A:71-73"],"tags":["World War II","Soldiers","Ships","Identity"]},{"name":"New York","definition":"City from which Finnegan flees with the stolen suitcase and Doll Delancy. Doll says she has no friends there, and he puts her at the wheel of an appropriated car headed through the tunnel to New Jersey [D:166-167]. Loy mentions a Duffey’s in New York [A:2]. Finnegan later establishes himself in Manhattan as Gregory van Ghi, lodging at the Waldorf with McGregor, renting a Village studio and an uptown showroom [A:191]. Hans meets James Fitzjames and Trinali Peterson there and rents a three-room apartment with their help [A:225-227].","citations":["D:166-167","A:2","A:191","A:225-227"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Visual Arts","Bars & Taverns","Identity"]},{"name":"New Young Royal Pop girls","definition":"Duffey’s name for the young Pop women, including the hoyden and young countess, who dispute the reality and history of his city. They insist that a real place needs mountains and thunder, and that mountains are recent. Mary Virginia recognizes them as performers in that evening’s Horse Opera, whose advertised spectacle celebrates the destruction of an old human stronghold [MTM B11:333].","citations":["MTM B11:333"],"tags":["Women Characters","Social Class","Theater","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Niccolo Crotolus (Nick the Rattle, The Sidewinder)","definition":"Old left-footed professional killer, a large shapeless man whose pocketful of loose objects rattles as he walks [A:278-279]. The narrator offers a possible link between Crotolus, Nick the Rattle, and the Sidewinder; Niccolo instead claims Crotolo is his family name, meaning castanet, inherited from Zingaro dancers and musicians. He compares himself with his namesake the Florentine political thinker and debates ignoring his employer’s repeatedly reversed telegrams because a canceled job pays only half [A:279]. His police contact, whose bribe would also be halved if the job were canceled, encourages him to disregard the latest delay [A:279]. The employer specifies the <em>piccola vendetta</em>, killing the victim while possibly in grace rather than ensuring damnation; Niccolo has become fond of Finnegan and believes him ready [A:280]. Dotty finds Niccolo’s car, slashes its tires, tracks him to the beach, and shoots first [A:281-282]. Gut-shot but still able to fire left-handed, he uses a weapon described as greater than a carbine and less than a rifle. In three exchanges Dotty fires three shots and Niccolo fires six, hitting both Finnegan and Dotty each time; none of the nine shots misses [A:282]. The last page closes the narrative without establishing Niccolo’s death or resolving the three wounded characters’ fates [A:283].","citations":["A:278-279","A:279","A:280","A:281-282","A:282","A:283"],"tags":["Finnegan","Combat","Weapons","Crime","Occupations","Language & Words","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Nick the Rattle","definition":"Nickname of Niccolo Crotolus, associated with the loose objects rattling in his pockets and his lumbering movements. The narrator also connects the name with the Sidewinder; Niccolo gives a different explanation of his family name [A:278-279]. See <em>Niccolo Crotolus</em>.","citations":["A:278-279"],"tags":["Identity"]},{"name":"Nicolette","definition":"Heroine evoked in Hans’s reflection on Marie Monaghan’s green eyes. He remembers a fragment about Nicolette’s eyes of vair and yellow hair, insists vair was green, and rejects scholars’ gray interpretation [A:24]. The name and eye description recall the medieval romance <em>Aucassin and Nicolette</em>; the color is Hans’s reading, not an uncontested translation.","citations":["A:24"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Nie dobrze","definition":"Polish reply glossed in the dialogue as “not real well.” The amnesiac soldier says it when officers test whether his knowledge of Polish will identify him; he explains that all the Dirty Five enjoyed exchanging dialects [A:73].","citations":["A:73"],"tags":["Eastern Europe","Soldiers","Language & Words","Identity"]},{"name":"Nietzsche, Friedrich (1844--1900)","definition":"German philosopher and cultural critic (1844-1900), known for challenges to inherited religion and morality, the will to power, the Übermensch, and the death of God. His major works include <em>Thus Spoke Zarathustra</em> (1883-1885) and <em>Beyond Good and Evil</em> (1886); his thought influenced existentialist and postmodern philosophy. In the novel, Nietzsche is among the sources mixed into the inflammatory journal <em>The Answer</em>, whose blend of insight and foolishness alarms Duffey [MTM B4:114].","citations":["MTM B4:114"],"tags":["Germany","Literary Figures","Literature","Theology","Journalism"]},{"name":"Night Charley","definition":"One of the first three clandestine <em>Crock</em> magazines Casey starts during his college years, 1938-1942, alongside <em>Nattkarlet</em> and <em>Nocnik</em> [A:167]. Its later circulated form is called definitely salacious, a departure from Casey’s own humor and intentions [A:168].","citations":["A:167","A:168"],"tags":["Journalism","Literature"]},{"name":"Night club comedy","definition":"One of the lively arts the narration says are deliberately degraded after the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207]. In the later <em>My Heart Leaps Up</em> world, Otto Glotglutz is called a sick night-club comedian; Hiram III’s retort reduces him to tears and scandalizes the audience [MB:564].","citations":["MTM B6:207","MB:564"],"tags":["Theater","The Devil","Social Class"]},{"name":"Night thoughts","definition":"Fearful nocturnal thoughts in Finnegan’s sonnet on the Naxos mountain, alongside a <em>Hawk</em> on the sea and Oreads on the summit. Anastasia is still beside him: when he asks for pencil and paper to preserve the sonnet, she tells him to sleep [D:134]. The expression also recalls Edward Young’s <em>The Complaint: or, Night-Thoughts on Life, Death, & Immortality</em>, published in parts in 1742-1745, though a deliberate allusion is not explicit.","citations":["D:134"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature","Dreams","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Night Watch, The","definition":"Rembrandt’s 1642 painting of Captain Frans Banninck Cocq’s militia company. In Finnegan’s story about painting a devil, Van Ghi says the same minor demonic figure recurs in the background of <em>The Night Watch</em> and other works. This is the alleged old repeater whose painted likeness the Jesuit orders burned [D:255].","citations":["D:255"],"tags":["Visual Arts","The Devil","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Nights, the (Arabian Nights)","definition":"The <em>Thousand and One Nights</em>, or <em>Alf Laylah wa-Laylah</em>, a developing collection of tales drawing on Middle Eastern and Asian storytelling traditions and recorded in Arabic during the Middle Ages. The introduction to <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> says old Burton urged his subscribers to keep their copies locked away, comparing the present book’s hazards with those literary precipices [D:7]. During Finnegan’s winning week at the Little Oyster, Anastasia is described as a girl straight out of the <em>Arabian Nights</em> [D:20].","citations":["D:7","D:20"],"tags":["Folklore","Literature","Middle East","Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Nimrod Flesh Plant","definition":"Rare vegetation growing abundantly on Zabotski Flats. The apparently grass-eating lions and leopards are actually grazing on the Nimrod Flesh Plant [MTM B12:362].","citations":["MTM B12:362"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Nine Dollar Dog","definition":"Game Margaret Stone uses as an analogy when Charlotte Garfield asks whether she can beat the rap at the end. Margaret says its outcome is fixed nine plays before the finish, with no subsequent chance to change it and no warning that those last plays have begun. Charlotte nevertheless chose the game [MTM B7:217].","citations":["MTM B7:217"],"tags":["Women Characters","Sin & Redemption","Paradox"]},{"name":"Nine Man Free-Style Midget Wrestler Melee","definition":"Wrestling event at the old Decatur Street Arena in New Orleans in which Finnegan recalls seeing Herman Hercules. Hercules says it was before he became champion [MB:585].","citations":["MB:585"],"tags":["Combat","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Nine Mile Depth","definition":"Named ocean depth in which the <em>Argo</em> Masters deliberately hide the Sword and Scabbard of Saint Sécaire. They first weld sword and sheath together with molten fillers, bury them a hundred meters into mud and lime ooze, and charge the sea’s creatures to leave them lost. After four hundred years, Cyril and Cyrus Dimbeau recover them during bathysphere coring, contrary to the Masters’ wishes: the object is an anti-prize and danger to the world [MTM B13:422].","citations":["MTM B13:422"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Weapons","Argonautica","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Nineveh","definition":"Ancient Assyrian city, a substantial settlement by about 3000 BC and sacked in 612 BC. In Jonah 3 its people repent after Jonah’s warning. Seaworthy invokes that repentance to condemn the Texas beer served to him and Finnegan, saying that Nineveh did penance for less [D:12].","citations":["D:12"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Sin & Redemption","Alcohol","Middle East"]},{"name":"Nisi esses sollicitus","definition":"Unfinished Latin clause, roughly “if you were not anxious,” written in Finnegan’s looping left-handed script on a pennant of Henry’s dream ship <em>Navicula Petri</em>. The Latin is scratched out and replaced in English by “If you can’t be careful, be good” [A:44].","citations":["A:44"],"tags":["Ships","Finnegan","Dreams","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Noah","definition":"Biblical builder of the ark, obedient to God’s command to preserve his household and animal life through the Flood, and recipient of the subsequent covenant. Casey tells Hinnegan that knowledge of the Milesians before the Flood would require Noah to have taken two Irishmen aboard along with the other animals [A:164].\n\nIn <em>Not to Mention Camels</em>, Noah Zontik, called the Umbrella, is Pilgrim Dusmano’s professional protector, a good but crafty man whose work includes shielding the guilty and thwarting their pursuers. God commissions him as Pilgrim’s powerful friend and surety, responsible for following even if Pilgrim goes to hell; Noah regards Pilgrim as his millstone and finds the assignment strangely familiar. He comes to love his difficult charge, suspecting that someone has ordered even that love  (Not to Mention Camels, PDF pp. 36-38). He confronts Pilgrim about Hut’s murder  (Not to Mention Camels, PDF pp. 48-49). A Lord Temporal and Spiritual, he nevertheless joins Pilgrim’s cult while regarding Pilgrim as utterly wrong and the enterprise as madness. He believes his commission predates the world; he protects Evenhand as well, seeking to save both men’s lives and souls, and vows to follow when Pilgrim says that both protections must fail  (Not to Mention Camels, PDF pp. 100-103). In the novel’s discussions of derivative worlds, Noah initially regards Pilgrim as a projection of one shallow person; he later revises this to many people and doubts Pilgrim’s account of other worlds, while remaining committed to his commission  (Not to Mention Camels, PDF pp. 31-32)  (Not to Mention Camels, PDF pp. 122-123). He challenges the militia’s warrant when they come for Pilgrim, and is taken with the others  (Not to Mention Camels, PDF pp. 134-136). After Pilgrim is torn apart by horses, Noah volunteers to follow his charge and is killed in the same fashion  (Not to Mention Camels, PDF pp. 139-141). The later offer of a powerful friend beyond death belongs to another protector, Og Scath, addressing Polder Dossman, rather than to Noah addressing Pilgrim  (Not to Mention Camels, PDF pp. 244-245).","citations":["A:164"],"tags":["Old Testament","Theology","Heaven & Hell","Ireland","Literature","Crime","Occupations","Metaphysics","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Nocnik","definition":"Polish for “chamber pot”; one of the first three <em>Crocks</em>, with <em>Nattkarlet</em> and <em>Night Charley</em>, begun during Casey’s college years, 1938-1942. These are clandestine student magazines; the narrator distinguishes Casey’s high country humor from the later publications’ obscenity [A:167-168].","citations":["A:167-168"],"tags":["Eastern Europe","Language & Words","Journalism"]},{"name":"Nolan, Fisher","definition":"Theatrical member of Duffey’s Chicago circle, listed together with the other Nolan among the theatrical people [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Theater","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Nolan, John","definition":"Theatrical member of Duffey’s Chicago circle, listed together with the other Nolan among the theatrical people [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Theater","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Non-people","definition":"Category Papa Devil, the impersonating Noonan, applies to himself, Anastasia and Finnegan. When Finnegan objects that people do not literally have ghostly doubles, Papa suggests that non-people may, and includes all three of them. He connects Dopey’s duality with Finnegan to their strange blood, while admitting uncertainty about its closeness [D:120-121].","citations":["D:120-121"],"tags":["Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","Women Characters","Identity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Non-Romanies","definition":"The Gorgios, described as the disorganized part of the world outside the Dirty Five [A:73].","citations":["A:73"],"tags":["Language & Words","Identity"]},{"name":"Nookonos","definition":"Unexplained term in the demiurge Teilhard’s blessing of the Unfaithful Assembled. It occurs with peduncle, neo-anthropocentrism and corpusculization in a scene where Teilhard, flanked by McLuhan and McGonigal, appears transfigured on the altar and the congregation proposes three tents [GD:497].","citations":["GD:497"],"tags":["Language & Words","Liturgy","Theology"]},{"name":"Noon-day devils","definition":"Devils encountered during Finnegan’s sickness and delirium in the Texas chaparral, especially after his Southern Comfort and Old Crow have run out. Speaking a little Latin and much Greek, they tempt him with stones and a cactus kingdom; he says he cannot change the stones and already possesses the kingdom for as long as he wishes [A:204-205].","citations":["A:204-205"],"tags":["Angels & Demons","Finnegan","Alcohol","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Noonan, Gregory McColum Papadiabolous","definition":"See <em>Papadiabolous (II) (Gregory McColum Papadiabolous, Papadiabolous II, Papa Devil, Papa D, Noonan)</em>. Gregory McColum is the Irish cop Noonan who takes his murdered brother’s place [D:285-289].","citations":["D:285-289"],"tags":["Ireland","Devils (Characters)","Family","Identity","Crime","Finnegan","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Noonan, Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch Papadiabolous","definition":"See <em>Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch</em>. This is the elder Ifreann, father of Papadiabolous I and II, whose grave is marked I.N.C.G.P. Bogovitch [D:277-281] [D:285-288].","citations":["D:277-281","D:285-288"],"tags":["The Devil","Devils (Characters)","Ireland","Eastern Europe","Family","Crime","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Noonan, Katie","definition":"Irish governess employed by the Gregory family in Poland, mother of the elder Ifreann and thus grandmother of the two Papadiabolous brothers. X calls her a good, placid girl, says she left the household months before the birth and returned weeks afterward, and recounts the claim that the Devil fathered her child. Her heart breaks over her son’s monstrosity [D:278-280]. X says she used Ifreann affectionately and glosses it as Devil [D:279].\n\nCatherine Dembinska gives a different naming account in <em>The Flame Is Green</em>: Katie insists that the Devil be recorded as father on the certificate for her son, born in a hayfield between Skawine and Krakow in 1830. Asked for a name, she exclaims “Och Ifreann,” glossed there as “Aw hell!”, and the words become his name. Catherine maintains that he has no Polish blood and recalls childhood testimony about his murders. Dana declines certainty about Ifreann’s half-Irish ancestry and jokes that Catherine’s telling sounds Irish  (<em>The Flame Is Green</em>, PDF pp. 210-211).","citations":["D:278-280","D:279"],"tags":["Ireland","Eastern Europe","Women Characters","Family","The Devil","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Noosphere","definition":"The sphere of collective human thought. In Teilhard de Chardin’s account, its formation links increasing complexity with heightened consciousness and a collective human organism, within a spiritual understanding of evolution and its ultimate convergence. The Majesty’s spoken address uses noosphere and socialization as fashionable garnish. Its targets are Pharisees, Legalists, Rigidists, members of the Curia and hierarchies; the word noosphere is not itself listed as a target [MTM B8:243].","citations":["MTM B8:243"],"tags":["Theology","Metaphysics","Language & Words","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Norman Shipman","definition":"Bookish or literary member of Duffey’s Chicago circle [MTM B3:98].","citations":["MTM B3:98"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Norse","definition":"One of the five men collectively called the Swedes is Norse; the others are a Dane, an Icelander, a German and a man from Orkney, so none is Swedish [D:217]. In Anastasia’s account of sea-women, the Norse netted blond, mammalian, vertically tailed sea-cows in the tenth century [D:35]. An unnamed Norse hero’s longing for towering oceans requiring constant uphill sailing supplies a comparison for Finnegan’s dissatisfaction [D:18].","citations":["D:217","D:35","D:18"],"tags":["Other Mythologies","Nymphs & Mermaids","Sailing","Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"North","definition":"Direction recurring in Finnegan’s journeys and the company’s comparisons. Finnegan is usually already in the North when autumn brings the urge to migrate [D:10], and in an early wandering walks north through meadows with Howland [A:178]. Australian air and sunset are contrasted with those of the North [A:30]; Hans invokes differing motion north and south of the equator in his own explanation [A:4]. Casey notes that Venice lies farther north than Vladivostock [A:163]. The buffalo herd has drifted north for summer, leaving old bulls behind [A:206]; Hans’s St. Gallen is placed in Wisconsin’s Northern Wilderness [A:207]. The Paradise of the later voyage is assigned latitude sixteen degrees north [D:234].","citations":["D:10","A:178","A:30","A:4","A:163","A:206","A:207","D:234"],"tags":["Finnegan","Australia","Islands","Sailing"]},{"name":"North African littoral","definition":"North African coastal route followed by the <em>Brunhilde</em> after Tangier, then onward to Sicily and Greece [D:114]. Earlier, after Lanzarote in the Canaries, the ship calls at Ifni, Agadir, Magadar, Safti, Mazagan, Casablanca and Rabat before reaching Tangier [D:101].","citations":["D:114","D:101"],"tags":["Africa","Ships","Seas & Oceans","Ports & Harbors"]},{"name":"North American Bursar","definition":"Duffey’s claimed office in the Argo Balloon Club, according to Bagby’s account: he collects three thousand dollars each from twenty-three sportsmen, pays six thousand for an old balloon, and is left holding the remaining funds because the supposed Geneva headquarters has never existed [MTM B2:63].","citations":["MTM B2:63"],"tags":["Duffey","Clubs & Societies","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"North Coast","definition":"Poorest region of the independent nation of San Simeon, a rough coast of rocks, dry swamps and dangerous shoals near the Campeche Banks [MB:559]. There the forbidden Sea Bell of Saint Peter the Martyr sounds, summoning the Hidden Church; difficult terrain and water make the gathering hard for the police to stop [MB:560].","citations":["MB:559","MB:560"],"tags":["Fictional Places","Liturgy","Catholic Church","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Northern Platoon","definition":"Platoon said to have been swallowed by quicksand in the Chosky Bottoms, one of the area’s old stories. The conversation does not place Finnegan in that unit or identify its war [D:274].","citations":["D:274"],"tags":["Folklore","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Northern Valkyrie","definition":"Finnegan’s epithet for Nada Patkaniowska, the tall, golden-haired, three-hundred-pound dowager who carries him on her shoulder. He calls her Brynhilde in rounded, spherical form, remembers voyaging on her when she was the Ship, and wonders whether she is also the <em>Argo</em> [A:192-193].","citations":["A:192-193"],"tags":["Women Characters","Finnegan","Other Mythologies","Argonautica","Ships"]},{"name":"Northwestern","definition":"One of the seven colleges Casey attends between autumn 1938 and spring 1942; Notre Dame is another institution in the list. He starts one or more of the <em>Crocks</em> at each of the seven [A:167-168].","citations":["A:167-168"],"tags":["Journalism"]},{"name":"Norwegian Seamen's Club","definition":"One of the special clubs on the upper floors of the Plaza building visited by Finnegan and his companions in Sydney [A:3].","citations":["A:3"],"tags":["Australia","Clubs & Societies","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Nose, Finnegan's","definition":"Finnegan’s conspicuous banana nose appears from his first look in the bar mirror and recurs in the prewar Dotty scene [A:1] [A:174]. Other descriptions call it a bugle nose [A:6] [D:23-24]; its prominence helps support his oversized digger hat [A:13]. Stein’s ears are compared with it [A:29]. Vincent introduces the nose as Finnegan when meeting Nauti [A:64]. Dotty uses banana-nosed brat and little banana-nose [A:93] [A:95], while the narrator calls him a banana-nosed river-rat [A:169]. Finnegan accuses his dead father of giving him the nose despite lacking one like it himself [A:245], and Hilton uses bugle-nose [A:250].\n\nDopey’s letter identifies him as the bugle-nosed dago with the Irish moniker [D:51]. Finnegan tells Anastasia his gargoyle nose is real and will not come off [D:39]. Doll reports a pursuer’s description of him as a hooked-nosed ape [D:173]. X says he is saved by a nose when kidnappers expecting Finnegan’s unmistakable feature are about to seize another man with an even bigger nose [D:177-178]. A railroad man later breaks it in a fight [D:192].","citations":["A:1","A:174","A:6","D:23-24","A:13","A:29","A:64","A:93","A:95","A:169","A:245","A:250","D:51","D:39","D:173","D:177-178","D:192"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Family","Combat"]},{"name":"Nostradamus (Michel de Nostredame) (1503--1566)","definition":"Michel de Nostredame, French physician and astrologer, 1503-1566, known for the enigmatic quatrains of <em>Les Prophéties</em>, first published in 1555. In “The Great Day,” Duffey interprets the tower clock whose hands have been sawn off as a murky Nostradamus prediction of the turning-over, though he resists accepting that the Great Thing has arrived [GD:483]. <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> concludes by attributing to Nostradamus a prediction made 430 years earlier that fits Pope Finnegan: the Pope of Bells and Bay, with a Green Bay Tree, staff, paint-box, rose and conspicuous holy nose. This is the narrative’s attribution, not an authenticated historical quatrain [MB:598-599].","citations":["GD:483","MB:598-599"],"tags":["Prophecy & Visions","France","Literature","Time & Eternity","Finnegan","Popes"]},{"name":"Nostrodomo-type prophecies","definition":"Prophecies Mr. X exchanges with Jesuit experts: he hears theirs and returns some of his own. The narrator’s form Nostrodomo-type appears to evoke Nostradamus [A:137].","citations":["A:137"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Notary Public","definition":"The office Papa Devil says he is seeking ashore on the Mexican side. Finnegan recognizes that he needs more than a notary and supplies <em>un falsario</em>, a forger; Papa and Manuel then dismiss Finnegan and the two girls so they can conduct the business [D:57].","citations":["D:57"],"tags":["Crime","Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","Occupations"]},{"name":"Noted with Interest","definition":"Heading Gabby uses on the bulletin board for a clipping asserting that people with large noses are passionate. His own prominent nose makes the interest personal [A:127].","citations":["A:127"],"tags":["Journalism"]},{"name":"Nothing people","definition":"Cyrus Roundhead’s name for the people cleared from a building during the Royal Pop Historians’ murderous expedition. He claims both people and building are unreal, and threatens Mary Virginia with replacement among the victims if she will not finish them. Their resistance makes her question the alleged lack of reality and the destruction of people whose only fault seems to be unimportance [MTM B11:337-338].","citations":["MTM B11:337-338"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","Death & Mortality","Identity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Notre Dame (1)","definition":"One of the seven colleges Casey attends between autumn 1938 and spring 1942. He begins one or more of the <em>Crocks</em> at each college [A:167-168].","citations":["A:167-168"],"tags":["Journalism"]},{"name":"Notre Dame (2)","definition":"Paris cathedral whose principal medieval construction extended from 1163 to 1345. Van Ghi says that the old repeater devil represented in Finnegan’s painting also appears on Notre Dame in stone, among many appearances in artworks [D:255].","citations":["D:255"],"tags":["The Devil","France","Finnegan","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"November","definition":"Month in which the <em>Brunhilde</em> sails, on Friday November 7 in an unspecified year [D:27]. The narrator later notes that Finnegan has associated closely with Anastasia since early November, although his Romaic is still insufficient for confession [D:118].","citations":["D:27","D:118"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Finnegan","Women Characters","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Nueve y uno","definition":"Spanish for “nine and one.” Finnegan answers Elena’s list of possible troubles with this phrase, expanding it as nine yesses and a no: he accepts the troubles but says he drinks because drinking is good, and excessively because of his own evil streak [A:274].","citations":["A:274"],"tags":["Language & Words","Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Numbers","definition":"Biblical book named as the source of the chapter-seventeen epigraph in <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>. The excerpt brings together the giants descended from Enac, the people’s fear, and the assurance that their enemies can be overcome [D:258].","citations":["D:258"],"tags":["Scripture","Old Testament","Giants & Titans","Literature"]},{"name":"Nursery Plant catalog","definition":"Source of the extravagant adjectives applied to Dotty, including floriferous, succulent, hardy and disease-resistant. Even the plant catalogue’s vocabulary, the narrator says, cannot adequately describe her [MTM B7:213].","citations":["MTM B7:213"],"tags":["Women Characters","Language & Words"]},{"name":"O quam beata Civitas!","definition":"“O how blessed a city!” Latin exclamation in Finnegan’s “Antipodal Ode to Loy Larkin,” following his description of Loy’s pinkish hair as a tipsy crown lending grace to Sydney Town [A:32].","citations":["A:32"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Women Characters","Language & Words","Literature"]},{"name":"O'Boyle, Crystal (the untraditional glass goblet lady)","definition":"Artist called the untraditional glass goblet lady, one of the artists whose work Duffey sells as part of the company’s project of rebuilding the world through art [MTM B6:198-199].","citations":["MTM B6:198-199"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Visual Arts","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"O'Brien's Polynesian Palace (bar)","definition":"Waterfront establishment in Duffey’s catalogue of remembered places. Eva seems to follow his thoughts, then asks about buying Kate’s Neanderthal Bar [MTM B13:407].","citations":["MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Duffey","Ports & Harbors","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"O'Brien, Francis","definition":"One of Duffey’s ladies who comes to his Rounders’ Club in St. Louis and kisses him goodbye on his farewell evening. His departure is announced as a seven-year assignment [MTM B2:69-70].","citations":["MTM B2:69-70"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"O'Brien, Gilberto Levine y","definition":"Gilberto Levine y O’Brien of Rio, the double or shadow of Carlos Cardinal Artemis of Santa Cruz [MB:579]. He sails toward Mooring-Stone aboard the <em>Argo</em> with Count Finnegan and Herman Hercules after their primaries have died [MB:589-590]. Finnegan asks to inspect the marks Gilberto made to identify his skin. Gilberto fears that their nearly identical appearance may conceal forgery, yet refuses inspection and prefers to leave the matter doubtful [MB:590-591]. Artemis had been flayed, and the narrative explicitly leaves uncertain which skin belonged to which man. Finnegan’s secret painting on the inside of the Cardinal’s skin could now be with Gilberto or on John Mogul’s trophy [MB:591].","citations":["MB:579","MB:589-590","MB:590-591","MB:591"],"tags":["Finnegan","The Dead","Identity","Death & Mortality","Visual Arts","Ships"]},{"name":"O'Connel, Miles","definition":"Attributed author of <em>Handbook of Treasons</em>, quoted on the devils of the word-mills as a spiritual army of evil in high stations, sometimes within the Church itself [MTM B5:175-176].","citations":["MTM B5:175-176"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Catholic Church","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"O'Conner, Gabriella","definition":"Young grade-school girl, one of Duffey’s ladies who comes to the Rounders’ Club in St. Louis and kisses him goodbye during his farewell evening [MTM B2:70].","citations":["MTM B2:70"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"O'Dwyer, Edith","definition":"Schoolgirl acquaintance of Duffey, one of four girls joining him, Charley Murray, Sebastian and Rattigan at the formal dance. Lily Koch leads all eight to the soundproof attic billiard room, where they eat, play pool and dance informally. Edith talks with Duffey while Lily teases that he is really her boy [MTM B1:32-33].","citations":["MTM B1:32-33"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"O'Gnive","definition":"Name credited beneath the epigraph about being strangers at home and exiles in Erin, introducing Chapter Two, section 9 of <em>Archipelago</em> [A:55].","citations":["A:55"],"tags":["Ireland","Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"O'Higgins, Elena","definition":"Journalist in Duffey’s North Chicago literary circle during its golden age of 1925-1935 [MTM B3:98]. She proposes a feature on Giulio for the <em>Chicago Herald and Examiner</em> [MTM B3:106]. Present when Morris Poor challenges Duffey’s powers, she calls a photographer for the mule demonstration [MTM B4:128] [MTM B4:130]. Her November 14 report is modest and attempts factual description: witnesses agree the mule traveled thirty feet through the air, though their explanations differ. The photograph accompanying her piece shows the airborne mule without giant hands [MTM B4:132]. She later attends the party at Randal’s where Duffey projects Casey’s grotesque head on television [MTM B12:380-381] [MTM B12:383]. In her surviving commentary on Casey, she condemns his <em>Crock</em> advertisement offering consecrated hosts for experiments, describes the fragment of sacred organ music she salvaged before he burned the larger work, and deplores his replacement scoring for kazoo, peewee guitar and musical saw [PG:524-525].","citations":["MTM B3:98","MTM B3:106","MTM B4:128","MTM B4:130","MTM B4:132","MTM B12:380-381","MTM B12:383","PG:524-525"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Journalism","Literature","Magic & Sorcery","Music","Sacraments"]},{"name":"O'Higgins, Januarius","definition":"Bookish or literary member of Duffey’s Chicago golden-age circle [MTM B3:98]. Josephine McSorely’s <em>Underground Eagle</em> report places him at the Casey Machine discussion: he calls Casey a hypocrite and predicts he will buy a machine through a middleman despite his disavowals. Casey’s curse and Evelyn Apostolo’s embrace of hell follow Januarius’s challenge [CM:507]. The account’s narrator claims to possess the only remembered copy of that newspaper issue and subsequently names Januarius among the opportunistic promoters who helped develop the machine’s market [CM:507-508].","citations":["MTM B3:98","CM:507","CM:507-508"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Heaven & Hell","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"O'Hoolihan, Hallohan J.","definition":"Papa Piccone’s stock comic impersonation of Patrick Stranahan at the Star and Garter. Patrick imitates Piccone with hands, voice and motion, but does not know that Piccone’s reciprocal imitation has put him onstage for years as Hallohan J. O’Hoolihan. When Show Boat calls Patrick by that name, he understands only half the joke [A:104].","citations":["A:104"],"tags":["Theater","Identity","Family"]},{"name":"Oatmeal, John Bently","definition":"Potter named among the artists whose work Duffey sells during the New Orleans rebuilding project. The narrator says the Renaissance of Pottery began with him, and includes these artists’ ideas among contributions to the company’s Rebuilding Thesis [MTM B6:198-199].","citations":["MTM B6:198-199"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Visual Arts","Occupations"]},{"name":"Octavian, Cecil (of England)","definition":"English double or shadow-man of Paul Cardinal Brokenbolt of Freemantle, Australia [MB:579]. After his primary’s death, he travels along the Mexican coast in a tramp steamer with Niku Kazuko and Daniel Jean Boulle; they pose as archaeologists to elude killers and acquire the company of the stone Pyramid Lord [MB:592-594]. He is among the thirteen assembled in the underground Room of the Conclave at Mooring-Rock for the conclave that elects Finnegan the First [MB:596-597].","citations":["MB:579","MB:592-594","MB:596-597"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Australia","Identity","Ships","Popes"]},{"name":"Octopus Joe's (bar)","definition":"Waterfront establishment in Duffey’s catalogue of remembered places. Eva seems to follow his thoughts, then asks about buying Kate’s Neanderthal Bar [MTM B13:407].","citations":["MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Duffey","Ports & Harbors","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Oh Boy bird","definition":"Unidentified jungle bird whose call sounds like Donald Duck saying “Oh Boy.” It is heard as Casey and Henry descend to the bivouac after swimming; the narrator says they never learned its proper name [A:44].","citations":["A:44"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","World War II"]},{"name":"OI sounds","definition":"Sounds the narrator says occur more often in some New Orleans blocks than in Brooklyn. They are one ingredient in Finnegan’s extraordinary composite dialect, together with waterfront Irish, Little Italy speech, Cajun French, mud-cat language and an element peculiar to himself. His voice sounds almost magical at a distance but grotesque when understood [A:6].","citations":["A:6"],"tags":["New Orleans","Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Old American Blood and Thunder","definition":"American adventure books remaining in the looted library at Harlow House, as described by Gautier the goat-man: <em>Old Wild West, Train Robber, Buffalo Hunter, Indian Scalper, Bank Robber</em> and <em>Brigand</em>, the “blue-jowled romances” he loves [A:198]. He reads these silently after the goats are asleep; the French books are the ones he reads aloud to them on winter nights [A:199].","citations":["A:198","A:199"],"tags":["Literature","Crime","Folklore"]},{"name":"Old Clootie","definition":"Familiar name Duffey uses for his adversary, calling the Majesty Clootie during the latter’s address [MTM B8:243]. In the closed place associated with the Sixth Contingency, the adversary first claims to be the Devil’s agent, then reveals himself as the Majesty. Duffey’s use of Clootie disconcerts him because the name suits a minor devil, not His Majesty. The passage nevertheless leaves open whether this is the Devil himself or a lesser devil using the claim as a tactic [MTM B10:302-304].","citations":["MTM B8:243","MTM B10:302-304"],"tags":["The Devil","Duffey","Devils (Characters)","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Old Country","definition":"France, the homeland of Gautier the goat-man, who came from southern France in 1908 with six goats [A:196]. His surviving French books include Balzac’s <em>Contes Drolatiques</em>, Stendhal, <em>Tartarin of Tarascon</em>, <em>Gil Blas</em> and <em>The Count of Monte Cristo</em>. He reads them aloud to the goats on winter nights, recalling the speech of their shared Old Country [A:198-199].","citations":["A:196","A:198-199"],"tags":["France","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Old Crow","definition":"Brand of bourbon whiskey drunk by Finnegan. An inch remains in his bottle when he wakes in a foul dump after his “shipwreck” [A:177]. He carries more, with Southern Comfort and other liquors, in his shoulder-bag while walking in Texas [A:202]. The noon-day devils trouble him particularly after the Southern Comfort and Old Crow are gone [A:205].","citations":["A:177","A:202","A:205"],"tags":["Alcohol","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Old Dogs Lying in the Sun","definition":"Pomodorus’s epigram, quoted by Newton Prescott: “Old dogs, lying in the sun, are one form of history.” Prescott places it beside residual magnetism and the idea that the earth retains impressions of its history [CM:510].","citations":["CM:510"],"tags":["History & Memory","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Old French","definition":"Language in which Gautier says the pun about cutting capers works better. Finnegan’s joke combines dancing or tricks with castrating a goat; Gautier then discusses Italian <em>capra</em> and <em>capro</em> and Spanish goat-related words [A:199].","citations":["A:199"],"tags":["France","Language & Words","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Old Kind of People","definition":"People whom the Royal Pop Historians regard as obsolete. Margaret Stone reports their term “People of the Old Kind” and their claim that such people will not understand Pop History [MTM B11:308]. The townspeople’s description of Zabotski as almost the last of his kind appears just before this [MTM B11:307].","citations":["MTM B11:308","MTM B11:307"],"tags":["History & Memory","Identity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Old Man of the Sea","definition":"Figure from the Sindbad adventures whom Finnegan intends William Gerecke to represent in the <em>Brunhilde</em> murals, because Gerecke looks like an incubus [D:62].","citations":["D:62"],"tags":["Literature","Visual Arts","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Old Original Juju Joy Pills","definition":"New Orleans pills described in Don Barnaby’s extravagant account of a cure for a terrible hangover. His general recipe is sawdust, honey and cat urine; Madame Le Mourcheor’s special version uses Arabian frankincense wood, honey from bees near Megapolis in Arcady, and a black cat with glanders. He attributes the result to simpler, purer ingredients [D:248-249]. One pill makes an interior garden expand to contain the world and other worlds. He marries a wife whose appearance changes and acquires children whose names, appearances and number vary while their identities persist [D:250]. Sleep transports him between places and periods, and he later encounters Sour John in this boundless garden. John rejects the offered pill, says only fifteen minutes have passed, and returns him to his bunk under a strap until sailing [D:251-252]. Three years later Barnaby has not returned to his family, though he vows to do so and bring back the secrets, alive or dead [D:252].","citations":["D:248-249","D:250","D:251-252","D:252"],"tags":["New Orleans","Dreams","Family","Time & Eternity","Folklore"]},{"name":"Old Pop Potter","definition":"Man cited by Finnegan in the conversation about Chosky Bottoms. His saying places the spook country in the next valley or among the people next door, illustrating how neighbors become the subjects of ghost stories [D:274].","citations":["D:274"],"tags":["Folklore","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Old Stone Ship","definition":"Waterfront establishment run by Eva Ewaglouwshkoul’s father in her ancient world. After forty thousand years in the ice, Eva asks Duffey to take her to Biloxi, where she plans to acquire a saloon or hotel and wait for a seaman of her own blood. Her father’s Old Stone Ship supplies a precedent for that plan; the text does not place his establishment in Biloxi [MTM B13:401].","citations":["MTM B13:401"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Family","History & Memory","Women Characters","Ports & Harbors"]},{"name":"Old Testament","definition":"Biblical setting Cleo Mahoney assigns to the Melchisedech of her play, a role she asks Duffey to take. She identifies the character as “Melchisedech of the Old Testament” while insisting that Duffey is particularly suited to portray him [MTM B9:265].","citations":["MTM B9:265"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Theater","Duffey"]},{"name":"Old Wooden Ship","definition":"Tavern in Galveston, Texas, an unpainted, pungent building whose four stories, numerous doors, loose shutters and swaying creak make it resemble a ship. Lazarus Reilly’s company includes some fifty regulars, among whom Finnegan and Le Marin wait while murderers lurk outside [D:214-216]. Francis Paradise’s account helps draw Le Marin toward the Paradise voyage [D:233]. Much later, following Father Brown’s advice to use an English bulldog against the Man of Mystery, Melchisedech recruits Gunboat Smith, the tavern’s Bulldog-in-Residence, for the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:458].","citations":["D:214-216","D:233","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Finnegan","Duffey","Ships"]},{"name":"Olga Sanchez","definition":"Employee of Duffey’s Rounders’ Club in St. Louis, noted for her “torchy shoulders” [MTM B2:49]. On first hearing “The King Shall Ride,” immediately Duffey’s favorite tune, she carries him on her shoulders; afterward either Olga or Lucille repeats the celebration [MTM B2:52]. At his farewell Olga carries him through the rooms and into the street [MTM B2:70]. When he returns after the hidden years, she welcomes him lovingly; she has married the club’s beautiful Mexican high chef [MTM B2:74]. Duffey still counts her among the women he loves [MTM B4:110], and she caresses him when the Animations arrive at Rounders’ [MTM B5:159].","citations":["MTM B2:49","MTM B2:52","MTM B2:70","MTM B2:74","MTM B4:110","MTM B5:159"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Music","Occupations","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Oliver Oscar Omygosh","definition":"Burlesque figure Papa Piccone claims to have created for the Star and Garter. Piccone calls him a hopeless botch: a wrong-colored putty nose, swollen red eyes, an unshapely real rump, size fifteen shoes, falls and a stuttering “O-O-O.” A man identifying himself with the character complains backstage; the supposed putty nose bleeds when Piccone strikes it [MTM B5:167-168]. Piccone says the 1939 St. Louis telephone book lists Oscar Oliver Omygosh. Bascom Bagby, rather than Piccone, supplies the nearby O-O-O What Fun Novelty Store and describes the man’s real glandular troubles, dizzy spells, pain, melancholy, stumbling and tears. Bagby denies that Piccone made him; Piccone insists that he did, accidentally [MTM B5:168].","citations":["MTM B5:167-168","MTM B5:168"],"tags":["Theater","Creation","Identity","Minor Characters","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Ongoing Quest Adventure","definition":"Cleo Mahoney’s interpretation of the fourth contingency: repeated successful quests become addictive. Their goodness acquires something poisonous through endless success, provoking revenge and the death of some questers. Momentum keeps them from admitting death even when shown their bones and ashes; they retreat a little into the past and live again, leaving the problem unsolved [MTM B10:295]. Cleo calls the pursuit of excellence a broken record, but Rebeka Stein enthusiastically disagrees [MTM B10:296].","citations":["MTM B10:295","MTM B10:296"],"tags":["Prophecy & Visions","Time & Eternity","Death & Mortality","Duffey"]},{"name":"Onree","definition":"Cajun pronunciation of Henri, distinguished from the English pronunciation of Henry, in the discussion of Henri Salvatore’s name [MTM B6:195].","citations":["MTM B6:195"],"tags":["Louisiana","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Orba, St. Angela (Orphanage)","definition":"Chicago orphanage named by Charlotte Garfield during Duffey’s psychosomatic trance. She says two sisters there know who she is, while the others mistake her for a little girl; she wants commitment papers and love, and proposes that Duffey adopt her. She subsequently insists that the experience was a trance rather than a dream [MTM B3:86].","citations":["MTM B3:86"],"tags":["Fictional Places","Religious Orders","Women Characters","Dreams"]},{"name":"Oreibates","definition":"One of the three Oreads Finnegan meets in his dreams on the mountain at Naxos, with Anemotrephes and Akroreia. They call Anastasia their sister. Finnegan sees the mark of the polypus on Oreibates’s wrist, though the girls deny having such marks [D:137]. When he recognizes their kinship with the teras, Oreibates laments their condition and says they try to climb toward the light. The three push him off the mountain; Anastasia subsequently confirms their kinship with herself and Finnegan [D:139].","citations":["D:137","D:139"],"tags":["Women Characters","Dreams","Greece","Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Original Argo crew (aka Animated Marvels)","definition":"Earlier heading called this the original crew, contrary to Teresa’s explicit statement that it was not the first. See <em>Argo Company (Teresa's account)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Argonauts (Characters)","Argonautica","Creation","Identity","Duffey","The Devil","Resurrection"]},{"name":"Orpheus","definition":"Thracian singer and Argonaut identified with John G. Hans in the <em>Argo</em> books [A:38] [A:89] [A:207]. Trinali’s poem asks whether Hans has forgotten that identity [A:227]. Hans himself writes to Marie that he was Orpheus and is traveling again on the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B6:186]. Teresa identifies Hans as Orpheus and later Faust, and Marie as Eurydice, while giving Eurydice a deliberately different journey to Australia [MTM B7:234].\n\nIn Apollonius’s <em>Argonautica</em>, Book 1, Orpheus is Calliope’s son, whose music moves trees and rocks; Jason accepts him on Cheiron’s advice. His cosmogonic song settles a quarrel, his lyre sets the departing rowers’ rhythm, and he sings to Artemis. He directs the visit for secret initiation on Electra’s island and, after Cyzicus’s death, orders an armed dance whose noise covers the people’s lament during the rites of Rhea. In Book 2 his lyre accompanies the celebration of Polydeuces after the Bebrycian fighting; he directs worship of Apollo at dawn and dedicates a lyre at Sthenelus’s tomb. In Book 4 his playing overcomes the Sirens, although Butes leaps into the sea and requires Aphrodite’s rescue. He provides wedding music for Jason and Medea, appeals to the Libyan nymphs for water, and recommends the propitiatory offering of Apollo’s tripod.\n\nIn Ovid’s <em>Metamorphoses</em>, Books 10-11, his attempt to bring Eurydice back from the dead fails when he looks behind him. Maenads later kill him; his severed head and lyre continue their lament as they travel down the Hebrus to Lesbos. His shade is reunited with Eurydice.","citations":["A:38","A:89","A:207","A:227","MTM B6:186","MTM B7:234"],"tags":["Argonautica","Greek Mythology","Music","Literature","Identity","Underworld"]},{"name":"Orthodoxer","definition":"A member of the party that defends the established scientific chronology and account of origins against Zabotski. Armed Orthodoxers accuse him of rejecting their time scale, associating with Fundamentalers, and substituting false history for the accepted account. Their badges proclaim “Darwin or Death,” “Herbert Spencer Forever,” and “Huxley the Bulldog forever!” [MTM B9:286]. Earlier, they burn a “Clear Bright Flame of Science” on Zabotski’s lawn; Duffey’s account associates such burnings with punishments ranging from horse-whipping to hanging, drawing, and quartering [MTM B9:284]. The armed party threatens to shoot Zabotski, who obscures their sight with darkness or clouds and climbs aboard his contraption [MTM B9:286]. Later news reports that Orthodoxer police have killed nineteen unarmed Fundamentalers in a “shootout” [MTM B9:288]. See also <em>Vigilante Orthodoxers.</em>","citations":["MTM B9:286","MTM B9:284","MTM B9:288"],"tags":["History & Memory","Creation","Time & Eternity","Combat"]},{"name":"Ostwald, Howard Stone","definition":"One of the Big Stones, Princes of the Ekklesia, at the last Conclave in Babylonia Bagascia. Responding to Efram Gate Gratz’s attack on Peter and his grave, Ostwald says they intended the building to fall when they removed the capstone. He wants every building to fall because buildings divide people [MB:561].","citations":["MB:561"],"tags":["Ekklesia","Ecclesiastical","Church History"]},{"name":"Otto I (of the Germanies)","definition":"Otto I, Holy Roman Emperor, born in 912 and dead in 973. In Henri Salvatore’s longer, unsent letter, Otto quietly bolts the door on the Devil at Aachen in 946; the Devil is then transferred to a firmer prison at the Euxine Chersonese in the Crimea [MTM B5:180].","citations":["MTM B5:180"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Germany","The Devil","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Ouspensky, P.D.","definition":"Pyotr Demianovich Ouspensky (1878-1947), Russian philosophical and esoteric writer, author of <em>Tertium Organum</em>. The Book Eleven epigraph credits him with questions about the real becoming phenomenal, creation and uncreation [MTM B11:306]. The passage occurs in <em>Tertium Organum</em>, Chapter XXII, within Ouspensky’s quotation of Max Müller; the novel prints the title as <em>Tertium Organus</em>. Duffey later wonders how Ouspensky would account for the grotesque transformation of the world [MTM B11:341]. Jon Nelson’s essay “1974 From the Thunder Colt’s Mouth” argues for extensive Ouspensky influence on <em>Not to Mention Camels</em> and reads the Thunder Colt episode as a parody of <em>Tertium Organum</em>.","citations":["MTM B11:306","MTM B11:341"],"tags":["Literature","Metaphysics","Mysticism","Historical Figures","Duffey"]},{"name":"Overlark, Carmody","definition":"Author to whom <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> attributes the epigraph from <em>Studies in the Imagination</em>. Its speculative account links Neanderthal brains, the unconscious, ghostliness and imaginative power, then extends its back-brain type to Grimaldi, Aurignacian and related early humans [D:270-272].\n\nIn <em>Fourth Mansions</em>, Overlark is Special Assistant to the Secretary of State. According to Michael Fountain, the former Charles became Carmody about two years earlier for numerological reasons; he had been a wealthy party contributor and brilliant amateur. Foley notices his resemblance to the historical Khar-ibn-Mod and the ancient mask of Kir-ha-Mod; Bagley treats his archaic ears as a repeater sign. The identity explanations differ: Bagley proposes recurring men, O’Claire a recurring shape of mind, and Oriel a replacement of the former Charles (PDF pp. 4, 23-25, 30-31, 44-46, 64-66, 127-129).\n\nThe Harvesters prompt Foley’s investigation. Overlark resists their mental attack and can enter dreams about himself (PDF pp. 8-10, 123). Confronting Foley, he claims his group lives repeatedly by choice in a nonaging state, seeks heightened experience, allows no interference and descends from ancient sea-born gods. He calls his tribe the Intelligentsia. Foley considers him mad and rejects a straightforward account of literal reincarnation, suspecting a wraith working through a borrowed human body. Overlark is formidable, direct and cruel, enjoying secrets, fear and his subordinates’ suffering, yet betrays fear himself (PDF pp. 147-160).\n\nHis slight body has remarkable musculature, fine fishlike skin and strange eyes and complexion. Foley watches him submerge his head and upper body in brackish water for about fifteen minutes; the water and fish display impossible behavior, and he emerges refreshed. Overlark claims a sea origin, admits eating live rats and appears to be testing Foley as a recruit. He threatens confinement on grounds of madness or a fabricated murder charge; Foley is subsequently taken to an institution (PDF pp. 149, 155-161).","citations":["D:270-272"],"tags":["Literature","Metaphysics","Identity","Immortality","Dreams","Crime","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"P.F.C. Timothy Lorrigan","definition":"Boston private first class, one of the three Americans in Don Lewis’s story who disappear on October 1, 1944, on Pulau Petir, called Willy Jones Island. The others are Sergeant Charles Santee of Orange, Texas, and Corporal Robert Casper of Gobey, Tennessee [D:44]. Lorrigan lags behind when the others go to kill a little man apparently taken for a Japanese soldier. Don Lewis slips into the first person while describing this reluctance, then refuses to confirm which soldier he was [D:45]. He later calls the little man Yokipoki, explicitly warning that this probably was not his real name [D:46].","citations":["D:44","D:45","D:46"],"tags":["World War II","Soldiers","Pacific Theater","Islands","Identity"]},{"name":"Padre Island","definition":"Desolate island off the Texas coast where most of the <em>Brunhilde</em> company go ashore for a beach party and fish fry. Their preceding ports include Freeport, Palacios, Port O’Conner, Aransas Pass and Corpus. They dig a pit, burn mesquite to aromatic coals, and swim in the Gulf [D:34]. Supper includes barbecued and fried pork, boiled and fried shrimp, red snapper and pompano. Papa Devil compounds drinks until Anastasia takes over; DePolis dislikes the beach cooking, while Joe Cross, Anastasia and Finnegan contribute their expertise [D:40]. Don Lewis tells his campfire story afterward [D:43].","citations":["D:34","D:40","D:43"],"tags":["Islands","Food & Dining","Crew & Sailors","Finnegan","Fishing"]},{"name":"Pal","definition":"Friend or companion; an English word borrowed from Romani words for brother or comrade. Casey lists pal among words of Romani origin, but the knife gloss in his sentence belongs to <em>chiv</em> [A:164].","citations":["A:164"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Palacios","definition":"Port visited by the <em>Brunhilde</em> during its coastwise passage through Texas ports, before the company’s Padre Island beach party [D:34].","citations":["D:34"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Ships"]},{"name":"Panebianco, Marie Caterina","definition":"Marie Caterina Panebianco, mother of Theresa Antoinette Piccone, called Show Boat. Theresa’s father is Gaetano Piccone, proprietor of the Star and Garter [A:94].","citations":["A:94"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family","Theater"]},{"name":"Panther calls","definition":"One of the animal calls Giulio made for his son Finnegan during trips to the Cajun swamps. He whittled them from tupelo-gum wood, along with alligator, devil-fish, swamp-boa, hairy-man and white-shark calls. Finnegan recalls never knowing what he might summon from the water or trees [MTM B5:174].","citations":["MTM B5:174"],"tags":["Finnegan","Family","Louisiana","Folklore"]},{"name":"Papa (Loy Larkin's Father)","definition":"Loy Larkin’s father. In her letter from Cambeltown, New South Wales, dated February 11, 1943, Loy says he neglected the garden she planted in November but killed the fatted calf for his returning prodigal daughter the day before [A:27].","citations":["A:27"],"tags":["Australia","Family"]},{"name":"Papa Papadiabolous (Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch Papadiabolous Chortovitch)","definition":"See <em>Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch</em>. This is the elder Ifreann, father of Papadiabolous I and II, whose grave is marked I.N.C.G.P. Bogovitch [D:277-281] [D:285-288].","citations":["D:277-281","D:285-288"],"tags":["The Devil","Devils (Characters)","Family","Ireland","Eastern Europe","France","Ghosts & Spirits","Shapeshifting","Crime","Combat","Naval Warfare","Ships","Dreams","Death & Mortality","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Papa-D","definition":"Familiar shortening of Papa Devil’s name, used by the bargirl when his appearance startles Seaworthy and Finnegan [D:15], and by Finnegan himself as Papa D [D:30]. The man accompanying the <em>Brunhilde</em> is the impersonating Papadiabolous II, Gregory McColum, whom X later identifies as the Irish cop Noonan [D:285] [D:287-289].","citations":["D:15","D:30","D:285","D:287-289"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Identity","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Papadiabolous (I) (Gregory McIfreann Papadiabolous, Papadiabolous I)","definition":"The Asiatic Papadiabolous, Gregory McIfreann, whom X describes as a son of the elder Ifreann and the quasi-twin of Gregory McColum, Papadiabolous II. In X’s account the brothers were conceived together and born on the same night at the turn of the century, in Smyrna and Cork, to different mothers. Gregory McIfreann uses Sanditen, Chrysostom, Protagoras and Hochstruwelpieter-Goltz; both brothers use forms of John and may be called John Gregory [D:285].<br><br>X portrays him as a purposeful evil revolutionary with extraordinary physical and diabolical power, trained in the conspiracy. He names Seaworthy, Genof, Wirt, Gerecke, Wedekind, Askadrian, Sanditen and Sorensen among his associates, some reputedly his half-brothers. While the father lives, the son is crown prince [D:285-286]. After the father’s burial in 1930, he heads an unstable regency until Seaworthy is selected to murder him. X reconstructs a shooting during the opening of their father’s grave, while admitting uncertainty about several details [D:288].<br><br>Finnegan remembers Seaworthy striking the still-moving body with a shovel, then helping him deepen the old grave and bury the new corpse above earlier remains [D:75-76]. X identifies the grave marked I.N.C.G.P. Bogovitch as the father’s [D:277-278]. He says Papadiabolous II takes his murdered brother’s place the following morning, creating Seaworthy’s apparent encounter with a man returned from death [D:288-289].","citations":["D:285","D:285-286","D:288","D:75-76","D:277-278","D:288-289"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Crime","Family","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Papadiabolous (II) (Gregory McColum Papadiabolous, Papadiabolous II, Papa Devil, Papa D, Noonan)","definition":"Gregory McColum Papadiabolous, the Irish quasi-twin, born in Cork at the turn of the century. X says he and the Smyrna-born Gregory McIfreann share their father, the elder Ifreann, but have different mothers and were conceived together and born on the same night. Both use forms of John; II’s other names include Noonan, Flaherty, Christy, Clarenden, Cobbett, Malory, Ingraham and Arbuthnot [D:285]. Identical in build, coloring and skull shape, the brothers differ in their fleshed faces. X calls II comparatively good despite youthful work as gentleman burglar, contrabandist, swindler and forger. Excluded from the family’s revolutionary training, he enters police work at twenty-five and becomes the undercover N, keeping track of his kin [D:286-287]. His extraordinary mimicry and access to police stations around the world serve his hatred of the gang [D:154-155].\n\nAfter Seaworthy kills I while opening their father’s grave, II assumes his brother’s place the next morning and deceives the associates for months [D:288-289]. His club appearance terrifies Seaworthy and Finnegan [D:15]; Anastasia notices that Papa has become nicer [D:24]. In the impersonation he is a huge, heavy man with a high purplish complexion, expensive purple shirts and a reddish fringe around a bald pate. His red-eyed glare supports his claim to authority aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em> [D:22-23]. He carries the unconscious Finnegan aboard and appears unaffected by seasickness [D:28-29]. At Padre Island he mixes drinks and becomes unusually gay and florid [D:40]; the narrator says he never sleeps [D:51].\n\nHe often sights his rifle on distant men and cultivates a reputation as a great shot, though seldom firing [D:54]. He obtains forged papers for Manuel [D:57] [D:59]. Joe Cross suspects him of killing Doppio di Pinne, whose screams end after Papa enters the cabin and whose body cannot be found [D:64]. When Finnegan confronts him about the buried predecessor and the mimicry, Papa evades him and suggests that Dopey may instead have been Finnegan’s double [D:87-89]. He says Don Lewis contacted him at a distance without recognizing the substitution, and describes his own small <em>Hawk</em> boat, <em>Seabach</em> [D:120] [D:122].\n\nKilled on Naxos, he lies shot repeatedly and bludgeoned, with pockets emptied or cut out, an unfamiliar olive shirt shredded, and shoes removed. Finnegan recognizes the face beneath the impersonation [D:146]; his sketch lets X identify Noonan [D:154]. According to X, Seaworthy kills II without recognizing him as the man who has been masquerading as I, and the surviving gang continues to think Papa Devil is alive [D:289].","citations":["D:285","D:286-287","D:154-155","D:288-289","D:15","D:24","D:22-23","D:28-29","D:40","D:51","D:54","D:57","D:59","D:64","D:87-89","D:120","D:122","D:146","D:154","D:289"],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Ireland","Family","Identity","Crime","Finnegan","Ships","Weapons","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Papapaleologus","definition":"Donkey accompanying Finnegan and Anastasia on Naxos. The narrator jokingly presents him as a lineal descendant of the last Roman emperors of the East [D:125]. He understands Anastasia Grandmother’s Romaic perfectly and carries the supplies up to her house [D:126]; later he grazes in the narrow glen beneath the pair on the mountain [D:132].","citations":["D:125","D:126","D:132"],"tags":["Greece","Islands","Finnegan","Folklore"]},{"name":"Paradise","definition":"The Earthly Paradise found by Finnegan and his three companions, Charles Le Marin, Don Barnaby and Johnny Duckwalk, at Basse-Terre in the Antilles. The narrator gives its coordinates as 61°44′42″ west and 16° north and distinguishes it from the Heavenly Paradise intended by St. Francis [D:234-236]. Its playful requirements include freedom from frost, breeze and bloom, a weekly ship, French-speaking black people and English-speaking Frenchmen, centuries of history, a Lost Dauphin legend, a volcano named Soufriere, discovery by Columbus, those three friends and a little money [D:237]. Their haven eventually fails through its remaining snakes [D:235]. The grave marked I.N.C.G.P. Bogovitch belongs to the elder Ifreann, father of Papadiabolous I and II, whose identity X unfolds there; it is not the grave of a single man identifiable as both father and I [D:277-281] [D:285-288].","citations":["D:234-236","D:237","D:235","D:277-281","D:285-288"],"tags":["Caribbean","Heaven & Hell","Finnegan","Folklore","Money & Trade","Family"]},{"name":"Paradise Birds","definition":"Clay birds Duffey makes in a just-before-morning dream as the Boy King or Boy Magician. He gives them the color of living gold, releases them, and sees further brilliant colors appear in flight. Rough men shoot them down; the falling clay lets them claim they were only clay pigeons, always in season. The game warden nevertheless kills the men but angrily warns Duffey against another such chase. Beth reads the dream as doubt about his powers and creations [MTM B2:67].","citations":["MTM B2:67"],"tags":["Duffey","Dreams","Creation","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Paraiso","definition":"Mexican coastal stop off which the <em>Brunhilde</em> anchors while Finnegan sketches on deck for the planned Sindbad murals in the barroom [D:59]. The later Unaccountable Corpse episode may occur at Paraiso, Campeche or another stop; the narrator explicitly leaves the location uncertain [D:67].","citations":["D:59","D:67"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Ships","Finnegan","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Parakeet (Amoy)","definition":"Amoy, the talking parakeet in the ward. He calls himself a big and beautiful bird, though the narrator denies both claims. Ward boy Ignatius Ti translates his compressed Tagalog-Parakeet into expansive tales of previous lives, including life as a jewel-stealing red-winged blackbird and as a fish [A:79-81]. A sadistic medico poisons him to watch him suffer; though gravely ill, he clings to life. An official order to kill the stinking bird drives him into the trees by day and back to his friends at night. The narrator calls him possibly the best-balanced inhabitant of the ward: his persecution and unpopularity are real, and he tries to remain cheerful [A:81-82].","citations":["A:79-81","A:81-82"],"tags":["Language & Words","Death & Mortality","Identity","Folklore"]},{"name":"Parakeet Tagalog","definition":"Compressed language of Amoy the parakeet, called Tagalog-Parakeet by the narrator. Three or four words commonly expand into many paragraphs in Ignatius Ti’s translations [A:79].","citations":["A:79"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Parallel Lives","definition":"One of the fantasy types, together with Successive Lives, listed by Doctor Saul Rafelson in his notes on Duffey. Rafelson also insists that Duffey can make such fantasies happen, considering several possible explanations for his other persons and selves [MTM B4:146].","citations":["MTM B4:146"],"tags":["Duffey","Identity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Paramaribo","definition":"Port from which the <em>Sarcophagus</em> sails in Le Marin’s story. He says Castleman infiltrated it before departure, then replaced crewmen with lookalikes at successive ports; the supposedly untouched cargo was partly stolen [D:244].","citations":["D:244"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Ships","Crew & Sailors","Identity"]},{"name":"Paris","definition":"City Patrick Stranahan includes, with Dublin, London and Rome, among the journeys he thinks a Christian man should make for his soul’s sake [A:110]. X later reports seeing Levi van Wei there and relays his interest in obtaining more Finnegan pieces [MTM B6:201].","citations":["A:110","MTM B6:201"],"tags":["France","Catholic Church","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Paros","definition":"Aegean island Finnegan includes in his account of the original five-island cluster or Pentanesia [A:35]. After the Naxos massacre, Finnegan and Manuel swim there, the distance being guessed at seven miles. They tow their clothes, shoes and Finnegan’s bag of stones on floats, rest on shore, hide until dawn and check that the <em>Brunhilde</em> has not followed. At breakfast in Paros town they divide their $720 equally. Finnegan’s unexplained roll may have been left by Anastasia. Manuel leaves for Rome to tell the Pope of the evil men [D:151].","citations":["A:35","D:151"],"tags":["Greece","Islands","Finnegan","Money & Trade","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Parousia Himself","definition":"Christ of the Second Coming, the third cargo in a prophecy Teresa quotes from an unnamed latter-day prophetic book. It describes ghostly, bony Melchisedech Duffey and a crew of wraiths carrying Count Finnegan to election and coronation in the Indies, Prince Casimir to his reign as Antichrist near Armageddon, and finally the Parousia Himself on the <em>Argo</em>’s last voyage [MTM B12:388].","citations":["MTM B12:388"],"tags":["New Testament","Apocalyptic","Prophecy & Visions","Ghosts & Spirits","Ships","Duffey","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Party of One","definition":"Duffey’s expression for solitary membership, used when Absalom Stein calls himself a neo-Jew and wishes he could find others to form a group [A:131]. Earlier, Casey describes Duffey as the sole member of the Catholic Center Party of America and explicitly calls this a party of one [A:42].","citations":["A:131","A:42"],"tags":["Duffey","Catholic Church","Identity"]},{"name":"Party, the","definition":"Political Party joined by Casey Szymansky, whose imagined role in Fortress Christianity as a persecuted minority helps draw him into it [A:157]. Margaret Stone has also belonged to the Party before joining the Church and the <em>Bark</em> circle [A:143].","citations":["A:157","A:143"],"tags":["Dreams","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Pascha nostrum immolatus est Christus","definition":"Latin words from 1 Corinthians 5:7, rendered by Duffey as “Christ our Passover is sacrificed for us.” He exclaims the fuller phrase beginning <em>Etenim</em> when he and August Koch place the eighth Christ with the seven others in his Priceless Item Room [MTM B3:103].","citations":["MTM B3:103"],"tags":["New Testament","Scripture","Duffey","Language & Words","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Passagoule","definition":"Earlier heading omitted two letters from the place name printed in the poem. See <em>Passamagoule</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Passamagoule","definition":"Place printed as <em>Passamagoule</em> in the opening poem of the Finnegan chapter, paired with Castres among the speaker’s imagined destinations [A:171].","citations":["A:171"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Passion Sunday","definition":"Day identified as March 14 when the <em>Brunhilde</em> reaches Naxos [D:115]. Finnegan seeks his Easter Duty confession from a young Greek Capuchin who speaks Italian [D:118]. After confession and Mass he breakfasts with Anastasia and the impersonating Papa Devil at a breakwater cafe [D:120]. In Teresa Piccone Stranahan’s private letter, Stein’s first Finnegan Visitation occurs on the afternoon of Passion Sunday, 1948, and frightens him as an apparent death visitation [MTM B6:188].","citations":["D:115","D:118","D:120","MTM B6:188"],"tags":["Liturgy","Sacraments","Greece","Finnegan","Ghosts & Spirits"]},{"name":"Paterson, Trinali","definition":"Earlier misspelling of the surname. See <em>Trinali Peterson</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Women Characters","Literary Figures","Literature","Visual Arts","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Patkaniowska, Nada","definition":"Magnificent, hilarious dowager who likes to be escorted by Finnegan in his role as the painter van Ghi. Six feet two inches tall, three hundred pounds and abundantly golden-haired, she replaces her boa-constrictor shoulder-piece with Finnegan seated on her right shoulder in her raised arm [A:192]. Noticing that he has grown fat, Finnegan recalls her heavier breathing after carrying him upstairs. He calls her Brynhilde, the Northern Valkyrie, <em>in forma rotunda et globosa</em>, says he voyaged on her as the Ship and wonders whether she is also the <em>Argo</em> [A:193].","citations":["A:192","A:193"],"tags":["Women Characters","Finnegan","Ships","Other Mythologies","Identity"]},{"name":"Patmos","definition":"Island used as a landmark in Anastasia’s mermaid history: she places Christian mermaids in the second century on an unnamed small island between Patmos and Lipsos. Their chapel was dedicated to Our Lady of the Dolphins and, she says, still exists in poor condition [D:34-35].","citations":["D:34-35"],"tags":["Islands","Nymphs & Mermaids","Greece","Folklore"]},{"name":"Patriarch","definition":"Margaret Stone invokes “the Patriarch” who could not find enough righteous people to avert destruction while explaining her urgent street preaching [A:144]. The allusion is to Abraham’s plea for Sodom in Genesis 18:23-33; Margaret’s version begins with seventy, whereas the biblical negotiation begins with fifty and ends with ten. Separately, Cleo Mahoney calls Duffey the “Patriarch Melchisedech” while explaining the seven contingent futures in her play: he cannot end, yet cannot continue in his accustomed way [MTM B10:293].","citations":["A:144","MTM B10:293"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Duffey","Prophecy & Visions","Theater"]},{"name":"Patricia","definition":"Patricia Solli, also called Patti and Patrish, sister of Giovanni/Finnegan and Giacomo/Jake, daughter of Giulio Solli and Mary McCracken [A:173-174]. An acrobatic dancer of striking, weirdly comic beauty, she shares Finnegan’s sudden, almost inhumanly lithe movements. She is often with him and asks Dotty about his absences. She also identifies Doppio di Pinne as the same brother in another state [A:174]. Her family recollections contradict his: their father was a poor dockworker, literally antediluvian; Finnegan had spent only two days in Italy between ships and had invented van Ghi. She says she partly raised him, though he remained a stranger to her, and insists that he is incapable of wrongdoing despite appearances [A:174-175].","citations":["A:173-174","A:174","A:174-175"],"tags":["Women Characters","Finnegan","Family","Identity","Occupations"]},{"name":"Patrick","definition":"Saint Patrick, one of the saints Henry recognizes aboard the dream ship <em>Navicula Petri</em>, near Thomas, Hildebrand and Adrian the Dutchman [A:44-45]. A fifth-century bishop and missionary from Roman Britain, he is honored as Ireland’s patron and apostle. His <em>Confessio</em> recounts capture at about sixteen, six years of slavery and sheep-tending in Ireland, escape, return to his family and a visionary call to return as a missionary. He describes thousands of baptisms, the ordination of clergy and the growth of Christian and monastic life. His other surviving work, the letter against the soldiers of Coroticus, condemns the killing and enslavement of baptized converts. These writings are early firsthand evidence of Irish Christianity. The commonly given dates 432 for his return and 461 for his death are uncertain; his feast is March 17. Later traditions include the shamrock explanation of the Trinity and the banishment of snakes. Tírechán’s <em>Collectanea</em> lists Magonus and Succetus among four names for him; these should not be treated as a securely documented birth name “Magonus Sucatus.”","citations":["A:44-45"],"tags":["Saints","Ireland","Church History","Folklore","Dreams","Ships"]},{"name":"Patrick J. Stranahan","definition":"Successful St. Louis attorney and former magistrate, husband of Monica and father of Philip, Hugh, Timothy Navisius, Vincent, Norma and Maurine. <em>Archipelago</em> spells Monica’s maiden name Murry; <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> uses Murray [A:94] [MTM B5:161]. Patrick is large, hairy, muscular and paunchy, with a blue-emery-paper face, enormously hairy eyebrows and a rumble even when silent. Fey and discerning, he lives in the Cat Castle and is “Little-Irish Rich,” of the lace-curtain and grand-piano kind [A:102]. He is an excellent easy-chair pistol shot and an old lawyer-friend of Papa Piccone; the two imitate one another, Piccone turning Patrick into the stage character Hallohan J. O’Hoolihan. Patrick calls Show Boat “the Urchin” [A:104]. He expounds his compact philosophy of reading, art, travel, drink and religion in late conversation with Piccone, Father McGuigan and Charley Murry [A:109-110]. He is the father of one of Duffey’s Animated Marvels, Vincent, rather than one of that listed twelve himself [MTM B5:161-162]. Patrick is dead by Duffey’s late visit to the Cat Castle [MTM B12:385].","citations":["A:94","MTM B5:161","A:102","A:104","A:109-110","MTM B5:161-162","MTM B12:385"],"tags":["Family","Occupations","Social Class","Ireland","Theater","Weapons","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Paul","definition":"Saint Paul, the first-century apostle whose missionary journeys and letters shaped Christianity’s teaching about salvation, grace and the Church. His birth is uncertain, often estimated near the beginning of the century; ancient traditions place his martyrdom at Rome under Nero, approximately AD 64-68. Finnegan says he never saw an Ocean Island [A:35], and Henry recognizes him with Stephen and the Baptist aboard <em>Navicula Petri</em> [A:44-45]. Finnegan’s comparison of Anastasia to Diana humorously recasts Paul’s opposition to Demetrios as criticism of the silversmith’s oversized, pasty statue heads [D:133]. Book Six quotes Hebrews on Melchisedech under the traditional attribution “Paul. Hebrews.” [MTM B6:185]. Hebrews itself is anonymous, and its Pauline authorship is not accepted by the modern scholarly consensus. The Majesty, boasting of his own gibberish, recalls Paul’s contrary teaching that God is not its god [MTM B8:243].","citations":["A:35","A:44-45","D:133","MTM B6:185","MTM B8:243"],"tags":["Saints","New Testament","Theology","Church History","Finnegan","Dreams"]},{"name":"Pausanias","definition":"Pausanias, the Greek traveler and author of the ten-book <em>Description of Greece</em>, approximately AD 115-180. His account of sites, monuments, local history and religious practices preserves eyewitness evidence of ancient Greece. Hans, wondering about Marie’s green eyes, recalls that the statues were still painted when “Pausanes” saw them and asks what color words he might have used [A:24]. In <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>, Finnegan compares Anastasia’s head to the small Diana Artemis that “Pausanius” saw in the sanctuary of Asclepius at Messene [D:133]. The classical passage is <em>Description of Greece</em> 4.31.10, which lists Artemis Bringer of Light among the sanctuary’s statues; the small size, paint and natural expression belong to Lafferty’s description.","citations":["A:24","D:133"],"tags":["Greece","Historical Figures","Literary Figures","Literature","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Pausanius","definition":"Pausanius and Pausanes are the forms used in the novels for the Greek traveler Pausanias. See <em>Pausanias</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Greece","Historical Figures","Literary Figures","Literature","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Pearson, Shakes","definition":"Marie Monaghan’s whiskey-shaking poet uncle in her comic family story to Hans. Shakes enters a chewing-tobacco jingle competition, but repeatedly leaves his unfinished limericks for more pop-skull whiskey. Because he writes on bark with oak-ball juice, squirrels eat each attempt [A:25-26]. Marie says he kills himself, leaving Miriam, after whom she says she is named, a note urging her to do something about the squirrels. When Hans asks why they spared that note, Marie says it frightened them away. His further question about Australian squirrels exposes the story’s deliberate absurdity; she says wallabies would require explanation and cannot read [A:26-27].","citations":["A:25-26","A:26-27"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Alcohol","Family","Death & Mortality","Australia"]},{"name":"Peleus","definition":"The Argonaut identity of Kasmir W. Szymansky, Casey [A:39] [PG:535]. In his commentary, Duffey remembers Peleus as a good, brave crewman whose heroism remains largely hidden: called the great hero of Thessaly, yet indecisive and buffeted by accidents. In Duffey’s account he accidentally kills his half-brother Phocus and father-in-law Eurytion and is trapped by Acastus of Iolcus and his wife. The heroism concealed in Peleus appears openly in his son Achilles [PG:534-535].","citations":["A:39","PG:535","PG:534-535"],"tags":["Argonautica","Argonauts (Characters)","Greek Mythology","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Pelican","definition":"Dotty’s proposed name for the New Orleans magazine. Duffey explains its liturgical symbolism through the belief that a pelican feeds its young with its own blood, an image of Christ’s blood, and through its place as Louisiana’s symbol. Finnegan backs Henry’s proposal <em>Bark</em>, settling the choice [A:121]. The Holy Pelican also appears on the ornate door of the Pelican Press, bronzed by Finnegan and so vivid that Duffey wants to throw it a fish [MTM B12:359].","citations":["A:121","MTM B12:359"],"tags":["Journalism","New Orleans","Louisiana","Catholic Church","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Pelican Land","definition":"Casey’s mocking designation for the Louisiana home of the Pelican Press in his <em>Crock</em> editorial attacking the newly launched <em>Bark</em>: the new magazine comes “From Pelican Land and off the Pelican Press” [A:128]. The surrounding magazine venture is based in New Orleans [A:142].","citations":["A:128","A:142"],"tags":["New Orleans","Louisiana","Journalism"]},{"name":"Pelican Press","definition":"New Orleans printing and publishing operation associated with the <em>Bark</em>. Its old, worn fonts allow Dotty to identify the press used for Mr. X’s fabricated clipping about his own death: she says he printed it there and added the reverse before leaving [A:141-142]. The independently financed <em>Investigator</em> is also printed there [A:143]. After Dotty disappears, the people at the press keep expecting her return. Letitia becomes editor, loses some of its paying publication accounts and keeps the <em>Bark</em> afloat with private funds [MTM B8:238-239]. In “The Great Day,” Duffey seeks refuge in the old Quarter building he started with Finnegan and Dotty. Its schedule is the seamen’s paper on Monday, union sheet Tuesday, <em>Sporting News</em> Wednesday, jazz paper Friday, and the <em>Bark</em> Thursday or whenever it appears. Mary Virginia Schaeffer, Salvation Sally and Margaret Stone are there when he arrives [GD:490].","citations":["A:141-142","A:143","MTM B8:238-239","GD:490"],"tags":["Journalism","New Orleans","Duffey","Women Characters","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Penelope","definition":"Nickname Dotty reports in her letter to Marie. Immediately after complaining that Finnegan has not written, she says the boys call her Penelope and that her own suitors keep her spirits up [A:147-148]. The joke likens her waiting to that of Odysseus’s wife in Homer; <em>Archipelago</em> elsewhere identifies Finnegan with Ulysses [A:39].","citations":["A:147-148","A:39"],"tags":["Women Characters","Finnegan","Greek Mythology","Identity"]},{"name":"Pentanesia","definition":"Five-island cluster in Finnegan’s account of the original archipelagos. His Aegean list is Andros, Tinos, Paros and Amargos, with Hans supplying the forgotten Naxos; Finnegan insists that five is the magic number [A:35-36]. Later Ignacio calls the Leewards a group of five little islands. Finnegan says every pentanesia has a rogue island, and Ignacio nominates Barbuda as useless, a judgment Finnegan first questions and then accepts [A:272].","citations":["A:35-36","A:272"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Caribbean","Finnegan","Folklore","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Pentecost Morning","definition":"The original Pentecost as invoked by Margaret Stone to justify the urgency of street preaching. She says it was not easy then and contrasts that first urgency with the complacency of people for whom Christianity came two thousand years earlier [A:144].","citations":["A:144"],"tags":["New Testament","Liturgy","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Pentecost Sunday","definition":"Day Margaret Stone names for her own recent Pentecost: it came to her one year before the conversation in which she explains her urgent street preaching [A:144]. The preceding account describes her leaving the Party and joining the Church [A:143].","citations":["A:144","A:143"],"tags":["New Testament","Liturgy","Catholic Church","Women Characters"]},{"name":"People of the Book","definition":"Phrase in Marie Monaghan’s playful comparison of Arabs and Americans over coffee with Hans. She calls both “Peoples of the Book” despite irreligious intellectuals, then compares their distrust of alcohol, plainsmen, thugs and slum children [A:7].","citations":["A:7"],"tags":["Language & Words","Middle East","Alcohol"]},{"name":"People of the Nose","definition":"Comic grouping that includes Gabby Gabrielovitch and Finnegan. Gabby’s nose is likened to the craggy trans-Adriatic coast and a Montenegrin shepherd’s crozier [A:127]; Finnegan’s is introduced as a banana nose [A:1].","citations":["A:127","A:1"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Peso","definition":"Currency in the Philippine episodes. Nauti charges a peso for a bottle of Four Feathers or Five Roses whiskey, and the girls charge a peso for a dozen bananas because they have no change [A:64-65]. The soldiers scrape together two pesos for one bottle of Black Label [A:66]. Sammy Guiterrez then temporarily steals a shoeshine box while Finnegan distracts its owners with music and earns four pesos for four shines; the money buys more whiskey, including a final bottle for two pesos [A:67-68].","citations":["A:64-65","A:66","A:67-68"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Pacific Theater","Finnegan","Soldiers","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Peter (1)","definition":"Saint Peter, apostle and a leading figure of the early Church, regarded in Catholic tradition as the first Bishop of Rome and first pope. Christian tradition places his martyrdom in Rome during Nero’s persecution. The inherited glossary associates him with the line “A firster Peter for a rock” in the <em>Paean</em>; that preliminary text is absent from the available <em>Archipelago</em> PDF, so the quotation and its locator remain unverified.","citations":[],"tags":["Catholic Church","Saints","Popes","New Testament","Church History","Italy"]},{"name":"Peter (Salvatore Cousin)","definition":"Henry Salvatore’s younger cousin, part of the extended family around Morgan City, Louisiana. The nearby relatives comprise thirteen families. Peter follows Henry in everything, along with the older cousins Basil, Sebastian, Fabian and Adrian [A:122-123].","citations":["A:122-123"],"tags":["Family","Louisiana","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Petrarch (Francesco Petrarca) (1304--1374)","definition":"Francesco Petrarca, Petrarch (1304-1374), Italian poet, scholar and leading early humanist. His recovery of Cicero’s letters contributed to the revival of classical learning. His <em>Canzoniere</em>, or <em>Rerum vulgarium fragmenta</em>, comprises 366 poems in several forms, especially sonnets, much of it centered on Laura; its intense self-examination and poetic forms strongly influenced European lyric poetry. Endymeon Ellenbogen boasts to Hans that he has translated Petrarch [A:236-237]. When Anastasia tells Finnegan to sleep instead of writing down his sonnet, he asks what would have happened if Laura had refused Petrarch paper and pencil [D:133-134].","citations":["A:236-237","D:133-134"],"tags":["Italy","Literary Figures","Literature","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Petrof, Lord Peter","definition":"Alias of Ali ben Taife, the jewel-thief master whom Amoy serves in Karachi in Ignatius Ti’s story [A:80-81].","citations":["A:80-81"],"tags":["Crime","Identity","Folklore"]},{"name":"Petrus, Tu es","definition":"Latin for “You are Peter,” from Matthew 16:18. It appears on a banner of <em>Navicula Petri</em>, Henry’s dream ship [A:44].","citations":["A:44"],"tags":["New Testament","Ships","Dreams","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Pharisees","definition":"Father McGuigan calls Patrick Stranahan a Pharisee of the Right and says such people are no different from the Sadducees of the Left; Patrick replies that the Sadducees denied the resurrection [A:110]. The Devil’s mouth-spoken address to Duffey attacks Pharisees alongside Legalists, Rigidists and the Reactionary Curia; his accompanying mental address is essentially the same speech [MTM B8:243].","citations":["A:110","MTM B8:243"],"tags":["The Devil","Duffey","Theology","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Philip Stranahan","definition":"Vincent Stranahan’s older brother, ten years old when Duffey emerges from the Mississippi beneath the Eads Bridge in St. Louis in 1923. Philip is fishing from the pier. He is the eldest of Philip, Hugh, Timothy and Vincent, and has the eyes Duffey remembers in the adult Vincent. In this altered past, however, Philip says the baby Vincent was born on April 5, 1921, and died on April 10. Philip is in fifth grade, Teresa Piccone in first; he invites Duffey to the Cat Castle [MTM B13:470-471]. On Duffey’s return to the pier, Philip protests that it is afternoon when Duffey sings about morning [MTM B13:473].","citations":["MTM B13:470-471","MTM B13:473"],"tags":["Family","Duffey","Fishing","Time & Eternity","Paradox"]},{"name":"Philology","definition":"The study Papa Piccone recommends to policemen and colonels during a dispute over the sign advertising his theater as burlesque. In Show Boat’s letter, Papa is a purist who means the word in its original sense; suggesting that the authorities take a short philology course only worsens the dispute [A:48].","citations":["A:48"],"tags":["Language & Words","Theater"]},{"name":"Phoenecian, old","definition":"Old Phoenician, spelled “old Phoenecian” in the text: the language Casey dreams of speaking fluently with an exceptionally odd traveler during the seventh of his twelve hidden worlds [A:159].","citations":["A:159"],"tags":["Dreams","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Piccone, Papa (Gaetano)","definition":"Gaetano Piccone, proprietor of the Star and Garter theater, husband of Marie Caterina Panebianco and father of Theresa, Show Boat [A:94]. Flamboyant and fond of gambling, he likes to display hundred-dollar bills [A:95]. Show Boat’s letter describes his quarrel with authorities over the original meaning of burlesque [A:48]. An old friend and agreeable argumentative opponent of Patrick Stranahan, he can talk until sunrise; each imitates the other, and Papa turns Patrick into Hallohan J. O’Hoolihan onstage [A:104]. For Vincent and Theresa’s wedding he proposes playing accordion in the church aisles instead of hiring an organist, but Maurine says he is kidding. His wine preparations comprise three barrels and a collection of jugs: he intends to avoid the shortage at Cana [A:105]. He joins Charley Murray’s older family party at the Rounders’ Club [MTM B5:162]. He claims to have made a fictional man who became real, named Oliver Oscar Omygosh in his account and Oscar Oliver Omygosh in the ensuing recollection. Bagby remembers the novelty-shop proprietor but rejects Papa’s claim to have created him [MTM B5:167-168]. Papa is dead by Duffey’s late visit to the Cat Castle [MTM B12:385].","citations":["A:94","A:95","A:48","A:104","A:105","MTM B5:162","MTM B5:167-168","MTM B12:385"],"tags":["Theater","Family","Money & Trade","Alcohol","Liturgy","Clubs & Societies","Creation","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Piccone, Teresa (Theresa Antoinette Piccone, Show Boat, Showboat, Teresa Piccone Stranahan, Margie la Maga, La Maga-La Strega)","definition":"Theresa Antoinette Piccone, also called Teresa or Show Boat, is born in St. Louis on October 15, 1923, to Gaetano “Papa” Piccone, owner of the Star and Garter, and Marie Caterina Panebianco [A:94]. She is Beth Keegan’s goddaughter [MTM B5:162]. She marries Vincent Stranahan on the last Saturday of May 1946 [A:95] [A:113]. Patrick Stranahan calls her the Urchin [A:104]. Her shifting expressions make her difficult to describe; Duffey encounters her as a person of dazzling presence and great kindness [MTM B5:162-163] [MTM B5:180-181].<br><br>She and Finnegan are the two central figures among Duffey’s creations [MTM B5:162]. She later disputes the language of creation: Duffey awakened their ancient selves and brought about their rebirth [MTM B7:233]. She identifies herself as Atalanta, only one part of her identity [MTM B7:234], and describes their talismans as pieces of the Holy <em>Argo</em> [MTM B5:179]. Before the assembled friends, she washes her hands, face, and hair in flame drawn from Duffey’s ashes [MTM B7:232-233].<br><br>On a New Orleans visit, she writes and sets an entire issue of the <em>Bark</em>, then produces a <em>Show-Bill</em> with an advertisement for the newly appearing Decatur Street Opera House. She also cooks elaborate Italian meals that her friends find difficult to eat [MTM B7:229-230]. Her street-preaching with Margaret Stone is disrupted by disguised hecklers, who prove to be Finnegan, Vincent, and Stein. Once she recognizes them, she turns the crowd against them [MTM B7:231-232].<br><br>Her intimacy with Finnegan crosses ordinary identities and time. He writes to her of a love only the two of them understand [A:146]; she describes his ghostly visits in a private letter [MTM B6:188]. He calls her a witch while she calls herself a magician and clairvoyante [A:108], and later, while studying his portraits, he identifies her with Anastasia [A:278]. In <em>Archipelago</em>, Chiara, Rafaello, and Theresa Anna belong to her uncertain twelve-year life with Finnegan; the text says her children with Vincent at that time have different names [A:204]. <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> later gives the same three names to her younger children with Vincent [MTM B12:385].<br><br>She writes to Dotty about Casey, Vincent, and the ordination she has attended [A:262-263]. She recommends Carmelo Mendoza to Duffey as a companion for his journeys [MTM B12:369]. After Duffey deeds her his half of the Rounders’ Club, she runs it with Vincent, who has inherited Charley Murray’s half [MTM B12:385]. She discusses the amnesia surrounding the <em>Argo</em> and recites a prophecy of its final cargoes [MTM B12:386] [MTM B12:388]. Near the end, a five-year-old Teresa recognizes the ghostly Duffey and speaks with him about Vincent’s uncertain life and their possible births [MTM B13:472].","citations":["A:94","MTM B5:162","A:95","A:113","A:104","MTM B5:162-163","MTM B5:180-181","MTM B7:233","MTM B7:234","MTM B5:179","MTM B7:232-233","MTM B7:229-230","MTM B7:231-232","A:146","MTM B6:188","A:108","A:278","A:204","MTM B12:385","A:262-263","MTM B12:369","MTM B12:386","MTM B12:388","MTM B13:472"],"tags":["Women Characters","Argonauts (Characters)","Finnegan","Duffey","Family","Journalism","Theater","Clubs & Societies","Magic & Sorcery","Ghosts & Spirits","Identity","Creation"]},{"name":"Pig-Sticker","definition":"Duffey’s preferred name for the projected New Orleans magazine, which he considers direct and inclusive of its aims. Henry proposes <em>Bark</em>, and Finnegan’s support settles that choice [A:121].","citations":["A:121"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Pillar","definition":"The “Pillar and the Cloud” invoked by Absalom Stein when asking Duffey whether he had been in Egypt, the desert and Babylon with the Jews. Duffey then asks why Stein is a neo-Jew rather than a Christian [A:131]. The biblical background is the Lord’s guidance of Israel by a pillar of cloud by day and fire by night in Exodus 13:21-22.","citations":["A:131"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Duffey"]},{"name":"Pinne, Doppio di (Dopey the Seaman)","definition":"Seaman called Dopey, whose identity overlaps Finnegan’s. Patti calls Doppio di Pinne the same brother in another state [A:174]. Joe Cross notes the same size, shape and coloring, especially an alarming resemblance in peripheral vision; Finnegan, who remembers only having Dopey pointed out to him, sees little facial resemblance [D:65]. Dopey occupied Finnegan’s bunk on the <em>Brunhilde</em>. Joe reports that Papadiabolous entered the cabin and shut the door; three screams followed, but Joe found only Papa and a little blood. No body was produced. The account does not establish a locked door or an unambiguous killing [D:63-64]. Finnegan sometimes feels another presence in the bunk [D:64] [D:78]. A letter hidden behind an upper balk reveals Dopey’s bargain, hopes of wealth and power, the intended replacement of Finnegan, impatience that the “bugle nose” is still alive and fear that Dopey himself will die trapped [D:50-51] [D:78]. Papa first suspects that Finnegan killed Dopey or is Dopey with an altered nose [D:89]. Later he calls Dopey a fetch, ghost or absorbed double and admits uncertainty about who, if anyone, killed him. He describes Dopey as of the strange blood, a sometime killer brought by Seaworthy to kill Papadiabolous [D:121-122]. X’s later account links the intended victim to the old Papadiabolous and explains Seaworthy’s mistake of Finnegan for the hired killer [D:153]. While shaving, Finnegan sees Dopey in the mirror: the same coloring but coarser follicles and a different skin texture, dazed and afraid of the razor. Dopey says he is not dead and guesses he is there. After Finnegan smashes the mirror, its fragments still show Dopey with his eyes closed in tortured sleep [D:187-188].","citations":["A:174","D:65","D:63-64","D:64","D:78","D:50-51","D:89","D:121-122","D:153","D:187-188"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Ships","Finnegan","Identity","The Dead","Ghosts & Spirits","Death & Mortality","Crime"]},{"name":"Pirates' Alley","definition":"New Orleans location where, according to Patricia Solli, Finnegan received his art instruction from drunken painters, apart from grade-school teaching by sisters [A:175]. Duffey also sells magazines there and around its studios [A:143]. Mary Virginia mentions the new painters around Pirates’ Alley among the attractions distracting Duffey from the <em>Populist History</em> [MTM B11:310].","citations":["A:175","A:143","MTM B11:310"],"tags":["New Orleans","Finnegan","Visual Arts","Duffey"]},{"name":"Pirogue, Candy Sue","definition":"One of three twenty-one-year-old women drawn by Honeybucket Kincaid’s frequency modulation to the dock where Duffey and Biloxi Brannagan are visiting. She arrives in a rowboat with Janeway Celeste Lynne, who introduces her as a cousin, and Etta Mae Mansion [MTM B13:419]. Candy Sue asks for Puff Fish despite finding them disgusting. After Duffey removes their frequency and existence, she can no longer remember their name [MTM B13:420–421].","citations":["MTM B13:419","MTM B13:420–421"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Women Characters","Duffey","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Plato (427--347 BC)","definition":"Greek philosopher, approximately 428/427-348/347 BC, a pupil of Socrates, founder of the Academy at Athens and teacher of Aristotle. His dramatic dialogues examine knowledge, ethics and politics; the <em>Republic</em> presents rule by philosophers, and his theory of Forms concerns intelligible, unchanging realities. His portrayal of Socrates is influential but is not a verbatim record of the historical man. Anastasia jokes that she and Finnegan could give Plato lessons: he was not very platonic, whereas they develop their own ideas [D:124]. The fictional Carmody Overlark lists broad-headed Plato among supposed Neanderthals in an epigraph from <em>Studies in the Imagination</em> [D:271].","citations":["D:124","D:271"],"tags":["Greece","Historical Figures","Literature","Metaphysics","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Plaza Hotel","definition":"Vast Sydney building described as the city’s center and a place where shimmering people pass pleasurable hours. It contains forty-eight bars, railway and tram stations, shops, arcades and clubs. Finnegan and Vincent arrive with Loy and Margaret, and Hans and Marie join them; Margie is Margaret, not an additional visitor [A:2-3]. During the Sergeant’s strange passage out of the hospital ward and island setting, he returns to Sydney and drinks gin there with Tom Shire and Freddy Castle [A:88].","citations":["A:2-3","A:88"],"tags":["Australia","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Finnegan","Soldiers","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Pleistocene Ice Age, fourth","definition":"Stage of geological history in Vincent Stranahan’s Theory of the Uncreated Creation. He asks whether the earth could have been created ten minutes earlier, already descending from a fourth Pleistocene ice age and supplied with fossils, vestiges, carbon balances, atomic clocks and sediments suggesting fifteen hundred million years. This is his thought experiment about created evidence of age [A:56].","citations":["A:56"],"tags":["Creation","Time & Eternity","Paradox"]},{"name":"Pliny the Elder (Gaius Plinius Secundus) (23/24--79 AD)","definition":"Gaius Plinius Secundus, approximately AD 23/24-79, Roman author, naturalist and naval commander. His <em>Natural History</em> is an encyclopedic account of the natural world and human knowledge. His nephew’s account describes him commanding the fleet at Misenum, taking ships to rescue people during the eruption of Vesuvius and dying at Stabiae. In Ignacio’s fanciful history of a cannon-fired lunar journey, Father Joseph Maintz takes Pliny’s <em>Natural History</em> along with the Bible, a missal, Augustine and Jerome [A:271].","citations":["A:271"],"tags":["Italy","Historical Figures","Literary Figures","Literature","Death & Mortality","Folklore"]},{"name":"Poland","definition":"Country invoked in Casey’s hostile <em>Crock</em> editorial: he accuses the <em>Bark</em> of supporting clerical-reactionary elements and Gabrielovitch of opposing what Casey calls a rational Polish-Russian settlement [A:129-130]. X’s account places the birth of Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch in a hayfield near Krakow [D:278-279]. Duffey’s Argonaut commentary identifies Prince Casimir with Poland [PG:536]. In Duffey’s encounter with Kasmir Gorshok, the stranger’s face resembles a map of Poland with its changing, lopsided outlines [MTM B13:425-426].","citations":["A:129-130","D:278-279","PG:536","MTM B13:425-426"],"tags":["Eastern Europe","Journalism","Duffey","Identity","Family"]},{"name":"Polish","definition":"Language that the Sergeant says the Dirty Five taught the Jojos, who thought they were learning English while the Gorgios took it for Malay [A:73]. Casey is willing to learn Polish from his father, and the account of his hidden worlds says his father knew it [A:156] [A:159]. The adjective also occurs in Casey’s attack on Gabrielovitch’s support of the London Polish government [A:129], X’s account of Katie Noonan as a governess with a landed Polish family [D:279], and the description of Zabotski’s large Polish hands [MTM B11:343].","citations":["A:73","A:156","A:159","A:129","D:279","MTM B11:343"],"tags":["Language & Words","Eastern Europe","Family","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Polly Polyglot (Mary Frances Rattigan)","definition":"Mary Frances Rattigan’s pen name for “Books Abroad” in the <em>Crock</em> during Duffey’s wartime editorship [A:41]. Under that name she supplies translations for the magazine [MTM B4:138].","citations":["A:41","MTM B4:138"],"tags":["Women Characters","Literature","Journalism","Language & Words","Identity","Duffey"]},{"name":"Pop skull whisky","definition":"Whiskey drunk by Shakes Pearson, Marie Monaghan’s uncle in her comic family story. She describes him drinking and continually shaking [A:25].","citations":["A:25"],"tags":["Alcohol","Family"]},{"name":"Pope, Alexander (1688--1744)","definition":"Alexander Pope (1688-1744), English poet noted for heroic couplets and satire, including <em>The Rape of the Lock</em> and <em>The Dunciad</em>. <em>An Essay on Criticism</em> treats poetry and critical judgment; <em>An Essay on Man</em> examines humanity, morality and the order of the world. During the account of Casey’s problem of evil, <em>Archipelago</em> quotes the master passion growing “Like Aaron’s serpent” and swallowing the rest [A:154], from <em>An Essay on Man</em>, Epistle II, lines 121-122.","citations":["A:154"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Popotov, Sarkis","definition":"Russian resident of New Orleans whose account Margaret Stone quotes during the Royal Pop History episode. He has lived in the same place for forty years, but cannot remember the buildings that stood next door before the appearance of Artaguette Park. The park looks unfamiliar, though tourists assure him it will look familiar by the next day [MTM B11:309].","citations":["MTM B11:309"],"tags":["Minor Characters","New Orleans","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Populus tremuloides","definition":"Aspen name used by X when comparing his trembling with trees and recalling his young wife. He calls <em>Populus tremuloides</em> the aspen of his own land and contrasts it with <em>Populus grandidentata</em>, using American and European labels [A:140-141]. His botanical comparison mixes the names: <em>P. tremuloides</em> and <em>P. grandidentata</em> are both native to North America, while the European or Eurasian aspen is <em>P. tremula</em>.","citations":["A:140-141"],"tags":["Language & Words","Family"]},{"name":"Porpoise","definition":"Animal in Trinali’s account of driftwood in the Old Wood Graveyard: it has learned to leap like a dolphin and plunge like a porpoise [A:233]. Anastasia compares mermaids to porpoises because they must surface to breathe air [D:34]. The concluding account of bells says creatures from plankton to porpoises enjoy submerged bells and their signals [MB:575].","citations":["A:233","D:34","MB:575"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Nymphs & Mermaids","Music"]},{"name":"Port Said","definition":"Port named by Loy Larkin as a location of a Duffey’s Tavern, along with Cork and New York [A:2].","citations":["A:2"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Duffey","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Portae inferi non prevalebunt","definition":"“The gates of hell shall not prevail,” words from Matthew 16:18 on one of the banners of Henry’s dream ship <em>Navicula Petri</em> [A:44]. The novel spells the last word <em>prevalebunt</em>; the standard Latin biblical form is <em>praevalebunt</em>.","citations":["A:44"],"tags":["New Testament","Heaven & Hell","Ships","Dreams","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Possession","definition":"Private Gregory’s proposed perfect ghost story: possession as hypnotism from beyond the grave by an immortal will. He identifies ghosts with devils and claims that powerful leaders can become shells inhabited by devils through a bargain for power. In his account, such possessed men do much of the world’s running [A:84-85].","citations":["A:84-85"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Immortality","The Devil","Soldiers","Literature"]},{"name":"Pot, The","definition":"One college version of Casey Szymansky’s student <em>Crocks</em>, whose related names include <em>Nattkarlet</em>, <em>Night Charley</em> and <em>Nocnik</em>. At successive colleges he loses control of the papers within two or three weeks. The <em>Pot</em> subsequently circulates in a foul form; the account distinguishes this deterioration from Casey’s original, nonfilthy humor [A:167-168].","citations":["A:167-168"],"tags":["Journalism","Literature"]},{"name":"Potator Incognitus, Sanctus Apostolus","definition":"“Holy Apostle, Unknown Drinker,” the playful invocation Show Boat uses in her letter to Dotty while defending Finnegan. Papa Piccone has told her an Italian legend of a tippling apostle, either Thomas, Bartholomew or Jude, who finished the abandoned wine yet converted tens of thousands; Papa cannot remember which apostle it was [A:262].","citations":["A:262"],"tags":["Prayer & Devotion","New Testament","Folklore","Italy","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Power Factor","definition":"Electrical term in entry XIV of Casey Szymansky’s high-school notebook. He defines it as the cosine of the phase-lag angle between voltage and current in an alternating-current circuit [A:162-163].","citations":["A:162-163"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Prague","definition":"City Vincent proposes visiting with Finnegan while trying to escape his approaching marriage: with his three thousand dollars and mustering-out money, they could sail to Venice and Prague. Finnegan answers with a joke about the sea-coast of Bohemia [A:112].","citations":["A:112"],"tags":["Sailing","Finnegan","Eastern Europe"]},{"name":"Preadamite","definition":"Speculative element in the narrator’s description of Margaret Stone’s ancestry: she is Italian and Jewish, with “possibly a little” Preadamite in her [A:144].","citations":["A:144"],"tags":["Old Testament","Women Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Prescott, Newton","definition":"Electronics repairman and designer who narrates “The Casey Machine.” He writes his journal as therapy for a compulsion to forget the events surrounding the machine [CM:503]. In Josephine McSorely’s account, he credits Casey with the impetus for the device, proposes giving its initially mental operation physical components, and describes using electrical discharges from the dead [CM:506–507]. In the addenda, Prescott doubts that he exists as an independent person and calls himself a minor aspect of Casey. He recalls Casey saying that he gave a splinter of his personality a ghostly body and the name Newton Prescott, to remain his eyes and ears after death [CM:515].","citations":["CM:503","CM:506–507","CM:515"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Identity","Technology","Ghosts & Spirits","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Presence","definition":"Hypnotic stage quality, paired with “the Mystic,” that Hans cannot develop during his apprenticeship with Professor Kirol. Although below the Professor’s class as a performer, Hans genuinely hypnotizes people in cases where the Professor sometimes fakes it; the narrator jokes that the real thing must serve when the counterfeit is beyond one’s ability [A:219].","citations":["A:219"],"tags":["Theater","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Priestism, Judas","definition":"One of the seven contemporary heads Bascom Bagby assigns to the old enemy. His full list is Dialectic Materialism, Artistic Degradation, Judas Priestism, Secular Liberalism, Panaceic Pentecostalism, Murderous Molochism and Atheistic Communism [MTM B5:169].","citations":["MTM B5:169"],"tags":["The Devil","Cosmic Struggle","Theology"]},{"name":"Prince Casimir","definition":"Casey Szymansky’s medieval identity in Duffey’s Argonaut commentary: Prince and Saint Casimir of Poland, defender of the poor who thwarts the mighty and whose apparent death is a withdrawal from the world [PG:536]. Teresa also quotes a prophecy that the ghostly Duffey will carry Prince Casimir aboard the <em>Argo</em> to reign as Antichrist near Armageddon [MTM B12:388]. During the later voyage, the passenger is explicitly revealed as Peleus, Kasmir Gorshok, Prince Casimir, Casey and Antichristus, and is landed at Habonim near the Plains of Megiddo [MTM B13:462]. Duffey, Brannagan and Gunboat then leave this future, admitting they failed to prevent it this time and wondering about another chance [MTM B13:463].","citations":["PG:536","MTM B12:388","MTM B13:462","MTM B13:463"],"tags":["Argonautica","Ships","Sailing","Duffey","Identity","Apocalyptic","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Pristine Time","definition":"See <em>Pristine World.</em> This index heading refers to time in the Pristine World, whose chronology Zabotski distinguishes from that of the everyday world [MTM B9:279] [MTM B9:280].","citations":["MTM B9:279","MTM B9:280"],"tags":["Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Pristine World","definition":"The world that Zabotski visits during absences of a day or two which contain years of experience [MTM B9:269] [MTM B9:271]. He calls it “the real and essential world,” occupying the same space as the Everyday-or-Molasses World without occupying it in the same way. In his account, it excludes duplicated and non-valid persons and situations, while adding much that ordinary history lacks [MTM B9:279]. He compares it to a film of a football game reduced from three hours of clock time to a few minutes containing the game’s complete essence. Its brevity therefore accompanies an abundance of experience: he can live overflowing years there during a day or two in the Molasses World [MTM B9:279]. Zabotski says he remains imperfectly accelerated for it and returns here to rest and see friends [MTM B9:277]. Duffey’s narrative confirms his own visit of three or five years, followed by a return in two days [MTM B9:284]. Zabotski’s <em>Pristine History of the World</em> approaches the Fundamentalers’ chronology by a different method, excluding duplication and non-validity rather than simply adopting their dates [MTM B9:279] [MTM B9:280].","citations":["MTM B9:269","MTM B9:271","MTM B9:279","MTM B9:277","MTM B9:284","MTM B9:280"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","History & Memory","Paradox"]},{"name":"Prosody, Latin","definition":"Subject of Henry Salvatore’s seminary verse assignment, which ends <em>Deus tam dilexit mundum!</em> His instructor marks him down with the joke that Latin is not Italian enough to have italics and therefore cannot use them [A:149].","citations":["A:149"],"tags":["Literature","Language & Words","Religious Orders"]},{"name":"Protagoras","definition":"Name used by Gregory McIfreann Papadiabolous, the Asiatic brother or Papadiabolous I, in X’s account of the brothers’ aliases [D:285]. It is also the name of Protagoras of Abdera, the Greek Sophist, approximately 490-420 BC.","citations":["D:285"],"tags":["The Devil","Greece","Historical Figures","Identity"]},{"name":"Psychosis, Korsakof's","definition":"Finnegan’s own name for his condition when the Travelers’ Aid woman questions his confusion and memory loss in a railway station. He calls it “Korsakofs Psychosis,” declines a doctor, and says doctors blame drinking, as they did with his father [A:243-244]. Korsakoff syndrome is a severe memory disorder associated with thiamine deficiency and often chronic alcohol misuse; the novel’s identification remains Finnegan’s self-report.","citations":["A:243-244"],"tags":["Finnegan","History & Memory","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Pula","definition":"Tagalog word for red or redness. Amoy says it after Ignatius Ti describes an earlier bird incarnation as a crow or blackbird; Ti freely expands the reply into the statement that Amoy was a red-winged blackbird [A:80].","citations":["A:80"],"tags":["Language & Words","Folklore"]},{"name":"Purgatory","definition":"Afterlife realm appearing in several different voices. The narrator’s fantastic account of Black Papua says its inhabitants die nightly, visit Purgatory and return shaken with instructions from the dead [A:37]. Professor Kirol places Purgatory, Hell and Heaven within the spiritual universe already surrounding us [A:214]. Show Boat wishes Vincent a long afternoon in Purgatory when he challenges her use of “inordinate” in the tale of the tippling apostle [A:262]. Cardinal Salvatore locates the dead Bascom Bagby on Purgatory’s north slope [MTM B12:384]; Bagby’s posthumous letters describe work, strengthened passions and complex relationships outside ordinary time [MTM B8:248-249]. Patrick describes a particularly shabby, dank, mud-colored section where persistent makers compulsively form things over and over [MTM B12:391]. Bagby’s commentary places Casey among the Poor Souls, repairing the exhausted scapegoats that bring them further burdens from the living [PG:546].","citations":["A:37","A:214","A:262","MTM B12:384","MTM B8:248-249","MTM B12:391","PG:546"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Death & Mortality","Metaphysics","Creation","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Purple Prince","definition":"One of the adversaries of the <em>Argo</em> Masters, named with the Evil Prince, the Mocking Prince and the Laughing Prince. The narrative calls this group among the most formidable of the Masters’ opponents, while suggesting that the Laughing Prince of Tartary may be the most powerful and trickiest of them [MTM B13:437].","citations":["MTM B13:437"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Argo","Good & Evil"]},{"name":"Putiphar","definition":"Biblical name the narrator gives Adrian Shapiro, the wealthy, well-connected husband of Mercedes. Finnegan is commissioned to paint Mercedes and sees an opening into a dazzling circle. Her playfulness casts him as a goatish Joseph: he departs with his coat but leaves his paints of many colors, fearing ridicule rather than imprisonment and welcoming an excuse to return to the road [A:193].","citations":["A:193"],"tags":["Old Testament","Finnegan","Visual Arts","Identity","Social Class"]},{"name":"Putty","definition":"Material whose recipe Casey records in entry XVII of his high-school notebook: chalk and linseed oil [A:162] [A:164].","citations":["A:162","A:164"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Pygmies","definition":"Patrick Stranahan’s belittling contrast to giants when he tells Father McGuigan that the Church’s age of giants has ended and an age of pygmies is beginning [A:110]. The inherited glossary also records “They never knew the race of pygmies” from the <em>Paean</em>, concerning the early Argonauts; that preliminary text is absent from the available <em>Archipelago</em> edition, so the quotation remains unverified.","citations":["A:110"],"tags":["Argonautica","Giants & Titans","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Pythagoras","definition":"Greek thinker from Samos, approximately 570-490 BC, who established a religious way of life in southern Italy associated with the transmigration of souls and an ordered, harmonious cosmos. Later tradition emphasizes mathematics and the theorem bearing his name, but a proof by Pythagoras himself is not securely attested. In the discussion of Casey’s problem of evil, the narrator attributes to him a catalogue dividing qualities into good and evil, then calls it incomplete [A:154]. This echoes the Pythagorean table of opposites reported by Aristotle, <em>Metaphysics</em> I.5; the novel reverses the traditional positions of odd and even.","citations":["A:154"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Greece","Mysticism","Metaphysics","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Pythagoras (c. 570--c. 495 BC)","definition":"Earlier heading supplied an approximate death date differing from the date used in the checked account. See <em>Pythagoras</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Historical Figures","Greece","Mysticism","Metaphysics","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Quantum law","definition":"The narrator’s term for the threshold needed to ring an old rope-operated church bell in an Iowa town during one of Duffey’s childhoods: an insufficient pull produces no movement. A fat altar boy normally supplies the necessary weight. When that boy has pneumonia, eight-year-old Duffey, weighing sixty-three pounds, takes his place [MTM B1:14]. Duffey commands an invisible giant to help; the giant’s hand itself is visible to the other boys and successfully pulls the rope [MTM B1:15].","citations":["MTM B1:14","MTM B1:15"],"tags":["Giants & Titans","Duffey","Miracles","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Quarter","definition":"New Orleans neighborhood in which Finnegan and Dotty revisit friends, carouse, talk and visit her former workplace while avoiding the noisier jazz haunts Finnegan dislikes [A:115]. He knows everyone there and claims to have grown up in its heart, while Patti places their home in Irish Channel [A:175]. During his later confusion, it becomes a spinning, gin-soaked city of altered street names and apparently part-animal people, mixing remembered and future places from other cities [A:200-201]. Margaret Stone reports that people in the Quarter have been drinking Green Ladies during the transformations surrounding the Pop History meetings: like peppermint schnapps, but with absinthe replacing the schnapps [MTM B11:309].","citations":["A:115","A:175","A:200-201","MTM B11:309"],"tags":["New Orleans","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Music","Dreams","Identity"]},{"name":"Queen (Virgin Queen)","definition":"Protective figure in the Tristram-and-Isolde ballad opening the Casey chapter of <em>Archipelago</em>. Isolde dreams of standing before a pit, guarded by the Virgin Queen’s watchful hands [A:151]. Later she recalls them as holy hands and asks to return, yet finally feels drawn toward the pit and Tristram’s distant island despite the storm [A:152].","citations":["A:151","A:152"],"tags":["Dreams","Literature","Islands","Women Characters","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Quest Accomplished motif","definition":"The Fourteenth Voyage of the <em>Argo</em>, presented among the alternatives of Duffey’s world, is the “Quest Accomplished” motif: the fleece has been found, the moment of finding endures, and continuing beatitudes promise Final Beatitude [MTM B10:300]. The opening poem of Vincent’s chapter likewise makes the Dirty Five sailors on the ancient <em>Argo</em> in search of golden cargo [A:91].","citations":["MTM B10:300","A:91"],"tags":["Argonautica","Ships","Sailing","Theology","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Questions 382, 383 (Baltimore Catechism)","definition":"Questions quoted as numbers 382 and 383 in the <em>Casey Machine</em> epigraph. The just rise glorious and immortal; its explanation describes bodies shining like the sun, swift as thought, able to penetrate matter, beautiful and free of pain or change. The damned also rise but receive eternal punishment. The following narration applies bodily splendor and penetration of time to the Casey condition [CM:511]. The same basic questions are numbered 418 and 419 in the consulted <em>Baltimore Catechism</em> text.","citations":["CM:511"],"tags":["Resurrection","Immortality","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","Heaven & Hell"]},{"name":"Quick, Catherine","definition":"One of Duffey’s Chicago friends of a scientific bent [MTM B3:99] and one of the adult women he loves [MTM B4:111]. She appears among the women with whom photographs show him playing the funny lover in the blackmail portfolio associated with Hugo Stone [MTM B4:139]. In <em>Proud & Glorious</em>, Stein names her among the scientific friends he has consulted about brain-reading and mind-reading [PG:539].","citations":["MTM B3:99","MTM B4:111","MTM B4:139","PG:539"],"tags":["Women Characters","Duffey","Minor Characters","Crime"]},{"name":"Quintessence of Ibsenism","definition":"George Bernard Shaw’s <em>The Quintessence of Ibsenism</em>, read by Casey in high school. His notebook uses it to illustrate his rule to read originals: books about Shaw did not explain Shaw to him, and Shaw’s book did not explain Ibsen, so he had to read each author directly [A:166].","citations":["A:166"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Quirt, The Milford","definition":"Whip in the turbaned Pop History speaker’s nostalgic list. A specially enduring flagstone on Decatur Street remembers its snap and swish along with those of other whips and the traffic of horses, carriages, streetcars and automobiles [MTM B11:329].","citations":["MTM B11:329"],"tags":["New Orleans","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Quo vadis, filia mea?","definition":"“Where are you going, my daughter?” The old church-tower bells ask this in the Tristram-and-Isolde ballad, as Isolde fears the storm and the voyage toward the island [A:151-152]. It may echo Peter’s question to Christ, “Lord, where are you going?”, in the apocryphal <em>Acts of Peter</em> 35, where Christ’s answer turns Peter back toward Rome and martyrdom.","citations":["A:151-152"],"tags":["Language & Words","Dreams","Literature","Saints","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"R.C.s","definition":"Abbreviation for Roman Catholics. Salvation Sally guesses Marie and Hans are R.C.s from their flippancy about the Second Coming, though they alone have contributed to her cup. She calls established churches traps and theirs the deepest. Marie speaks the French sentence about Rome's power; Sally answers that she knows that power, adding that she knows Bohemia's heresy too [A:8].","citations":["A:8"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Rabat","definition":"Moroccan city on the Atlantic coast, called at by the <em>Brunhilde</em> after Casablanca and before Tangier on its northward voyage [D:101].","citations":["D:101"],"tags":["Africa","Ports & Harbors","Ships"]},{"name":"Rabbit raising","definition":"One of the small arts and activities the narrator says were trashed during the cultural decline following the Devil's release [MTM B6:207]. The catalogue concludes that these activities have lost their class and style [MTM B6:208].","citations":["MTM B6:207","MTM B6:208"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Racquets","definition":"Game advertised on the sign of Duffey's Rounders' Club in St. Louis, an establishment for gentlemen rounders offering racquets, whist, poker, horseshoes and other amusements [MTM B2:49]. Charley Murray subsequently becomes its half owner [MTM B2:53].","citations":["MTM B2:49","MTM B2:53"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","Duffey"]},{"name":"Rafelson, Doctor Saul","definition":"Chicago analyst whom Duffey and Letitia call Doctor Raffles. He never releases Duffey as cured and considers a cure impossible. He catalogues Duffey's fantasies: Child of God, Child of Gold, Master of Ebony Slaves, Master of Giants, Riding King, Boy King, Miracle Worker, immense age, successive and parallel lives, a many-layered self, the monster within, living shadows, conferring power, making people, the company of elites, conferring talismans and lives, and saving the world by his intervention. Rafelson calls these ordinary fantasies of unhealthy boys' minds; Duffey's extraordinary quality is making them happen. He has seen weaker examples in two other patients [MTM B4:146]. He considers psychic projections, captured real people and independent split images as explanations. Two freestanding images he analyzes have healthy, intelligent, substantial bodies; one donates blood. These images are distinct from Duffey's twelve creations, which Rafelson interprets as aspects of monumental egomania. He acknowledges Duffey's magic and entertains the possibility of a topological inversion placing the world within Duffey. The twelve are contingent: if they attain congruent fulfillment they will always have existed, with a past supplied by preternatural plot construction; otherwise they will never have existed [MTM B4:147]. He warns that three embodied unrealities might murder Duffey and have already tried. He believes Duffey's phenomena bear on overlapping persons, creator and created, shared reality and the meaning of matter, but refuses to pursue the implications because they frighten him [MTM B4:148].","citations":["MTM B4:146","MTM B4:147","MTM B4:148"],"tags":["Duffey","Magic & Sorcery","Identity","Creation","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Raft racing","definition":"One of the activities the narrator says were trashed during the cultural decline following the Devil's release; it appears among minor arts and recreations said to have lost their class and style [MTM B6:207-208].","citations":["MTM B6:207-208"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Rag music","definition":"Music played on two victrolas, one cylinder and one disc, in the soundproof attic billiard room at the house of Lily Koch's relatives during the first compulsory school dance. Duffey, Charley Murray, John Rattigan, Sebastian Hilton, Lily, Mary Anne Michaels, Sedalia Schoefeld and Edith O'Dwyer dance there away from the formal gathering [MTM B1:33]. A player piano supplies rag music during Beth Keegan's balloon dinner party; a serving man pumps it, and Beth takes over while the after-dinner wine is served. The Black Midnight Rag accompanies Duffey's descent [MTM B2:65-66].","citations":["MTM B1:33","MTM B2:65-66"],"tags":["Music","Duffey","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Ragosa, the","definition":"Earlier heading altered the source’s printed Ragosy to Ragosa. See <em>Ragosy, the</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Argonautica","Ships","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Ragosy, the","definition":"The source's spelling is <em>Ragosy</em>. Gabby Gabrielovitch tells Stein that the Argosy is really the Ragosy, a fleet out of Ragusa in Illyria, and that Ragusa was the original Iolcus [A:127]. This is Gabby's account of the Argonaut tradition.","citations":["A:127"],"tags":["Argonautica","Ships","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Ragusa","definition":"Historical Latin name of Dubrovnik. Gabby Gabrielovitch identifies Ragusa as a city of Illyria, the original Iolcus and the home of the fleet he calls the Ragosy, underlying the name Argosy [A:127]. His Iolcus identification belongs to the novel's account of the Argonaut tradition.","citations":["A:127"],"tags":["Argonautica","Ships","Language & Words","Eastern Europe"]},{"name":"Raleigh, Sir Walter","definition":"English courtier, explorer and colonial promoter, born in the early 1550s and executed under James I in 1618. Favored by Elizabeth I, he sponsored the Roanoke colonial expeditions and published <em>The Discovery of Guiana</em> in 1596. Dotty recalls the thought that whoever knows only land knows only half the world and is only half a person; she cannot remember whether Raleigh wrote it or Finnegan said it [A:280].","citations":["A:280"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Literature","Sailing"]},{"name":"Ramires","definition":"Elena Musquiz's husband, who recognizes Finnegan after seven years when they find him near D'Hannis following his 105-hour ordeal in the chaparral [A:203]. Elena carries Finnegan to their cart; the couple, traveling to Sunday Mass, bring him to help [A:203] [A:206].","citations":["A:203","A:206"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Finnegan","Family"]},{"name":"Randwick","definition":"The novel's spelling of Randwick racecourse in Sydney, now Royal Randwick. Finnegan initially takes Ranwick for a bar. He remarks that the horses run clockwise, or backwards, and Hans offers a comic explanation involving the Coriolis effect [A:3-4]. Finnegan, Vincent, Hans, Marie and the stewardesses Loy and Margaret go there, meet Australian soldiers Tom Shire and Freddy Castle, win bets, drink beer and watch the ponies from the infield. The course is the best the boys have seen, with perhaps twenty thousand spectators [A:2-4].","citations":["A:3-4","A:2-4"],"tags":["Australia","Alcohol","Finnegan","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Rang-Dang-Doo","definition":"Valley in Don Lewis's story of three servicemen, Sergeant Santee, Corporal Casper and PFC Lorrigan, who kill an apparent Japanese soldier they call Yokipoki and become lost. Lewis admits being one of the three but withholds which [D:44]. The surrounding maze includes Cotton-Picker Valley, Pepper-Belly Valley, Hound Dog, Horse Collar, Dog-Dirt Draw, Jackass Flats, Little Joker Canyon, Aching Back, Slit Trench, Sore-Head Gulch, Little Fat-Head and Big Fat-Head [D:45-46].","citations":["D:44","D:45-46"],"tags":["World War II","Soldiers","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Rangoon","definition":"Former English name of Yangon, Myanmar. The poem opening Finnegan's chapter places Rangoon among Roi, Kandi, Kuri and Cai in an imagined region of eternal summer [A:171].","citations":["A:171"],"tags":["Literature","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Ranwick","definition":"Ranwick is the spelling used in the novel for the Sydney racecourse. See <em>Randwick</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Australia","Alcohol","Finnegan","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Ranwick, Edward","definition":"Art-school acquaintance in Duffey's St. Louis circle. Duffey's outings with Mary Louise Byrne and Beth Keegan include one of Charley Murray, Edward or Philip Manford, the music-school student. They sometimes ride in Duffey's carriage and sometimes in Manford's Overland automobile. At the Bavarian Club Edward compares the strong blonde hostesses to brewery horses, a likeness the narrator disputes. Edward already has a reputation at art school, but Duffey dismisses his work as skinny art [MTM B2:58].","citations":["MTM B2:58"],"tags":["Duffey","Visual Arts","Clubs & Societies","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Rasputin, Grigori","definition":"Russian religious figure Grigori Rasputin (1869-1916), named among the broad-headed visionaries in the fictional Carmody Overlark's <em>Studies in the Imagination</em> epigraph. The passage speculates about a back-brain type, supposed Neanderthal traits, criminality, religious pathfinding, prophecy, poetry and invention [D:270-272]. Its remark attributed to Santana concerns mystics' ugly faces generally, rather than making an individual judgment about Rasputin [D:271].","citations":["D:270-272","D:271"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Mysticism","Prophecy & Visions","Eastern Europe"]},{"name":"Rat","definition":"Finnegan recognizes rats by their smell when he wakes bloody in a foul dump; the surrounding language of shipwreck and Odysseus is figurative [A:177]. In Don Lewis's story, large rats make the sounds and movements in Yokipoki's grave that Lewis initially takes for the dead soldier's return [D:47]. The river rats at Demetrio's bar are human patrons described by that slang term [D:58].","citations":["A:177","D:47","D:58"],"tags":["Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","The Dead"]},{"name":"Rat-leaf palm","definition":"Two palms under which X and Finnegan take cover after Finnegan notices the glint of a rifle scope during their graveyard conversation with Doll Delaney [D:291]. They are visiting the grave marked I.N.C.G.P. Bogovitch [D:277].","citations":["D:291","D:277"],"tags":["Finnegan","The Dead","Weapons"]},{"name":"Rattigan, John","definition":"Chicago boarding-school friend of Duffey, Charley Murray and Sebastian Hilton, about two years older than Duffey, with a witty look and a calculating grin. A resourceful trader, he knows wholesalers and clandestinely sells sundries, candy, fruit, tobacco, gadgets and new and used books [MTM B1:22]. Refused a talisman, he copies Duffey's and tricks him into holding the copy; Duffey thinks he understands only half the process. In another school-year identity he is Delbert Dugan [MTM B1:23-24]. He climbs walls like a monkey and sells a hundred books a week, sharing the business with Duffey [MTM B1:25]. Their merchandise sales run three nights weekly after curfew by candle or kerosene light. Rattigan wears a green eyeshade; Duffey pirates his mind and thinking fingers and eyes to learn money's worth [MTM B1:26]. Though himself thirteen, Rattigan admits only students sixteen or older to his whisky bar, charging ten cents for watered whisky and clearing seven. Curiously modest, he undresses in darkness and removes his eyeshade last [MTM B1:27]. He knows girls but not “girls,” in the narrator's distinction [MTM B1:31], and becomes very friendly with Sedalia Schoefeld at the attic dance [MTM B1:33]. Duffey later remembers him among school friends who knew him as a disguised king [MTM B13:464].","citations":["MTM B1:22","MTM B1:23-24","MTM B1:25","MTM B1:26","MTM B1:27","MTM B1:31","MTM B1:33","MTM B13:464"],"tags":["Duffey","Magic & Sorcery","Money & Trade","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Literature","Identity"]},{"name":"Rattigan, Mary Frances (Polly Polyglot)","definition":"Translator who contributes to the <em>Crock</em>'s “Books Abroad” material under the name Polly Polyglot while Duffey runs the magazine during Casey's army service [A:41] [MTM B4:138]. The narrator calls her one of the quasi-creatures of a talisman that only half worked [MTM B3:99]. As a girl of eight or nine, she visits Duffey's Chicago bookstore and is involved, often with Ethyl Ellenberger, in his funny-uncle wrestling on the old black leather sofa. The narration explicitly questions its harmlessness when he is alone with Mary Frances [MTM B4:111]; compromising photographs of such encounters appear in the later portfolio [MTM B4:139]. She is subsequently listed as Mary Frances Rattigan (Gompers) among the gathering at Pelican Press [MTM B12:383].","citations":["A:41","MTM B4:138","MTM B3:99","MTM B4:111","MTM B4:139","MTM B12:383"],"tags":["Literature","Language & Words","Duffey","Women Characters","Magic & Sorcery","Creation","Identity"]},{"name":"Rattle-snakes","definition":"During his 105 hours in the chaparral Finnegan may have visited the rattlesnakes' ancestral city; the narration leaves the experience uncertain and allows that he may have seen more than was there [A:203-205]. The following buffalo episode, rather than the rattlesnake city, is located in Frio County [A:205]. The sidewinder, <em>Crotalus cerastes</em>, also supplies a possible explanation learned by Niccolo Crotolus's employers for his nickname Nick the Rattle; he offers a family-name and castanet explanation himself [A:279].","citations":["A:203-205","A:205","A:279"],"tags":["Finnegan","Dreams","Identity"]},{"name":"Ravel, Alexi","definition":"Tenant in Zabotski's apartment building near Duffey's own place. After Duffey returns from the Fifth Contingency to an ordinary Monday he has already lived, Ravel confronts him about a dream in which Duffey abandoned him to die [MTM B12:354-356]. Duffey recognizes the neglected, unsplendid bit of trash as Ravel in mortal agony and feels ashamed. Ravel rejects the excuse that it was only a dream, spits in disgust and goes back inside [MTM B12:356].","citations":["MTM B12:354-356","MTM B12:356"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Dreams","Death & Mortality","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Ravenna","definition":"City in northeastern Italy. In the poem opening Finnegan's chapter, the speaker reports being told Ravenna is sweet in spring [A:171-172].","citations":["A:171-172"],"tags":["Italy","Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Raviola Joe aka Giuseppe Gabrielangelo","definition":"Giuseppe Gabrielangelo, nicknamed Raviola Joe, one of the Company of Fifty associated with Galveston's Old Wooden Ship [D:216]. With Saddle-Sore Sandy and Bay-Rum Johnny he is described as unkempt and uncouth beside the establishment's otherwise high standards [D:224].","citations":["D:216","D:224"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Identity"]},{"name":"Razzle Daz","definition":"Duffey's comic strip in the <em>Crock</em>, whose characters include Finnegan, Hans and Show Boat. Marie praises inclusion in it as the summit of splendor but disputes Duffey's account of creating their souls: she says he collects them from old junk stories and provides a new setting and sparkle, while he insists that he made them. Dotty threatens to change the danger awaiting her [MTM B5:159]. Finnegan is John Solli, Hans is John Schultz and Show Boat is Teresa Piccone [MTM B5:161].","citations":["MTM B5:159","MTM B5:161"],"tags":["Literature","Visual Arts","Duffey","Finnegan","Creation","Identity"]},{"name":"Reading","definition":"Pennsylvania city on the Pittsburgh bus route from Newark, via Allentown. Finnegan sends Doll Delancy to buy tickets for both Pittsburgh and Baltimore [D:169-170]. She explains that the Pittsburgh bus goes through Reading rather than Philadelphia; they ultimately take the Baltimore bus and get off in Philadelphia [D:170-171].","citations":["D:169-170","D:170-171"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Real Presence","definition":"Doctrine Margaret Stone proclaims on Dauphin Street at midnight, defending the urgency of preaching to people whose only home is the nighttime street [A:144]. She later reports that men claiming to be assassins of the Jebel Shammar sect threaten her unless she stops preaching it at night, when they claim their jealous Djinn is the only Real Presence. Margaret doubts the sect exists: the three men attend Tulane and may be joking [MTM B7:225].","citations":["A:144","MTM B7:225"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Theology","Sacraments","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Rebeka Stein","definition":"Absalom Stein's daughter, sent to Ursuline Academy at her mother's insistence for a narrower education after her earlier education had become too broad [MTM B9:264-266]. She joins Crissie Cristofero, Therese Doucet and Cleo Mahoney at the Ghost Coffee Shop to discuss <em>Seven Roads</em> with Duffey [MTM B10:293]. Fifteen years old, she has known him all her life. She interprets the play's potholes as appropriately ridiculous, incongruous dream-transformation activators dredged from Cleo's unconscious and warns Duffey where he steps [MTM B10:294]. She calls Crissie a sibyl or pythoness for producing prophetic couplets without understanding them [MTM B10:295]. Against the complaint that the ongoing quest is a broken record, Rebeka insists its repeated excellence cannot be excessive [MTM B10:296]. After the seventh contingency she wishes Crissie understood her own verses, which would make their work easier [MTM B10:297].","citations":["MTM B9:264-266","MTM B10:293","MTM B10:294","MTM B10:295","MTM B10:296","MTM B10:297"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family","Theater","Dreams","Prophecy & Visions","Duffey"]},{"name":"Red Crow","definition":"Erroneous heading for <em>Old Crow</em>. The bottle with an inch remaining when Finnegan wakes in the dump and the whisky in his Texas shoulder-bag are both explicitly called Old Crow [A:177] [A:202]. See <em>Old Crow</em>.","citations":["A:177","A:202"],"tags":["Alcohol","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Red Decade","definition":"The narrator's name for the 1930s, when an aggressive ideological element is said to be especially strong in Chicago while extending across cities and persons. It is one deadly head of the old monster. This follows the account of Shirley Israel's salon circle trying to use Duffey and of the trashy campaign that savages him; the narrator also places most university and newspaper people under the red influence [MTM B4:137]. A later passage describes the mass emergence of deformed counterparts to the bright arts after the Devil's release [MTM B6:207].","citations":["MTM B4:137","MTM B6:207"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle","The Devil","Duffey"]},{"name":"Red Dog Motor Freight Line","definition":"Schaeffer-family trucking business for which Henry Salvatore drives after leaving the sea; through that work he meets Mary Virginia Schaeffer [A:124]. Its routes run from Galveston and Houston to Morgan City and New Orleans and also cover Baton Rouge, Shreveport, Port Arthur, Orange and Beaumont. Mary Virginia transfers to its New Orleans terminal and works both for the company and with Dotty on the <em>Bark</em> and other projects [MTM B6:198].","citations":["A:124","MTM B6:198"],"tags":["Occupations","Women Characters","Journalism","Louisiana"]},{"name":"Red Fisters","definition":"Sardinians who, Margaret Stone says, threaten to wear her guts as scarves. Yet she meets them over dawn coffee at Messina's or Anthony Ghost's, tells them a shaggy-duck joke and hears that they let her live to tell the next one despite calling her a Whore of Rome. She finds the silent Slant-Faced Men much more frightening [MTM B7:225].","citations":["MTM B7:225"],"tags":["Catholic Church","New Orleans","Crime"]},{"name":"Red Fox","definition":"One of the ships on Angela Cosquin's father's master map of fifty or more real or imaginary treasure wrecks. Prospective buyers could select a ship and even alter its location before commissioning a forged treasure map and supporting papers [D:260-261].","citations":["D:260-261"],"tags":["Ships","Shipwrecks","Crime"]},{"name":"Red Ridge","definition":"Words on axe heads in Duffey's childhood catalogue of iron inscriptions, alongside Hubbard's and Jamestown. He appropriates many such household details from other people's minds and loves the stolen words [MTM B1:17].","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","Magic & Sorcery","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Red River","definition":"River north of the Cajun country in the narrator's deliberately extravagant account: people south of it were already speaking Cajun, driving buggies, raising rice, attending Mass, trapping muskrats and drinking poor wine and beer before Rome was built [A:124].","citations":["A:124"],"tags":["Louisiana","Catholic Church","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Red Rooster","definition":"Erroneous heading for <em>Black Rooster</em>, one of Don Barnaby's named varieties of joy pills, alongside Haiti hoodoo, High Harry, Deep Romany and Dead Frenchman [D:248]. See <em>Black Rooster</em>.","citations":["D:248"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Red Sea","definition":"Sea in Anastasia Demetriades's account of mermaid history. Mermaids whose ancestral home was the Cyclades had reached the Red Sea by Alexander's time, by an unknown route; later, after their medieval peak, they disappeared from both the Red Sea and the Persian Gulf [D:34-36].","citations":["D:34-36"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Nymphs & Mermaids","Folklore"]},{"name":"Red Sky in the Morning","definition":"Poem in the collection of Kasmir Szymansky's work, following Demetrio Glauch's introductory assessment. Its speaker rejects rescuing the “nobly lost,” fears the blade and severed head, and accuses God of failing to recognize certain shades of red [PG:519-520]. The preceding discussion of Casey's faults, failures and elective affinities is Glauch's introduction, not an essay by Casey [PG:518-519].","citations":["PG:519-520","PG:518-519"],"tags":["Literature","Theology","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Red-headed women","definition":"Loy says camel drivers in one port rejected her and Margaret because they considered red-headed women witches; Vincent agrees with the label [A:2]. Loy Larkin and Margaret Murphy are red-headed Sydney companions [A:2] [A:5-6], as are Marie [A:24] and the later picnic companions Virginia and Dorothy [A:29]. In Hans's meditation on Marie's appearance, the narration also describes the Virgin in early Flemish Annunciations as red-headed and green-eyed [A:24].","citations":["A:2","A:5-6","A:24","A:29"],"tags":["Women Characters","Australia","Folklore","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Redfox, Margery","definition":"Member of Duffey's Chicago golden-age circle, listed among its slippery people as Marjory Redfox [MTM B3:100]. Under the spelling Margery, she joins the salon supper but, unlike the other named women, does not hiss at Demetrio when he leaves [MTM B4:127]. She attends Morris Poor's challenge to Duffey's powers and asks where the nearest mule can be found for a demonstration [MTM B4:128-129].","citations":["MTM B3:100","MTM B4:127","MTM B4:128-129"],"tags":["Women Characters","Duffey","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Redheart, Elroy","definition":"Artist of a four-chambered red-heart autobiographical construction in porcelain or ceramic. Duffey has displayed it several times; Hennessy has it when Bagby sees it. Redheart sells some miniature paintings and statuettes out of it when hungry and makes replacements, so it changes somewhat [MTM B7:221]. Its scenes move from rural youth and a dead Jill of Hearts to opulence and war, with a mutilated, bone-thin Jack of Hearts [MTM B7:221-222]. The later Jack and a beautiful, adventurous woman inhabit a more feverish world. Hot artificial colors signify artificial sins, while cheap Hong Kong plastic figures and prismatic reflections deliberately represent cheapness without careless workmanship [MTM B7:223]. The woman finally pins her Queen of Hearts card to her extracted heart; the fourth chamber is vacant and offers an optional noose. Bagby pays Hennessy one thousand dollars and has the work sent directly to St. Louis [MTM B7:224].","citations":["MTM B7:221","MTM B7:221-222","MTM B7:223","MTM B7:224"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Sin & Redemption","Money & Trade","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Redwine, Katherine","definition":"Named with Martin Redwine among the ecclesial or vineyard people of Duffey's Chicago golden age, the group the narrator credits with keeping the world turning [MTM B3:98-99].","citations":["MTM B3:98-99"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Duffey"]},{"name":"Redwine, Martin","definition":"Named with Katherine Redwine among the ecclesial or vineyard people of Duffey's Chicago golden age, the group the narrator credits with keeping the world turning [MTM B3:98-99].","citations":["MTM B3:98-99"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Duffey"]},{"name":"Regal brewery","definition":"Brewery that sends Duffey a keg of beer every week. Added to his continually simmering charitable soup, it changes the flavor for a while [A:133-134].","citations":["A:133-134"],"tags":["Alcohol","Food & Dining","Duffey"]},{"name":"Regnum Dei auferetur","definition":"“The kingdom of God will be taken away,” the opening line of Henry Salvatore's seminary assignment in Latin prosody. The poem continues over the next page and ends <em>Deus tam dilexit mundum</em>; his instructor marks it down with a joke that Latin cannot have italics because it is not Italian enough [A:148-149].","citations":["A:148-149"],"tags":["Language & Words","Literature","Religious Orders"]},{"name":"Reid, Jenny","definition":"Tennis player listed with Peter Reid among the sporty people in Duffey's Chicago golden-age circle [MTM B3:98-99]. She is also among the adult women whom the narrator says Duffey loved [MTM B4:111].","citations":["MTM B3:98-99","MTM B4:111"],"tags":["Women Characters","Duffey"]},{"name":"Reid, Peter","definition":"Tennis player listed with Jenny Reid among the sporty people in Duffey's Chicago golden-age circle [MTM B3:98-99].","citations":["MTM B3:98-99"],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Reilly, Lazarus","definition":"Proprietor of Galveston's Old Wooden Ship, whose Company of Fifty regulars live at the establishment [MTM B13:458]. Having taken the lesson about entertaining angels unaware to heart, he refuses no newcomer and gives rooms, beds, beer credit and small loans, hoping not to slight a disguised angel. Enough guests eventually pay some of the time to sustain the others. The narrator reveals that barmaid Soft-Talk Susie Kutz really is an angel unaware, whose favorably shaded reports help the Ship prosper [D:215-216].","citations":["MTM B13:458","D:215-216"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Angels & Demons","Money & Trade","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Remember It","definition":"Memory-jog capsule implanted in Newton Prescott's brain and tuned to preserve the whole idea of the Casey Machine [CM:503] [CM:512]. He anticipates people stepping out of the revelatory Moment and forgetting it. Even under specific, directed amnesia, he expects the implant to bring him nearer remembering than anyone else, rather than guarantee perfect recall [CM:513].","citations":["CM:503","CM:512","CM:513"],"tags":["History & Memory","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Renard Volante","definition":"“Flying Fox,” one of the French venturer ships on Angela Cosquin's father's master map of fifty or more real or imaginary treasure wrecks. Buyers choose wrecks and locations from the master map to commission detailed fraudulent treasure maps [D:260-261].","citations":["D:260-261"],"tags":["Ships","Shipwrecks","France","Crime"]},{"name":"Repertoire theatre","definition":"One of the popular arts the narrator says were trashed after the Devil's release, in a broad loss of class and style [MTM B6:207-208].","citations":["MTM B6:207-208"],"tags":["Theater","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Requiem","definition":"Prayer for eternal rest and perpetual light for the dead. Don Lewis abbreviates it to “Requiem” and “lux perpetua and all that” after removing Yokipoki's boots, gagging him, binding his wrists and ankles, bandaging his eyes and burying him in the valley mud. He then drinks to the dead man and calls it Christian burial [D:46].","citations":["D:46"],"tags":["Prayer & Devotion","Death & Mortality","Liturgy","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Resurrection","definition":"Return from death, literal or figurative in several distinct passages. Finnegan calls Anastasia the resurrection after she washes him, treats his wounds and restores his respectability with an electric shaver [D:16]; he later teases her as Little Miss Resurrection [D:33]. Mr. X, not Saxon X. Seaworthy, tells Finnegan that deaths are easier to explain than resurrections while they discuss the buried Papadiabolous and his apparent return; their conversation identifies the substitute as the Irish policeman Noonan or Flaherty [D:153-154]. Patrick Stranahan invokes the Sadducees' disbelief against their supposed modern counterparts [A:110]. Trinali's wine poem calls the making of wine the birth of a god, Resurrection and Life [A:231]. Enemies gather to prevent Count Finnegan's emergence from his sea cave after word of his Resurrection reaches them [MTM B12:374]. John Mogul places on Cardinal Gabrailovitch's monument the words about becoming a witness of His resurrection; the same epitaph is repeated in the list of murdered cardinals [MB:578] [MB:589].","citations":["D:16","D:33","D:153-154","A:110","A:231","MTM B12:374","MB:578","MB:589"],"tags":["Resurrection","Finnegan","Theology","The Dead","Identity","Literature"]},{"name":"Resurrection of Count Finnegan, The","definition":"Twelve-by-eight-foot, sea-sounding painting by John Solli (Finnegan), received at Duffey's New Orleans art bijou in the mid-1950s and initially taken for a magnificent joke [MB:550]. A Latin scroll identifies the half-risen figure in the ocean-cave crypt as Papal Count Finnegan. He stands partly in dark-green water and partly in bright-green air, replacing a long strip of fallen flesh and preparing to mend other decay. He looks thirty years older than Finnegan at his reported death, which this account places two or three years before the painting's arrival. His banana nose has become noble, his artist's hands more intricate, his eyes combine humor and torment, and his strength, speed and apparently moving mouth remain recognizable. His flesh shows simultaneous transfiguration and corruption, a sea change of the uncoffined dead; a possible winding sheet hangs in tatters and old traveling clothes await him [MB:551]. The cave-roof fissure also divides the composition from the Annunciation of Joseph Cardinal Hedayat of Antioch in a Syrian or Lebanese scene. The aged Hedayat and Finnegan are identical in appearance despite their younger selves' differences [MB:552]. Duffey sells it to Hilary Hilton for twenty thousand dollars, but Hilton leaves it in the bijou for its possible role in the foretold events [MB:553]. It hangs there at least thirty years [MB:554]. Duffey later uses it as a clue with Carmelo Mendoza; a description attributed to the fictional <em>How Many Miles to Babylon</em> is quoted in the account [MTM B12:367-369].","citations":["MB:550","MB:551","MB:552","MB:553","MB:554","MTM B12:367-369"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Finnegan","Resurrection","Identity","Time & Eternity","Duffey"]},{"name":"Retiarius","definition":"Roman gladiator or net-man armed with a net and trident. The image describes Saxon X. Seaworthy's trapping tactics: Papa Devil fears his <em>Hawk</em> will be caught by a waiting retiarius [D:123], and the old net-man waits aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em> with Gerecke and Wirt during the Naxos raid [D:136]. Later Finnegan's own trap fails while Seaworthy, explicitly named as the old net-man, has already cast a net around him [D:230].","citations":["D:123","D:136","D:230"],"tags":["Combat","Ships","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Return of the Neanderthal, The","definition":"Fictional book by Royce Rollins, subtitled <em>The Alien Within</em>, presented through Charles O. A. Harrington's hostile review in <em>Science for Today</em> [D:240-242]. Its thesis is that defeated Neanderthals survive both as isolated elites and within human bloodlines, periodically producing primordials bent on destroying humanity. It attributes apparitions, doubles, second sight and related powers to them [D:241]. Rollins nevertheless appeals to returnees of good will to resist their destructive fellows and allows in an appendix that they might be Grimaldi or another ancient people rather than Neanderthals [D:242].","citations":["D:240-242","D:241","D:242"],"tags":["Literature","Monsters & Creatures","Identity","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Revision and/or correction of history","definition":"The Royal Pop Historians' project in Duffey's Fifth Contingency [MTM B12:354]: alter people, places and remembered history while presenting the imposed result as what always existed. Margaret describes vanished buildings replaced by apparently ancient parks whose former sites people can no longer recall [MTM B11:307-310]. The lecturers claim that all history is present now and available for reconstruction and revision [MTM B11:324-325]. Hugh de Turenne announces the Black Sea's destruction to expunge Himself's imprisonment and weaken Colchis, the <em>Argo</em> and the Fleece; he and Cyrus Roundhead deny that the sea, Athens, Rome or Jerusalem ever existed. Their historical sessions alternate with violent hunts for human remnants [MTM B11:336-337].","citations":["MTM B12:354","MTM B11:307-310","MTM B11:324-325","MTM B11:336-337"],"tags":["History & Memory","Cosmic Struggle","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Rhine","definition":"River where Finnegan's verse imagines a hut on a rock, among several prospective homes. Dotty finds the stanza among his worn papers; the narration describes it as marked through but later questions whether the marks really indicate cancellation [A:173].","citations":["A:173"],"tags":["Literature","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Rhineland Reverie","definition":"One of the nine proposed volumes under which Endymeon Ellenbogen classifies his uncollected, unpublished poetry, which he says he nevertheless recites without invitation [A:237].","citations":["A:237"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Rhinelander","definition":"Wisconsin town used to locate Hans Schultz's childhood home: St. Gallen lies in the northern lake country beyond Rhinelander and not far from Eagle River [A:207].","citations":["A:207"],"tags":["Family"]},{"name":"Rhodes","definition":"Island Finnegan names among several possible origins of the <em>Hawk</em> while he and Anastasia keep their Naxos vigil; she declines to specify where the boat will come from [D:131]. In Anastasia's mermaid history, Rhodes has had no authentic sighting for more than three hundred years, part of a retreat toward the Cyclades [D:35-36].","citations":["D:131","D:35-36"],"tags":["Islands","Greece","Nymphs & Mermaids","Ships"]},{"name":"Rice ribby","definition":"Dish Aunt Gertrude Brannagan prepares during Finnegan's stay in Biloxi. The narrator celebrates her extravagant cooking by saying the whole world gapes in amazement when she makes rice ribby [D:201] [D:208].","citations":["D:201","D:208"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Women Characters","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Richard of England (Richard I 'Lionheart,'1157-1199)","definition":"Richard I, the Lionheart (1157-1199), king of England from 1189. In the <em>Argo</em>'s legendary history, told by the talking oak in its wheel, King Richard recaptures the ship from the Saracens [MTM B13:404].","citations":["MTM B13:404"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Argonautica","Ships","Naval Warfare"]},{"name":"Rico, Ron","definition":"Earlier heading inverted a rum brand as though it were a person’s name. See <em>Ron Rico</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Alcohol","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Rifle shooting","definition":"One of the minor arts the narrator says were trashed, losing class and style, during the cultural decline after the Devil's release [MTM B6:207-208].","citations":["MTM B6:207-208"],"tags":["Weapons","Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Riflemen (Two)","definition":"Two unnamed riflemen in a boat initially three hundred yards off the rocky shore near Count Finnegan's sea cave [MB:555]. After the shore assassin drops a depth charge and falls into the water, the boat closes to under fifty yards. The riflemen riddle a man thrown upward by the water, then another man or the same man again. The narration leaves their victims unidentified and allows that Finnegan escapes beneath the shark and boat, leaving two enemies dead [MB:556-557]. The action is on Cuba's Mariano Coast near Havana [MB:557].","citations":["MB:555","MB:556-557","MB:557"],"tags":["Weapons","Crime","Finnegan","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Rink skating","definition":"One of the minor arts the narrator says were trashed, losing class and style, during the cultural decline after the Devil's release [MTM B6:207-208].","citations":["MTM B6:207-208"],"tags":["Cosmic Struggle"]},{"name":"Riorden, Rosemary","definition":"Red-haired girl the unnamed Sergeant meets with Moira Monroney and Minnie McGinty at Sydney's Plaza in his hospital daydream [A:88-90]. He sits on her lap. She identifies him as Aeson's son, despite his belief that his father was Giulio; names Orpheus, Peleus, Euphemus and Meleager as his companions; and foretells the Dioscuri, dragon's teeth, Aeetes' daughter in many forms, an unrecognized finding of the fleece and no bodily return to Iolcus. She says she is one form of Aeetes' daughter [A:89]. At Oswald Lane she carries him on her shoulders to save him from sticking to the pavement in a fog, linking her name to the protective herb [A:89]. Later Stein remembers her Illyrian claim; X insists he was not that Sergeant, while Stein says he was so to some extent [A:127].","citations":["A:88-90","A:89","A:127"],"tags":["Women Characters","Dreams","Argonautica","Australia","Identity","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Rita","definition":"Sister of Henry Francis Salvatore, with Angela and Lucy. Their parents are Lawrence Salvatore and Mary Genevieve Hebert, in the family account centered on Henry's Morgan City, Louisiana, birthplace [A:122].","citations":["A:122"],"tags":["Family","Women Characters","Louisiana"]},{"name":"Rittenhouse, Sister Mary Aurora","definition":"Sister Mary Aurora (Rittenhouse), listed among the ecclesial or vineyard people in Duffey's Chicago golden age. The narrator credits those workers with keeping the world turning and calls everyone else parasites upon their labor [MTM B3:98-99].","citations":["MTM B3:98-99"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Catholic Church","Duffey"]},{"name":"Ritz","definition":"Bar in Finnegan's travel poem: its speaker will pass St. Vince in a sloop, shine at the Ritz, drink like a sailor, dine like a prince and sleep on the sand at St. Kitts [A:172].","citations":["A:172"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Literature","Finnegan","Sailing"]},{"name":"River Itself","definition":"The Mississippi at St. Louis, where Duffey emerges from the Sea of Lost Years below the Eads Bridge as a happy twenty-three-year-old [MTM B13:465]. In the return dated 1923, he again swims ashore below that bridge from the Sea of the Seven Lost Years and encounters a young Stranahan on a small fishing pier [MTM B13:470].","citations":["MTM B13:465","MTM B13:470"],"tags":["Duffey","Time & Eternity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Riviera","definition":"One of the places Finnegan says he could be while studying to become a playboy, along with Basse Terre, St. Kitts and Tahiti. He invokes those lost pleasures as his personal sacrifice in coming to persuade Hilary Hilton to take Mary Jean back and dislodge Casey, allowing another attempt to straighten Casey out [A:252].","citations":["A:252"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Road Songs","definition":"Title under which verses are attributed to Finnegan in <em>More Than Melchisedech</em>. A stanza about Clio's erroneous history opens Book Eleven [MTM B11:306]; further verses accompany that book's destruction and suffering [MTM B11:331] [MTM B11:341-342]. A stanza assigning distinctive qualities to New Orleans, Havana, Chicago and St. Louis is credited to Count Finnegan's <em>Road Songs</em> in Book Twelve [MTM B12:384].","citations":["MTM B11:306","MTM B11:331","MTM B11:341-342","MTM B12:384"],"tags":["Literature","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Robber Cassino","definition":"Card game Falo, Nauti, Finnegan and Vincent all know and play together. Their worn deck lacks the four of clubs and six of diamonds, replaced by written slips, with English added to the four of clubs. Falo always wins because she has known the individual cards since childhood and hopes the same advantage will work in poker [A:65].","citations":["A:65"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Robinson, Edwin A.","definition":"Edwin Arlington Robinson (1869-1935), American poet whose “Two Men” supplies Book Five's epigraph: its speaker cannot escape thinking about Melchisedech and Ukalegon. The novel follows the quotation with Skalsky's assertion that Ukalegon was a woman and the negation of Melchisedech. Robinson's original poem spells the names “Melchizedek” and “Ucalegon.” [MTM B5:149]","citations":["MTM B5:149"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Duffey"]},{"name":"Roc","definition":"Giant bird in the Sindbad panels Finnegan paints for the <em>Brunhilde</em>'s barroom. He cannot reconcile its extraordinary size with plausible flight. Anastasia advises him to make Sindbad very small; Finnegan instead wants the bird to express “Evil with grace and balance,” taking Papa Devil's disturbing perfection as his model. [D:59-60]","citations":["D:59-60"],"tags":["Folklore","Ships","Finnegan","The Devil","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Rock Island Pump Company","definition":"Name in iron on pump handles, among the household words the young Melchisedech Duffey appropriates through observation, memory, and theft from other minds. [MTM B1:17]","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Rocking the Crock","definition":"Expression linking two disturbances: Sergeant Bushmaster accuses his opponent of twice knocking the crock with an elbow during the beer contest; in a letter to Casey, Duffey says enemies are “rocking the Crock” by trying to corrupt the magazine, threatening and beating him when he refuses. [A:20] [A:39-40]","citations":["A:20","A:39-40"],"tags":["Alcohol","Soldiers","Duffey","Journalism","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Rogers, Tom (Big Bear)","definition":"Sports writer whose article “The Floating Screen - The Next Important Development in Football” appears in Casey Szymansky's <em>Crock</em>. [A:41] Tom “Big Bear” Rogers also belongs to the sporty set of Duffey's Chicago golden years. [MTM B3:99]","citations":["A:41","MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Journalism","Literary Figures","Duffey"]},{"name":"Roland's Horn","definition":"Roland's legendary olifant or horn, sounded in <em>The Song of Roland</em>; among the notable objects recovered by the <em>Argo</em> Masters. [MTM B13:422]","citations":["MTM B13:422"],"tags":["Folklore","Literature","Argonautica"]},{"name":"Rolfe, Ralph","definition":"Young Englishman encountered by Duffey, Brannagan, and Casey Gorshok at Karl Marx Stadt. A new Perverse Logic comes to him in sleep and, as he puts it, employs him; he writes it on tiny scraps left after mice eat his paper, before the system leaves his mind. He expects it to overturn the world. [MTM B13:427-428] The Masters seize the scraps and withdraw from that time and place as Rolfe fires at Gorshok's temple. Gorshok destroys the papers only under repeated pressure; one last piece flutters overboard, and Duffey fears Gorshok's devil-fish may retrieve it. The mission's completeness remains in doubt as Duffey's memory of it fades. [MTM B13:429-430]","citations":["MTM B13:427-428","MTM B13:429-430"],"tags":["Argonautica","Duffey","Dreams","Magic & Sorcery","Weapons"]},{"name":"Rollins, Royce","definition":"Author of <em>The Return of the Neanderthal: The Alien Within</em>, a book reviewed within <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> by Charles O. A. Harrington in <em>Science for Today</em>. Harrington calls Rollins an unacademic crank and attacks his thesis as an insane creed; these judgments belong to the hostile reviewer. [D:240-242]","citations":["D:240-242"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"Romaic","definition":"Modern Greek, the language spoken by Anastasia's grandmother on Naxos. Papapaleologus the donkey understands her perfectly, Anastasia almost entirely, and Finnegan only about half. [D:126] Finnegan also finds his Romaic inadequate for confession to the young Capuchin priest. [D:118]","citations":["D:126","D:118"],"tags":["Language & Words","Greece","Islands","Finnegan","Sacraments"]},{"name":"Roman","definition":"A shifting cultural and religious descriptor. Demetrio's lined face looks simultaneously like an old Roman's and an old Aztec's. [D:58] Anastasia is not Roman Catholic, although she carries the Roman rosary Finnegan gave her. [D:147] In his discussion of names, Monica, and Augustine, Patrick Stranahan identifies himself as the Roman Patrick rather than the Irish one. [A:111]","citations":["D:58","D:147","A:111"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Prayer & Devotion","Italy","Identity"]},{"name":"Roman Way of the Cross","definition":"The Way of the Cross as invoked in Marie Courtois's mystical talk with Finnegan. Its thirteenth station depicts Christ taken down and received by his mother. Marie associates its Pietà form with an old station on Rome's Aventine Hill and an earlier grouping of Demeter and Pontos; she links the <em>gremium matris</em>, the mother's lap, with Earth and her own lap. These pagan connections form part of Marie's earthy, seductive exposition. [D:92]","citations":["D:92"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Prayer & Devotion","New Testament","Mysticism","Italy","Finnegan","Greek Mythology"]},{"name":"Romanies","definition":"The Romani people, called Gypsies in Casey's school notebook. In entry XXII Casey says that they claim to have founded Rome and that their name derives from it. He also says they use one word for yesterday and tomorrow, one for beginning and ending, and <em>Meropen</em> for both life and death; he connects English “pal,” “chiv,” and “stir” with their speech, giving <em>stiropen</em> for prison. These are claims within Casey's notebook. [A:162-164]","citations":["A:162-164"],"tags":["Language & Words","Folklore","Italy","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Rome","definition":"City Manuel intends to visit after Naxos, to warn the Pope that evil men are abroad. [D:151] Patrick Stranahan lists Rome with Dublin, London, and Paris among the destinations a Christian man ought to reach on a journey for his soul's salvation. [A:110] In Duffey's scrapbook, the unnamed president of the Popular Revolutionary Priests' Senate says that even the Pope of Rome could enter his door, provided he crawled. [MTM B8:252]","citations":["D:151","A:110","MTM B8:252"],"tags":["Italy","Catholic Church","Popes","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Ron Rico","definition":"Ron Rico, the novel's spelling of the rum brand Ronrico. Finnegan carries a bottle in his heavy shoulder-bag, along with Southern Comfort, Old Crow, Hiram Walker, Don Q and other drinks, when he leaves D'Hannis on foot for the chaparral [A:202-203].","citations":["A:202-203"],"tags":["Alcohol","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Roof observatory","definition":"Observatory on the roof of Chicago's Colony Club, where Sebastian Hilton joins Duffey and Letitia after the Harvesters' Dinner and shares their sense of oneness. [MTM B3:94]","citations":["MTM B3:94"],"tags":["Duffey","Clubs & Societies","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Room of the Conclave, The","definition":"Subterranean chamber below sea level in the mooring rock north of San Simeon, reached from its hewn well-shaft and remembered by Count Finnegan as large enough for fifty people. [MB:577] Its unexplained name dates from its carving four hundred years earlier. Here the thirteen apparent doubles of the Princes assemble; Giwa insists that at least one is actually a surviving Cardinal. Monsignor X serves the conclave, and the golden-masked Melchisedech speaks. Count Finnegan abstains while the other twelve vote for him; X crowns him Pope Finnegan the First as the land rises from the sea. [MB:596-597]","citations":["MB:577","MB:596-597"],"tags":["Popes","Ecclesiastical","Finnegan","Ekklesia","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Roosevelt, Franklin D. (1882--1945)","definition":"Franklin Delano Roosevelt (1882-1945), thirty-second U.S. president, in office from 1933 to 1945. His New Deal enlarged federal economic and social activity during the Depression; he later led the United States in World War II and helped organize the Allied coalition and plans for the United Nations. In <em>More Than Melchisedech</em>, the journal <em>The Answer</em> mixes material from the old and new Roosevelts with other thinkers, then disparages Roosevelt and Hoover while proclaiming its own Leader. [MTM B4:114-115]","citations":["MTM B4:114-115"],"tags":["Historical Figures","World War II","Journalism"]},{"name":"Rosa Ivanova's Kofeinik","definition":"Earlier misspelling of Rose as Rosa. See <em>Kofeinik (Rose Ivanova's)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","Dreams","Fictional Places"]},{"name":"Rosin","definition":"Material Mr. X carries in his pockets and uses in a handkerchief to disguise his telephone voice. [A:135] [A:142] Dotty Yekouris recognizes its smell on the supposed newspaper report of his death, identifies the clipping as a product of the Pelican Press, and exposes his hoax. [A:142]","citations":["A:135","A:142"],"tags":["Identity","Journalism","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Rounders' Club","definition":"St. Louis restaurant, bar, and residential club founded by Duffey, with Charley Murray becoming half-owner. Its attractions include hired carriages and automobiles, an Elegant Riverboat Deck, hired tuxedos, the Rounders' String Band, automatic membership in the Famous Steeplechase Club, a House of Ten Thousand Duty-Free Bargains, and games including racquets, whist, poker, and horseshoes. [MTM B2:49] [MTM B2:53] Dining spaces include the Golden Buffet and Bread and Wine Room. [MTM B2:58] After the seven hidden years Duffey returns to St. Louis, but declines to resume active management; Murray has prospered at the club, where Olga Sanchez still works. [MTM B2:73-74] Patrick Stranahan is a former patron. [MTM B5:154] In 1946 Duffey, the Bagbys, and the Erlenbaums gather there with Hans Schultz, Marie Monaghan, Henri Salvatore, Dotty Yekouris, Mary Virginia Schaeffer, and Absalom Stein, recognized as Duffey's creations; Murray remains acting manager. [MTM B5:149] [MTM B5:156-158] [MTM B5:162] Late in Duffey's life he deeds his half-interest to Teresa Showboat Piccone Stranahan; Murray has left his half to Vincent Stranahan, and the couple run the club. [MTM B12:385]","citations":["MTM B2:49","MTM B2:53","MTM B2:58","MTM B2:73-74","MTM B5:154","MTM B5:149","MTM B5:156-158","MTM B5:162","MTM B12:385"],"tags":["Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Food & Dining","Music","Money & Trade","Clubs & Societies","Creation"]},{"name":"Rounders' String Band","definition":"Musical group at Duffey's Rounders' Club in St. Louis, advertised for every evening except Tuesday. [MTM B2:49] Duffey takes over its banjo after demonstrating unexpected skill with an instrument he has never held; drinking men from the loafers' bench substitute when he is busy. The band earns an award as the city's third best string band. Its repertoire includes “Rock Island Rag,” “Cincinnati Zoo Rag,” “Missouri Valley Shuffle,” “Gadarene Swine Song” (adapted from Bagby's tune), “Whistle Stop Jump,” “Morgan County Fair Strut,” “The King Shall Ride,” “Show Boat Shuffle,” “Honeysuckle Hop,” “River Road,” “Gloria! Gloria!,” “Sawdust Trail Drag,” and “Startime Trolley Car.” [MTM B2:51-52]","citations":["MTM B2:49","MTM B2:51-52"],"tags":["Duffey","Music","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Roundhead, Cyrus","definition":"Royal Pop Historian in New Orleans, introduced as a forthcoming speaker and later wearing a monocle with additional lenses. After Hugh de Turenne announces the Black Sea's destruction, Roundhead offers pseudo-explanations that the sea and the cities Rome, Athens, and Jerusalem never existed. [MTM B11:327] [MTM B11:336-337] He directs hunts against supposed unreal people and the deflation of their buildings, insisting that the important must eliminate the unimportant. [MTM B11:338] At Girardeau's dinner he describes the selective recording and destruction of humanity, distinguishes the splendid people's minds and persons from their temporary bodies, and denounces human guilt, introspection, and lack of kinetic intuition. [MTM B11:344-346] At the Decatur Street Opera House he participates in the elimination of human remnants; afterward, from the loge, he envies the depth, intensity, and legacy his own species lacks. [MTM B11:349-351]","citations":["MTM B11:327","MTM B11:336-337","MTM B11:338","MTM B11:344-346","MTM B11:349-351"],"tags":["New Orleans","Clubs & Societies","History & Memory","Metaphysics","Theater","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Roving Magistrate Number Sixteen","definition":"Signature Finnegan uses on his note to George Brubaker after taking Brubaker's unlocked car in New York under the supposed “Lien of Extreme Need.” He leaves the car in Newark, warns its owner about leaving it unlocked, and directs him to Room 101 of the Epimetheos Building. [D:167-169]","citations":["D:167-169"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Crime"]},{"name":"Royal Malarky of Salem","definition":"Comic Crown and Image on the figurative counterfeit coins Lily Koch finds in Duffey and Letitia, whom she otherwise calls treasures made of pure gold. Her metaphor challenges their shared belief in subjective creation while affirming her love for them. [MTM B3:96]","citations":["MTM B3:96"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words","Creation","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Royal Pop History","definition":"New Orleans meeting of the Royal Pop People, also called the Society for Creative History. Margaret Stone intends to attend because they may discard something she wants to preserve. [MTM B11:307] The Countess says the session will continue until certain remnants are rooted out, while Absalom Stein worries about forgotten people being erased from existence. [MTM B11:315] The meeting develops into historical revision and destructive excursions against the remaining humans. [MTM B11:336-338]","citations":["MTM B11:307","MTM B11:315","MTM B11:336-338"],"tags":["New Orleans","Clubs & Societies","History & Memory","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Royal Pop People","definition":"The splendid, newly awakened people of New Orleans in the Fifth Contingency, associated with enhanced intuition and the thunder dimensions. They organize the Royal Pop History meeting and reject human identity; Roundhead regards their similar bodies as temporary dwellings for an essentially different species. [MTM B11:307] [MTM B11:315] [MTM B11:345-346] The Countess and Child Hero belong to this company, as do Cyrus Roundhead and apparently Hugh de Turenne, described as a genuine member of its inner historians. [MTM B11:334] [MTM B11:336] Their revision of history extends to erasing places, memories, and human populations, with recent recruits joining extermination hunts. Roundhead nonetheless longs for the depth and inherited glory they lack. [MTM B11:336-338] [MTM B11:351]","citations":["MTM B11:307","MTM B11:315","MTM B11:345-346","MTM B11:334","MTM B11:336","MTM B11:336-338","MTM B11:351"],"tags":["New Orleans","Clubs & Societies","Metaphysics","History & Memory","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Royal Rogues' And Graceful Swindlers' Ninth Annual Convention and Confidence-Men's Congress","definition":"Convention at the Royal Orleans Hotel, with about five hundred confidence people and thieves attending and expert seminars on specialized subjects. Charlotte Garfield comes for the event and visits Duffey's circle in cowgirl boots and a seven-and-a-half-gallon hat, with little between. [MTM B7:216]","citations":["MTM B7:216"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","Crime","New Orleans","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Rubbing alcohol","definition":"Liquid used for the fevered Sergeant's rubdown in the hospital ward. His daydream of swimming underwater with a Malay boy ends in the recognition that the remarkable wetness belongs to rubbing alcohol, not seawater. The Sergeant has been noon-dreaming rather than sleeping. [A:89-90]","citations":["A:89-90"],"tags":["Dreams","Soldiers","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Ruch, Alma","definition":"Member of the ecclesial or vineyard group among Duffey's Chicago friends. The narrator credits these churchly workers with keeping the world turning while others live upon their labor. [MTM B3:99]","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Rugutini, Arnoldo","definition":"New Yorker listed as the double of Nicholas Cardinal Gregorio of Messina. [MB:579] He reaches Mooring-Stone by army helicopter after the bounty hunters believe they have closed its approaches; the narrator remarks that Arnoldo has connections for such things. He is among the thirteen apparent double-men who assemble for the conclave. [MB:595-596]","citations":["MB:579","MB:595-596"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Identity","Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia"]},{"name":"Rule of St. Benedict","definition":"Benedict of Nursia's monastic rule, composed after 529, with a prologue and seventy-three chapters governing communal life under an abbot: prayer, reading, manual work, silence, meals, and the reception of new members. It became central to Western monasticism, with observance standardized across the Carolingian empire in the early ninth century, and remains a guide for Benedictine communities. At St. Bonaventure's boarding school it is supplied, alongside a Dictionary, Bible, Missal, and Atlas, in the six-boy chambers of St. Martin's Hall where Casey lives. [A:161-162]","citations":["A:161-162"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Prayer & Devotion","Literature","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Rum","definition":"Island drink enjoyed by Finnegan, Charles Le Marin, Don Barnaby, and Johnny Duckwalk in their Basse-Terre paradise, alongside beer and gin. Danish beer costs more than the local rum. [D:236] Finnegan can obtain a bottle for fifty cents, and the air of their favorite bar, nicknamed Sloppy Joe's but properly the Imperial Bar or Royal, smells of good rum and lime. [D:235]","citations":["D:236","D:235"],"tags":["Alcohol","Caribbean","Islands","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Runosake, Xavier Cardinal","definition":"Cardinal of Kobe, Japan, and one of the thirteen Princes targeted by John Mogul's Track and Total contracts. [MB:571] Mogul chooses him for X's apparently impossible delivery wager while Runosake has just escaped a Buddhist compound in Kobe. [MB:572] Minutes later a man recognized as Runosake staggers into Mogul's Marseille apartment and falls dead, with a knife hilt between his shoulders; he is the first of the contracted Cardinals reported dead. His double is Niku Kazuko, probably of Honolulu. [MB:574] [MB:579]","citations":["MB:571","MB:572","MB:574","MB:579"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Crime","Identity","Death & Mortality","France","Weapons"]},{"name":"Ruskin, John (1819--1900)","definition":"English writer, art critic, and social reformer (1819-1900), author of <em>Modern Painters</em> and <em>The Stones of Venice</em>. His studies of Gothic architecture and connection of artistic workmanship with social well-being influenced William Morris and the Arts and Crafts movement. In a letter to Dotty, Finnegan tentatively attributes to “Ruskin (or was it Erskine?)” the claim that music cannot say an unkind thing, then argues at length that music can be vindictive, sneaky, derisive, or mean. [A:263]","citations":["A:263"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Visual Arts","Music","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Russ","definition":"Regular at the Old Wooden Ship, identified elsewhere as a Galveston tavern. [D:215] [MTM B13:458] Russ, Bob, and Charleyhorse Heckel are steerers who bring generous drink-buyers to the bar, and rollers who rob men in the camel-lot afterward. With Smokehouse and Bill “Buffalo Chips” Dugan, they are described as five men cut from the same side of the animal. [D:220]","citations":["D:215","MTM B13:458","D:220"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Crime"]},{"name":"Russian","definition":"Language and literature in several characters' judgments. Casey's school notebook calls Russian fiction the world's best, comparing it to German music, but names no source for that judgment. Its linguistic entry says a Greek G is likely to become Russian ZH and labels this part of Grimm's law. [A:162-163] Separately, Patrick Stranahan calls the old Russian novelists supreme while objecting that reading them consumes days of a limited life. [A:109]","citations":["A:162-163","A:109"],"tags":["Language & Words","Literature","Eastern Europe"]},{"name":"Rusty Harpoon, The","definition":"Waterfront establishment in Duffey's catalogue of remembered seaside places. [MTM B13:407]","citations":["MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Bars & Taverns","Duffey"]},{"name":"S. J.","definition":"Abbreviation of <em>Societas Jesu</em>, the Society of Jesus or Jesuits. Jesuit masters approve the boys' Sunday sales at Duffey's boarding school; his school companions include Charley Murray, John Rattigan, and Sebastian Hilton, although the account combines more than one school and substitutes other boys during part of the interval. [MTM B1:24] [MTM B1:28]","citations":["MTM B1:24","MTM B1:28"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Duffey","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"S.R. & Co.","definition":"Iron inscription on anvils, paired with “Peter Wright,” in the young Duffey's catalogue of household names appropriated through observation, memory, and other people's minds. [MTM B1:17]","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Sabbath of the mind","definition":"Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni's name for the mental respite necessary for thought. He tells Hans not to become absorbed in the world's furniture, but to remember humanity's position always at the beginning: fragile as morning dew, yet also manna, a contingent species whose hoped-for realization has a purpose. [A:212]","citations":["A:212"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","Creation"]},{"name":"Sabina, Sister Mary","definition":"Nun teaching five-year-old Duffey on his first day at school. His precocious proposals make her pray aloud that there might not be another smart child that year; she nevertheless lets him divide the class into mixed groups of four under responsible leaders. She asks Jesus, Mary, and Joseph to remember at her last agony how cheerfully she bore such trials. [MTM B1:11-12]","citations":["MTM B1:11-12"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Religious Orders","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Sabinal","definition":"Town in Uvalde County, Texas, on Finnegan's itinerary before his strange interval in the chaparral. He is in Uvalde on Monday, then Sabinal, D'Hannis, and Hondo on Tuesday, returning to D'Hannis to look for his old cavalry outfit. [A:202]","citations":["A:202"],"tags":["Finnegan","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Sacraments","definition":"Religious observances to which Henry Salvatore returns while the other four members of the Dirty Five are on furlough in Australia in 1943. Henry stays in the jungle, believing himself safer there; after their reunion the narrator says they will live together in all ways for a while. [A:31] [A:38]","citations":["A:31","A:38"],"tags":["Sacraments","World War II","Australia"]},{"name":"Sadducees of the Left","definition":"Father McGuigan's label for the churchly Left in his quarrel with Patrick Stranahan: he says it differs no more from Patrick's Right than Sadducees do from Pharisees. Patrick rejects the tidy alignment but insists on the distinction, accusing modern counterparts of the Sadducees, including McGuigan, of denying the Resurrection. [A:110]","citations":["A:110"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Resurrection","New Testament"]},{"name":"Safti","definition":"Name printed in the <em>Brunhilde</em>'s coastal itinerary between Magadar and Mazagan, before Casablanca, Rabat, and Tangier; apparently Safi, the Moroccan Atlantic port. [D:101]","citations":["D:101"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Ports & Harbors","Africa"]},{"name":"Sahirn","definition":"One of Gautier's seven youngest goats, a group of three twin pairs and a single kid born to four sisters. Sahirn ends the hebdomadal list Bownee, Luin, Mairt, Keadin, Ordin, Haoine, and Sahirn, apparently representing Saturday (compare Irish <em>Dé Sathairn</em>). [A:194-195] The goat-man is named Gautier, though neighbors mock him as Goat-Ear. [A:196]","citations":["A:194-195","A:196"],"tags":["Language & Words","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Salem","definition":"City of the biblical Melchisedech, priest of God Most High; Hebrews interprets his name as righteous king and his kingship of Salem as kingship of peace. The Genesis 14:18 epigraph to Book Two recalls his offering of bread and wine. [MTM B2:38] Lily Koch playfully invokes the “Royal Malarky of Salem” in her metaphor about Duffey and Letitia. [MTM B3:96] Absalom Stein alternates between regarding Duffey as the ancient King of Salem himself and regarding him as a man who shares that patron saint's memories through unusual accord. [MTM B10:291]","citations":["MTM B2:38","MTM B3:96","MTM B10:291"],"tags":["Old Testament","New Testament","Duffey","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Salina","definition":"Island off Sicily named with Filicudi in the narrator's fanciful history of mermaids, who are said to have lived there in the Middle Ages. [D:35]","citations":["D:35"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Islands","Italy"]},{"name":"Sally, Salvation (Sally)","definition":"Australian street preacher first met by Hans Schultz and Marie Monaghan in a harbor coffee house during the Sydney furlough. She preaches the Second Coming, strums hymns on her guitar, and collects donations in a cup suspended from it. She accepts coffee but rejects brandy, distrusts churches, and claims the gift of tongues. Her appearance and voice are odd and her speech full of catchphrases, yet the narrator calls her electric and authentic. She accepts Marie's offer of a room after insisting on love rather than charity; the three make lasting commitments to one another. Years later Hans finds that an ordinary-looking letter cannot convey her extraordinary Australian voice. [A:7-9] [A:31]<br><br>Marie pays her passage to America, and Sally joins the New Orleans undertaking to rebuild the world. [A:147] [MTM B6:198] Dotty's letter calls her a crow and bushwhacker and jokingly complains that Margaret failed to drown her; it is Dotty who calls <em>Marie</em> a filthy Irishman for sending Sally. Mary Virginia insists on keeping her, and Sally has given her remaining money to people less fortunate. [A:147] Her later portrait includes an aggressive bony face and a pewter heart; she cites Chesterfield for leaving good reading for good company. [MTM B7:218]<br><br>In the Fifth Contingency she joins the Royal Pop extermination hunts with Margaret and Mary Virginia, reporting that she and the Child Hero have cleared a building's third floor. [MTM B11:336-338] In “The Great Day” she is at the Pelican Press, drinking tea without a cup and sharing the new nakedness. She rejects Duffey's proposal that laughter might save the world, declares salvation outside time impossible, and repeatedly urges him to remove his clothes. [GD:490-492] [GD:496]","citations":["A:7-9","A:31","A:147","MTM B6:198","MTM B7:218","MTM B11:336-338","GD:490-492","GD:496"],"tags":["Australia","New Orleans","Women Characters","Music","Theology","Prayer & Devotion","Apocalyptic","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Salvatore, Henry Francis (Henri)","definition":"Member of the Dirty Five, born in Morgan City, Louisiana, on December 8, 1920, to Lawrence Salvatore and Mary Genevieve Hebert [A:122]. Before the army he works at sea and drives trucks, including for the Schaeffer family’s Red Dog Motor Freight Line, where he meets Mary Virginia. He joins the army on his twenty-first birthday [A:124]. Mary Virginia had been his betrothed; he gives her up for God [MTM B7:214].<br><br>After the war he enters seminary and sets his friends to work rebuilding the world. His dream of an endangered boat inspires the journal <em>The Bark</em>, which he names; Finnegan draws its masthead [A:121] [A:124–126]. He is one of its seven founding trustees and directors. After seminary he serves a parish deep in the swamps [MTM B7:214].<br><br>Later, as Henri Cardinal Salvatore of New Orleans, he joins the gathering in St. Louis during Duffey’s last days [MTM B12:384–387].","citations":["A:122","A:124","MTM B7:214","A:121","A:124–126","MTM B12:384–387"],"tags":["Argonauts (Characters)","Louisiana","Catholic Church","Journalism"]},{"name":"Sammy (Saddle Sore) Sanders","definition":"Resident regular at the Old Wooden Ship tavern in Galveston. [D:215-216] [MTM B13:458]","citations":["D:215-216","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"San Angelo","definition":"Texas city included in Demetrio's boast about the places he visited in his youth, before returning to run his own bar. [D:58]","citations":["D:58"],"tags":["Minor Characters"]},{"name":"San Antonio","definition":"Texas city where, Finnegan is told, some of the men from his old Moonlight Cavalry outfit still live; people in D'Hannis urge him to seek them there. [A:202] Patrick Stranahan includes it among the gracious American cities. [A:110] George Koran names it as a planned stop later that day on the Devil's busy lecture tour. [MTM B8:244]","citations":["A:202","A:110","MTM B8:244"],"tags":["Finnegan","Soldiers","The Devil"]},{"name":"San Carlo, Pedro","definition":"Person with whom Duffey associates in New Orleans as part of a circle engaged in building or inventing worlds, moments, situations, and scenes. He is listed among artists and other creators, without a particular art or occupation specified. [MTM B6:198]","citations":["MTM B6:198"],"tags":["Duffey","New Orleans","Minor Characters","Creation"]},{"name":"San Cristobal","definition":"Port named after Limon and before Portobello on the <em>Brunhilde</em>'s Caribbean itinerary. [D:81] [D:85] Here Finnegan and Saxon Seaworthy get extravagantly drunk on rum for a night and day, possibly two nights and days, around December 9 or 10. After exhausting the town's drinking places they form their own drinking club in a garden Seaworthy buys for a thousand American dollars. [D:82]","citations":["D:81","D:85","D:82"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","Caribbean","Alcohol","Finnegan"]},{"name":"San Francisco","definition":"City where Finnegan is paid off at the end of his obscure voyage around the world. He has been working in the engine room and expects an Atlantic port, so he is surprised to find himself in San Francisco. [A:145] Patrick Stranahan includes the city among the gracious American cities. [A:110]","citations":["A:145","A:110"],"tags":["Finnegan","Ports & Harbors","Sailing"]},{"name":"San Francisco cemetery","definition":"Cemetery in the Cuban city where La Dorotea is reported buried. An informant tells Duffey, X, and Horace Pie that she died about three weeks after having the Sleeping Man placed in his sea-door tomb, having never recovered her strength after a gunshot wound. Her medallion identified her as belonging to the Third Order of St. Francis and explains her burial in this cemetery on the other side of the city. [MTM B12:372]","citations":["MTM B12:372"],"tags":["Death & Mortality","Religious Orders","Women Characters","Duffey","Caribbean"]},{"name":"San gre del Cristo","definition":"Sunken treasure ship shown with the <em>St. Marc</em> on Angela Cosquin's father's map, near Basse-Terre. Angela calls the paired wrecks a fabrication to misdirect her friends, but the narrator says they really lie no more than three hundred yards away, loaded with gold. Angela dives to their hoard and eventually uses the wrecks as safekeeping for her own money. [D:261-262]","citations":["D:261-262"],"tags":["Ships","Shipwrecks","Caribbean","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Sanchez, Olga","definition":"Woman of the “torchy shoulders” who works at Duffey's Rounders' Club in St. Louis and mixes drinks at its main bar. [MTM B2:49] [MTM B2:59] She and Lucille Sisler are devoted partisans of Duffey; when “The King Shall Ride” is played, Olga or Lucille carries him around the main room on her shoulders. [MTM B2:52] Olga still works there when he returns after seven years away, greeting him as her love, boy, and prince while noticing that he is not quite all back. She has married a handsome Mexican man who has become head chef of the club's main dining room. [MTM B2:73-74]","citations":["MTM B2:49","MTM B2:59","MTM B2:52","MTM B2:73-74"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Clubs & Societies","Food & Dining","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Sanctimonious Sam","definition":"One of the extraordinary Pop People whom Duffey recognizes among the crowds filling his businesses. Although his name sounds like a type, the narrator insists that he, the Child-Hero, the Hoyden, and the Countess are unique, everlasting, omnipresent people, with only one of each. [MTM B11:322]","citations":["MTM B11:322"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Identity","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Sanders, Sammy (Saddle Sore)","definition":"See <em>Sammy (Saddle Sore) Sanders</em>, resident regular at the Old Wooden Ship tavern in Galveston. [D:215-216] [MTM B13:458]","citations":["D:215-216","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Santa Claus","definition":"Unverified cross-reference to <em>Papadiabolous (II) (Gregory McColum Papadiabolous, Papadiabolous II, Papa Devil, Papa D, Noonan)</em>. The identification of him as Santa Claus has not been located in the available texts.","citations":[],"tags":["Devils (Characters)","Identity"]},{"name":"Santa Maria","definition":"Port named between Cartagena and Willemsted on the <em>Brunhilde</em>'s itinerary. [D:85] Also the Italian invocation “Holy Mary,” used by Dotty Yekouris when she laments that Finnegan has been in Arcadia and asks why everyone cannot be there. [A:176]","citations":["D:85","A:176"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Ships","Language & Words","Prayer & Devotion","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Santana","definition":"Name cited in the epigraph attributed to Carmody Overlark's <em>Studies in the Imagination</em>. Santana is credited with wondering why mystics of beautiful, singing thought were facially ugly; the allusion's personal identity remains unverified. [D:271-272]","citations":["D:271-272"],"tags":["Mysticism","Literature"]},{"name":"Santee, Charles","definition":"American sergeant from Orange, Texas, one of the three soldiers in Don Lewis's account of a patrol that disappears on October 1, 1944, on Pulau Petir, also called Willy Jones Island. The other two are Corporal Robert Casper and PFC Timothy Lorrigan. Don Lewis claims to have been one of them but withholds which one. [D:44] Santee shoots and wounds the elusive little man called Yokipoki; he and Casper later go ahead and kill him while Lorrigan hangs back. The soldiers lose their way among the island's valleys; after a quarrel Santee and Casper sleep on separate heights while Don Lewis stays below. [D:45-46] Their eventual fates remain uncertain: Don Lewis reports hearing both that the other two were killed and that they returned separately with incredible stories. [D:48]","citations":["D:44","D:45-46","D:48"],"tags":["Soldiers","World War II","Pacific Theater","Crime","Identity"]},{"name":"Santo Spirito!","definition":"Italian invocation meaning “Holy Spirit!” It punctuates the narrative's loving evocation of Theresa making bread by hand in the house of her impossible twelve-year life with Finnegan. [A:204]","citations":["A:204"],"tags":["Language & Words","Prayer & Devotion","Family","Food & Dining","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Sao Luis","definition":"São Luís, a Brazilian coastal city, named between Belem and Camocim among the <em>Brunhilde</em>'s ports before its Atlantic crossing. [D:86]","citations":["D:86"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Ships","Sailing"]},{"name":"Sarcophagus","definition":"Coffin-shaped ship in Charles Le Marin's account of a supposed mysterious derelict. He claims to have been one of the seamen who disappeared and were never seen again. [D:243-244] In his story Castleman infiltrates the ship before it leaves Paramaribo, then replaces successive crewmen in port with lookalike dock rats. Le Marin says that he too was replaced and never knew what happened to himself, but subsequently narrates the takeover from among the infiltrators. [D:244] Castleman arrives in the <em>Tercel Hawk</em>, transfers the captain and loyal crew to it, and has them bound, weighted, and drowned. Le Marin hides, then swims ashore in Florida three days later. Castleman reports the <em>Sarcophagus</em> found inexplicably deserted, concealing his theft of its cargo. Whether the switch of Le Marin was ever completed remains doubtful, even to Castleman. Johnny Duckwalk challenges the tale as a version of the <em>Mary Celeste</em> story. [D:245-246]","citations":["D:243-244","D:244","D:245-246"],"tags":["Ships","Crime","Crew & Sailors","Identity","Sailing"]},{"name":"Sardinia","definition":"Island from which the Red Fisters in Margaret Stone's New Orleans account come. She says they threaten to wear her guts as scarves, but also share dawn coffee with her at Messina's or Anthony Ghost's and claim to spare her in order to hear the next morning's shaggy-duck joke. She fears the humorless Slant-Faced Men more. [MTM B7:225]","citations":["MTM B7:225"],"tags":["Islands","Crime","Women Characters","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Satan","definition":"Devil invoked by Duffey as a comparison in his letter to Casey: people trying to take over the <em>Crock</em> offer him wealth, distribution, and the world's kingdoms, as Satan once offered riches. These offers would make the journal an organ of the infiltrates. [A:39-40] In Show Boat's later letter to Dotty, Casey says that the Seven Devils have found him swept and garnished and want to return. He has refused them, although they hold his debts and have taken his press and building; they cannot use the <em>Crock</em>'s name. [A:262]","citations":["A:39-40","A:262"],"tags":["The Devil","Duffey","Journalism","Money & Trade","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Saturday","definition":"Weekday apparently represented by Sahirn, last in Gautier's hebdomadal list of seven goat-kids; compare Irish <em>Dé Sathairn</em>. [A:194-195] The last Saturday of May 1946 is the announced date of Vincent and Theresa's wedding in <em>Archipelago</em>. [A:95] Bagby's funeral is also on a Saturday; Duffey and Letitia return to New Orleans on the following Monday. [MTM B8:237]","citations":["A:194-195","A:95","MTM B8:237"],"tags":["Time & Eternity","Language & Words","Family","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Satyrus","definition":"Historical medical teacher of Galen. After an epigraph attributed to Galen describes the daemon-begotten Tityroi or Satyroi, the narrator asks where Satyrus obtained his name and where Galen obtained his information. The juxtaposition invites a connection between the teacher and the mythical race without asserting that Satyrus was nonhuman. [D:160-161]","citations":["D:160-161"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Greek Mythology","Monsters & Creatures","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Saucerite (from the Big Triangle)","definition":"An eleven-year-old visitor from the Big Triangle who joins the <em>Argo</em> shortly before the Mooring-Stone conclave. He attends Gaetano Polytech in Balbo City, takes human classmates to his undersea nest in his saucer, and hopes to obtain a chess scholarship to the University of Miami. Herman Hercules calls him “little owl-eyes.” The visitor says that he is real although fingers can pass through him. He calls Earth the “World of the Covenant” and thinks that its inhabitants probably carry “super-seed,” while finding their attainments uneven. He intends to report on the conclave for a small press network [MB:591-592].","citations":["MB:591-592"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Ships","Theology","Journalism"]},{"name":"Saul, King","definition":"The biblical king named by Duffey among the rulers with whom he claims to have attended royal conclaves [MTM B5:166].","citations":["MTM B5:166"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Duffey"]},{"name":"Saul, King (reigned c. 1047--1007 BC)","definition":"Earlier heading with unverified reign dates. See <em>Saul, King</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Duffey"]},{"name":"Savu Sea","definition":"One of the many seas sailed by the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Sazarac","definition":"Drink named in Casey Szymansky's attack on the <em>Bark</em>. He jokes that Dotty's talents as a barmaid extend only “from the Absinthe Frappe to the Sazarac,” a range of four blocks [A:128-129]. Duffey orders a sazarac at the Stumble-Bum on the Great Day, but the bar no longer serves it [GD:498].","citations":["A:128-129","GD:498"],"tags":["Alcohol","Bars & Taverns","Journalism","Duffey"]},{"name":"Scanlon, Adam","definition":"Painter associated with Hans Schultz, James Fitzjames, Trinali Peterson, and Francis Schraffenberger. At the driftwood shore he paints seascapes and explains a High Renaissance sky above a sea of the Low Middle Ages, with the <em>Argo</em> suggested by its absence. He likes to talk to an audience while painting. His friends compare him to Leonardo, and Finnegan later calls him an accomplished professional of comparable ability [A:230] [A:233-234] [A:236]. The narrative places Scanlon and Finnegan among the two greatest American artists [MTM B6:199]. Duffey uses Scanlon's painting of Casey, made without Casey's knowledge, for the terrifying televised apparition of Casey's head; Scanlon disliked Casey but was Finnegan's friend [MTM B12:381].","citations":["A:230","A:233-234","A:236","MTM B6:199","MTM B12:381"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Finnegan","Duffey","Minor Characters","Ships"]},{"name":"Schaeffer","definition":"Surname of Mary Virginia Schaeffer, whom Henry Salvatore meets while driving for her family's Red Dog Motor Freight Line [A:124]. Also the surname of Sebastian “Shot Gun” Schaeffer, named for a drink he invented [D:222].","citations":["A:124","D:222"],"tags":["Family","Women Characters","Minor Characters","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Schaeffer family","definition":"Owners of the Red Dog Motor Freight Line, for which Henry Salvatore drives when he meets Mary Virginia Schaeffer [A:124]. The line connects Galveston and Houston with Morgan City and New Orleans, also covering Baton Rouge, Shreveport, Port Arthur, Orange, and Beaumont. Mary Virginia transfers to its New Orleans terminal while working with Dotty on the <em>Bark</em> [MTM B6:198].","citations":["A:124","MTM B6:198"],"tags":["Family","Occupations","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Schaeffer, Mary Virginia","definition":"Mary Virginia Schaeffer, also called Mary Schaeffer or Mary V., is Henry Salvatore’s former fiancée. <em>Archipelago</em> calls her Henry’s girl from Morgan City, Louisiana, and one of the sweetest persons in the world [A:97]; <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> also identifies her with Galveston [MTM B5:157]. Dotty calls her the Saccharine Kid, partly affectionately and partly jealously. Henry gives her up for God while doubting that God is good enough for her; the narrator calls her a bonus beyond price and a valuable working ornament [MTM B7:214].\n\nShe attends the St. Louis gathering for Vincent and Teresa’s wedding in May 1946 [MTM B5:171]. She drives with Mary Catherine, Finnegan, Henry, and Duffey through the Illinois towns [A:98], then takes Finnegan, Henry, Duffey, and Dotty to New Orleans in her Ford after the wedding [A:114]. Finnegan includes her among the friends of his ordinary life [D:90]. Her family owns Red Dog Motor Freight, serving Galveston, Houston, Morgan City, New Orleans, Baton Rouge, Shreveport, Port Arthur, Orange, and Beaumont. <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> describes her transferring herself to the New Orleans terminal and working both for the firm and with Dotty on the <em>Bark</em> [MTM B6:198]; <em>Archipelago</em> says she leaves her father’s firm to work with Dotty [A:143]. She helps run the <em>Bark</em>, although she is not one of its designated Seven Pillars of Righteousness [MTM B7:214-215].\n\nOne of Duffey’s Animated Marvels, she is the gentle figure who recognizes him at Rounders’ Club [MTM B5:156-157]. Teresa identifies her ancient self as Laonome and describes Duffey as the awakener, rather than the maker, of the old <em>Argo</em> company [MTM B7:233-234]. Mary Virginia remembers fragments and says she hates her ancient name, printed as Laonone in her reply [MTM B7:235]. Stein alternatively proposes that she and the others helped make Duffey what he is, while admitting that on other days he believes Duffey made them [MTM B10:304].\n\nShe plays the piano while the group develops the song about Bagby’s liver [MTM B7:226]. Duffey asks her to record The Majesty’s speech so that its recorded, remembered, and inward versions can be compared [MTM B8:241]. After Letitia’s death she says the Glorious Mysteries of the rosary with neighbors and people from the <em>Seaman’s Paper</em>, <em>Jazz Sheet</em>, and <em>Sporting News</em> [MTM B8:253]. Her own reflections in her <em>Duffey Book</em> suggest that he comes from one of the discarded worlds [MTM B10:291-292]. She worries about his wandering, warns that he may not return from a doomsday walk, and recognizes him as a magus [MTM B10:304-305].\n\nIn the Royal Pop History episode, her beauty has deepened over decades, while her kindness has acquired a vacant quality. She reports the Dominicans’ warning that the world will end that week [MTM B11:310]. She jokes that she once created more history herself [MTM B11:307]. Seeing broken things that resemble forgotten friends, she questions their destruction; the young Pop countess calls her “the Mary Virginia” and warns that such questions could make her a target. Gray hairs appear in the halo around her face [MTM B11:318]. She nevertheless joins Margaret Stone and Salvation Sally in the exterminating hunts [MTM B11:336-337]. While destroying a half-grown boy, she asks why unimportant people must be destroyed [MTM B11:338]. Later she carries a child she has killed, horrified that its body remains flesh and blood. She refuses to surrender it as trash; Margaret takes it and secretly arranges its burial [MTM B11:342] [MTM B11:344].\n\nWhen Duffey visits a year ahead of his own time, she greets him at the Pelican Press with affection, exasperation, and a gently burning look. The narrator calls her open, intelligent, kind, and complex [MTM B12:359]. She tells him he has been dead for months and asks why he has not found peace. When he withdraws from that future, she is again demanding his overdue review column [MTM B12:361].\n\nIn “Great Day in the Morning,” she joins the Pelican people’s experiments in living without ordinary containment. Laughter collapses her unsupported coffee and burns her [GD:490-491]. She hands Duffey the old <em>Bark</em> containing his Great Day article, treating his satire as fulfilled prediction and calling laughter a blasphemy [GD:492]. The narrator finds her alone sufficiently well formed to go unclothed. She proposes abandoning print for wordless song, faith-painting, and other Great Day communication [GD:494], and looks forward to everyone shedding their skin [GD:495].","citations":["A:97","MTM B5:157","MTM B7:214","MTM B5:171","A:98","A:114","D:90","MTM B6:198","A:143","MTM B7:214-215","MTM B5:156-157","MTM B7:233-234","MTM B7:235","MTM B10:304","MTM B7:226","MTM B8:241","MTM B8:253","MTM B10:291-292","MTM B10:304-305","MTM B11:310","MTM B11:307","MTM B11:318","MTM B11:336-337","MTM B11:338","MTM B11:342","MTM B11:344","MTM B12:359","MTM B12:361","GD:490-491","GD:492","GD:494","GD:495"],"tags":["Women Characters","Duffey","Journalism","Occupations","Family","New Orleans","Louisiana","Music","Prayer & Devotion","Argonauts (Characters)","History & Memory","Identity","Creation","Death & Mortality","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Schaeffer, Sebastian (Shot Gun)","definition":"Regular at the Old Wooden Ship tavern in Galveston; nickname derived from a drink he invented, not from weaponry [D:222]; mistaken for a hitman by a beautiful lady who pays him $5000 after seeing a \"distinguished looking gentleman with his head pretty well-blown off\" in a mansion she drove him to [D:222-223]; subsequently hounded for the money by George (Basin Street) Becaud, who claimed the job was his [D:223]; spent terrified hours locked in his room [D:224].","citations":["D:222","D:222-223","D:223","D:224"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Crime","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Schofields","definition":"One of the names on grindstones in Duffey's remembered childhood world. It occurs among the household “iron words,” many appropriated from other people's minds [MTM B1:17].","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory","Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Schotts","definition":"St. Louis restaurant where Duffey and his friends eat before going to the theatre. It is also among the places visited during the farewell celebrations before his seven-year absence [MTM B2:58] [MTM B2:69].","citations":["MTM B2:58","MTM B2:69"],"tags":["Duffey","Food & Dining","Theater"]},{"name":"Schrade (Crock poet)","definition":"Author identified only as Schrade whose sonnet is included in a twenty-page issue of the <em>Crock</em> [A:41]. The passage does not supply the first name Cyrus. <em>Schrade, Clarence</em> is separately named among Duffey’s literary friends [MTM B3:98], but the two references are not explicitly joined.","citations":["A:41","MTM B3:98"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Schrade, Clarence","definition":"One of the Duffeys' bookish and literary friends in the North Chicago golden age [MTM B3:98]. As art editor of the <em>Crock</em>, he refuses Casey's drawings, though Casey slips some into the magazine while Schrade is in hospital. His commentary on Casey's verse, music, and drawings calls attention to the strange force of the badly drawn hellscapes [PG:522].","citations":["MTM B3:98","PG:522"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Visual Arts","Journalism","Duffey"]},{"name":"Schrade, Cyrus","definition":"Earlier heading supplied the unsupported first name Cyrus. See <em>Schrade (Crock poet)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Schraffenberger, Francis","definition":"Companion of Hans Schultz, James Fitzjames, Adam Scanlon, and Trinali Peterson on their driftwood expedition. He explains the butterflies' attraction to salt and sulphur, catches a fish for the group's lunch, and declares that no sane man willingly enters a swamp while Trinali cuts cypress knees [A:233-234] [A:236].","citations":["A:233-234","A:236"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Schultz, Cecilia","definition":"One of Hans and Marie Schultz's daughters-in-law. She is listed among those probably present during the continuing farewell conversation with Duffey, whose setting shifts among Rounders' Club, Cabramatta Castle, and other St. Louis houses [MTM B12:385].","citations":["MTM B12:385"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Schultz, John G. (Hans, Who was Orpheus, Aquinas in a T Shirt, John Schultz y Hayden, John Gottfried Schultz, Dutchman, Hanschen, Milwaukee Seidel)","definition":"John Gottfried Schultz, called Hans, is one of the Dirty Five and their Orpheus [A:38]. He is the only child of John William Schultz and Mary Irene Hayden, born January 2, 1915, at St. Gallen, Wisconsin, in the lake country beyond Rhinelander near Eagle River. Called John until half grown, he remembers his childhood country as a place of splendid sunlight. His father, a nurseryman and the strongest man in St. Gallen, teaches him German, Latin, horticulture, physical strength, and morality. Hans does not rebel against this last education, though his morality is less fierce than his father’s. He attends St. Joseph’s by pony or on foot. His father quarrels with the nuns and Father Baumgartner, demanding Greek, Aquinas, and Albert in the fourth grade [A:207-209].\n\nAt fourteen, according to the narrator, Hans leaves for his <em>Wanderjahr</em> with his father’s old friend, Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni. The departure is placed in early spring 1930, a date that does not fit the stated age and birth date. The Professor is presented as the last man who knew everything before knowledge multiplied beyond one mind [A:210-211]. They travel in a truck like an old medicine-show wagon. Kirol teaches rapid calculation and prodigious memory as cerebral athleticism, distinct from thought; he discusses the trees of knowledge, the Tree of Language, and his literal belief in Babel [A:212-213]. Hans learns to calculate the right ascension of likely requested stars, including Alpha Centauri, Procyon, CC624, Kapteyn’s Star, Rigel, Vega, and Betelgeuse. Kirol describes the visible universe as the reverse of Heaven’s tapestry and insists that they are already within eternity [A:214].\n\nHans spends five or six years with the carnival, traveling several continents, learning fortune-telling or <em>dukkerin</em> and Deep Romany, reading minds, answering unasked questions, and taking the platform to answer any question in the world. He is the sorcerer’s apprentice, still fifty years behind his master. His strongman feats include lifting a horse in a harness, supporting the Donkey Walk across his stomach, and bending coins, horseshoes, and bars [A:218-219]. He is a genuine hypnotist, though without Kirol’s commanding theatrical presence, and performs with Betty Hochstapler and Mabel the bear. Betty’s swindler father had been killed by a wool-hat with his own little mallet [A:217] [A:219]. Hans leaves in the spring of 1935 or 1936, fearing that he will inherit the carnival apparatus, and goes to Bohemia as a way of life rather than a country [A:220-221].\n\nWith several thousand dollars saved, he joins a New York circle of young artists in a period both joyful and sad. He gives the scholarly panhandler James Fitzjames money for <em>Jaufre and Brunissende</em>, buys <em>Les Cents Novelles Nouvelles</em> for himself, and shares bagels and beer with him. Fitzjames calls him Dutchman; they then collect Trinali Peterson uptown [A:224-226]. Hans rents a three-room apartment for forty-five dollars a month. To Trinali, ten unencumbered dollars would make him rich [A:227] [A:229]. At Lupido’s he meets Endymeon Ellenbogen, formerly Elmer Eggleton Ellenbogen, who praises him as a good listener [A:236-237]. After Trinali’s death, Hans questions whether the companions were real even in their sudden youth. This Bohemia probably lasts only one spring and summer; it is followed by four, five, or six hidden years, depending on the recension, in which he acquires resources that later allow him to obtain money from a distance [A:240-241].\n\nHe enters the Army in the spring of 1941 or 1942 [A:241], serving in the same battery as Vincent, Casey, Henry, and Finnegan [MTM B5:171]. In Sydney he wins the Dominion Rules beer-drinking contest against Joe Bushmaster at the Harbor House. Jimmy Hansen bills him as the Milwaukee Seidel, opposite Bushmaster’s Lion of the South; Absalom Stein presides [A:15] [A:19-23]. Hans attributes his prospects to a pure heart and crafty mind [A:17]. He marries Marie Anna Monaghan at the Annunciation Church in Sydney on February 15, 1943, without Army permission [A:31]. He is reduced from staff sergeant to basic for the marriage, but remains exuberantly happy. In New Guinea he climbs to the cliff people, eats with them, and learns their speech. Finnegan dismisses such reports as liars’ tales; the narrator both calls Hans a liar in those days and affirms the encounters [A:58]. The shore boy Sulem teaches him to distinguish <em>tujuan</em>, life’s direction or orders, from <em>semangat</em>, the soul, and says Finnegan has the latter but lacks the former [A:59-60].\n\nHans hears Marie’s talk in his mind as well as in her presence [A:16] [A:25]. He writes her a love poem; she protests that it makes her old and answers with stories about her uncle Shakes Pearson. Shakes is a figure in her story, not another listener at their table [A:25-26]. Hans’s unpublished ballad of Li Tai Po belongs to his Chinese period, in which Heine is a strong influence. The simultaneous flute-playing and singing is Li Tai Po’s feat inside that ballad [A:221-223]. Salvation Sally joins Hans and Marie at the coffee house, and the three make lasting commitments to one another. She learns the familiar name Hanschen. Years later Hans marvels at hearing her distinctive speech through a letter whose written words look ordinary [A:8-9]. Henry calls Hans a scholar [A:43].\n\nAfter the war, Hans and Marie settle in St. Louis, where Hans pursues building business during the wedding gathering of May 1946 [A:97] [MTM B5:171]. Hans becomes one of the original Seven Pillars of Righteousness, the <em>Bark</em>’s trustees and directors. Finnegan labels his masthead portrait Aquinas in a T Shirt [A:121-122] [MTM B7:214]. In a hostile <em>Crock</em> article, Casey calls him Acquinas in a T shirt, remembers his dinner jacket and highball, and reports that the contractor has put his own money and other people’s money, without their knowledge, into the project. These are Casey’s allegations [A:128-129]. Hans hoped to make enough money in three or five years and then turn to other pursuits, but becomes trapped by his success, with his wealth in heavy equipment, developing property, and futuristic paper. Finnegan answers his letters; Marie does not understand his discontent or envy of Finnegan [A:146]. Later the narrator says Hans cannot keep himself divested of money, but has restored a consensus of conscience in one field of business [MTM B8:247].\n\nFinnegan’s absence takes a savor from Hans’s life, more strongly felt than Vincent’s corresponding loss [A:187]. Hans’s early history is largely drawn from his talks with Finnegan during a week on a construction job, probably in summer 1950; their conversations also return to the carnival and the talent of his New York friends [A:207] [A:220] [A:236]. In Duffey’s view Hans is one of his Animated Marvels: a huge, loud, vividly colored holy ox like Aquinas, a mythological thunderhead [MTM B5:158-161]. The narrator calls him big-brained and big-hearted, apparently open yet hard to know [MTM B5:182]. Marie disputes Duffey’s claim of creation, saying that he collects them and supplies their settings; Duffey insists that he makes them [MTM B5:159].\n\nHans describes the Argonauts as exiles on a mundane shore, awaiting their return to the <em>Argo</em>. In a letter to Marie he remembers sailing as Orpheus, and Teresa identifies him both as Orpheus and as a later manifestation of Faust [MTM B5:178] [MTM B6:186] [MTM B7:234]. Stein reports that Hans experienced a rapport with one of the <em>Brunhilde</em> incidents, without identifying that incident [MTM B6:188]. At Duffey’s last St. Louis gathering, Hans and Marie’s Cabramatta Castle is one of several possible settings for the continuing conversation. Their daughter-in-law Cecilia is probably among those present [MTM B12:385]. Hans believes he has lately sailed on the <em>Argo</em>; Marie says she knows she has. Duffey counts Hans among the five good friends who have helped rebuild the world [MTM B12:387-388]. Hans and Marie supply the ship with sea biscuit [MTM B13:446]. The inherited heading’s form John Schultz y Hayden has not been located in the supplied texts.","citations":["A:38","A:207-209","A:210-211","A:212-213","A:214","A:218-219","A:217","A:219","A:220-221","A:224-226","A:227","A:229","A:236-237","A:240-241","A:241","MTM B5:171","A:15","A:19-23","A:17","A:31","A:58","A:59-60","A:16","A:25","A:25-26","A:221-223","A:8-9","A:43","A:97","A:121-122","MTM B7:214","A:128-129","A:146","MTM B8:247","A:187","A:207","A:220","A:236","MTM B5:158-161","MTM B5:182","MTM B5:159","MTM B5:178","MTM B6:186","MTM B7:234","MTM B6:188","MTM B12:385","MTM B12:387-388","MTM B13:446"],"tags":["Argonauts (Characters)","Argonautica","Family","Soldiers","Australia","Carnival","Magic & Sorcery","Literature","Language & Words","Alcohol","Money & Trade","Journalism","Creation","Identity","Finnegan","Duffey","Ships","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Schultz, John William","definition":"Father of Hans Schultz, a nurseryman in St. Gallen, WI [A:208]; strongest man there; provided Hans intensive education in German, Latin, horticulture, physical culture (dynamic tension, cable exercisers, bar bells, kettle drums, turning pole, flying rings, mile run) [A:208]; quarreled with nuns and Father Baumgartner at St. Joseph's school over the curriculum, advocating for Greek and Aquinas in 4th grade (\"'This that you teach, it is little stuff. It is for children,' John William said.\" \"'Well, John is only nine years old.'\" \"'They should know these things already. Some of them they should know before they are born.'\") [A:208-209]. John William had himself traveled with Professor Kirol and wanted Hans to do the same. Before their marriage, John William and Irene had separately come to America from different countries at Kirol’s expense [A:211].","citations":["A:208","A:208-209","A:211"],"tags":["Family","Minor Characters","Language & Words","Theology"]},{"name":"Schultz, Marie Monaghan","definition":"Marie Anna Monaghan, Australian wife of Hans Schultz. She describes the Monaghans as an old Irish family established in Sydney for 156 years; to Hans's friends she appears chubby, roguish, tart-talking, and given to brushing her hair from her eyes with the back of her hand [A:6-7]. She and Hans marry secretly at Sydney's Annunciation Church on 15 February 1943 because he lacks army permission; they later settle in St. Louis [A:31] [A:97]. Hans calls her queenly and imagines her speaking in his mind; she calls him Hanschen [A:9] [A:16]. One of Duffey's Animated Marvels, she disputes his account of creating them: he collects them and gives them their settings, she says, while he insists that he makes them [MTM B5:159]. Teresa addresses her with the former <em>Argo</em> company [MTM B7:233]. Marie says she has recently been aboard the ship and wakes refreshed because of it; she and Hans supply its sea biscuit [MTM B12:387] [MTM B13:446].","citations":["A:6-7","A:31","A:97","A:9","A:16","MTM B5:159","MTM B7:233","MTM B12:387","MTM B13:446"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family","Australia","Ships","Creation","Duffey","Argonauts (Characters)"]},{"name":"Schultz, Mr.","definition":"Form of address used by Salvation Sally for Hans Schultz before she learns that Marie calls him Hanschen [A:9].","citations":["A:9"],"tags":["Identity","Argonauts (Characters)"]},{"name":"Sciocco","definition":"The “maiden name” of the current lead goat Mephistopheles, who is called a dope and is not respected by his flock. The name suggests foolishness in Italian [A:200].","citations":["A:200"],"tags":["Language & Words","Identity"]},{"name":"Scott, Elvira","definition":"Arty friend of the Duffeys during their Chicago golden age [MTM B3:98].","citations":["MTM B3:98"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Scott, Harper","definition":"Earlier heading that omitted the surname Howland. See <em>Howland, Harper Scott</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Family","Minor Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Scott, Harry","definition":"Crewman of the <em>Brunhilde</em>, described as neat and classified among the people who do not belong [D:31]. Anastasia observes him scrubbing his T-shirts and says that what he wants to scrub out is not on the shirts [D:68]. He scatters into Tangier with the rest of the company during the hunt for Don Lewis; Don believes that Harry would kill him if ordered [D:103] [D:106].","citations":["D:31","D:68","D:103","D:106"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Ships","Crime","Africa"]},{"name":"Scott, Mordecai","definition":"Arty friend of the Duffeys during their Chicago golden age [MTM B3:98].","citations":["MTM B3:98"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Seaworthy, Saxon X. (Sindbad, The Old Fox)","definition":"Eccentric millionaire and owner of the <em>Brunhilde</em>, whom Finnegan first encounters in the narrative as a shabby, unshaven man with the shakes, claiming to be a true eccentric [D:9]. They are sitting outside a bar across from a graveyard; neither initially remembers how he arrived there. Seaworthy has a scar or tattoo inside his left wrist and says that Finnegan has the same mark below the skin. Much older than Finnegan, he has a deeply lined, granite-like face whose apparent dullness has a bottomless depth. He speaks of their shared amnesia as a characteristic of changelings, then remembers his name [D:10-12]. He gives Finnegan a hundred dollars, which Finnegan understands as payment for some service, though he does not know whether past or future. When Papa Devil enters the club, Seaworthy suddenly looks so old that the bargirl says he has aged forty years in three seconds; he quickly regains his composure. He later gives Finnegan another hundred dollars and promises their voyage once unexpected business is settled [D:13-17]. Finnegan also perceives a killer's mark on him, distinct from the wrist mark [D:16-17].\n\nSeaworthy proposes a voyage around the world, and Finnegan and Anastasia are entered on the papers of his diesel yacht <em>Brunhilde</em> [D:11] [D:21]. He is associated with Papa Devil, William Gerecke, and Peter Wirt; Anastasia says that these men control the captain and crew [D:24] [D:30] [D:40]. He conducts mysterious business in coastal ports. According to Anastasia, he owns many ships, undertakes illegitimate work, and profits from transporting people as cargo [D:34] [D:55]. His Greek origin, initially suggested by her remark that his X does not stand for Xavier, is explicitly confirmed by the narrator [D:55] [D:115]. He commissions Finnegan to paint the ship's barroom with the voyages of Sindbad. The narrator identifies Seaworthy with Sindbad and stresses the murkiness behind the supposedly upstanding adventurer [D:59].\n\nSeaworthy recounts a gray, Hindi-speaking dream set in India, where he cannot dispose of an unaccountable corpse that eventually asks who will bury him. The narrator confirms that he has dreamed it for three nights and already understands it [D:72-73] [D:77]. It prompts Finnegan to remember their actual first meeting in the graveyard: Seaworthy was burying a man who stirred again, and silenced him with a shovel blow that broke his skull. Finnegan helped deepen the grave, broke into an older burial, and handled its bones; Seaworthy stood over him with the shovel raised but did not strike. They buried the new corpse above the old one [D:74-76]. Finnegan later confirms that the newly buried man was Papadiabolous. Seaworthy is certain that he killed him, yet cannot account for his apparent return to life. Finnegan knows that the man now accompanying them is a different Papadiabolous, a distinction Seaworthy does not understand [D:82] [D:84-85].\n\nX's later account identifies Seaworthy as a halfbrother of the two Papadiabolous brothers and a leader of the mutiny against Papadiabolous I. X reconstructs the killing as a scheme to open their father's grave for valuable information: Seaworthy shot Papadiabolous I with a small-caliber weapon during the digging, before Finnegan's intervention. X acknowledges uncertainty about parts of this reconstruction [D:286-289]. He also attributes the killing of Papadiabolous II on Naxos to Seaworthy, who still failed to understand the substitution. In X's account Seaworthy is a materialist who keeps Finnegan as a witness to the first killing and a reassurance of his own sanity. X lures him back to the grave with forged notes in Papadiabolous's handwriting and predicts that he will die in the trap that night; this is a prediction, disputed by Finnegan [D:289-291].\n\nYears after the voyage, Finnegan tracks the old fox to a gathering in another island city. Seaworthy now has pherea, neck protuberances described as a sign of aging in his species. Finnegan attacks with both Seaworthy's throat and the group's treasury as his targets, but kills Gerecke instead, hearing Seaworthy laugh as he escapes [D:162-165]. The description of Seaworthy as smarter than the Devil belongs here to the narrator; Finnegan later makes the same comparison to X, while X calls Seaworthy smart and able to feign incapacity [D:165] [D:288-290]. <em>Archipelago</em> reports his oath to have Finnegan killed while expressly warning of improbabilities and incompatible versions in these travel accounts [A:187]. When Finnegan later draws his face, he thinks of Seaworthy as an enemy who will have him killed, then asks whether even this prevents their friendship [A:278].\n\nFor comparison, Lafferty's <em>Sindbad, The Thirteenth Voyage</em> develops competing claims to the Sindbad identity. Master Mariner and Master Spy Essindibad Copperbottom, a citizen of Kentauron Mikron, calls himself the original Sindbad; his rival John Scarlatti Thunderson concedes that Essindibad possessed the identity by birth and inheritance before John replaced him through trickery. Essindibad says that his thirteen voyages, including the present one, were diplomatic or espionage adventures on seven planets, whose coded logs accidentally reached Earth as fantastic stories. His wife is Grand-Dame Of-The-Seven-Musics Goodlife Tumblehome, whom he says accompanied four previous voyages, dying and returning on two of them. He instructed spies for the Kentauron Foreign Office, including the later Citizen Heifritz. His present mission begins with a commission to find the reborn Harun and bring him back to Kentauron (<em>Sindbad, The Thirteenth Voyage</em>, PDF viewer pp. 6-7, 10-14, 28-29, 74).\n\nJohn, from Chicago's North Side, uses open-ended analytics to build an Almost-Anything machine and travels to the Baghdad he calls the Arabian Middle Ages, adopting the espionage name Ali ben Raad. Buried alive beside his apparently dead bride Blue Moon, he discovers that she is a mechanical woman who can be revived by winding her mechanism; she subsequently explains that she is a spirit inhabiting it. He describes this as an improved version of Sindbad's Fourth Voyage. He also creates an alternative Kentauron past for himself, including a prior marriage to Grand-Dame Tumblehome, which Essindibad does not remember. Blue Moon invokes a purported Arabian proverb making Sea-Weed growing at the body's shouka, or bifurcation, the proof of the true Sindbad. She removes Essindibad's Sea-Weed and implants it on John, who then claims the identity (<em>Sindbad, The Thirteenth Voyage</em>, PDF viewer pp. 7, 61-74).\n\nThe competing accounts make this criterion unstable. Essindibad calls John false; Scheherazade inspects John's genuine Sea-Weed but calls it an implant and him a forgery. Blue Moon insists that the transplant makes John genuine and that Essindibad cannot regrow it. Scheherazade says that she originally wrote voyages of Slattery the Sailor, named for Joe Slattery of Galveston, and that oral storytellers changed the name to Sindbad. It is Essindibad, commenting on John's account, who says that John's drivel reflects facts upon which Scheherazade builds stories (<em>Sindbad, The Thirteenth Voyage</em>, PDF viewer pp. 78, 82-86).\n\nCaliph Al-Amin commissions Essindibad to inspect and protect hell's iron doors, invoking the Sea-Weed criterion although Essindibad has already lost his growth. A hostile False Ship subsequently tests him, declares him false, and identifies the approaching John as true. Essindibad and his wife survive their captivity aboard that ship; Scheherazade, John, and Blue Moon help defeat their captors, and the party inspects the doors before returning to Baghdad. Mamun's later denunciation of a false Sindbad attacks Al-Amin's commissioned traveler, Essindibad, rather than naming John (<em>Sindbad, The Thirteenth Voyage</em>, PDF viewer pp. 90-110, 118-119). Finally, Essindibad's wife imprisons him in a bottle. His closing journal reports Sea-Weed growing on him again, which he takes as proof that he has regained the identity and John has lost it. His opening account claims a happy retirement on Kentauron, but the closing journal leaves his escape precarious: corrosive fish food is making a hole in the bottle, and he may escape or die. The renewed Sea-Weed does not by itself establish a completed happy ending (<em>Sindbad, The Thirteenth Voyage</em>, PDF viewer pp. 6-7, 149-155).","citations":["D:9","D:10-12","D:13-17","D:16-17","D:11","D:21","D:24","D:30","D:40","D:34","D:55","D:115","D:59","D:72-73","D:77","D:74-76","D:82","D:84-85","D:286-289","D:289-291","D:162-165","D:165","D:288-290","A:187","A:278"],"tags":["The Devil","Devils (Characters)","Ships","Sailing","Crew & Sailors","Ports & Harbors","Maritime Trade","Greece","Middle East","Finnegan","Crime","Combat","Bars & Taverns","Visual Arts","Literature","Money & Trade","Metaphysics","History & Memory","Dreams","Identity","Death & Mortality","Heaven & Hell","Underworld","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Sedalia Schoefeld","definition":"Student at St. Mary Majors who proposes making herself vomit to escape a formal dance. Instead she joins Lily Koch and the others in the house's attic billiard room, where she becomes friendly with John Rattigan [MTM B1:32-33].","citations":["MTM B1:32-33"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Sedgewick, Gideon","definition":"One of the Duffeys' “confidence and gamine friends” in the North Chicago golden age [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Crime"]},{"name":"Seelenfresser","definition":"Unverified term. The earlier glossary attributes Seelenfresser, glossed as soul-eater, to a conversation involving Finnegan and Teufel. That term and conversation have not been located in the supplied novels.","citations":[],"tags":["Angels & Demons","Ecclesiastical","The Devil","Germany","Food & Dining","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Seeress","definition":"Unverified role. The earlier glossary identifies Doll Delancy as a seeress predicting Anastasia’s death by water, but the prophecy and attribution have not been established.","citations":[],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Prophecy & Visions","Women Characters","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Self","definition":"The integrity of the person, threatened in Duffey's understanding when a shadow or double is threatened with destruction. The narrative calls the destruction of a shadow “a mortal wounding of the Self” [MTM B4:141].","citations":["MTM B4:141"],"tags":["Identity","Duffey","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Semangat","definition":"Sulem's name for the soul, which he distinguishes from <em>tujuan</em>, the direction or life assignment nearly everyone receives. He says that everybody has a soul or semangat; Finnegan's missing faculty is his tujuan, not his semangat. Sulem places the soul in the diaphragm [A:60].","citations":["A:60"],"tags":["Language & Words","Finnegan","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Seminary","definition":"The training Henry Salvatore undertakes in preparing for the priesthood. He must first spend at least a year in minor seminary before entering the major seminary in New Orleans [A:125]. While he is studying, others carry on the <em>Bark</em>; his Latin prosody assignment appears near the close of his section [A:143] [A:149]. He is nicknamed the Merry Monk, but is later assigned to a church deep in the Louisiana swamps [MTM B7:214].","citations":["A:125","A:143","A:149","MTM B7:214"],"tags":["New Orleans","Louisiana","Catholic Church","Occupations"]},{"name":"Senescals","definition":"Earlier misspelling of seneschals. See <em>Seneschals</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Creation"]},{"name":"Seneschals","definition":"Attendants of the Boy King Melchisedech. One asks why the boy cannot look at the sun-squirrels he has made, and he replies that he had not expected them to become so bright [MTM B5:157].","citations":["MTM B5:157"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Creation"]},{"name":"Senior Youth World Congress","definition":"Organization supplying fifty selected senior youths to garrote King Hiram III with the entrails of Paul the Eleventh during the Voltaire Festival at Babylonia Bagascia [MB:564].","citations":["MB:564"],"tags":["Popes","Crime","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Serpent (1)","definition":"One of the devices Henry sees on the flags and pennants of the <em>Navicula Petri</em> in his dream, listed with a Woman and a Crescent Moon [A:44].","citations":["A:44"],"tags":["Ships","Dreams","Scripture"]},{"name":"Serpent (2)","definition":"Snakes occur as creatures, figures of speech, and images in Finnegan's travels. The Chosky Bottoms are said to harbor eighteen-foot bull snakes [D:274]; the killers closing on Don Lewis in Tangier are compared to snakes entering a burrow [D:106]; and small snakes inhabit the Naxos ruins [D:68]. The narrative's earthly Paradise still has snakes, which eventually drive Finnegan and his companions out [D:235]. Finnegan's sonnet imagines an ancient sacred snake biting the world [D:133]. The <em>Flying Serpent</em> is a name of the <em>Argo</em> when Willy Jones sails her in the Moluccas [MTM B13:405].","citations":["D:274","D:106","D:68","D:235","D:133","MTM B13:405"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Ships","Finnegan","Literature","Heaven & Hell"]},{"name":"Serpent of the Garden","definition":"Show Boat identifies the Serpent of the Garden with an eight-armed hydra or water monster whose tentacles include Communism and secular liberalism. She claims “special charisma” for the identification in her letter to Dotty [A:262-263].","citations":["A:262-263"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Scripture","Theology","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Seti darts","definition":"Weapon used to petrify men into brown stone statues of Sjahrir, according to Brannagan's tall tales [D:206].","citations":["D:206"],"tags":["Weapons"]},{"name":"Seven Devils","definition":"Devils recur in groups of seven. Show Boat reports Casey's claim that seven devils want to return to him and have seized his press and building for his debts [A:262]. Seven devils more evil than the four senior conspirators attend the meeting Finnegan attacks [D:163]. Giulio says that whenever he ejects his “poor guy's devil,” it returns with seven worse devils [MTM B3:106].","citations":["A:262","D:163","MTM B3:106"],"tags":["Angels & Demons","The Devil","Finnegan","Journalism"]},{"name":"Seven Golden Years","definition":"Alternate name for the Seven Lost Years, used within their own pretertemporal context by those experiencing them [MTM B10:300].","citations":["MTM B10:300"],"tags":["Time & Eternity","Ships"]},{"name":"Seven Just Men","definition":"Righteous drinkers who visit the saloon at Beelzebub and Hell-Raiser Streets in Trinali Peterson's poem about herself, Lot, and Lot's wife. The Brimstone Combo plays there, and the Seven Just Men come for a nightly toddy [A:235].","citations":["A:235"],"tags":["Old Testament","Bars & Taverns","Literature","Music"]},{"name":"Seven Lost Years","definition":"The seven years of Duffey's disappearance from St. Louis, lived aboard the <em>Argo</em> in nonconsecutive time. Intervening land adventures do not count toward either the seven years or the years of his life [MTM B2:71]. The Sea of the Seven Lost Years has scattered channels and shores, some apparently inland; Duffey continues to encounter stray days from it [MTM B13:406]. He can reenter it from shores corresponding to different ages of his life, becoming young again on leaving shore [MTM B13:445]. Within their own context these are the Seven Golden Years [MTM B10:300]. Duffey collects seven representations of Christ during the lost years and sends them back to himself [MTM B3:103-104]. The talisman given to Gabriel Szymansky for his future son belongs to the hidden years; the Wisconsin, Morgan City, New Orleans, Galveston, Chicago, and Australian gifts are placed in those years or possibly earlier [MTM B4:120-121].","citations":["MTM B2:71","MTM B13:406","MTM B13:445","MTM B10:300","MTM B3:103-104","MTM B4:120-121"],"tags":["Time & Eternity","Ships","Duffey","Creation","Visual Arts","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Seven Pillars of Righteousness","definition":"Finnegan's name for the seven figures beneath his charcoal drawing for the <em>Bark</em> masthead: Henry, “The Merry Monk”; Duffey, “Thou art Forever”; Stein, “Absalom to Omega”; Dotty, “The Beautiful Barmaid”; Hans, “Aquinas in a T Shirt”; Gabrielovitch, “Don't Know It in Croat”; and Giovanni, Finnegan himself, “In Absentia” [A:121-122]. They are the founding trustees and directors of the <em>Bark</em> [MTM B7:214].","citations":["A:121-122","MTM B7:214"],"tags":["Journalism","Visual Arts","Finnegan","Duffey","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Seven Roads","definition":"Play by Cleo Mahoney, presented in the Ursuline Academy auditorium. Its seven roads are possible futures for the Patriarch Melchisedech, entered through encounters with seven special potholes in the city; the choices and their order are accidental [MTM B10:293]. Duffey plays his own part, performs real magic at the interval, and plays his banjo and recorder flute [MTM B12:366].","citations":["MTM B10:293","MTM B12:366"],"tags":["Duffey","Theater","Time & Eternity","Music"]},{"name":"Seven Steps Down","definition":"One of the establishments on Chicago's Fleshpot Row praised by Shirley Israel. Rollo McSorley says the row is the only place still offering real Chicago-Hot Jazz [MTM B4:126].","citations":["MTM B4:126"],"tags":["Music","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Seventy","definition":"The number of righteous people Margaret Stone says the Patriarch could not find, even as the required number was reduced. Her street preaching turns the episode into an urgent fear that one more convert may be needed that night to keep the world from destruction [A:144].","citations":["A:144"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Theology","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Seventy-Five Miles","definition":"Approximate distance into the outback from Sydney to the farm of one of Marie Monaghan's uncles, where she and Hans spend several days after their secret wedding [A:31].","citations":["A:31"],"tags":["Australia","Family"]},{"name":"Severed Giant Hands","definition":"Picture hanging over the doorway leading to the Elegant Riverboat Deck at Rounders' Club; an old dream/fantasy of Duffey's (from his dream of the chasm [MTM B2:68]) related to his perceived loss of creative power when near his own powerful \"Animated Marvel\" creations [MTM B5:156].","citations":["MTM B2:68","MTM B5:156"],"tags":["Giants & Titans","Duffey","Clubs & Societies","Dreams","Creation"]},{"name":"Sexton-beetles","definition":"Unverified reference to beetles in the preliminary “Paean” of <em>Archipelago</em>. The supplied copy lacks the required preliminary page, and the earlier glossary’s quotation and identification with <em>Nicrophorus</em> remain unchecked.","citations":[],"tags":["Islands","Minor Characters","Food & Dining","History & Memory"]},{"name":"SFM (Slant-Faced Men)","definition":"See Slant faced boys (Three).","citations":[],"tags":[]},{"name":"Shadow","definition":"In Finnegan’s phrase “shadow life,” the existence remaining to him after his first death. The narrator describes it as release from the unities and the heroic conventions [D:158] [D:161]. See <em>First Death of Finnegan</em> and <em>Shadow life</em>.","citations":["D:158","D:161"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Shadow life","definition":"Finnegan's existence after the first death described at Naxos, distinguished from the later physical death on another island. He vows to devote the shadow life left to him to hunting Seaworthy and his associates [D:157-158]. The narrative says this existence relaxes conventions, particularly the Unities of Aristotle and the Heroics of Plutarch [D:161].","citations":["D:157-158","D:161"],"tags":["Finnegan","Metaphysics","Death & Mortality","Literature"]},{"name":"Shadows","definition":"Doubles or fetches whose relation to their originals varies. Papa Devil identifies Dopey as Finnegan's double, but admits uncertainty about the closeness of their duality; the possibility of ghostly absorption arises in Finnegan's objection [D:121]. Duffey believes that his shadows may become flesh, and visits one in Chicago [MTM B4:141]. Doctor Rafelson distinguishes these shadows from the twelve young people Duffey believes he has made [MTM B4:147]. The thirteen doubles hunted by John Mogul belong to the faithful Cardinals in hiding, who are distinguished from the abdicated Cardinals [MB:578-579].","citations":["D:121","MTM B4:141","MTM B4:147","MB:578-579"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Identity","Finnegan","Duffey","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Shagbarks","definition":"Shagbark hickories (<em>Carya ovata</em>), trees with loose, peeling bark. They appear in one of the “Unwritten poems of Hans Schultz,” whose speaker describes a private aerie shared with God, Germanus, and Gabriel [A:210].","citations":["A:210"],"tags":["Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Shakes Pearson","definition":"Marie Monaghan's uncle, introduced in a joke on Shakespeare's name. She says he was called Shakes because he shook from drinking pop-skull whisky. Trying to win a chewing-tobacco jingle contest, he writes limericks on bark with oak-ball juice, but squirrels eat them whenever he leaves for more drink [A:25]. According to Marie, he eventually kills himself, leaving a note asking Miriam to do something about the squirrels. Her claim that she alone understands the affair is met by Hans's question about why the squirrels spared that last note [A:26].","citations":["A:25","A:26"],"tags":["Literature","Alcohol","Family","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Shakespeare","definition":"English playwright (1564-1616). Marie Monaghan invokes Shakespeare when Hans complains that she will neither scan nor rhyme, then turns the name into a joke about her uncle Shakes Pearson [A:25]. The narration invokes Shakespeare's “Africa and golden joys” while discussing the primordial joy of money [MTM B4:124].","citations":["A:25","MTM B4:124"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Shaman","definition":"Finnegan's term for the person who analyzed him and, in plain language, diagnosed him as a “natural born bum” [A:255].","citations":["A:255"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Shamus McGregor","definition":"One of Marie Monaghan's uncles, introduced as “Shaw” in her series of jokes about literary names. She says they call him Shamus because he is a night watchman for the Royal Imperial Dominion Detective Agency [A:28].","citations":["A:28"],"tags":["Literature","Occupations","Family"]},{"name":"Shandygaff (1)","definition":"A mixture of ale or beer and ginger beer. In the “Antipodal Ode of Finnegan to Loy Larkin,” Loy is said to have drunk it from her mother [A:32].","citations":["A:32"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol","Literature"]},{"name":"Shandygaff (2)","definition":"Drink with which Hans proposes to “spar” for quickness before the drinking contest, before moving to porter for strength [A:18].","citations":["A:18"],"tags":["Alcohol"]},{"name":"Sharion","definition":"The name used by Finnegan when he quotes Psalm 29:6 during his exchange of scriptural lines with Duffey: “Sharion like a young buffalo” [MTM B12:375]. It corresponds to Sirion, the Phoenician name for Mount Hermon.","citations":["MTM B12:375"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Sharivaries","definition":"One of the popular social activities listed among the lively arts of daily life said to have been spoiled after the Devil's release [MTM B6:207].","citations":["MTM B6:207"],"tags":["History & Memory","The Devil"]},{"name":"Shaw, George Bernard (1856--1950)","definition":"Irish playwright, critic, and Fabian pamphleteer, author of <em>Pygmalion</em>, <em>Man and Superman</em>, and <em>Saint Joan</em>; recipient of the Nobel Prize in Literature for 1925, awarded in 1926. Marie's “Shaw” is a joke about her uncle Shamus McGregor [A:28]. Casey's notebook uses Shaw and <em>The Quintessence of Ibsenism</em> to argue for reading original works instead of accounts of them [A:166]. Shaw is among the writers contributing to the ideological mixture in <em>The Answer</em> [MTM B4:114]. The Postscript names him among famous people whom Havelock Ellis regarded as no more innately able than obscure people [PS:476].","citations":["A:28","A:166","MTM B4:114","PS:476"],"tags":["Ireland","Literary Figures","Literature","Theater"]},{"name":"Sheehan, Elmo","definition":"One of the “slippery people” in the Duffeys' Chicago circle [MTM B3:100]. He defends Chicago-hot jazz as “our kind of worthlessness” and is among the Fleshpot Row company from which Duffey eventually withdraws [MTM B4:126-127]. He accompanies Rollo McSorley among the portfolio man's supporters during the attempt to blackmail Duffey into admitting their group to the <em>Crock</em> [MTM B4:140].","citations":["MTM B3:100","MTM B4:126-127","MTM B4:140"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Music","Crime","Journalism"]},{"name":"Sheleno","definition":"The Romany term glossed as “Gypsy green” in Hans's reflections on the color of Marie Monaghan's eyes. It is compared with Greek and French color words as he wonders whether her green eyes have classical precedents [A:24].","citations":["A:24"],"tags":["Language & Words","Literature"]},{"name":"Shenandoah Saga","definition":"One of Endymeon Ellenbogen's three published novels, issued through a subsidy press at his expense [A:237].","citations":["A:237"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Shire, Tom","definition":"Australian soldier whom Finnegan, Vincent, and Hans befriend in Sydney in 1943. Initially large and fair, he and Freddy Castle are said to be unmistakably Australian. Tom explains Australian knife-and-fork habits [A:4-5]. He and Freddy disguise Finnegan and Vincent in winter tunics and digger hats to enter a wet canteen; Tom gives Vincent a disastrous haircut and inspires the anti-Yank affidavit Finnegan signs [A:11] [A:13]. Tom and Freddy are the trusted judges of the Schultz-Bushmaster drinking contest and join the later pub crawl [A:15] [A:20] [A:23]. When the Sergeant revisits Sydney and meets them at the Plaza Hotel, Tom is small and dark instead of large and fair, although otherwise unchanged [A:88]. Finnegan includes him among the familiar people of his normal life and later sketches his face [D:90] [A:278].","citations":["A:4-5","A:11","A:13","A:15","A:20","A:23","A:88","D:90","A:278"],"tags":["Australia","Soldiers","World War II","Bars & Taverns","Finnegan","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Shirley Israel","definition":"One of the “slippery people” in the Duffeys' Chicago circle [MTM B3:100]. She promotes Fleshpot Row and joins the hissing when Demetrio Glauch, then Duffey, leave in disgust [MTM B4:126-127]. Her increasingly potent mixed drink inflames the party that challenges Duffey to move a mule by magic [MTM B4:129-130]. Her salon is reputed to rival the Duffeys', and her circle wants to exploit Duffey's magic [MTM B4:137]. A compromising photograph of her with Duffey is used in the blackmail attempt against him; the narrative calls the encounter an entrapment or badger-game trick [MTM B4:139-140].","citations":["MTM B3:100","MTM B4:126-127","MTM B4:129-130","MTM B4:137","MTM B4:139-140"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters","Alcohol","Music","Crime"]},{"name":"Show-Bill","definition":"Weekly show-business paper printed by the Pelican Press on Friday mornings. The Star and Garter in St. Louis advertises in it until the theatre closes. Teresa produces an issue herself containing an advertisement for the Decatur Street Opera House, whose existence Dotty initially denies [MTM B7:230].","citations":["MTM B7:230"],"tags":["Journalism","Theater","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Shrake, Jessica (Princess of Porcelain)","definition":"Earlier heading that misspelled the artist’s surname. See <em>Shrike, Jessica (Princess of Porcelain)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Visual Arts","Duffey","Minor Characters","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Shrike, Jessica (Princess of Porcelain)","definition":"Artist listed among those whose work Duffey sells in New Orleans, called “that princess of porcelain” [MTM B6:198-199].","citations":["MTM B6:198-199"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Duffey","Minor Characters","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Silas Whiterice","definition":"See <em>Whiterice, Silas</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Literature","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Sindbad","definition":"Voyager of the <em>Nights</em> with whom Saxon X. Seaworthy is identified. Finnegan paints scenes of Sindbad's voyages in the <em>Brunhilde</em> barroom; the narration hints at a darker Sindbad behind the familiar story [D:53] [D:59]. Anastasia becomes the dead wife of the fourth voyage and Marie Courtois the wife of the seventh, who expects a happy ending. Marie says Seaworthy has traveled, traded, and suffered shipwrecks on a still greater scale [D:61-62]. The talking oak aboard the <em>Argo</em> says that Abd-Aliah of the Sea sold the ship to Sindbad of El-Basrah [MTM B13:404].","citations":["D:53","D:59","D:61-62","MTM B13:404"],"tags":["Folklore","Ships","Sailing","Shipwrecks","Finnegan","Visual Arts","Identity","Middle East"]},{"name":"Sindbad Lounge","definition":"Drinking bar aboard the <em>Argo</em>, decorated to suggest sunlit underwater space filled with fish and golden sands. Its oceanic paintings were made by Count Finnegan when the ship was called the <em>Brunhilde</em>, long after Sindbad's ownership [MTM B13:404].","citations":["MTM B13:404"],"tags":["Ships","Bars & Taverns","Visual Arts","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Singulars","definition":"Particular beings considered individually. Don Lewis applies the term to furtive men like himself and asks whether they have been forgotten “behind God's back”: “Does God have knowledge of singulars?” [D:48].","citations":["D:48"],"tags":["Theology","Metaphysics","Identity"]},{"name":"Sinhor Dedos","definition":"Rio alias of Amoy's master Ali hen Taife, also called Lord Peter Petrof and Count von Vinger. Ignatius Ti's story glosses it as “Mr. Fingers.” The master takes the gems Amoy steals and finally cuts the bird's throat to recover a blue diamond it cannot disgorge [A:81].","citations":["A:81"],"tags":["Crime","Identity","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Sirnuka","definition":"People among whom men bear children, according to Biloxi Brannagan’s account. He claims to have witnessed the births; Finnegan gives this story little credit [D:206-207].","citations":["D:206-207"],"tags":["Folklore","Family"]},{"name":"Siros","definition":"Island where, according to Brannagan, small shops sell mermaids' hair brought from Naxos; he names Tinos and Kea as other outlets [D:207].","citations":["D:207"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Islands","Greece"]},{"name":"Sisler, Gretchen","definition":"A sturdy girl, almost nine, who recognizes Duffey's magic in St. Louis and calls herself his fan. She suggests that he make a gold coin rather than sparks, takes the five-dollar piece he produces, and says her mother has just lost her restaurant job. She promises to ensure that they meet again [MTM B2:39]. She later kisses him goodbye with the other visitors at Rounders' Club [MTM B2:70]. In the Chicago years he still loves her and visits St. Louis, while she writes to remind him that she is no longer so young [MTM B4:110].","citations":["MTM B2:39","MTM B2:70","MTM B4:110"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters","Magic & Sorcery","Family"]},{"name":"Sisler, Lucille","definition":"Mother of Gretchen Sisler. After losing a restaurant job, she goes to work at Duffey's Rounders' Club in St. Louis. Though only twenty-eight, she is jokingly called his mother-in-law because Gretchen claims him as hers [MTM B2:39] [MTM B2:49]. Gretchen arranges Lucille's work and dates with Duffey, but the encounters end with Duffey and the daughter together on the family sofa [MTM B3:78]. Lucille and Olga Sanchez take turns carrying Duffey on their shoulders when “The King Shall Ride” is played, and both are his devoted partisans [MTM B2:52].","citations":["MTM B2:39","MTM B2:49","MTM B3:78","MTM B2:52"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Family","Clubs & Societies","Occupations"]},{"name":"Sister Mary Aurora (Rittenhouse)","definition":"One of the ecclesial or “vineyard” people in the Duffeys' Chicago golden-age circle [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Women Characters","Duffey"]},{"name":"Sister Mary Cornelia (Sullivan)","definition":"One of the ecclesial or “vineyard” people in the Duffeys' Chicago golden-age circle [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Women Characters","Duffey"]},{"name":"Sister Mary Merhione","definition":"A nun interviewed in the topless bar where she works, in a clipping preserved in the scrapbook Duffey reads. She defends her work in a “gentle and forgiving voice” and sells reprints of her article “Topless Before God” from <em>Paralplegic Church Today</em> [MTM B8:252].","citations":["MTM B8:252"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Women Characters","Journalism","Catholic Church"]},{"name":"Sister Mary Sabina","definition":"See <em>Sabina, Sister Mary</em>.","citations":[],"tags":[]},{"name":"Sister Marys","definition":"Unverified nickname. The earlier glossary attributes Sister Marys to Marie Monaghan as a name for Hans Schultz’s eyes; the cited passage does not establish that usage.","citations":[],"tags":["New Testament","Women Characters","Theater","Family"]},{"name":"Situation Bit","definition":"Phrase attributed to the preliminary “Paean” of <em>Archipelago</em> in the earlier glossary. The required preliminary page is absent from the supplied copy; both the wording and the proposed connection with situational ethics remain unverified.","citations":[],"tags":["Islands","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Sjahrir","definition":"Name attached to the brown stone statues which, in Brannagan's account, are people petrified by Seti darts and the preparations in which they are dipped [D:206].","citations":["D:206"],"tags":["Folklore","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Skalsky","definition":"Authority cited at the opening of Book Five for the claim that Ukalegon was a woman and in every way the negation of Melchisedech [MTM B5:149].","citations":["MTM B5:149"],"tags":["Literature","Literary Figures","Identity"]},{"name":"Skawine","definition":"Place near Krakow, Poland, mentioned in X's account of Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch Papadiabolous Chortovitch's birth in 1830. X places the birth in a hayfield near Krakow, possibly nearer Skawine [D:278].","citations":["D:278"],"tags":["The Devil","Eastern Europe"]},{"name":"Skinny Art","definition":"Duffey's dismissive description of Edward Ranwick's work. Ranwick has already gained a reputation at art school, but his art leaves Duffey unimpressed [MTM B2:58].","citations":["MTM B2:58"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Skinny Countess","definition":"Description Charlotte Garfield uses for Sebastian Hilton’s companion at the Colony Club, naming her as one of the psychics present [MTM B3:93]. See Countess Margaret, The (Margaret Hochfelsen).","citations":["MTM B3:93"],"tags":["Women Characters","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Skylla","definition":"Greek word used by Anastasia as an insult for Marie Courtois. Anastasia tells Finnegan that she cannot say directly what Marie really is, but that the Greek word is <em>skylla</em> [D:33]. The modern Greek <em>skýla</em> means a female dog and is also an insult for a woman.","citations":["D:33"],"tags":["Greece","Women Characters","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Slainthe","definition":"Irish toast meaning “health,” used by Finnegan when drinking with Casey and Henry after their return through the jungle to the bivouac. He then takes another drink as Solli the Italian and says “salute” [A:44].","citations":["A:44"],"tags":["Ireland","Pacific Theater","Finnegan","Alcohol","Language & Words","Identity"]},{"name":"Slant faced boys (Three)","definition":"Recurring enemies of Duffey, first seen when he is about six. Two years older and larger than he is, they have slack mouths, slanted faces, and knives. They come to the house with the Prisoner John Window intending to kill him, and return to wait for him inside it [MTM B1:12-13]. Duffey later supposes that they caused the house fire by overturning a lamp [MTM B1:14]. At about eleven he escapes their ambush in a timothy field with help from his kit-fox, but is badly knifed and nearly bleeds to death [MTM B1:19-20]. He sees them again on his school roof after they nearly kill Sebastian Hilton [MTM B1:36]. Mary Louise says they are Bagby's principal brawling opponents [MTM B2:62]. Bagby later says that he and Duffey have each killed one, yet three, or many groups of three, remain. Dotty Yekouris reports their appearance in <em>Flame Man</em>, <em>White Avenger</em>, and <em>Captain Justice</em> [MTM B7:225]. <em>Flame Man</em> supplies their names, Amraphel, Arioch, and Thadal; the narrative identifies them as three crooked kings and followers of Chodorlahomor [MTM B7:227-228]. Duffey reports seeing them kill Sebastian and carve the Devil's tetragrammaton on his chest in a detached experience, but his subsequent inspection finds that Sebastian died of plague and that the corresponding marks are sores, not knife cuts [MTM B7:226]. When they threaten Margaret Stone and her singing companions with switchblades at the Majesty's assembly, Quarter rowdies remove the winders from their backs and they collapse with racing gears and stuttering sprockets [MTM B8:244]. Duffey later sees them as the Laughing Prince's bully boys in the desert sequence [MTM B13:440].","citations":["MTM B1:12-13","MTM B1:14","MTM B1:19-20","MTM B1:36","MTM B2:62","MTM B7:225","MTM B7:227-228","MTM B7:226","MTM B8:244","MTM B13:440"],"tags":["Duffey","Monsters & Creatures","The Devil","Weapons","Old Testament","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Slant-Faced Men (SFM)","definition":"See Slant faced boys (Three).","citations":[],"tags":[]},{"name":"Slavic language press","definition":"Press described in Casey's polemic against <em>The Bark</em>. Casey accuses Gabrielovitch of filling it with comments favorable to Draja Mihailovitch and attacks on Tito and the “major Slavic ally” [A:129].","citations":["A:129"],"tags":["Eastern Europe","Journalism","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Slick, Sherman","definition":"Sherman Slick, one of the friends of scientific bent in Duffey and Letitia’s North Chicago circle. The roster lists him immediately before Silas and Maud Whiterice [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Slit Trench","definition":"One of the named valleys in Don Lewis's account of the wartime pursuit involving PFC Lorrigan, Sergeant Santee, and Casper. They know these valleys but become lost in a different, unrecognized one [D:45-46].","citations":["D:45-46"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Sloe gin","definition":"Liqueur made with sloes, the fruit of the blackthorn. In his high-school notebook Casey says that drinking it marks one as an adolescent; he and Hinnegan have moved on to whisky and hot water [A:165].","citations":["A:165"],"tags":["Alcohol"]},{"name":"Sloppy Joe's","definition":"Finnegan and his three companions' joking name for the Imperial Bar, or Royal, at Basse-Terre in their Terrestrial Paradise. The establishment is clean and even fastidious; the friends are Charles Le Marin, Don Barnaby, and Johnny Duckwalk [D:235-236].","citations":["D:235-236"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Caribbean","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Slurry","definition":"Unverified name. The earlier glossary proposes that Slurry is a misprint for Lucille Sisler, Gretchen’s mother. No passage establishes that identification.","citations":[],"tags":["Family"]},{"name":"Smith, Gunboat","definition":"English bulldog whom Duffey trusts to detect the familiar turning strange. Following Father Brown's advice, Duffey brings him from the Old Wooden Ship Tavern in Galveston, where he is Bulldog-in-Residence, to help protect the <em>Argo</em> from the Man of Mystery. Gunboat and Kasmir Gorshok growl at one another [MTM B13:458]. After their failure to stop the future unfolding around the Antichrist, Gunboat speaks about returning and trying again. Rebuked by Brannagan for talking, he gives a final growl and fades out after Brannagan [MTM B13:463].","citations":["MTM B13:458","MTM B13:463"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","Ships","Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Smith, Joe","definition":"Vagabond painter who begins the Dotty O'Toole triptych in Galveston; Finnegan later finishes it. Smith's work has a disturbing excellence, but he never completes anything, and leaves more than a dozen unfinished masterpieces with art dealer Duffey [MTM B6:198].","citations":["MTM B6:198"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Visual Arts","Occupations"]},{"name":"Smokehouse","definition":"Regular at the Old Wooden Ship tavern, grouped with Russ, Bill “Buffalo Chips” Dugan, Bob, and Charleyhorse Heckel [D:215] [D:220]. Smokehouse and Bill work at the same garage. Because the proprietor confuses them, one quits and they thereafter work alternate days under one job and split the pay. This halves their earnings but doubles their time off [D:220].","citations":["D:215","D:220"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Occupations"]},{"name":"Smooch","definition":"Kissing sound in Beth Keegan's playful “telephone operator's kiss”: “Smooch, smooch, smooch, your three minutes are up, please” [MTM B2:60].","citations":["MTM B2:60"],"tags":["Language & Words","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Smyrna","definition":"One of the places where Ifreann Gregorovitch lives in X's account [D:280]. X also identifies it as the birthplace of Gregory McIfreann Papadiabolous I, the Asiatic of the two sons he calls twins [D:285].","citations":["D:280","D:285"],"tags":["The Devil","Devils (Characters)"]},{"name":"Socrates","definition":"Athenian philosopher, conventionally dated about 469-399 BC, remembered for examining ethical questions through conversation and exposing unrecognized ignorance. He left no writings; Plato, Xenophon, and Aristophanes provide differing portraits of him. Tried on charges involving impiety and corruption of the young, he was executed by drinking hemlock. His life and death became enduring models of philosophical inquiry. In <em>Archipelago</em>, Captain Sammy compares the serious, dedicated, kindly Absalom Stein with his idea of Socrates, while Casey rejects Sammy's admiration for Stein [A:67].","citations":["A:67"],"tags":["Greece","Historical Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Socrates (470--399 BC)","definition":"Earlier heading gave an approximate birth year as an exact date. See <em>Socrates</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Greece","Historical Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Solli y Gordonasco, Signor","definition":"Name Gertrude Brannagan uses when calling Finnegan to a telephone call. After he takes it, the caller sounds like a man speaking from a badly ventilated grave. Finnegan recognizes X [D:208].","citations":["D:208"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Solli, Giulio (The Monster Forgotten, Monster Giulio)","definition":"Giulio Solli, father of Giovanni (John) A. Solli, or Finnegan, Giacomo (Jake), and Patricia (Patti); his wife is Mary McCracken [A:173]. Dark, powerful, and carelessly put together, he is the monster who becomes Duffey's first customer. He works on riverboats, ocean ships, and docks [MTM B2:41-43]. Bagby knows him as a troubled visitor who appears about once a year. Giulio asks Duffey for something to help an unborn son and receives a talisman; Bagby associates him with the Gadarene Swine and their song [MTM B2:47-48]. Giulio says a poor man's devil afflicts him and returns with seven worse devils whenever expelled [MTM B3:106]. The Countess identifies him as a Teras, and he agrees. He reports holding the talisman to his wife's belly and placing it in his Italian son's hands at birth [MTM B3:107]. The account of Duffey's talismans says Giulio alone understood from the beginning to apply it at conception and during gestation, then give it to the special son at birth [MTM B4:120-121]. Finnegan is explicitly identified as Monster Giulio's son [MTM B5:161]. Finnegan recalls swamp journeys with him and the extraordinary animal calls he whittled from tupelo-gum: calls for panthers, alligators, devil-fish, swamp-boas, hairy men, and white sharks [MTM B5:174]. Patricia derides Giulio as a poor dock worker and literally antediluvian, reporting that someone called him the Monster Forgotten [A:174-175]. Years after Giulio's death, Finnegan sees him shabby and sad at a railway station and takes him to a beer place. He reproaches his father's work, drinking, poverty, and marriage, then tells him how difficult things became after his death. Giulio silently cries and leaves. Finnegan at last understands that his father loved them and did all he could, while the family derided and possibly killed him [A:244-245]. In <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>, Finnegan reflects that Giulio belonged to a monstrous apparatus and died as the Monster Forgotten [D:229].","citations":["A:173","MTM B2:41-43","MTM B2:47-48","MTM B3:106","MTM B3:107","MTM B4:120-121","MTM B5:161","MTM B5:174","A:174-175","A:244-245","D:229"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","The Devil","Ships","Ports & Harbors","Finnegan","Duffey","The Dead","Music","Occupations","Family","Identity","Death & Mortality","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Solli, Jake","definition":"Giacomo Solli, Finnegan's brother, who has become Jake, a smooth-talking Irishman driving taxis and steering tourists. The narrative says he cares little for his Italian brother except to borrow money and make jokes about him [A:174].","citations":["A:174"],"tags":["Ireland","Finnegan","Money & Trade","Family","Identity","Occupations"]},{"name":"Solli, John A.","definition":"See <em>Finnegan (John A. Solli)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Solli, Patricia aka Patti, Patrish (Patrish)","definition":"Sister of Finnegan and Giacomo (Jake) Solli [A:173]. An acrobatic dancer with striking, weird, comical beauty, she moves with sudden strength like Finnegan's and is often with him. Her intimacy with Doppio di Pinne unsettles Dotty; Patti calls him her brother too and says he is the same person as Finnegan [A:174]. Her accounts of their childhood contradict Finnegan's. She calls their father a poor dock worker and literally antediluvian [A:174]. Only a year older than Finnegan, she says she partly raised him but always felt him a stranger or changeling. She defends him as incapable of wrongdoing and says they came from Irish Channel, despite his claim to a Quarter upbringing [A:175]. Finnegan includes his sister Patty among those friendly to him in his normal life [D:90].","citations":["A:173","A:174","A:175","D:90"],"tags":["New Orleans","Finnegan","Women Characters","Occupations","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Solomon","definition":"Biblical king of Israel, traditionally placed in the tenth century BC, noted for wisdom and wealth and for building the Temple in Jerusalem. First Kings also connects his later conduct with the coming division of his kingdom. In <em>Archipelago</em>, the men of Solomon and Hiram take three years on the journey to Ophir [A:16]. Duffey claims to have sat with Solomon in Kings' conclaves [MTM B5:166]. Bagby invokes Solomon when asking how to choose between good jazz and good conversation [MTM B7:220].","citations":["A:16","MTM B5:166","MTM B7:220"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Duffey","Music","Historical Figures"]},{"name":"Solomon Islands","definition":"Earlier heading confused a sea with the Solomon Islands. See <em>Solomon Sea</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Solomon Sea","definition":"The Solomon Sea appears among the seas the <em>Argo</em> has sailed. The list recalls voyages made by Melchisedech [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Son of God","definition":"X's translation of <em>Bogovitch</em>, the surname carved on the Devil's gravestone. X explains it as a euphemism replacing <em>Chortovitch</em>, “son of the Devil” [D:277-278].","citations":["D:277-278"],"tags":["The Devil","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Son of the Devil","definition":"X's translation of <em>Chortovitch</em>. He says that this name has been softened on the Devil's gravestone to <em>Bogovitch</em>, which he translates as “son of God” [D:277-278].","citations":["D:277-278"],"tags":["The Devil","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Sonnets and Succotash","definition":"One of the nine projected volumes under which Endymeon Ellenbogen has classified his poetry. He says the poetry remains uncollected and unpublished [A:237].","citations":["A:237"],"tags":["Literature"]},{"name":"Sore-Head Gulch","definition":"One of the named valleys in Don Lewis's account of the wartime pursuit involving PFC Lorrigan, Sergeant Santee, and Casper. They know these valleys but become lost in a different, unrecognized one [D:45-46].","citations":["D:45-46"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Sorensen","definition":"One of Papadiabolous I's revolutionary associates in X's account. X says that several members of this group are rumored to be Papadiabolous's half-brothers, without specifying which ones [D:286].","citations":["D:286"],"tags":["The Devil","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Sorrell, Joe","definition":"Cab driver who takes Finnegan and Anastasia to the dock where the <em>Brunhilde</em> is berthed [D:21]. He joins them for a drink aboard [D:22]. Papa Devil orders all three off the ship, and Sorrell drives them away [D:23].","citations":["D:21","D:22","D:23"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","Finnegan","Minor Characters","Occupations"]},{"name":"Soufriere","definition":"Name required for a volcano in the narrator's list of conditions for a Terrestrial Paradise: among its history, climate, ships, and friends, the place must have “a volcano named Soufriere” [D:237]. The preceding account locates the friends at Basse-Terre while emphasizing Finnegan's confusion between it and Basseterre on St. Kitts [D:235].","citations":["D:237","D:235"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Soule, Jacob (Deutero-Finnegan, Finnegan Image)","definition":"Young painter whom Stein calls Deutero-Finnegan. He resembles Finnegan slightly, leaves pictures on consignment with Duffey, and paints work resembling Finnegan's orange period. During the Fifth Contingency he momentarily seems to be Finnegan himself and speaks of Dotty, who disappeared before Soule arrived [MTM B11:343]. Named Jacob Soule, he joins Duffey's party at Girardeau's [MTM B11:344]. Though accepted as splendid and noetic, he voluntarily puts his hands into the testing ashes outside the Opera House. They burn with a yellow, then orange flame; he refuses the Marshal's invitation to withdraw and insists on being human even if he must die for it [MTM B11:347-348]. The thunder colt kills him in the arena, where Margaret Stone remarks on the orange color of his blood before the colt kills her too [MTM B11:351]. After Duffey returns, Soule is dead in the ordinary setting as well, one of at least two deaths brought back from the splendid Devildom [MTM B12:355].","citations":["MTM B11:343","MTM B11:344","MTM B11:347-348","MTM B11:351","MTM B12:355"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Visual Arts","Theater","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Sour John","definition":"See Sourwine, John (Sour John).","citations":[],"tags":[]},{"name":"Sourwine, John (Sour John)","definition":"John Sourwine, called Sour John, is one of the resident regulars at the Old Wooden Ship [D:215-216], celebrated for his storytelling [D:221]. In Don Barnaby's account of the Juju Joy Pill, Barnaby considers Sour John the world's unhappiest man and tries to share his new happiness with him [D:251]. Sour John refuses the pill, preferring a hangover to Barnaby's condition. He takes Barnaby to the ship, straps him into his bunk, and stays until the ship sails [D:252].","citations":["D:215-216","D:221","D:251","D:252"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol"]},{"name":"South Americans","definition":"In Marie Monaghan's comparison of national drinking habits, South Americans drink coffee, like Arabs and Yanks, while English, Chinese, Russians, and good Aussies favor tea [A:7].","citations":["A:7"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Language & Words"]},{"name":"South China Sea","definition":"One of the many seas sailed by the <em>Argo</em> [MTM B13:406].","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Southern Beauty","definition":"Horse-drawn vehicle named by the Royal Pop speaker while describing memories supposedly preserved by an old Decatur Street flagstone [MTM B11:329].","citations":["MTM B11:329"],"tags":["New Orleans","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Southern Comfort","definition":"Whiskey liqueur among the bottles in Finnegan's shoulder-bag when he leaves D'Hannis on foot [A:202]. In the brush that evening he “comforted himself with Southern Comfort,” beginning the unreconstructable 105-hour interval [A:203]. The noon-day devils appear particularly after it and his Old Crow have run out [A:205].","citations":["A:202","A:203","A:205"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Southern Nature's Solitary Boast","definition":"“Our Southern Nature's Solitary Boast,” an epithet for Marie Monaghan in the narrative celebration of Duffey's animated creations [MTM B5:183].","citations":["MTM B5:183"],"tags":["Australia","Women Characters","Duffey"]},{"name":"Spain","definition":"One of the countries where X says an Irish accent in English could result from instruction by Irish nuns or governesses [D:279]. A King of Spain formerly owned the cigar canister into which the slack-faced attendants put Duffey's ashes after his death in the Laughing Prince episode [MTM B13:442].","citations":["D:279","MTM B13:442"],"tags":["Spain","Ireland","Duffey","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Spain, Ira and Rebecca","definition":"Ira and Rebecca Spain, included among the Duffeys' “confidence and gamine friends” in the Chicago period and described with that group as the better grade of confidence people [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Crime"]},{"name":"Spanish Golden Book","definition":"Title of a work Endymeon Ellenbogen says he has translated, alongside the <em>Greek Anthology</em>, Horace, and Petrarch [A:237].","citations":["A:237"],"tags":["Spain","Literature"]},{"name":"Spanish Prisoner Trick","definition":"Confidence trick in which a supposedly wealthy prisoner needs advance money to secure release and promises the helper a reward. Charlotte Garfield tells Duffey that the Devil Released from Prison is an old con of this kind, a grossly magnified Spanish Prisoner Trick; she reports an enormous take in 1946 [MTM B8:260].","citations":["MTM B8:260"],"tags":["The Devil","Duffey","Money & Trade","Crime"]},{"name":"Spanish Stairs","definition":"The Spanish Steps in Rome. In <em>My-Heart-Disconsolate</em>, the severed head of the Cap-Stone Paul the Eleventh is displayed there on a pike before the Last Conclave; it continues to speak while being mutilated by its tormentors [MB:561-562].","citations":["MB:561-562"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Popes","Italy","Ekklesia","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Spanish-Trunk Swindlers","definition":"One class of confidence men who, in Angela Cosquin's account, come from around the world to commission her father's forged treasure maps. His master map shows real and imagined sunken ships; customers choose a ship and location and buy detailed maps and supporting documents [D:259-261].","citations":["D:259-261"],"tags":["Crime","Ships","Shipwrecks","Money & Trade","Family"]},{"name":"Spartan","definition":"The narrative's description of the life led by the group around the Pelican Press. Gabrielovitch does not adopt their Spartan life: because <em>The Bark</em> pays nothing and he likes money, he takes paying work elsewhere [A:126].","citations":["A:126"],"tags":["Journalism","Money & Trade","Occupations"]},{"name":"Speaking Oak of Dodona","definition":"Source of the piece of wood Finnegan tells a fellow traveler he stole from the prow of a ship he once sailed. Asked whether the wood bears writing, he answers, “Not all the time,” and identifies it as part of the Speaking Oak of Dodona [A:244].","citations":["A:244"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Finnegan","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Special Dove","definition":"Prodigious dove with a wingspan exceeding ten feet. Its cote survives aboard the <em>Argo</em> among the relics and identifying objects of the Great Ship [MTM B13:403-404].","citations":["MTM B13:403-404"],"tags":["Ships","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Special Service Press","definition":"Absalom Stein's answer when Jimmy Hansen asks whether he too is press, during their meeting at the Binnacle. Stein agrees to cover the Hans Schultz-Joe Bushmaster drinking championship [A:19].","citations":["A:19"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","Journalism","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Special Services","definition":"Military service with which Sergeant Absalom Stein is associated. He lectures at service clubs and writes for army publications [A:19]. His duties include providing troops with interests and entertainment, and he establishes the small G.I. radio station called the Anopheles Network [A:46].","citations":["A:19","A:46"],"tags":["Soldiers","Journalism"]},{"name":"Sphere","definition":"Casey's high-school notebook gives the volume of a sphere as its diameter cubed, multiplied by pi and divided by six: V = πd³/6. Casey adds that he was the only one who knew it that day [A:163].","citations":["A:163"],"tags":[]},{"name":"Spider","definition":"William Gerecke is described as “wiry as a spider” [D:163]. In Hans's climbing dreams, a giant spider waits in a web inside an immense cave shaft. Its red eyes resemble locomotive headlights and its movement shakes the web with thunder [A:61]. Hans attacks with a Roman sword, which seems oddly late for a creature associated in the narration with an older Greek or Hindu myth [A:62].","citations":["D:163","A:61","A:62"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Combat","Weapons","Dreams"]},{"name":"Spider's web","definition":"Web Hans climbs in a dream to reach a giant spider in a cave shaft. Its threads are cables eight inches thick; before he reaches the web's center, the bottom of the shaft has dwindled to a point and disappeared [A:61].","citations":["A:61"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Combat","Dreams"]},{"name":"Spink","definition":"One word in X's coded telephone sequence, between Vlissinger and Tineo. The recipient evidently has trouble with the codebook, and X says Y has one [D:184].","citations":["D:184"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Splendid Animations","definition":"Name for Duffey's living, talismanic creations, including Finnegan, Hans, Henry, Vincent, Casey, Dotty, Marie Monaghan, Mary Virginia, Teresa Piccone, Absalom Stein, Mr. X, and Mary Catherine Carruthers [MTM B5:161-162] [MTM B8:261]. Mary Catherine has seemed the least of them, despite her closeness to Duffey [MTM B8:261]. The Fourth Contingency says that all have sailed, are sailing, and will continue to sail on the <em>Argo</em>, even when they believe themselves engaged elsewhere [MTM B10:300]. Patrick Plunket challenges Duffey's account of making them, saying he only woke them [MTM B12:391]. The Countess Margaret is separately described as an Animation of someone whose identity remains unclear [MTM B7:215].","citations":["MTM B5:161-162","MTM B8:261","MTM B10:300","MTM B12:391","MTM B7:215"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Finnegan","Duffey","Creation","Identity","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Splendid People","definition":"Name claimed by the supposedly new, noetic species in Duffey's Fifth Contingency. Cyrus Roundhead declares that they are the splendid, final persons and denies that they were ever human [MTM B11:345] [MTM B11:350]. Their presentation at the Decatur Street Opera House includes the slaughter of human victims [MTM B11:349-351]. Duffey returns from his time among these people with a jaundiced view of his city [MTM B12:355-356].","citations":["MTM B11:345","MTM B11:350","MTM B11:349-351","MTM B12:355-356"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Theater","Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Splitting Image","definition":"Term from popular lore invoked by Dr. Saul Rafelson for living images that may have split off from Duffey into independent existence. He treats this as one possibility alongside psychic projections or real people drawn into Duffey's orbit [MTM B4:146]. Rafelson says he has examined two flesh-and-blood images, and distinguishes the twelve young people Duffey believes he made from his “shadows” [MTM B4:147].","citations":["MTM B4:146","MTM B4:147"],"tags":["Duffey","Creation","Identity"]},{"name":"Sponsor","definition":"One of the godparent terms used when the narrative asks whether Duffey's relation to talismanic Casey is more than ordinary sponsorship. It compares that relation with non-species sponsors such as fairy godmothers, without settling whether these are equivalent to a sorcerer's relation to his creatures [MTM B4:119]. Duffey had given Casey's father a talisman before Casey's conception or birth [MTM B4:120].","citations":["MTM B4:119","MTM B4:120"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Language & Words","Family"]},{"name":"Spook place","definition":"A haunted or uncanny place, the sense Finnegan reports Old Pop Potter giving to Chosky Bottoms. Such a place is always just over the next ridge or in the next river valley: home to the man-killing panther, swamp ghost, masked riders, giant coons and fish, Bloody Mulligans, eighteen-foot bull snakes, and the story of the Northern Platoon lost in quicksand [D:274].","citations":["D:274"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Folklore","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Sporting News","definition":"Wednesday sporting paper printed on the Pelican Press, one of the paying jobs supporting <em>The Bark</em> [A:118] [MTM B7:212]. Bello Belonki is associated with it in <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> [MTM B7:213]. <em>Archipelago</em> instead names Berny Cacciatore in connection with the sporting news sheet [A:126].","citations":["A:118","MTM B7:212","MTM B7:213","A:126"],"tags":["Journalism","New Orleans"]},{"name":"Sporting News Sheet","definition":"Description of the Pelican Press sporting paper. See <em>Sporting News</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["New Orleans","Journalism","Occupations"]},{"name":"Sports","definition":"Newspaper department that Jimmy Hansen says should cover the Bushmaster-Schultz drinking contest. A novice crime reporter, he explains that Sports is almost always unavailable so early in the day [A:18].","citations":["A:18"],"tags":["Journalism"]},{"name":"Square mile","definition":"Measure illustrating the extraordinary density of Black Papua. Casey invents the phrase \"Coefficient of Scenic Density,\" and the narration describes a square mile with over fifty waterfalls, eighteen hundred feet of elevation change, pools less than ten yards across and three times as deep, and dozens of miles of caves. Its deliberately extravagant abundance includes a thousand kinds of lizards, a million kinds of birds, and three dozen families speaking three languages, with a fourth tribe possibly still undiscovered [A:36].","citations":["A:36"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Squid","definition":"Animal used by Margaret Stone to characterize Stein, whom the narration calls neither fish nor flesh: she says he is some sort of squid [A:132]. Squids also appear in the shore song Finnegan sings to Elena; she understands their curling as relaxing for the night [A:276].","citations":["A:132","A:276"],"tags":["Language & Words","Music","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Squirrels","definition":"In Marie Monaghan's story of Shakes Pearson, squirrels repeatedly eat his poems, written on bark with oak-ball juice; his last note asks Miriam to do something about them [A:25-26]. Asked whether Australia has squirrels, Marie answers, \"Not that I know of,\" and admits that calling them wallabies would have complicated her story [A:27]. At the dump Finnegan briefly guesses that the unseen small animals might be squirrels, before recognizing rats by their smell [A:177]. Duffey's childhood narration describes coal-black squirrels in the Council Bluffs park, claiming that they occur nowhere else, and contrasts Iowa's red squirrels and St. Louis's gray ones with Boston, where people supposedly do not know what a squirrel is [MTM B1:9] [MTM B1:17].","citations":["A:25-26","A:27","A:177","MTM B1:9","MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Literature","Family"]},{"name":"St. Angela Orba Orphanage","definition":"Chicago refuge Charlotte Garfield uses when she needs to go to ground. She says that all but two of the sisters think she is a little girl; the two who know what she is provide commitment papers and love [MTM B3:86].","citations":["MTM B3:86"],"tags":["Women Characters","Religious Orders"]},{"name":"St. Anthony","definition":"Anthony the Abbot (251-356), an Egyptian ascetic. The narration compares the beautiful, treacherous temptations of this saint with the amazons, female gladiators, Cretan girl bullfighters, and circus acrobats in Casey Szymansky's second imaginary world, adding that they might even be the same girls [A:157].","citations":["A:157"],"tags":["Saints","Women Characters","Dreams"]},{"name":"St. Augustine","definition":"North African bishop and Latin Church Father (354-430). Casey invokes the \"early Augustine\" when challenging Finnegan's attempt to dissociate himself from his sins; the quoted belief that another kind of being sinned within him is a former belief, not Augustine's mature doctrine of original sin [A:154]. Patrick Stranahan recommends reading Augustine once in youth for background [A:109]. In Ignacio's story, Joseph of Maintz carries Augustine's writings in his moon-bound chamber alongside Scripture, the Missal, Jerome, and Pliny [A:271]. The narrator of <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>, rather than Papa Devil, says Augustine seems to refer to the worm in the forbidden fruit [D:264]. Notes near the end of <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> attribute to him the statement that eddying thoughts have no part in the saints' vision of the world [MTM B13:469]. The <em>Confessions</em> (397-400) recount his life and conversion in a sustained address to God. <em>The City of God</em> (413-426) interprets human history through the conflicting loves of God and self. His treatment of original sin, grace, free will and the Church profoundly influenced Western theology. He adapted Neoplatonic philosophy to Christian thought and is venerated as a Doctor of the Church. The former belief invoked by Casey comes from <em>Confessions</em> V.10.18, where Augustine rejects his earlier excuse for sin. The later note appears to echo <em>Summa theologiae</em> I.10.3, which cites Augustine on the saints’ unchanging vision of the Word; the supplied novel reads “world.”","citations":["A:154","A:109","A:271","D:264","MTM B13:469"],"tags":["Saints","Theology","Sin & Redemption","Church History","Patristics","Scripture","Africa","Finnegan","Literature","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"St. Barthelemy","definition":"Name in Angela's list of seven false or shoal passages through the Antilles, places where wrecks piled up or should have piled up [D:260].","citations":["D:260"],"tags":["Islands","Caribbean","Shipwrecks"]},{"name":"St. Benedict of Nursia (c.480--547)","definition":"Author of the <em>Rule of St. Benedict</em>, one of the books on the shelves of Casey’s room at St. Bonaventure’s [A:162]. Benedict (c.480-547) came from Norcia in Umbria. After living as a hermit at Subiaco, he organized monastic communities and settled at Monte Cassino in 529. His Rule joined common prayer, sacred reading, manual work, obedience and care for others in a disciplined communal life. Its moderation and capacity for local adaptation helped it spread; it became the prevailing monastic rule in much of medieval Western Europe. Benedictine monasteries subsequently supported learning, manuscript copying, agriculture, hospitality and missionary work. Benedict is venerated as the Father of Western Monasticism, though he did not found a centralized Benedictine order. Paul VI proclaimed him patron of Europe in 1964.","citations":["A:162"],"tags":["Saints","Prayer & Devotion","Religious Orders","Church History"]},{"name":"St. Bonaventure's","definition":"Boarding school forty miles from Casey Szymansky's home, where he attended high school [A:161]. Its <em>Towers</em>, dominated by boys from Gregory Hall, inspired a rival manuscript paper called the <em>Chambers</em> among the St. Martin's boys; Casey later traced the idea for the <em>Crock</em> to those school papers [A:167].","citations":["A:161","A:167"],"tags":["Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"St. Brandon","definition":"Irish voyager saint, generally known as Brendan (traditionally c. 484-c. 577). <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> attributes an epigraph about unredeemable creatures and long sublunar lives to \"Saint Brendon, <em>Trachtas</em>\" [D:130]. The <em>Argo</em>'s history says he sailed her as the <em>Land of Behest</em> from Ireland to America, encountered the great fish Jascoyne, and carried Judas [MTM B13:404]. Brannagan declares that he was and still is St. Brandon of Ireland [MTM B13:413].","citations":["D:130","MTM B13:404","MTM B13:413"],"tags":["Saints","Monsters & Creatures","Ships","Sailing","Ireland","Identity"]},{"name":"St. Christopher","definition":"Name Ignacio gives for St. Kitts. He calls it a landfall of Cristobal Colon and one of five little Leeward Islands, while Finnegan has his own imagined paradise in mind [A:272]. Finnegan later considers going to St. Kitts but admits that the name is loosely applied, at least by himself, to several islands [D:273-274]. See also St. Kitts (St. Christopher).","citations":["A:272","D:273-274"],"tags":["Islands","Caribbean","Finnegan","Heaven & Hell"]},{"name":"St. Christopher (3rd century? AD)","definition":"Ignacio identifies the island of St. Kitts as St. Christopher [A:272]. The saint’s story supplies background to that name, rather than a biography narrated in the novel. Christopher is a legendary martyr and patron of travelers. In the <em>Golden Legend</em>, the giant Reprobus seeks the greatest lord: first a king, then the Devil whom the king fears, and finally Christ, whose cross frightens the Devil. A hermit teaches him the faith and proposes that he serve Christ by carrying travelers across a dangerous river. One child becomes so heavy during the crossing that Christopher fears drowning. The child reveals himself as Christ and explains that the giant has carried the world and its creator. The name Christopher means Christ-bearer. The legend continues with his preaching, conversions, miraculous survival of tortures and eventual beheading. Accounts place his martyrdom under different rulers, including Decius or Maximinus; the narrative is not secure historical biography. In Western art he commonly carries the Christ Child across water with a staff. His commemoration was removed from the general Roman calendar in 1969, but he remained in the Roman Martyrology and continued to be venerated. Travelers’ medals and prayers reflect that continuing devotion.","citations":["A:272"],"tags":["Saints","Prayer & Devotion","New Testament","Folklore","The Devil","Giants & Titans","Miracles"]},{"name":"St. Cristina's Church","definition":"Chicago parish where, according to Elena O'Higgins, a substitute for Casey Szymansky's destroyed sacred organ music is performed on kazoo, peewee guitar, and musical saw. She calls it a parish working to be the city's trendiest [PG:525].","citations":["PG:525"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Music","Creation"]},{"name":"St. Elmo","definition":"Parish that X calls the nearest to Krakow. He claims to have inspected a record there listing \"The Devil\" as the father of Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch [D:279].","citations":["D:279"],"tags":["Catholic Church","The Devil","Eastern Europe","Family"]},{"name":"St. Francis de Sales","definition":"Catholic writer (1567-1622) and bishop of Geneva from 1602. Finnegan reads his description of Paradise while Le Marin reads over his shoulder and claims to know the place [D:233]. The narration observes that the saint intended Heavenly Paradise, while the two men understood it as terrestrial; Finnegan reaches the wrong Basseterre and thus Paradise by accident [D:235]. A passage on chastity, beginning with its comparison to a lily among virtues, is also attributed to him [MTM B1:27].","citations":["D:233","D:235","MTM B1:27"],"tags":["Saints","Heaven & Hell","Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"St. Gallen, Wisconsin","definition":"Wisconsin birthplace of John Gottfried Schultz (Hans), born January 2, 1915, to John William Schultz and Mary Irene Hayden. Hans remembers it as a place of splendid sunlight and larger summer [A:207]. His father operated a nursery there, and Hans attended St. Joseph's school [A:208].","citations":["A:207","A:208"],"tags":["Family","History & Memory"]},{"name":"St. Henry II","definition":"Finnegan is shriven on the feast of “St. Henry Emperor” in preparation for possible death, and thereafter drinks only a little wine [A:277]. Henry II (c.973-1024) became king of Germany in 1002 and was crowned Holy Roman Emperor in 1014. He strengthened royal authority through the Church, founded and endowed the diocese of Bamberg in 1007, and supported monastic and ecclesiastical reform. His use of bishops as royal officers complicates an unqualified portrayal of him as a defender of ecclesiastical independence. His marriage to Cunigunde was childless; their supposed mutual vow of chastity belongs to disputed later tradition. Pope Eugene III canonized Henry in 1146, and Cunigunde was canonized in 1200. His feast falls on July 15 in the older Roman calendar, as retained in the 1962 missal, and on July 13 in the modern calendar. Catholic devotion presents the couple as examples of holiness within royal and married life.","citations":["A:277"],"tags":["Saints","Church History","Germany","Historical Figures","Sacraments","Finnegan"]},{"name":"St. Henry II (972--1024)","definition":"Earlier heading gave a disputed birth year as exact; the checked account uses c.973. See <em>St. Henry II</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Saints","Church History","Germany","Historical Figures","Sacraments","Finnegan"]},{"name":"St. John Chrysostom","definition":"Church Father (c. 347-407). Finnegan suggests to Le Marin that Chrysostom, or failing him Jerome, might support the idea of Terrestrial Paradise as an island surrounded by four rivers, although such authorities looked in the Near East [D:234].","citations":["D:234"],"tags":["Saints","Heaven & Hell","Patristics","Islands","Finnegan"]},{"name":"St. John of the Cross","definition":"Spanish Carmelite mystic and poet (1542-1591), born Juan de Yepes. Finnegan reads him alongside Father Farrell’s books on the Summa during his late Havana days of Mass, beach walks, sketching and books, recognizing the fleece itself in his reading [A:277]. A chapter epigraph attributed to Wimbish A. McDearmott’s <em>Panegyrica</em> calls John de Yepes an exemplary scientist of Divinity whose direct experimental method is mysticism [D:176].\n\nJohn helped Teresa of Avila reform the Carmelites and suffered opposition and imprisonment. His works include <em>The Ascent of Mount Carmel</em>, <em>The Dark Night</em>, <em>The Spiritual Canticle</em> and <em>The Living Flame of Love</em>, exploring purification, detachment and union with God through poetry and prose commentary. He was canonized in 1726 and declared a Doctor of the Church in 1926.\n\nIn Heaven, Duffey identifies John and Teresa with sixteenth-century Spanish elements in what he thinks is mystical symbolism; Plunket insists that the place is real [MTM B12:390].","citations":["A:277","D:176","MTM B12:390"],"tags":["Saints","Religious Orders","Theology","Mysticism","Spain","Finnegan","Literary Figures","Literature","Duffey"]},{"name":"St. Joseph's","definition":"School in St. Gallen, Wisconsin, attended by Hans Schultz, who rode a pony or walked or ran there. His father John William quarreled with the nuns and Father Baumgartner because he thought the teaching too easy for his son [A:208].","citations":["A:208"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Family"]},{"name":"St. Jude","definition":"Saint whom Dotty Yekouris jokingly claims as patron of printers and little magazines and the only apostle who was a printer. When Finnegan calls him the advocate of impossible causes, she answers, \"Same thing\" [A:115]. Finding his tomb appears among the <em>Argo</em> Masters' discoveries [MTM B13:422]. Christian tradition identifies Jude Thaddaeus, one of the Twelve, with the Thaddaeus named in Matthew and Mark and Judas son of James in Luke and Acts. The New Testament letter attributed to Jude warns against corrupt teachers and urges fidelity. Traditions of his later preaching range through Judea, Samaria, Syria and Mesopotamia, often linking him with Simon the Zealot; accounts of his martyrdom differ, placing it around AD65 in Persia or Syria. He is invoked as patron of desperate or impossible causes. One devotional explanation associates his former neglect with confusion between his name and Judas Iscariot’s; this is a tradition, not an established account of the patronage’s origin. Devotional literature also recounts Bridget of Sweden’s vision directing her to invoke him. His attributes include a club or axe, an image of Christ associated with the Edessa legend, and a flame representing Pentecost. Modern shrines have helped spread this devotion.","citations":["A:115","MTM B13:422"],"tags":["Saints","Prayer & Devotion","New Testament","Folklore","Finnegan","Literature","Ships"]},{"name":"St. Katherine's","definition":"Church from which Duffey obtains a priest to bless his continuously boiling crock of barley soup at the Pelican in New Orleans [A:133]. During the later New Orleans episode, Mary Virginia mentions two Dominicans giving a hellfire mission at \"Ste. Katherine's\" and predicting that their world will end that week [MTM B11:310].","citations":["A:133","MTM B11:310"],"tags":["Religious Orders","New Orleans","Duffey","Food & Dining","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"St. Kitts (St. Christopher)","definition":"Island name associated in Finnegan's mind with an intended paradise. Ignacio identifies St. Kitts as St. Christopher, a landfall of Columbus in the Leewards, which Ignacio therefore calls a holy island [A:272]. Finnegan later considers going there, while admitting that he loosely applies the name to several islands [D:273-274]. Duffey owns a cigar-store Christ from St. Kitts, adapted by an island artist from the cigar-store Indian form, with fingers shaped to hold cigars [MTM B3:103].","citations":["A:272","D:273-274","MTM B3:103"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Islands","Caribbean","Finnegan","Duffey","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"St. Kobarid","definition":"Small kingdom in the Julian Alps, ruled by Hiram III and described as the last nation not yet abolished. Winter snow delays armed abolition there [MB:563]. Hiram is killed at the Voltaire Festival in Babylonia Bagascia, garroted by a youth team using Paul the Eleventh's entrails [MB:564].","citations":["MB:563","MB:564"],"tags":["Fictional Places","Social Class","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"St. Louis","definition":"Birthplace of Vincent Stranahan, on April 5, 1921, and Theresa Antoinette Piccone, on October 15, 1923. Her father owns the Star and Garter [A:94]. Their wedding, planned for the last Saturday of May 1946, brings the Dirty Five and their friends together in the city [A:95]. Hans and Marie have decided to live there [A:97]; Patrick Stranahan and his family occupy the Cat Castle [A:102].<br><br>St. Louis also belongs to one of Duffey’s competing childhoods, whose memories may have been taken from a sister-person. Pecans and gray squirrels are among its remembered details [MTM B1:16-17]. As a young man he buys a former livery stable on Walnut Street and begins his business [MTM B2:40-41]. The account of these early business years is disputed within the narrative; a later account has him leave from below the Eads Bridge aboard the disguised <em>Argo</em> for the Seven Lost Years [MTM B2:71].<br><br>At the later reunion Duffey still has business interests, partners, a sister, and a share of the Rounders’ Club in the city [MTM B5:152]. Patrick has defended Doppio di Pinne there for homicide [A:111], and Bagby reports a recent visit by Giulio [MTM B5:164]. Bagby’s own funeral takes place in St. Louis, after which his weekly letters continue to arrive in New Orleans [MTM B8:237].","citations":["A:94","A:95","A:97","A:102","MTM B1:16-17","MTM B2:40-41","MTM B2:71","MTM B5:152","A:111","MTM B5:164","MTM B8:237"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","Clubs & Societies","History & Memory"]},{"name":"St. Louis Globe","definition":"One of the two St. Louis newspapers Bagby calls the \"Heavenly Twins,\" to which he sends his flamboyant boxing battle statements [MTM B2:50]. Cyrus and Edith Summerfield are society commentators in both this newspaper and its counterpart [MTM B2:65].","citations":["MTM B2:50","MTM B2:65"],"tags":["Journalism","Combat","Social Class"]},{"name":"St. Louis Murrays","definition":"Family to which Charley Murray belongs. His membership explains why he has money during his school years with Duffey and the other boys [MTM B1:24].","citations":["MTM B1:24"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Money & Trade","Family"]},{"name":"St. Louis Post Dispatch","definition":"One of the two St. Louis newspapers Bagby calls the \"Heavenly Twins,\" to which he sends his flamboyant boxing battle statements [MTM B2:50]. Cyrus and Edith Summerfield are society commentators in both this newspaper and its counterpart [MTM B2:65].","citations":["MTM B2:50","MTM B2:65"],"tags":["Journalism","Combat","Social Class"]},{"name":"St. Louis University","definition":"University where Duffey takes courses from the Jesuits in the summer of 1915 [MTM B2:49]. Charley Murray attends as a day student; although he shares none of Duffey's classes, they see much of each other [MTM B2:53].","citations":["MTM B2:49","MTM B2:53"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Duffey"]},{"name":"St. Louis IX (1214--1270)","definition":"Louis IX (1214-1270), king of France from 1226, is the namesake of St. Louis, Missouri. That city is the place where Melchisedech recalls boarding the disguised <em>Argo</em> below Eads Bridge [MTM B13:406]; the king’s life is historical background to the place-name. Louis strengthened royal justice and arbitration, restrained abuses by officials, and helped develop the royal court into specialized institutions, including the Parlement. His 1254 reforms prohibited bribery and regulated punishment, while his moral legislation restricted blasphemy, gambling and interest-bearing loans. He founded or supported hospitals, religious houses and the college of Robert de Sorbon. The Sainte-Chapelle housed the Crown of Thorns and other Passion relics he acquired. Biographical tradition remembers him hearing grievances beneath an oak at Vincennes, feeding the poor and washing their feet; Joinville describes his daily attendance at a Requiem Mass and a sung Mass. Louis led the Seventh Crusade in 1248, was captured in Egypt in 1250, and remained in the eastern Mediterranean after his release until 1254. His second expedition, the Eighth Crusade, ended with his death from illness near Tunis in 1270. Catholic devotion presents him as a model of Christian kingship, but an unqualified portrait of benevolent rule overlooks his coercive religious policies, including the requirement that Jews wear a distinguishing badge. Boniface VIII canonized him in 1297; his feast is August 25.","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Saints","Church History","France","Historical Figures","Social Class"]},{"name":"St. Lucia","definition":"Name in Angela Cosquin's list of seven true passages through the Antilles, spelled \"Ste. Lucia\" in her account of her father's treasure maps [D:260].","citations":["D:260"],"tags":["Islands","Caribbean"]},{"name":"St. Malachy's Church (1)","definition":"Little stone church two blocks north of Duffey's Chicago hotel, which he visits with Charlotte. Its south front has three spread-winged archangels and each of its east and west sides has nine large angels; to Duffey the stone seems alive with wings [MTM B3:87]. Letitia says she has already arranged most of their wedding there, scheduled for the day after tomorrow [MTM B3:92]. They visit the church during their preparations [MTM B3:96], and their marriage is subsequently recorded [MTM B3:98].","citations":["MTM B3:87","MTM B3:92","MTM B3:96","MTM B3:98"],"tags":["Angels & Demons","Duffey","Sacraments"]},{"name":"St. Malachy's Church (2)","definition":"St. Louis church where Bascom Bagby attends Mass, traveling across town because he believes it comes nearest to preserving the true faith amid the clergy's loss of orthodoxy [MTM B2:63].","citations":["MTM B2:63"],"tags":["Catholic Church"]},{"name":"St. Marc","definition":"One of two treasure ships near Angela Cosquin's island, represented on her father's maps as lying one atop the other with the <em>San gre del Cristo</em>. Angela calls the story a fabrication, but the narrator says both ships are real, within three hundred yards of her, and extraordinarily full of gold. She dives to the wrecks and later uses them to safeguard her own money [D:262].","citations":["D:262"],"tags":["Ships","Shipwrecks","Caribbean","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"St. Martin's","definition":"Hall at St. Bonaventure's boarding school where Casey lives in six-boy chambers, each furnished with beds, lockers, desks, and a communal table [A:161]. The hall's boys produce the manuscript <em>Chambers</em> in rivalry with Gregory Hall's <em>Towers</em>; Audifax O'Hanlon draws chamber pots on its mastheads. These school papers underlie Casey's later <em>Crock</em> ventures [A:167].","citations":["A:161","A:167"],"tags":["Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"St. Mary Major's","definition":"Girls' boarding and day school where Lily Koch is both a boarder with a private room and, when she wishes, a day student living with wealthy kindred across the road. She holds private Wednesday-night sales in her schoolroom and public Saturday sales at their mansion [MTM B1:28]. Twenty-five girls from the school are to be escorted by twenty-five boys from Duffey's school to a compulsory dance at the mansion [MTM B1:31].","citations":["MTM B1:28","MTM B1:31"],"tags":["Women Characters","Money & Trade","Social Class"]},{"name":"St. Marys","definition":"Church where Hans and Marie stop for Mass as morning light begins, after talking through the night and walking in the rain during their Sydney stay [A:10].","citations":["A:10"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Liturgy","Australia"]},{"name":"St. Michael","definition":"Saint whom Papa Devil names with God as responsible for worrying about whether the Devil can be killed [D:87]. On the Great Day, Duffey talks with the damaged stone statue of St. Michael at the church in New Orleans. Duffey asks where an oasis can be found; Michael replies that the living water has left this place. One of the statue's eyes has been gouged out [GD:496].","citations":["D:87","GD:496"],"tags":["Saints","Angels & Demons","The Devil","New Orleans","Duffey"]},{"name":"St. Michael's","definition":"New Orleans church Duffey visits at dawn on the Great Day. People are \"unstructuring\" it through faith, and most of the building has fallen. Duffey speaks with the stone St. Michael in the former entry; the statue says the living water has gone and no oasis remains. Its eye has been gouged out, perhaps by an ordinary hammer and chisel, perhaps by their faith equivalents [GD:496].","citations":["GD:496"],"tags":["Catholic Church","Angels & Demons","New Orleans","Duffey"]},{"name":"St. Paul","definition":"New Testament apostle whom Finnegan says never saw an Ocean Island [A:35]. The narration of <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> comically recasts his quarrel with Demetrios over Diana as a dispute over the silversmith's poor workmanship [D:133]. Trinali compares the basket in which Lupido lowers food to her with Paul's basket escape; Hans prompts her to explain that Lupido and Paul were not contemporaries [A:229]. Passages attributed to Paul's Philippians 4:4-9 and Ephesians 6:12-13 are among the original documents inserted into Duffey's story [MTM B5:175]. The Devil contrasts Paul's God with himself as the god of gibberish [MTM B8:243]. The <em>Argo</em>'s history names Paul among those she has carried [MTM B13:404].","citations":["A:35","D:133","A:229","MTM B5:175","MTM B8:243","MTM B13:404"],"tags":["Saints","New Testament","Scripture","The Devil","Ships","Finnegan"]},{"name":"St. Petersburg","definition":"City where X says Papadiabolous I was present on November 7, 1917, aged about eighteen and serving his practical revolutionary apprenticeship [D:286].","citations":["D:286"],"tags":["The Devil","Devils (Characters)","World War I"]},{"name":"St. Philomena","definition":"The name occurs through <em>St. Philomena's</em>, the school Maurine would claim Finnegan attended to get him admitted to a high-school dance [A:101]. Philomena is venerated as a young virgin martyr, but her dates and biography are not established by ancient testimony. Her nineteenth-century devotion followed the discovery of remains in Rome’s Catacomb of Priscilla in 1802. Reordering the tomb’s three inscribed tiles produced the name Filumena. The identification of the remains, the possible reuse of the tiles, and the supposed evidence of martyrdom have all been disputed; the vessel found in the tomb cannot simply be described as proven martyr’s blood. In the later devotional narrative associated with Sister Maria Luisa di Gesù’s claimed revelations, Philomena is a Greek king’s daughter who vows virginity, rejects Diocletian, survives scourging, arrows and attempted drowning through heavenly aid, and is finally beheaded at thirteen. These are elements of her legend. The relics were taken to Mugnano in 1805. Reported cures, including Pauline Jaricot’s in 1835, spread the devotion, and Gregory XVI authorized a local liturgical cult in 1837. August 11 became her traditional feast day. A 1961 liturgical decree removed her feast from calendars; this did not forbid private devotion. Devotees continue to invoke her as a wonder-worker and patron of children, young people and the sick.","citations":["A:101"],"tags":["Saints","Prayer & Devotion","Church History","Folklore","Miracles","Italy"]},{"name":"St. Philomena (c.291--304)","definition":"Earlier heading supplied life dates that are not established by ancient testimony. See <em>St. Philomena</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Saints","Prayer & Devotion","Church History","Folklore","Miracles","Italy"]},{"name":"St. Philomena's","definition":"School named in Maurine Stranahan's proposed excuse for bringing Finnegan to her junior-year high-school dance. Maurine invites him and says that, if challenged by the sister, she will present him as a junior from St. Philomena's and explain his age by calling him 'retarded.' She also wants to tell the other girls that they are engaged [A:100] [A:101].","citations":["A:100","A:101"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"St. Stephen","definition":"Saint named in Casey’s crown decoration. See <em>Crown of St. Stephen</em>; for the Hungarian ruler, see <em>King Stephen of Hungary</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Saints","Identity"]},{"name":"St. Sécaire","definition":"Saint named in the Sword and Scabbard of Saint Sécaire, an anti-prize the <em>Argo</em> Masters deliberately lost three times. At its final concealment they sealed sword and scabbard with molten metals and brimstone, buried them deep in the Nine Mile Depth, and charged the ocean's creatures to keep them lost. Four hundred years later, Cyril and Cyrus Dimbeau recover them in a bathysphere on the morning Duffey and Brannagan go to sea [MTM B13:422].","citations":["MTM B13:422"],"tags":["Saints","Weapons","Sailing"]},{"name":"St. Vincent","definition":"Name in Angela Cosquin's list of seven true passages through the Antilles [D:260]. The voyage poem opening Finnegan's chapter imagines sliding a sloop through a pass at \"St. Vince,\" followed by shining at the Ritz and sleeping on the sand at St. Kitts [A:172].","citations":["D:260","A:172"],"tags":["Islands","Caribbean","Sailing","Literature"]},{"name":"St. Zermatto","definition":"Place Finnegan names, with Chamonix and Guebwiller, in a dispute over prehistoric rock paintings. He says shapes interpreted as birds and butterflies are actually elaborate speech balloons in an original complex language, and includes himself among the Neanderthal artists. Duffey disputes their capacity for speech and writing [MTM B6:193].","citations":["MTM B6:193"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Visual Arts","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Staghorn Christ","definition":"One of Duffey's seven exceptional Christ figures, from Mesa Negra. The narration dates the hard-stone carving nine thousand years before Christ's birth and identifies its figure, impaled on a giant stag's antlers in a crucified posture, as Christ [MTM B3:104].","citations":["MTM B3:104"],"tags":["New Testament","Duffey","Visual Arts","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Star and Garter, The","definition":"St. Louis burlesque theater owned and run by Gaetano \"Papa\" Piccone, father of Show Boat [A:94]. His stock character Hallohan J. O'Hoolihan caricatures Patrick Stranahan [A:104]. Duffey devotes an evening a week to the theater during his St. Louis period [MTM B2:50]; he and friends take Mary Louise Byrne and Beth Keegan there, and the two women sometimes join the skits [MTM B2:58]. Beth later works there, writing skits and playing comic roles [MTM B2:74]. Two of its acrobats attend Duffey's farewell at the Rounders' Club [MTM B2:70]. Piccone recounts creating at least one new character weekly for over thirty years, including Oliver Oscar Omygosh, whom he claims to have brought into actual existence, though Bagby disputes the claim [MTM B5:167-168].","citations":["A:94","A:104","MTM B2:50","MTM B2:58","MTM B2:74","MTM B2:70","MTM B5:167-168"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Theater","Creation"]},{"name":"Star Barrel","definition":"Inscription on churns among the household \"iron words\" in Duffey's childhood memories. Some of these memories come from observation and some from other minds; the passage does not single out Star Barrel as one kind or the other [MTM B1:17].","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Star of the Ninth Fusiliers","definition":"One of the numerous decorations Casey wears in his scarlet Leader regalia when Letitia intercepts him before he can mount the white horse [MTM B4:117].","citations":["MTM B4:117"],"tags":["Identity"]},{"name":"States (the States)","definition":"The United States. Finnegan compares clockwise Australian horse racing with the counterclockwise racing he knows \"in the States\" [A:3]. A bargirl contrasts beer from the states with the Texas beer she serves [D:12]. At Palermo, Finnegan receives his first mail since leaving the States, including letters from New Orleans, St. Louis, and Chicago [D:114-115]. When he is hunted in Basse-Terre, he begins considering a return [D:267].","citations":["A:3","D:12","D:114-115","D:267"],"tags":["Australia","Caribbean","Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Station Hospital","definition":"Military hospital to which the amnesiac Sergeant is returned after the Captain's interrogation breaks him. The Sergeant threatens to remember the Captain's name and find him even in Hell; after being taken back to the hospital and sleeping, he feels better [A:74].","citations":["A:74"],"tags":["Soldiers","Identity","Heaven & Hell"]},{"name":"Ste. Ann Street","definition":"New Orleans street included in the narrator's picture of ghosts converging on a coffee shop before dawn. The narrator immediately retracts the story, retaining only Finnegan's painting of gathering ghosts; the next page explains that the real establishment is named for its proprietor Anthony Ghost [MTM B10:292-293].","citations":["MTM B10:292-293"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","New Orleans","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Ste. Genevieve","definition":"Island associated with the Martyr-Bell. The narrative says English raiders on the <em>Lord Cramner</em> killed thirty-nine Christians there in 1594 for refusing to abjure their faith. After the ship wrecked nearby, islanders recovered bronze cannon and cast the bell, which rang for four hundred years when enemies approached [MB:576]. Islanders later intercept Count Finnegan and instruct him to take it south to the mooring rock, where it is to summon good people rather than warn enemies away [MB:576-577].","citations":["MB:576","MB:576-577"],"tags":["Islands","Finnegan","Ships","Prophecy & Visions","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Stein Collins","definition":"One of the drinks Absalom Stein makes from gin and ice during the Bay Hotel picnic [A:29-30].","citations":["A:29-30"],"tags":["Alcohol"]},{"name":"Stein Julip","definition":"The Stein Julip, one of the fancifully named gin drinks Absalom Stein mixes with ice and bottles of Gilbeys during the group’s Bay Hotel picnic [A:29-30]. Its recipe is not given.","citations":["A:29-30"],"tags":["Alcohol"]},{"name":"Stein the Red","definition":"Name applied to Absalom Stein in the narration of Casey's compulsory \"Reaffirmation of American Values\" course after his stockade term and reduction in rank [A:50].","citations":["A:50"],"tags":["Soldiers"]},{"name":"Stein Zombie","definition":"One of Absalom Stein's gin drinks at the Bay Hotel picnic. Finnegan jokes that its name is descriptive of its great originator [A:29-30].","citations":["A:29-30"],"tags":["Alcohol","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Stein, Absalom","definition":"American Special Service sergeant, publisher, and member of the <em>Argo</em> company, also called A. Stein, Kephos, and Ab-Salom. He comes from Chicago; his identification with Hugo Stone is disputed within the books [A:19] [MTM B5:158] [MTM B9:266] [MTM B7:234].\n\nDuffey remembers Hugo Stone as a boy who visited his Chicago bookstore and insists that Hugo became Absalom. Margaret Stone disputes this, saying that Hugo was a joke name used by several of her cousins and that Absalom’s branch always used Stein. She names twenty Stone boys in four families: David, Hershel, Jacob, Samuel, Max, Nathan, Avram, Yosef, Stuart, Isaac, Myron, Efram, Barnard, Sidney, Joel, Robert, Milton, another David, another Nathan, and another Robert. She says that Absalom was their second cousin and lived six miles north; Myron and Efram, twins, most often played Hugo. Casey denies knowing Absalom before their army service in New Guinea about 1943, although Duffey remembers Casey’s clashes and two lost fistfights with Hugo [MTM B4:111-113].\n\nThe Dirty Five know Stein as a sergeant who lectures in service clubs and writes for army publications. He appoints himself master of ceremonies for Hans’s beer-drinking contest and quickly masters its Dominion Rules [A:19-20]. At the seaside picnic he mixes the Flying Steinmetz, Steinheim Stinger, Stein Zombie, Stein Collins, Ein-Stein, Stein Julip, and Dr. Stein’s Barnacle Remover [A:29-30]. He establishes the Anopheles Network, a small army radio station, and provides entertainment for the troops; Casey writes squibs for it despite hating him [A:46]. Sammy, who teaches alongside him, calls him serious, dedicated, and kind, comparing him to his idea of Socrates [A:67].\n\nCasey identifies Stein with the former Hugo Stone whom the <em>Crock</em>’s warning letter calls an infiltrator [A:40]. When Casey challenges his lectures, Colonel Laycheck admits writing Stein’s speeches and threatens Casey with punishment [A:47]. Stein subsequently teaches the rehabilitation course Casey must attend [A:50]. The Dirty Five and their Australian allies try to overthrow Stein and his patrons by exploiting his apparent love for Tommy Trouncer in the role of Tania. Tommy beats him, and Casey puts him to bed and treats his injuries before reviving the scheme. The resulting raid temporarily lowers Stein’s standing and brings down Laycheck and the three majors [A:51-54]. A later account from Hans reverses the apparent deception: Stein knew Tommy’s identity, helped invent the joke, and saw how to turn it against the officers, though he underestimated Tommy’s blows [A:55].\n\nDuffey says that Stein was born into the Party and is showing signs of leaving it; this reverses Casey’s movement in the opposite direction [A:98]. At the Pelican Press, Stein appears at the typewriter with a publisher’s placard. His arrival helps precipitate Finnegan’s departure, although Stein understands and wishes to help him [A:120]. He is one of the <em>Bark</em>’s seven founding directors and the Seven Pillars of Righteousness, appearing on Finnegan’s masthead as ‘Absalom to Omega. Oh, the big O’ [A:121] [MTM B7:214-215]. His one-shot Bite burlesques the <em>Bark</em> with vinegar and kindness [MTM B6:198]. He later joins Duffey and Gabrielovitch in starting the independent Investigator and writes for both publications [A:143] [MTM B11:315].\n\nHe is a large, owl-faced young man with horn-rimmed glasses and a cigar [A:120]. At the St. Louis gathering Duffey finds him overwhelming, larger than life, and apparently splendidly jeweled although he wears no jewelry; the estimate of size is explicitly Duffey’s [MTM B5:160]. Stein wishes for a more imposing nose and expounds the importance of noble noses [A:127]. His big hands, brains, heart, and gall support an elegant war of interlocking promotions: he remains loyal to the ordered arts and restores form where it has been abandoned [MTM B8:246].\n\nMargaret Stone is his kindred [A:143]. His daughter Rebeka, or Becky, attends Ursuline at his wife’s suggestion, so that her education may become narrower rather than excessively broad. Rebeka takes part in <em>Seven Roads</em> and has known Duffey throughout her fifteen years [MTM B9:266] [MTM B10:294]. After Duffey returns from the Fifth Contingency, Becky grieves for Aunt Margaret, killed by a horse; the narration explains that Margaret is a sort of aunt to her [MTM B12:354].\n\nThe novels keep several accounts of Stein’s origins in play. Duffey regards him as one of the high twelve talismanic creations but admits past uncertainty about this [MTM B5:158-160]. Teresa identifies him as Ab-Salom, Father of Peace, a chandler and provisioner on the Euxine who joined the <em>Argo</em> to protect his investments. In her account Duffey awakens the old company into new bodies rather than creating it from nothing [MTM B7:233-234]. When X denies that Stein could know Rosemary Riorden’s words, Stein answers that to some extent he was that sergeant and that members of the old company partake of one another, though amnesia seals the connection [A:127-128].\n\nStein’s Notes in a Motley Notebook alternates between accepting Duffey’s ancient identity and explaining it as unusual accord with a patron saint. It similarly alternates between treating the released Devil as the Devil himself and as a decoy [MTM B10:291]. In another passage Stein reverses the creation story, imagining that he and the others made Duffey, their beloved moth-eaten magus, before admitting that on other days he believes Duffey made or awakened them [MTM B10:304]. The narrator later allows Duffey some creative role while placing the Marvels among an ancient, interdependent company of <em>Argo</em> Masters; Stein’s precise function in their mutual life remains a question. He supplies the ship with Jew bread [MTM B13:446].\n\nHis attributed writings include Notes on the Finnegan Cycle [MTM B6:185], Notes on the Argo Legend and Further Notes on the Finnegan Cycle [MTM B6:187-188], Notes in a Motley Notebook [MTM B10:291] [MTM B10:304], Notes on the Argo Cycle [MTM B11:330-331], and Notes on the Argo Legend [MTM B11:341]. His account of Finnegan distinguishes reports, apparent deaths, and uncertain identities, and admits uncertainty about the relation of the Argo Legend to the Finnegan Cycle [MTM B6:187-188].\n\nStein calls himself a neo-Jew, perhaps the only one, and refuses Duffey’s suggestion that a gentile could join him [A:131]. Mary Virginia reports his maxim that everything can be described in two words [MTM B11:308]. In the Pop History episode he tries to remember people threatened with oblivion, believing that his recollection might save them, but cannot yet recover their faces [MTM B11:315-316]. He investigates courthouse records, tax rolls, and directories, and questions the supposed antiquity of a newly appeared park [MTM B11:320] [MTM B11:331]. He insists on scientific methods without submitting to accepted scientific content, and questions whether the strange events are happening at all [MTM B11:339].\n\nHis teasing account of the Morning People has a demiurge put them to sleep on a mountain, then accidentally awaken them with the reserved tone of thunder; the child hero recognizes elements of it as true [MTM B11:334]. Stein withdraws with Duffey and Zabotski to examine the events from his apartment [MTM B11:335]. Later he tells Cyrus Roundhead that he mostly likes the Pop Historians’ effect but has reservations [MTM B11:344]. After the return from the Fifth Contingency, Becky worries that her father now regards the truly aware as a separate, superior species and is writing for <em>Gentleman Rounder</em> [MTM B12:355].\n\nIn the future year Duffey visits, Stein appears splendid and noetic, afflicted with the overweening superbity Becky identified. He roars with laughter on seeing Duffey, who is treated there as a ghost [MTM B12:360-361]. When Duffey returns to his own present and asks what was so funny, Stein is taken aback; Duffey then gives a laugh rivaled only by Stein’s [MTM B12:362].\n\nIn the Great Day episode, Duffey first greets him as an old friend of kindred intelligence, then discovers that he is clothed only in smoke and a cigar and has lost his brains. Stein declares brains useful only during the transition and now a handicap; faith and freedom are what matter. His growing cigar, made of faith-tobacco, is to replace his name. The narrator describes his later obesity and his old habit of straight-faced kidding, while leaving him a distant possibility of hope [GD:493-494].\n\nAccounts of the soul trade differ. Stein says that he exchanged an old soul, Hugo Stone the Chicago red, for an old soul of Casey’s, leaving Casey with his later muddled one; Casey is now red and Stein ironically white as dirty Gary snow [MTM B5:169]. Hans’s letter to Marie says that Casey compassionately agreed to go to Hell for Stein and that Stein acquired Casey’s brains and talents while Casey diminished [MTM B6:186]. Stein’s own later commentary says that they exchanged kabouters, interior goblins or fixations, which made each privy to the other’s secrets without greatly changing their relative quantities of goodness and sleaze. He recognizes compassion in Casey’s desire to suffer for another, professes affection for him, and offers to use his access to powerful people to help [PG:537-538].\n\nX’s letter, rather than Stein’s own declaration, tells him that he has seen the burning bush, must wander forty years in the desert, and must remain intelligent and serene [A:265-266]. After a separate letter in which Duffey calls himself the fifty-ninth King of Salem, Stein wonders about his own dynastic number and the possibility of rapport with previous or future bearers of a dynastic identity [PS:478-479].","citations":["A:19","MTM B5:158","MTM B9:266","MTM B7:234","MTM B4:111-113","A:19-20","A:29-30","A:46","A:67","A:40","A:47","A:50","A:51-54","A:55","A:98","A:120","A:121","MTM B7:214-215","MTM B6:198","A:143","MTM B11:315","MTM B5:160","A:127","MTM B8:246","MTM B10:294","MTM B12:354","MTM B5:158-160","MTM B7:233-234","A:127-128","MTM B10:291","MTM B10:304","MTM B13:446","MTM B6:185","MTM B6:187-188","MTM B11:330-331","MTM B11:341","A:131","MTM B11:308","MTM B11:315-316","MTM B11:320","MTM B11:331","MTM B11:339","MTM B11:334","MTM B11:335","MTM B11:344","MTM B12:355","MTM B12:360-361","MTM B12:362","GD:493-494","MTM B5:169","MTM B6:186","PG:537-538","A:265-266","PS:478-479"],"tags":["Argonautica","Finnegan","Duffey","Argonauts (Characters)","Soldiers","Literature","Journalism","Family","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","History & Memory","Identity","Creation","Paradox"]},{"name":"Stein, Rebeka (Becky)","definition":"Absalom Stein's daughter, sent to Ursuline at her mother's suggestion for a narrower education [MTM B9:266]. A fifteen-year-old Jew who has known Duffey all her life, she joins the schoolgirls at the Ghost Coffee Shop and discusses the potholes as symbolic activators in <em>Seven Roads</em> [MTM B10:293-294]. She plays the Countess in the expanded Fifth Road and read Duffey's lines at the rehearsal he missed [MTM B12:352]. She welcomes his name \"Thunder Colt\" for the play's animal [MTM B12:353]. Margaret Stone's death by a reportedly rabid horse devastates her; she satirizes the changed funeral Mass in a verse of her own [MTM B12:354].","citations":["MTM B9:266","MTM B10:293-294","MTM B12:352","MTM B12:353","MTM B12:354"],"tags":["Women Characters","Theater","Family","Liturgy","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Steinheim Stinger","definition":"One of the drinks Absalom Stein makes from gin and ice during the Bay Hotel picnic [A:29-30].","citations":["A:29-30"],"tags":["Alcohol"]},{"name":"Steinmetz","definition":"Charles Proteus Steinmetz (1865-1923), electrical engineer and mathematician. An epigraph attributed to Carmody Overlark includes him among exceptional modern people with supposedly Neanderthal-type heads, described as pumpkin-shaped with bulbous noses and a basal bulge [D:271-272].","citations":["D:271-272"],"tags":["Historical Figures"]},{"name":"Stendhal","definition":"Stendhal, the French novelist Henri Beyle (1783-1842), author of <em>The Red and the Black</em> and <em>The Charterhouse of Parma</em>. The goat-man's younger kids fall asleep during his readings, but the older goats delight in the intrigues of Gina Pietranera, Fabrizio, and Count Mosco, identifying the work as <em>The Charterhouse of Parma</em> [A:198].","citations":["A:198"],"tags":["France","Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Stendhall","definition":"Stendhall is the novel’s spelling of the French novelist’s name. See <em>Stendhal</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["France","Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Stephen","definition":"First Christian martyr or Protomartyr, traditionally a deacon of the Jerusalem church, whose preaching, vision, and death by stoning are recounted in Acts 6-7. He appears with Paul and John the Baptist among the saints aboard Henry's dreamed <em>Navicula Petri</em>; the figures may be dolls or living sailors [A:45]. The Stephens associated with Casey's crown decoration and Duffey's royal conclaves are Stephen of Hungary, a different figure [MTM B4:117] [MTM B5:166].","citations":["A:45","MTM B4:117","MTM B5:166"],"tags":["Saints","New Testament","Dreams"]},{"name":"Stephen, Saint (died c. 34 AD)","definition":"Earlier heading with an uncertain traditional death date. See <em>Stephen</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Saints","New Testament","Church History"]},{"name":"Stevenson, Robert Louis (1850--1894)","definition":"Scottish novelist, essayist, and travel writer (1850-1894), author of <em>Treasure Island</em>, <em>Strange Case of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde</em>, and <em>Kidnapped</em>. Rick Norwood's publisher's note at the back of <em>Archipelago</em> mentions Stevenson's sequel to <em>Kidnapped</em> in its discussion of overlooked and unpublished books [A:285]. The sequel is <em>Catriona</em>, also published as <em>David Balfour</em>.","citations":["A:285"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Stiropen","definition":"Word Casey's school notebook gives as Romany for prison, in a note that also calls English \"stir\" a Romany word [A:164].","citations":["A:164"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Stockton Stone Crocker","definition":"Prince of the Ekklesia who votes for Joseph Cardinal Hedayat of Antioch despite Hackenschmidt's order to elect the straw effigy. As the other princes draw away, Conrad Stone Hackenschmidt kills him with a Browning Automatic Rifle [MB:567].","citations":["MB:567"],"tags":["Ekklesia","Weapons","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Stone, Cooper","definition":"See Howland, Cooper Stone [A:178].","citations":["A:178"],"tags":["Family"]},{"name":"Stone, David","definition":"Name shared by two of the twenty Stone boys Margaret enumerates as first cousins in four Chicago families. She says all lived within three blocks of Duffey's old bookstore [MTM B4:112].","citations":["MTM B4:112"],"tags":["Family"]},{"name":"Stone, Howard","definition":"See <em>Ostwald, Howard Stone</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Ekklesia","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Stone, Hugo","definition":"Contested identity associated with Absalom Stein. Duffey recalls a camera-carrying boy who frequented his Chicago bookstore, addressed him as \"Duffey, baby,\" and later became Stein; Margaret says Hugo was a joke name used by several Stone cousins, most often the twins Myron and Efram [MTM B4:111-112]. Duffey insists that he made Hugo, while Casey denies knowing Stein before their army service despite having twice lost fights with Hugo in the bookstore [MTM B4:112-113]. Duffey later attributes compromising photographs of himself with young girls to Hugo; his blackmailer calls Hugo a clever kinsman who accumulated useful material [MTM B4:139]. During the war, a letter from Duffey warns Casey about a torpedo formerly called Hugo Stone, whom Casey identifies as Stein [A:40]. Casey's later editorial accuses Stein, under that name, of being a card-carrying Communist [A:129].","citations":["MTM B4:111-112","MTM B4:112-113","MTM B4:139","A:40","A:129"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism","Identity","Creation","Crime"]},{"name":"Stone, Margaret","definition":"Chicago member of the <em>Bark</em> circle and kinswoman of Absalom Stein, formerly in the Party and subsequently a Catholic. The narration attributes that conversion's possibility to intricate ethnic reasons and describes her as small, intense, Italian and Jewish, perhaps partly Preadamite, with a large musical voice and no complacence [A:143-144]. She preaches the Real Presence on Dauphin Street at midnight, driven by the urgency of her own recent Pentecost and the fear that she may be responsible for finding the one further soul who will save the world [A:144]. X calls her \"Margaret the bonfire\" in a letter to Stein [A:266]. Duffey knew her as a young bookstore visitor; she was a cousin, not the sister she sometimes claimed to be, of Hugo Stone [MTM B3:99]. Her sharp hearing produces the puzzling report that Bagby asked what his own letter said, though the narration raises the possibility of a misunderstanding [MTM B7:219]. She sings a Gadarene Swine verse about Duffey [MTM B10:305]. In the Fifth Contingency she attends the Pop History sessions with Mary Virginia and Sally, and the women join the hunts against old humans [MTM B11:336]. The Thunder Colt kills her after she jokes about the blood of Deutero-Finnegan; her laugh is all it cannot reach [MTM B11:351]. In the returned world Becky mourns her death by a reportedly rabid horse [MTM B12:354]. In the separate Great Day account she is at the Pelican Press and pushes down a wall, killing a child in the street while crying for freedom [GD:490] [GD:493].","citations":["A:143-144","A:144","A:266","MTM B3:99","MTM B7:219","MTM B10:305","MTM B11:336","MTM B11:351","MTM B12:354","GD:490","GD:493"],"tags":["New Orleans","Women Characters","Sacraments","Prayer & Devotion","Music","Family","Death & Mortality","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Stone, Myron","definition":"One of the Stone cousins in Margaret's account, twin brother of Efram. She says that the pair most often impersonated \"Hugo Stone\" in Duffey's bookstore, making him think one child could move extraordinarily fast and enabling easier theft [MTM B4:112].","citations":["MTM B4:112"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Stone-cutters","definition":"One of Patrick Stranahan's dismissive labels for artists, alongside bronze-casters, scratchers, and egg-white mixers. Answering Papa Piccone's invocation of Buonorroti, Sancho, and Cellini, he concedes that they were good but says they should have remained anonymous and that their names clutter the mind [A:111].","citations":["A:111"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Occupations"]},{"name":"Strabo (c. 64 BC--c. 24 AD)","definition":"Greek geographer and author of the <em>Geographica</em>, an account of the ancient inhabited world including Europe, Asia, and Africa. Finnegan says Strabo had a sense of islands but never saw an Ocean Island, contrasting him with Xavier's knowledge of the greater archipelagos [A:35].","citations":["A:35"],"tags":["Greece","Literary Figures","Literature","Islands"]},{"name":"Stranahan's Theory of the Uncreated Creation","definition":"Vincent's proposal that creation might have occurred only ten minutes ago with all appearances of prior history built in: redshift, expansion, fossils, partly run-down atomic clocks, memories, old resentments, printed books, sports results, and even Finnegan's hangover [A:56-57]. He refuses Casey's objection that the idea is old, arguing that priority does not disprove it. He names it Stranahan's Theory of the Uncreated Creation and mentions a Shadow and Substance motif that he declines to explain [A:57].","citations":["A:56-57","A:57"],"tags":["Metaphysics","History & Memory","Creation"]},{"name":"Stranahan, Hugh","definition":"Second of the four Stranahan sons, brother of Philip, Timothy, and Vincent. He offers Finnegan work as a salesman [A:100]. His bridge-playing friends belong to the lively company at the Stranahan house [A:104]. Philip confirms the brothers' birth order in the 1923 encounter with Duffey [MTM B13:471].","citations":["A:100","A:104","MTM B13:471"],"tags":["Finnegan","Family"]},{"name":"Stranahan, Maurine","definition":"Vincent Stranahan's youngest sister, fifteen and younger-looking during the 1946 wedding visit, described as chubby, freckled, pretty, and lively [A:95] [A:100-101]. She wants Finnegan to escort her to her junior dance and proposes presenting him as a student from St. Philomena's, while borrowing a stage ring from Show Boat to pretend they are engaged. She gives him a piggyback ride and jokingly urges him to marry her [A:101]. She calls the men of her sporting family the Rod and Gun Club and their room the Fish Market, and outdoes their fly-fishing with live bait [A:103]. She is among Finnegan's friends with whom he romps at the wedding reception [A:113].","citations":["A:95","A:100-101","A:101","A:103","A:113"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Fishing","Family"]},{"name":"Stranahan, Monica (Murry)","definition":"Wife of Patrick J. Stranahan and mother of Philip, Hugh, Timothy Navisius, Vincent, Norma, and Maurine [A:94]. She is Charley Murray's sister [MTM B5:162]. During Vincent and Theresa's wedding preparations she worries about the open invitation, Papa Piccone's threatened accordion performance at Mass, Finnegan's behavior, and whether Maurine is meant to be a bridesmaid [A:105]. Finnegan sits on her lap at the reception [A:113]. She attends the Rounders' Club gathering with Patrick, Charley, Papa Piccone, and Theresa [MTM B5:162].","citations":["A:94","MTM B5:162","A:105","A:113"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family","Finnegan","Liturgy"]},{"name":"Stranahan, Norma","definition":"Daughter of Patrick and Monica Stranahan and sister of Vincent [A:94]. Before the wedding, Monica remarks that Finnegan sits on her lap and on Norma's [A:105].","citations":["A:94","A:105"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Stranahan, Patrick J.","definition":"Successful St. Louis lawyer, former magistrate, husband of Monica, and father of Philip, Hugh, Timothy, Vincent, Norma, and Maurine [A:94] [A:102]. He is a large, hairy, rumbling man with a busy stomach, wrestler's shoulders, a blue-emery-paper face, and heavy eyebrows. His wealth is described as lace-curtain and grand-piano Irish richness; the Cat Castle, his large three-story home, was left to him by the elderly Cat Woman, a legal client [A:102]. Called fey, he sees Finnegan as living several lives and perhaps being alien, on a dark road but capable of a happy death [A:102]. He and Papa Piccone imitate one another; Piccone's stage caricature is Hallohan J. O'Hoolihan, which Patrick does not fully recognize as himself. The two friends drink and argue through long evenings [A:104]. Patrick advocates a deliberately limited cultural education, a philosophy short enough for one sheet, select travel and food and drink, and denounces what he sees as the passing of the Church's giants [A:109-110]. He tells Finnegan that Doppio di Pinne, whom he defended for homicide, is the same man despite little physical resemblance and incompatible locations [A:111]. Duffey recalls having given Patrick a gift [MTM B5:154]. At the Rounders' gathering with Monica and Piccone [MTM B5:162], Patrick describes creation as reciprocal: he accepts Duffey's part in making Vincent and others but says he himself consciously helped create Duffey. Father McGuigan objects to the language, while Duffey insists that his own magical creations are literal [MTM B5:165-166].","citations":["A:94","A:102","A:104","A:109-110","A:111","MTM B5:154","MTM B5:162","MTM B5:165-166"],"tags":["Family","Occupations","Finnegan","Duffey","Prophecy & Visions","Literature","Theater","Creation","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Stranahan, Philip","definition":"Oldest of the four Stranahan brothers, whose poker-playing friends visit the family house [A:104]. In the 1923 shore scene Duffey meets him fishing below the Eads Bridge; Philip says he is ten and that Vincent died five days after his birth in April 1921, contradicting Duffey's memories of Vincent's adulthood. He identifies Teresa Piccone as a comical first-grader and invites Duffey to the Cat Castle [MTM B13:470-471]. After Duffey reappears as a young man, Philip mistakes him for the earlier visitor's grandson, about fifty years younger. Duffey then sees a touch of unreality in Philip and boasts that his own ghost is more solid [MTM B13:473].","citations":["A:104","MTM B13:470-471","MTM B13:473"],"tags":["Family","Fishing","Duffey","Ghosts & Spirits","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Stranahan, Theresa Picone (Show Boat)","definition":"Earlier married-name heading, with Piccone misspelled. See <em>Piccone, Teresa (Theresa Antoinette Piccone, Show Boat, Showboat, Teresa Piccone Stranahan, Margie la Maga, La Maga-La Strega)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Women Characters","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Stranahan, Timothy Navisius","definition":"Third of Patrick and Monica Stranahan's sons, between Hugh and Vincent in age [A:94] [MTM B13:471]. He offers Finnegan a place in his ready-mix concrete business [A:100]. His mostly literate acquaintances form part of the household's social circle [A:104].","citations":["A:94","MTM B13:471","A:100","A:104"],"tags":["Family","Finnegan","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Straw Man","definition":"See <em>StrawPeter (Straw Man)</em> [MB:567-568].","citations":["MB:567-568"],"tags":["Ekklesia"]},{"name":"StrawPeter (Straw Man)","definition":"Straw effigy elected Cap-Stone or Top-Peter of the Ekklesia under Hackenschmidt's armed coercion. Six hundred sixty princes vote for it; the six dissenting votes for Hedayat are forcibly counted for it as well [MB:567]. At Hackenschmidt's order Monsignor X burns it. Red smoke fills the hall and chimney, blood-red rain falls inside and out, and blood floods from the stove in quantities far exceeding the lamb's blood used to soak the effigy [MB:568].","citations":["MB:567","MB:568"],"tags":["Ekklesia","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Street Arab","definition":"Narrator's epithet for Margaret Stone, part of a description that combines several ethnic and spiritual kinships [MTM B7:212].","citations":["MTM B7:212"],"tags":["Identity"]},{"name":"Strix Maximus","definition":"Owl name Finnegan explicitly excludes when comparing a buffalo's front view to a giant barn owl. He specifies <em>Strix Pythaules</em>, not <em>Strix Maximus</em>, and chuckles at his own exactitude [A:205-206].","citations":["A:205-206"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Strix Pythaules","definition":"Owl name Finnegan chooses for the giant-barn-owl appearance of a buffalo seen from the front, distinguishing it from <em>Strix Maximus</em> with amused precision [A:205-206].","citations":["A:205-206"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Sturgis","definition":"Inscription on churns among the household \"iron words\" Duffey remembers from childhood. The surrounding account mixes observation with memories taken from other minds [MTM B1:17].","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","History & Memory","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Subiaco Hall","definition":"Hall whose bell tower Casey's school notebook says has a poltergeist. Casey and Hinnegan investigate at midnight without a light on a bet; someone sacks their heads and beats them. Casey insists the attackers were not a poltergeist but that the assault does not disprove one's existence [A:164].","citations":["A:164"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Combat"]},{"name":"Successive Lives","definition":"One of the recurrent fantasies listed by Doctor Saul Rafelson in his notes on Duffey. Rafelson nevertheless accepts that Duffey makes his fantasies happen in the external world [MTM B4:146].","citations":["MTM B4:146"],"tags":["Duffey","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Sudan","definition":"Country named as the source of Duffey's African Christ from Bahr El Ghazal, one of his Seven Christs: a freestanding, life-sized tufa statue of a tall man holding a cattle herder's crooked rod [MTM B3:104].","citations":["MTM B3:104"],"tags":["New Testament","Africa","Duffey","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Sugar Cane Schaeffer","definition":"Nickname for Mary Virginia Schaeffer in the gathering before breakfast at Brennan's [MTM B7:218].","citations":["MTM B7:218"],"tags":["Women Characters"]},{"name":"Sugarland, Texas","definition":"Texas town associated with two brothers formerly married to Dotty Danvers and Little Dotty Nesbitt [D:220].","citations":["D:220"],"tags":["Family"]},{"name":"Suitcase (the chrysus)","definition":"Heavy, shabby suitcase, also called the chrysus or thesaurus, which Finnegan seizes after killing William Gerecke in his attempt on Seaworthy [D:165]. It contains 36,000 pieces of paper, each 2.5 by 6.125 inches, bundled in hundreds, arranged three bundles lengthwise and six across, and stacked twenty bundles high; it weighs eighty pounds. Finnegan guesses it is the world's most valuable suitcase [D:166]. Doll retrieves it from a locker and checks it through to Baltimore, though they leave the bus at Philadelphia [D:171]. She later tells X that it contains a million dollars [D:174]. X takes the claim check from her bag, retrieves the suitcase, and plants a similar one containing 36,000 newspaper pieces as a decoy [D:178]. Brannagan compares its great value with that of his own sea-bag, then fears Gertrude will spread word that it contains over half a million dollars [D:204-205]. <em>Archipelago</em> also alludes to Finnegan's theft of the world's most valuable suitcase [A:188].","citations":["D:165","D:166","D:171","D:174","D:178","D:204-205","A:188"],"tags":["Finnegan","Money & Trade","Crime"]},{"name":"Sulem","definition":"Shore boy and friend of Hans Schultz in the Green Islands who carves heavy black wood. He proposes that Hans rough out a hundred carvings daily with power tools, with his uncles finishing them and girls selling them. Hans rejects the mass-production scheme, but permits rasps, files, and saws [A:59]. Sulem explains <em>tujuan</em> as a person's direction or orders for life and <em>semangat</em> as the soul. He says that tai leaves reveal a person's aura, and that Finnegan lacks both aura and tujuan, though everyone has a semangat [A:60]. To protect Finnegan from ruin he carves a small Grandfather amulet, saying that he and his uncles must dream its appearance. The result resembles a lizard and toad; Finnegan baptizes it Heliogabalus and wears it with his Miraculous Medal [A:60-61].","citations":["A:59","A:60","A:60-61"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Islands","Finnegan","Money & Trade","Dreams","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Sullivan, Sulphur-Bottom","definition":"Resident regular at the Old Wooden Ship in Galveston, Texas, baptized Clarence and always called Clarence by his parents. The narrator calls the origin of his Sulphur-Bottom nickname remarkable but leaves the story untold [D:215-217]. The tavern’s city is explicit in the later <em>Argo</em> account [MTM B13:458].","citations":["D:215-217","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Identity"]},{"name":"Sulphur boats","definition":"One of the kinds of vessel on which Henry Salvatore signs out from New Orleans, Port Arthur, and Galveston, alongside cotton boats, cattle boats, and tankers [A:124].","citations":["A:124"],"tags":["Ships","Ports & Harbors","New Orleans","Occupations"]},{"name":"Sulu Ritz","definition":"Waterfront place named in Duffey's catalogue of remembered establishments [MTM B13:407].","citations":["MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors"]},{"name":"Sulu Sea Five Star Whisky","definition":"Whisky served with stewed oysters to the first people emerging from the ocean, in Duffey's fanciful account of the origin of waterfront establishments and sea stories [MTM B13:407].","citations":["MTM B13:407"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Seas & Oceans","Duffey","Alcohol","Creation"]},{"name":"Suma","definition":"Spelling used for the <em>Summa</em> of Thomas Aquinas when Finnegan reads Father Farrell's books on it, along with St. John of the Cross, during his penitential beach walks [A:277]. The likely reference is Walter Farrell's four-volume <em>A Companion to the Summa</em>.","citations":["A:277"],"tags":["Theology","Finnegan","Literature","Prayer & Devotion"]},{"name":"Summerfield, Cyrus","definition":"Cyrus Summerfield, one of a glittering young couple in St. Louis high society who also comment on that society for the Globe and the Post Dispatch. Cyrus and Edith accept Beth Keegan’s invitation to a formal dinner in a balloon gondola two miles high; Edith is described as a sort of cousin of Beth [MTM B2:65].","citations":["MTM B2:65"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Journalism","Family"]},{"name":"Summerfield, Edith","definition":"Edith Summerfield, one of a glittering young couple in St. Louis high society who also comment on that society for the Globe and the Post Dispatch. Cyrus and Edith accept Beth Keegan’s invitation to a formal dinner in a balloon gondola two miles high; Edith is described as a sort of cousin of Beth [MTM B2:65].","citations":["MTM B2:65"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Journalism","Family","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Sun-Drake","definition":"Name invoked by Duffey in his delight at the seemingly bright noontime of his life with Letitia: he says it would be wonderful even to be dead in such light, though he speaks a couple of hours after midnight [MTM B7:211].","citations":["MTM B7:211"],"tags":["Duffey","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Sunday","definition":"Day apparently represented by Bownee, first in the goat-man's sequence of seven hebdomadally named kids [A:194]. The <em>Brunhilde</em> reaches Naxos on Passion Sunday, March 14 [D:115]. Elena Musquiz finds and rescues Finnegan in the scrub brush very early on a Sunday morning [A:206].","citations":["A:194","D:115","A:206"],"tags":["Ships","Islands","Greece","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Superundate","definition":"Word in Casey Szymansky's school notebook discussion of <em>surround</em>. Casey says that surround is a short doublet of <em>superundate</em>, meaning to be completely covered as with waves, rather than a relative of round. On that reading he argues that a lake, rather than an island, is a body of land completely surrounded by water [A:165].","citations":["A:165"],"tags":["Islands","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Surabaja","definition":"Place in Java whose rope-jumping children instantly know the fuzzy-cubs verse as John Mogul recites it, just as children in West Chicago know it in their own tongue [MB:578].","citations":["MB:578"],"tags":["Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Surround","definition":"Word in Casey Szymansky's school notebook discussion of <em>surround</em>. Casey says that surround is a short doublet of <em>superundate</em>, meaning to be completely covered as with waves, rather than a relative of round. On that reading he argues that a lake, rather than an island, is a body of land completely surrounded by water [A:165].","citations":["A:165"],"tags":["Islands","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Susiana","definition":"Name equated with Elam in the account of Chodorlahomor's realm, described as a Devildom east of the Tigris. Duffey recalls Abraham confronting the four kings with only 318 men and says he blessed Abraham [MTM B7:228].","citations":["MTM B7:228"],"tags":["Old Testament","Duffey","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Swanson, Big Swede","definition":"Resident regular at the Old Wooden Ship in Galveston, Texas and one of its five so-called Swedes. The narrator says none of the five is actually Swedish, but does not assign their individual nationalities [D:215-217]. The tavern’s city is explicit in the later <em>Argo</em> account [MTM B13:458].","citations":["D:215-217","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Sailing","Identity"]},{"name":"Swede Aansen","definition":"Resident regular at the Old Wooden Ship in Galveston, Texas and one of its five so-called Swedes. The narrator says none of the five is actually Swedish, but does not assign their individual nationalities [D:215-217]. The tavern’s city is explicit in the later <em>Argo</em> account [MTM B13:458].","citations":["D:215-217","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Sailing","Identity"]},{"name":"Swede Bergen","definition":"Resident regular at the Old Wooden Ship in Galveston, Texas, one of five men called Swedes though none is actually Swedish. Unlike the other four, Bergen sailed only once: he was so seasick that he swore never to leave land. His wife also refuses to cross the Atlantic because of childhood seasickness; for twenty years each has written begging the other to cross [D:215-217]. The tavern’s city is explicit in the later <em>Argo</em> account [MTM B13:458].","citations":["D:215-217","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Sailing","Seas & Oceans","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Swede Bjonk","definition":"Resident regular at the Old Wooden Ship in Galveston, Texas and one of its five so-called Swedes. The narrator says none of the five is actually Swedish, but does not assign their individual nationalities [D:215-217]. The tavern’s city is explicit in the later <em>Argo</em> account [MTM B13:458].","citations":["D:215-217","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Sailing","Identity"]},{"name":"Swede Knistjanses","definition":"Resident regular at the Old Wooden Ship in Galveston, Texas and one of its five so-called Swedes. The narrator says none of the five is actually Swedish, but does not assign their individual nationalities [D:215-217]. The tavern’s city is explicit in the later <em>Argo</em> account [MTM B13:458].","citations":["D:215-217","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Sailing","Identity"]},{"name":"Sweeny, Enniscorthy","definition":"Musical friend of the Duffeys during their Chicago golden age, named with Mary Sweeny among their composers, players, and stagers. [MTM B3:99] An editorial interruption before an alternate conclusion calls him ruddy-faced and a near-genius. It quotes his comparison of an event to a box whose past, present, alternative present, and future are different, equally valid views: accepting one must not cancel another. [MTM B13:467]<br><br>He is the protagonist of “The Three Armageddons of Enniscorthy Sweeny,” the second part of <em>Apocalypses</em>, which gives his dates as 1894-1984. Widely recognized even by strangers, he has a famously difficult-to-describe, ruddy, peeling face and a Midwestern Irish voice. His apparent age and size resist ordinary comparison, and his handling of time makes his records impossible to arrange securely in sequence. The same box-and-event passage appears in this portrait. Within the novel's alternative twentieth century, Doctor Henry Devonian's November 1910 formulae make Sweeny the unique answer to the Particular Equation, the individual on whom the era's reality depends (<em>Apocalypses</em>, PDF pp. 199, 201, 203-205, 219-221, 233, 236-243).<br><br>His portrait balances genius against “spacious bungler.” A genuine genius on the flute and exceptionally gifted on other instruments, he reaches near-genius or occasional unquestionable genius in cartooning, drama, opera, film, art, and competitive sport despite supposedly lacking the necessary killer instinct. He is also an excellent craftsman and stone carver. His journalism is often vivid but defective: he sometimes omits results rather than impose an outcome, and insists on recording everything because things have not truly happened until written down. His many businesses flourish under his animating presence and sell brilliantly, but buyers discover shambles once that presence departs; he nevertheless has ready money from childhood. His apparent failures as a prophet are entangled with conflicting versions of events and his disregard for ordinary sequence (<em>Apocalypses</em>, PDF pp. 205-219, 372).<br><br>His works include the <em>Tyrrhene Triptych</em>, comprising <em>Armageddon I</em>, <em>II</em>, and <em>III</em>; the play <em>The Great Depression</em>; essays such as “On the Construction of the World”; the novel <em>Secret Agents Six</em>; and the late farce <em>My Name is Sweeny and I'm in Trouble</em>, in which a younger actor plays the Sweeny who represents Everyman. Sweeny calls his catastrophic works scapegoats intended to carry horrors away from the world. Their effects are dangerous nonetheless: the narrative reports suicides after performances of <em>Armageddon III</em> and the spread of enormous horror-for-its-own-sake cults. Whether his fictions produce the wars or might prevent them remains disputed, even as their influence and his involvement become overwhelming (<em>Apocalypses</em>, PDF pp. 214-216, 265, 320-324, 340-341, 356, 375-378).<br><br>His wife is Mary Margaret McGronsky Sweeny. He brings about her extraordinary beauty and then her preeminence as an opera singer before he wins her, after five dangerous years; their subsequent marriage lasts in almost perfect amity for another sixty-five years. Other early episodes show the extent and instability of his influence. His mother's dated account records President Harold Standpipe crediting the seven-year-old Ennis with his rise to office. A boyhood wish is fulfilled when Sweeny knocks out the reigning heavyweight champion amid startling changes of apparent size and injury. At twelve he preaches at St. Malachy's, performs poltergeistic feats, and makes the clock run backward through a day before the retreat master removes him from the pulpit (<em>Apocalypses</em>, PDF pp. 207-211, 221-231).<br><br>Alistar Grogg, Wimbish McDearmott, and Elton Quartermas infer from the Devonian equations that killing Sweeny might prevent catastrophe. Grogg dies before his first intended attack but continues the pursuit as a ghost. Sweeny hires six detectives to protect him from these three and from the opposing Friends-of-the-Catastrophes, code-named Marshals Mosco, Tosco, and Rosco, who insist that he continue producing his desolating visions. He regards the mathematicians as friends trying to save the world and the marshals as friendly enemies. The protectors and threats eventually live with him, thirteen persons in the Royal Assassins-and-Detectives Club, also called by Mary Margaret the Old Mens' Menagerie (<em>Apocalypses</em>, PDF pp. 233-245, 319-324, 338, 357-358).<br><br>Detective Branagan counters Grogg's psychic infestation by bringing his bones into Sweeny's home, the safe center of the psychic storm, and making a portable locket containing bone shavings, spirit essence, and charms. Sweeny's gift for illusion also diverts an assassination onto Ellis Swinton. The thirteen Drayman's Alley murders over forty years all substitute victims for Sweeny; each dead face acquires his likeness, although Flanagan cannot establish who makes the resemblance permanent. An invisible spiked collar initially tortures Sweeny when he resists the marshals. Later it is put on another person during repair, and Sweeny feigns its effects while the unknown substitute suffers (<em>Apocalypses</em>, PDF pp. 322-323, 334-340, 345-346, 350-357).<br><br>Near the end, one explicitly possibly subjective account gives nearly a thousand consecutive days of maximum length, prolonging 1983 and echoing Sweeny's “The Three-Year-Long Year.” He withdraws into a hollow tree, calling himself the scapegoat and an impediment to the final resolution. Comfortable there and spending much time in prayer and prophetic gestures, he resists Mary Margaret's appeals to come down. He has long engraved 1984 on his own tombstone and predicts death when Achilles catches the rabbit in his version of Zeno's paradox. When Ultimate Armageddon arrives and 1984 is finally allowed to begin, people burn his tree: seven dark spirits sink into the ground, and a golden spirit flies away whistling music from his old <em>Prohibition, A Farce</em>. This is the recorded end of the “old and almost forgotten prestidigitator” (<em>Apocalypses</em>, PDF pp. 372-383).","citations":["MTM B3:99","MTM B13:467"],"tags":["Duffey","Literary Figures","Music","Visual Arts","Literature","Theater","Journalism","Crime","Money & Trade","Magic & Sorcery","Ghosts & Spirits","Apocalyptic","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","Identity","Death & Mortality","Paradox","Creation","Family"]},{"name":"Sweeny, Mary","definition":"Musical friend of the Duffeys during their Chicago golden age [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Music"]},{"name":"Sweeny, Newbold","definition":"Earlier heading attached Newbold to the preceding surname Sweeny in a list. See <em>McGeehan, Newbold</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey","Music"]},{"name":"Swiss movements","definition":"Uncased Swiss watch mechanisms among the goods Hillary Hilton says he buys and sells [A:255].","citations":["A:255"],"tags":["Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Sword and Scabbard of Saint Sécaire","definition":"Dangerous anti-prize that the <em>Argo</em> Masters repeatedly hide. They weld sword and scabbard together with iron, lead, brimstone, and babbet metal, then bury them a hundred meters into the ooze of Nine Mile Depth, where they remain four hundred years until Cyril and Cyrus Dimbeau recover them by bathysphere [MTM B13:422]. Duffey warns that drawing the sword an inch will put two-thirds of humanity into a fatal sleep unless it is resheathed, while drawing it completely will end the world. The scientists experiment and ten assistants fall asleep [MTM B13:423]. Brannagan reseals the sword with the molten flux; Duffey drops it back to its old hiding place, and Brannagan covers the party with the Forgetfulness Mesh [MTM B13:424].","citations":["MTM B13:422","MTM B13:423","MTM B13:424"],"tags":["Saints","Ships","Seas & Oceans","France","Duffey","Weapons","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Sydney","definition":"Australian city where the wartime friends socialize with Marie Monaghan, Salvation Sally, Tom Shire, Freddy Castle, and their circle [A:3] [A:8-14]. Hans Schultz and Marie marry secretly in its Annunciation Church on 15 February 1943 before the soldiers return to New Guinea [A:31]. Sydney also appears in the Sergeant's uncanny journey out of the hospital ward: he visits the Plaza Hotel and drinks with Tom and Freddy, though Tom's appearance has changed [A:88].","citations":["A:3","A:8-14","A:31","A:88"],"tags":["Australia","World War II","Soldiers","Family"]},{"name":"Sydney Town","definition":"Phrase in the “Antipodal Ode of Finnegan to Loy Larkin,” where Loy's hair lends grace to Sydney Town [A:32]. In an introspective exchange aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em>, Finnegan recalls one beginning of his world as walking through Sydney with Vincent Stranahan during the war years [D:91].","citations":["A:32","D:91"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Literature","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Syman, Casey","definition":"Name of Casey's imagined great athlete in the record books he writes as a boy. His mother mocks the name and the projected special edition for his eightieth birthday in 2001, then tears up the records [A:156-157].","citations":["A:156-157"],"tags":["Family","Identity","Creation"]},{"name":"Syrinx","definition":"Panpipe possessed by Gautier the goat-man, who is described as collateral with Pan. He plays several syrinxes as well as bagpipes, producing much of the ghost music of the wasteland and swamps [A:197].","citations":["A:197"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Music"]},{"name":"Szymansky, French","definition":"Earlier inverted heading obscured that this is Casey’s imagined label for Balzac. See <em>French Szymansky</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Literature","Identity","Creation"]},{"name":"Szymansky, Gabriel","definition":"Chicago pawnbroker and antique dealer, husband of Miriam Lessing and father of Kasmir W. (Casey) Szymansky [A:153-154]. His rich-street antique shop and poor-street pawnshop stand back to back, connected beneath an alley; he is wealthy but his stinginess leads Casey to suppose the family poor [A:160]. Father and son have a natural antipathy, though Casey learns Polish and German from him [A:156]. Duffey joins Gabriel's business, adding book and art shops; Gabriel owns the building where the Duffeys live [MTM B3:81-82] [MTM B4:118]. Before Casey's birth Duffey gives Gabriel a talisman for him, during the hidden years [MTM B4:120]. Duffey later sells medals left from Gabriel's pawnshop [MTM B4:115]. Casey inherits enough from his father's estate never to run out of money [A:168]; the division of partnership property between Casey and Duffey causes friction despite legally sound papers [MTM B5:149].","citations":["A:153-154","A:160","A:156","MTM B3:81-82","MTM B4:118","MTM B4:120","MTM B4:115","A:168","MTM B5:149"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Family","Duffey","Magic & Sorcery","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Szymansky, Kasmir W. (Casey)","definition":"Kasmir W. Szymansky, called Casey, born in Chicago on October 7, 1921, the only child of Gabriel Szymansky, a pawnbroker and antique dealer, and Miriam Lessing [A:153-154]. One of the Dirty Five, he is identified with Peleus among the Argonauts and with the ninth-century scholar and necromancer Kasmir Gorshok, Casey the <em>Crock</em> [A:38-39].<br><br><em>Archipelago</em> presents his life as he tells it to Finnegan, adjusted by Finnegan’s knowledge of him [A:153]. The problem of evil is his overriding concern [A:154]. His twelve childhood dream worlds concern sports, erotic adventures, fortress religion, literature, exploration, a secret higher ethic, languages, art, revolution, worldly success, sadistic fantasies, and revenge. He recounts these dreams to Finnegan; they are imagined lives and ambitions, not twelve separately established universes [A:156-161]. His invented athlete is named Casey Syman [A:156-157].<br><br>Duffey tells Casey that he helped form him through a talisman given to Gabriel before Casey’s birth. Casey remembers holding the object from birth until six months old and confirms that it is solid gold [MTM B5:151]. For other roles and related accounts, see <em>Crock (4) (Casey the)</em>, <em>Prince Casimir</em>, <em>Zodiac, Casey of the</em>, and <em>Casey Machine</em>.","citations":["A:153-154","A:38-39","A:153","A:154","A:156-161","A:156-157","MTM B5:151"],"tags":["Argonauts (Characters)","Identity","History & Memory","Dreams","Literature","Family","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Sécaire, St.","definition":"Saint named in the Sword and Scabbard of Saint Sécaire, the dangerous anti-prize whose recovery the <em>Argo</em> Masters must undo [MTM B13:422-424].","citations":["MTM B13:422-424"],"tags":["Saints","Weapons"]},{"name":"T Shirt, Aquinas in a","definition":"Caption for Hans Schultz in Finnegan's charcoal masthead of the <em>Bark</em>: Hans is Aquinas in a T Shirt, one of the Seven Pillars of Righteousness listed beneath the endangered boat [A:121-122].","citations":["A:121-122"],"tags":["Finnegan","Visual Arts","Journalism","Theology"]},{"name":"T. (initial)","definition":"Letter Angela Cosquin says her father puts on the chest symbol in forged treasure maps, beside alternatives K and BP. Buyers can be led to think the initials refer to Teach, Kidd, or Bartolomo Portuguese, while believing they discovered the connection themselves [D:260].","citations":["D:260"],"tags":["Crime","Money & Trade","Family"]},{"name":"Ta'a nuts","definition":"Protective nuts in Don Lewis's Willy Jones Island story. Ali takes two before entering the Big House, whose residents prolong their lives by eating visitors' organs; he plants one outside and keeps the other in his pocket [D:48]. The retained nut prevents his killing. After he barricades his room and snaps his fingers three times to make morning, the planted nut has become a tree with a branch at his window, allowing his escape [D:49]. Anastasia says that of course they all carry such nuts [D:49].","citations":["D:48","D:49"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Immortality","Islands","Literature"]},{"name":"Tagalog","definition":"Language Finnegan tries to speak to Falo and Nauti at their newly opened cabaret, asking about price and then asking their names again because these are the only two sentences he knows. Hans and Casey can speak it [A:64]. Captain Guiterrez is separately described as a Tagalog [A:66].","citations":["A:64","A:66"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words","Soldiers"]},{"name":"tai leaves","definition":"Leaves Sulem says are chewed to gain second sight: the seer perceives a person's aura and through it the direction of the <em>tujuan</em>. Sulem says Finnegan has neither aura nor tujuan [A:60].","citations":["A:60"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Taine","definition":"Name of the source from which the narration calls Finnegan of the Two Lives a story and character, as he feels alien to himself while meeting Dotty at the St. Louis station [A:92]. Stein later associates Finnegan with the <em>Yellow Book of Lecan</em> and the <em>Táin Bó Cúailinge</em>, proposing identities with Fingal and Cú Chulainn [MTM B6:187-188].","citations":["A:92","MTM B6:187-188"],"tags":["Ireland","Finnegan","Literature","Identity"]},{"name":"Talaud Islands","definition":"Islands at four degrees north passed by the Dirty Five on their voyage to Luzon after the war. Hans tells them that Chinese birds'-nest soup comes from these coral and limestone islands and gives an account of seabirds building nests from regurgitated fish and limestone [A:62-63]. During the remembered beautiful night offshore, Casey calls their vessel the <em>Argo</em>, for that night at least [A:63].","citations":["A:62-63","A:63"],"tags":["Ships","Seas & Oceans","Islands","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Talisman","definition":"<p>Object through which Duffey helps form or awaken a person. One recipient describes a talisman as a flat, engraved gold stick about an inch wide and eight inches long, bearing the designated person’s being, encounters and destiny. The narrator says that a child usually absorbs it after holding it for days. Duffey bestows twelve primary talismans, though not every talisman belongs to that group [MTM B4:120]. His childhood talisman sticks receive lightning during his contest with the clouds [MTM B1:10-11].</p><p>At about three years old he gives his first talisman to an Italian confectioner with a pushcart, to bring about the first member of the Duffey Nation [MTM B1:18-19]. Other gifts go to Charley Murray and Sebastian Hilton [MTM B1:35], to Giulio Solli for an unborn son [MTM B2:47], and to Gabriel Szymansky for Casey. Charley’s is intended for his sister’s child [MTM B4:120]. Casey remembers holding his own from birth until six months old and confirms that it is solid gold [MTM B5:151]. A separate, rib-shaped talisman passes through Lily Koch to her sister Letitia, Duffey’s future wife; Letitia is explicitly additional to the twelve primary creations [MTM B1:29-30] [MTM B3:90-93].</p><p>John Rattigan, refused a talisman, makes a convincing copy and tricks Duffey into holding it. Duffey warns that, if it works, it will work wrongly [MTM B1:23-24]. Dr. Rafelson calls Duffey’s claims fantasies but also acknowledges evidence that his creations may become persons with real histories [MTM B4:146-147]. The account of how these objects work therefore includes disagreements among Duffey, his recipients and his interpreters.</p>","citations":["MTM B4:120","MTM B1:10-11","MTM B1:18-19","MTM B1:35","MTM B2:47","MTM B5:151","MTM B1:29-30","MTM B3:90-93","MTM B1:23-24","MTM B4:146-147"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Family","Identity","Creation"]},{"name":"Talking dolphin","definition":"Ancestral dolphin who communicates with Bolo Manolo, Douglas McAfee, and Lloyd Cardigan-Pembroke through a coded apparatus lowered from their yacht. It knows the Testament, Pact, and Covenant and says the dolphins expected to receive the Pact as the most intelligent and obedient creatures but accepted humanity's selection. Other dolphins are towing their bell, said to be the world's largest, toward Mooring-Stone; Lloyd tells it to ring [MB:594].","citations":["MB:594"],"tags":["Theology","Creation","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Talking Oak of Dodona, The","definition":"Talking wood set into the <em>Argo</em>'s wheel among its relics, which recounts the ship's history when questioned [MTM B13:403-404]. When X asks it to recognize him, it says it does not know him and questions whether he appears in Scripture or Inscription [MTM B13:434-435]. <em>Archipelago</em> names the Speaking Oak of Dodona in Finnegan's account of a piece he stole from a ship's prow [A:244].","citations":["MTM B13:403-404","MTM B13:434-435","A:244"],"tags":["Ships","History & Memory","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Talking Oak of Dordona, The","definition":"Earlier heading misspelled Dodona as Dordona. See <em>Talking Oak of Dodona, The</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Ships","History & Memory","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Tall Tree","definition":"Destination toward which Vincent and Finnegan start after their visit to Falo and Nauti's makeshift cabaret [A:66].","citations":["A:66"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Taller star than Vega","definition":"Phrase attributed to the “Paean” in the earlier glossary. The earlier glossary cites the opening “Paean” of <em>Archipelago</em>, page ix. That preliminary page is absent from the supplied copy, so the reading remains unverified.","citations":[],"tags":["Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Tampa Reefers","definition":"Distressed lot of 300,000 dried, flaking, unbranded and unwrapped cigars that Hilary Hilton plans to buy in Cicero. He proposes a light coat of tar and new wrapping, branding, and boxing, then a four-cent selling price. He estimates a cent and a half profit per cigar [A:253].","citations":["A:253"],"tags":["Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Tangier","definition":"Place where Stein says Finnegan did not die with Don Lewis, despite two bodies at the bottom of the tell. This is one of the apparently incompatible deaths examined in Stein's notes on the Finnegan cycle [MTM B6:187-188].","citations":["MTM B6:187-188"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Africa","Finnegan","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Tania","definition":"Female stage persona of Tommy Trouncer, a tow-headed Australian lance corporal with a double voice. His comrades dress him as a woman to audition for the Coconut Grove show, and the short-sighted Stein falls in love, believing their story that a brigadier has brought Tania to the island for sexual purposes [A:51]. Stein wants to rescue her and share a better, cleaner world with her; Casey recruits Tommy into a larger plot against Stein and his superiors [A:52]. Hans later says that Stein recognized the impersonation from the outset, originated the gag, and used it to bring down the colonel and majors. The narrator leaves a final doubt about whether the other Dirty Fivers really remained unaware [A:55].","citations":["A:51","A:52","A:55"],"tags":["Australia","Soldiers","Theater","Identity"]},{"name":"Tankersley (moustache)","definition":"Name of a moustache worn by an Englishman known to Le Marin, mentioned in Le Marin's conversation with Finnegan and Hildegarde Katt about the name of Tommy Katt's moustache [D:229].","citations":["D:229"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"tao-bunduk","definition":"Term Nauti uses for Finnegan, glossed in her speech as a mountain creature and an older creature. She tells Vincent that his friend is something other than a man, one of the beings found in stories [A:64].","citations":["A:64"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Tarkington, Dorothy","definition":"Young woman in Duffey's St. Louis circle, among the friends who swim in summer, sled in winter, and tour and celebrate together through the seasons [MTM B2:60].","citations":["MTM B2:60"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Tartarin of Tarascon","definition":"Alphonse Daudet's 1872 novel. In the goat-man's account of the Harlow House library, it is one of the French books loved by his goats and read aloud to them, along with <em>Gil Blas</em> and <em>The Count of Monte Cristo</em> [A:197-198].","citations":["A:197-198"],"tags":["France","Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Tartary","definition":"Region visited by the <em>Argo</em> Masters while investigating the Laughing Prince. The narrative says it has been under the New Infidels for generations and that Tartary is no longer an official name; inhabitants profess to know no prince except the First Secretary of the Oblast [MTM B13:438]. At Our'yev, at the east mouth of the Ural, Duffey is killed, stripped of flesh, and the flesh cremated [MTM B13:436].","citations":["MTM B13:438","MTM B13:436"],"tags":["Ships","Duffey","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Tasmania","definition":"Distant sea island named in Finnegan's rhapsody about defeated primordial aliens. Some, he says, retreated to remote places such as Tasmania, while others hid in the bloodlines of their conquerors and continued the struggle from within [D:214].","citations":["D:214"],"tags":["Islands","Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Tattoo","definition":"Scar or tattoo on the inside of Seaworthy's left wrist. He claims Finnegan bears the same mark below the skin, though Finnegan denies having it [D:10]. Later Finnegan recognizes on Seaworthy a mark that is not actually a mark and is not merely the wrist marking, something his clairvoyance has revealed on other people [D:16].","citations":["D:10","D:16"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Tauric Chersonese","definition":"Name X gives as the Greek name of Crimea in his account of the Devil's imprisonment. X distinguishes a Roman Euxine Chersonese and Gothic Cherson, says the imprisonment began in 946 or 947, and describes the peninsula as then divided between Kiev and Tmutarakan, which he calls an advance host of the Golden Horde [MTM B6:204]. His account continues with the Devil's release after a thousand years [MTM B6:205-206].","citations":["MTM B6:204","MTM B6:205-206"],"tags":["The Devil","History & Memory","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Teach, Edward","definition":"Pirate Edward Teach, called Blackbeard (c. 1680-1718). Angela Cosquin explains that the letter T on her father's forged treasure maps could subtly suggest Teach to a credulous buyer [D:260].","citations":["D:260"],"tags":["Crime","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Techne Iatrike","definition":"Galen's <em>Art of Medicine</em>, cited as the source of the Chapter Eleven epigraph in <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>. The epigraph describes Tityroi or Satyroi as children of daemons and human women, outwardly close to humans but hostile to them; it says only the first generation is fertile, requiring periodic renewed begetting by daemons [D:160-161]. Galen lived in 129-c. 216 AD.","citations":["D:160-161"],"tags":["Literature","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Teilhard de Chardin, Pierre","definition":"French Jesuit priest and paleontologist (1881-1955) who brought evolutionary science into his religious vision. His posthumously published <em>The Phenomenon of Man</em> presents human evolution toward Omega. In “Great Day in the Morning,” a satirical transfiguration at St. Michael's casts Teilhard as a holy demiurge, flanked by McLuhan and McGonigal, blessing the Unfaithful Assembled with a string of technical-sounding words [GD:497].","citations":["GD:497"],"tags":["Religious Orders","Theology","Historical Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Teleportation (book on)","definition":"Book left by the former sculptor occupant of Endymeon Ellenbogen's room. Endymeon buys the already-present marble block and carves nereids from it, then discovers that neither block nor finished group could fit through the doors or windows. He cuts the sculpture apart to remove it; the teleportation book is the final clue in his story [A:239].","citations":["A:239"],"tags":["Literature","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Telescope making and lens grinding","definition":"Minor arts included in the narrative's catalogue of practices stripped of style and class during the great trashing of the arts [MTM B6:207-208].","citations":["MTM B6:207-208"],"tags":["Occupations"]},{"name":"Temple (of the Jews)","definition":"Stein's figure for the Jewish religious inheritance in his exchange with Duffey. When Duffey urges him toward the Church if he has left the Temple, Stein says that his grandfather left it, that he himself has never personally been in it, and that he will stand within it and require the Church to come to him there. He nevertheless says his genes prevent him from joining [A:131-132].","citations":["A:131-132"],"tags":["Theology","Duffey","Identity","Family"]},{"name":"Temple, Clement","definition":"One of the money-and-commerce people in the Duffeys’ Chicago circle, listed with Irene Temple. Their relationship is not specified [MTM B3:100].","citations":["MTM B3:100"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Ten minutes ago (theory)","definition":"Vincent Stranahan's Theory of the Uncreated Creation: the universe might have begun ten minutes ago with redshift, fossils, carbon balances, partly run-down atomic clocks, historical records, memories, and even dreams already built in. Casey objects that the idea is not new; Vincent insists that prior invention does not disprove it. Finnegan counters that the world may not yet have begun at all [A:56-57].","citations":["A:56-57"],"tags":["Metaphysics","History & Memory","Dreams","Creation"]},{"name":"Ten thousand items at reduced prices!","definition":"Sign Duffey paints in red on a board from a horse stall for his converted livery-stable establishment in St. Louis. It advertises food and lodging, convivial whisky, wine and beer, shaves, haircuts and baths, continuous entertainment, and a gentlemen's bar and club under his proprietorship. The narrator remarks on his perfect lettering and the thousands of assorted items in the stable [MTM B2:41].","citations":["MTM B2:41"],"tags":["Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Food & Dining","Visual Arts","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Tent","definition":"Small tent Gautier sets on a grassy spine in a New Jersey swamp. He spreads another pallet for Finnegan, and only the seven smallest goat kids are allowed to sleep inside with them [A:193-196].","citations":["A:193-196"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Tent shows","definition":"Popular art included in the narrative's catalogue of practices stripped of style and class in the trashing of the arts [MTM B6:207-208].","citations":["MTM B6:207-208"],"tags":["Carnival","Theater"]},{"name":"Teras","definition":"Term the Countess applies to Giulio Solli instead of Troll, which Giulio himself confirms [MTM B3:107]. Finnegan believes himself half Human and half Teras, but the later narration says that only narrow vision makes them essentially different: they are among the intermingled peoples of God [MTM B5:181].","citations":["MTM B3:107","MTM B5:181"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Identity","Theology"]},{"name":"Terrestrial Paradise","definition":"Earthly Paradise given exact bearings in the novel: 61 degrees, 44 minutes, 42 seconds west and exactly 16 degrees north [D:234], fulfilling the promise in the Promantia [D:6]. Reading St. Francis de Sales prompts Finnegan to seek it; Le Marin offers to guide him, and they go with Don Barnaby and Johnny Duckwalk [D:233]. The narration identifies the place as Basse-Terre in the Antilles, distinguishing it from Basseterre on St. Kitts, where Finnegan had intended to go. He reaches the earthly Paradise by accident, although St. Francis meant the heavenly one; snakes ultimately drive the company away [D:235]. <em>Archipelago</em> says Finnegan knows its exact location [A:188], while he later describes St. Kitts as a paradise he may or may not have visited [A:272].","citations":["D:234","D:6","D:233","D:235","A:188","A:272"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Caribbean","Finnegan","Islands"]},{"name":"Terry Anderson","definition":"One of the Forty Scribbling Seamen using the name Terry who wrote for the <em>Seaman's Paper</em> [MTM B7:213].","citations":["MTM B7:213"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Journalism","Identity"]},{"name":"Terry Bannon","definition":"One of the Forty Scribbling Seamen using the name Terry who wrote for the <em>Seaman's Paper</em> [MTM B7:213].","citations":["MTM B7:213"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Journalism","Identity"]},{"name":"Terry Cork","definition":"One of the Forty Scribbling Seamen. He writes The Plimsoll Line for the <em>Bark</em> and about half the <em>Seaman's Paper</em> when in town; another Terry takes over when he leaves [MTM B7:213]. His four-line tribute to Dotty asks whether hers was the face that launched a dozen tugs and assorted other boats, and the form that drove Frenchtown wild and darkened the Quarter [MTM B7:214].","citations":["MTM B7:213","MTM B7:214"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","New Orleans","Journalism","Literature"]},{"name":"Terry Jamil","definition":"One of the Forty Scribbling Seamen using the name Terry who wrote for the <em>Seaman's Paper</em> [MTM B7:213].","citations":["MTM B7:213"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Journalism","Identity"]},{"name":"Terry Renier","definition":"One of the Forty Scribbling Seamen using the name Terry who wrote for the <em>Seaman's Paper</em> [MTM B7:213].","citations":["MTM B7:213"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Journalism","Identity"]},{"name":"Tertium Organum","definition":"Form of the title <em>Tertium Organum</em> printed in the Book Eleven epigraph and attributed to P. D. Ouspensky. It asks how reality becomes phenomenal and how a created world can be uncreated [MTM B11:306]. The corresponding passage occurs in Chapter XXII of Ouspensky's book within his quotation of Max Müller's discussion of Vedanta.","citations":["MTM B11:306"],"tags":["Literature","Metaphysics","Creation"]},{"name":"Tertium Organus","definition":"Tertium Organus is the form printed in the novel; the book’s published title is Tertium Organum. See <em>Tertium Organum</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Literature","Metaphysics","Creation"]},{"name":"Terwilliger, Major","definition":"One of the three livid majors, with Meyerhofer and Twicherby, present when Colonel Laycheck threatens Casey for challenging Stein's lectures. None is a line officer [A:47].","citations":["A:47"],"tags":["Soldiers"]},{"name":"Tetteraglots","definition":"Plural form of the word Angela Cosquin uses for her father, whom she calls a tetteraglot from childhood, a speaker of four languages. She links this to his expertise in archaic spelling and handwriting when fabricating old treasure maps [D:260].","citations":["D:260"],"tags":["Family","Language & Words","Crime"]},{"name":"Texas beer","definition":"Only cold drink available when Finnegan and Seaworthy enter the newly opened bar after sitting across from the graveyard. Seaworthy condemns the beer in mock-biblical language, saying they asked for a drink in Christ's name and were given this [D:10] [D:12].","citations":["D:10","D:12"],"tags":["Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Thadal","definition":"King of the Nations or Goyim, one of the Three Slant-Faced Men whose names Duffey discovers in the <em>Flame Man</em> comic strip. With Amraphel and Arioch he is called a crooked king and an anti-magus without magic [MTM B7:227]. The narration identifies the three as followers of Chodorlahomor of Elam or Susiana, a connection not given in the strip [MTM B7:228].","citations":["MTM B7:227","MTM B7:228"],"tags":["Old Testament","Duffey","Literature"]},{"name":"Thalassocratic Adventure","definition":"Phrase for the wartime military and sea campaign that the narration leaves outside its account. Its little iron insects have been eating up bird-shaped New Guinea before the company moves four hundred miles beyond the Vogelkop to a small Dutch island [A:50].","citations":["A:50"],"tags":["World War II","Seas & Oceans","Islands"]},{"name":"Thanatos","definition":"Name Stein gives Finnegan's sought-for fleece, contrasting <em>thanatos</em> with <em>mallion</em> in his account of Finnegan's repeated search for death without finding it [MTM B6:187-188].","citations":["MTM B6:187-188"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Thatcher, Hugh (the Yellow Kid)","definition":"Artist known as the Yellow Kid whose work Duffey sells during the New Orleans rebuilding project, among the local and distant artists contributing to that effort [MTM B6:198-199].","citations":["MTM B6:198-199"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"The Analyst and the Essence of Man","definition":"Theme connecting analysis of Duffey with the difficulty of locating a person's essence. An unnamed analyst concludes that Duffey was physically, mentally, and psychically a girl for several childhood years; Duffey pays and leaves, while the surrounding narration describes his appropriation of other minds [MTM B1:16]. Doctor Saul Rafelson accepts that Duffey's fantasies become external realities, examines two of his independent images, and recognizes his sorcery and possible world-inversion; he ultimately refuses to follow the implications further because he is frightened [MTM B4:146-148]. Finnegan tells Duffey of a powerful analyst who dissects a person and finds no centralized essence, only an empty jug and its wrappings. Yet the scattered parts glow, suggesting essence diffused through them; the analyst dies before investigating further. The narration applies this to Finnegan's essence, which cannot be bottled in one place [MTM B5:181-182].","citations":["MTM B1:16","MTM B4:146-148","MTM B5:181-182"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Metaphysics","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"The Answer","definition":"Newsletter sent to about three hundred prominent Chicago citizens near the end of 1931, issued on Monday, Wednesday, and Friday. It covers economics, ethics, government, education, religion, and militarism with a startling, quippy style [MTM B4:113]. Its mixture of Augustine, Aquinas, Pope Benedict, Nietzsche, Shaw, the Webbs, Machiavelli, and both Roosevelts alarms Duffey, who finds much of it intelligent but fears its appeal to followers. The second issue expands from four to sixteen pages and proclaims that the Leader has been found [MTM B4:114]. Friday's issue announces the Appearance in Henry Horner Park [MTM B4:116]. Ten-year-old Casey secretly prints it on Duffey's press. Letitia intercepts the costumed Leader, prevents Casey's promised ascent to power, and takes him to enjoy the park festivities without his regalia. The paper does not reappear; Casey later calls it a hoax, but Letitia knew his intention was serious [MTM B4:117-118].","citations":["MTM B4:113","MTM B4:114","MTM B4:116","MTM B4:117-118"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism","Literature","Identity"]},{"name":"The Appearance","definition":"Rally announced by <em>The Answer</em> for seven o'clock on Friday evening at Henry Horner Park, where the Leader is to mount a decorated white horse. About a hundred thousand people and three bands assemble, but Letitia intercepts the Leader, ten-year-old Casey Szymansky, and makes him remove his costume and medals before they join the crowd [MTM B4:116-118].","citations":["MTM B4:116-118"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Journalism","Identity"]},{"name":"The Argosy","definition":"Name Gabrielovitch connects with the Ragosy, a fleet from Ragusa in Illyria. He identifies Ragusa as the original Iolcus, adding a philological claim to the company's <em>Argo</em> legend; Stein agrees about sailing from Illyria, provoking X to challenge his access to that memory [A:127].","citations":["A:127"],"tags":["Argonautica","Ships","Language & Words","Identity"]},{"name":"The Assembly","definition":"Gloss of Ekklesia, also rendered the Crowd, the roughly two-thousand-year-old institution whose termination is declared at the Last Conclave in Babylonia Bagascia. Its princes assemble with the intention of dismantling its structure [MB:561].","citations":["MB:561"],"tags":["Ekklesia","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"The Back-Door of History","definition":"Work attributed to Arpad Arutinov as the source of the epigraph to Chapter Two, Mermaid and Ogress, in <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>. The passage rejects rigid distinctions between persons and species, describes bloodlines and ghost and flesh mingling, imagines devils whose removal would kill their hosts, and presents the children of Ahriman as returning ancient monsters or mutations [D:26]. These claims frame the chapter's interest in mixed and hidden identities.","citations":["D:26"],"tags":["Literature","Ghosts & Spirits","Monsters & Creatures","Identity"]},{"name":"The Baltimore Catechism","definition":"Catechism quoted in Newton Prescott's journal in “The Casey Machine,” in a passage numbered 382-383 that contrasts the glorious immortal bodies of the just with the punishment of the damned. Its account of bodily radiance, swiftness, beauty, freedom from pain and ability to penetrate matter prompts Prescott's speculation that such powers are already available in a single splendid moment [CM:503] [CM:511].","citations":["CM:503","CM:511"],"tags":["Theology","Immortality","Time & Eternity","Resurrection"]},{"name":"The Broadway Oyster House","definition":"St. Louis seafood restaurant where Duffey meets the big-nosed John Solli, Finnegan, who orders one hundred oysters and shares them with Duffey and other diners. The meal evokes Fingal or Finn McCool eating a hundred enormous oysters. Finnegan shows Duffey his paintings and scrimshaw, identifies himself as Van Ghi and Count Finnegan, and explains that a first-class beach bum needs the aura of a fortune. The conversation reveals his parentage as the son of the Monster Giulio and his plan to wander for seven years before being buried on the Marianao Coast of Cuba [MTM B5:172-174].","citations":["MTM B5:172-174"],"tags":["Finnegan","Duffey","Food & Dining","Visual Arts","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"The Cadillac Bar","definition":"Bar visited on the Mexican side by Finnegan, Anastasia, and Marie Courtois. The narration describes it as dingy outside and rich within, with mostly Texan customers and white-jacketed bar-boys amid chrome and crystal. The three drink and mellow there [D:57-58].","citations":["D:57-58"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Finnegan","Women Characters","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol"]},{"name":"The Colossal Noisearama","definition":"Entertainment event in the Babylonia Bagascia sequence where Hiram III and several sheriffs capture part of the amplification system. Hiram yodels loudly during a solemn Rock Movement, breaking up the performance and becoming marked for death [MB:564].","citations":["MB:564"],"tags":["Music","Theater","Social Class"]},{"name":"The Comedy Music Box","definition":"Morgenstein's entertainment venue on Randolph Street in Chicago, where Duffey, Letitia, and Lily attend an extravaganza after supper. On leaving, they buy a midnight newspaper and learn that Gloria and Manolo Mullens have been tortured and murdered, while Charlotte is missing [MTM B3:97].","citations":["MTM B3:97"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Theater","Crime"]},{"name":"The Indiaman","definition":"English venturer named by Angela Cosquin among the supposedly treasure-laden sunken ships in her father's map lore, alongside the <em>Golden Mastiff</em>, <em>Hind</em>, and <em>Crane</em> [D:261].","citations":["D:261"],"tags":["Ships","Shipwrecks","Money & Trade","Family"]},{"name":"Thompson, Francis","definition":"English poet addressed by Casey Szymansky in the poem titled “To Francis Thompson” [PG:523].","citations":["PG:523"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Thorndyke, Doctor","definition":"Code name for one of the detectives consulted by <em>Argo</em> Masters; advised always noticing the frame of a problem or picture closely and suggested the answer might lie within the frame's material itself, requiring minute examination of the ship plank by plank [MTM B13:456].","citations":["MTM B13:456"],"tags":["Ships","Identity"]},{"name":"Three Apparitions (Teilhard, McLuhan and McGonigal)","definition":"Satirical transfiguration at St. Michael's during the Great Day: the demiurge Teilhard appears on the altar with McLuhan at his right and McGonigal at his left, all transfigured with light. They bless the Unfaithful Assembled with a sequence of technical-sounding terms [GD:497].","citations":["GD:497"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Liturgy","Theology"]},{"name":"Three monkey-like wraith-like creatures","definition":"Seamen seen on the <em>Argo</em>'s ice-sheeted rigging; wraiths or of lighter flesh, they climbed the frozen cliffs \"like ascending lightning\" carrying ice axes, cheerful and ready for assignment [MTM B13:398].","citations":["MTM B13:398"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Monsters & Creatures","Ships","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Three young toughs","definition":"Three large, drunken young men who accost Melchisedech Duffy before dawn on the Great Day. Two pinion him while the first grips his wrist, carefully smashes the watch glass and removes every shard, then breaks off its minute hand. He says the Great Day no longer requires such precision in time [GD:481-482].","citations":["GD:481-482"],"tags":["Duffey","Alcohol","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Thrombocyte-shaped remnants","definition":"One of the elaborate bodily tests cited by <em>The Eighteenth Book of Strange Encounters</em> for its claim that the New Orleans art dealer Melchisedech Duffey is more than four thousand years old [MTM B8:250].","citations":["MTM B8:250"],"tags":["Duffey","Literature","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Thunder Colt Complex, The","definition":"Name for the fifth of the seven possible contingencies confronting Duffey, also called the Decatur Street Opera House Presentation of the World. Its ending of humanity accompanies the beginning of another species [MTM B10:300-301].","citations":["MTM B10:300-301"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Theater","Time & Eternity","Identity"]},{"name":"Thunder Colt Game, The","definition":"Climactic performance at the Decatur Street Opera House, called the Thunder Colt Game in the text and orchestrated to Thunder Torus music. A human is thrust alive into the great egg and eaten by the still-unconscious colt; lightning then breaks the egg and awakens it. Its ensuing rampage kills Deutero-Finnegan, also called the Finnegan effigy, and Margaret Stone [MTM B11:350-351].","citations":["MTM B11:350-351"],"tags":["New Orleans","Finnegan","Music","Theater","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Thunder Colt Games, The","definition":"Earlier heading changed the source’s singular Game to Games. See <em>Thunder Colt Game, The</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["New Orleans","Finnegan","Music","Theater","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Thunder Colt, The actual","definition":"Totem animal of the Royal Pop Persons, whose awakening Cyrus Roundhead presents as a symbol of their own. It devours a human inside its egg, then hatches when lightning strikes during the Thunder Colt Game at the Decatur Street Opera House [MTM B11:350]. It kills Deutero-Finnegan, the Finnegan effigy, and Margaret Stone [MTM B11:351].","citations":["MTM B11:350","MTM B11:351"],"tags":["New Orleans","Finnegan","Theater","Monsters & Creatures","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Thunder Harps, The","definition":"A self-designation used by the Pop Countess for the Royal Pop Persons [MTM B11:316].","citations":["MTM B11:316"],"tags":["Social Class","Identity"]},{"name":"Thunder Torus music","definition":"Music accompanying the Thunder Colt Game at the Decatur Street Opera House. Its stark music picks up tempo as the colt hatches and runs riot [MTM B11:350], then crashes to an end after Margaret Stone is killed [MTM B11:351].","citations":["MTM B11:350","MTM B11:351"],"tags":["New Orleans","Music","Theater"]},{"name":"Thurneysen, Rudolf","definition":"Swiss scholar Rudolf Thurneysen (1857-1940), author of <em>Die irische Helden- und Königsage</em>. Absalom Stein recommends the work while considering Finnegan's possible identities as Fingal and Cú Chulainn [MTM B6:187-188].","citations":["MTM B6:187-188"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Thursday","definition":"Day on which Duffey initially supposes Bagby wrote and mailed the letter received after his Saturday funeral. More letters subsequently arrive, unsettling that explanation [MTM B8:237]. Thursday is also the usual publication day of the <em>Bark</em>, though it appears on Thursday or whenever and sometimes skips a week [GD:490].","citations":["MTM B8:237","GD:490"],"tags":["Journalism","Death & Mortality","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Ti, Ignatius","definition":"Christian Chinese ward boy whose family has lived in the islands for centuries. He translates the parakeet Amoy's stories [A:79]. Ti explains midnight as the hour when souls cross and returning ghosts can slip through a hole in the veil. He warns against turning one's back on corpses and describes medicos pretending to be dead to frighten ward boys [A:85-86]. His predecessor countered such tricks with a blow to the throat and a displayed knife, remarking that even a pig has its throat cut. Ti also tells of a murdered husband whose ghost returns to bed with his wife before she learns of his death [A:86-87]. <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> names Ignatius Cardinal Ti of Manila among the Princes in Hiding targeted by John Mogul and lists Bolo Manolo as his double [MB:578-579]. A later passage instead calls Lloyd Cardigan-Pembroke the double of the dead Cardinal Ti [MB:594]. The identity of the ward boy with the cardinal is not established in these passages.","citations":["A:79","A:85-86","A:86-87","MB:578-579","MB:594"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ghosts & Spirits","Finnegan","Occupations","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Tibeau, George (Tippio)","definition":"George Tibeau, called Tippio, a regular at the Old Wooden Ship bar [D:215]. In a tavern tale he repeatedly tries to fool a fortune-telling penny scale, but after changing clothes with a bum loses the money needed to leave town [D:217-218].","citations":["D:215","D:217-218"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Tide-water (blood type)","definition":"Narratorial hyperbole in the discussion of Cajun identity: after listing supposed racial classifications and blood types, the narration says many Cajuns have tide-water instead of blood and no heads at all [A:123].","citations":["A:123"],"tags":["Louisiana","Identity"]},{"name":"Tiger (Papa Devil)","definition":"Tiger imagery attached to Papa Devil on Naxos: he says he was once a tiger and intends to regrow his lost stripes after dark [D:122]. When his corpse is found shot, bludgeoned, stripped of its shoes, with pockets turned out or cut away and its olive shirt shredded, the narration says he briefly regained his stripes before death [D:146]. Finnegan's later feral condition is distinguished from Papa's tiger, resembling instead another cat, an ape, or a climbing grotesquerie [D:162]. X identifies the Papa Finnegan knew as Papadiabolous II, Gregory McColum, the Irish undercover policeman [D:285] [D:287].","citations":["D:122","D:146","D:162","D:285","D:287"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","The Devil","Islands","Ireland","Greece","Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Tigger","definition":"Earlier heading used Tigger, a form not found in the cited passages. See <em>Tiger (Papa Devil)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","The Devil","Islands","Ireland","Greece","Finnegan","Devils (Characters)","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Timeless (scent)","definition":"Scent worn by the Pop Countess, whose youthful appearance leaves her actual age uncertain [MTM B11:316-317].","citations":["MTM B11:316-317"],"tags":["Social Class","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Tin-Horn Forca, Freddy","definition":"Regular at the Old Wooden Ship bar [D:215]. The narrator declines to tell his story, saying that he is called Tin-Horn because that is the kind of sport he is [D:218].","citations":["D:215","D:218"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Tinder, Rita","definition":"Artist whose work Duffey sells during the New Orleans rebuilding project, among the local and distant artists contributing to it [MTM B6:198-199].","citations":["MTM B6:198-199"],"tags":["New Orleans","Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Tinos","definition":"Aegean island named in Finnegan's account of a primordial five-island group. His creation story makes the Aegean islands fragments of an electrum cup [A:35]. Brannagan says mermaids' hair from Naxos can be bought in shops on Siros, Tinos, and Kea, although an ancient reluctance prevents its sale on Naxos itself [D:207].","citations":["A:35","D:207"],"tags":["Nymphs & Mermaids","Seas & Oceans","Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Folklore"]},{"name":"Tippio, George Tibeau","definition":"See <em>Tibeau, George (Tippio)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Tito, Josip Broz (1892--1980)","definition":"Yugoslav communist leader Josip Broz Tito (1892-1980), who led the Partisans during World War II and later became president of Yugoslavia. His break with the Soviet Union in 1948 was followed by a leading role in founding the Non-Aligned Movement in 1961. In Casey's polemic against Gabby, Tito is called their noble ally, while Gabby is accused of attacking him and supporting Draja Mihailovitch [A:129].","citations":["A:129"],"tags":["Historical Figures","World War II","Eastern Europe"]},{"name":"Titoists","definition":"Followers of the Yugoslav communist leader Tito. The narration states that the Titoists shot at Gabrielovitch, Gabby, during the group's years of publishing and political conflict [A:132].","citations":["A:132"],"tags":["Eastern Europe","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Tityroi","definition":"Beings described as Satyrs in the chapter-eleven epigraph, which the novel attributes to Galen’s <em>Techne Iatrike</em>. In that account, Daemons beget them on human women; only the first generation is fertile, so the race requires repeated Daemonic renewal [D:160-161].","citations":["D:160-161"],"tags":["Angels & Demons","Greek Mythology","Literature"]},{"name":"Tmutarakan, Principality of","definition":"Principality in X's account of the Devil's imprisonment. X calls it an advance host of the Khanate of the Golden Horde and says it shared the Crimean peninsula with the Kingdom of Kiev when the Devil was imprisoned in 946 or 947 [MTM B6:204].","citations":["MTM B6:204"],"tags":["The Devil","History & Memory","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"To Karkik Kariki","definition":"One of the continuations or imitations of Casey's clandestine student <em>Crock</em> magazines. The narration says that its Greek name is accompanied by repeated references to an attributed ancient Greek trait, unlike the humor Casey himself intended [A:167-168].","citations":["A:167-168"],"tags":["Greece","Journalism","Literature"]},{"name":"To Weigh","definition":"Meaning Finnegan assigns to the common root of the Greek and English uses of talent, adding tolerant to the same etymological family. He makes the remark during Askandan's challenge about the parable of the talents and how Finnegan will answer the Master for their use [A:258].","citations":["A:258"],"tags":["Finnegan","Money & Trade","Language & Words","Theology"]},{"name":"Toast, Mary Regina","definition":"One of the confidence and gamine friends in Duffey's Chicago circle during its golden age [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Togetherness (concept)","definition":"Ideal Casey mocks as the seculars' only god and urges Christian men to keep in perspective. The narration recalls this saying when he is irritated that the others have read his mail before him [A:38].","citations":["A:38"],"tags":["Theology","Identity"]},{"name":"Tolerant","definition":"Word Finnegan connects etymologically with talent and the meaning to weigh, in his conversation with Askandan about the parable of the talents [A:258].","citations":["A:258"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Tom","definition":"Regular at the Old Wooden Ship bar [D:215].","citations":["D:215"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Tom Collins (drink)","definition":"Drink Duffey jokingly says Dives should have requested from Lazarus instead of water, together with the alternative of a cool Mint Julip. Duffey claims that this was the failed test of the parable, during his New Orleans discussion with Finnegan about jazz and salvation [A:117]. The underlying story is the rich man and Lazarus in Luke 16:19-31.","citations":["A:117"],"tags":["New Testament","New Orleans","Duffey","Alcohol","Theology"]},{"name":"Tommy (moustache)","definition":"Name jokingly attached to the moustache of Hildegarde Katt's husband. When she identifies him as the cop with the little moustache named Tommy, Finnegan treats Tommy as the moustache's name and persists in the joke after she corrects him [D:229]. The next page names the man Tommy Katt and calls him the toughest cop in town [D:230].","citations":["D:229","D:230"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Tommy Trouncer","definition":"See Trouncer, Tommy [A:51-53].","citations":["A:51-53"],"tags":["Australia","Soldiers","Identity"]},{"name":"Tompkinson, Christopher","definition":"Author of \"The Mythology of Liberalism,\" an article published in the <em>Crock</em> magazine [A:41].","citations":["A:41"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"Tone-Control Artist","definition":"Painter in Jimmy Fitzjames's account of artistic categories, ranked above a Primitive, who in turn outranks an Orthoclast. Jimmy says the Tone-Controller considers colors as musical equivalents and orchestrates hues into vibrations perceptible only to the more intense observers [A:230-231].","citations":["A:230-231"],"tags":["Music","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Tone-Control painting","definition":"Painting described by Jimmy Fitzjames as arranging colors according to their musical equivalents. He claims that ambient vibrations are its whole effect, so it can be enjoyed with one's back turned and its visual aspect ignored [A:230-231].","citations":["A:230-231"],"tags":["Music","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Tongues, gift of","definition":"Ability Salvation Sally claims during her Sydney conversation with Marie Monaghan and Hans. After Marie quotes French verse about Rome and Bohemia, Sally invokes her gift of tongues and answers its religious challenge [A:8].","citations":["A:8"],"tags":["Theology","Australia","Bars & Taverns","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Tooth's (beer)","definition":"Beer named with Toohey's and K. B. Lager in Finnegan and Vincent's opening pub sequence in Sydney, after Finnegan finds the Garden replaced by the World [A:1-2].","citations":["A:1-2"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Top Sergeant","definition":"Rank Show Boat says Finnegan held in his army outfit, in a letter to Dotty defending him and calling him the outfit's best man [A:262].","citations":["A:262"],"tags":["Finnegan","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Top-Peter","definition":"Title paired with Cap-Stone of the Ekklesia. At the Final Conclave, Conrad Stone Hackenschmidt forces the election of the Straw-Man to this role [MB:567], then orders Monsignor X to burn the effigy [MB:568].","citations":["MB:567","MB:568"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia","Popes"]},{"name":"Tornabuoni, Maria","definition":"Italian girl from New Orleans seeking stripping work at the Star and Garter in St. Louis. Show Boat describes her in a letter to Vincent, says the penniless girl is staying with the Piccones and taking small parts, and jokes that her Renaissance name suits her Renaissance figure [A:48-49].","citations":["A:48-49"],"tags":["Italy","New Orleans","Women Characters","Occupations","Theater"]},{"name":"Torpedo (person)","definition":"Duffey's term in his warning letter for the smooth-talking, jug-eared man assigned to target Casey, who once used the name Hugo Stone. Henry asks Casey whether Stein is this torpedo, and Casey confirms that he is [A:40].","citations":["A:40"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words","Identity"]},{"name":"Torre del Marifi","definition":"Spanish treasure ship among the real or imaginary wrecks on the master map made by Angela Cosquin's father. Angela lists it with <em>Estrella del Mar</em>, <em>Virgen la mas Benigna</em>, and <em>Casa del Oro</em>, comparing the names to a litany of the Blessed Virgin [D:260-261].","citations":["D:260-261"],"tags":["Ships","Shipwrecks","Spain","Family","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Trachtas","definition":"Work named in the Chapter Nine epigraph and attributed there to Saint Brendon. The passage describes unredeemable species denied eternal life but given long sublunar lives as flesh, returning ghosts, or continuing legend; some are helpful, yet none are to be trusted [D:130].","citations":["D:130"],"tags":["Saints","Heaven & Hell","Immortality","Monsters & Creatures","Ghosts & Spirits","Literature"]},{"name":"Track and Total","definition":"Contract-killing firm hired after the Final Conclave to hunt and kill the thirteen Princes in Hiding [MB:569]. Its chief, John Mogul, also tracks their doubles and organizes a lavishly rewarded contest for colorful murder suggestions [MB:578-579].","citations":["MB:569","MB:578-579"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","Crime","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Traffic Trestle, Pont du Sable","definition":"Chicago bridge Duffey visits before dawn on the morning he wakes knowing he has lost his golden touch. A heavy truck has knocked out an underpinning during the night, weakening the structure [MTM B4:109].","citations":["MTM B4:109"],"tags":["Duffey","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Tramp steaming","definition":"Popular art of travel included in the narration's catalogue of pursuits stripped of style and class during the great trashing of the arts [MTM B6:207].","citations":["MTM B6:207"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing"]},{"name":"Trams","definition":"Sydney transport vehicles whose brightness belongs to the fresh morning of the opening creation conceit; the narration says trams were made on the fifth day [A:2]. Finnegan and Vincent later take a tram following Tom and Freddy's elaborate directions to meet them near their barracks [A:10].","citations":["A:2","A:10"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Creation"]},{"name":"Transport (of Finnegan)","definition":"Visionary state that comes over Finnegan as he speaks of shadowy history and identifies with a primordial people. His speech asks how earth's earlier inhabitants became aliens and monsters, describes their ancient life among animals, and claims that defeated people hid within the victors' bloodstream, periodically returning to war [D:212-214]. The transport fades into vague rhapsody and leaves him weak; he returns to awareness with Le Marin in the Old Wooden Ship [D:214].","citations":["D:212-214","D:214"],"tags":["Finnegan","History & Memory","Identity","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Transylvania","definition":"Region in Countess Margaret Hochfelsen's extravagant invitation to Duffey. She claims nineteen long-lived generations of her family still live there, that its extent reaches Colchis, and that wolves larger than horses serve her family and frighten the Devil. She asks Duffey to marry her and share centuries of life there; he repeatedly challenges her geography and tall tales [MTM B8:255-256].","citations":["MTM B8:255-256"],"tags":["Argonautica","Duffey","Family","Social Class","Eastern Europe"]},{"name":"Trashman's Girl-a-Rama","definition":"Establishment where Duffey, Dotty, Mary Virginia Schaeffer, Margaret Stone, Bagby, and their companions gather in the Slant-Faced Men discussion. Dotty's line about Bagby's liver becomes a song, with Mary Virginia at the piano, Duffey on a house banjo, and the unofficial Pelican Glee Club joining in [MTM B7:225-227]. The narrator says more songs have been born there than anywhere else on the block [MTM B7:226].","citations":["MTM B7:225-227","MTM B7:226"],"tags":["Duffey","Women Characters","Music","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Traveler's Aid","definition":"Railroad-station assistance desk, printed Travelers’ Aid, where a confused Finnegan asks a woman to read his ticket and tell him his destination. It is only an old race-track ticket showing that he was in Hot Springs seven weeks earlier [A:243]. When she asks what is wrong, Finnegan answers Korsakofs Psychosis and refuses her offer to take him to a doctor [A:244].","citations":["A:243","A:244"],"tags":["Finnegan","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Traveler's Saloon","definition":"Saloon visited by Duffey and his school friends Rattigan, Murray, and Hilton. Traveler serves sensible twelve- and thirteen-year-old boys small glasses of whisky for five cents; Rattigan also buys a quart twice a week to resell in mixtures to schoolmates [MTM B1:25]. The boarding-school episode deliberately blends several possible cities rather than fixing one location [MTM B1:24-25].","citations":["MTM B1:25","MTM B1:24-25"],"tags":["Duffey","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Trevino (family name)","definition":"One of the surnames within the Angelino clan, alongside Obermeyer, Viskochil, Borg, and Larrigan. The narration describes a family mostly Italian, then German, with Bohemian, Mexican, and Irish Tinker ancestry and cousins around the world [A:218].","citations":["A:218"],"tags":["Ireland","Italy","Germany","Eastern Europe","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Trifles","definition":"Used by a complaining magus for the seemingly trivial payments received for losing major magical powers [MTM B1:5].","citations":["MTM B1:5"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Trinali Cocktail","definition":"Mixture of Trinali's old wine and Hans's new wine, which Trinali calls a dialectic and a synthesis. She elaborates its interplay as water and vinegar, sun and bacteria, then invokes old wine in new bottles and new wine in old bottles as a scriptural contradiction [A:231].","citations":["A:231"],"tags":["Alcohol","Literature","Theology"]},{"name":"Trinali Peterson","definition":"Young poet and maker of dolls and novelties in Hans Schultz’s New York Bohemian circle, during the spring and summer after he leaves the carnival in 1935 or 1936 [A:220-221] [A:240]. Hans meets James Fitzjames among the secondhand bookstores; they walk uptown and pick up Trinali [A:224-225]. Her mother named her Mary Jane. She adopts the Gypsy name Trinali and writes hundreds of pages about herself: “Ode to Trinali,” “Trinali at Sundown,” “To Trinali in a Red Dress,” “To Trinali at Dawn on March 4th,” and the taxi-composed “Ode to Trinali on the Way to the Village” [A:226].\n\nShe is slight, immature in figure, and just short of beauty, with rusty black hair she insists is red. Her electricity has been cut off; she obtains bread, wine, and candle ends from Lupido, steals longer candles, and makes figures from bottles, corks, and raffia [A:228]. She also makes lamps and toys, dyes corks in the dishpan used for dishes, and works with driftwood and cypress knees. She admits that her creations tend to look alike [A:229]. Hans sees her metaphorically as driftwood with cypress knees, rough, sea-stained, and remarkably convoluted [A:234].\n\nShe mixes old and new wine into the Trinali Cocktail and writes about wine as a bottled god [A:231-232]. At twenty she calls “To Trinali in a Rainstorm” her greatest ode and says her style has matured in the preceding eleven days. She romanticizes dying young but denies having a plan, imagining future works called “To Trinali on the Brink” or “To Trinali with Clarity” [A:232]. She dies from the luminescent lilac dye used on corks, either through the unwashed dishpan or, in Adam Scanlon’s account, from coloring added to wine. She is buried on September 1 with “To Trinali in Her Coffin with Her Red Hair Loose About Her.” The nondenominational service seems chilly and inadequate to Hans and Fitzjames [A:239-240].","citations":["A:220-221","A:240","A:224-225","A:226","A:228","A:229","A:234","A:231-232","A:232","A:239-240"],"tags":["Women Characters","Literary Figures","Alcohol","Literature","Visual Arts","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Triple Crown","definition":"Papal crown, or at least a double of it, that Monsignor X brings to the conclave inside Mooring-Rock [MB:596]. After twelve members vote for John Finnegan Solli and Finnegan abstains, X places it on his head and John Giwa hails him as Pope Finnegan the First [MB:597].","citations":["MB:596","MB:597"],"tags":["Popes","Ecclesiastical","Finnegan","Ekklesia"]},{"name":"Triple O","definition":"Bagby's nickname for Oscar Oliver Omygosh, a painful, melancholy novelty-shop owner near the Star and Garter. Papa Piccone claims that this man materialized from a badly made burlesque character of his own invention. Bagby remembers the man but disputes that Piccone created him; Piccone insists he did [MTM B5:168].","citations":["MTM B5:168"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Theater","Creation"]},{"name":"Triple-decade vast","definition":"Phrase denoting thirty years, attributed to the “Paean” in the earlier glossary. The earlier glossary cites the opening “Paean” of <em>Archipelago</em>, page ix. That preliminary page is absent from the supplied copy, so the reading remains unverified.","citations":[],"tags":["Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Triple-stall","definition":"Tommy Trouncer's boast that he can pull a double-stall or triple-stall and keep Stein on a loose hook during the Tania deception. He makes it before the tryst that ends with his beating Stein and ruining the conspirators' plan [A:53].","citations":["A:53"],"tags":["Language & Words","Identity"]},{"name":"Triumphant (state/concept)","definition":"Word attributed to the “Paean” in the earlier glossary. The earlier glossary cites the opening “Paean” of <em>Archipelago</em>, page ix. That preliminary page is absent from the supplied copy, so the reading remains unverified.","citations":[],"tags":["Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Trobriands","definition":"Islands skirted with the d'Entrecasteau group on the boys' voyage from the south back to their New Guinea bay, by way of the China Straits [A:36].","citations":["A:36"],"tags":["Islands","Pacific Theater","Seas & Oceans"]},{"name":"Troll","definition":"Creature type the Countess Margaret distinguishes from Giulio Solli: she says he is a Teras rather than a Troll, and Giulio confirms her classification [MTM B3:107].","citations":["MTM B3:107"],"tags":["Monsters & Creatures","Folklore","Identity"]},{"name":"Troop Movement Office","definition":"Office to which Hans and the other boys on Australian furlough report on Thursday, February 18, 1943, before being sent back to New Guinea [A:31]. Henry had stayed behind and was not part of the furlough party [A:38].","citations":["A:31","A:38"],"tags":["Australia","Soldiers","World War II"]},{"name":"Trouncer, Tommy","definition":"Tow-headed Australian lance corporal with a double voice and a talent for impersonation. Dressed as Tania, he auditions for Stein's Coconut Grove show and becomes the object of Stein's infatuation [A:51]. Tommy calls himself the best in the world as Casey enlists him and another Australian in the Dirty Five's scheme to discredit Stein and his superiors [A:52]. After starring in the show, Tommy loses his temper during a tryst and beats Stein against trees and into a slough, disrupting the encounter, though he says Stein still has not recognized the deception [A:53]. Hans’s later account says Stein knew who Tommy was and had devised the gag himself, though he had not expected Tommy to hit so hard [A:55].","citations":["A:51","A:52","A:53","A:55"],"tags":["Australia","Soldiers","Identity","Theater"]},{"name":"Trout (river fish)","definition":"The earlier glossary associates trout with Hans Schultz's St. Gallen childhood and the St. Croix River, but this association remains unverified in the supplied text. Its former citation, A:225, concerns Hans's New York companions.","citations":[],"tags":["Fishing"]},{"name":"Troyat, Martin","definition":"Name replacing Sebastian Hilton for a year or so in the composite account of Duffey's boarding-school quartet, while John Rattigan similarly becomes Delbert Dugan. The narration says the changes preserve the group of four; Sebastian reappears in the final school year after an interval in Europe [MTM B1:24].","citations":["MTM B1:24"],"tags":["Identity","Duffey"]},{"name":"Tshowax","definition":"Witch Casey says he conjured at five, the summer before he started school, by reciting a formula from a story at midnight. He says she still visits when she comes to Chicago [PG:524].","citations":["PG:524"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Literature"]},{"name":"Tuba mirum spargans sonam","definition":"Tuba mirum spargans sonam is the printed variant in Archipelago; the standard liturgical line is Tuba mirum spargens sonum. See <em>Tuba mirum spargens sonum</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Liturgy","New Orleans","Duffey","Music","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Tuba mirum spargens sonum","definition":"The <em>Dies irae</em> line normally given as <em>Tuba mirum spargens sonum</em>, about the trumpet spreading its wondrous sound. The novel prints <em>Tuba mirum spargans sonam</em>. In Duffey’s joke about jazz and salvation, Finnegan may rise from his coffin when the funeral sequence reaches this line and urge the player to blow that horn, though Duffey advises against waiting until death [A:117-118].","citations":["A:117-118"],"tags":["Liturgy","New Orleans","Duffey","Music","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Tucalul","definition":"Clandestine <em>Crock</em> imitation said by the narration to circulate behind the Iron Curtain, in the account of Casey's student magazines and their later continuations [A:167-168].","citations":["A:167-168"],"tags":["Literature","Journalism"]},{"name":"Tufa stone","definition":"Stone used for the freestanding, life-sized Black Christ from Bahr El Ghazal in the Sudan, one of Duffey's Seven Christs. The figure holds a cattle herder's crooked rod [MTM B3:104].","citations":["MTM B3:104"],"tags":["New Testament","Visual Arts","Africa","Duffey"]},{"name":"tujuan","definition":"Sulem's term for a person's direction or allotted life, distinct from the soul, which he calls <em>semangat</em>. He describes it as orders written on cuscus-skin vellum, rolled small and placed somewhere within the body. Chewing tai leaves allows him to see an aura and understand the tujuan, but he says Finnegan has neither. Sulem thinks this lack leaves Finnegan vulnerable to ruin and makes a grandfather for him as possible protection [A:60].","citations":["A:60"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words","Identity","Prophecy & Visions"]},{"name":"Tupelo-gum tree","definition":"Type of wood from which Giulio Solli whittled intricate animal calls (panther, alligator, etc.) for his son Finnegan [MTM B5:174].","citations":["MTM B5:174"],"tags":["Finnegan","Family"]},{"name":"Turban","definition":"Headwear of the laughing Christ from Turkish Anatolia, one of Duffey's Seven Christs. The figure is carved from reused monumental marble [MTM B3:104].","citations":["MTM B3:104"],"tags":["New Testament","Middle East","Duffey","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Turkestan","definition":"Distant location in the narrator's exaggeration of Tom Shire and Freddy Castle's unmistakably Australian appearance. Even a femur found in Turkestan thirty thousand years later, the narrator suggests, would still be recognized as Australian [A:5].","citations":["A:5"],"tags":["Australia","Soldiers","Time & Eternity","Identity"]},{"name":"Turpin of Rheims, Archbishop","definition":"Archbishop in the Charlemagne legends, invoked as the last prelate and general comparable to Henri Salvatore [MTM B5:183]. The legendary figure grew around a historical archbishop of Reims, traditionally dated to about 800 at death; the chronicle once credited to him is a later work now known as the <em>Pseudo-Turpin</em>.","citations":["MTM B5:183"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Soldiers","Literature","Historical Figures"]},{"name":"Tuthill, Tim and Gale","definition":"Members of the ecclesial/vineyard/churchy circle associated with Duffey in Chicago during the Golden Age [MTM B3:99].","citations":["MTM B3:99"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Ecclesiastical"]},{"name":"Twain, Mark (Samuel Langhorne Clemens) (1835--1910)","definition":"American author and humorist Samuel Langhorne Clemens (1835-1910), known as Mark Twain, author of <em>The Adventures of Tom Sawyer</em> (1876) and <em>Adventures of Huckleberry Finn</em> (1884, with U.S. publication in 1885). The <em>Argo</em>'s fantastic history lists him among its passengers [MTM B13:404]. Rick Norwood's publisher's note in the supplied <em>Archipelago</em> edition also mentions Twain's unfinished <em>Tom Sawyer and the Indians</em> [A:285].","citations":["MTM B13:404","A:285"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Ships"]},{"name":"Tweed, John","definition":"Student d'Alesandro calls his most brilliant, who has become world-famous by the time d'Alesandro asks him about a classroom joke involving Casey's altered equations. Tweed calls it a pair of mathematical puns in a kind of dialect and doubts they can be put into words [PG:526].","citations":["PG:526"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Twicherby, Major","definition":"One of the three livid majors, with Meyerhofer and Terwilliger, present when Colonel Laycheck threatens Casey over his challenges to Stein's lectures. None is a line officer [A:47].","citations":["A:47"],"tags":["Soldiers"]},{"name":"Two Lives, Finnegan of the","definition":"Narrative designation for Finnegan as a story and character out of the Taine. Meeting Dotty at the St. Louis train station, he feels like a stranger inside his own body, watching and feeling through the confused Finnegan person he inhabits, whose identity he is not ready to accept as his own [A:92].","citations":["A:92"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"Two-headed eagle","definition":"Living eagle conjured from sparks by Kasmir Gorshok. One head supplies half a coin, and the other supplies Kasmir's name; the completed coin bears the eagle on one side and a <em>Crock</em> or Pot on the other. The narration calls it an eagle of Poland, and Kasmir releases it to restore the endangered creatures' number to nine [MTM B13:426].","citations":["MTM B13:426"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Eastern Europe","Money & Trade","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Tywyn","definition":"Town in Gwynedd, Wales, home of Lloyd Cardigan-Pembroke. The list of cardinal doubles pairs him with David Cardinal Lloyd Spencer [MB:579]. The later account calls him a pig butcher of Tywyn but then identifies him as the double of the dead Cardinal Ti, an unresolved inconsistency [MB:594].","citations":["MB:579","MB:594"],"tags":["Identity","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Táin, The","definition":"Irish epic <em>Táin Bó Cúailnge</em>, a version of which survives in the <em>Yellow Book of Lecan</em>. Absalom Stein connects Finnegan with that book and supposes him identical with both Fingal and Cú Chulainn, declaring him capable of being all of them [MTM B6:187-188].","citations":["MTM B6:187-188"],"tags":["Literature","Ireland","Other Mythologies"]},{"name":"U.S. Army","definition":"Military organization that brings the Dirty Five together in the same battery: Vincent Stranahan, John Schultz or Hans, Kasmir Szymansky or Casey, Henri Salvatore, and John Solli or Finnegan [MTM B5:171]. Casey joins in April 1942 and asks Duffey to run the <em>Crock</em> during his service [MTM B4:138]. He insists that he first knew Absalom Stein in New Guinea around 1943, although Duffey remembers their earlier Chicago connection [MTM B4:112]. Private Gregory claims over a century of continuous service as a sergeant before changing identity in 1907, with missing records from the Indian Wars and Civil War. He then obtains an appointment to the U.S. Military Academy [A:77-78]. Don Lewis says he was one of three American soldiers who disappeared on Willy Jones Island: Sergeant Charles Santee, Corporal Robert Casper, or PFC Timothy Lorrigan. He refuses to say which [D:44].","citations":["MTM B5:171","MTM B4:138","MTM B4:112","A:77-78","D:44"],"tags":["Soldiers","World War II","Identity","Finnegan"]},{"name":"U.S. Mail","definition":"Postal service that delivers the first issue of the <em>Answer</em> to prominent Chicago citizens, including Duffey, on a Monday morning. The newsletter is scheduled for Monday, Wednesday, and Friday publication [MTM B4:113].","citations":["MTM B4:113"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism"]},{"name":"Ukalegon","definition":"Name paired with Melchisedech in Book Five's epigraph, attributed to Edwin Arlington Robinson's “Two Men.” The following commentary says Skalsky considers Ukalegon a woman and, in every way, Melchisedech's negation [MTM B5:149]. The classical Ucalegon is a Trojan elder in the <em>Iliad</em>; in the <em>Aeneid</em>, his house catches fire during Troy's destruction.","citations":["MTM B5:149"],"tags":["Literature","Greek Mythology","Duffey","Identity"]},{"name":"Ulysses","definition":"Greek mythological wanderer, also called Odysseus, whose identity the narrator associates with Finnegan, alongside Dionysus, Terras, and Arracht [A:39]. Finnegan waking battered and shoeless is likened to Odysseus washed ashore, beginning another day as Ulysses [A:177]. Hans imagines the Australian south as farther than Ulysses traveled [A:16]; Finnegan's difficult annual visits to Dotty are likewise treated as Ulysses keeping appointments [A:145]. A related Ulyssean wanderer is Roadstrum in Lafferty's <em>Space Chantey</em>, whose relationship to Finnegan is a literary comparison. His hornet spacecraft takes him through Odyssey-like episodes: the pleasure-induced lassitude of Lotophage, giants on Lamos explicitly called Laestrygons, man-eating shepherds on Polyphemia, the witch Aeaea's transformation of him and his companions into animals, and the deadly song of Sireneca. On Sireneca a crewman recalls the old remedy of waxed ears and a captain bound to the mast. Their fall into the Vortex brings extreme aging and death, reversed by a device installed by Hondstarfer; a later underworld episode brings them before Tiresias on Hellpepper Planet. Roadstrum returns after twenty years to Big Tulsa on World, his son Little Tele-Max, and his wife Penny. Tele-Max recalls the older story of shooting an arrow through twelve holes and killing the suitors. Roadstrum then kills Penny's suitors, but this homecoming parodies marital fidelity: Penny and the suitors have partied through his absence, and she later recounts her intimacies with them. Roadstrum's forked tongue gives him persuasive, deceptive eloquence, recalling Ulysses' cunning speech. His eventual rejection of a peaceful ending and departure with a new crew extend the wandering pattern (<em>Space Chantey</em>, PDF viewer pp. 8-15, 30-35, 80-92, 126-132, 161-165, 176-188, 217-222, 225-238).","citations":["A:39","A:177","A:16","A:145"],"tags":["Greek Mythology","Finnegan","Ships","Literature","Underworld","Shapeshifting","Giants & Titans","Language & Words","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Un-Duffeying a Duffey","definition":"Casey Szymansky's phrase in the <em>Crock</em> for undoing what he considers Duffey's harm and influence. Casey declares it harder than unsaying a lie or proverbially unscrambling an egg [A:130].","citations":["A:130"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Unaccountable Corpse","definition":"Corpse Saxon Seaworthy dreams he is trying to dispose of in an unidentified Indian city, in a gray dream where its face repeatedly disappears [D:72-73]. Seaworthy asks Finnegan whether he has ever tried to dispose of an unaccountable corpse. Finnegan later understands the dream as connected to the actual corpse he helped Seaworthy bury when they first met: Seaworthy had struck its reviving body with a shovel in a graveyard [D:74-75].","citations":["D:72-73","D:74-75"],"tags":["Dreams","Death & Mortality","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Uncle Joe Tompkinson's Torpedo Juice","definition":"Pseudo-sponsor of Absalom Stein's Anopheles Network G.I. radio station, advertised as providing “Explosive Personality by the Natural Method” [A:46].","citations":["A:46"],"tags":["Soldiers","Alcohol","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Uncollectable Art Collection","definition":"Narrator's name for Duffey's imaginary collection of living works of art. Charlotte Mullens asleep in her beauty would be one of its supreme pieces, alongside the ivory figurine Beth Keegan and others yet to appear [MTM B3:89]. Beth is elsewhere described as a laughing, living ivory statue, fashioned by herself and friends, with Duffey perhaps her final creator [MTM B2:57] [MTM B2:60].","citations":["MTM B3:89","MTM B2:57","MTM B2:60"],"tags":["Duffey","Creation","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Uncreated Creation","definition":"Vincent Stranahan's theory that creation might have occurred only ten minutes ago with its apparent past already built in: red shift, momentum, partly spent life, expanded matter, fossils, vestiges, an earth halfway down from the fourth Pleistocene ice age, carbon balances, partly run-down atomic clocks, sediments indicating fifteen hundred million years, and insects and tumbleweeds at their present development [A:56]. Human memories and dreams would likewise have been supplied without the remembered events ever occurring. Vincent calls it Stranahan's Theory of the Uncreated Creation and hints at a further Shadow and Substance motif he refuses to explain [A:57].","citations":["A:56","A:57"],"tags":["Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","History & Memory","Dreams","Creation"]},{"name":"Underground Eagle","definition":"Newspaper in which Josephine McSorely publishes her account of the Casey Machine's origin, beginning “Oh salving hatred, Oh revivifying derision, Oh nourishing slander!” [CM:506].","citations":["CM:506"],"tags":["Journalism","Creation"]},{"name":"Underthing people","definition":"Narrative term for the other human race Finnegan senses in Seaworthy, the Devil, Gerecke, Peter Wirt, Marie, and even Anastasia: peculiar people with old black blood and disturbing strength. He wonders whether he belongs among them while still doubting their existence [D:50].","citations":["D:50"],"tags":["Finnegan","Identity","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Underwood","definition":"One of seven Iowa locations claiming to be Duffey's birthplace during one of his possible childhoods, with Minden, Beebee Town, Neola, Crescent, Avoca, and Union Township [MTM B1:7].","citations":["MTM B1:7"],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"Union Sheet","definition":"Tuesday newspaper printed by Dotty and Duffey's Pelican Press [MTM B7:212]. Its half-dozen associated men are always seen in committees [MTM B7:213]. Letitia pays it less attention after becoming editor of the <em>Bark</em> [MTM B8:238].","citations":["MTM B7:212","MTM B7:213","MTM B8:238"],"tags":["Duffey","Journalism"]},{"name":"Union Township","definition":"One of seven Iowa locations (along with Minden, Underwood, Beebee Town, Neola, Crescent, Avoca) suggested as a possible birthplace for Melchisedech Duffey during one of his contested childhoods, though not properly a city itself [MTM B1:7].","citations":["MTM B1:7"],"tags":["Duffey"]},{"name":"United States","definition":"Nation containing the American settings of <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>. Finnegan first meets Seaworthy in a Texas seaport, later returning to the same town's Old Wooden Ship and its Company of Fifty [D:10-12] [D:215] [D:225]. <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> identifies the Old Wooden Ship Tavern as being in Galveston, Texas [MTM B13:458]. The <em>Brunhilde</em> calls at Freeport, Palacios, Port O'Conner, Aransas Pass, Corpus, Port Isabel, and Brownsville, and anchors off Padre Island [D:34] [D:55]. Demetrio recalls travels to Alice, Eagle Pass, Uvalde, Beeville, San Angelo, Weiniar, and Port Isabel [D:58]. Finnegan and Doll escape New York through Newark and Philadelphia to Baltimore; Pittsburgh is an alternative bus destination for which they buy tickets [D:167-172]. Their southern journey reaches the Florida lake country and Orlando [D:180] [D:190]. Finnegan later stays in Biloxi and is warned away from New Orleans [D:201] [D:209-210]. During another interval he visits friends in New Orleans, St. Louis, and Chicago [D:272].","citations":["D:10-12","D:215","D:225","MTM B13:458","D:34","D:55","D:58","D:167-172","D:180","D:190","D:201","D:209-210","D:272"],"tags":["Ports & Harbors","Ships","New Orleans","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"United States Five Dollar Gold Pieces","definition":"Coins Duffey produces while rubbing his hands together: seven appear before Patrick Stranahan and the others at the 1946 gathering. Patrick identifies them as United States five-dollar gold pieces minted about ten years earlier and suggests Duffey could have been carrying them [MTM B5:167] [MTM B5:171]. As a boy, Duffey also produces one for Gretchen Sisler in an unnamed city [MTM B2:38-39].","citations":["MTM B5:167","MTM B5:171","MTM B2:38-39"],"tags":["Duffey","Magic & Sorcery","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Unities of Aristotle","definition":"Dramatic conventions of action, time, and place later associated with Aristotle. The narrator invokes the Unities of Aristotle and Heroics of Plutarch as accidental constraints of the mainstream world, from which Finnegan's shadow life after death is free [D:161].","citations":["D:161"],"tags":["Theater","Time & Eternity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Universal rule (re:rogue islands)","definition":"Finnegan's assertion that every pentanesia, or five-island group, has a rogue island. Ignacio answers that he had not known it was a universal rule but agrees that Barbuda is good for nothing [A:272]. The earlier creation story presents five as the original number of islands in each archipelago, with the Aegean as an example [A:35].","citations":["A:272","A:35"],"tags":["Islands","Folklore","Creation","Finnegan"]},{"name":"University of Chicago","definition":"One of several colleges and universities (including Notre Dame, DePaul, Northwestern, Marquette) attended intermittently by Kasmir Szymansky (Casey) [MTM B4:119].","citations":["MTM B4:119"],"tags":[]},{"name":"Unreality Fringe","definition":"Smoky halo surrounding Duffey's magnificent animations, called the unreality fringe in the language of sorcerers. The narrator twice insists that these people are nevertheless real [MTM B5:158].","citations":["MTM B5:158"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Metaphysics","Creation"]},{"name":"Urals, wine of the","definition":"Wine mixed with goats' milk in the drink Finnegan shares with Askandanakandrian while receiving his advice about distressed merchandise and life [A:256].","citations":["A:256"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Urbe et Orbe","definition":"Section of the <em>Crock</em> containing the month's notes on Chicago and the world [A:41]. Its title plays on the papal blessing <em>Urbi et Orbi</em>, “to the city and to the world.”","citations":["A:41"],"tags":["Popes","Journalism","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Urchin (1)","definition":"Patrick Stranahan's habitual nickname for his future daughter-in-law Show Boat. The narrator describes her as half Juliette and half urchin [A:104]. X also calls her the Urchin in his letter listing people who must pass through the passion [A:266-267].","citations":["A:104","A:266-267"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Urchin (2)","definition":"Name Finnegan uses for Anastasia Demetriades when she asks if she is beautiful. She asks whether she is a beautiful urchin, and he agrees [D:38]. Later she reminds him that he calls her both an urchin and a tramp [D:70].","citations":["D:38","D:70"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Urchin disguise","definition":"Narrative name for the body Duffey wears while concealing his person and powers as the Boy King, whose powers include invisibility [MTM B1:18].","citations":["MTM B1:18"],"tags":["Duffey","Identity","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Ursine","definition":"Bear-like strain or quality; Elena Angelino, who handled trained bears for the carnival, insisted her great-grandfather was a bear (a belief the Professor shared), contrasted with the wilder Romany strain also present in the Angelino family [A:218].","citations":["A:218"],"tags":["Carnival","Family","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Ursuline Academy","definition":"New Orleans girls' school whose students prepare the <em>Seven Roads</em> sign as an advertisement and prop for their play [MTM B9:264]. The students include painter Crissie Cristofero, playwright Cleo Mahoney, and casting director Therese Doucet; some day girls breakfast at the Ghost Coffee Shop after early Mass at the Cathedral [MTM B10:293]. The academy's auditorium hosts <em>Seven Roads</em>, with Duffey acting himself and performing magic and music during the interval [MTM B12:366].","citations":["MTM B9:264","MTM B10:293","MTM B12:366"],"tags":["New Orleans","Women Characters","Theater","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Uvalde","definition":"Texas town Finnegan visits on a Monday during his chaparral wandering, before Sabinal, D'Hannis, and Hondo. He remembers earlier army maneuvers in the neighborhood [A:202]. Uvalde also appears in Demetrio's list of youthful travels [D:58].","citations":["A:202","D:58"],"tags":["Finnegan","Soldiers"]},{"name":"Vagaries","definition":"One of the kinds Duffey names with Gargoyles, Neanderthals, Boogers, and Variants when discussing the Teras element in his creations. He says everyone has some variant blood, but he has put more than usual into his creations [MTM B5:170].","citations":["MTM B5:170"],"tags":["Duffey","Creation","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Vair","definition":"In Hans Schultz’s reflections on Marie’s green eyes, the color of Nicolette’s eyes in a remembered French song, contrasted with Romany <em>sheleno</em>. Hans imagines <em>vair</em> degenerating into <em>vert</em> with the French soul and rejects its interpretation as gray [A:24]. This is the passage’s poetic philology: historically <em>vair</em> derives from Latin <em>varius</em> and can describe gray-blue eyes, while <em>vert</em> derives separately from <em>viridis</em>.","citations":["A:24"],"tags":["France","Music","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Valley of the New Men","definition":"The valley-name claimed by Finnegan for his ancestral people, the “people before the people,” in his account of their prehistoric life and displacement. He insists that theirs is the Valley of the New Men, “not of the old” [D:212-213].","citations":["D:212-213"],"tags":["Finnegan","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Valleys (Pepper-Belly, etc.)","definition":"The named valleys and draws on Willy Jones Island, also called Pulau Petir, in Don Lewis’s account of the pursuit of Yokipoki. Santee and Casper kill the little man; the three soldiers then discover they are lost, and Don Lewis later buries him [D:44-46]. Their familiar landscape includes Cotton-Picker Valley, Pepper-Belly Valley, Hound Dog, Horse Collar, Dog-Dirt Draw, Rang-Dang-Doo, Jackass Flats, Little Joker Canyon, Aching Back, Slit Trench, Sore-Head Gulch, Little Fat-Head, and Big Fat-Head. Jackass Flats is described, despite its name, as exceptionally precipitous. They know these valleys but cannot identify the one they have entered [D:45-46].","citations":["D:44-46","D:45-46"],"tags":["Islands","Pacific Theater","Soldiers","Crime"]},{"name":"Van Dusen, Augustus S.F.X.","definition":"Name used by one of the experts consulted by Duffey, Biloxi Brannagan and Kasmir Gorshok to prevent the Antichrist from boarding the <em>Argo</em>. The experts include fictional persons and actual persons under fictional names; Van Dusen is also called the Thinking Machine [MTM B13:454–455]. He warns that fireplaces can provide entrances overlooked in a locked-room problem, and recommends stationing a firedrake at each fireplace, guarding the other entrances and checking that the intruder has no ally already aboard [MTM B13:455–456].","citations":["MTM B13:454–455","MTM B13:455–456"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Argo","Antichrist","Detection"]},{"name":"Van Ghi","definition":"Gregory van Ghi is an artist’s identity explicitly used by Finnegan, although Finnegan also tells a conflicting story of studying under a great master Van Ghi in Florence. Patricia says that Van Ghi is a name her brother invented [A:175]. Dotty recognizes the signed Van Ghi paintings in Finnegan’s possession as his own work and does not dispute his claim that their painter is the greatest traditionalist-modernist-experimentalist [A:176]. In New York he has Gregory van Ghi cards printed, rents a Village studio, and successfully sells his Orange Period paintings; the narrator calls Finnegan the man underneath the constructed persona [A:191-192]. He tells Duffey outright that he is Van Ghi when consigning paintings to him, and later speaks of knowing Scanlon “when I was Van Ghi” [MTM B5:173] [A:236].<br><br>In the Florence account, Van Ghi teaches him the painter’s trade. After Finnegan paints a devil, Father LeFaye calls the master an old mountebank. Van Ghi identifies the depicted devil as an old repeater appearing in <em>The Night Watch</em>, Bruegel’s paintings, Giotto’s <em>Betrayal</em>, the “Burial” of Count Ortega, El Grecos, Notre Dame stonework, Etruscan vases, and Aztec statuettes. He agrees that the picture should be burned and advises painting without inspiration [D:252-255]. The supplied edition prints <em>Van GM</em> repeatedly on D:255.","citations":["A:175","A:176","A:191-192","MTM B5:173","A:236","D:252-255"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Finnegan","Duffey","Identity","Italy","The Devil"]},{"name":"Van Gogh, Vincent (1853--1890)","definition":"Dutch Post-Impressionist painter (1853-1890), known for expressive color and dynamic brushwork. His paintings include <em>The Starry Night</em> and <em>Sunflowers</em>. Commercial success was limited during his lifetime, though critical interest was growing by 1890; his work later influenced Fauvism and Expressionism. The specific allusion to him in the <em>Argo</em> novels remains unidentified.","citations":[],"tags":["Historical Figures","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Vanadium steel","definition":"A merciless streak in the narrator’s metaphorical alloy of Betty Hochstapler’s heart, alongside brass, extremely resilient phosphor bronze, and a warmer metal that often glows cherry red [A:216].","citations":["A:216"],"tags":["Women Characters","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Vance, Philo","definition":"Code name of one of the experts consulted by Duffey, Biloxi Brannagan and Kasmir Gorshok about keeping the Antichrist off the <em>Argo</em>. The experts include fictional detectives and actual persons using fictional names [MTM B13:454–455]. After invoking music, African violets and games, Vance reduces his advice to “Get a dog.” Father Brown recommends an English bulldog, and Duffey brings Gunboat Smith aboard [MTM B13:457–458].","citations":["MTM B13:454–455","MTM B13:457–458"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Argo","Antichrist","Detection"]},{"name":"Vanity","definition":"Patrick Stranahan’s assessment of scholarship beyond the little Latin and Law he considers sufficient [A:109].","citations":["A:109"],"tags":["Language & Words","Family"]},{"name":"Vast caverns","definition":"The spaces in the Papuans’ “bottomless unconscious” in the novel’s mythic account of them as the Well of the World. Other peoples and lands are entities maintained in these caverns of their under-persons; the Kiriwinans and other neighboring peoples are said to understand that their existence depends on the Papuans [A:37].","citations":["A:37"],"tags":["Dreams","Metaphysics","Creation"]},{"name":"Vaudeville","definition":"Theatrical entertainment among the forms in which Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni works. As a mind-reader, memory and information expert, and hypnotist, he has appeared in circuses, music halls, and vaudeville theatres around the world; he is also a nightclub magician and, chiefly, an answer man [A:215-216].","citations":["A:215-216"],"tags":["Carnival","Magic & Sorcery","Theater","Occupations"]},{"name":"Vecchia zimarra senti","definition":"“Old coat, listen,” the opening of Colline’s Act IV aria in Puccini’s <em>La Bohème</em>. Finnegan sings it during the gathering around a charcoal-filled trencher outside the Australian hotel, which has forbidden a bonfire [A:31].","citations":["A:31"],"tags":["Music","Australia","Finnegan","Theater"]},{"name":"Vega","definition":"One of the stars whose movements Hans learns to calculate under Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni [A:214]. The earlier glossary also connected Vega with the Paean’s comparison of stellar heights; that reference remains unverified because the Paean is absent from the available source copy.","citations":["A:214"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Veil","definition":"The partition between the living and the dead in Ignatius Ti’s account: a hole opens at midnight as souls cross, allowing nimble old dead to return. The narrative subsequently describes loose ghosts entering the psycho ward through that hole [A:86-87].","citations":["A:86-87"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","The Dead","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Velikovsky, Immanuel (1895--1979)","definition":"Russian-born American author and independent scholar (1895-1979), whose <em>Worlds in Collision</em> (1950) proposed catastrophic celestial events in human history. The popular book met strong scientific rejection. A hostile review of Royce Rollins’s <em>The Return of the Neanderthal</em> invokes Velikovsky disparagingly [D:240]. Duffey later names him with Braden, Cayce, and Otto while wondering about possible events becoming real through human insistence [MTM B11:340].","citations":["D:240","MTM B11:340"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Literature","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Vellum","definition":"Cuscus-skin writing material in Sulem’s account of the <em>tujuan</em>, a person’s divinely given directions for life. The writing is rolled as small as a pebble and placed inside the body, variously located in the brain, liver, or diaphragm [A:60].","citations":["A:60"],"tags":["Theology","Creation"]},{"name":"Vendetta, grande","definition":"The “great revenge” in Niccolo Crotolus’s account of assassination: killing someone almost certainly outside a state of grace. He says it cost several times more than the <em>piccola</em>, since the advertised outcome included damnation, and claims that his family delivered what it was paid for [A:280].","citations":["A:280"],"tags":["Theology","Heaven & Hell","Crime"]},{"name":"Vendetta, piccola","definition":"The “little revenge” in Niccolo Crotolus’s account of assassination: killing someone probably or possibly in a state of grace, sometimes after allowing a chance to attain it. It ensures death, not necessarily damnation. Crotolus says his employer has specified only the <em>piccola</em> for Finnegan, whom he has grown fond of following [A:279-280].","citations":["A:279-280"],"tags":["Theology","Heaven & Hell","Finnegan","Crime","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Venice","definition":"Italian city to which Vincent Stranahan proposes sailing with Finnegan, along with Prague, while considering flight before his wedding. His three thousand dollars are in addition to his mustering-out money [A:112].","citations":["A:112"],"tags":["Sailing","Italy","Finnegan","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Ventriloquist","definition":"Performer at the Star and Garter whose act Theresa “Show Boat” Piccone joins. She operates his figures and also brings men, usually soldiers, from the audience to sit on her lap while she supplies their voices [A:49].","citations":["A:49"],"tags":["Soldiers","Theater","Carnival","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Vernal ecstasy","definition":"Phrase attributed to Absalom Stein by an Australian informant describing his attraction to the perfumed Tommy Trouncer in the guise of Tania. Stein is said to want to make Tommy a good woman and live with her in a better, cleaner world [A:52].","citations":["A:52"],"tags":["Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Verne, Jules","definition":"French novelist (1828-1905) and a pioneer of science fiction. Marie Monaghan uses his name as a nickname for her purported Dutch uncle, the barefoot carnival painter Shoeless Veronese van Rijn [A:28].","citations":["A:28"],"tags":["France","Literary Figures","Literature","Visual Arts","Family"]},{"name":"Veronese van Rijn, Shoeless","definition":"Marie Monaghan’s purported Dutch uncle, nicknamed Jules Verne, a carnival foot-painter who works in watercolors, oils, and charcoal and goes barefoot. Hans questions the pronunciation involved in the Shoeless/Jules nickname [A:28].","citations":["A:28"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Carnival","Family","Language & Words"]},{"name":"vert","definition":"French for green. Hans’s reflection on Marie’s eyes treats it as a degeneration of the older poetic <em>vair</em> [A:24]. The words actually have separate Latin origins: <em>vert</em> from <em>viridis</em>, <em>vair</em> from <em>varius</em>.","citations":["A:24"],"tags":["France","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Vestige","definition":"The narrator’s description of the buffalo with whom Finnegan feels an affinity in their ancestral capital. They are said to be older than the earth’s other cattle, as his own strain is older than its proper people [A:206].","citations":["A:206"],"tags":["Finnegan","History & Memory","Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Vettura","definition":"Italian for a carriage or automobile. Finnegan uses it, with <em>faeton</em>, <em>drozhe</em>, and <em>carrozza</em>, to praise Mr. X’s extraordinary multicolored vehicle [D:179].","citations":["D:179"],"tags":["Italy","Language & Words","Finnegan"]},{"name":"Vices","definition":"The unspecified activities in which Henry Salvatore is said to have dabbled, from Copenhagen to Aruba, during his seafaring life as a rake before his trucking work and army service [A:124].","citations":["A:124"],"tags":["Language & Words","Crew & Sailors"]},{"name":"Vickers, Little Eva","definition":"Resident of the Old Wooden Ship, named in its roster of regulars and among the women called “salt water widows” [D:216] [D:221].","citations":["D:216","D:221"],"tags":["Women Characters","Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Vienna (Wien)","definition":"City on the Danube, called Wien in the text, where Duffey and Brannagan join a group debating whether it is better to do right for the wrong reason or wrong for the right reason. Casey leaves the discussion to look at buildings and pastry shops [MTM B13:432].","citations":["MTM B13:432"],"tags":["Duffey","Theology"]},{"name":"Vigilante Orthodoxers","definition":"The armed Orthodoxers who confront Zabotski and threaten to shoot him for rejecting their chronology and account of origins. He covers their sight with darkness or clouds and reaches his contraption [MTM B9:286]. See <em>Orthodoxer.</em>","citations":["MTM B9:286"],"tags":["History & Memory","Time & Eternity","Combat"]},{"name":"Vincent J. Stranahan (Vincent, Austin, Meleager)","definition":"One of the Dirty Five, born in St. Louis on 5 April 1921 to Patrick J. and Monica (Murry) Stranahan; <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> spells his mother’s maiden name Murray [A:94] [MTM B5:161]. The opening presents Vincent as born in the World beside Finnegan, who is of the other world; the later roll of the five nevertheless associates them all with Wonder and identifies Vincent with Meleager, Mercurius, Don Vincent de Ollos, and Austin [A:2] [A:39]. The narrator describes his crooked eyebrows and mobile mouth, and Hans remembers his recitations of “Corrymeela” and “Downfall of the Gael” [A:5] [A:9]. He belongs to a sporting family of amateur boxers and footballers [A:103]. He marries Theresa “Show Boat” Piccone after the war; <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> places their wedding on the Saturday of the last week of May 1946 [A:113] [MTM B5:171].<br><br>Finnegan names him among friends of his normal life and later recalls long visits with him [D:90] [D:273]. Vincent and Teresa visit New Orleans with two children. With Finnegan and Absalom Stein, he becomes one of the three disguised “hyenas” heckling Showboat during her street mission with Margaret Stone; the women turn the crowd against the hecklers [MTM B7:229] [MTM B7:231-232].","citations":["A:94","MTM B5:161","A:2","A:39","A:5","A:9","A:103","A:113","MTM B5:171","D:90","D:273","MTM B7:229","MTM B7:231-232"],"tags":["Argonauts (Characters)","Family","Australia","New Orleans","Finnegan","Greek Mythology"]},{"name":"Vinobianco, Dominic","definition":"One of Casey Szymansky’s roommates at St. Bonaventure’s boarding school, with Johnny Carruthers, Hinnegan, Germany Metzger, and a senior assigned to their room. Dominic comes from Elgin [A:161-162].","citations":["A:161-162"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Violet","definition":"Resident of the Old Wooden Ship tavern and boarding house, named among its “salt water widows,” women married to seamen [D:216] [D:221].","citations":["D:216","D:221"],"tags":["Crew & Sailors","Women Characters","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Virgen la mas Benigna","definition":"“Virgin most Benign,” one of the Spanish ship names on the master treasure map described by Angela Cosquin. Her father supplies aged maps and supporting papers for confidence schemes; customers can choose a purported sunken treasure ship and even relocate it on the map [D:261].","citations":["D:261"],"tags":["Spain","Ships","Crime","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Virgil (70--19 BC)","definition":"Publius Vergilius Maro (70-19 BC), Roman poet of the age of Augustus, author of the pastoral <em>Eclogues</em>, the agricultural <em>Georgics</em>, and the <em>Aeneid</em>. Ancient biographical tradition describes his education in rhetoric and philosophy. The <em>Aeneid</em> follows the Trojan Aeneas toward Italy, connecting the Homeric past with Rome’s destiny and Augustus. Its treatment of duty, piety, and empire includes both Roman glory and the suffering involved in achieving it. A school text since antiquity, it became a central work of later European literature; Dante made Virgil his guide through Hell and Purgatory. Patrick Stranahan invokes the <em>Georgics</em> as his standard of poetry, declaring that there has been no real poetry since [A:109].","citations":["A:109"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Italy","Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Virgin","definition":"The Blessed Virgin, invoked in Hans Schultz’s reflections on Marie’s green eyes. The passage describes her as red-haired and green-eyed in early Flemish Annunciations, then turns to witches and Lilith in its fanciful genealogy of green-eyed women [A:24].","citations":["A:24"],"tags":["Women Characters","Visual Arts","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Virginia","definition":"One of the red-haired town girls at the Bay Hotel picnic in Australia, with Dorothy, Tom Shire, Freddy Castle, and members of the Dirty Five. Casey declines to attend because Stein is there [A:29].","citations":["A:29"],"tags":["Australia","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Virginia Dare White and Red","definition":"White and red wines in Finnegan’s heavy shoulder bag when, after being refused further service in the bars around D’Hannis, he sets out into the Texas chaparral. The list gives three or four bottles of each [A:202].","citations":["A:202"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Virtual miracle","definition":"What Duffey says is required, with prayer and fasting, to bring mice to the new, mouse-free Gurdon S. Hubbard Elementary School. After the doors are opened and he concentrates or prays, seven exhausted mice arrive and perform in the puppet show; a teacher identifies them as Central American mice [MTM B4:135-136].","citations":["MTM B4:135-136"],"tags":["Prayer & Devotion","Miracles","Duffey","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Virtue","definition":"A quality Casey’s hostile <em>Crock</em> editorial says the <em>Bark</em> finds in Chiang’s refusal to cooperate with the “futuristic elements” in his country [A:128-130]. Elsewhere the narrator discusses Duffey’s retained but increasingly scruffy virtue, distinguishing his continuing honesty and courage from the complications of his affections [MTM B3:77] [MTM B4:109-110].","citations":["A:128-130","MTM B3:77","MTM B4:109-110"],"tags":["Journalism","Duffey","Sin & Redemption"]},{"name":"Viskochil","definition":"One of the surnames of the extended Angelino carnival clan, with Obermeyer, Borg, Trevino, and Larrigan. The narrator describes the clan collectively as largely Italian and German, with Bohemian, Mexican, and Irish Tinker ancestry; the passage does not assign an ethnicity to each surname [A:218].","citations":["A:218"],"tags":["Carnival","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Vocation","definition":"A calling that Henry Salvatore recognizes in his dream of the endangered <em>Navicula Petri</em>, which he understands to be the sanctified form of the <em>Argo</em>. Before waking he knows that the vocation has come to him whether he accepts it or not [A:44-46]. Later, lying in a pasture, he offers to give things up if God will take them in exchange [A:69]. Finnegan compares his unwanted artistic gift to Henry’s vocation: it frightens him, and he says that he has not accepted it and will try to escape it [A:99].","citations":["A:44-46","A:69","A:99"],"tags":["Theology","Ships","Finnegan","Visual Arts","Dreams"]},{"name":"Vogelkop","definition":"The Bird’s Head peninsula at the western end of New Guinea. The soldiers leave it behind on their transfer to a Dutch island four hundred miles farther on [A:50].","citations":["A:50"],"tags":["Islands","Pacific Theater","Sailing"]},{"name":"Voice roles","definition":"The intelligible speaking parts of the live mice in the Duffeys’ puppet shows. The narrator leaves open whether the mice themselves speak, which would be first-degree magic, or whether Melchisedech’s or Letitia’s voices speak through them, second- or third-degree magic. Their correctly timed actions are called first-degree magic [MTM B4:134].","citations":["MTM B4:134"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Duffey","Theater"]},{"name":"Voila!","definition":"French “there it is” or “there you are,” used by Trinali Peterson as she announces the Trinali Cocktail. She mixes her old wine with Hans’s new wine and invokes a dialectical synthesis achieved by putting old and new wines into the scripturally wrong bottles [A:231].","citations":["A:231"],"tags":["Scripture","France","Alcohol","Paradox","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Voltaire (François-Marie Arouet) (1694--1778)","definition":"François-Marie Arouet (1694-1778), French Enlightenment writer, historian, and philosopher, known for <em>Candide</em> (1759), religious toleration, and campaigns against injustice and fanaticism.<br><br>In <em>More Than Melchisedech</em>, a grotesque fictional Voltaire is kept in a senile stasis and awakened for a festival celebrating his three-hundredth birthday. The authorities call him the Father of Reason and stage the killing of Hiram III with the entrails of Paul XI as the fulfillment of a wish they attribute to him. He watches delightedly before being allowed to sleep again [MB:563-565].","citations":["MB:563-565"],"tags":["France","Historical Figures","Literary Figures","Literature","Apocalyptic"]},{"name":"Volvox colonial","definition":"A preparation of colonial green algae that Biloxi Brannagan requests as the fruit course of his meal. His other choices include desmid salad with ulothrix, spirogyra bread, hydrodictyon soup, and a Foraminifera Julip [MTM B13:418].","citations":["MTM B13:418"],"tags":["Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Volvoxa","definition":"Aquatic material that Honeybucket Kincaid gathers with ectocarpus by manipulating frequencies while preparing Duffey and Brannagan’s food and drinks. The narrative says the two can arrive together and then be separated [MTM B13:418].","citations":["MTM B13:418"],"tags":["Food & Dining","Alcohol"]},{"name":"Vomiting","definition":"Sedalia Schoefeld’s proposed trick for escaping the formal dance: she says she can vomit at will and will pretend to be ill. Lily Koch first asks to be splashed so that she too can leave, then leads the group to the attic billiard room instead [MTM B1:33].","citations":["MTM B1:33"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Von Vinger, County","definition":"An alias of Ali ben Taife, also called Lord Peter Petrof and Sinhor Dedos or Mr. Fingers. In Ignatius Ti’s account, he employs Amoy as a jewel-stealing red-winged blackbird, then cuts the bird’s throat to recover a blue diamond it will not regurgitate [A:80-81].","citations":["A:80-81"],"tags":["Crime","Identity","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"Voyages of Sindbad the Sailor","definition":"The subject of the <em>Brunhilde</em> barroom panels Finnegan paints for Saxon Seaworthy, whom the narrative identifies with a morally dubious Sindbad [D:59]. Anastasia requests the dead wife of the fourth voyage; Marie claims the happy-ever-after wife of the seventh. Finnegan paints those roles and proposes Papa Devil as a man-eating ogre and Gerecke as the Old Man of the Sea [D:60-62]. His difficulty portraying Papa Devil leads him to recognize that the apparent face is not the Devil’s own [D:62].","citations":["D:59","D:60-62","D:62"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Finnegan","The Devil","Visual Arts","Women Characters","Identity"]},{"name":"Vulgate","definition":"The Latin Bible translation Henry Salvatore prefers to the French. During his dream of the storm-tossed <em>Navicula Petri</em>, a Latin account of the storm is followed by French, prompting him to tell the dream that he has already recognized the passage [A:44-45].","citations":["A:44-45"],"tags":["Scripture","Dreams","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Waldo","definition":"Earlier heading omitted Waldo’s surname, Waldorf. See <em>Waldorf, Waldo</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Minor Characters","Family","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Waldo, Wife of Zabotski","definition":"Zabotski’s wife, still called the Widow Waldo, resident with him in the extraordinary house on Pristine Cove, Lake Borgne. The magazine account describes them as gracious hosts surrounded by children, strange animals, and an atmosphere of “top speed without hurry” [MTM B9:267]. Formerly Miss America, she married the promoted strongman Waldo Waldorf; after his murder by a jealous rival she devoted herself to orphans. She cooks for Duffey and Zabotski during construction and insists that many children accompany the vessel [MTM B9:275]. She relays the state inspector’s order to remove the part encroaching on the Golden Childrens Home and Haven Orphanage grounds [MTM B9:274]. She feeds the visiting Fundamentalers, joking that there is almost no limit to the food she can take from the orphans’ mouths [MTM B9:280].<br><br>Duffey visits her and Zabotski in their floating boat palace, already shared with many children and animals [MTM B10:290]. During a later dinner she says Zabotski has announced their departure daily for nine years, urges him to rest his injured tongue, and explains their large steak meals [MTM B12:363].","citations":["MTM B9:267","MTM B9:275","MTM B9:274","MTM B9:280","MTM B10:290","MTM B12:363"],"tags":["Women Characters","Ships","Family","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Waldorf, Waldo","definition":"Waldo Waldorf, deceased husband of the Widow Waldo. He was Mr. Body Beautiful of the West North Central States, including Illinois; their marriage was a publicity stunt devised by promoters. His killing by a jealous rival preceded his widow’s dedication to orphans [MTM B9:275].","citations":["MTM B9:275"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Family","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Walnut Street","definition":"Downtown St. Louis street where Duffey buys the empty horse barn that becomes his establishment [MTM B2:40]. Bagby’s cabinet shop occupies the adjoining building; Bagby says both were made from the walnut grove felled before the street was laid out and named [MTM B2:46].","citations":["MTM B2:40","MTM B2:46"],"tags":["Duffey","Occupations","Bars & Taverns"]},{"name":"Wandering Jew","definition":"Howell’s comparison for Private Gregory’s account of never aging and periodically changing identity. Howell calls “The Wandering Jew, are there Others?” his six-hundred-and-twenty-third subject of conversation, out of the thousand he claims to have catalogued [A:77].","citations":["A:77"],"tags":["Folklore","Soldiers","Immortality"]},{"name":"Ward Fourteen","definition":"The hospital ward occupied by the Sergeant, Private Gregory, Howell, George Buckram, Martin Benning, and Green. The Sergeant considers it a possible test of whether its inmates differ from other people, wonders about their kinship with “gooney birds,” and concludes that they are confined through misunderstanding [A:75].","citations":["A:75"],"tags":["Soldiers","Identity"]},{"name":"Warneke, Fanny","definition":"One of the arty friends in the Duffeys’ North Chicago golden age of 1925-1935 [MTM B3:98].","citations":["MTM B3:98"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts"]},{"name":"Washington University","definition":"One of the institutions at which Duffey takes courses in the summer of 1915, alongside St. Louis University, Concordia, a pharmacy school, and a music school. He calculates twenty-eight hours of activity per day before sleep, already knowing tricks with time [MTM B2:49].","citations":["MTM B2:49"],"tags":["Duffey","Time & Eternity"]},{"name":"Watson (Dr. John H. Watson)","definition":"Earlier heading identified an unspecified Watson with Sherlock Holmes’s companion without supporting evidence. See <em>Watson (unidentified allusion)</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Literature","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Watson (unidentified allusion)","definition":"A surname in Duffey’s list of unconventional scientific investigators, with O’Connell, Field, and Spraggett, during the discussion of strange events and possible changes of human species [MTM B11:339]. Lyall Watson, author of <em>Supernature</em>, is a plausible identification, but the passage supplies no first name; it does not establish Sherlock Holmes’s companion.","citations":["MTM B11:339"],"tags":["Literature","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Watteau","definition":"One of the painters Patrick Stranahan names with Durer and Hogarth in his account of sufficient culture. He says one minor original and a dozen good copies are all an honest man can afford [A:109].","citations":["A:109"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Waves of the Future","definition":"Absalom Stein’s expression for forces from the future breaking backward into the past. His <em>Notes on the Finnegan Cycle</em> treats Hitler as one such raider and argues that the future’s evil and cheapness should encourage resistance [MTM B6:185].","citations":["MTM B6:185"],"tags":["World War II","Time & Eternity","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Way Back in the Hills","definition":"Tune Finnegan plays on his mouth organ to occupy the shoe-shine boys while Captain Sammy borrows their box for a money-making trick. It follows “A-Rang-Dang-Doo” and precedes “The Wreck of the Old Ninety-Seven” and “The Wabash Cannon Ball” [A:67].","citations":["A:67"],"tags":["Finnegan","Music","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Weaver, Rufus","definition":"One of the musical friends in the Duffeys’ North Chicago golden age. The group collectively includes operatic, ragtime, Chicago-hots, and string-band composers, players, and stagers; Weaver’s individual specialty is not given [MTM B3:98-99].","citations":["MTM B3:98-99"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Music"]},{"name":"Webbs, The","definition":"Probably Sidney and Beatrice Webb, the Fabian social thinkers. The Webbs appear with Nietzsche, Shaw, Machiavelli, and both Roosevelts among the sources mixed into the <em>Answer</em>, a journal that Duffey reads with mingled admiration and alarm [MTM B4:114].","citations":["MTM B4:114"],"tags":["Historical Figures","Journalism","Duffey"]},{"name":"Wedekind","definition":"One of the associates of Papadiabolous I named by X, alongside Seaworthy, Genof, Wirt, Gerecke, Askadrian, Sanditen, and Sorensen. X says several of this group were rumored to be Papadiabolous’s illegitimate half-brothers, without specifying which [D:286].","citations":["D:286"],"tags":["Minor Characters","The Devil","Crime"]},{"name":"Weinar","definition":"Earlier heading altered the printed place-name; identification with Weimar remains unestablished. See <em>Weiniar</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Weiniar","definition":"A place Demetrio includes in the travels of his youth, with Alice, Eagle Pass, Uvalde, Beeville, San Angelo, and Port Isabel. The supplied text actually prints <em>Weiniar</em> [D:58].","citations":["D:58"],"tags":["Bars & Taverns","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Weinsberg-Valeni, Kirol von","definition":"See <em>Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni, Professor</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Carnival","Magic & Sorcery","Germany","Theology","Language & Words","Time & Eternity","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Welch, Art (Two Shot)","definition":"Art “Two Shot” Welch, resident of the Old Wooden Ship and a card sharp and trickster paired with Art “One Shot” Yodel. The narrator says the pair are repeatedly outwitted by better sharpers [D:216] [D:221]. The earlier resident list calls his partner Ant “One Shot” Yoder [D:216].","citations":["D:216","D:221"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Crime","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Well of the World","definition":"The narrator’s name for the Papuans in the novel’s mythic cosmology. Their bottomless unconscious contains the other peoples and lands, which survive only while maintained in its caverns. The Kiriwinans and neighboring peoples are said to understand this dependence, while more distant peoples do not [A:37].","citations":["A:37"],"tags":["Dreams","Creation","Metaphysics"]},{"name":"Wellington (Arthur Wellesley, 1st Duke of)","definition":"Arthur Wellesley, first Duke of Wellington, included by the narrator among people said to have seven hidden or dark years in their lives. The list provides a precedent for Duffey’s missing years and also names Caesar, Diocletian, Boëthus, Carl the Great, Lincoln, Sam Houston, George Barrow, and Hans Schultz [MTM B2:72].","citations":["MTM B2:72"],"tags":["Historical Figures","History & Memory"]},{"name":"West","definition":"The direction Finnegan travels after leaving the goat-people. He walks chiefly in the hours before dawn and later remembers the summer as continually foggy, though the narrator allows that the fog may not have been physical [A:200].","citations":["A:200"],"tags":["Finnegan","History & Memory"]},{"name":"West Beach","definition":"Place on Galveston Island where Doll Delancy finds a body she swears is Finnegan’s. Stein reports her identification while doubting that Finnegan died at Seaworthy’s hand [MB:548].","citations":["MB:548"],"tags":["Islands","Finnegan","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"West Kansas","definition":"The comparison used to convey Black Papua’s densely crowded landscape: an equal square mile of West Kansas is imagined as open enough to count fence posts a mile away, with nothing larger between [A:37].","citations":["A:37"],"tags":["Language & Words"]},{"name":"Whammo","definition":"Ignatius Ti’s word for the delayed impact of his ghost story. He defends its quiet climax by saying that the after-effect hits the listener “Whammo” [A:87].","citations":["A:87"],"tags":["Ghosts & Spirits","Literature","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Where Three Men Stand Together Are Kingdoms Less By Three","definition":"The line Hans quotes during his solemn three-way handshake with Henry and Finnegan after the group’s drinking excursion in Luzon. The narrative likens the gesture to the formation of a mystic trio within the Dirty Five; the group later sleeps in the pasture near the two white horses [A:68-69].","citations":["A:68-69"],"tags":["Argonauts (Characters)","Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Whistle Stop Jump","definition":"One of the tunes in the Rounders’ String Band’s St. Louis repertoire, remembered among its sweet old songs. Duffey plays banjo in the group and associates that instrument with the flat straw hat [MTM B2:52].","citations":["MTM B2:52"],"tags":["Duffey","Music"]},{"name":"White Avenger","definition":"One of three comic strips in which Dotty says the Slant-Faced Men have appeared, with <em>Flame Man</em> and <em>Captain Justice</em>. She would find them funny if she did not know they were real [MTM B7:225].","citations":["MTM B7:225"],"tags":["Literature","Journalism","Monsters & Creatures"]},{"name":"White Horse(s)","definition":"The two horses in a Luzon field to which Hans leads the drinking party after a brief fight with other soldiers and a walk through the jungle. He runs down the larger white horse, catches it by the mane, and rides it through low branches in the darkness. The narrator counts this capture among the Argonauts’ appointed heroic labors [A:68].","citations":["A:68"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","Argonautica","Combat"]},{"name":"White wine","definition":"One of the drinks Patrick Stranahan considers sufficient for a settled life, with one red wine, one whisky, one brandy, one rum, and four brews [A:110]. The narrator also says white wine is made on Naxos [D:125]. Finnegan and Anastasia bring wine among their provisions for Anastasia Grandmother, although that list does not specify its color [D:126].","citations":["A:110","D:125","D:126"],"tags":["Greece","Alcohol","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Whiteduck, Dorcas","definition":"One of the artists or makers Duffey associates with during the New Orleans rebuilding project. The group is described as building or inventing worlds, moments, situations, or scenes; Whiteduck’s individual medium is not specified [MTM B6:198].","citations":["MTM B6:198"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Visual Arts","Creation"]},{"name":"Whiterice, Maud","definition":"One of the friends of scientific bent in the Duffeys’ North Chicago golden age, listed with Silas Whiterice [MTM B3:98-99]. She and Silas attend Duffey’s later Chicago reunion, where her name is spelled Maude [MTM B12:383]. Their joint commentary discusses the strange encyclopedia entry identifying Casey Szymansky as the Antichrist; they call it a hoax while affirming the accuracy of its birth details [PG:528-529].","citations":["MTM B3:98-99","MTM B12:383","PG:528-529"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Literature"]},{"name":"Whiterice, Sherman","definition":"Earlier heading combined Sherman Slick’s given name with the surname of the following Silas and Maud Whiterice. See <em>Slick, Sherman</em>.","citations":[],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Whiterice, Silas","definition":"One of the friends of scientific bent in the Duffeys’ North Chicago golden age, listed with Maud Whiterice [MTM B3:98-99]. Both attend Duffey’s later Chicago reunion [MTM B12:383]. Their joint commentary describes a variant encyclopedia entry naming Casey Szymansky as the Antichrist; they call the insertion a hoax but affirm its account of his birth [PG:528-529].","citations":["MTM B3:98-99","MTM B12:383","PG:528-529"],"tags":["Duffey","Minor Characters","Literature"]},{"name":"Whites","definition":"One of the family names given by an older relative or pretended relative who disputes Duffey’s lack of kindred in the account of his possible Iowa childhood. Duffey questions whether she is remembering his twin instead [MTM B1:7].","citations":["MTM B1:7"],"tags":["Duffey","Family","History & Memory","Identity"]},{"name":"Who is Iason, but Who is Also of the Other People","definition":"Subtitle attached to “Finnegan” in the heading of Chapter Seven [A:171].","citations":["A:171"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature","Greek Mythology","Identity"]},{"name":"Who is Meleager","definition":"Part of the Chapter Four heading, “Stranahan, Who Is Meleager” [A:91].","citations":["A:91"],"tags":["Literature","Greek Mythology","Identity"]},{"name":"Widow Waldo","definition":"See Waldo, Wife of Zabotski [MTM B9:267] [MTM B9:275].","citations":["MTM B9:267","MTM B9:275"],"tags":["Women Characters","Family"]},{"name":"Widow wooing","definition":"One of the minor arts of everyday life that the narrator says lost style and was trashed during the cultural decline after the Devil’s release [MTM B6:207-208].","citations":["MTM B6:207-208"],"tags":["Women Characters","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Wild Beasts Devouring Entrails in the Holy Places","definition":"An “old saying” in the narrative that supplies the theme for a second grotesque presentation at the Voltaire Festival. After Hiram III’s murder, fifty members of the Consenting Adults’ World Congress eat the entrails of Paul XI in a large holy place, struggling with their toughness and gamey taste [MB:565].","citations":["MB:565"],"tags":["Apocalyptic","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Wild onion hunting","definition":"One of the minor arts whose cultural destruction is lamented after the Devil’s release. It appears in the narrator’s catalogue and is repeated in Duffey’s protest that hardly a joy has survived [MTM B6:208-209].","citations":["MTM B6:208-209"],"tags":["History & Memory","Duffey"]},{"name":"Wild Turkey","definition":"Birds among the creatures along the mile-long track through John William Schultz’s nursery at St. Gallen. Hans runs the meander every morning and evening during his physical education [A:208].","citations":["A:208"],"tags":["Family"]},{"name":"William Count of Poitou","definition":"Named in item V of Casey Szymansky’s school notebook as the figure to whom poetry in the mother tongue can be traced. Casey tentatively attributes this statement to Belloc, or to another writer he read around the same time [A:163].","citations":["A:163"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature"]},{"name":"Willy Jones Island","definition":"Island also called Pulau Petir where, in Don Lewis’s account, he and two other American soldiers disappear on 1 October 1944. Santee and Casper kill Yokipoki; Don Lewis buries him, is subsequently taken into a peculiar custody, and returns under another name, no longer his own man [D:44-48]. He also tells the island’s story of the Big House, whose inhabitants live on broth made from murdered visitors’ organs [D:48-49]. The narrative connects Yokipoki’s burial with Don Lewis’s becoming a furtive man [D:50].","citations":["D:44-48","D:48-49","D:50"],"tags":["Islands","World War II","Soldiers","Crime","Folklore"]},{"name":"Windward Passage","definition":"One of the seven true passages through the Antilles in Angela Cosquin’s account of her father’s forged shipwreck maps. She contrasts these with seven false or shoal passages: Jardines Banks, Cabo Rojo, Vieques, St. Barthelemy, the Basseterre-Nevis pass, the Basse-Terre-Marie Galante pass, and the Grenadines. This is Angela’s explanation of where her father placed plausible wrecks on his maps. [D:260]","citations":["D:260"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Caribbean"]},{"name":"Wine making","definition":"A traditional craft included in the narrator’s catalogue of activities condemned or “trashed” during cultural decline. [MTM B6:207-208] Trinali Peterson mixes her old wine with Hans’s new wine to make the “Trinali Cocktail.” Her accompanying poem gives a grape, water, and sugar recipe with optional brandy and six months’ gestation, mentions a thirteen-day potato wine, and presents winemaking as the birth and imprisonment of a god. [A:231]","citations":["MTM B6:207-208","A:231"],"tags":["Alcohol","Occupations","Creation"]},{"name":"Wine of the Urals","definition":"Wine associated with the drink Finnegan shares with Askandan after Hillary Hilton leaves. The passage says that Finnegan imbibes knowledge and “a drink made of goats’ milk and a wine of the Urals”; it does not establish that the wine itself is made from milk. [A:256]","citations":["A:256"],"tags":["Finnegan","Alcohol","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Winter Garden","definition":"Place where, according to Finnegan, the leftfooted killer had been asking for him the previous day. Finnegan reports this while X briefs him on the continuing pursuit. [D:193]","citations":["D:193"],"tags":["Finnegan"]},{"name":"Wiper, The (giant)","definition":"A “cloud-high giant” described to Newton Prescott by a little boy. The Wiper uses a rag to erase part of a cloud and, with it, part of something in people’s heads. Prescott accepts the boy’s intuitive account of this personification of forgetting and subsequently notices drawings of the Wiper on walls and sidewalks. [CM:503] [CM:513-514]","citations":["CM:503","CM:513-514"],"tags":["Giants & Titans","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Wirt, Peter","definition":"One of Saxon Seaworthy’s two cronies aboard the <em>Brunhilde</em>, with William Gerecke, sharing the mystery surrounding Seaworthy and the Devil. [D:30] Anastasia includes him among the powerful men who own the captain and crew; Finnegan senses a strong, earthy power in him at the beach gathering. [D:40] [D:50] Wirt goes ashore with Seaworthy and Gerecke and later waits with them aboard the ship at Naxos, an experienced combatant rather than a nonentity. [D:57] [D:136] X names him among the four rulers after the two Papadiabolouses die. [D:155] At the subsequent meeting he has the “mind of a wolverine.” [D:163] Papadiabolous I’s associates include Wirt; several of that group are rumored to be illegitimate half brothers, without identifying which ones. [D:286]","citations":["D:30","D:40","D:50","D:57","D:136","D:155","D:163","D:286"],"tags":["The Devil","Ships","Devils (Characters)","Minor Characters"]},{"name":"Wisconsin","definition":"The state of Hans Schultz’s childhood. During Hans and Marie’s exchange of childhood memories, the narrator observes that Wisconsin and New South Wales are very much alike in summer. [A:10] Hans’s birthplace, St. Gallen, is described as Northern Wilderness lake country beyond Rhinelander and near Eagle River. He remembers its splendid sunlight and calls it the land of the larger summer. [A:207]","citations":["A:10","A:207"],"tags":["Australia","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Wisconsin Rapids","definition":"Town reached by Hans Schultz and Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni after an eighty-mile drive from St. Gallen in a truck resembling an old medicine-show wagon. The Professor lectures Hans on the journey; they meet the rest of the carnival at Wisconsin Rapids. [A:212] [A:215]","citations":["A:212","A:215"],"tags":["Carnival"]},{"name":"Wisdom of Finnegan","definition":"The source Hans names for the reversed maxim “If you can’t be careful, be good.” He is correcting Salvation Sally, who has offered the conventional wording; Marie Monaghan is also present. [A:9]","citations":["A:9"],"tags":["Finnegan","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Wolf hunting","definition":"One of the traditional activities included in the narrator’s catalogue of pursuits condemned or “trashed” during cultural decline. [MTM B6:207-208]","citations":["MTM B6:207-208"],"tags":["Occupations"]},{"name":"Wolff, Ignace","definition":"Name credited with the epigraph to Chapter Five, “The Unaccountable Corpse,” from a work styled <em>Gedanken von Leibern</em> (Thoughts on Bodies). The epigraph says that bodies thwart non-contradiction and negate the principle of sufficient reason: “There is not sufficient reason for bodies.” [D:67]","citations":["D:67"],"tags":["Literary Figures","Literature","Paradox"]},{"name":"Wooden Ship (Old Wooden Ship)","definition":"A Sydney pub visited by Finnegan, Vincent, Hans, Casey, Tom, and Freddy, after the Commodore and before the Green Tree, during the preparations for Hans’s drinking contest. Marie joins them later at the Harbor House. [A:17-19]\n\nThe Old Wooden Ship in <em>The Devil Is Dead</em> is a four-story, unpainted, pungent tavern at the end of a garlic block, with raked shells for a sidewalk, many doors and stairs, loose shutters, and a ship-like creaking sway. Finnegan and Le Marin frequent it alongside its Company of Fifty resident regulars. Proprietor Lazarus Reilly supplies accommodation, credit, and loans and refuses nobody lest an applicant be an angel in disguise; barmaid Soft-Talk Susie Kutz is called an “angel unaware.” [D:215-216] [D:225] A camel lives in the weed patch behind it. [D:219] Finnegan’s journey to Paradise begins there after his reading of St. Francis de Sales. [D:233] <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> explicitly locates the Old Wooden Ship Tavern, where Gunboat Smith is Bulldog-in-Residence, in Galveston, Texas. [MTM B13:458]","citations":["A:17-19","D:215-216","D:225","D:219","D:233","MTM B13:458"],"tags":["Heaven & Hell","Angels & Demons","Australia","Finnegan","Bars & Taverns","Literature"]},{"name":"Wordless Repartee Between Melchisedech Duffey and the Invisible God, The","definition":"The seventh of Duffey’s possible contingencies: a silent exchange with the invisible God in a garden one afternoon. Everything and a solution to all problems are promised. A failure would have to be sought out through stubbornness and confusion; its disaster might affect only Duffey, or return him to a beginning. The text leaves unclear whether he can choose between the contingencies. [MTM B10:301]","citations":["MTM B10:301"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words","Theology"]},{"name":"Wordsworthian Intimations of a Beginning","definition":"The beginning of a life pattern Finnegan wishes to escape after his death: “Wordsworthian Intimations of a Beginning, Baconian Turgidity in the Middle, and Bierceian Irony at the End.” [D:237] The opening phrase alludes to William Wordsworth’s “Ode: Intimations of Immortality from Recollections of Early Childhood.”","citations":["D:237"],"tags":["Finnegan","Death & Mortality","Language & Words","Literature"]},{"name":"Workman","definition":"The workman in “Great Day in the Morning” who rebukes Melchisedech Duffy for asking what has happened to vehicle wheels, calling him a “roundskin” and “roundheel.” Nearby, workmen and owners are replacing wheels with an indistinct device from kits. [GD:489]","citations":["GD:489"],"tags":["Duffey","Occupations"]},{"name":"World (1) (the World)","definition":"The World into which Finnegan looks with new-made eyes after the Garden disappears, doubting he will find a place there. Vincent, in this opening account, was born in it and belongs there. [A:1-2] Later the Dirty Five, including Vincent, are described as belonging in their fleeting moments to the Wonder rather than the World; the difference between these accounts is left standing. [A:39] Vincent argues that the world might have been created ten minutes ago with its fossils and vestiges already in place, and later declares that the present world exists for an elite of one, himself. These are Vincent’s arguments, not settled narration. [A:56] [A:58]","citations":["A:1-2","A:39","A:56","A:58"],"tags":["Finnegan","Metaphysics","Time & Eternity","Creation"]},{"name":"World (2)","definition":"One of the names X gives for the ship on which Finnegan sailed, alongside <em>Delay</em> and <em>Argo</em>. X’s report is an account of the ship’s uncertain identity, not a verified registry name. [A:187]","citations":["A:187"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Finnegan","Identity"]},{"name":"World Consensus","definition":"The authority whose order to abolish St. Kobarid is defied by King Hiram III, leaving it the world’s last unabolished nation in the fictional account. The passage does not specify what kind of organization the World Consensus is. [MB:563]","citations":["MB:563"],"tags":["Clubs & Societies","Social Class"]},{"name":"World Labor Czar (Poot Plambert)","definition":"Title of Poot Plambert, voted the World’s Most Beloved Man of the Year. King Hiram III refuses to contribute to the worldwide cash benefice for him. [MB:564]","citations":["MB:564"],"tags":["Social Class","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"World of Record, The","definition":"Dotty Yekouris’s name for the place where people wake, sleep, and wake again while their rich other life of the <em>Argo</em> Voyages continues in reserved places. Her statement insists that the voyages are ongoing rather than merely events of many centuries ago. [MTM B5:180]","citations":["MTM B5:180"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Women Characters","History & Memory"]},{"name":"World-Wide Argonauts Argo Balloon Club","definition":"Club of rich sportsmen which Duffey joins or, in another version, founds. Its entrance fee is $3,000. Bagby later claims Duffey collected that sum from each of twenty-three members as North American Bursar; the Geneva headquarters to which he was supposed to remit it never existed. Duffey spends $6,000 on a large custom silk balloon using hydrogen and sandbag ballast. [MTM B2:63-64] He first calls it <em>The Argo Twelve</em>, then changes the name to <em>The Argo Twelve and Half</em> after a powerful misty visitor tells him that <em>Argo Twelve</em> is already active and that his balloon may serve as its pinnace. [MTM B2:64]","citations":["MTM B2:63-64","MTM B2:64"],"tags":["Argonautica","Duffey","Argonauts (Characters)","Money & Trade","Clubs & Societies"]},{"name":"Wright, Peter","definition":"Name on anvils among the household and trade words young Duffey encounters during his mind-piracy. [MTM B1:17]","citations":["MTM B1:17"],"tags":["Duffey","Language & Words"]},{"name":"X","definition":"Mr. X, the former magician and escape artist called the Great X-Capo, is an elusive associate of Finnegan and Duffey [A:137] [D:152]. <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> introduces him as an ally or counter-ally, a shabby, wistful clown of a north Italian type, apparently about Duffey’s age [MTM B6:200]. Duffey tells him that he is one of the people Duffey made [MTM B1:6]. The narrator of <em>Archipelago</em> says that almost all his information is true, but that he cannot evaluate it and is drawn to the excitement of the game. His convictions change: he has joined the Party sincerely, yet makes peace with the Church at Easter, becomes agnostic by midsummer, and atheist by fall [A:138].\n\nIn <em>Archipelago</em> he calls Duffey at three in the morning, disguising his voice with a rosin-impregnated handkerchief or by talking through his hat. He then arrives with nested black, gray, and brown boxes, the last containing the <em>Crock</em>’s intelligence files, which he has stolen from Casey [A:135-136]. He says Casey has ceased properly using the information. X has memorized and annotated it, and spends about two hours writing more cards. He boasts that he opens such locks with mittens on. He visits congressmen and Justice Department agents at midnight, receives tips from Dutch importers and Bucharest rivermen, exchanges Nostrodomo-type prophecies with Jesuit experts, and has been a Party member under different names in France and Italy [A:137-138].\n\n<em>More Than Melchisedech</em> gives another account of this first New Orleans visit: Letitia opens the door to the shy visitor and embraces him, while Duffey is slow to understand who is expected [MTM B6:200]. X brings both the nested boxes and a canister containing Duffey’s own ashes. His old red-box trick becomes the occasion for a real intervention by Duffey [MTM B6:201-203]. Most of the index cards were written by Duffey, with others by X and further contributors. X has added an orange-red card, apparently three times the others’ size yet fitting the box, for the newly released Devil. He claims to have witnessed the release in the Crimea under one of his assumed identities [MTM B6:203-205].\n\nX says that the glassy eye, or <em>oculum vitreum</em>, sometimes reveals that a powerful man receiving his secret information is himself in the Conspiracy. He carries a dagger blade in his shoulder and a pellet in his neck, and recounts escaping after being bound, weighted, and dropped from a garbage scow [A:138]. He calls pursuing the Conspiracy a courageous instance of flogging a dead horse, and credits the Devil with inventing that discouraging phrase. His account of a nocturnal visitor who might have been the Devil remains uncertain; he admits it could have been a hoax. In another sense and another life, he says, he did know the Devil and dug him up [A:139-140]. He has a wife in Milano whom he loves but has not seen for years. Danger and intervening circumstances first delay his return; then fear of her irony and his renewed shyness keep him away [A:140-141].\n\nHe sends his New Orleans friends a false clipping describing his own murder, including the identifying blade and pellet. Dotty recognizes both his rosin and the Pelican Press’s worn type, and he later returns sheepishly [A:141-142]. In Paros he admits the trick to Finnegan, whom he knows in both his lives, and proposes pooling their knowledge of Papadiabolous. He acknowledges that he missed the burial but says he may tell the story later as an eyewitness [D:151-153].\n\nHe joins Finnegan and Doll Delancy during their night out in Baltimore, seeming a comical little drunk, though Finnegan realizes he is sober [D:172-174]. He helps them leave as pursuers mistake another large-nosed man for Finnegan. X has lifted the suitcase claim check from Doll’s bag, retrieved the <em>chrysus</em>, and substituted a similar suitcase containing thirty-six thousand pieces of cut newspaper [D:177-178]. The original case contains thirty-six thousand pieces of currency [D:166] [D:185]. He buys an eight-toned car with a D.C. plate, the hurtling rainbow or Celestial Omnibus. He explains its conspicuousness through a sequence of butterfly and klunker counter-plans, but admits Doll is right: he chose it because he liked it, and the explanation is rationale [D:177] [D:179-180]. He divides the money among three bags, giving Finnegan the heaviest and Doll the smallest [D:180].\n\nIn Florida he makes frequent long-distance calls and speaks a coded sequence beginning with Xochilmileo. That passage does not clearly establish the word as his own alias [D:183-184]. Doll proposes their eventual separation in three vehicles; X arranges signals, briefing, and a rendezvous a year, a day, and some odd weeks later beside Ifreann’s grave. He warns that he will select, arrange, and fictionalize details when telling their story [D:185-186]. After Finnegan’s crisis he gives the lengthy briefing, then leaves in the Celestial Omnibus [D:193]. Later he calls Finnegan in Biloxi with a disguised voice, warning him to leave and avoid his old haunts [D:208-209].\n\nAt the graveyard rendezvous X still needs Finnegan to locate the grave. He explains its I.N.C.G.P. Bogovitch inscription as a softened form of Chortovitch, distinguishing the elder Ifreann Noonan Columkill Gregorovitch Papadiabolous Chortovitch from his sons [D:275-278]. X believes that the Devil who fathered Ifreann was also his own grandfather and Finnegan’s and Anastasia’s great-grandfather. He admits the alternative that Gregory, Ifreann’s adoptive father, was his biological father, though he rejects it [D:279]. His account distinguishes the quasi-twins born to different mothers: Papadiabolous I, Gregory McIfreann, and Papadiabolous II, Gregory McColum, the undercover policeman he knew as Noonan or N. X is unsure even whom he himself now serves [D:285-287].\n\nX says the elder Ifreann was buried in 1930, that Seaworthy killed Papadiabolous I while reopening that father’s grave, and that II assumed his brother’s place; several details are explicitly conjectural [D:288-289]. He has forged taunting notes in Papadiabolous’s handwriting to bring Seaworthy back. He intends to reopen the grave for a key document and trap Seaworthy there, while warning that Finnegan may not survive his own encounter [D:289-291]. X regards ordinary people as incapable sheep and expects resistance to come from a minority of the older race. Doll challenges this contempt and insists that people can act to save themselves [D:290] [D:292].\n\nIn Duffey’s later travels X appears as Carmelo Mendoza and Karl Metz, with Mr. Eggs as another form of his name. Horace Pie recognizes him before Duffey does; X supplies information about Finnegan’s submerged resting place near Cuba’s Marianao coast [MTM B12:371]. With Kasmir Gorshok he finds and identifies Duffey’s remains in Gur’yev. He keeps the ashes, anticipating a chance to return them to Duffey alive, while the bones are carried aboard the <em>Argo</em>. He is explicitly its Half-Master [MTM B13:442-443].\n\nAs Monsignor X he serves the Final Conclave in <em>How Many Miles to Babylon</em>, including putting the straw effigy and six bodies into the stove [MB:566] [MB:568]. When Hackenschmidt orders his death, X produces a submachine gun and leaves. He declares that he will sometimes work for the former princes, sometimes for the thirteen absent princes, and sometimes for their contracted killers, Track and Total: nothing less than a triple agent would suit him. He also promises to ornament the story across its disordered tenses. Here his vaudeville name is printed Great Ex-Capo [MB:569].","citations":["A:137","D:152","MTM B6:200","MTM B1:6","A:138","A:135-136","A:137-138","MTM B6:201-203","MTM B6:203-205","A:139-140","A:140-141","A:141-142","D:151-153","D:172-174","D:177-178","D:166","D:185","D:177","D:179-180","D:180","D:183-184","D:185-186","D:193","D:208-209","D:275-278","D:279","D:285-287","D:288-289","D:289-291","D:290","D:292","MTM B12:371","MTM B13:442-443","MB:566","MB:568","MB:569"],"tags":["The Devil","Devils (Characters)","Argonauts (Characters)","Finnegan","Duffey","Identity","History & Memory","Theater","Crime","Weapons","Italy","France","Family","Ships","Ekklesia","Time & Eternity","Death & Mortality","Creation"]},{"name":"X-Capo, The Great","definition":"See <em>Great X-Capo, The</em> [A:137] [D:276].","citations":["A:137","D:276"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Theater","Identity"]},{"name":"Xavier (St. Xavier)","definition":"St. Francis Xavier (1506-1552), the Jesuit missionary to India, the Indonesian archipelago, and Japan. Finnegan hails him as apostle to the greater Archipelagos and imagines his recognition of a miracle: after the world’s end was reached, there was still an ocean full of islands. [A:35] Xavier also appears among the saints on Henry’s dream-ship <em>Navicula Petri</em>. [A:45]","citations":["A:35","A:45"],"tags":["Saints","Miracles","Ships","Seas & Oceans","Islands","Finnegan","Dreams"]},{"name":"Xenoi","definition":"The word the talking oak translates as “Strangers” when X asks to join the <em>Argo</em>. X claims that Attic ephebic inscriptions use X for <em>Xenoi</em>, answering the oak’s demand that every passenger be found in Scripture or Inscription. The oak’s translation reinforces its claim not to know him. The speaking piece of oak is in the ship’s wheel. [MTM B13:434-435]","citations":["MTM B13:434-435"],"tags":["Ships","Greece","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Xochilmileo","definition":"Word X uses at the beginning of a coded telephone exchange. It is followed by Vinschoten, Vlissinger, Spink, Tineo, Abo, Imazu, Ligueria, Sheboygan, Absecon, Xariia, Oquawka, and Neffs, and by a question about the listener’s code book. The passage does not establish that “Xochilmileo” is X’s own alias or explain its meaning. [D:184]","citations":["D:184"],"tags":["Language & Words","Identity"]},{"name":"Yalta Conference","definition":"The historical conference of Roosevelt, Churchill, and Stalin at Yalta in the Crimea, February 4-11, 1945. In X’s fictional account, notables had visited the neighborhood for years to consult the imprisoned Devil and learn when he would be released; X further claims that the conference itself depended on daily or hourly consultations with him. [MTM B6:204]","citations":["MTM B6:204"],"tags":["The Devil","World War II","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Yanks","definition":"Colloquial name for Americans, used variously in jokes, rivalry, and mistaken expectations. Tom Shire and Freddy Castle pretend that Finnegan and Vincent need disguises to enter an Australian wet canteen. Sergeant Bushmaster actually greets the “Yanks” hospitably, although he later taunts their drinking capacity and suggests goats’ milk. [A:11-15] Finnegan is induced to sign an anti-Yank affidavit conceding Australian superiority at beer drinking, calling Americans wet behind the ears, and adding explicit obscenities. [A:13] Bushmaster calls Hans a “red-eyed Yankee” during their drinking contest. [A:22]\n\nMarie thinks of Americans and Arabs as similarly close “Peoples of the Book”; this is her comparison. [A:7] In Cuba, Ignacio is surprised that Finnegan is a Yankee because he lacks Ignacio’s expected New England traits. Visiting American tourists speak pidgin Spanish to Finnegan as though he were a barefoot Latino. [A:273]","citations":["A:11-15","A:13","A:22","A:7","A:273"],"tags":["Australia","Finnegan","Soldiers","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Yekouris (family name)","definition":"Family name of Dorothy Mary (“Dotty”) Yekouris. [A:95] In <em>The Devil Is Dead</em>, Finnegan includes her among the friends of his normal life while talking to another self across a table. Despite knowing these people, he feels a barrier between himself and humankind. [D:90]","citations":["A:95","D:90"],"tags":["Finnegan","Women Characters","Family","Identity"]},{"name":"Yekouris, Dorothy (Dotty)","definition":"Dorothy Mary Yekouris, usually Dotty, a New Orleans printer’s daughter raised in the trade who also takes up bartending when printing does not provide a living. She comes to St. Louis for Vincent and Show Boat’s wedding in the last week of May 1946, staying with Show Boat. She and Finnegan love each other, but his wandering and conviction of irretrievable loss keep them apart; the narrator says no sharp word will pass between them. [A:95] [A:106] [MTM B5:171] She has attended bartenders’ school and is particularly angered when Casey’s hostile editorial questions her ability to distinguish drinks. [A:129] [A:131] <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> also makes her a member of the Poison Pen Society. [MTM B5:183]\n\nDotty and Duffey establish <em>The Bark</em> at the Pelican Press in New Orleans. She is one of its original seven trustees and directors, the Seven Pillars of Righteousness; Finnegan’s masthead calls her “The Beautiful Barmaid.” She, Duffey, Stein, and Mary Virginia do the actual work of running the paper. [A:121-122] [MTM B6:198] [MTM B7:214-215] Dotty’s immense journalistic talent is acknowledged by both Stein and Duffey; under her editorship the paper outdoes its diabolical rivals in liveliness, intelligence, printing, writers, and features. The press also issues seamen’s, union, sporting, and jazz papers to support its work. [MTM B7:212-213] Her many suitors include men associated with these papers, but the narrator says she loves only Finnegan. Called a “beautiful bonus forever,” she is extravagantly described through a borrowed nursery catalogue’s flower and fruit adjectives, ending “hardy and disease-resistant.” She is the New Orleans native whom Stein calls a gracious French lady who turns out to be Lithuanian. It is Dotty who calls Mary Virginia the “Saccharine Kid,” partly affectionately and partly jealously. [MTM B7:212-214]\n\nDotty belongs among Duffey’s Animated Marvels. During the argument over whether Duffey created or merely collected these people, she warns him that changing them would be at his peril: they like themselves and him as they are. [MTM B5:159] She is credited with <em>Pelican Pellets</em>, including a limerick about Duffey’s inventions, and with a statement that the <em>Argo</em> Voyages continue as their rich other life beyond the “World of Record.” The wedding gathering identifies her among the people of the Argo Legend and the original crew. [MTM B5:171] [MTM B5:177] [MTM B5:180] [MTM B5:183] Her New Orleans circle includes Duffey, Mary Virginia, Margaret Stone, Salvation Sally, Stein, Zabotski, and X. She jokes that X should be given milk and put out as an unaffordable pet, then welcomes him; later she firmly refuses to publish his list of those present at the Devil’s release. [A:136] [MTM B6:195-198] [MTM B6:206] [MTM B7:212-215]\n\nIn <em>Archipelago</em>, a Havana lawyer’s letter prompts Dotty’s voyage to Finnegan; she mentions earlier letters and money from Finnegan himself. After arrival, Ignacio maps Finnegan’s walks and explains the danger. Dotty buys a pistol, tracks the left-footed Nick the Rattle, disables his car, and shoots him. Nick returns fire on her and Finnegan; the book leaves the three exchanging unfailing shots, with both lovers repeatedly wounded. [A:277] [A:280-282] <em>More Than Melchisedech</em> instead emphasizes letters from Ignacio and Elena and says Finnegan did not write to her directly. Dotty leaves, does not return, and no solid news follows. X reports both lovers slain near Havana but will not swear that their deaths are final and still feels their presences. These accounts preserve uncertainty about their ultimate fate. [MTM B8:237-238]","citations":["A:95","A:106","MTM B5:171","A:129","A:131","MTM B5:183","A:121-122","MTM B6:198","MTM B7:214-215","MTM B7:212-213","MTM B7:212-214","MTM B5:159","MTM B5:177","MTM B5:180","A:136","MTM B6:195-198","MTM B6:206","MTM B7:212-215","A:277","A:280-282","MTM B8:237-238"],"tags":["Miracles","Magic & Sorcery","Ships","New Orleans","Caribbean","Finnegan","Duffey","Women Characters","Weapons","Bars & Taverns","Literature","Journalism","Identity","Creation"]},{"name":"Yellow Sea","definition":"Sea between mainland China and the Korean Peninsula, included among the many seas sailed by the <em>Argo</em> and remembered by Melchisedech. [MTM B13:406]","citations":["MTM B13:406"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans","Duffey"]},{"name":"Yenbo","definition":"Place paired with Lith in the opening poem of the chapter “Finnegan: Who Is Iason, but Who Is Also of the Other People”: “The Desert is burning at Yenbo and Lith.” [A:171]","citations":["A:171"],"tags":["Finnegan","Literature"]},{"name":"Yiddish","definition":"Language invoked in several character descriptions and verbal performances. Duffey’s voice is Boston Irish with a touch of stage Yiddish. [A:97] Hans’s mother Irene, recalling Professor Kirol, says that “phoney” is Yiddish rather than English or German and once carried more humor and less shoddiness; this is her etymological claim. [A:211] In the graveyard, X boasts that although his listeners hear his explanation of the Papadiabolous family, watchers reading his lips through binoculars see mouth movements telling humorous Yiddish stories. [D:278]","citations":["A:97","A:211","D:278"],"tags":["The Devil","Duffey","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Yoder, Ant (One Shot)","definition":"Resident regular at the Old Wooden Ship, listed as Ant (One Shot) Yoder in its Company of Fifty. [D:215-216] The later account calls Art (One Shot) Yodel the partner of Art (Two Shot) Welch: they are unsuccessful card sharps and tricksters, often outwitted by better sharpies. The matching nickname and partner suggest a textual name variant, which should not be silently regularized. [D:221]","citations":["D:215-216","D:221"],"tags":["Minor Characters","Bars & Taverns","Crime"]},{"name":"Yokipoki","definition":"Name Don Lewis gives, while admitting it is probably not the real one, to the ragged man who seems to be a Japanese soldier on Pulau Petir or Willy Jones Island on October 1, 1944. The patrol consists of Sergeant Charles Santee, Corporal Robert Casper, and PFC Timothy Lorrigan. Lewis initially withholds which soldier he was, but his later correction of “I” to Lorrigan strongly identifies him with the private. [D:44-46]\n\nThe little man repeatedly disappears when encircled and reappears hundreds of yards farther away. Santee wounds him; after a long pursuit, Santee and Casper kill him while Lorrigan hangs back. Lost and quarrelling, the three soldiers separate for the night. [D:44-46] Lewis remains in the valley and uses his short shovel and the dead man’s double-edged butcher knife to dig a grave. He removes Yokipoki’s boots, gags him, binds wrists and ankles, bandages his eyes, covers him with mud, says “Requiem” and “lux perpetua,” and drinks to him as a Christian burial. Later squealing and movement in the grave terrify Lewis, who imagines the dead man cutting his bonds with the buried knife; after an attack on his ankle, he recognizes rats at the corpse. [D:46-47]","citations":["D:44-46","D:46-47"],"tags":["Islands","Pacific Theater","Minor Characters","The Dead","World War II","Soldiers","Weapons","Identity","Death & Mortality"]},{"name":"Z'drovie","definition":"Casey Szymansky’s toast when he, Henry, and Finnegan share a drink in their jungle bivouac. Henry answers with “Sante,” and Finnegan with Irish “Slainthe” and Italian “salute.” [A:44] Casey’s spelling resembles Slavic health toasts, particularly Polish <em>na zdrowie</em>; the novel does not explicitly label its language.","citations":["A:44"],"tags":["Pacific Theater","Eastern Europe","Finnegan","Alcohol","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Zabotski (Zab)","definition":"Zabotski, often Zab, is an experimenter, artist, prophet, historian, and probably wealthy landlord, formerly a chemist. Abrasive and loud, he looks smelly without smelling bad; he carries tenants more generously than his detractors. [MTM B11:306] He supplies the glass-lined thousand-gallon crock for Duffey’s Big Pot and courts the Widow Waldo, who becomes Wife Waldo. [MTM B6:195] [MTM B6:197] [MTM B9:285] He lives on Dumaine Street between Duffey and Homer Hoose before moving with her to his floating home on Lake Borgne. His claim to be gentle coexists with quarrelsome behavior and his neighbors’ complaints. [MTM B9:267-269]\n\nHe builds an enormous backyard contraption from an ancient illustrated instruction text he thinks is in Hazh-Khazh, initially guessing it is a barn or castle. After talking with the Fundamentalers, he recognizes the ark’s manual and purpose. [MTM B9:272] [MTM B9:284] He calls himself an artist and prophet before a historian and writes <em>Pristine History of the World</em>. His Pristine World occupies the same space as the slow Everyday-or-Molasses World but excludes duplication and invalidity, allowing years of experience within ordinary days. He and Duffey debate these accounts of time, origins, and history with the Fundamentalers; Duffey then makes a Pristine journey himself. [MTM B9:277-284]\n\nZabotski curses Blackstone’s sycamore and predicts Blackstone’s death while denying that he can cause a man’s death. The apparent next morning brings a missing tree and an older Bandicoot, who says it died and was cut down years ago. Blackstone’s later death completes Zabotski’s “five days” against a much longer family chronology. [MTM B9:269-270] [MTM B9:276] [MTM B9:288] In one account at the Decatur Street Opera House, bulls kill him during the Cretan Bull Drama; he gives a last bellow after death and earns the Ugly Ovation. The narrator, rather than Zabotski, says he makes a good show. [MTM B11:349] In the later boat-castle visit, he instead reports surviving an attempted lynching near the Opera House with help from poor neighbors, although his tongue is mangled. He is living with Wife Waldo and fifty-two children. These divergent outcomes remain part of the narrative. [MTM B12:362-364]","citations":["MTM B11:306","MTM B6:195","MTM B6:197","MTM B9:285","MTM B9:267-269","MTM B9:272","MTM B9:284","MTM B9:277-284","MTM B9:269-270","MTM B9:276","MTM B9:288","MTM B11:349","MTM B12:362-364"],"tags":["Old Testament","Magic & Sorcery","Ships","Duffey","The Dead","Theater","Money & Trade","Time & Eternity","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Zabotski Flats","definition":"About two hundred acres bought by Zabotski on Lake Borgne, where his boat-castle rides at a kedge anchor. Two-thirds are sweet meadows and one-third salt, extraordinarily lush though neighboring land is sparse. Zabotski attributes the transformation to God’s blessing. [MTM B12:362] Lions and leopards eat the abundant Nimrod Flesh Plant; hippopotami and leviathan graze in the salt meadows, whales visit the deeper water, and water buffaloes use both portions. Zebras, asses, cattle, sheep, goats, rabbits, coneys, and smaller game occupy the sweet-water parts. [MTM B12:362-363]","citations":["MTM B12:362","MTM B12:362-363"],"tags":["Old Testament","Monsters & Creatures","Ships","Ports & Harbors","Food & Dining"]},{"name":"Zabotski Folly (Zabotsky House, Zabotski Happening)","definition":"The giant, outlandish structure Zabotski builds in his Dumaine Street backyard, intruding onto Duffey’s, Hoose’s, Blackstone’s, and the orphanage’s land. [MTM B9:268-271] He works from ancient rock drawings and writing he thinks is in Hazh-Khazh, at first unable to tell whether he is constructing a barn, castle, or something else. Waldo identifies its overlapping prototypes as ship, castle, anti-time machine, and the old woman’s shoe. Zabotski eventually recognizes the ark’s instruction manual. [MTM B9:272-275] [MTM B9:284]\n\nWith Wife Waldo, children, and strange animals aboard, it floats away in a sudden flood toward the Shoal Street Viaduct. Zabotski insists this will be the original Flood and says they can travel on water, air, or space; the structure comes to rest at anchor on his Lake Borgne property, near Pristine Cove. [MTM B9:267] [MTM B9:285-287] The magazine <em>House and Home Happenings</em> introduces a projected two-part feature, publishing Duffey’s “myth of origin” and promising the architectural description later. Its rapidity blurs fast photographs and changes a reporter’s experience of elapsed time. [MTM B9:267] [MTM B9:288] At Duffey’s later visit, the ark houses fifty-two unrelated children whom Zabotski praises as exceptionally capable, along with many animals; its surrounding meadows support hippopotami, leviathan, buffaloes, zebras, lions, and leopards. [MTM B12:362-364]","citations":["MTM B9:268-271","MTM B9:272-275","MTM B9:284","MTM B9:267","MTM B9:285-287","MTM B9:288","MTM B12:362-364"],"tags":["Old Testament","Monsters & Creatures","Ships","Duffey","Literature","Time & Eternity","Creation"]},{"name":"Zabotsky House","definition":"See <em>Zabotski Folly (Zabotsky House, Zabotski Happening)</em>, the structure introduced in <em>House and Home Happenings Magazine</em>. [MTM B9:267]","citations":["MTM B9:267"],"tags":["Ships","Literature"]},{"name":"Zack","definition":"Australian slang for a sixpence. The Sydney wet-canteen barman tells Finnegan that a zack, not a supposed beer chit, is all he needs to buy a beer. [A:14]","citations":["A:14"],"tags":["Australia","Bars & Taverns","Alcohol","Money & Trade"]},{"name":"Zakinthos","definition":"Greek stop reached by the <em>Brunhilde</em> after Messina. The narrator places it within Anastasia’s sphere. Finnegan initially feels alien while his companions are at home, then suddenly feels himself one of the oldest and most other people: it is a region where duality is strongest. [D:115]","citations":["D:115"],"tags":["Ships","Sailing","Seas & Oceans","Islands","Greece","Finnegan","Women Characters"]},{"name":"Zauberkonzert","definition":"Composition attributed to Andreyev by the expert codenamed Philo Vance, who says that one answer to the Man of Mystery problem lies in its eighth movement. He then broadens the claim absurdly to the eighth movement of anything, African violets, cribbage, and checkers, finally advising Duffey to get a dog. [MTM B13:457]","citations":["MTM B13:457"],"tags":["Music","Paradox"]},{"name":"Zauberlehrling","definition":"“Sorcerer’s Apprentice,” the carnival nickname Betty tells Hans Schultz he has acquired while studying with Professor Kirol von Weinsberg-Valeni. [A:217] It recalls Goethe’s poem “Der Zauberlehrling,” in which an apprentice’s borrowed magic escapes his control.","citations":["A:217"],"tags":["Magic & Sorcery","Germany","Literature","Carnival"]},{"name":"Zeno Paradoxes","definition":"The philosophical paradoxes of Zeno of Elea, a fifth-century BC thinker, concerning such problems as plurality, motion, and divisibility. During Duffey’s garden encounter, the Invisible God says, without words, that the Melchisedech Paradoxes are at least as wonderful as Zeno’s and that every person is uniquely surrounded by paradoxes. [MTM B12:389]","citations":["MTM B12:389"],"tags":["Duffey","Time & Eternity","Paradox","Theology"]},{"name":"Zero Growth lobby","definition":"One of the lobbies represented among the new Princes, Gates, and Stones in the attempted broadening of the Ekklesia before its Last Conclave. The passage gives no detailed program for this lobby. [MB:561-562]","citations":["MB:561-562"],"tags":["Ecclesiastical","Ekklesia","Social Class"]},{"name":"Ziggarat","definition":"Name the hoyden gives the one low mountain supposedly present before the multiplication of mountains. In her mythic account it served as the Mountain of the Commandments, was rolled from country to country, and prefigured Ararat. [MTM B11:333-334]","citations":["MTM B11:333-334"],"tags":["Folklore","Other Mythologies"]},{"name":"Zingaro (Gitano)","definition":"Italian <em>zingaro</em> and Spanish <em>gitano</em>, terms traditionally rendered “Gypsy.” Niccolo Crotolus uses them for his family’s claimed background as dancers and musicians. He says that Crotolo is their family name and an old word for a castanet; this is his explanation, offered against the narrator’s alternative nickname derivation. [A:278-279]","citations":["A:278-279"],"tags":["Italy","Spain","Music","Family","History & Memory","Identity","Language & Words"]},{"name":"Zippo the Fire-Eater","definition":"Carnival fire-eater whose image Hans Schultz says he painted, along with Edgar the Seal and other acts, for carnival advertising. Hans recalls spectators saying the performances were not as good as his pictures. [A:230]","citations":["A:230"],"tags":["Visual Arts","Carnival"]},{"name":"Zoar","definition":"The big African drummer in the Brimstone Combo in Trinali’s poem about the Green Groves of Gomorrah and the saloon at Beelzebub and Hell-Raiser Streets. [A:235] His name recalls the biblical Zoar, the town to which Lot escapes from Sodom in Genesis 19.","citations":["A:235"],"tags":["Old Testament","Scripture","Africa","Bars & Taverns","Music","Literature"]},{"name":"Zodiac, Casey of the","definition":"Identity of Casey Szymansky, also Kasmir Gorshok and Casey of the Crock, explicitly recognized as a true <em>Argo</em> Master. [MTM B13:426] Duffey earlier reports Casey’s offer to exchange souls with the Devil and suffer in his place, while Stein claims a lesser trade of old souls between himself and Casey. [MTM B5:169] Hilary Hilton later identifies Casey as Antichrist; Casey himself announces “I am Casey of the Zodiac, the Anti-Christus.” [MTM B12:378] [MTM B12:382]\n\nCasey has projected his idealized face and voice over television programs, building his cult. The grotesque head shown at Hilton’s party, however, is projected by Duffey from an Adam Scanlon painting. Casey denounces it as a fraud before finally yielding to Duffey’s demand to meet. [MTM B12:380-382]","citations":["MTM B13:426","MTM B5:169","MTM B12:378","MTM B12:382","MTM B12:380-382"],"tags":["New Testament","Apocalyptic","The Devil","Metaphysics","Magic & Sorcery"]},{"name":"Zombie devil","definition":"The figure invoked when the narrator calls the <em>Argo</em>’s mechanical crewmen “as robotic as the zombie devil himself, only not cute.” The crewmen are place tokens or materialized residues of Argonauts who have sailed before and will return, including Duffey and Brannagan during their absences. The passage does not name this “zombie devil.” [MTM B13:415]","citations":["MTM B13:415"],"tags":["Argonautica","The Devil","Ships","Crew & Sailors","Argonauts (Characters)","Time & Eternity","History & Memory"]},{"name":"Zoot suit","definition":"An exaggerated suit style associated with the 1940s. In Askandan’s sales pitch, the outdated zoot suit he offers a naked Casey is rare and will supposedly fit anyone because nobody can tell whether it fits. In fact it is painfully too small. Finnegan bought it for nine dollars for the prank; Askandan demands sixty-five and then seventy-five dollars before selling it with pointed shoes and a monstrous hat on credit. Casey’s predicament finally makes him laugh. [A:260-261]","citations":["A:260-261"],"tags":["Money & Trade","Identity"]},{"name":"Zuqar","definition":"Place where “The Sea is on Fire” in the opening poem of Finnegan’s chapter. The adjacent burning-desert line concerns Yenbo and Lith, not Zuqar. [A:171] A plausible geographical referent is Zukur or Zugar, the volcanic island in the southern Red Sea’s Zukur-Hanish group; the poem does not explicitly establish the identification.","citations":["A:171"],"tags":["Seas & Oceans","Islands","Finnegan","Literature"]}]);
